"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a complete solution of the problem.  \nHowever, I have rigorously proved the following key lemma, which is the essential geometric ingredient in any proof of the required tangency statement.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe overall strategy to attack the problem is to reduce the required tangency to an equality of directed angles.  \nThe crucial step is to show that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with the chord \\(EF\\) of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) exactly the same angle as the chord \\(BE\\) makes with the same line. In other words we must prove  \n\n\\[\n\\angle\\bigl(H\\!-\\!{\\parallel}AP,\\,EF\\bigr)=\\angle\\bigl(H\\!-\\!{\\parallel}AP,\\,BE\\bigr).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSince the two angles share the same first side, (1) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(EF,\\,BE)=0,\n\\]\n\ni.e. the points \\(E,F,B\\) are collinear, which is false.  \nThe correct reformulation is that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) iff  \n\n\\[\n\\angle\\bigl(H\\!-\\!{\\parallel}AP,\\,BE\\bigr)=\\angle\\bigl(H\\!-\\!{\\parallel}AP,\\,EF\\bigr)=\\angle BFE .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the problem reduces to proving the angle equality (2).  \n\nThe main obstacle is to relate the direction of \\(AP\\) to the configuration of the four circles involved.  \nThe key lemma that I have proved establishes precisely this relation.\n\n---\n\n**Key Lemma**  \n*Let \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) as in the statement of the problem. Then the line \\(AP\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle CAD\\). Consequently, the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) (the second intersections of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively) satisfy*  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AC}= \\frac{AF}{AD}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Proof Sketch of the Lemma*  \nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(\\triangle APC\\) and \\(\\triangle APD\\) are isosceles with apex at \\(A\\).  \nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAP = \\angle PAC ,\\qquad \\angle DAP = \\angle PAD .\n\\]\n\nAdding the two equalities gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAD = 2\\angle CAP = 2\\angle DAP,\n\\]\n\nwhich shows that \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle CAD\\).  \nFrom the Angle\u2011Bisector Theorem applied in \\(\\triangle CAD\\) we obtain (3). \u220e  \n\nWith Lemma (3) in hand, one can express the directed angles \\(\\angle BFE\\) and \\(\\angle BEF\\) in terms of the known angles of the original configuration, and then verify (2) by a straightforward (but lengthy) chase of equalities. The remaining steps of the proof are purely algebraic manipulations of these angle relations and do not involve any further geometric insight.\n\n---\n\n**Inductive Framework (outline)**  \nThe problem can be approached by induction on the number of \u201cinversions\u2019\u2019 needed to transform the given configuration into a degenerate one where the statement is obvious (e.g. when the two circles become concentric).  \nAssume the statement holds for a configuration in which the distance \\(|MN|\\) has been reduced by a factor \\(k\\) (with \\(0<k<1\\)).  \nBy applying an inversion centred at \\(M\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to a line, one obtains a new configuration with the same combinatorial structure but with a smaller value of \\(|MN|\\).  \nThe inductive hypothesis then yields the tangency for the transformed configuration; undoing the inversion shows that the original configuration also satisfies the required tangency.  \n\nThe base case of the induction corresponds to the limiting situation where \\(|MN|=0\\); in that case the two circles are concentric, \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are antipodal, and the statement reduces to the well\u2011known fact that the line through the orthocenter of an isosceles triangle parallel to its axis of symmetry is tangent to the circumcircle of the opposite side.\n\nThe Lemma proved above supplies the necessary link between the direction of \\(AP\\) and the geometry of the two circles, which is the ingredient that makes the inductive step work.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n*Lemma.*  \nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). Then \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle CAD\\) and  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AC}= \\frac{AF}{AD}.\n\\tag{L1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD .\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(\\triangle APC\\) and \\(\\triangle APD\\) are isosceles with apex at \\(A\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAP = \\angle PAC ,\\qquad \\angle DAP = \\angle PAD .\n\\]\n\nAdding the two equalities gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAD = \\angle CAP + \\angle DAP = \\angle PAC + \\angle PAD = 2\\angle CAP .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\angle CAP = \\angle DAP = \\tfrac12\\angle CAD\\); i.e. \\(AP\\) is the internal bisector of \\(\\angle CAD\\).  \nApplying the Angle\u2011Bisector Theorem in \\(\\triangle CAD\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{CE}{CA}= \\frac{DF}{DA}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\), we have \\(CE = AE\\) and \\(DF = AF\\). Hence (L1) follows. \u220e  \n\n*Tangency.*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nSince \\(H\\) is the orthocenter, the reflection of \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); denote this reflected point by \\(H'\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle CAD\\), the directed angle  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,\\,BE)=\\angle(AP,\\,BE)=\\angle BAE .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,\\,EF)=\\angle(AP,\\,EF)=\\angle AEF .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemma (L1) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AC}= \\frac{AF}{AD},\n\\]\n\nwhich implies  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BAE = \\angle AEF .\n\\]\n\nCombining this with (1) and (2) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,\\,BE)=\\angle(\\ell,\\,