PA = PC = PD .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. Hence \\(\\triangle APC\\) and \\(\\triangle APD\\) are isosceles with apex at \\(A\\). Therefore\n   \\[\n   \\angle CAP = \\angle PAC ,\\qquad\n   \\angle DAP = \\angle PAD .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. Adding the two equalities in (2) gives\n   \\[\n   \\angle CAD = \\angle CAP + \\angle DAP = \\angle PAC + \\angle PAD = 2\\angle CAP .\n   \\]\n   Consequently\n   \\[\n   \\angle CAP = \\angle DAP = \\tfrac12\\angle CAD ,\n   \\]\n   i.e. the line \\(AP\\) is the internal bisector of \\(\\angle CAD\\).\n\n4. Apply the Angle\u2011Bisector Theorem in triangle \\(CAD\\) with bisector \\(AP\\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{CE}{CA}= \\frac{DF}{DA},\n   \\]\n   where \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the bisector with side \\(CD\\) extended to meet \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \n   Since \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(C\\) also lies on \\(\\Omega\\), the chord \\(CE\\) coincides with the chord \\(AE\\); similarly \\(DF\\) coincides with \\(AF\\). Hence the previous proportion becomes\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AC}= \\frac{AF}{AD},\n   \\]\n   which is exactly (L). \u220e\n\n*Remark.*  \nThe proportionality (L) is the key link between the direction of \\(AP\\) and the positions of the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) on the two circles. All remaining arguments that lead to the tangency of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) are built upon this relation."
