"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full.  \nHence the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by a sequence of clean geometric observations that reduce the problem to a well\u2011known configuration.\n\n1. **Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point and equal angles.**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); consequently \\(AP\\perp CD\\) and \\(AP\\) is the external angle bisector of \\(\\angle CAB\\) and \\(\\angle DAB\\).  \n   From the intersecting\u2011chords theorem applied to the two given circles we obtain\n   \\[\n   AC\\cdot AD = AB^{2}=AE\\cdot AF .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Similarity of triangles.**  \n   Using (1) we get\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AC}= \\frac{AF}{AD},\n   \\]\n   which together with \\(\\angle EAP=\\angle CAP\\) (since \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\)) yields the similarity\n   \\[\n   \\triangle AEC\\sim\\triangle AFD .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   From (2) we deduce\n   \\[\n   \\angle CEA = \\angle DFA .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Cyclic quadrilateral \\(BEF\\).**  \n   Because \\(A,B\\) belong to both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the power relation (1) also gives\n   \\[\n   AB^{2}=AE\\cdot AF=BE\\cdot BF .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(B\\) has equal power with respect to the circle \\((AEF)\\) and the circle \\((BEF)\\); consequently the four points \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic.\n\n4. **Location of the orthocenter \\(H\\).**  \n   In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Since \\(AP\\perp CD\\) and \\(CD\\) is collinear with \\(MN\\), we have\n   \\[\n   AP\\parallel \\text{altitude from }P .\n   \\]\n   Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is precisely the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the altitude from \\(P\\); denote this line by \\(\\ell\\).\n\n5. **Angle chase.**  \n   Using the orthocenter property we have\n   \\[\n   \\angle HPN = 90^{\\circ}-\\angle MPN = \\angle MNP .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(M,N,P\\) are collinear with the midpoint of \\(CD\\), the right\u2011angle at \\(P\\) gives\n   \\[\n   \\angle HPN = \\angle CDE .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   Combining (5) with (3) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle (\\ell , BE)=\\angle CEA =\\angle DFA .\n   \\]\n   But \\(\\angle DFA\\) is exactly the angle subtended by chord \\(DF\\) at the point \\(E\\) on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Hence\n   \\[\n   \\angle (\\ell , BE)=\\angle BFE .\n   \\]\n\n6. **Tangency.**  \n   The equality \\(\\angle (\\ell , BE)=\\angle BFE\\) shows that \\(\\ell\\) makes with the chord \\(BE\\) the same angle as the tangent at \\(E\\) to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to that circumcircle at \\(E\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the two given circles be \\(\\Omega\\) (center \\(M\\), radius \\(r\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (center \\(N\\), radius \\(R\\)), with \\(r<R\\).  \nThey intersect at distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) (different from \\(M\\)) and meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) (different from \\(N\\)), with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\) on the line.\n\nDenote by \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nSince \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(A,C,D\\), the segment \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nAP\\perp CD\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad AC=AD .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nLet the line \\(AP\\) intersect \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and intersect \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Power relations\n\nBecause \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) lie on both circles, the intersecting\u2011chords theorem applied to \\(\\Omega\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nAC\\cdot AD = AB^{2}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nApplying the same theorem to \\(\\Gamma\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AF = AB^{2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nAC\\cdot AD = AE\\cdot AF .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Similarity of \\(\\triangle AEC\\) and \\(\\triangle AFD\\)\n\nFrom (4) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AC}= \\frac{AF}{AD}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\), \\(\\angle EAC = \\angle CAC = \\angle CAP\\); similarly \\(\\angle FAD = \\angle DAP\\).  \nThus \\(\\angle EAP = \\angle CAP\\) and \\(\\angle FAP = \\angle DAP\\).  \nTogether with (5) this gives the similarity  \n\n\\[\n\\triangle AEC \\sim \\triangle AFD .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CEA = \\angle DFA .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. The quadrilateral \\(B,E,F\\) is cyclic\n\nFrom (2) and (3) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AF = BE\\cdot BF .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus the power of point \\(B\\) with respect to the circle \\((AEF)\\) equals its power with respect to the circle \\((BEF)\\); therefore the three points \\(E,F\\) together with \\(B\\) lie on a common circle. Denote this circle by \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Position of the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nBecause \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear, \\(MN\\) is the same line as \\(CD\\).  \nFrom (1) we have \\(AP\\perp CD\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nAP\\parallel \\text{altitude from }P\\text{ in }\\triangle PMN .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the altitude from \\(P\\); denote this line by \\(\\ell\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Angle chase linking \\(\\ell\\) and the circle \\(\\omega\\)\n\nSince \\(H\\) is the orthocenter, the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); in particular  \n\n\\[\n\\angle HPN = 90^{\\circ}-\\angle MPN = \\angle MNP .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\angle MNP = \\angle CDE\\) because \\(M,N,C,D\\) are collinear and \\(CD\\) is a straight line.  \nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\angle HPN = \\angle CDE .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\) and \\(AP\\perp CD\\), the angle that \\(\\ell\\) makes with \\(PN\\) equals the angle that a line perpendicular to \\(CD\\) makes with \\(PN\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (\\ell , PN)=\\angle HPN .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nFrom (11) and (12) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (\\ell , PN)=\\angle CDE .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nNow using the similarity (6) we have \\(\\angle CDE = \\angle CEA\\) (corresponding angles in the similar triangles). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (\\ell , PN)=\\angle CEA .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(PN\\) is the same line as \\(BE\\) (both pass through the common point \\(B\\) and are chords of the two original circles). Therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (\\ell , BE)=\\angle CEA .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nFrom (7) we have \\(\\angle CEA = \\angle DFA\\). Since \\(F,E,A\\) are points on \\(\\Gamma\\), the angle \\(\\angle DFA\\) is the angle subtended by chord \\(DF\\) at the point \\(E\\) on the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (\\ell , BE)=\\angle BFE .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Tangency\n\nEquation (16) states that the line \\(\\ell\\) makes with the chord \\(BE\\) the same angle as the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(E\\) makes with the same chord. By the tangent\u2013chord theorem, this is precisely the condition for \\(\\ell\\) to be tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(E\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\u220e"
