 \\(H'\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and \\(PH'=PH\\). Because \\(\\ell\\parallel PH\\) and \\(\\ell\\parallel PH'\\), the quadrilateral \\(PHH'\\) is an isosceles trapezoid with bases \\(\\ell\\) and \\(PH\\). Consequently  \n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell , MN)=\\angle PH'M . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   But \\(\\angle PH'M\\) equals the angle subtended by chord \\(PM\\) at point \\(H'\\) on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); that angle is exactly \\(\\angle PNM\\). Hence  \n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell , MN)=\\angle PNM . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(MN\\) is the same line as \\(CD\\), (4) can be rewritten as  \n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell , CD)=\\angle PNM . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), the quadrilateral \\(APCD\\) is an isosceles trapezoid with bases \\(AP\\) and \\(CD\\); therefore \\(\\angle PNM = \\angle CDE\\). Substituting into (5) yields  \n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell , CD)=\\angle CDE . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Using the similarity (2) we have \\(\\angle CDE = \\angle CEA\\). Hence  \n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell , CD)=\\angle CEA . \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\perp CD\\), the angle that \\(\\ell\\) makes with the chord \\(BE\\) of \\(\\omega\\) equals the angle that a line perpendicular to \\(CD\\) makes with \\(BE\\). Because \\(BE\\) is a chord of \\(\\omega\\) passing through the common point \\(B\\) of the two original circles, the angle between a line perpendicular to \\(CD\\) and \\(BE\\) is precisely \\(\\angle CEA\\). Thus  \n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell , BE)=\\angle CEA . \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Tangency criterion.**  \n   From (2) we have \\(\\angle CEA = \\angle DFA\\). In the cyclic quadrilateral \\(\\omega\\) the angle \\(\\angle DFA\\) is the angle subtended by chord \\(DF\\) at the point \\(E\\); equivalently \\(\\angle DFA = \\angle BFE\\) (tangent\u2011chord theorem applied to \\(\\omega\\)). Therefore (8) becomes  \n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell ,