y  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CEA = \\angle DFA . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. The quadrilateral \\(B,E,F\\) is cyclic\n\nFrom (1) we also have  \n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AF = BE\\cdot BF . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus the power of point \\(B\\) with respect to the circle through \\(A,E,F\\) equals its power with respect to the circle through \\(B,E,F\\); therefore the three points \\(E,F\\) together with \\(B\\) lie on a common circle. Denote this circle by \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Geometry of the orthocentre \\(H\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nAP\\perp CD . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear, so \\(CD\\) coincides with the line \\(MN\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); by (6) this altitude is parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\); then \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Angle relations involving \\(\\ell\\)\n\nReflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\); call the image \\(H'\\). In any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circumcircle of the triangle, therefore \\(H'\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nBecause the reflection is across \\(MN\\), the segment \\(HH'\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) and \\(PH=PH'\\). Consequently the quadrilateral \\(PHH'\\) is an isosceles trapezoid whose bases are the lines \\(PH\\) and \\(PH'\\); the line \\(\\ell\\) (parallel to \\(PH\\) and \\(PH'\\)) is therefore symmetric with respect to \\(MN\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell , MN)=\\angle PH'M . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\angle PH'M\\) is the angle subtended by chord \\(PM\\) at the point \\(H'\\) on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); that angle equals the inscribed angle \\(\\angle PNM\\). Thus  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell , MN)=\\angle PNM . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(MN\\) is the same line as \\(CD\\), (8) can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell , CD)=\\angle PNM . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), quadrilateral \\(APCD\\) is an isosceles trapezoid; the base angles satisfy \\(\\angle PNM = \\angle CDE\\). Substituting into (9) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell , CD)=\\angle CDE . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nFrom the similarity (2) we have \\(\\angle CDE = \\angle CEA\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell , CD)=\\angle CEA . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nNow \\(CD\\) is perpendicular to \\(\\ell\\) (by (5) and the definition of \\(\\ell\\)), so the angle that \\(\\ell\\) makes with any line is equal to the complement of the angle that \\(CD\\) makes with that line. In particular, for the chord \\(BE\\) of \\(\\omega\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell , BE)=\\angle CEA . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Tangency to \\(\\omega\\)\n\nEquation (3) gives \\(\\angle CEA = \\angle DFA\\). In the cyclic quadrilateral \\(\\omega\\) the angle \\(\\angle DFA\\) is the angle subtended by chord \\(DF\\) at the point \\(E\\); by the tangent\u2011chord theorem this angle equals \\(\\angle BFE\\). Hence (12) becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell , BE)=\\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nThe equality of the angle formed by \\(\\ell\\) with chord \\(BE\\) and the angle subtended by the same chord at the opposite point of the circle is precisely the condition for \\(\\ell\\) to be tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(E\\).\n\nTherefore the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e"
