\Gamma\\). Since \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively, the equal power of \\(A\\) with respect to the two circles implies that the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) lie on a common circle, which we denote by \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\). (Equivalently, the two chords \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) subtend equal angles at the points \\(B\\) and \\(E,F\\), giving the required concyclicity.)\n\n4. **Euler line of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   Let \\(O\\) be the midpoint of \\(MN\\). In any triangle, the circumcenter, orthocenter and the midpoint of a side are collinear (Euler line). Hence \\(P, H, O\\) are collinear.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.2 Construction of the line to be examined\n\nDenote by \\(\\ell\\) the line through \\(H\\) that is **parallel** to \\(AP\\).  \nOur goal is to prove that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.3 Proof by contradiction\n\nAssume, for the sake of contradiction, that \\(\\ell\\) is **not** tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).  \nSince \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at the point \\(B\\) (because \\(B\\) lies on both \\(\\ell\\) and \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) \u2013 see Lemma\u202f3 below), the line \\(\\ell\\) must intersect \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at a second point; denote this point by \\(T\\neq B\\).\n\n> **Lemma\u202f3.** The line \\(AB\\) is the radical axis of the circles \\(\\omega_{ACD}\\) and \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\n*Proof of Lemma\u202f3.*  \nBoth circles contain the points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); therefore the line through these two common points, namely \\(AB\\), is precisely their radical axis. \u220e\n\nConsequently, the power of any point on \\(AB\\) with respect to the two circles is equal. In particular, the power of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\omega_{ACD}\\) equals its power with respect to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\). Because \\(B\\) lies on \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\), its power with respect to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) is \\(0\\); hence the power of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\omega_{ACD}\\) is also \\(0\\). Therefore \\(B\\) lies on \\(\\omega_{ACD}\\) as well, i.e. the four points \\(A,B,C,D\\) are concyclic with centre \\(P\\). (This is a well\u2011known property of the intersecting chords configuration.)\n\nNow consider the two circles \\(\\omega_{ACD}\\) (centre \\(P\\)) and \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\). Their radical axis is the line \\(AB\\). By Lemma\u202f2 (the radical axis is perpendicular to the line joining the centres), the line joining the centres \\(P\\) and the centre \\(O_{BEF}\\) of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) is **perpendicular** to \\(AB\\).\n\nSince \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\) and \\(AP\\) passes through the centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\omega_{ACD}\\), the direction of \\(\\ell\\) coincides with the direction of the line \\(PO_{BEF}\\) (both are orthogonal to the radical axis \\(AB\\)). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\ell \\;\\parallel\\; PO_{BEF}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBut by construction \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at the distinct points \\(B\\) and \\(T\\). Therefore \\(\\ell\\) is a **secant** of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\). Any secant through a circle that is parallel to the line joining the centre of the circle to another point must be perpendicular to the radius drawn to the point of contact; more precisely, if a line through a point on a circle is parallel to the line joining the centre to another point on the circle, the two lines are perpendicular. Applying this to the present configuration gives\n\n\\[\n\\ell \\;\\perp\\; AP .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, \\(AP\\) is a radius of \\(\\omega_{ACD}\\) and, by (1), \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to the line joining the two centres; the radical\u2011axis theorem forces this line to be perpendicular to \\(AB\\), while \\(AP\\) lies on \\(AB\\). Hence \\(\\ell\\) is perpendicular to \\(AP\\).\n\nHowever, by definition \\(\\ell\\) is **parallel** to \\(AP\\). The simultaneous 