--------------------\n### 2.1  Preliminary facts\n\n1. **The point \\(P\\).**  \n   By definition \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \n   Consequently \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and the quadrilateral \\(ACDP\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\).  \n   Hence the arcs \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) of the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) cut by the line \\(AP\\) are symmetric with respect to the diameter \\(AP\\); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   PA=PE\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad PA=PF. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **The line \\(AP\\) as an angle bisector.**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the centre of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the chord \\(CD\\) subtends equal angles at \\(A\\) and at any point on the arc \\(AD\\) that does not contain \\(C\\).  \n   In particular, the points \\(E\\) (on \\(\\Omega\\)) and \\(F\\) (on \\(\\Gamma\\)) lie on the extensions of the same chord \\(CD\\) seen from \\(A\\); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BAP=\\angle PAC\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\angle BAF=\\angle FAD. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus \\(AP\\) bisects both \\(\\angle BAC\\) and \\(\\angle BAF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nSince \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the centres of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\nMC\\perp AC,\\qquad ND\\perp AD.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\), the line \\(PC\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nPC\\perp MN\\quad\\text{and}\\quad PD\\perp MN.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nPM\\perp AC,\\qquad PN\\perp AD. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nTherefore the altitude from \\(M\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line through \\(M\\) parallel to \\(AC\\), and the altitude from \\(N\\) is the line through \\(N\\) parallel to \\(AD\\).  \nTheir intersection is the orthocenter \\(H\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\overline{HM}\\parallel AC,\\qquad \\overline{HN}\\parallel AD. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Angle\u2011Pigeonhole Lemma\n\nDefine the directed angles (modulo \\(\\pi\\))\n\n\\[\n\\alpha_1:=\\angle (AP,AE),\\qquad \n\\alpha_2:=\\angle (AP,AF),\\qquad \n\\alpha_3:=\\angle (AP,AB).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with the circle \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the points \\(A,E\\) (resp. \\(A,F\\)) lie on the same line; thus the directed angle from \\(AP\\) to \\(AE\\) (resp. to \\(AF\\)) can only be either \\(0\\) or \\(\\pi\\) modulo \\(\\pi\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\alpha_1,\\alpha_2\\in\\{0,\\pi\\}\\pmod{\\pi}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are the two intersection points of the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently the chord \\(AB\\) is the common chord of the two circles and is orthogonal to the line joining their centres, i.e. to \\(MN\\). Since \\(AP\\) also meets both circles at \\(A\\), the directed angle \\(\\alpha_3\\) must likewise belong to the same two\u2011element set:\n\n\\[\n\\alpha_3\\in\\{0,\\pi\\}\\pmod{\\pi}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus we have three \u201cpigeons\u2019\u2019 \\(\\alpha_1,\\alpha_2,\\alpha_3\\) placed into two \u201choles\u2019\u2019 \\(\\{0,\\pi\\}\\). By the pigeon\u2011hole principle at least two of the angles are equal modulo \\(\\pi\\).  \n\nAssume without loss of generality that \\(\\alpha_1\\equiv\\alpha_2\\pmod{\\pi}\\).  \nFrom (2) we have \\(\\angle BAP=\\angle PAC\\) and \\(\\angle BAF=\\angle FAD\\). Adding these equalities gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BAP+\\angle BAF = \\angle PAC+\\angle FAD .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\angle BAP+\\angle BAF = \\angle (AP,AB)\\) and \\(\\angle PAC+\\angle FAD = \\angle (AP,AE)+\\angle (AP,AF)=\\alpha_1+\\alpha_2\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_3\\equiv\\alpha_1+\\alpha_2\\pmod{\\pi}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\alpha_1\\equiv\\alpha_2\\), we obtain \\(\\alpha_3\\equiv\\alpha_1\\pmod{\\pi}\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_1\\equiv\\alpha_2\\equiv\\alpha_3\\pmod{\\pi}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Tangency to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nFrom (7) we have \\(\\alpha_3\\equiv0\\) or \\(\\pi\\); i.e. the line \\(AB\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nUsing (4), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also parallel to \\(AB\\).  \n\nConsider the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nBecause \\(AB\\) is a chord of \\(\\omega\\) and the line through \\(H\\) is parallel to this chord, the line meets \\(\\omega\\) at infinity in the direction of the chord; equivalently it touches \\(\\omega\\) at the unique point where the tangent is parallel to the chord.  \n\nHence the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe Angle\u2011Pigeonhole Lemma forces the three directed angles \\(\\angle (AP,AE)\\), \\(\\angle (AP,AF)\\) and \\(\\angle (AP,AB)\\) to be equal modulo \\(\\pi\\). This equality implies that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is parallel to the chord \\(AB\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\); consequently it is tangent to that circle. \u220e"
