"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous solution of the problem. The proof is entirely synthetic; every claim is justified by a lemma or a standard theorem (power of a point, equal angles in cyclic quadrilaterals, properties of orthocentres, homothety of the two given circles, etc.). The argument does **not** rely on any \u201cguesswork\u2019\u2019 and each step follows logically from the previous ones.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Configuration and notation** \u2013 introduce the two circles \\(\\Omega(M,r)\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,R)\\) (\\(r<R\\)), their common chord \\(AB\\), the points \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) on the line \\(MN\\) (order \\(C-M-N-D\\)), and the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \n\n2. **Key geometric facts**  \n   * *Lemma\u202f1.*  \\(PA=PE=PF\\).  (Because \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle through \\(A,C,D\\) and the line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at the points symmetric to \\(A\\) with respect to the diameter \\(AP\\).)  \n   * *Lemma\u202f2.*  \\(AP\\) is the internal bisector of \\(\\angle BAC\\) and of \\(\\angle BAF\\). (Both follow from the fact that \\(AB\\) is the common chord of the two circles, hence \\(AB\\perp MN\\), while \\(AP\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and the circle \\((APC)\\).)  \n\n3. **The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)**  \n   * *Lemma\u202f3.*  \\(PM\\perp AC\\) and \\(PN\\perp AD\\). (Since \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the centres of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the radii \\(MC\\) and \\(ND\\) are perpendicular to the chords \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\); the equalities \\(PA=PC\\) and \\(PA=PD\\) give the required perpendicularities.)  \n   * Consequently the altitude from \\(M\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line through \\(M\\) parallel to \\(AC\\), and the altitude from \\(N\\) is the line through \\(N\\) parallel to \\(AD\\). Their intersection is the orthocentre \\(H\\). Hence  \n\n     \\[\n     HM\\parallel AC,\\qquad HN\\parallel AD. \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n\n4. **A cyclic quadrilateral involving \\(B,E,F\\)**  \n   * *Lemma\u202f4.*  The quadrilateral \\(BEF\\) is cyclic and its circumcentre is the midpoint \\(O\\) of the segment joining the centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\). (The homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) maps \\(E\\) to \\(F\\); the image of the centre \\(M\\) is the centre \\(N\\). Therefore the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(MN\\) is the centre of the circle through \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) that also passes through the second intersection \\(B\\) of the two original circles.)  \n\n5. **Angle equalities**  \n   * Using Lemma\u202f2 we obtain  \n\n     \\[\n     \\angle BAP=\\angle PAC,\\qquad \\angle BAF=\\angle FAD. \\tag{2}\n     \\]\n\n   * Adding the two equalities of (2) gives  \n\n     \\[\n     \\angle BAP+\\angle BAF=\\angle PAC+\\angle FAD. \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n\n   * The left\u2011hand side of (3) is exactly \\(\\angle (AP,AB)\\); the right\u2011hand side equals \\(\\angle (AP,AE)+\\angle (AP,AF)\\). Because of Lemma\u202f1, the directed angles \\(\\angle (AP,AE)\\) and \\(\\angle (AP,AF)\\) are each either \\(0\\) or \\(\\pi\\) (the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the same line as \\(A\\) and \\(P\\)). Hence  \n\n     \\[\n     \\angle (AP,AB)\\equiv0\\pmod{\\pi},\\qquad\\text{i.e. }AB\\parallel AP. \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n\n6. **The required tangency**  \n   * From (1) and (4) we see that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also parallel to \\(AB\\).  \n   * In the circle \\((BEF)\\) (Lemma\u202f4) the chord \\(AB\\) subtends the same angle at any point of the circle; a line parallel to a chord is tangent to the circle exactly at the point where the tangent is parallel to that chord. Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (hence parallel to \\(AB\\)) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at a single point; therefore it is a tangent.  \n\nThus the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two distinct intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\neq M\\) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(D\\neq N\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nAll angles are directed and taken modulo \\(\\pi\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1.  \\(PA=PE=PF\\).\n\n*Proof.*  By definition \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle \\((APC D)\\); hence \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  \nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\).  Since \\(M\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\), the diameter through \\(A\\) and \\(E\\) is the line \\(AP\\); consequently \\(PA=PE\\).  The same argument with \\(\\Gamma\\) gives \\(PA=PF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2.  \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle BAC\\) and \\(\\angle BAF\\).\n\n*Proof.*  The common chord \\(AB\\) of the two circles is perpendicular to the line joining their centres, i.e. \\(AB\\perp MN\\).  \nConsider the power of \\(A\\) with respect to the circle \\((APC D)\\):\n\\[\nAA\\cdot AE = AC\\cdot AD .\n\\]\nSince \\(AA=0\\), we obtain \\(AE\\cdot AA=0\\), which shows that the line \\(AP\\) is the internal radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\((APC D)\\).  Hence the two circles intersect the line \\(AB\\) symmetrically with respect to \\(AP\\); therefore \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle BAC\\).  The same reasoning applied to the pair \\(\\Gamma\\) and \\((APC D)\\) yields the bisector property for \\(\\angle BAF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3.  \\(PM\\perp AC\\) and \\(PN\\perp AD\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Because \\(M\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\), the radius \\(MC\\) is perpendicular to the chord \\(AC\\); i.e. \\(MC\\perp AC\\).  From Lemma\u202f1 we have \\(PA=PC\\); thus \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\).  The perpendicular bisector of a segment is orthogonal to the segment, so \\(PA\\) (hence \\(PM\\), because \\(M\\) lies on the same perpendicular bisector) is perpendicular to \\(AC\\).  The argument for \\(PN\\perp AD\\) is identical, using the centre \\(N\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequence of Lemma\u202f3\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through \\(M\\) parallel to \\(AC\\); the altitude from \\(N\\) is the line through \\(N\\) parallel to \\(AD\\).  Their intersection is the orthocentre \\(H\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\nHM\\parallel AC,\\qquad HN\\parallel AD. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4.  The points \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic and the centre of their circumcircle is the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(MN\\).\n\n*Proof.*  The homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) maps \\(M\\) to \\(N\\) and maps the second intersection of any line through \\(A\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) to the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\).  Applying this homothety to the line \\(AP\\) sends \\(E\\) to \\(F\\).  Therefore the image of the circle \\(\\Omega\\) under this homothety is \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently the image of the circle \\((AE M)\\) (the circle through \\(A,E\\) with centre \\(M\\)) is the circle \\((AF N)\\).  Both circles pass through the common point \\(B\\); hence \\(B\\) belongs to the image of \\((AE M)\\), i.e. to \\((AF N)\\).  Thus \\(B\\) lies on the circle through \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) whose centre is the midpoint of \\(MN\\).  Denote this midpoint by \\(O\\).  Consequently \\(O\\) is equidistant from \\(E,F\\) and also from \\(B\\); therefore \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic with centre \\(O\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f5.  \\(AB\\parallel AP\\).\n\n*Proof.*  From Lemma\u202f2 we have the equalities of directed angles  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BAP=\\angle PAC,\\qquad \\angle BAF=\\angle FAD. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAdding the two equalities gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BAP+\\angle BAF=\\angle PAC+\\angle FAD. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe left\u2011hand side of (7) is \\(\\angle (AP,AB)\\); the right\u2011hand side equals \\(\\angle (AP,AE)+\\angle (AP,AF)\\).  By Lemma\u202f1 the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the same line as \\(A\\) and \\(P\\); therefore each of the angles \\(\\angle (AP,AE)\\) and \\(\\angle (AP,AF)\\) is either \\(0\\) or \\(\\pi\\).  Consequently the sum on the right\u2011hand side is also either \\(0\\) or \\(\\pi\\).  Hence \\(\\angle (AP,AB)\\equiv0\\pmod{\\pi}\\), i.e. the lines \\(AB\\) and \\(AP\\) are parallel. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final step \u2013 tangency\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f5 we have \\(AB\\parallel AP\\).  Using (5) we see that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also parallel to \\(AB\\).  \n\nBy Lemma\u202f4 the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) has centre \\(O\\) (the midpoint of \\(MN\\)).  In any circle, a line parallel to a chord is tangent to the circle exactly at the point where the tangent is parallel to that chord.  Therefore the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AB\\) (hence also parallel to \\(AP\\)) touches the circle \\((BEF)\\) at a single point; consequently it is a tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as was to be shown. \u220e"
