lain why the reasoning fails and note that any subsequent steps that depend on it are consequently invalid.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f1  \n> \u201cBy definition \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle \\((APC D)\\); hence \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  \n> The line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\).  Since \\(M\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\), the diameter through \\(A\\) and \\(E\\) is the line \\(AP\\); consequently \\(PA=PE\\).  The same argument with \\(\\Gamma\\) gives \\(PA=PF\\).\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \n- The first sentence is correct: the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is equidistant from \\(A,C,D\\).  \n- The second claim is false: the line through the centre \\(M\\) and a point \\(A\\) on the circle is a radius, not a diameter. The diameter through \\(A\\) is the line through \\(M\\) and the antipodal point of \\(A\\), which is not generally the line \\(AP\\). Hence we cannot conclude \\(PA=PE\\) from this argument.  \n**Result:** **Critical Error** (the equality \\(PA=PE\\) is not justified; the same applies to \\(PA=PF\\)).  \n\nBecause Lemma\u202f1 is used repeatedly, the whole proof built on these equalities is compromised.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f2  \n> \u201cThe common chord \\(AB\\) of the two circles is perpendicular to the line joining their centres, i.e. \\(AB\\perp MN\\).  \n> Consider the power of \\(A\\) with respect to the circle \\((APC D)\\):\n> \\[\n> AA\\cdot AE = AC\\cdot AD .\n> \\]\n> Since \\(AA=0\\), we obtain \\(AE\\cdot AA=0\\), which shows that the line \\(AP\\) is the internal radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\((APC D)\\).  \n> Hence the two circles intersect the line \\(AB\\) symmetrically with respect to \\(AP\\); therefore \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle BAC\\).\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \n- The statement \\(AB\\perp MN\\) is correct for intersecting circles.  \n- The power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point formula is misapplied: for a point \\(A\\) on a circle, the power is zero, but the product \\(AA\\cdot AE\\) is not defined (the segment \\(AA\\) has length zero, not a directed segment). The correct relation would be \\(0 = AC\\cdot AD\\) only if \\(A\\) lay on the circle \\((APCD)\\), which it does, but this yields no information about \\(AE\\).  \n- Consequently the deduction that \\(AP\\) is a radical axis is unfounded.  \n- Even assuming \\(AP\\) were a radical axis, symmetry of the two circles with respect to a line does **not** imply that the line bisects the angles formed by their common chord.  \n\n**Result:** **Critical Error** (the whole argument collapses).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f3  \n> \u201cBecause \\(M\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\), the radius \\(MC\\) is perpendicular to the chord \\(AC\\); i.e. \\(MC\\perp AC\\).  \n> From Lemma\u202f1 we have \\(PA=PC\\); thus \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\).  \n> The perpendicular bisector of a segment is orthogonal to the segment, so \\(PA\\) (hence \\(PM\\), because \\(M\\) lies on the same perpendicular bisector) is perpendicular to \\(AC\\).\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \n- The first sentence is correct: a radius to the midpoint of a chord is perpendicular to the chord.  \n- The second sentence uses Lemma\u202f1, which we have already shown to be invalid; therefore the premise \\(PA=PC\\) is not established.  \n- Even if \\(PA=PC\\) were true, it only tells us that \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\); it does **not** imply that \\(M\\) also lies on that bisector. The claim \u201c\\(PM\\) is the same line as the perpendicular bisector\u201d is unjustified.  \n\n**Result:** **Critical Error** (the conclusion \\(PM\\perp AC\\) is not proved).\n\n---\n\n### Consequence of Lemma\u202f3  \n> \u201cIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through \\(M\\) parallel to \\(AC\\); the altitude from \\(N\\) is the line through \\(N\\) parallel to \\(AD\\). Their intersection is the orthocentre \\(H\\). Hence\n> \\[\n> HM\\parallel AC,\\qquad HN\\parallel AD. \\tag{5}\n> \\]\