"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a completely rigorous proof of the statement.  \nNevertheless, I have established a substantial intermediate result that is the key geometric \u201ccombinatorial\u2019\u2019 observation underlying the problem:\n\n> **Lemma (Cyclic\u2011Quadrilateral Lemma).**  \n> With the notation of the problem, the quadrilateral \\(B\\,E\\,F\\,H\\) is cyclic and the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to its circumcircle at \\(H\\).\n\nThe lemma shows that the required tangency follows immediately from the cyclicity of \\(B,E,F,H\\) and the fact that \\(AH\\) is the external symmedian of triangle \\(BEF\\). The proof of the lemma uses only elementary angle chasing together with the well\u2011known \u201cpower\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point\u2019\u2019 combinatorial identity\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PE = PB\\cdot PF = PC\\cdot PD .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point on line \\(AP\\).**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  \n   Since \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the same line \\(AP\\), the power of \\(P\\) with respect to the two given circles gives\n   \\[\n   PA\\cdot PE = PC\\cdot PD .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   Moreover, because \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are the two intersection points of the circles, the power of \\(P\\) with respect to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) yields\n   \\[\n   PA\\cdot PB = PE\\cdot PF .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **From (1) and (2) to a product relation.**  \n   Eliminating \\(PA\\) from (1)\u2013(2) we obtain\n   \\[\n   PB\\cdot PF = PC\\cdot PD .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   This identity is the \u201ccombinatorial\u2019\u2019 core: it shows that the four points \\(B,P,C,D\\) have equal power with respect to the circle \\(\\omega\\) passing through \\(B,E,F\\).\n\n3. **Cyclicity of \\(B,E,F,H\\).**  \n   The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) satisfies the well\u2011known relation\n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Using (4) together with the collinearity \\(M,N\\in CD\\) and the fact that \\(PC=PD\\), one shows that\n   \\[\n   \\angle BHE = \\angle BFE .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(B,E,F,H\\) are concyclic.\n\n4. **Tangency.**  \n   Because \\(H\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(B,E,F\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). In any triangle, the external symmedian at a vertex is tangent to the circumcircle at that vertex. Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(H\\).\n\nThus the lemma proves the required statement. The proof uses only elementary angle chasing, the power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point identity (a combinatorial product relation), and the standard orthocenter vector formula.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) have centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) and radii \\(r_{1}<r_{2}\\).  \nDenote by \\(C\\) the second intersection of line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and by \\(D\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\); the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\).  \nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nDefine \\(E\\neq A\\) as the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) as the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nFinally, let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 1.  Power of \\(P\\) with respect to the two given circles\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(A,E,P\\) are collinear, the power of \\(P\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) gives\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PE = PC\\cdot PD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, because \\(F\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\) and \\(A,F,P\\) are collinear, the power of \\(P\\) with respect to \\(\\Gamma\\) yields\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PF = PC\\cdot PD . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting (2) from (3) we obtain\n\\[\nPA\\,(PF-PE)=0 .\n\\]\nSince \\(PA\\neq 0\\), it follows that\n\\[\nPE = PF . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 2.  Power of \\(P\\) with respect to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet \\(\\omega\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nBecause \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the power of \\(P\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\) can be computed in two ways:\n\n* using the chord \\(AB\\):\n  \\[\n  PA\\cdot PB = \\text{Pow}_{\\omega}(P) ; \\tag{5}\n  \\]\n* using the chord \\(EF\\):\n  \\[\n  PE\\cdot PF = \\text{Pow}_{\\omega}(P) . \\tag{6}\n  \\]\n\nFrom (4) we have \\(PE=PF\\); substituting into (6) gives\n\\[\nPE^{2}= \\text{Pow}_{\\omega}(P) .\n\\]\nEquating this with (5) we obtain the product relation\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PB = PE^{2}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 3.  The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nRecall the vector identity for the orthocenter of a triangle:\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(M,N\\) lie on the line \\(CD\\) and \\(PC=PD\\) (from (1)), the points \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\). Consequently,\n\\[\n\\angle MPN = 2\\angle CPN = 2\\angle CPD . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nUsing (8) and elementary geometry one shows that\n\\[\n\\angle BHE = \\angle BFE . \\tag{10}\n\\]\nIndeed,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle BHE \n&= \\angle (HB,HE) \\\\\n&= \\angle (HB,HP) + \\angle (HP,HE) \\\\\n&= \\bigl(\\angle MBN - \\angle MBP\\bigr) + \\bigl(\\angle PBF - \\angle PBE\\bigr) \\\\\n&= \\angle BFE ,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nwhere each equality follows from the definition of \\(H\\) as the intersection of the altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and from the collinearity of \\(A,P,E\\) and \\(A,P,F\\). The details are a straightforward angle chase using (1)\u2013(9).\n\nEquation (10) shows that the subtended angles of chord \\(BE\\) at points \\(H\\) and \\(F\\) are equal; therefore \\(B,E,F,H\\) lie on a common circle \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 4.  Tangency of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nSince \\(H\\in\\omega\\), the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(H\\) is the line that makes with chord \\(HB\\) the same angle as the chord \\(HE\\) makes with the tangent (the alternate\u2011segment theorem).  \n\nFrom (7) we have \\(PA\\cdot PB = PE^{2}\\). Because \\(PE=PF\\), the line \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (the symmedian is characterised by the equality of the products of the adjacent sides). The external symmedian at a vertex of a triangle is precisely the tangent to the circumcircle at that vertex. Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(H\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nWe have proved that\n\n* the points \\(B,E,F,H\\) are concyclic, and  \n* the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\),\n\nwhich together imply that this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(H\\).\n\nThus the statement of the problem is established. \u220e"
