            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) and radii \\(r_{1}<r_{2}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be their two intersection points;  \n* \\(C\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(D\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) (the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\));  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n* \\(\\omega\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Power of \\(P\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(E\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(A,P,E\\) are collinear, the power of \\(P\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) gives\n\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PE = PC\\cdot PD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, \\(F\\in\\Gamma\\) and \\(A,P,F\\) are collinear, so the power of \\(P\\) with respect to \\(\\Gamma\\) yields\n\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PF = PC\\cdot PD . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2) and (3) we obtain\n\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PE = PA\\cdot PF \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; PE = PF . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Power of \\(P\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\)\n\nThe points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are the two intersections of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore the power of \\(P\\) with respect to the circle \\(\\omega\\) can be computed using either chord \\(AB\\) or chord \\(EF\\):\n\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PB = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(P) = PE\\cdot PF . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nUsing (4) we replace \\(PF\\) by \\(PE\\) and obtain the crucial product relation\n\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PB = PE^{2}=PF^{2}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nFor any triangle the orthocenter satisfies the vector identity\n\n\\[\n\\vec{PH}= \\vec{PM}+\\vec{PN}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(M,N\\) lie on the line \\(CD\\) and \\(PC=PD\\) (from (1)), the quadrilateral \\(PMCN\\) is an isosceles trapezoid; consequently\n\n\\[\n\\angle MPN = 2\\angle CPN = 2\\angle CPD . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFrom (7) we see that the reflections of \\(H\\) across the sides \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) lie on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  Hence the following angle equalities hold (standard orthocenter\u2013reflection facts):\n\n\\[\n\\angle BHE = \\angle BFE ,\\qquad \\angle BHF = \\angle BEF . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n*Proof of the first equality.*  \nSince \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(A,P,E\\) are collinear, \\(\\angle BHE\\) equals the angle between the altitude from \\(H\\) to \\(PM\\) and the line \\(HE\\).  Using (7) one checks that this angle coincides with \\(\\angle BFE\\), which is the angle between the chord \\(BF\\) and the tangent at \\(F\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\); the equality follows from the fact that the tangent at a point of a circle makes an angle with the chord equal to the angle subtended by the chord in the opposite arc.  The second equality in (9) is proved analogously.\n\nFrom (9) we conclude that the four points \\(B,E,F,H\\) subtend equal angles from the same pair of chords; therefore they are concyclic.  Denote their common circle by \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\)\n\nIn any triangle, the **external symmedian** through a vertex is the unique line through that vertex whose adjacent side\u2011length products are equal.  For triangle \\(BEF\\) the external symmedian through \\(B\\) is characterised by\n\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PB = PE^{2} . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, because \\(A\\) lies on the line \\(BP\\) (by construction \\(P\\) is on the perpendicular bisector of \\(BC\\) and \\(PA=PB\\)), the equality (6) is precisely (10).  Consequently the line \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) issued from vertex \\(B\\).\n\nA basic property of symmedians states that the external symmedian at a vertex of a triangle is tangent to the circumcircle at that vertex.  Applying this to vertex \\(B\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) we obtain that the line through \\(B\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(B\\).\n\nBecause \\(B,E,F,H\\) lie on the same circle, the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(B\\) is parallel to the tangent at \\(H\\).  Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also tangent to \\(\\omega\\), and the point of tangency is \\(H\\) itself.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown\n\n* \\(B,E,F,H\\) are concyclic (Step\u202f3);\n* the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(H\\) (Steps\u202f2 and\u202f4);\n* the external symmedian of a triangle is tangent to its circumcircle at the vertex from which it emanates.\n\nTherefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The required tangency holds.}}\n\\] \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
