ement:** \u201c\\(PA\\cdot PB = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(P) = PE\\cdot PF\\).\u201d\n\n  **Analysis:** For this to hold, points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) must lie on the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). The problem never establishes that \\(A\\) belongs to that circle; indeed, \\(\\omega\\) is defined as the circumcircle of \\(B,E,F\\) only. Thus the equality \\(PA\\cdot PB = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(P)\\) is not justified. Moreover, the right\u2011hand side \\(PE\\cdot PF\\) would be the power of \\(P\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\) **only if** the line through \\(P\\) intersected \\(\\omega\\) at \\(E\\) and \\(F\\), which is true, but the left\u2011hand side remains unjustified.\n\n  **Classification:** **Justification Gap** \u2013 the claim is made without proof; it also depends on the earlier false statement \\(PE = PF\\).\n\n#### Step\u202f5 \u2013 Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\n- **Statement:** \u201cFor any triangle the orthocenter satisfies the vector identity \\(\\vec{PH}= \\vec{PM}+\\vec{PN}\\).\u201d\n\n  **Analysis:** This vector identity holds when the origin is taken at \\(P\\) and the triangle is considered with vertices \\(P,M,N\\); indeed \\(\\vec{PH} = \\vec{PM} + \\vec{PN}\\) is a well\u2011known relation. This part is correct.\n\n- **Statement:** \u201cBecause \\(M,N\\) lie on the line \\(CD\\) and \\(PC=PD\\) \u2026 the quadrilateral \\(PMCN\\) is an isosceles trapezoid; consequently \\(\\angle MPN = 2\\angle CPN = 2\\angle CPD\\).\u201d\n\n  **Analysis:** The fact that \\(M,N\\) lie on line \\(CD\\) does not imply that \\(PMCN\\) is a trapezoid; a trapezoid requires one pair of opposite sides to be parallel. No argument is given that \\(PM\\) is parallel to \\(CN\\) or that \\(PN\\) is parallel to \\(CM\\). Hence the claim that the quadrilateral is an isosceles trapezoid is unfounded, and the derived angle relation is unsupported.\n\n  **Classification:** **Justification Gap**.\n\n- **Statement:** \u201c