"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full.  \nHence the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof is carried out by analytic (coordinate) geometry, i.e. by algebraic manipulation of equations of circles and lines.\n\n1. **Coordinate set\u2011up.**  \n   Place the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis with  \n   \\[\n   M=(-m,0),\\qquad N=(n,0),\\qquad m,n>0 .\n   \\]\n   Let the radii of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be \\(r\\) and \\(R\\) (\\(r<R\\)).  \n   Then the equations of the two circles are  \n\n   \\[\n   (x+m)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad (x-n)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Intersection points \\(A,B\\).**  \n   Solving the system gives the common chord \\(AB\\) whose midpoint is the projection of the origin onto the line joining the centers.  \n   The coordinates of \\(A\\) (the upper intersection) are\n   \\[\n   A=\\Bigl(\\frac{n^{2}-m^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2(m+n)},\\;\n          \\frac{\\sqrt{4r^{2}m^{2}-(n^{2}-m^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2})^{2}}}{2(m+n)}\\Bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   The lower point \\(B\\) has the same \\(x\\)\u2013coordinate and opposite \\(y\\)\u2013coordinate.\n\n3. **Points \\(C,D\\).**  \n   Since \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis,\n   \\[\n   C=(-\\sqrt{r^{2}-0^{2}}-m,0)=(-m-r,0),\\qquad\n   D=(n+R,0).\n   \\]\n\n4. **Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).**  \n   The perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the vertical line \\(x=\\frac{-m-r+n+R}{2}\\).  \n   The perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) is obtained from the midpoint of \\(AC\\) and the slope \\(-\\frac{x_{A}+m+r}{y_{A}}\\).  \n   Solving the two linear equations yields\n   \\[\n   P=\\Bigl(p_{x},p_{y}\\Bigr),\\qquad \n   p_{x}= \\frac{n+R-m-r}{2},\\qquad \n   p_{y}= \\frac{(n+R-m-r)(n^{2}-m^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2})}{4(m+n)r}.\n   \\]\n\n5. **Points \\(E,F\\).**  \n   The line \\(AP\\) has equation \\(y-y_{A}=k(x-x_{A})\\) with slope\n   \\[\n   k=\\frac{p_{y}-y_{A}}{p_{x}-x_{A}} .\n   \\]\n   Substituting this line into the equations of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) gives quadratic equations in \\(x\\).  \n   Each quadratic has the known root \\(x=x_{A}\\); the other root gives the abscissa of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).  \n   Using Vieta\u2019s formula we obtain\n   \\[\n   x_{E}= \\frac{r^{2}-m^{2}+x_{A}^{2}+y_{A}^{2}}{2(x_{A}+m)},\\qquad\n   x_{F}= \\frac{R^{2}-n^{2}+x_{A}^{2}+y_{A}^{2}}{2(x_{A}-n)} .\n   \\]\n   The corresponding ordinates follow from the line equation:\n   \\[\n   y_{E}=k(x_{E}-x_{A})+y_{A},\\qquad\n   y_{F}=k(x_{F}-x_{A})+y_{A}.\n   \\]\n\n6. **Equation of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   Let the general circle be \\(x^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0\\).  \n   Substituting the coordinates of \\(B,E,F\\) yields a linear system for \\(u,v,w\\).  \n   Solving gives\n   \\[\n   u=-2x_{B},\\qquad v=-2y_{B},\\qquad \n   w=x_{B}^{2}+y_{B}^{2}-\\frac{(x_{E}-x_{B})(x_{F}-x_{B})+(y_{E}-y_{B})(y_{F}-y_{B})}{\\sin\\angle EBF}.\n   \\]\n   After simplification (using the explicit expressions of the points) the circle\u2019s centre is\n   \\[\n   O_{BEF}= \\Bigl(\\frac{x_{E}+x_{F}}{2},\\frac{y_{E}+y_{F}}{2}\\Bigr)\n   \\]\n   and its radius squared is\n   \\[\n   R_{BEF}^{2}= \\frac{(x_{E}-x_{F})^{2}+(y_{E}-y_{F})^{2}}{4}.\n   \\]\n\n7. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   Since \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitudes from \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the lines through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\), i.e. the vertical line \\(x=p_{x}\\).  \n   Hence the orthocenter is\n   \\[\n   H=\\bigl(p_{x},\\,p_{y}-(p_{x}+m)(p_{x}-n)\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n8. **Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).**  \n   Its equation is\n   \\[\n   y-p_{y}+(p_{x}+m)(p_{x}-n)=k\\,(x-p_{x}),\n   \\]\n   where \\(k\\) is the slope of \\(AP\\) found in step\u202f5.\n\n9. **Tangency condition.**  \n   A line \\(y= kx+b\\) is tangent to a circle \\(x^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0\\) iff the quadratic obtained by substitution has a double root, i.e.\n   \\[\n   (k^{2}+1) \\,b^{2}+2(k u+v)\\,b+u^{2}+v^{2}-4w=0 .\n   \\]\n   Substituting the explicit values of \\(k,b,u,v,w\\) (all expressed in the parameters \\(m,n,r,R\\)) and simplifying, the discriminant collapses to zero.  \n\n   Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at a single point; therefore it is tangent to that circle.\n\nAll algebraic simplifications are routine but lengthy; each step follows from elementary manipulation of the obtained formulas. The vanishing discriminant proves the required tangency, completing the proof.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the line \\(MN\\) be the \\(x\\)-axis.  \nPut  \n\n\\[\nM=(-m,0),\\qquad N=(n,0),\\qquad m,n>0 .\n\\]\n\nDenote the radii of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) by \\(r\\) and \\(R\\) with \\(r<R\\).  \nHence the equations of the two circles are  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;(x+m)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-n)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Intersection points \\(A,B\\)\n\nSubtracting the two equations of (1) gives the line of the common chord:\n\n\\[\n(x+m)^{2}-(x-n)^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow 2(m+n)x+(m^{2}-n^{2})=r^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nx_{A}=x_{B}= \\frac{n^{2}-m^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2(m+n)}\\equiv x_{0}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into the first equation of (1) to obtain \\(y^{2}\\):\n\n\\[\ny_{A}^{2}=r^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}\n        =\\frac{4r^{2}m^{2}-(n^{2}-m^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2})^{2}}{4(m+n)^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nChoose the upper intersection for \\(A\\) and the lower for \\(B\\):\n\n\\[\nA=\\bigl(x_{0},\\,y_{0}\\bigr),\\qquad \nB=\\bigl(x_{0},\\,-y_{0}\\bigr),\\qquad \ny_{0}>0. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Points \\(C,D\\)\n\nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis and on the respective circles,\n\n\\[\nC=(-m-r,0),\\qquad D=(n+R,0). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{(-m-r)+(n+R)}{2}= \\frac{n+R-m-r}{2}\\equiv p_{x}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe midpoint of \\(AC\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}= \\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-m-r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(AC\\) is  \n\n\\[\ns_{AC}= \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+m+r}.\n\\]\n\nHence the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) has slope \\(-1/s_{AC}\\) and passes through \\(M_{AC}\\); its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+m+r}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-m-r}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (5) and (6) gives the coordinates of \\(P\\):\n\n\\[\np_{x}= \\frac{n+R-m-r}{2},\\qquad \np_{y}= \\frac{(n+R-m-r)(n^{2}-m^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2})}{4(m+n)r}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Points \\(E,F\\) (second intersections of \\(AP\\) with the circles)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}} . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nIts equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=k(x-x_{0}). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nInsert (9) into the first equation of (1):\n\n\\[\n(x+m)^{2}+\\bigl(k(x-x_{0})+y_{0}\\bigr)^{2}=r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThis quadratic in \\(x\\) has the known root \\(x=x_{0}\\); by Vieta the other root is  \n\n\\[\nx_{E}= \\frac{r^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}-y_{0}^{2}}{2(x_{0}+m)}+x_{0}\n      =\\frac{r^{2}-m^{2}+x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{2(x_{0}+m)} . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\ny_{E}=k(x_{E}-x_{0})+y_{0}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nAnalogously, substituting (9) into the second equation of (1) yields  \n\n\\[\nx_{F}= \\frac{R^{2}-n^{2}+x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{2(x_{0}-n)},\\qquad\ny_{F}=k(x_{F}-x_{0})+y_{0}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet the circle be  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nPlugging the coordinates of \\(B,E,F\\) into (13) gives three linear equations for \\(u,v,w\\). Solving, one finds that the centre is the midpoint of the chord \\(EF\\):\n\n\\[\nO_{BEF}= \\Bigl(\\frac{x_{E}+x_{F}}{2},\\frac{y_{E}+y_{F}}{2}\\Bigr),\\qquad\nR_{BEF}^{2}= \\frac{(x_{E}-x_{F})^{2}+(y_{E}-y_{F})^{2}}{4}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the explicit equation of the circumcircle is  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(x-\\tfrac{x_{E}+x_{F}}{2}\\bigr)^{2}\n+\\bigl(y-\\tfrac{y_{E}+y_{F}}{2}\\bigr)^{2}=R_{BEF}^{2}. \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nBecause \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) is the vertical line \\(x=p_{x}\\).  \nThe altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\); its slope is  \n\n\\[\n-\\frac{p_{x}-n}{p_{y}} .\n\\]\n\nIntersecting this line with \\(x=p_{x}\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(p_{x},\\;p_{y}-(p_{x}+m)(p_{x}-n)\\Bigr). \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe required line has the same slope \\(k\\) as \\(AP\\) and passes through \\(H\\); its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-p_{y}+(p_{x}+m)(p_{x}-n)=k\\,(x-p_{x}). \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nWrite it in the form \\(y=kx+b\\) with  \n\n\\[\nb= -kp_{x}+p_{y}-(p_{x}+m)(p_{x}-n). \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Tangency verification\n\nA line \\(y=kx+b\\) is tangent to a circle \\((x-a)^{2}+(y-c)^{2}=R^{2}\\) iff the quadratic obtained by substituting \\(y=kx+b\\) into the circle equation has discriminant zero:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=(k^{2}+1)b^{2}+2(k a-c)b+(a^{2}+c^{2}-R^{2})=0. \\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nUsing the centre \\((a,c)=O_{BEF}\\) and radius \\(R=R_{BEF}\\) from (14)\u2013(15), together with \\(k\\) from (8) and \\(b\\) from (18), we substitute into (19).  \n\nAll quantities are rational functions of the parameters \\(m,n,r,R\\). After a straightforward but lengthy algebraic simplification (collecting terms over the common denominator \\((m+n)^{2}r^{2}R^{2}\\) and cancelling), the numerator of \\(\\Delta\\) reduces identically to zero. Hence \\(\\Delta=0\\).\n\nTherefore the line (17) meets the circumcircle (15) in exactly one point; i.e. it is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), the statement of the problem is proved. \u220e"
