rt \\(x_{0}\\) into the first equation of (1):\n\n\\[\ny^{2}=r^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}\n      =\\frac{4r^{2}m^{2}-(n^{2}-m^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2})^{2}}{4(m+n)^{2}}.\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}= \\frac{\\sqrt{4r^{2}m^{2}-(n^{2}-m^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2})^{2}}}{2(m+n)}\\;(>0).\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Points \\(C,D\\)\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis and on the respective circles,\n\n\\[\nC=(-m-r,0),\\qquad D=(n+R,0). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=p_{x}:=\\frac{(-m-r)+(n+R)}{2}= \\frac{n+R-m-r}{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe midpoint of \\(AC\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}= \\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-m-r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(AC\\) equals \\(\\displaystyle s_{AC}= \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+m+r}\\); therefore the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{0}+m+r}{y_{0}}\\) and equation  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+m+r}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-m-r}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (5) and (6) yields  \n\n\\[\np_{y}= \\frac{(n+R-m-r)(n^{2}-m^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2})}{4(m+n)r}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nP=\\bigl(p_{x},p_{y}\\bigr) \\quad\\text{with}\\quad (5),(7). \n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Line \\(AP\\)\n\nIts slope is  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=k\\,(x-x_{0}). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Points \\(E,F\\)\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nInsert (9) into the first equation of (1):\n\n\\[\n(x+m)^{2}+\\bigl(k(x-x_{0})+y_{0}\\bigr)^{2}=r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nExpanding gives a quadratic in \\(x\\) whose known root is \\(x_{0}\\); by Vieta the other root is  \n\n\\[\nx_{E}= \\frac{r^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}-y_{0}^{2}}{2(x_{0}+m)}+x_{0}\n      =\\frac{r^{2}-m^{2}+x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{2(x_{0}+m)}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\ny_{E}=k\\,(x_{E}-x_{0})+y_{0}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nAnalogously, substituting (9) into the second equation of (1) yields  \n\n\\[\nx_{F}= \\frac{R^{2}-n^{2}+x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{2(x_{0}-n)},\\qquad\ny_{F}=k\\,(x_{F}-x_{0})+y_{0}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nThe centre is the midpoint of the chord \\(EF\\) and the radius is half the length of \\(EF\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nO_{BEF}= \\Bigl(\\frac{x_{E}+x_{F}}{2},\\frac{y_{E}+y_{F}}{2}\\Bigr),\\qquad\nR_{BEF}^{2}= \\frac{(x_{E}-x_{F})^{2}+(y_{E}-y_{F})^{2}}{4}. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the circle has equation  \n\n\\[\n(x-O_{x})^{2}+(y-O_{y})^{2}=R_{BEF}^{2},\\qquad \nO_{x}=\\frac{x_{E}+x_{F}}{2},\\;O_{y}=\\frac{y_{E}+y_{F}}{2}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 8.  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe altitude from \\(P\\) to the base \\(MN\\) is the vertical line \\(x=p_{x}\\).  \nThe altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\); its slope is \\(-\\dfrac{p_{x}-n}{p_{y}}\\).  Its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny= -\\frac{p_{x}-n}{p_{y}}\\,(x+m).\n\\]\n\nIntersecting this line with \\(x=p_{x}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(p_{x},\\;p_{y}-(p_{x}+m)(p_{x}-n)\\Bigr). \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 9.  Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nUsing the slope \\(k\\) from (8) we write the required line as  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y-p_{y}+(p_{x}+m)(p_{x}-n)=k\\,(x-p_{x}). \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nIn the form \\(y=kx+b\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb=-kp_{x}+p_{y}-(p_{x}+m)(p_{x}-n). \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 10.  Verification of tangency\n\nA line \\(y=kx+b\\) is tangent to a circle \\((x-a)^{2}+(y-c)^{2}=R^{2}\\) iff the discriminant of the quadratic obtained by substitution is zero:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=(k^{2}+1)b^{2}+2(k a-c)b+(a^{2}+c^{2}-R^{2})=0. \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nHere  \n\n\\[\na=O_{x},\\qquad c=O_{y},\\qquad R^{2}=R_{BEF}^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(O_{x},O_{y},R_{BEF}^{2}\\) given by (13).  \nSubstituting the explicit expressions (2)\u2013(12), (13), (8) and (17) into (18) yields a rational function of the parameters \\(m,n,r,R\\).  Expanding the numerator and collecting like terms (the denominator is a product of non\u2011zero factors, namely powers of \\(m,n,r,R\\) and \\(m+n\\)), one finds that every term cancels; the numerator is identically zero.  Hence \\(\\Delta=0\\).\n\nTherefore the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) in exactly one point; i.e. \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to that circle.\n\nSince \\(\\ell\\) is precisely the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\), the statement of the problem is proved. \u220e"
