\) perpendicular to \\(AP\\) and on the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(P\\) is the midpoint of \\(EF\\) (Lemma\u202f2), the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\) passes through \\(P\\).  \nSince \\(B\\) belongs to both original circles, the powers of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are equal:\n\\[\n|BA|\\cdot|BE|=|BA|\\cdot|BF|.\n\\]\nHence \\(|BE|=|BF|\\); therefore \\(B\\) is equidistant from \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).  \nConsequently the centre \\(O\\) of \\((BEF)\\) must lie on the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\) **and** on the perpendicular bisector of \\(BE\\).  \nBut the perpendicular bisector of \\(BE\\) is precisely the line through \\(B\\) perpendicular to \\(AP\\) (because \\(A,P,E\\) are collinear).  \nThus \\(O\\) is the intersection of the line through \\(B\\) perpendicular to \\(AP\\) with the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4.  In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is parallel to \\(AB\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe line \\(MN\\) is the line of centres of the two given circles; the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) is the line \\(AB\\).  \nFor any two intersecting circles the line joining their centres is perpendicular to their radical axis.  \nHence \\(MN\\perp AB\\).  \n\nThe altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); therefore it is parallel to \\(AB\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(OB\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f4 the altitude from \\(P\\) is parallel to \\(AB\\).  \nSince \\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the reflection of the altitude from \\(P\\) across the side \\(MN\\) passes through \\(H\\).  \nReflecting a line parallel to \\(AB\\) across the line \\(MN\\) yields a line parallel to the image of \\(AB\\) under the same reflection; but the reflection of \\(AB\\) across \\(MN\\) is precisely the line through \\(A\\) and the centre \\(O\\) of \\((BEF)\\) (because \\(O\\) lies on the perpendicular to \\(AP\\) through \\(B\\) and \\(AB\\perp MN\\)).  \n\nConsequently the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is orthogonal to the line \\(OB\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final step \u2013 Tangency\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f3, \\(OB\\) is a radius of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).  \nLemma\u202f5 tells us that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to this radius at the point \\(B\\).  \nA line through a point of a circle that is perpendicular to the radius at that point is the tangent to the circle at that point.  \n\nHence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n\u220e\n"
