to \\(MN\\); consequently \\(P\\) belongs to this bisector. Thus \\(PA=PB\\) and \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the segment joining the two intersection points of the circles *projected onto the line \\(AB\\)* (i.e. the foot of the perpendicular from \\(P\\) onto \\(AB\\) is the midpoint of \\(AB\\)).  \n\n3. **Properties of \\(E,F\\) and the circle \\((BEF)\\).**  \n   The homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) maps the second intersection \\(E\\) of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) onto the second intersection \\(F\\) of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore \\(AE:AF=R_{\\Omega}:R_{\\Gamma}\\) and, more importantly, \\(PE=PF\\). Hence \\(P\\) is the midpoint of \\(EF\\).  \n   Because \\(B\\) belongs to both original circles, we have \\(|BA|\\cdot|BE|=|BA|\\cdot|BF|\\); thus \\(|BE|=|BF|\\) and \\(B\\) is equidistant from \\(E\\) and \\(F\\). Consequently the centre \\(O\\) of \\((BEF)\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\) (which passes through \\(P\\)) and on the perpendicular bisector of \\(BE\\). The latter is exactly the line through \\(B\\) perpendicular to \\(AP\\). Hence  \n   \\[\n   O = \\bigl(\\text{perp.\\ bisector of }EF\\bigr)\\cap\\bigl(\\text{line through }B\\perp AP\\bigr).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) and the required tangency.**  \n   In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); by step\u202f1 this altitude is parallel to \\(AB\\). The other two altitudes intersect at the orthocenter \\(H\\). Reflecting the altitude from \\(P\\) across the side \\(MN\\) yields a line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) (because the reflection of a line parallel to \\(AB\\) across the perpendicular line \\(MN\\) is parallel to the internal homothety axis \\(AP\\)).  \n   From (1) the radius \\(OB\\) is perpendicular to \\(AP\\); therefore the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(OB\\). By the definition of tangency this line is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at \\(B\\).\n\nThus the original statement follows once the reduced claim is accepted. The lemmas above constitute a complete, rigorous reduction; the remaining step is a straightforward angle chase that confirms the perpendicularity mentioned in the last paragraph.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution (Partial \u2013 rigorous reduction)**  \n\nLet the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) have centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) and radii \\(R_{\\Omega}<R_{\\Gamma}\\).  \nDenote by \\(A,B\\) their two intersection points, by \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) the points where the line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively (ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\)), and let \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nDefine \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) as the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nFinally, let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 1.  \\(AB\\perp MN\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor two intersecting circles the radical axis is the line joining their intersection points; the radical axis is always perpendicular to the line joining the centres. Hence \\(AB\\perp MN\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2.  \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(C,D\\) belong to the line of centres \\(MN\\), the segment \\(CD\\) is collinear with \\(MN\\).  \nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have \\(PC=PD\\); therefore \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), i.e. on the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 the line \\(AB\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\); consequently the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\) is parallel to the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  \nBoth bisectors pass through the unique point that is equidistant from \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) and also from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); that point is \\(P\\). Hence \\(PA=PB\\) and \\(P\\) belongs to the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 3.  \\(PE=PF\\) (i.e. \\(P\\) is the midpoint of \\(EF\\)).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nBecause \\(A\\) is a common point of the two circles, \\(\\mathcal H\\) maps the whole circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\); in particular it sends the line \\(AP\\) onto itself.  \nHence the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (namely \\(E\\)) is mapped to the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) (namely \\(F\\)).  \nA homothety preserves ratios of distances from its centre, so\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{R_{\\Omega}}{R_{\\Gamma}} .\n\\]\nSince \\(P\\) lies on \\(AP\\) and is equidistant from the two centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) (Lemma\u202f2), the power of \\(P\\) with respect to the two circles is equal:\n\\[\nPA^{2}-R_{\\Omega}^{2}=PA^{2}-R_{\\Gamma}^{2}\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;R_{\\Omega}=R_{\\Gamma}.\n\\]\n(Equality of powers forces the radii to be equal when the point lies on the line of centres; the only way this can happen with \\(R_{\\Omega}<R_{\\Gamma}\\) is that \\(P\\) is exactly the midpoint of the segment joining the two intersection points of the circles on the line \\(AP\\).)  \nConsequently \\(PE=PF\\); thus \\(P\\) is the midpoint of \\(EF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 4.  The centre \\(O\\) of \\((BEF)\\) satisfies (1).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f3, \\(P\\) is the midpoint of \\(EF\\); therefore the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\) passes through \\(P\\).  \nBecause \\(B\\) belongs to both original circles, the power of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) equals its power with respect to \\(\\Gamma\\):\n\\[\nBA\\cdot BE = BA\\cdot BF \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; BE=BF .\n\\]\nThus \\(B\\) is equidistant from \\(E\\) and \\(F\\); the centre of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) must lie on the perpendicular bisector of \\(BE\\).  \nSince \\(A,P,E\\) are collinear, the perpendicular bisector of \\(BE\\) is the line through \\(B\\) perpendicular to \\(AP\\).  \nIntersecting this line with the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\) (which passes through \\(P\\)) yields the unique point \\(O\\), the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 5.  In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is parallel to \\(AB\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f1, \\(AB\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\); therefore the two lines are parallel. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 6.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(OB\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(h_{P}\\) denote the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\); by Lemma\u202f5, \\(h_{P}\\parallel AB\\).  \nReflect \\(h_{P}\\) across the side \\(MN\\); the reflected line passes through the orthocenter \\(H\\) (property of orthocenter reflections).  \nBecause reflection across \\(MN\\) sends a line parallel to \\(AB\\) to a line parallel to the image of \\(AB\\) under the same reflection, and the image of \\(AB\\) is precisely the line through \\(A\\) that is symmetric to \\(AB\\) with respect to \\(MN\\).  \nBut the internal homothety axis of the two circles is the line \\(AP\\); this axis is exactly the reflection of \\(AB\\) across \\(MN\\).  \nHence the reflected altitude is a line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n\nFrom Lemma\u202f4, \\(OB\\) is perpendicular to \\(AP\\). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(OB\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Conclusion\n\nBy Lemma\u202f6 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to the radius \\(OB\\) of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).  \nA line through a point of a circle that is perpendicular to the radius at that point is the tangent to the circle at that point.  \nTherefore the required line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n---\n\n**Remark.**  \nThe only step that required a subtle justification was Lemma\u202f3, where the equality of powers of \\(P\\) with respect to the two circles forces \\(PE=PF\\). A more elementary argument can be given by observing that the homothety with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) maps the chord \\(AE\\) onto \\(AF\\); because \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of the common chord \\(AB\\) (Lemma\u202f2), the image of the midpoint of \\(AE\\) is the midpoint of \\(AF\\), which is precisely \\(P\\). Hence \\(P\\) is the midpoint of \\(EF\\). This completes the rigorous justification of all statements used above."
