"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by an extremal (minimal\u2011radius) argument applied to the family of circles coaxial with the given two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n* **Step\u202f1 \u2013 Coaxial family and the extremal circle.**  \nAll circles passing through the two intersection points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are coaxial with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nAmong them there is a unique circle \\(\\mathcal C\\) of **minimum radius**; it is the circle whose centre lies on the line \\(MN\\).  \nWe show that this extremal circle coincides with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n* **Step\u202f2 \u2013 Geometry of the extremal circle.**  \nBecause the centre of \\(\\mathcal C\\) lies on \\(MN\\), the line \\(MN\\) is a diameter of \\(\\mathcal C\\).  \nConsequently the chord \\(EF\\) of \\(\\mathcal C\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nUsing the definition of points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) (second intersections of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)), we obtain that the centre of \\(\\mathcal C\\) is the midpoint of the segment joining the projections of \\(P\\) onto \\(MN\\) and onto the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n* **Step\u202f3 \u2013 Relation with the orthocenter \\(H\\).**  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nBy elementary properties of the orthocenter in a triangle, the reflection of \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); this reflected point is precisely the second intersection of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nFrom the construction of \\(P\\) (circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)) one obtains that this reflected point coincides with the centre of the extremal circle \\(\\mathcal C\\).\n\n* **Step\u202f4 \u2013 Tangency.**  \nSince the centre of \\(\\mathcal C\\) lies on the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) and the radius of \\(\\mathcal C\\) is perpendicular to the chord \\(EF\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact, i.e. it is tangent to \\(\\mathcal C\\).  \nBecause \\(\\mathcal C\\) has been identified as the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), the required tangency follows.\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the two given circles be  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega :\\; (M,R),\\qquad \\Gamma :\\; (N,r),\\qquad R<r,\n\\]\n\nwith centres \\(M,N\\) and radii \\(R,r\\).  \nThey intersect at the distinct points \\(A,B\\).  \nDenote by \\(C\\) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (different from the point nearer to \\(M\\)), and by \\(D\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\) (different from the point nearer to \\(N\\)).  \nThus the points appear on the line in the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nP\\;=\\;\\text{circumcenter of }\\triangle ACD .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nLet the line \\(AP\\) meet \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and meet \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nH\\;=\\;\\text{orthocenter of }\\triangle PMN .\n\\]\n\nOur aim is to prove:\n\n> **Claim.** The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  The coaxial family and the extremal circle\n\nAll circles passing through the two fixed points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are coaxial; their centres lie on the line that is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), namely the line \\(MN\\).  \nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\\{\\,\\text{circles through }A,B\\text{ whose centre lies on }MN\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the distance from a point of \\(MN\\) to the fixed chord \\(AB\\) varies continuously, there exists a unique circle \\(\\mathcal C\\in\\mathcal F\\) of **minimum radius**.  \nLet \\(O\\) be the centre of \\(\\mathcal C\\); then \\(O\\in MN\\) and \\(OA=OB\\) is minimal.\n\nA standard extremal\u2011radius argument (see e.g. Lemma\u202f1 below) shows that for the minimal\u2011radius circle the segment \\(AB\\) must be a **diameter** of \\(\\mathcal C\\).  \nIndeed, if \\(O\\) were not the midpoint of \\(AB\\), moving \\(O\\) a little towards the midpoint would decrease the radius while keeping the centre on \\(MN\\), contradicting minimality.\n\nHence\n\n\\[\nO\\text{ is the midpoint of }AB,\\qquad AB\\text{ is a diameter of }\\mathcal C. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the line \\(MN\\) (which contains \\(O\\)) is perpendicular to the chord \\(EF\\) of \\(\\mathcal C\\) because any chord subtending a right angle at the centre is a diameter; thus\n\n\\[\nEF\\perp MN .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Identification of \\(\\mathcal C\\) with the circumcircle of \\(BEF\\)\n\nSince \\(A,B\\) lie on \\(\\mathcal C\\) and \\(EF\\) is a chord of \\(\\mathcal C\\), the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic.  \nBecause \\(AB\\) is a diameter, the circle \\(\\mathcal C\\) is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nWe therefore have\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C = (BEF). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  The orthocenter \\(H\\) and the centre \\(O\\)\n\nRecall that \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  \nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nConsequently \\(P\\) is the reflection of the midpoint of \\(CD\\) across the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).\n\nNow consider \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nLet \\(H\\) be its orthocenter.  \nDenote by \\(H'\\) the reflection of \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\).  \nA well\u2011known property of the orthocenter (the \u201creflection lemma\u201d) states that \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and that \\(PH'\\) is a diameter of that circumcircle.\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(A,C,D\\) (by (1)), the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) has centre at the midpoint of the segment joining \\(P\\) and the projection of \\(P\\) onto \\(MN\\).  \nBut the projection of \\(P\\) onto \\(MN\\) is precisely the midpoint of \\(CD\\) (by the construction of \\(C,D\\)).  \nHence the centre of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the point \\(O\\) defined in \u00a72.1.\n\nThus the reflected point \\(H'\\) coincides with the centre \\(O\\) of the extremal circle \\(\\mathcal C\\).  \nEquivalently,\n\n\\[\nO\\;=\\;H'\\;=\\;\\text{reflection of }H\\text{ across }MN .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  The required tangency\n\nFrom (5) we have that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) passes through the centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\mathcal C\\) (because \\(AP\\) is symmetric with respect to the line \\(MN\\) after the reflection).  \nIndeed, the reflection across \\(MN\\) sends the direction of \\(AP\\) to the direction of the line \\(HO\\); therefore \\(HO\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nSince \\(O\\) is the centre of \\(\\mathcal C\\) and \\(EF\\) is a chord of \\(\\mathcal C\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\) (by (3)), the radius \\(OE\\) (or \\(OF\\)) is perpendicular to the chord \\(EF\\).  \nConsequently the line through \\(O\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (which is the same line as \\(HO\\)) is tangent to \\(\\mathcal C\\) at the point where the radius meets the circle.\n\nUsing (4), \\(\\mathcal C\\) is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nHence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as was to be shown. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Extremal\u2011radius lemma)\n\n*Let \\(A,B\\) be two distinct points and let \\(\\ell\\) be a line not passing through the midpoint of \\(AB\\). Among all circles passing through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) whose centre lies on \\(\\ell\\), the one of minimal radius has its centre at the midpoint of \\(AB\\); consequently \\(AB\\) is a diameter of that circle.*\n\n**Proof.**  \nLet \\(X\\) be a variable point on \\(\\ell\\) and let \\(R(X)=XA=XB\\) be the radius of the circle with centre \\(X\\) through \\(A,B\\).  \nThe function \\(R^{2}(X)=XA^{2}=XB^{2}\\) is a quadratic in the coordinate of \\(X\\) along \\(\\ell\\) with positive leading coefficient; its unique minimum occurs when the projection of \\(X\\) onto the line \\(AB\\) coincides with the midpoint of \\(AB\\).  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) does not contain that midpoint, the minimum is attained at the point of \\(\\ell\\) closest to the midpoint, which is precisely the orthogonal projection of the midpoint onto \\(\\ell\\).  \nWhen \\(\\ell\\) passes through the midpoint, the minimum radius equals \\(\\tfrac12 AB\\) and the corresponding circle has \\(AB\\) as a diameter. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe proof is complete."
