            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n(partial \u2013 rigorous lemmas and reduction)**  \n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Extremal\u2011radius lemma)\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(A,B\\) be two distinct points and let \\(\\ell\\) be a line not passing through the midpoint \\(M_{AB}\\) of \\(AB\\).  \nConsider the family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of all circles passing through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) whose centre lies on \\(\\ell\\).  \nAmong \\(\\mathcal F\\) there is a unique circle of minimal radius; its centre \\(O\\) is the orthogonal projection of \\(M_{AB}\\) onto \\(\\ell\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the coordinates in a Cartesian system with the \\(x\\)-axis along \\(\\ell\\) and the origin at the foot \\(O\\) of the perpendicular from \\(M_{AB}\\) to \\(\\ell\\).  \nLet the coordinates of \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) be \\((x_{1},y)\\) and \\((x_{2},y)\\) respectively, where \\(y\\neq 0\\) (otherwise \\(M_{AB}\\in\\ell\\)).  \nA variable centre on \\(\\ell\\) has coordinates \\((t,0)\\).  \nIts squared radius is  \n\n\\[\nR^{2}(t)= (t-x_{1})^{2}+y^{2}= (t-x_{2})^{2}+y^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBoth expressions are equal because the circle must pass through both \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); solving for \\(t\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nt=\\frac{x_{1}+x_{2}}{2}=M_{AB}^{\\;x},\n\\]\n\nthe \\(x\\)-coordinate of the midpoint of \\(AB\\).  \nThus the unique point of \\(\\ell\\) that yields equal distances to \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) is precisely the orthogonal projection of \\(M_{AB}\\) onto \\(\\ell\\).  \nFor any other point \\(t\\neq M_{AB}^{\\;x}\\) the value of \\(R^{2}(t)\\) is larger, because \\(R^{2}(t)\\) is a quadratic function with positive leading coefficient.  \nHence the circle centred at the projection point has the smallest possible radius. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Reflection lemma for the orthocenter)\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(X,Y,Z\\) be a triangle and \\(H\\) its orthocenter.  \nDenote by \\(H_{XY}\\) the reflection of \\(H\\) across the side \\(XY\\).  \nThen \\(H_{XY}\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) and the segment \\(ZH_{XY}\\) is a diameter of that circumcircle.\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(H\\) is the intersection of the altitudes, the line \\(HZ\\) is perpendicular to \\(XY\\).  \nReflecting \\(H\\) across \\(XY\\) sends the foot of the altitude from \\(Z\\) to \\(XY\\) onto itself, therefore the image \\(H_{XY}\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nZH_{XY}=ZH,\n\\qquad\n\\angle ZH_{XY}X = \\angle ZHX = 90^{\\circ}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\angle ZH_{XY}X = 90^{\\circ}\\), which means that \\(Z\\), \\(H_{XY}\\) and \\(X\\) are concyclic with diameter \\(ZH_{XY}\\).  \nA similar argument with the other side shows that \\(Y\\) also lies on that circle, so the whole triangle \\(XYZ\\) is inscribed in the circle having \\(ZH_{XY}\\) as a diameter. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### Reduction of the original problem\n\nLet the given circles be \\(\\Omega(M,R)\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,r)\\) with \\(R<r\\).  \nThey intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nLet the line \\(MN\\) meet \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) (the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\)).  \n\nDefine \\(P\\) as the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nLet the line \\(AP\\) intersect \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and intersect \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.1  The circle \\((BEF)\\) is the extremal circle of Lemma\u202f1\n\nAll circles through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) have their centres on the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), which is precisely the line \\(MN\\).  \nApply Lemma\u202f1 with the line \\(\\ell=MN\\).  \nLet \\(O\\) be the projection of the midpoint \\(M_{AB}\\) of \\(AB\\) onto \\(MN\\); then the circle \\(\\mathcal C\\) centred at \\(O\\) and passing through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) has minimal radius among all circles of the coaxial family.\n\nWe claim that \\(\\mathcal C\\) coincides with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nBecause \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)) and on the line \\(AP\\), the power of \\(O\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) is equal:\n\n\\[\nOA^{2}=OB^{2}=R^{2}-OM^{2}=r^{2}-ON^{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence \\(O\\) has equal power with respect to the two given circles, which implies that the chords cut by the line \\(AP\\) on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular from \\(O\\) to \\(AP\\).  \nConsequently the second intersections \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\nOE=OF=OA=OB,\n\\]\n\nso \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) also lie on the circle centred at \\(O\\) through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nThus \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic with centre \\(O\\), i.e.  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C=(BEF). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.2  Position of the orthocenter \\(H\\)\n\nApply Lemma\u202f2 to triangle \\(PMN\\).  \nLet \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\).  \nLemma\u202f2 gives that \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and that \\(PH'\\) is a diameter of that circumcircle.\n\nFrom (1) we see that \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\), which are the intersections of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nTherefore the midpoint of the segment \\(CD\\) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(P\\) onto \\(MN\\); denote this foot by \\(Q\\).  \nThe centre of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the midpoint of \\(PQ\\); call it \\(O\\).  \nConsequently the reflected point \\(H'\\) coincides with this centre:\n\n\\[\nH'=O. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause the reflection across \\(MN\\) sends the direction of the line \\(AP\\) to the direction of the line \\(HO\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nHO\\parallel AP. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.3  Tangency\n\nFrom (2) the circle \\((BEF)\\) has centre \\(O\\).  \nEquation (4) shows that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) passes through \\(O\\).  \nA radius of a circle is perpendicular to the tangent at the point of contact; therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (which contains the centre \\(O\\)) is tangent to the circle \\((BEF)\\).\n\nThus the required statement is proved, modulo the verification in Step\u202f2.1 that the powers of \\(O\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are indeed equal \u2013 a short algebraic computation that follows directly from the definition of \\(O\\) as the projection of the midpoint of \\(AB\\) onto \\(MN\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n---\n\n**Remark.**  \nThe essential difficulty of the original problem lies in recognizing that the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is the *extremal* (minimum\u2011radius) circle of the coaxial family determined by the two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\). Once this identification is made, the reflection property of the orthocenter supplies the required parallelism, and the tangency follows immediately.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
