em with the line \\(\\ell\\) as the \\(x\\)-axis and the origin at the foot \\(O\\) of the perpendicular from the midpoint \\(M_{AB}\\) to \\(\\ell\\).  \n   - The algebraic derivation \\(R^{2}(t)=(t-x_{1})^{2}+y^{2}=(t-x_{2})^{2}+y^{2}\\) correctly forces \\(t=\\frac{x_{1}+x_{2}}{2}\\), i.e. the \\(x\\)-coordinate of the projection of \\(M_{AB}\\).  \n   - The argument that \\(R^{2}(t)\\) is a quadratic with positive leading coefficient, hence minimal at this \\(t\\), is sound.  \n   - **Conclusion:** Lemma\u202f1 is proved correctly.\n\n2. **Lemma\u202f2 (Reflection lemma for the orthocenter).**  \n   - The statement that the reflection \\(H_{XY}\\) of the orthocenter across side \\(XY\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) is a standard fact.  \n   - The proof correctly notes that \\(HZ\\perp XY\\) and that reflection preserves the right angle, giving \\(\\angle ZH_{XY}X=90^{\\circ}\\). Hence \\(Z\\), \\(H_{XY}\\), and \\(X\\) are concyclic with diameter \\(ZH_{XY}\\).  \n   - By symmetry the same circle contains \\(Y\\). Thus the lemma is valid.  \n   - **Conclusion:** Lemma\u202f2 is correct.\n\n3. **Setup of the original problem (definition of points).**  \n   - The notation \\(\\Omega(M,R)\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,r)\\) and the definitions of \\(C,D,E,F,P,H\\) are consistent with the problem statement.  \n   - The relation \\(PA=PC=PD\\) follows from \\(P\\) being the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \n   - **Conclusion:** No issues here.\n\n4. **Step\u202f2.1 \u2013 Claim: \u201cAll circles through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) have their centres on the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), which is precisely the line \\(MN\\).\u201d**  \n   - **Critical Error:** For two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the radical axis is the line **through their intersection points**, i.e. the line \\(AB\\). The line \\(MN\\) is the line joining the centres, not the radical axis. Consequently the family of circles through \\(A\\) and \\(