r across a side does **not** coincide with the centre of the circumcircle; it coincides with the antipode of the opposite vertex on that circumcircle, not with the centre. Therefore the claim \\(H'=O\\) is false.  \n   - **Critical Error:** Misapplication of Lemma\u202f2 leads to an erroneous statement about the location of \\(H'\\).\n\n7. **Step\u202f2.2 \u2013 \u201cBecause the reflection across \\(MN\\) sends the direction of the line \\(AP\\) to the direction of the line \\(HO\\), we have \\(HO\\parallel AP\\).\u201d**  \n   - This inference assumes the previous (false) identification \\(H'=O\\) and also that the reflection of a line through a point yields a parallel line, which is not generally true. Hence this step lacks justification and rests on earlier false statements.  \n   - **Critical Error:** Dependent on earlier invalid claims.\n\n8. **Step\u202f2.3 \u2013 Tangency argument.**  \n   - The argument that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) passes through the centre \\(O\\) of \\((BEF)\\) and therefore is tangent is valid **only if** the centre of \\((BEF)\\) is indeed \\(O\\) and the parallelism holds. Both premises have been shown to be unfounded.  \n   - Consequently the final conclusion is not supported by the preceding reasoning.  \n\n9. **Overall Assessment.**  \n   - The proof hinges on the false statement that the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) is the line \\(MN\\). This invalidates the entire \u201cextremal\u2011circle\u201d identification.  \n   - Additional misinterpretations of Lemma\u202f2 (confusing the reflected orthocenter with the circumcenter) introduce further critical errors.  \n   - Because the logical chain is broken early, the solution does not constitute a valid proof of the problem statement."
