e for any point \\(X\\) on \\(\\Omega\\) the image on \\(\\Gamma\\) is the point \\(X'\\) on the ray \\(AX\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AX}{AX'}=\\frac{1}{k}.\n\\]\n\nApplying this to \\(C\\) and \\(E\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AC}{AD}= \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{1}{k}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Similarity of \\(\\triangle AEC\\) and \\(\\triangle AFD\\)\n\nFrom (1) we have the side\u2011ratio \\(\\dfrac{AE}{AC}= \\dfrac{AF}{AD}\\).  \nThe homothety preserves oriented angles, hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle EAC =\\angle CAD ,\\qquad \\angle FAC =\\angle DAF .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\triangle AEC\\sim\\triangle AFD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIn particular  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CEA =\\angle DFA . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. The quadrilateral \\(B,E,F\\) is cyclic\n\nBecause \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) belong to both circles, the measure of the angle subtended by chord \\(AB\\) is the same on each circle. Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB =\\angle AFB . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAngles (4) are equal angles subtended by the same chord \\(AB\\) at the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\); therefore the four points \\(B,E,F\\) lie on a common circle. Denote this circle by \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Geometry of the orthocentre \\(H\\)\n\n\\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); hence \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) and  \n\n\\[\nAP\\perp CD . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear, \\(CD\\) coincides with the line \\(MN\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); by (6) this altitude is parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\); then \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Relating \\(\\ell\\) to the chord \\(BE\\)\n\nReflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\); call the image \\(H'\\). In any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circumcircle, so \\(H'\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Moreover \\(PH=PH'\\) and \\(HH'\\perp MN\\). Because \\(\\ell\\parallel PH\\) and also \\(\\ell\\parallel PH'\\), the acute angle that \\(\\ell\\) makes with \\(MN\\) equals the acute angle that \\(PH'\\) makes with \\(MN\\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,MN)=\\angle PH'M . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle, \\(\\angle PH'M\\) is the inscribed angle subtended by chord \\(PM\\); it equals \\(\\angle PNM\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,MN)=\\angle PNM . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(MN\\) coincides with \\(CD\\), (8) can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,CD)=\\angle PNM . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nIn the isosceles trapezoid \\(APCD\\) (bases \\(AP\\) and \\(CD\\) are parallel by (5)) the base angles satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\angle PNM =\\angle CDE . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (10) into (9) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,CD)=\\angle CDE . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nFrom the similarity (2) we have \\(\\angle CDE =\\angle CEA\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,CD)=\\angle CEA . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\perp CD\\) (by (5)), the acute angle that \\(\\ell\\) makes with any line equals the complement of the angle that \\(CD\\) makes with that line. Applying this to the chord \\(BE\\) of \\(\\omega\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,BE)=\\angle CEA . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Tangency to \\(\\omega\\)\n\nFrom (3) we have \\(\\angle CEA =\\angle DFA\\). In the cyclic quadrilateral \\(\\omega\\) the angle \\(\\angle DFA\\) subtends chord \\(DF\\) at point \\(E\\); by the tangent\u2011chord theorem the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(E\\) makes with chord \\(EF\\) the angle \\(\\angle DFA\\). Because \\(B\\) also lies on \\(\\omega\\), the same tangent makes with chord \\(BE\\) the angle \\(\\angle BFE\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle DFA =\\angle BFE . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nCombining (13) and (14) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nEquality of the angle formed by a line with a chord and the angle subtended by that chord at the opposite point of the circle is precisely the tangent\u2011chord condition. Therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at the point \\(E\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
