sections of the same line \\(MN\\) with the two circles whose centres are \\(M\\) and \\(N\\)). Consequently the midpoint of \\(CD\\) belongs to \\(MN\\). The perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is therefore the line through \\(P\\) that is perpendicular to \\(MN\\). This line is precisely \\(AP\\) (the unique line through \\(A\\) and \\(P\\)), whence \\(AP\\perp MN\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n**Lemma\u202f3.** *Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\). Then \\(\\angle(\\ell ,BE)=\\angle BFE\\). Consequently \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(E\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f2, \\(AP\\) is an altitude of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (it is perpendicular to \\(MN\\)). Hence the altitude from \\(P\\) is parallel to \\(\\ell\\). Reflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\) to obtain \\(H'\\). In any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circumcircle; therefore \\(H'\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Moreover \\(PH=PH'\\) and \\(HH'\\perp MN\\). Because \\(\\ell\\parallel PH\\) and also \\(\\ell\\parallel PH'\\), the acute angle that \\(\\ell\\) makes with \\(MN\\) equals the acute angle that \\(PH'\\) makes with \\(MN\\):\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,MN)=\\angle PH'M .\n\\]\nSince \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle, \\(\\angle PH'M\\) is the inscribed angle subtended by chord \\(PM\\); it equals \\(\\angle PNM\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,MN)=\\angle PNM . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(MN\\) coincides with \\(CD\\), (13) can be rewritten as\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,CD)=\\angle PNM . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nIn the quadrilateral \\(APCD\\) we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\) (all radii of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)). Hence \\(APCD\\) is an isosceles trapezoid with bases \\(AP\\) and \\(CD\\) parallel (by Lemma\u202f2). In an isosceles trapezoid the base angles adjacent to a leg are equal; therefore\n\\[\n\\angle PNM =\\angle CDE . \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (15) into (14) yields\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,CD)=\\angle CDE . \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nFrom the similarity \\(\\triangle AEC\\sim\\triangle AFD\\) (proved in the outline) we have \\(\\angle CDE =\\angle CEA\\); consequently\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,CD)=\\angle CEA . \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\perp CD\\) (Lemma\u202f2), the acute angle that \\(\\ell\\) makes with any line equals the complement of the angle that \\(CD\\) makes with that line. Applying this to the chord \\(BE\\) of the circle \\(\\omega\\) gives\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,BE)=\\angle CEA . \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nFrom the similarity mentioned above we also have \\(\\angle CEA =\\angle DFA\\). In the cyclic quadrilateral \\(\\omega\\) the angle \\(\\angle DFA\\) subtends chord \\(DF\\) at point \\(E\\); by the tangent\u2011chord theorem the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(E\\) makes with chord \\(EF\\) the angle \\(\\angle DFA\\). Because \\(B\\) also lies on \\(\\omega\\), the same tangent makes with chord \\(BE\\) the angle \\(\\angle BFE\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\angle DFA =\\angle BFE . \\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nCombining (18) and (19) we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nEquality of the angle formed by a line with a chord and the angle subtended by that chord at the opposite point of the circle is precisely the tangent\u2011chord condition; therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(E\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n**Conclusion.**  \nLemmas\u202f1\u20133 together establish that the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) which is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). This completes the proof of the problem statement. \u220e"
