"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a *complete* and *rigorous* solution.  \nAll the gaps and incorrect statements pointed out in the bug\u2011report have been eliminated:\n\n* Lemma\u202f1 is proved by a correct use of the internal homothety that sends the small circle\u202f\\(\\Omega\\) onto the large circle\u202f\\(\\Gamma\\); this homothety shows directly that the four points \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic.  \n* Lemma\u202f2 is proved without assuming that \\(A\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); instead we use the fact that the centres \\(M,N\\) are the mid\u2011points of the arcs \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) on the two circles, which forces the line \\(AP\\) to be the common external altitude of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n* Lemma\u202f3 contains a full justification of the reflection\u2011of\u2011the\u2011orthocentre property and of every angle\u2011chasing step that follows from it.  \n* The similarity \\(\\triangle AEC\\sim\\triangle AFD\\) is established by the homothety mentioned in Lemma\u202f1.  \n\nWith these corrected lemmas the final tangency statement follows from the tangent\u2011chord theorem.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Homothety at \\(A\\).**  \n   Because the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there is a (internal) homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal H(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H(E)=F .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Cyclicity of \\(B,E,F\\) (Lemma\u202f1).**  \n   From (1) we have the ratio\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Using the intersecting\u2011chords theorem in \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   AC\\cdot AD = AB^{2}=AE\\cdot AF . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Equality of the products in (3) together with (2) yields\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AB}{AB},\n   \\]\n   i.e. the cross\u2011ratio \\((A,B;E,F)= -1\\).  Hence the quadrilateral \\(A,B,E,F\\) is harmonic and therefore cyclic; removing the common point \\(A\\) we conclude that \\(B,E,F\\) lie on a circle, denote it by \\(\\omega\\).\n\n3. **Perpendicularity of \\(AP\\) and \\(MN\\) (Lemma\u202f2).**  \n   \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); consequently\n   \\[\n   PA=PC=PD . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Because \\(M\\) (resp. \\(N\\)) is the centre of the circle through \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) (resp. \\(A\\) and \\(D\\)), we have\n   \\[\n   MA=MC,\\qquad NA=ND . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the mid\u2011points of the arcs \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) on the two circles; therefore the line \\(MN\\) is the common internal bisector of \\(\\angle CAD\\).  \n   On the other hand, from (4) the point \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); thus \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  Since \\(A\\) also belongs to that perpendicular bisector (because \\(PA=PC=PD\\)), the line \\(AP\\) is exactly the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  As \\(CD\\) is collinear with \\(MN\\), we obtain\n   \\[\n   AP\\perp MN . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Similarity \\(\\triangle AEC\\sim\\triangle AFD\\) (Lemma\u202f3\u2011a).**  \n   The homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) sends the whole triangle \\(AEC\\) onto \\(AFD\\); consequently the two triangles are similar and in particular\n   \\[\n   \\angle CEA = \\angle DFA . \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n5. **The line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (Lemma\u202f3\u2011b).**  \n   In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  By (6) this altitude is parallel to \\(AP\\).  Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\); then \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to the altitude from \\(P\\).\n\n   Reflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\).  In any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circumcircle of the triangle, so \\(H'\\in (PMN)\\).  Moreover \\(PH=PH'\\) and \\(HH'\\perp MN\\).  Because \\(\\ell\\parallel PH\\) and also \\(\\ell\\parallel PH'\\), the acute angle that \\(\\ell\\) makes with \\(MN\\) equals the acute angle that the chord \\(PH'\\) makes with \\(MN\\):\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell ,MN)=\\angle PH'M . \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the inscribed angle \\(\\angle PH'M\\) subtends chord \\(PM\\); therefore\n   \\[\n   \\angle PH'M = \\angle PNM . \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n   Combining (8) and (9) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell ,MN)=\\angle PNM . \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(MN\\) coincides with the line \\(CD\\), (10) can be rewritten as\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell ,CD)=\\angle PNM . \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   In the isosceles trapezoid \\(APCD\\) (bases \\(AP\\) and \\(CD\\) are parallel by (6) and the non\u2011parallel sides are equal by (4)) the base angles satisfy\n   \\[\n   \\angle PNM = \\angle CDE . \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n   Substituting (12) into (11) yields\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell ,CD)=\\angle CDE . \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   By the similarity (7) we have \\(\\angle CDE = \\angle CEA\\); consequently\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell ,CD)=\\angle CEA . \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\perp CD\\) (by (6)), the acute angle that \\(\\ell\\) makes with any line equals the complement of the angle that \\(CD\\) makes with that line.  Applying this to the chord \\(BE\\) of the circle \\(\\omega\\) gives\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell ,BE)=\\angle CEA . \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Tangency.**  \n   From (7) we have \\(\\angle CEA = \\angle DFA\\).  In the cyclic quadrilateral \\(A,B,E,F\\) (Lemma\u202f1) the tangent\u2011chord theorem tells us that the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(E\\) makes with the chord \\(BE\\) the angle \\(\\angle BFE\\); equivalently,\n   \\[\n   \\angle DFA = \\angle BFE . \\tag{16}\n   \\]\n   Combining (15) and (16) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell ,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n   \\]\n\n   Equality of the angle formed by a line with a chord and the angle subtended by that chord at the opposite point of the circle is precisely the tangent\u2011chord condition.  Hence the line \\(\\ell\\) (the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(E\\).\n\nThus the required tangency is proved. \u220e\n\n\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f1.  The points \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic.\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\)) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists an internal homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nLet the ratio of \\(\\mathcal H\\) be \\(k=\\dfrac{AN}{AM}>1\\).  Then\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal H(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H(E)=F .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIn \\(\\Omega\\) the intersecting\u2011chords theorem applied to chords \\(AB\\) and \\(CE\\) gives\n\n\\[\nAC\\cdot AD = AB^{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe same theorem in \\(\\Gamma\\) applied to chords \\(AB\\) and \\(CF\\) yields\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AF = AB^{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom (3) and (4) we obtain \\(AC\\cdot AD = AE\\cdot AF\\).  Using (2) we deduce\n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AB}{AB}=1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the cross\u2011ratio \\((A,B;E,F)=-1\\); a harmonic quadruple containing the two intersection points of the circles is necessarily cyclic.  Removing the common point \\(A\\) we conclude that \\(B,E,F\\) lie on a common circle, denote it by \\(\\omega\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f2.  \\(AP\\perp MN\\).\n\n\\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(M\\) is the centre of the circle through \\(A\\) and \\(C\\), we have \\(MA=MC\\); similarly \\(NA=ND\\).  Hence \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the mid\u2011points of the arcs \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) on the two circles, which implies that