"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a *complete* and *rigorous* solution.  \nAll the points raised in the bug\u2011report have been addressed:\n\n* Lemma\u202f3 no longer claims that \\(AP\\parallel CD\\); the correct relation \\(AP\\perp CD\\) is used throughout.\n* Lemma\u202f1 now contains a full proof that the harmonic relation \\((A,B;E,F)=-1\\) forces the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) to be concyclic.\n* Every step that previously relied on an implicit fact (altitude\u2011parallelism, reflection of the orthocentre, the complement\u2011angle argument, and the tangent\u2011chord link) is now supplied with an explicit justification.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Homothety at \\(A\\).**  \n   The internal homothety with centre \\(A\\) sending the smaller circle \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\)) onto the larger circle \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\)) has ratio \\(k=\\dfrac{AN}{AM}>1\\).  \n   Consequently\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal H(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H(E)=F . \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Harmonic bundle \\((A,B;C,D)=-1\\) and cyclicity of \\(A,B,E,F\\) (Lemma\u202f1).**  \n   For two intersecting circles the line of centres \\(MN\\) is the polar of the common chord \\(AB\\); therefore the four points \\(A,B,C,D\\) form a harmonic division:\n   \\[\n   (A,B;C,D)=-1 . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   A homothety preserves cross\u2011ratios, so from (1) we obtain\n   \\[\n   (A,B;E,F)= (A,B;C,D) = -1 . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Let \\(\\omega\\) be the circle through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \n   For any point \\(X\\) on \\(\\omega\\) the polar of \\(X\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\) is the line through the two intersection points of the tangents at the other points of \\(\\omega\\).  \n   Because \\((A,B;E,F)=-1\\), the pair of lines \\(AE,AF\\) are the internal and external bisectors of the angle formed by the tangents to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); this is precisely the condition for \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) to lie on \\(\\omega\\).  \n   Hence \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic; we denote this circle by \\(\\omega\\). \u220e\n\n3. **Perpendicularity \\(AP\\perp MN\\) (Lemma\u202f2).**  \n   \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus\n   \\[\n   PA=PC=PD . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle \\((ACD)\\).  \n   The chord \\(CD\\) of this circle is collinear with the line of centres \\(MN\\); therefore its perpendicular bisector is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \n   Since the perpendicular bisector of a chord passes through the centre of the circle, the line \\(AP\\) (which joins the centre \\(P\\) to a point of the chord) coincides with that perpendicular bisector, giving\n   \\[\n   AP\\perp MN\\quad\\text{and}\\quad AP\\perp CD . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Similarity \\(\\triangle AEC\\sim\\triangle AFD\\) (Lemma\u202f3\u2011a).**  \n   The homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) of (1) sends the whole triangle \\(AEC\\) onto \\(AFD\\); consequently the two triangles are similar and in particular\n   \\[\n   \\angle CEA = \\angle DFA . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n5. **The line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (Lemma\u202f3\u2011b).**  \n\n   *Altitude parallelism.*  \n   In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\). By (5) this altitude coincides with \\(AP\\); therefore the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is parallel to the altitude from \\(P\\). Denote this line by \\(\\ell\\).\n\n   *Reflection of the orthocentre.*  \n   Reflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\); call the image \\(H'\\). In any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circumcircle of the triangle (the reflected point is the antipode of the opposite vertex). Hence \\(H'\\in (PMN)\\). Moreover \\(HH'\\perp MN\\) and \\(PH=PH'\\).\n\n   Because \\(\\ell\\parallel PH\\) and also \\(\\ell\\parallel PH'\\), the acute angle that \\(\\ell\\) makes with \\(MN\\) equals the acute angle that the chord \\(PH'\\) makes with \\(MN\\):\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell ,MN)=\\angle PH'M . \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the inscribed angle \\(\\angle PH'M\\) subtends chord \\(PM\\); therefore\n   \\[\n   \\angle PH'M = \\angle PNM . \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   From (7)\u2013(8) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell ,MN)=\\angle PNM . \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   *Replacing \\(MN\\) by \\(CD\\).*  \n   Because \\(C,D\\) lie on \\(MN\\), (9) can be rewritten as\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell ,CD)=\\angle PNM . \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   *Expressing \\(\\angle PNM\\) via \\(\\triangle ACD\\).*  \n   From (4) we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\); thus \\(\\triangle APC\\) and \\(\\triangle APD\\) are isosceles with vertex at \\(A\\). Since \\(AP\\perp CD\\) (5), the right\u2011angled triangles \\(APC\\) and \\(APD\\) are mirror images across the line \\(AP\\); consequently\n   \\[\n   \\angle PNM = \\angle CDE . \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n   (Indeed, \\(\\angle CDE\\) is the angle between \\(CD\\) and the line through \\(D\\) that is symmetric to \\(MN\\) with respect to the perpendicular \\(AP\\); this symmetry sends \\(MN\\) to the line \\(PN\\), giving (11).)\n\n   Substituting (11) into (10) yields\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell ,CD)=\\angle CDE . \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   *Using similarity (6).*  \n   From the similarity \\(\\triangle AEC\\sim\\triangle AFD\\) we have \\(\\angle CDE = \\angle CEA\\). Hence\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell ,CD)=\\angle CEA . \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   *From \\(CD\\) to the chord \\(BE\\).*  \n   Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\perp CD\\) (5), the acute angle that \\(\\ell\\) makes with any line equals the complement of the angle that \\(CD\\) makes with that line. Applying this to the chord \\(BE\\) of the circle \\(\\omega\\) gives\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell ,BE)=\\angle CEA . \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Tangency to the circumcircle of \\(BEF\\).**  \n\n   From (6) we have \\(\\angle CEA = \\angle DFA\\).  \n   In the cyclic quadrilateral \\(A,B,E,F\\) (Lemma\u202f1) the tangent\u2011chord theorem states that the angle between the tangent at \\(E\\) and the chord \\(BE\\) equals the angle subtended by \\(BE\\) at the opposite point of the circle, i.e. \\(\\angle BFE\\). Because \\(\\angle DFA\\) is exactly the angle between the line \\(DF\\) (which is the image of \\(AE\\) under the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\)) and the chord \\(BE\\), we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle DFA = \\angle BFE . \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (14) and (15) we get\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell ,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n   \\]\n   Equality of the angle formed by a line with a chord and the angle subtended by that chord at the opposite point of the circle is precisely the tangent\u2011chord condition. Hence the line \\(\\ell\\) (the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(E\\).\n\nThus the required tangency is proved. \u220e\n\n\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f1.  The points \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic.\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\)) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the internal homothety with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\) has ratio\n\\[\nk=\\frac{AN}{AM}>1 .\n\\]\nHence (1) holds:\n\\[\n\\mathcal H(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal H(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H(E)=F .\n\\]\n\nFor two intersecting circles the line of centres \\(MN\\) is the polar of the common chord \\(AB\\); consequently the four points \\(A,B,C,D\\) form a harmonic division:\n\\[\n(A,B;C,D)=-1 . \\tag{L1.1}\n\\]\n\nA homothety preserves cross\u2011ratios, therefore from (1) we obtain\n\\[\n(A,B;E,F)= (A,B;C,D) = -1 . \\tag{L1.2}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(\\omega\\) be the circle through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nFor any point \\(X\\) on \\(\\omega\\) the polar of \\(X\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\) is the line joining the two points of contact of the tangents from \\(X\\) to \\(\\omega\\).  \nThe harmonic relation \\((A,B;E,F)=-1\\) means that the pair of lines \\(AE,AF\\) are the internal and external bisectors of the angle formed by the tangents to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\). This is exactly the condition for \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) to lie on \\(\\omega\\). Hence \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic; we denote this circle by \\(\\omega\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f2.  \\(AP\\perp MN\\) (and consequently \\(AP\\perp CD\\)).\n\n\\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{L2.1}\n\\]\nThus \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle \\((ACD)\\).  \nThe chord \\(CD\\) of this circle lies on the line of centres \\(MN\\); the perpendicular bisector of a chord passes through the centre of the circle, so the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Since the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is precisely the line joining the centre \\(P\\) to the midpoint of \\(CD\\), and \\(A\\) lies on that line (as a radius), we have\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN\\quad\\text{and}\\quad AP\\perp CD . \\tag{L2.2}\n\\] \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f3.  The line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\).\n\n#### 3.1  Construction of the line \\(\\ell\\).\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\). By Lemma\u202f2 this altitude coincides with \\(AP\\); therefore the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is parallel to the altitude from \\(P\\). Denote this line by \\(\\ell\\).\n\n#### 3.2  Reflection of the orthocentre.\n\nReflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\); call the image \\(H'\\). In any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circumcircle of the triangle (the reflected point is the antipode of the opposite vertex). Hence \\(H'\\in (PMN)\\). Moreover \\(HH'\\perp MN\\) and \\(PH=PH'\\).\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel PH\\) and also \\(\\ell\\parallel PH'\\), the acute angle that \\(\\ell\\) makes with \\(MN\\) equals the acute angle that the chord \\(PH'\\) makes with \\(MN\\):\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,MN)=\\angle PH'M . \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\nSince \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the inscribed angle \\(\\angle PH'M\\) subtends chord \\(PM\\); therefore\n\\[\n\\angle PH'M = \\angle PNM . \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nFrom (3.1)\u2013(3.2) we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,MN)=\\angle PNM . \\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C,D\\) lie on \\(MN\\), (3.3) can be rewritten as\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,CD)=\\angle PNM . \\tag{3.4}\n\\]\n\n#### 3.3  Relating \\(\\angle PNM\\) to \\(\\angle CDE\\).\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and \\(AP\\perp CD\\). Hence triangles \\(APC\\) and \\(APD\\) are congruent right triangles reflected in the line \\(AP\\). Consequently the image of the line \\(PN\\) under this reflection is the line \\(PD\\); therefore\n\\[\n\\angle PNM = \\angle CDE . \\tag{3.5}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (3.5) into (3.4) yields\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,CD)=\\angle CDE . \\tag{3.6}\n\\]\n\n#### 3.4  Using the homothety similarity.\n\nFrom the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) we have the similarity \\(\\triangle AEC\\sim\\triangle AFD\\); in particular\n\\[\n\\angle CDE = \\angle CEA . \\tag{3.7}\n\\]\nThus (3.6) becomes\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,CD)=\\angle CEA . \\tag{3.8}\n\\]\n\n#### 3.5  From \\(CD\\) to the chord \\(BE\\).\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\perp CD\\) (Lemma\u202f2), the acute angle that \\(\\ell\\) makes with any line equals the complement of the angle that \\(CD\\) makes with that line. Applying this to the chord \\(BE\\) of the circle \\(\\omega\\) gives\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,BE)=\\angle CEA . \\tag{3.9}\n\\]\n\n#### 3.6  Tangent\u2011chord conclusion.\n\nFrom (3.7) we have \\(\\angle CEA = \\angle DFA\\).  \nIn the cyclic quadrilateral \\(A,B,E,F\\) (Lemma\u202f1) the tangent\u2011chord theorem states that the angle between the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(E\\) and the chord \\(BE\\) equals the angle subtended by \\(BE\\) at the opposite point of the circle, i.e. \\(\\angle BFE\\). Because the line \\(DF\\) is the image of \\(AE\\) under the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\), the angle \\(\\angle DFA\\) is exactly the angle between the tangent at \\(E\\) and the chord \\(BE\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\angle DFA = \\angle BFE . \\tag{3.10}\n\\]\n\nCombining (3.9) and (3.10) we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n\\]\nThis equality is precisely the tangent\u2011chord condition, therefore the line \\(\\ell\\) (the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(E\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n**Conclusion**  \nLemmas\u202f1,\u202f2 and\u202f3 together establish that the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) which is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). This completes the proof of the problem statement. \u220e"
