ell_{\\Omega}= \\mathcal I(\\Omega\\setminus\\{A\\}),\\qquad\n\\ell_{\\Gamma}= \\mathcal I(\\Gamma\\setminus\\{A\\}).\n\\]\nThe second intersection point \\(B\\) of the two circles is sent to the point\n\\(B'=\\mathcal I(B)\\), which lies on both \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\); hence\n\\(B'=\\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) does not pass through \\(A\\); therefore its image under \\(\\mathcal I\\) is the circle\n\\[\n\\gamma = \\mathcal I(MN) = (AMN),\n\\]\nthe circle with diameter \\(AN\\) (the well\u2011known fact that the image of a line not through the centre of inversion is a circle through the centre of inversion).\n\nConsequently\n\\[\nC',D',M',N' \\in \\gamma ,\\qquad\nE'\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\; F'\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Harmonic division \\((A,B;C,D)=-1\\)\n\nFrom the intersecting\u2011circles configuration we have the equalities of powers of the centres:\n\\[\nMA\\cdot MC = MB\\cdot MD ,\\qquad\nNA\\cdot NC = NB\\cdot ND .\n\\]\nDividing the first equality by the second gives\n\\[\n\\frac{MC}{MD}= \\frac{MA}{MB}= \\frac{NC}{ND}.\n\\]\nHence the four points \\(A,B,C,D\\) are in harmonic division:\n\\[\n(A,B;C,D)=-1 . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the cross\u2011ratio (1) is a cross\u2011ratio of four collinear points; inversion preserves such a cross\u2011ratio, therefore the images satisfy\n\\[\n(A,B;C',D')=-1 . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3.  The quadrilateral \\(A B E F\\) is cyclic\n\nThe lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) intersect the circle \\(\\gamma\\) at the pairs of points\n\\[\n\\{C',E'\\}\\subset\\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\gamma ,\\qquad\n\\{D',F'\\}\\subset\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\cap\\gamma .\n\\]\nFor a circle, the cross\u2011ratio of the four intersection points of two lines with the circle equals the cross\u2011ratio of the corresponding four points on any line through the centre of the circle (projective invariance). Applying this to \\(\\gamma\\) we obtain\n\\[\n(A,B;E',F') = (A,B;C',D') = -1 .\n\\]\nThus the four points \\(A,B,E',F'\\) are harmonic on the circle \\(\\gamma\\).  \nFor four points on a circle, a cross\u2011ratio equal to \\(-1\\) is equivalent to the statement that the two chords \\(AE'\\) and \\(AF'\\) are isogonal with respect to the angle formed by the tangents at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\). This is precisely the condition for the original (non\u2011inverted) points \\(A,B,E,F\\) to be concyclic. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{A,B,E,F\\ \\text{lie on a common circle } \\omega } . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Geometry of the triangle \\(ACD\\)\n\n\\(P\\) is defined as the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD . \\tag{4}\n\\]\nConsequently \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); the line \\(AP\\) passes through the midpoint of \\(CD\\) and is perpendicular to \\(CD\\):\n\\[\nAP\\perp CD . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 5.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\). By (5) the line \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\); hence **\\(AP\\) coincides with the altitude from \\(P\\)**.\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\). Because \\(AP\\) is an altitude, \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to the altitude from \\(P\\); therefore \\(\\ell\\parallel PH\\).\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Reflection of the orthocentre\n\nReflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\); denote the image by \\(H^{*}\\).  \nIn any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circum\u2011circle of the triangle; thus\n\\[\nH^{*}\\in (PMN). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nMoreover \\(HH^{*}\\perp MN\\) and \\(PH=PH^{*}\\). Since \\(\\ell\\parallel PH\\) and also \\(\\ell\\parallel PH^{*}\\), the acute angle that \\(\\ell\\) makes with \\(MN\\) equals the acute angle that the chord \\(PH^{*}\\) makes with \\(MN\\):\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,MN)=\\angle PH^{*}M . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe inscribed angle \\(\\angle PH^{*}M\\) subtends the chord \\(PM\\) of the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\); consequently\n\\[\n\\angle PH^{*}M = \\angle PNM . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFrom (7)\u2013(8) we obtain\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle(\\ell ,MN)=\\angle PNM}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), (9) can be rewritten as\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle(\\ell ,CD)=\\angle PNM}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Relating \\(\\angle PNM\\) to \\(\\angle CDE\\)\n\nFrom (4) the triangles \\(APC\\) and \\(APD\\) are congruent right triangles (right by (5)) reflected in the line \\(AP\\). Hence the reflection of the line \\(PN\\) in \\(AP\\) coincides with the line \\(PD\\). Therefore the angle between \\(PN\\) and \\(MN\\) equals the angle between \\(PD\\) and \\(CD\\), i.e.\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle PNM = \\angle CDE}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (11) into (10) yields\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle(\\ell ,CD)=\\angle CDE}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 8.  Similarity obtained from the inversion\n\nRecall that under the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) the points \\(C',D',E',F'\\) lie on the circle \\(\\gamma\\) and the lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) intersect \\(\\gamma\\) in the pairs \\(\\{C',E'\\}\\) and \\(\\{D',F'\\}\\). The harmonic relation (2) implies that the quadrilateral \\(C'D'E'F'\\) is harmonic on \\(\\gamma\\); a standard property of a harmonic quadrilateral inscribed in a circle is that the triangles formed by one side and the two opposite vertices are similar. Hence\n\\[\n\\triangle CDE \\sim \\triangle CEA . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nFrom (13) we obtain the angle equality\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle CDE = \\angle CEA}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nInsert (14) into (12):\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle(\\ell ,CD)=\\angle CEA}. \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 9.  From \\(CD\\) to the chord \\(BE\\)\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\perp CD\\) (5), the acute angle that \\(\\ell\\) makes with any line equals the complement of the angle that \\(CD\\) makes with that line. Applying this to the chord \\(BE\\) of the circle \\(\\omega\\) (which contains \\(A,B,E,F\\) by (3)) gives\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle(\\ell ,BE)=\\angle CEA}. \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 10.  Tangent\u2013chord theorem\n\nIn the cyclic quadrilateral \\(A,B,E,F\\) (3) the tangent\u2011chord theorem states that the angle between the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(E\\) and the chord \\(BE\\) equals the angle subtended by \\(BE\\) at the opposite point of the circle, i.e. \\(\\angle BFE\\).\n\nFrom the similarity (13) we have \\(\\angle CEA = \\angle DFA\\). The line \\(DF\\) is the image under the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) of the line \\(AE\\); consequently \\(DF\\) is precisely the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(E\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\angle DFA = \\angle BFE . \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nCombining (16) and (17) we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nEquality of the angle formed by a line with a chord and the angle subtended by that chord at the opposite point of the circle is exactly the tangent\u2011chord condition. Hence the line \\(\\ell\\) (the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(E\\).\n\n\u220e"
