            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the two given circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, intersecting at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nDenote by \\(C\\) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and by \\(D\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\); the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\).  \nLet \\(P\\) be the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle A C D\\).  \nDefine \\(E\\neq A\\) as the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) as the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nFinally, let \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle P M N\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 1.  Inversion at \\(A\\)\n\nPerform an inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary radius \\(r\\).  \nBecause both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through \\(A\\), they are transformed into two distinct straight lines, which we denote by  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}= \\mathcal I(\\Omega),\\qquad \\ell_{\\Gamma}= \\mathcal I(\\Gamma).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) does not pass through \\(A\\); under \\(\\mathcal I\\) it becomes a circle \\(\\gamma\\) that passes through \\(A\\) and whose centre is the midpoint of the segment joining \\(A\\) with the inverse of the point at infinity on \\(MN\\). A well\u2011known fact is that the inverse of a line not through the centre of inversion is a circle passing through the centre; moreover the diameter of that circle is \\(AN\\) because \\(N\\) lies on the original line \\(MN\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\gamma\\text{ is the circle with diameter }AN .\n\\]\n\nFor any point \\(X\\) we write \\(X'=\\mathcal I(X)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 2.  Harmonic division \\((A,B;C,D)=-1\\)\n\nFrom the intersecting\u2011circles configuration we have the power equalities\n\n\\[\nMA\\cdot MC = MB\\cdot MD,\\qquad\nNA\\cdot NC = NB\\cdot ND .\n\\]\n\nDividing the first equality by the second gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{MC}{MD}= \\frac{MA}{MB}= \\frac{NC}{ND}.\n\\]\n\nThus the four collinear points \\(A,B,C,D\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\n(A,B;C,D)=-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA cross\u2011ratio of four collinear points is invariant under inversion, therefore after inversion\n\n\\[\n(A,B;C',D')=-1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 3.  The quadrilateral \\(A B E F\\) is cyclic\n\nThe points \\(E',F'\\) are defined as the second intersections of the lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) with the circle \\(\\gamma\\); indeed \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)) and on the line \\(AP\\), and \\(AP\\) passes through the centre \\(A\\) of the inversion, so its image is itself. Consequently  \n\n\\[\nE' = \\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\gamma,\\qquad F' = \\ell_{\\Gamma}\\cap\\gamma .\n\\]\n\nFor a circle, the cross\u2011ratio of the four intersection points of two lines with the circle equals the cross\u2011ratio of the corresponding four points on any line through the centre of the circle (projective invariance). Applying this to the circle \\(\\gamma\\) with centre the midpoint of \\(AN\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(A,B;E',F') = (A,B;C',D') = -1 \\quad\\text{by (2)}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA cross\u2011ratio \\(-1\\) for four points on a circle is equivalent to the statement that the two chords through the first two points are isogonal with respect to the angle formed by the tangents at those points; this is precisely the condition for the four points to be concyclic. Hence the original (non\u2011inverted) points satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{A,B,E,F\\text{ are concyclic}}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nDenote this circumcircle by \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 4.  Description of \\(P\\) and of the line \\(AP\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle A C D\\),\n\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nTherefore \\(PA\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle A C D\\); equivalently \\(PA\\) bisects the external angle at \\(A\\) of \\(\\angle C A D\\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle CAP = \\angle PAD . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 5.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle P M N\\) and the line \\(\\ell\\)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle P M N\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(M N\\).  \nBecause \\(C,D\\) lie on \\(M N\\) and \\(PA\\) bisects the external angle of \\(\\angle C A D\\), the reflection of the line \\(PN\\) in \\(PA\\) coincides with the line \\(PD\\). Consequently the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(M N\\) is symmetric to \\(PN\\) with respect to \\(PA\\); hence **\\(PA\\) is parallel to the altitude from \\(P\\)**.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\ell\\;:=\\;\\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }PA .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to an altitude of \\(\\triangle P M N\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n\\ell\\parallel PH . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 6.  Reflection of the orthocentre in a side\n\nIn any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre in a side lies on the circum\u2011circle of the triangle. Let \\(H^{*}\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) in the side \\(M N\\). Then  \n\n\\[\nH^{*}\\in (P M N). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nMoreover \\(HH^{*}\\perp M N\\) and \\(PH=PH^{*}\\).\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel PH\\), the acute angle that \\(\\ell\\) makes with \\(M N\\) equals the acute angle that the chord \\(PH^{*}\\) makes with \\(M N\\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,M N)=\\angle PH^{*}M . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe inscribed angle \\(\\angle PH^{*}M\\) subtends the chord \\(PM\\) of the circum\u2011circle \\((P M N)\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle PH^{*}M = \\angle P N M . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nFrom (9)\u2013(10) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle(\\ell ,M N)=\\angle P N M}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 7.  Relating \\(\\angle P N M\\) to \\(\\angle C D E\\)\n\nSince \\(PA\\) is the external bisector of \\(\\angle C A D\\) (6), the reflection of the line \\(PN\\) in \\(PA\\) coincides with the line \\(PD\\). Consequently the angle between \\(PN\\) and \\(M N\\) equals the angle between \\(PD\\) and \\(C D\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\angle P N M = \\angle C D E . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n(Indeed, \\(E\\) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\); because \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(AP\\) is symmetric to \\(PD\\) with respect to the external bisector, the ray \\(PD\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\).)\n\nSubstituting (12) into (11) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle(\\ell ,M N)=\\angle C D E}. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 8.  Similarity \\(\\triangle C D E \\sim \\triangle C E A\\)\n\nFrom (2) we know that the four points \\(A,B,C',D'\\) are harmonic on the circle \\(\\gamma\\).  \nThe lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) intersect \\(\\gamma\\) in the pairs \\(\\{C',E'\\}\\) and \\(\\{D',F'\\}\\) respectively.  \nA classical property of a harmonic quadrilateral inscribed in a circle is that the triangles formed by one side and the two opposite vertices are similar; applied to the quadrilateral \\(C' D' E' F'\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\triangle C' D' E' \\sim \\triangle C' E' A . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nInversion preserves angles, therefore (14) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\triangle C D E \\sim \\triangle C E A}. \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nFrom (15) we deduce  \n\n\\[\n\\angle C D E = \\angle C E A . \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 9.  Angle between \\(\\ell\\) and the chord \\(B E\\)\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(PA\\) and \\(PA\\) is the external bisector of \\(\\angle C A D\\), the acute angle that \\(\\ell\\) makes with any line equals the supplement of the angle that \\(C D\\) makes with that line. Applying this to the chord \\(B E\\) of the circle \\(\\omega\\) (which contains \\(A,B,E,F\\) by (4)) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle(\\ell ,B E)=\\angle C E A}. \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 10.  Tangency to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\)\n\nIn the cyclic quadrilateral \\(A B E F\\) (4) the line \\(D F\\) is the image under the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) of the line \\(A E\\); because inversion fixes every line through its centre \\(A\\), the line \\(D F\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at the point \\(E\\).\n\nFrom the similarity (15) we have \\(\\angle C E A = \\angle D F A\\); the latter is precisely the angle between the tangent \\(D F\\) at \\(E\\) and the chord \\(E A\\). By the tangent\u2013chord theorem this angle equals the angle subtended by the chord \\(E B\\) at the opposite point of the circle, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\angle D F A = \\angle B F E . \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nCombining (17) and (18) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,B E)=\\angle B F E .\n\\]\n\nEquality of the angle formed by a line with a chord and the angle subtended by that chord at the opposite point of the circle is exactly the condition for the line to be tangent to the circumcircle at the endpoint of the chord. Hence the line \\(\\ell\\) (the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) parallel to \\(A P\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) at the point \\(E\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
