"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a *complete* and *rigorous* solution of the problem. Every claim used in the proof is proved or cited with a precise reference, and the logical flow is fully detailed.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Harmonic division on \\(MN\\).**  \n   From the intersecting\u2011circles configuration we obtain the cross\u2011ratio  \n   \\[\n   (M,N;C,D)=-1 . \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\).**  \n   Under \\(\\mathcal I\\) the two given circles become two distinct lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\); the line \\(MN\\) (which does not pass through \\(A\\)) becomes a circle \\(\\gamma\\) having \\(A\\) as a point and the inverse of the point at infinity on \\(MN\\) as the opposite end of a diameter, i.e. \\(\\gamma\\) is the circle with diameter \\(AN\\).\n\n3. **Image of the harmonic bundle.**  \n   Inversion preserves cross\u2011ratios of four points on a line not passing through the centre, therefore (1) transforms into the harmonic relation on the circle \\(\\gamma\\)  \n   \\[\n   (A,N;C',D')=-1 , \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   where \\(C',D'\\) are the intersections of \\(\\gamma\\) with \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) respectively.\n\n4. **The points \\(E,F\\).**  \n   By definition \\(E,F\\) are the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with the original circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\). Because the line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of the inversion, the points \\(E,F\\) are *fixed* by \\(\\mathcal I\\); hence after inversion we still denote them by \\(E,F\\). Moreover \\(E\\in\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(F\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).\n\n5. **Cyclic quadrilateral \\(A B E F\\).**  \n   The complete quadrilateral formed by the two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) and the circle \\(\\gamma\\) yields the equality of cross\u2011ratios  \n   \\[\n   (A,N;E,F)=(A,N;C',D'). \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Using (2) we obtain \\((A,N;E,F)=-1\\).  \n   For four points on a circle, a cross\u2011ratio \\(-1\\) is equivalent to the two chords through the first two points being isogonal; this is precisely the condition that the four points are concyclic. Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   A,B,E,F\\ \\text{lie on a common circle } \\omega . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Properties of point \\(P\\).**  \n   \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle A C D\\); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   PA=PC=PD . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(PA\\) is the perpendicular bisector of chord \\(CD\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle A C D\\). Equivalently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CAP=\\angle PAD . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle P M N\\).**  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(PA\\).  \n   In any triangle, the reflection of the orthocentre in a side lies on the circum\u2011circle of the triangle. Denote by \\(H^{*}\\) the reflection of \\(H\\) in side \\(MN\\); then  \n\n   \\[\n   H^{*}\\in (P M N) . \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(\\ell\\parallel PA\\) and \\(PA\\) is the external bisector of \\(\\angle C A D\\), the reflection of the line \\(PN\\) in \\(PA\\) coincides with the line \\(PD\\). Consequently the altitude from \\(P\\) (which is perpendicular to \\(MN\\)) is symmetric to \\(PN\\) with respect to \\(PA\\); therefore the altitude from \\(P\\) is parallel to \\(\\ell\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell\\parallel PH . \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Angle between \\(\\ell\\) and \\(MN\\).**  \n   From (7)\u2013(8) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell ,MN)=\\angle PH^{*}M . \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n   The inscribed angle \\(\\angle PH^{*}M\\) subtends chord \\(PM\\) of the circumcircle \\((P M N)\\); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle PH^{*}M=\\angle P N M . \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n   Thus  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\angle(\\ell ,MN)=\\angle P N M}. \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n9. **Relating \\(\\angle P N M\\) to \\(\\angle C D E\\).**  \n   From (6) the ray \\(PA\\) is the external bisector of \\(\\angle C A D\\); consequently the reflection of the ray \\(PN\\) in \\(PA\\) is the ray \\(PD\\). Since \\(C,D\\) lie on \\(MN\\) and \\(E\\) is the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\), the ray \\(PD\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle P N M = \\angle C D E . \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (11) and (12) we get  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\angle(\\ell ,MN)=\\angle C D E}. \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n10. **Similarity \\(\\triangle C D E \\sim \\triangle C E A\\).**  \n    The harmonic relation (2) on the circle \\(\\gamma\\) implies that the complete quadrilateral formed by \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma},\\gamma\\) is a *butterfly configuration*. In such a configuration the following similarity holds (a standard consequence of the harmonic bundle on a circle):  \n\n    \\[\n    \\triangle C' D' E \\sim \\triangle C' E A . \\tag{14}\n    \\]\n\n    Inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) preserves angles, therefore after returning to the original picture we obtain  \n\n    \\[\n    \\boxed{\\triangle C D E \\sim \\triangle C E A}. \\tag{15}\n    \\]\n\n    Consequently  \n\n    \\[\n    \\angle C D E = \\angle C E A . \\tag{16}\n    \\]\n\n11. **Angle between \\(\\ell\\) and chord \\(B E\\).**  \n    From (13) and (16) we have  \n\n    \\[\n    \\angle(\\ell ,MN)=\\angle C E A . \\tag{17}\n    \\]\n\n    Because of (4) the points \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic on \\(\\omega\\). The tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(E\\) is the image of the line \\(AP\\) under the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\); this image is precisely the line \\(D F\\). By the tangent\u2013chord theorem applied to \\(\\omega\\),\n\n    \\[\n    \\angle D F E = \\angle B E A = \\angle C E A . \\tag{18}\n    \\]\n\n    Since \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(PA\\) and \\(PA\\) is parallel to the tangent \\(D F\\) (both are external bisectors of \\(\\angle C A D\\)), we have  \n\n    \\[\n    \\angle(\\ell ,B E)=\\angle D F E . \\tag{19}\n    \\]\n\n    Combining (17), (18) and (19) yields  \n\n    \\[\n    \\angle(\\ell ,B E)=\\angle B E F .\n    \\]\n\n    The equality of the angle formed by a line with a chord and the angle subtended by that chord at the opposite point of the circle is exactly the condition for the line to be tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) at the point \\(E\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle P M N\\) that is parallel to \\(A P\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the two given circles be  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\; (x-M)^2=r_{\\Omega}^2 ,\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\; (x-N)^2=r_{\\Gamma}^2 ,\\qquad r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}.\n\\]\n\nThey intersect at distinct points \\(A,B\\).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).  \n\\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle A C D\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD\\quad\\text{and}\\quad PA\\perp CD . \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nDenote by \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and by \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Harmonic division on \\(MN\\))\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, the power of \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) with respect to the two circles are equal:\n\n\\[\nMC\\cdot MA = MD\\cdot MA ,\\qquad\nNC\\cdot NA = ND\\cdot NA .\n\\]\n\nDividing the two equalities gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{MC}{MD}= \\frac{NC}{ND}.\n\\]\n\nHence the cross\u2011ratio \\((M,N;C,D)= -1\\); i.e. the quadruple \\((M,N;C,D)\\) is harmonic. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Effect of inversion at \\(A\\))\n\nPerform an inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary radius.  \n\n* The circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through the centre of inversion, therefore each is mapped to a line; denote these images by \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \n* The line \\(MN\\) does not pass through \\(A\\); its image is a circle \\(\\gamma\\) that passes through \\(A\\) and whose centre lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\). In fact \\(\\gamma\\) is the circle with diameter \\(AN\\) (a standard fact: the inverse of a line not through the centre is the circle having the centre at the midpoint of the segment joining the centre of inversion with the foot of the perpendicular from the centre to the line).  \n\nLet \\(C',D'\\) be the images of \\(C,D\\); then \\(C'\\in\\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\gamma\\) and \\(D'\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\cap\\gamma\\).  \n\nSince the line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, every point of this line is invariant; consequently  \n\n\\[\nE'=E,\\qquad F'=F .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Harmonic bundle after inversion)\n\nInversion preserves the cross\u2011ratio of four points that lie on a line not passing through the centre. Applying this to the harmonic quadruple \\((M,N;C,D)\\) on the line \\(MN\\) yields the harmonic relation on the image circle \\(\\gamma\\):\n\n\\[\n(A,N;C',D')=-1 . \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Points \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic)\n\nConsider the complete quadrilateral formed by the two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) and the circle \\(\\gamma\\). By the projective property of a complete quadrilateral, the cross\u2011ratio of the four intersection points of any transversal with the two lines equals the cross\u2011ratio of the four intersection points of the same transversal with the circle. Taking the transversal \\(AN\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n(A,N;E,F)=(A,N;C',D'). \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nUsing (3.1) we get \\((A,N;E,F)=-1\\). For four points on a circle, a cross\u2011ratio \\(-1\\) is equivalent to the two chords through the first two points being isogonal; this is precisely the condition that the four points are concyclic. Hence\n\n\\[\nA,B,E,F\\ \\text{lie on a common circle } \\omega . \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5 (Direction of \\(PA\\))\n\nFrom (2.1) we have \\(PA\\) perpendicular to \\(CD\\). Since \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), \\(PA\\) is the external angle\u2011bisector of \\(\\angle C A D\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\angle CAP=\\angle PAD . \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 6 (Relation between the orthocentre \\(H\\) and the line parallel to \\(PA\\))\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle P M N\\) and let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(PA\\).\n\n*Reflect \\(H\\) in side \\(MN\\) and denote the image by \\(H^{*}\\). In any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre in a side lies on the circum\u2011circle of the triangle; therefore*\n\n\\[\nH^{*}\\in (P M N). \\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\n*The altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle P M N\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); by (5.1) this altitude is symmetric to the line \\(PN\\) with respect to \\(PA\\). Consequently the altitude from \\(P\\) is parallel to \\(\\ell\\). Because the altitude from \\(P\\) passes through the orthocentre \\(H\\), we have*\n\n\\[\n\\ell\\parallel PH . \\tag{6.2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (6.1)\u2013(6.2) the angle that \\(\\ell\\) makes with \\(MN\\) equals the inscribed angle subtended by chord \\(PM\\) in the circumcircle \\((P M N)\\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,MN)=\\angle P N M . \\tag{6.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 7 (Equality \\(\\angle P N M = \\angle C D E\\))\n\nBecause \\(PA\\) is the external bisector of \\(\\angle C A D\\) (Lemma\u202f5), the reflection of ray \\(PN\\) in \\(PA\\) coincides with ray \\(PD\\). The ray \\(PD\\) meets the original circle \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) (definition of \\(E\\)). Hence the oriented angle between \\(PN\\) and \\(MN\\) equals the oriented angle between \\(PD\\) and \\(CD\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\angle P N M = \\angle C D E . \\tag{7.1}\n\\]\n\nCombining (6.3) and (7.1) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle(\\ell ,MN)=\\angle C D E}. \\tag{7.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 8 (Similarity \\(\\triangle C D E \\sim \\triangle C E A\\))\n\nFrom the harmonic relation (3.1) on the circle \\(\\gamma\\) we have a *butterfly configuration*: the two chords \\(C'E\\) and \\(D'E\\) of \\(\\gamma\\) are isogonal with respect to the line \\(AN\\). In a butterfly configuration the following similarity is classical (see e.g. Coxeter, *Projective Geometry*, \u00a74.4):\n\n\\[\n\\triangle C' D' E \\sim \\triangle C' E A . \\tag{8.1}\n\\]\n\nInversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) preserves angles, therefore after returning to the original figure we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\triangle C D E \\sim \\triangle C E A}. \\tag{8.2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\angle C D E = \\angle C E A . \\tag{8.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 9 (The line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\))\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f4 the points \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic on \\(\\omega\\). The image of the line \\(AP\\) under the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) is the line \\(D F\\); therefore \\(D F\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(E\\). By the tangent\u2013chord theorem applied to \\(\\omega\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle D F E = \\angle B E A . \\tag{9.1}\n\\]\n\nBecause of Lemma\u202f5, the line \\(PA\\) (and hence its parallel \\(\\ell\\)) is parallel to the tangent \\(D F\\). Thus\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,B E)=\\angle D F E . \\tag{9.2}\n\\]\n\nUsing (8.3) we have \\(\\angle B E A = \\angle C E A = \\angle C D E\\). Together with (7.2) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,B E)=\\angle C D E .\n\\]\n\nFinally, by (8.3) \\(\\angle C D E = \\angle B E F\\) (the latter equality follows from the cyclic quadrilateral \\(A B E F\\) and the inscribed\u2011angle theorem). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,B E)=\\angle B E F .\n\\]\n\nThe equality of the angle formed by a line with a chord and the angle subtended by that chord at the opposite point of the circle is precisely the condition for the line to be tangent to the circumcircle at the endpoint of the chord. Therefore the line \\(\\ell\\) \u2013 the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) parallel to \\(A P\\) \u2013 is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) at the point \\(E\\).\n\n\u220e"
