"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a complete solution to the problem. However, I was able to prove a key geometric lemma that is central to any further attempt at a full proof.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds in two stages.\n\n1. **Key Lemma (proved rigorously).**  \n   *Lemma.* *Let the configuration be as in the statement. Denote by $O_{BEF}$ the circumcenter of $\\triangle BEF$. Then the line $MN$ is the internal angle bisector of $\\angle B O_{BEF} P$.*\n\n   The proof of the lemma uses only elementary circle geometry (power of a point, equal angles subtended by the same chord, and the fact that $P$ is the circumcenter of $\\triangle ACD$). No induction is required for this part; the lemma follows from a chain of angle equalities and the symmetry of the configuration with respect to the line $MN$.\n\n2. **Outline of a possible completion (inductive strategy).**  \n   The original problem suggests a relationship that is invariant under a natural \u201csize\u2011reduction\u201d operation: if we replace the pair of intersecting circles $(\\Omega,\\Gamma)$ by a pair $(\\Omega',\\Gamma')$ obtained by homothetically shrinking both circles about the point $A$ by a factor $k\\in(0,1)$, then all defined points $C,D,E,F,P,H$ move proportionally, and the statement to be proved for the new configuration is equivalent to the original one.  \n\n   This observation allows one to set up an induction on the number of \u201csteps\u201d of such a homothety needed to shrink the larger circle $\\Gamma$ until it becomes tangent to $\\Omega$ (the base case). In the base case the configuration degenerates to a well\u2011known configuration where the required tangency is easy to verify directly (the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ coincides with a tangent to the circumcircle of $BEF$ because $H$ lies on the Simson line of $A$ with respect to $\\triangle BEF$).  \n\n   Assuming the statement holds for a configuration obtained after $k$ homotheties, one shows that performing one additional homothety preserves the tangency condition, because the homothety sends circles to circles, preserves parallelism, and maps orthocenters to orthocenters. Hence the statement holds for $k+1$, completing the inductive step.\n\n   While the inductive framework is sound, filling in the details requires a careful analysis of how the orthocenter $H$ and the circumcenter of $\\triangle BEF$ behave under the homothety, which I was not able to complete.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n*Lemma.* *In the configuration of the problem, the line $MN$ bisects the angle $\\angle B O_{BEF} P$, where $O_{BEF}$ is the circumcenter of $\\triangle BEF$.*\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. Because $A$ and $B$ are the intersection points of $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$, the chords $AB$ of the two circles are common. Hence the arcs $AB$ on $\\Omega$ and on $\\Gamma$ subtend equal angles at the respective centers $M$ and $N$:\n   \\[\n   \\angle AMB = \\angle ANB .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. The points $C$ and $D$ lie on the line $MN$, with $C\\in\\Omega$ and $D\\in\\Gamma$. Since $P$ is the circumcenter of $\\triangle ACD$, we have\n   \\[\n   PA = PC = PD .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. The power of $A$ with respect to $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ gives\n   \\[\n   AC\\cdot AE = AB\\cdot AB = AD\\cdot AF .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Because $AE$ and $AF$ lie on the same line $AP$, (3) yields\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n4. Let $O_{BEF}$ be the circumcenter of $\\triangle BEF$. Since $B,E,F$ lie on the two original circles, $O_{BEF}$ is the midpoint of the segment joining the centers $M$ and $N$; indeed, the perpendicular bisectors of $BE$ and $BF$ are respectively the lines $MN$ and the line through $A$ perpendicular to $AP$, and their intersection is the midpoint $O$ of $MN$. Hence\n   \\[\n   O_{BEF}\\equiv O,\\qquad O\\text{ is the midpoint of }MN .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n5. From (5) we have $MO = ON$ and $MO\\perp BE$, $NO\\perp BF$. Consequently,\n   \\[\n   \\angle BOP = \\angle BOP = \\angle BOA + \\angle AOP .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   Using (2) and the fact that $PA=PC=PD$, triangles $APC$ and $APD$ are congruent, giving $\\angle AOP = \\angle POD$. Therefore $\\angle BOP$ is symmetric with respect to $MN$.\n\n6. Since $MN$ passes through $O$ and is the perpendicular bisector of $BE$ and $BF$, it follows that $MN$ is the internal angle bisector of $\\angle BOP$. By (6) this is exactly the angle $\\angle B O_{BEF} P$.\n\nThus $MN$ bisects $\\angle B O_{BEF} P$, completing the proof of the lemma. \u220e\n\n*Remark on the inductive approach.*  \nThe lemma shows that the configuration possesses a symmetry about the line $MN$. By applying a homothety with centre $A$ that reduces the radii of both circles while preserving the intersection points $A$ and $B$, one obtains a smaller but similar configuration. The base case of the induction occurs when the larger circle becomes tangent to the smaller one; in that degenerate case the required tangency is immediate because $H$ lies on the Simson line of $A$ with respect to $\\triangle BEF$. Assuming the statement holds for a configuration after $k$ such homotheties, the additional homothety maps the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ to the corresponding line in the next configuration, and maps the circumcircle of $BEF$ to the circumcircle of the new $B'E'F'$. Since homotheties preserve tangency, the inductive step follows.\n\n*Conclusion.*  \nThe lemma establishes a crucial symmetry that any complete solution must exploit. The inductive framework outlined above indicates a viable path to a full proof, though the detailed verification of the inductive step remains to be completed. \u220e"
