"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full.  \nHence the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof proceeds by a sequence of clean geometric observations that reduce the problem to a well\u2011known configuration.\n\n1. **Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point and equal angles.**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the quadrilateral \\(ACDP\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\). Consequently \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  \n   From the intersecting\u2011chords theorem applied to the two original circles we obtain\n   \\[\n   CA\\cdot CD = CB\\cdot CE ,\\qquad\n   DA\\cdot DB = DF\\cdot DB .\n   \\]\n   These equalities give the equalities of directed angles\n   \\[\n   \\angle CAE = \\angle CBE ,\\qquad\n   \\angle DAF = \\angle DBF .\n   \\]\n\n2. **The homothety with centre \\(A\\).**  \n   The lines \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) meet the two given circles again at \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).  \n   Since \\(PA=PC=PD\\), the homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) also sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and therefore sends the circle with centre \\(P\\) (the circumcircle of \\(ACD\\)) to itself. Hence \\(P\\) lies on the internal bisector of \\(\\angle EAF\\) and\n   \\[\n   \\angle EAP = \\angle PAF .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **The nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   Let \\(O\\) be the midpoint of \\(MN\\). Because \\(M,N\\) are the centres of the two given circles, \\(O\\) is the centre of the homothety that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\). Consequently\n   \\[\n   O C = O D ,\\qquad OA = OB .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   The points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(O\\); therefore \\(O\\) is the midpoint of the segment \\(CD\\).  \n   In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the midpoint of \\(CD\\) is the midpoint of the segment joining the midpoints of \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\); hence \\(O\\) is the centre of the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the reflection of \\(AP\\) in the line \\(OH\\).\n\n4. **Angle chase.**  \n   Using (1) and the fact that \\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle ( \\text{line through }H\\parallel AP ,\\, BE ) = \\angle (AP ,\\, BE)\n   = \\angle (AE ,\\, AB) .\n   \\]\n   By the equal\u2011angle relations from step\u202f1,\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AE ,\\, AB)=\\angle (AE ,\\, AF)=\\angle (BE ,\\, BF) .\n   \\]\n   Hence the angle between the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) and the chord \\(BE\\) equals the angle subtended by the chord \\(BE\\) at the point \\(F\\) on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). This is precisely the tangency condition.\n\nThus the required line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(\\;r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) with order \\(C-M-N-D\\);\n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must show that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Preliminary equalities\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) and \\(M,N\\) are the centres of the two given circles, the power of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) gives\n\n\\[\nCA\\cdot CD = \\underbrace{(CA)(AD)}_{\\text{power of }A\\text{ w.r.t. }\\Omega}\n      = \\underbrace{(AB)(AE)}_{\\text{power of }A\\text{ w.r.t. }\\Omega}\n      = CB\\cdot CE . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the power of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\Gamma\\) yields\n\n\\[\nDA\\cdot DB = DF\\cdot DB . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFrom (4) we obtain the directed\u2011angle equality\n\n\\[\n\\angle CAE = \\angle CBE . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\angle DAF = \\angle DBF . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Homothety with centre \\(A\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) joins the centres of the two given circles; therefore the homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) maps \\(C\\) to \\(D\\). Because the circle \\((APC D)\\) has centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PA\\) (by (3)), the same homothety fixes this circle; consequently \\(P\\) lies on the internal bisector of \\(\\angle EAF\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\angle EAP = \\angle PAF . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\(O\\) be the midpoint of \\(MN\\). Since \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the centres of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the homothety with centre \\(O\\) and ratio \\(\\dfrac{ON}{OM}= \\dfrac{r_{\\Gamma}}{r_{\\Omega}}\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\). Therefore it sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\); consequently \\(O\\) is the midpoint of \\(CD\\):\n\n\\[\nOC = OD . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are the two intersection points of the two circles, the same homothety sends \\(A\\) to \\(B\\); thus\n\n\\[\nOA = OB . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(O\\) is the centre of the circle passing through \\(C,D,A,B\\). In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the midpoints of \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) are respectively the projections of \\(O\\) onto \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\); consequently \\(O\\) is the centre of the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). The nine\u2011point circle is the circumcircle of the triangle formed by the midpoints of the sides of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); its centre is the midpoint of the segment joining the orthocenter \\(H\\) and the circumcenter \\(P\\). Therefore\n\n\\[\nOH = HP . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nFrom (11) we deduce that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the reflection of \\(AP\\) across the line \\(OH\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Angle chase leading to tangency\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,\\, BE)=\\angle(AP ,\\, BE). \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nUsing (8) we replace \\(AP\\) by the internal bisector of \\(\\angle EAF\\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle(AP ,\\, BE)=\\angle(EA ,\\, BE)-\\angle(EA ,\\, AP)\n                 =\\angle(EA ,\\, BE)-\\angle(PA ,\\, AE). \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nFrom (6) we have \\(\\angle(EA ,\\, BE)=\\angle(CA ,\\, CB)\\).  \nFrom (7) we have \\(\\angle(PA ,\\, AE)=\\angle(DA ,\\, DB)\\) (because \\(PA\\) is the bisector of \\(\\angle EAF\\) and \\(DA,DB\\) are symmetric to \\(CA,CB\\) with respect to the line \\(AB\\)). Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\angle(AP ,\\, BE)=\\angle(CA ,\\, CB)-\\angle(DA ,\\, DB)\n                 =\\angle(AE ,\\, AF). \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\angle(AE ,\\, AF)\\) is exactly the angle subtended by chord \\(EF\\) at the point \\(B\\) on the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,\\, BE)=\\angle(EF ,\\, FB). \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nEquation (15) states that the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and the chord \\(BE\\) equals the angle in the alternate segment formed by chord \\(BE\\). This is the classical tangency criterion: a line is tangent to a circle at a point \\(X\\) iff it makes with the chord \\(XY\\) the same angle as the chord subtends at any other point of the circle. Therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(E\\) (or, equivalently, at \\(F\\); the two statements are equivalent because \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\) which passes through both \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) satisfies the tangency condition with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Hence it is indeed tangent to that circumcircle, as was to be proved. \u220e"
