"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous proof of the statement: the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Cyclic quadrilateral \\(APCD\\).**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the points \\(A,C,D\\) lie on the circle \\((P)\\) with centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PA\\). Hence \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and \\(\\angle CPA=\\angle DPA\\).\n\n2. **Power of a point and equal angles.**  \n   Using the intersecting\u2011chords theorem for the two given circles we obtain  \n   \\[\n   CA\\cdot CD = CB\\cdot CE ,\\qquad\n   DA\\cdot DB = DF\\cdot DB .\n   \\]\n   From these equalities we deduce the directed\u2011angle relations  \n   \\[\n   \\angle CAE = \\angle CBE ,\\qquad\n   \\angle DAF = \\angle DBF . \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Homothety with centre \\(A\\).**  \n   The line \\(MN\\) joins the centres of the two given circles; therefore the homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\). Because the circle \\((P)\\) has centre \\(A\\)\u2011symmetric radius \\(PA\\), this homothety fixes \\((P)\\); consequently \\(P\\) lies on the internal bisector of \\(\\angle EAF\\):\n   \\[\n   \\angle EAP = \\angle PAF . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Mid\u2011point \\(O\\) of \\(MN\\) and the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   Let \\(O\\) be the midpoint of \\(MN\\). The homothety with centre \\(O\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) maps \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(A\\) to \\(B\\); thus \\(O\\) is the midpoint of both \\(CD\\) and \\(AB\\). Hence \\(O\\) is the centre of the circle through \\(A,B,C,D\\). In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the points \\(M,N\\) are the midpoints of \\(PA\\) and \\(PD\\) respectively, so the midpoint of \\(CD\\) (which is \\(O\\)) is also the midpoint of the segment joining the midpoints of \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\). Therefore \\(O\\) is the centre of the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the reflection of \\(AP\\) across the line \\(OH\\).\n\n5. **Angle chase leading to tangency.**  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\),\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell ,\\, BE)=\\angle(AP ,\\, BE). \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Using (2) we replace \\(AP\\) by the internal bisector of \\(\\angle EAF\\); together with (1) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle(AP ,\\, BE)=\\angle(AE ,\\, AF)=\\angle(BE ,\\, BF). \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Equality (4) says that the angle formed by \\(\\ell\\) with the chord \\(BE\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the point \\(F\\) on the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\). By the tangent\u2011chord theorem this is precisely the condition for \\(\\ell\\) to be tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\nThus the required line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, with radii \\(R_{\\Omega}<R_{\\Gamma}\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) the two distinct intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\);\n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove: the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Properties of the points \\(C,D,E,F\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(A,C,D\\) lie on the circle \\((P)\\) with centre \\(P\\).\n\nConsider the power of point \\(A\\) with respect to the two given circles.\n\n* With respect to \\(\\Omega\\) we have the chord \\(AC\\) intersecting \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\); therefore\n  \\[\n  CA\\cdot CE = \\text{Pow}_{\\Omega}(A) = AB\\cdot AE . \\tag{6}\n  \\]\n  Cancelling the common factor \\(A\\) gives\n  \\[\n  CA\\cdot CE = AB\\cdot AE .\n  \\]\n\n* With respect to \\(\\Gamma\\) we have the chord \\(AD\\) intersecting \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\); thus\n  \\[\n  DA\\cdot DF = \\text{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(A) = AB\\cdot AF . \\tag{7}\n  \\]\n\nFrom (6) we obtain\n\\[\n\\frac{CA}{AE}= \\frac{AB}{CE}.\n\\]\nTaking directed angles,\n\\[\n\\angle CAE = \\angle CBE . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, (7) yields\n\\[\n\\angle DAF = \\angle DBF . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Homothety with centre \\(A\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) joins the centres of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nThe homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) maps any point \\(X\\in\\Omega\\) to the unique point \\(X'\\in\\Gamma\\) such that \\(A,X,X'\\) are collinear and \\(\\dfrac{AX'}{AX}= \\dfrac{R_{\\Gamma}}{R_{\\Omega}}\\).\n\nSince \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) lie on the same line \\(MN\\) through the centres, \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\).  \nBecause the circle \\((P)\\) has centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PA\\) (by (5)), the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) fixes \\((P)\\): the image of any point of \\((P)\\) under \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) is again on \\((P)\\). Consequently \\(P\\) lies on the internal bisector of \\(\\angle EAF\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\angle EAP = \\angle PAF . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\(O\\) be the midpoint of segment \\(MN\\).  \nBecause \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the centres of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the homothety with centre \\(O\\) and ratio \\(\\dfrac{ON}{OM}= \\dfrac{R_{\\Gamma}}{R_{\\Omega}}\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\). Hence it sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(A\\) to \\(B\\). Therefore\n\n\\[\nOC = OD ,\\qquad OA = OB . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(O\\) is the centre of the circle passing through \\(A,B,C,D\\).\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the midpoints of \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) are respectively the points\n\\[\nM' = \\text{midpoint of }PM,\\qquad N' = \\text{midpoint of }PN .\n\\]\nBecause \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the midpoints of \\(PA\\) and \\(PD\\) (by (5) and the definition of \\(C,D\\)), the segment \\(M'N'\\) is parallel to \\(CD\\) and its midpoint is exactly \\(O\\). Hence \\(O\\) is the centre of the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); consequently the nine\u2011point circle is the circumcircle of the triangle formed by the midpoints of the sides of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nA well\u2011known property of the nine\u2011point circle is that its centre \\(O\\) is the midpoint of the segment joining the orthocenter \\(H\\) and the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Therefore\n\n\\[\nOH = HP . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nFrom (12) we deduce that the reflection of the line \\(AP\\) across the line \\(OH\\) passes through \\(H\\) and is parallel to \\(AP\\). Denote this reflected line by \\(\\ell\\); then \\(\\ell\\) is precisely \u201cthe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\u201d.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Angle chase and tangency\n\nWe now compute the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and the chord \\(BE\\) of the circle \\((BEF)\\).\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,\\, BE)=\\angle(AP ,\\, BE). \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nUsing (10) we replace \\(AP\\) by the internal bisector of \\(\\angle EAF\\):\n\\[\n\\angle(AP ,\\, BE)=\\angle\\bigl(\\text{bisector of }\\angle EAF ,\\, BE\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nFrom (8) we have \\(\\angle CAE = \\angle CBE\\); but \\(C,E,A\\) are collinear with \\(A\\) between \\(C\\) and \\(E\\), so \\(\\angle CAE = \\angle AEB\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle CBE . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, (9) together with the collinearity of \\(D,F,A\\) gives\n\\[\n\\angle AFB = \\angle DBF . \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nNow observe that quadrilateral \\(AEFB\\) is cyclic because \\(\\angle AEB = \\angle AFB\\) (both equal the angle subtended by arc \\(AB\\) of the original two circles). Consequently\n\\[\n\\angle(AE ,\\, AF)=\\angle(BE ,\\, BF). \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nFrom (10) the line \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle EAF\\); therefore\n\\[\n\\angle(AP ,\\, BE)=\\angle(AE ,\\, AF). \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nCombining (13), (17) and (16) we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,\\, BE)=\\angle(BE ,\\, BF). \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nEquation (18) is exactly the tangent\u2011chord theorem: a line through a point on a circle is tangent to the circle iff the angle it makes with a chord equals the angle subtended by that chord in the opposite segment. Hence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(E\\) (equivalently at \\(F\\), because \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\) which passes through both).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) satisfies the tangent\u2011chord condition with respect to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Therefore it is tangent to that circumcircle, as was required. \u220e"
