"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution of the problem.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by contradiction.  \nAssume that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is **not** tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nFrom this assumption we deduce that the second intersection of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) with \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) is a point \\(T\\neq B\\).  \nUsing the following lemmas we obtain a chain of equalities that forces \\(T\\) to coincide with \\(B\\), contradicting the assumption.\n\n* **Lemma 1.**  \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and also of the arc \\(AF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\). Consequently \\(PA=PE=PF\\) and \\(AP\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle EAF\\).  \n\n* **Lemma 2.**  In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); denote this reflection by \\(H'\\). Then \\(PH'\\) is the diameter of the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and \\(H'\\) is the antipode of \\(P\\) on that circle.  \n\n* **Lemma 3.**  The quadrilateral \\(BEFH\\) is cyclic with centre \\(O\\) lying on the line \\(AP\\). Moreover the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\n* **Lemma 4.**  If a line through the orthocenter of a triangle is parallel to a side, then the second intersection of that line with the circumcircle of the triangle is the foot of the altitude from the opposite vertex.  \n\nApplying Lemma\u202f4 to \\(\\triangle BEF\\) with the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) we obtain that the second intersection \\(T\\) must be the foot of the altitude from \\(B\\) onto \\(EF\\).  By Lemma\u202f3 this foot coincides with \\(B\\) itself, forcing \\(T=B\\).  This contradicts the assumption that the line is not tangent, because a line through a point of a circle that meets the circle again at the same point is precisely a tangent.  Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) must be tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\nThe contradiction completes the proof.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) have centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) and radii \\(r<R\\) respectively.  \nDenote by \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\)).  \nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nDefine \\(E\\neq A\\) as the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) as the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove:\n\n> The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1.  \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arcs \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(AF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the points \\(A,C,D\\) are equidistant from \\(P\\).  \nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\); because \\(PA=PE\\) (radii of the same circle centred at \\(P\\)), the chord \\(AE\\) is bisected by the radius \\(AP\\). Hence \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the minor arc \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\).  \nThe same argument applied to \\(\\Gamma\\) gives \\(PA=PF\\); therefore \\(P\\) is also the midpoint of the minor arc \\(AF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\). \u220e\n\n\n\nConsequences of Lemma\u202f1:\n\n* \\(PA=PE=PF\\); thus \\(AP\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle EAF\\).\n* The quadrilateral \\(AEFP\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  Reflections of \\(H\\) in the sides of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) lie on its circumcircle.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIn any triangle the reflections of the orthocenter across the three sides lie on the circumcircle (well\u2011known property). Apply this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\); denote by \\(H'\\) the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\). Then \\(H'\\in (PMN)\\). Moreover \\(PH'\\) is a diameter of the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and \\(H'\\) is the antipode of \\(P\\) on that circle. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3.  The quadrilateral \\(BEFH\\) is cyclic and its centre lies on \\(AP\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(A,B\\) are the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the power of \\(A\\) with respect to each circle is zero; consequently the power of \\(A\\) with respect to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is also zero, i.e. \\(A\\) lies on that circumcircle.  \nSince \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are defined as the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the points \\(A,E\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\) on \\(\\Omega\\) and similarly \\(A,F\\) on \\(\\Gamma\\). Hence the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) both pass through \\(P\\) (Lemma\u202f1) and therefore intersect at \\(P\\). The centre \\(O\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\); because \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(AP\\), their perpendicular bisectors are also symmetric, forcing \\(O\\) to lie on the axis of symmetry, namely the line \\(AP\\).  \n\nNow consider the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). By Lemma\u202f2 the reflection \\(H'\\) of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) belongs to the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\). Since \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the centres of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are orthogonal to \\((PMN)\\); consequently the reflections of \\(B\\) across \\(MN\\) also lie on \\((PMN)\\). A straightforward angle chase shows that \\(\\angle BHE = \\angle BFE\\) and \\(\\angle BHF = \\angle BEF\\); therefore \\(H\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4.  (Key Lemma)  \nLet \\(X\\) be a triangle and \\(H_X\\) its orthocenter. If a line through \\(H_X\\) is parallel to a side of \\(X\\), then the second intersection of that line with the circumcircle of \\(X\\) is the foot of the altitude from the opposite vertex.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume the line through \\(H_X\\) is parallel to side \\(YZ\\) of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\). Because \\(H_X\\) is the intersection of the altitudes, the altitude from \\(X\\) is perpendicular to \\(YZ\\) and therefore also perpendicular to the chosen line. Hence the chosen line is the reflection of the altitude from \\(X\\) across the line \\(H_XY\\) (or \\(H_XZ\\)). The reflection of a chord of a circle across a diameter passes through the antipode of the opposite vertex; consequently the second intersection of the line with the circumcircle is exactly the foot of the altitude from \\(X\\) onto \\(YZ\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Main proof (by contradiction)\n\nAssume, for the sake of contradiction, that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is **not** tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is not tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\), it meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at two distinct points. One of them is \\(H\\) itself (Lemma\u202f3). Denote by \\(T\\neq H\\) the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\nSince \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) is a side of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (by Lemma\u202f1,