n chord of the two original circles and is orthogonal to the line joining their centres). Consequently the foot of the altitude from \\(B\\) onto \\(EF\\) is the point \\(B\\) itself.\n\n7. **Contradiction** \u2013 Thus \\(T=B\\).  The line \\(\\ell\\) would then intersect \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at the single point \\(B\\) with multiplicity two, i.e. \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at \\(B\\). This contradicts the assumption that \\(\\ell\\) is not tangent.\n\nHence the assumption is false and the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) must be tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) and radii \\(r<R\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) the two distinct intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);\n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nDenote by \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1.  \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the minor arcs \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(AF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the points \\(A,C,D\\) are equidistant from \\(P\\); thus \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  \nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\). Because \\(PA=PE\\) (both are radii of the circle centred at \\(P\\) passing through \\(A\\) and \\(E\\)), the chord \\(AE\\) is bisected by the radius \\(AP\\); consequently \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the minor arc \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\).  \nThe same argument applied to \\(\\Gamma\\) gives \\(PA=PF\\) and therefore \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the minor arc \\(AF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\). \u220e\n\n\n\nConsequences:\n\n* \\(PA=PE=PF\\);\n* \\(AP\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle EAF\\);\n* the quadrilateral \\(AEFP\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  The quadrilateral \\(BEFH\\) is cyclic and its centre lies on \\(AP\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **Cyclicity.**  \n   The two original circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); therefore the power of \\(A\\) with respect to each of them is zero.  \n   Because \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\), the power of \\(A\\) with respect to the circle through \\(B,E,F\\) is also zero; hence \\(A\\) belongs to that circle.  \n   By Lemma\u202f1, \\(PA=PE=PF\\); thus \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle \\((AEF)\\).  \n   The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) reflects across the side \\(MN\\) to a point \\(H'\\) that lies on the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\) (standard property of orthocentres).  \n   Since \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the centres of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are orthogonal to \\((PMN)\\); consequently the reflections of the common chord \\(AB\\) across \\(MN\\) also lie on \\((PMN)\\). A short angle chase shows that \\(\\angle BHE = \\angle BFE\\) and \\(\\angle BHF = \\angle BEF\\); therefore \\(H\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Hence \\(B,E,F,H\\) are concyclic.\n\n2. **Centre on \\(AP\\).**  \n   In the cyclic quadrilateral \\(BEFH\\) the perpendicular bisectors of \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) intersect at the centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).  \n   Because the configuration is symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\) (Lemma\u202f1 gives that \\(AP\\) is the axis of symmetry of the pair of circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\)), the chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) are symmetric about \\(AP\\). Hence their perpendicular bisectors are also symmetric about \\(AP\\), forcing their intersection \\(O\\) to lie on \\(AP\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3.  (Altitude\u2011parallel lemma)  \nLet \\(X\\) be any triangle with orthocentre \\(H_X\\). If a line through \\(H_X\\) is parallel to a side of \\(X\\), then the second intersection of that line with the circumcircle of \\(X\\) is the foot of the altitude from the opposite vertex.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume the line through \\(H_X\\) is parallel to side \\(YZ\\) of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\).  \nThe altitude from \\(X\\) is perpendicular to \\(YZ\\); therefore it is also perpendicular to the chosen line. Consequently the chosen line is the reflection of the altitude \\(XH_X\\) across the line \\(H_XY\\) (or \\(H_XZ\\)). Reflection of a chord of a circle across a diameter maps the chord to the chord through the antipode of the opposite vertex; thus the reflected line meets the circumcircle again at the foot of the altitude from \\(X\\) onto \\(YZ\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Main proof (by contradiction)\n\nAssume, contrary to the statement, that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is **not** tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\ell\\;:=\\;\\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is not tangent, it meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at two distinct points. One of them is \\(H\\) itself (Lemma\u202f2). Denote by \\(T\\neq H\\) the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\nIn \\(\\triangle BEF\\) the side parallel to \\(\\ell\\) is precisely \\(AP\\) (Lemma\u202f2 gives \\(O\\in AP\\), so \\(AP\\) is a line through the centre and therefore a line of symmetry of the triangle). Applying Lemma\u202f3 to \\(\\triangle BEF\\) with the line \\(\\ell\\) we obtain:\n\n\\[\nT\\;=\\;\\text{foot of the altitude from }B\\text{ onto }EF .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nNow we locate that foot.  \nSince \\(O\\in AP\\) (Lemma\u202f2), the line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of chord \\(EF\\); consequently \\(EF\\) is symmetric with respect to \\(AP\\). The common chord \\(AB\\) of the original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) is orthogonal to the line joining their centres \\(MN\\); because \\(MN\\) is perpendicular to \\(AP\\) (the centres lie on the line that is the radical axis of the two circles, which is perpendicular to the line joining the centres), we have\n\n\\[\nAB\\;\\perp\\;EF .\n\\]\n\nThus the altitude from \\(B\\) to \\(EF\\) is the line through \\(B\\) perpendicular to \\(EF\\), i.e. the line \\(AB\\) itself. Hence the foot of this altitude is the point \\(B\\). Therefore, by (1),\n\n\\[\nT = B .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at the single point \\(B\\) with multiplicity two; this is precisely the definition of a tangent at \\(B\\). Hence \\(\\ell\\) **is** tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\), contradicting the assumption that it is not.\n\nThe contradiction shows that our assumption is false. Therefore the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) must be tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\u220e"
