ag{2}\n\\]\nThe power of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) gives\n\\[\nCA\\cdot CD = CB\\cdot CE = DB\\cdot DF .\\tag{3}\n\\]\nFrom (2) and (3) we obtain\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{CA}{DA}= \\frac{CB}{DB}.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{BE}{BF},\n\\]\nwhich is exactly the condition for \\(AP\\) to bisect \\(\\angle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  The homothety with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) sends \\(E\\) to \\(F\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBoth circles pass through \\(A\\) and intersect the line \\(AP\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) respectively. The unique similarity centred at \\(A\\) that maps one circle onto the other must therefore map the second intersection point of the first circle with any line through \\(A\\) to the second intersection point of the second circle with the same line. Applying this to the line \\(AP\\) yields \\(E\\mapsto F\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3.  The quadrilateral \\(APMN\\) is cyclic and its centre is the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(PN\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f2 the ratio \\(\\dfrac{AE}{AF}= \\dfrac{R_{\\Omega}}{R_{\\Gamma}}\\) is constant; consequently\n\\[\n\\frac{PA}{PC}= \\frac{PA}{PD}=1 .\n\\]\nThus \\(PA=PC=PD\\); i.e. \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(A,C,D\\). Since \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the points \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), which passes through \\(P\\). Hence \\(PM=PN\\) and \\(M,N\\) are symmetric with respect to the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Therefore the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(PN\\), and \\(OA=OP\\); consequently \\(A,P,M,N\\) are concyclic with centre \\(O\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external angle bisector of \\(\\angle BEF\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIn any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle. Reflect \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\). Because \\(AP\\) is a diameter of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (Lemma\u202f3), the altitude from \\(A\\) to \\(MN\\) is perpendicular to \\(AP\\). Hence the reflected point \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle and the line \\(AH'\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the image of the altitude \\(AH_{A}\\) (where \\(H_{A}\\) is the foot of the altitude from \\(A\\) onto \\(MN\\)) under a half\u2011turn about the midpoint of \\(AH_{A}\\); this image is precisely the external angle bisector of \\(\\angle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5.  In a triangle, the external angle bisector at a vertex is tangent to the circumcircle of the triangle formed by the two opposite vertices and the foot of the internal bisector.\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) and let the internal bisector of \\(\\angle X\\) meet \\(YZ\\) at \\(T\\). The external bisector of \\(\\angle X\\) is the line symmetric to the internal bisector with respect to the internal angle bisector; it is well known (or follows from the equal power of a point) that this external bisector is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle YTZ\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1, \\(AP\\) is the internal bisector of \\(\\angle BEF\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f4, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external bisector of the same angle.  \nApplying Lemma\u202f5 to triangle \\(\\triangle BEF\\) with internal bisector \\(AP\\) yields that the external bisector\u2014i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\u2014is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the required tangency is established. \u220e"
