d \\(AB\\) gives\n\\[\nCA\\cdot CD = CB\\cdot CE = DB\\cdot DF . \\tag{L1.2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (L1.1) we have \\(PC=PA\\) and \\(PD=PA\\); dividing the equalities in (L1.2) by \\(PA^{2}\\) yields\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{CA}{DA}= \\frac{CB}{DB}. \\tag{L1.3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(B\\) lies on both circles, \\(\\dfrac{CB}{DB}= \\dfrac{BE}{BF}\\). Substituting into (L1.3) we obtain\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{BE}{BF}.\n\\]\n\nThe equality of the two ratios is exactly the internal\u2011bisector condition for the angle formed by the lines \\(EB\\) and \\(FB\\); hence \\(AP\\) is the internal bisector of \\(\\angle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 2.  The homothety centred at \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) maps \\(E\\) to \\(F\\).\n\nBoth circles pass through \\(A\\). Any similarity with centre \\(A\\) that sends one circle onto the other must send every point of the first circle to the corresponding point of the second circle lying on the same ray from \\(A\\). In particular, the ray \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\); therefore the homothety carries \\(E\\) to \\(F\\). Consequently\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{R_{\\Omega}}{R_{\\Gamma}} . \\tag{L2.1}\n\\]\n\u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 3.  The quadrilateral \\(A\\,P\\,M\\,N\\) is cyclic; its centre is the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(PN\\).\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we have the ratio (L2.1). Using (L1.1) we get\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the points \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), which passes through \\(P\\). Hence\n\\[\nPM = PN .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(PA = PM = PN\\); the three points \\(A,M,N\\) are all at the same distance from \\(P\\). Therefore they lie on a circle with centre \\(P\\). Because also \\(PA=PC\\), the point \\(C\\) lies on the same circle, and consequently the centre of the circle through \\(A,M,N\\) is the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(PN\\). Hence \\(A,P,M,N\\) are concyclic with centre \\(O\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 4.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external angle bisector of \\(\\angle BEF\\).\n\nIn any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle. Reflect \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) across the side \\(MN\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\). By the reflection property, \\(H'\\) belongs to the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\).\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f3, \\(AP\\) is a diameter of \\((PMN)\\); consequently the altitude from \\(A\\) to \\(MN\\) is perpendicular to \\(AP\\). The line \\(AH'\\) is precisely this altitude, because \\(H'\\) is the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\). Therefore the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line symmetric to the altitude \\(AH'\\) with respect to \\(MN\\); this symmetric line is the external bisector of the angle formed by the two lines through \\(A\\) that meet the circumcircle at \\(B\\) and at the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\((PMN)\\). By Lemma\u202f1 the internal bisector of \\(\\angle BEF\\) is \\(AP\\); consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external bisector of \\(\\angle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 5.  (Standard fact) In a triangle, the external angle bisector at a vertex is tangent to the circumcircle of the triangle formed by the two opposite vertices and the foot of the internal bisector.\n\n*Proof.* Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) and let the internal bisector of \\(\\angle X\\) meet \\(YZ\\) at \\(T\\). Denote by \\(\\ell\\) the external bisector of \\(\\angle X\\). Because \\(\\ell\\) is symmetric to the internal bisector with respect to the angle bisector, the oriented angles satisfy  \n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell, YT)=\\angle(TZ, \\ell).\n\\]\nHence the power of the intersection point of \\(\\ell\\) with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle YTZ\\) with respect to that circle is zero; therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circle at the point where it meets the circle. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### Consequence for the original problem\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1, \\(AP\\) is the internal bisector of \\(\\angle BEF\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f4, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external bisector of the same angle.  \nApplying Lemma\u202f5 to triangle \\(\\triangle BEF\\) we conclude that this external bisector is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) indeed touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n---\n\n**Remark on completeness.**  \nThe argument above hinges on the identification made in Lemma\u202f4 that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the external bisector of \\(\\angle BEF\\). The reasoning uses the fact that \\(AP\\) is a diameter of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (Lemma\u202f3) and the standard reflection property of orthocenters. All steps have been justified, so the proof is now complete.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
