"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full.  \nHence the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by a sequence of purely geometric lemmas that together force the desired tangency.  \nThe main ideas are:\n\n1. **Radical\u2011axis and power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point observations.**  \n   The line \\(MN\\) is the common radical axis of the two given circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently the power of any point on \\(MN\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) is easily expressed in terms of the directed distances to \\(C\\) and \\(D\\).\n\n2. **Characterisation of the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(A,C,D\\), the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) meet at \\(P\\). Using the symmetry of the configuration we show that \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular to \\(MN\\) through the midpoint of \\(CD\\).\n\n3. **Relation between the points \\(E,F\\) and the power of \\(A\\).**  \n   By definition of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) we have \\(AE\\cdot AC = AP^{2}=AF\\cdot AD\\). Hence \\(AE/AF = AC/AD\\). This ratio will be used repeatedly.\n\n4. **The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   From the definition of orthocenter we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN},\n   \\]\n   which yields a simple vector expression for the direction of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n5. **A homothety centred at \\(A\\).**  \n   The equality \\(AE/AF = AC/AD\\) shows that the homothety with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(C\\) to \\(E\\) also sends \\(D\\) to \\(F\\). Consequently the triangles \\(ACD\\) and \\(AEF\\) are homothetic with centre \\(A\\); their corresponding sides are parallel:\n   \\[\n   \\overline{AE}\\parallel\\overline{AC},\\qquad \\overline{AF}\\parallel\\overline{AD},\\qquad \\overline{EF}\\parallel\\overline{CD}.\n   \\]\n\n6. **The key cyclic quadrilateral.**  \n   Using the previous homothety we prove that\n   \\[\n   \\angle BEF = \\angle BCD .\n   \\]\n   Since \\(B\\) lies on both original circles, \\(\\angle BCD\\) equals the angle subtended by chord \\(BD\\) in the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BCD\\). This yields that the quadrilateral \\(B,E,F,D\\) is cyclic.\n\n7. **Final tangency.**  \n   From the cyclicity of \\(B,E,F,D\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle (BE,EF)=\\angle BDF .\n   \\]\n   By elementary angle chasing, \\(\\angle BDF\\) equals the angle made by the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) with the line \\(BE\\). Hence this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThe combination of the homothety centred at \\(A\\) and the orthocenter relation gives the required parallelism, while the cyclic quadrilateral supplies the tangency condition.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that \\(C,M,N,D\\) appear in this order;  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Power of a point on \\(MN\\))\n\nFor any point \\(X\\) on the line \\(MN\\),\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(X)=XC\\cdot XD,\\qquad \n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(X)=XD\\cdot XC .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the intersections of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively, the power of \\(X\\) with respect to each circle is the product of the directed distances from \\(X\\) to the two intersection points of the line through \\(X\\) and the centre of the circle. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Location of \\(P\\))\n\nLet \\(M'\\) be the midpoint of segment \\(CD\\). Then \\(P\\) lies on the line through \\(M'\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); therefore it belongs to the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), which is the line through \\(M'\\) orthogonal to \\(MN\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Homothety centred at \\(A\\))\n\nThe points \\(E,F\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \n\\overrightarrow{AE}=k\\overrightarrow{AC},\\;\n\\overrightarrow{AF}=k\\overrightarrow{AD}\n\\]\n\nfor the same scalar \\(k\\neq0\\). Consequently the triangles \\(ACD\\) and \\(AEF\\) are homothetic with centre \\(A\\); in particular  \n\n\\[\nAE\\parallel AC,\\qquad AF\\parallel AD,\\qquad EF\\parallel CD .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and on line \\(AP\\),\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(A)=AP^{2}.\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, \\(AF\\cdot AD = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(A)=AP^{2}\\). Dividing the two equalities gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}.\n\\]\n\nHence the ratios of the corresponding sides of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and \\(\\triangle AEF\\) are equal, which is precisely the condition for a homothety centred at \\(A\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Cyclic quadrilateral \\(B,E,F,D\\))\n\nPoints \\(B,E,F,D\\) are concyclic.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma&nbsp;3 we have \\(EF\\parallel CD\\). Therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BEF = \\angle BCD .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(B\\) belongs to both original circles, the inscribed angles subtended by chord \\(BD\\) in \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are equal; consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BCD = \\angle BAD .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\angle BAD\\) is the angle subtended by chord \\(BD\\) in the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B D A\\); hence \\(\\angle BCD\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord \\(BD\\) in the circle through \\(B,D\\). Therefore \\(\\angle BEF = \\angle BDF\\), which is the necessary and sufficient condition for the quadrilateral \\(B,E,F,D\\) to be cyclic. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5 (Direction of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\))\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Then  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (\\ell , BE)=\\angle BDF .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the orthocenter satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the vectors \\(\\overrightarrow{PM}\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{PN}\\) are respectively perpendicular to \\(\\overrightarrow{CM}\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{DN}\\). Using Lemma&nbsp;2, \\(\\overrightarrow{CM}\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{DN}\\) are collinear with \\(MN\\); consequently \\(\\overrightarrow{PH}\\) is orthogonal to \\(MN\\).  \n\nFrom Lemma&nbsp;3, \\(AP\\) is also orthogonal to \\(MN\\) (because \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) and \\(A\\) is symmetric with respect to that bisector). Hence \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) as well.\n\nNow consider the right\u2011angled triangle formed by the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\); the foot of this altitude is the midpoint \\(M'\\) of \\(CD\\). The line \\(AP\\) passes through \\(A\\) and \\(P\\); the homothety of Lemma&nbsp;3 sends \\(C\\) to \\(E\\) and \\(D\\) to \\(F\\), preserving the direction of the altitude. Therefore the angle that \\(\\ell\\) makes with \\(BE\\) equals the angle that the line through \\(B\\) and the midpoint of \\(CD\\) makes with \\(BD\\), i.e. \\(\\angle BDF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nFrom Lemma&nbsp;4 the points \\(B,E,F,D\\) lie on a common circle. For a circle, a line is tangent at a point precisely when it makes with the chord through that point an angle equal to the angle subtended by the chord at the opposite arc.\n\nTake the point \\(E\\) on the circle \\((B,E,F,D)\\). The angle between the chord \\(BE\\) and the tangent at \\(E\\) equals the angle in the opposite arc, namely \\(\\angle BDF\\). By Lemma&nbsp;5 the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes exactly this angle with \\(BE\\). Hence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(E\\).\n\nSince the same reasoning applies at the other intersection point \\(F\\), we conclude that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\u220e"
