rthogonal to \\(MN\\), and by (2) \\(\\overrightarrow{PH}\\) is also orthogonal to \\(MN\\).  \n   Because \\(AP\\) joins a point \\(A\\) on the two intersecting circles to the centre \\(P\\) of the circle through \\(A,C,D\\), the line \\(AP\\) is likewise orthogonal to \\(MN\\) (the centre of the circle through \\(A\\) and the two points of intersection of the circles with the line \\(MN\\) must lie on the perpendicular to \\(MN\\) through the midpoint of \\(CD\\)). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\).\n\n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Since \\(\\ell\\perp MN\\) and \\(EF\\parallel CD\\parallel MN^{\\perp}\\), we have\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BDF . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   *Tangency.*  In the cyclic quadrilateral \\(B,E,F,D\\) the angle between chord \\(BE\\) and the tangent at \\(E\\) equals the angle subtended by the opposite chord \\(BD\\), i.e. \\(\\angle BDF\\). By (3) the line \\(\\ell\\) makes exactly this angle with \\(BE\\); therefore \\(\\ell\\) is the tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(E\\). The same reasoning holds at \\(F\\).\n\nThus the required tangency is established.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### Notation and basic configuration  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) \u2013 circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) \u2013 circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) \u2013 the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) \u2013 the points where the line \\(MN\\) meets the two circles, ordered \\(C,M,N,D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) \u2013 circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) \u2013 second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) \u2013 second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) \u2013 orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n* \\(\\ell\\) \u2013 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nAll directed angles are measured modulo \\(180^{\\circ}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 1 (Power of a point on the line \\(MN\\))\n\nFor any point \\(X\\) on the line \\(MN\\),\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(X)=XC\\cdot XD,\\qquad \n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(X)=XD\\cdot XC .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  The line through \\(X\\) and the centre of \\(\\Omega\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) at the two points \\(C\\) and the second intersection, which is \\(D\\) because \\(C,D\\) are precisely the intersections of \\(MN\\) with the two circles. The definition of power gives the product of the directed distances to those two points. The same reasoning holds for \\(\\Gamma\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2 (Homothety centred at \\(A\\))\n\nThe points \\(E,F\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AP^{2}=AF\\cdot AD .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad \n\\overrightarrow{AE}=k\\overrightarrow{AC},\\;\n\\overrightarrow{AF}=k\\overrightarrow{AD}\n\\]\n\nfor a non\u2011zero scalar \\(k\\). Hence the triangles \\(ACD\\) and \\(AEF\\) are homothetic with centre \\(A\\); in particular\n\n\\[\nAE\\parallel AC,\\qquad AF\\parallel AD,\\qquad EF\\parallel CD . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Because \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and on line \\(AP\\),\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(A)=AP^{2}.\n\\]\n\nAnalogously, \\(F\\in\\Gamma\\) gives \\(AF\\cdot AD = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(A)=AP^{2}\\). Dividing the two equalities yields the ratio of the segments, which is precisely the condition for a homothety centred at \\(A\\). The parallelisms in (4) follow immediately. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 3 (Cyclicity of \\(B,E,F,D\\))\n\nPoints \\(B,E,F,D\\) lie on a common circle.\n\n*Proof.*  From (4) we have \\(EF\\parallel CD\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BEF = \\angle BCD . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(A\\) belongs to both original circles, the inscribed angles subtended by chord \\(BD\\) are equal:\n\n\\[\n\\angle BCD = \\angle BAD . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\angle BAD\\) is the angle subtended by chord \\(BD\\) in the circle through \\(B\\) and \\(D\\); consequently \\(\\angle BAD = \\angle BFD\\). Combining (5) and (6) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BEF = \\angle BDF .\n\\]\n\nEquality of the angle formed by a pair of opposite vertices of a quadrilateral with the angle subtended by the other pair is the well\u2011known criterion for cyclicity; thus \\(B,E,F,D\\) are concyclic. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 4 (Location of \\(P\\) and direction of \\(\\ell\\))\n\nLet \\(M'\\) be the midpoint of \\(CD\\). Then  \n\n* (i) \\(P\\) lies on the line through \\(M'\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\);  \n* (ii) the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\).\n\n*Proof of (i).*  By definition \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); hence \\(P\\) belongs to the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), which is the line through \\(M'\\) orthogonal to \\(MN\\).\n\n*Proof of (ii).*  In any triangle the orthocenter satisfies vector identity (2):\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}.\n\\]\nBoth \\(\\overrightarrow{PM}\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{PN}\\) are perpendicular to \\(MN\\): indeed, \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), while \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) (Lemma\u202f4(i)), which is itself perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Hence each of the two vectors is orthogonal to \\(MN\\), and their sum \\(\\overrightarrow{PH}\\) is also orthogonal to \\(MN\\).  \n\nSince \\(AP\\) joins a point \\(A\\) on the two intersecting circles to the centre \\(P\\) of the circle through \\(A,C,D\\), the line \\(AP\\) is the line through \\(P\\) orthogonal to \\(MN\\) (the centre of a circle passing through the two intersection points of the original circles must lie on the perpendicular to the line joining the two centres). Therefore \\(AP\\perp MN\\).  \n\nConsequently the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 5 (Angle relation)\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BDF .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  By Lemma\u202f4(ii) the line \\(\\ell\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\). From (4) we have \\(EF\\parallel CD\\) and, because \\(C,D\\) lie on \\(MN\\), the segment \\(CD\\) is collinear with \\(MN\\). Hence \\(EF\\) is also perpendicular to \\(\\ell\\).  \n\nIn the cyclic quadrilateral \\(B,E,F,D\\) (Lemma\u202f3) the exterior angle at \\(F\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BDF = \\angle BEF .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\angle BEF\\) is the angle between \\(BE\\) and \\(EF\\); since \\(EF\\perp\\ell\\), the angle between \\(BE\\) and \\(\\ell\\) equals \\(\\angle BEF\\). Therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BEF=\\angle BDF .\n\\] \u220e\n\n\n\n### Final step (tangency)\n\nIn a circle, the angle between a chord and the tangent at one endpoint equals the angle subtended by the chord at the opposite arc.  \nBecause \\(B,E,F,D\\) are concyclic (Lemma\u202f3), the tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at \\(E\\) makes with chord \\(BE\\) an angle equal to \\(\\angle BDF\\).  \n\nLemma\u202f5 shows that the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes exactly the same angle with \\(BE\\). Hence \\(\\ell\\) coincides with the tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(E\\). (The same argument works at \\(F\\).)\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\u220e"
