quently false. The derivation also implicitly uses Lemma\u202f1 (the product formula for power), which has already been shown to be incorrect.\n\n**Classification:** Critical Error \u2013 the core algebraic relation is wrong, so the claimed homothety and the parallelisms that follow are unsupported.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f3 (Cyclicity of \\(B,E,F,D\\))\n\n> \u201cFrom (4) we have \\(EF\\parallel CD\\); therefore \\(\\angle BEF = \\angle BCD\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe parallelism \\(EF\\parallel CD\\) is a direct consequence of Lemma\u202f2, which is invalid. Hence the angle equality \\(\\angle BEF = \\angle BCD\\) has no justification. The subsequent chain of equalities leading to the cyclicity of \\(B,E,F,D\\) rests entirely on this false premise.\n\n**Classification:** Critical Error \u2013 the conclusion that the four points are concyclic is not established.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f4 (Location of \\(P\\) and direction of \\(\\ell\\))\n\n> \u201c(i) \\(P\\) lies on the line through \\(M'\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\);  \n> (ii) the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis of (i):**  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), it is equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); thus it lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\). The perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) indeed passes through the midpoint \\(M'\\) and is orthogonal to \\(CD\\). Because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the perpendicular bisector is orthogonal to \\(MN\\). This part is correct.\n\n**Analysis of (ii):**  \nThe proof invokes the vector identity \\(\\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}\\) (a standard property of the orthocenter) and claims that both \\(\\overrightarrow{PM}\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{PN}\\) are perpendicular to \\(MN\\). While \\(\\overrightarrow{PM}\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{PN}\\) are indeed perpendicular to \\(MN\\) because \\(P\\) lies on th