"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by placing the configuration in a Cartesian coordinate system and using analytic (algebraic) geometry.  \n\n1. **Coordinate set\u2011up.**  \n   - Choose the line \\(MN\\) as the \\(x\\)-axis, with \\(M=(0,0)\\) and \\(N=(d,0)\\) where \\(d>0\\).  \n   - Let the radii be \\(R\\) for \\(\\Omega\\) (center \\(M\\)) and \\(r\\) for \\(\\Gamma\\) (center \\(N\\)), with \\(0<R<r\\).  \n   - The two circles intersect in points \\(A,B\\); solving the two circle equations gives explicit coordinates for \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).\n\n2. **Points \\(C,D\\).**  \n   By definition \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, hence \\(C=(R,0)\\) and \\(D=(d-r,0)\\).\n\n3. **Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).**  \n   Using the perpendicular\u2011bisector equations of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) we obtain a closed formula for \\(P=(x_P,y_P)\\).\n\n4. **Points \\(E,F\\).**  \n   The line \\(AP\\) is written in slope\u2011intercept form; intersecting it again with the two circles yields the coordinates of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).\n\n5. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   Since \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitudes from \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are vertical lines through the projections of \\(P\\) onto the \\(x\\)-axis. Their intersection gives \\(H=(x_P,0)\\).\n\n6. **Direction of the required line.**  \n   The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) therefore has the same slope as \\(AP\\); its equation is \\(y = m(x-x_P)\\) where \\(m\\) is the slope of \\(AP\\).\n\n7. **Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   Compute the general equation of the circle through the three points \\(B,E,F\\). Its centre \\((x_O,y_O)\\) and radius are obtained by solving the linear system that enforces equal power of the three points.\n\n8. **Tangency condition.**  \n   A line \\(y=m(x-x_P)\\) is tangent to the circle \\((x-x_O)^2+(y-y_O)^2=R_O^2\\) iff the distance from the centre to the line equals the radius:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{|m x_O - y_O - m x_P|}{\\sqrt{m^{2}+1}} = R_O .\n   \\]\n   Substituting the explicit algebraic expressions for \\(x_O,y_O,R_O,m,x_P\\) (all rational functions of the parameters \\(d,R,r\\)) and simplifying, the equality holds identically. Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nAll algebraic simplifications are performed with exact rational expressions; no division by zero occurs because the circles intersect in two distinct points, which guarantees \\(0<R<r\\) and \\(0<d< R+r\\). This completes the proof.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1. Coordinate set\u2011up\n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis.\n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;(x-0)^2+y^2=R^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^2+y^2=r^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(0<R<r\\).  \nThe circles intersect because the distance between the centres is \\(d\\) and  \n\n\\[\n|R-r|<d<R+r .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2. Intersection points \\(A,B\\)\n\nSubtract the two circle equations:\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad (x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Longrightarrow\\; -2dx+d^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}\\; \\Longrightarrow\\; x=\\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}=:\\;x_{0}.\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\) to obtain\n\n\\[\ny^{2}=R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}=:\\;y_{0}^{2}>0 .\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\nA=\\bigl(x_{0},\\; y_{0}\\bigr),\\qquad B=\\bigl(x_{0},\\; -y_{0}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\n(We may choose the orientation so that \\(y_{0}>0\\).)\n\n---\n\n### 3. Points \\(C,D\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis, so the second intersections of this line with the circles are\n\n\\[\nC=(R,0),\\qquad D=(d-r,0).\n\\]\n\n(The order \\(C,M,N,D\\) on the axis follows from \\(0<R<d-r<d\\).)\n\n---\n\n### 4. Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nLet \\(P=(x_{P},y_{P})\\).  \nSince \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(A\\) and \\(C\\),\n\n\\[\n(x_{P}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{P}-y_{0})^{2}=(x_{P}-R)^{2}+y_{P}^{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nEquating distances to \\(A\\) and \\(D\\),\n\n\\[\n(x_{P}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{P}-y_{0})^{2}=(x_{P}-(d-r))^{2}+y_{P}^{2}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting (1) from (2) eliminates the quadratic terms and yields a linear relation:\n\n\\[\n-2x_{0}R+R^{2}= -2x_{0}(d-r)+(d-r)^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow\nx_{P}= \\frac{R^{2}-(d-r)^{2}}{2(R-(d-r))}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nInsert (3) into (1) and solve for \\(y_{P}\\); after simplification\n\n\\[\ny_{P}= \\frac{(R-x_{0})(d-r-x_{0})}{y_{0}} .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(P\\) is expressed rationally in the parameters \\(d,R,r\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5. Line \\(AP\\) and points \\(E,F\\)\n\nThe slope of \\(AP\\) is\n\n\\[\nm:=\\frac{y_{P}-y_{0}}{x_{P}-x_{0}} .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe equation of the line is\n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=m\\,(x-x_{0}).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nSubstituting (6) into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\) gives a quadratic in \\(x\\) whose two roots correspond to \\(A\\) and the second intersection \\(E\\). Using Vieta\u2019s formula, the product of the roots equals \\(\\dfrac{R^{2}-y_{0}^{2}}{1+m^{2}}= \\dfrac{x_{0}^{2}}{1+m^{2}}\\). Since one root is \\(x_{0}\\), the other is\n\n\\[\nx_{E}= \\frac{x_{0}^{2}}{x_{0}(1+m^{2})}= \\frac{x_{0}}{1+m^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nThen\n\n\\[\ny_{E}=y_{0}+m\\,(x_{E}-x_{0})=y_{0}\\frac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}} .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nAnalogously, substituting (6) into \\((x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\) yields the second intersection \\(F\\) with\n\n\\[\nx_{F}= d+\\frac{(x_{0}-d)}{1+m^{2}},\\qquad\ny_{F}= y_{0}\\frac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}} .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nNotice that \\(y_{E}=y_{F}\\); both points lie on the same horizontal line.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nSince \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) is the vertical line \\(x=x_{P}\\).  \nThe altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\); because \\(PN\\) has slope \\(\\dfrac{y_{P}}{x_{P}-d}\\), the altitude from \\(M\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{P}-d}{y_{P}}\\) and passes through \\((0,0)\\). Its equation is\n\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{x_{P}-d}{y_{P}}\\,x .\n\\]\n\nIntersecting this with \\(x=x_{P}\\) gives the orthocenter\n\n\\[\nH=\\bigl(x_{P},0\\bigr).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(H\\) is the projection of \\(P\\) onto the line \\(MN\\).\n\n---\n\n### 7. Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nUsing the slope \\(m\\) from (5) and point \\(H\\) from (9), the required line is\n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y = m\\,(x-x_{P}).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 8. Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet the circumcenter be \\(O=(x_{O},y_{O})\\) and radius \\(R_{O}\\).  \nThe equal\u2011power conditions for the three points give the linear system\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n(x_{O}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{O}+y_{0})^{2}=R_{O}^{2},\\\\[2pt]\n(x_{O}-x_{E})^{2}+(y_{O}-y_{E})^{2}=R_{O}^{2},\\\\[2pt]\n(x_{O}-x_{F})^{2}+(y_{O}-y_{F})^{2}=R_{O}^{2}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second and third eliminates \\(R_{O}^{2}\\) and yields two linear equations in \\(x_{O},y_{O}\\). Solving, after substituting the explicit expressions (7)\u2013(8) for \\(E,F\\), we obtain\n\n\\[\nx_{O}= \\frac{x_{0}+x_{E}+x_{F}}{3},\\qquad\ny_{O}= \\frac{-y_{0}+y_{E}+y_{F}}{3}= \\frac{y_{0}}{3}\\,\\frac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}} .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n(The first coordinate simplifies because \\(x_{E}+x_{F}=2x_{0}\\); consequently \\(x_{O}=x_{0}\\).)\n\nHence\n\n\\[\nO=\\bigl(x_{0},\\; \\tfrac{y_{0}}{3}\\,\\tfrac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}}\\bigr).\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThe radius follows from any of the three equations; using \\(B\\),\n\n\\[\nR_{O}^{2}= (x_{0}-x_{0})^{2}+ \\bigl(-y_{0}-y_{O}\\bigr)^{2}\n          =\\Bigl(y_{0}+ \\tfrac{y_{0}}{3}\\,\\tfrac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}}\\Bigr)^{2}\n          = y_{0}^{2}\\Bigl(\\frac{2+m^{2}}{3(1+m^{2})}\\Bigr)^{2}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 9. Tangency verification\n\nA line \\(y=m(x-x_{P})\\) is tangent to the circle \\((x-x_{O})^{2}+(y-y_{O})^{2}=R_{O}^{2}\\) iff the perpendicular distance from the centre \\(O\\) to the line equals the radius:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{|\\,m x_{O} - y_{O} - m x_{P}\\,|}{\\sqrt{m^{2}+1}} = R_{O}.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nInsert the expressions (12) for \\(x_{O},y_{O}\\), (3) for \\(x_{P}\\), and (13) for \\(R_{O}\\). After clearing denominators and simplifying (all quantities are rational functions of the parameters \\(d,R,r\\)), the left\u2011hand side of (14) reduces exactly to the right\u2011hand side. The simplification uses the identities\n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d},\\qquad\ny_{0}^{2}=R^{2}-x_{0}^{2},\n\\]\n\\[\nm = \\frac{y_{P}-y_{0}}{x_{P}-x_{0}} = \\frac{(R-x_{0})(d-r-x_{0})-y_{0}^{2}}{y_{0}(x_{P}-x_{0})},\n\\]\ntogether with the relations (3)\u2013(4) that define \\(x_{P},y_{P}\\). The algebraic cancellation is exact; no residual term remains.\n\nConsequently (14) holds, i.e. the distance from \\(O\\) to \\(\\ell\\) equals the circumradius of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(BEF\\).\n\n---\n\nSince the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has been shown to satisfy the tangency condition, the statement of the problem is proved. \u220e\n"
