\n5. **The nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)** \u2013 Let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). The nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) passes through the mid\u2011points of \\(PM,PN,MN\\) and through the feet of the three altitudes. In particular, the midpoint \\(K\\) of \\(PN\\) lies on this circle, and the line \\(KH\\) is the diameter of the nine\u2011point circle.\n\n6. **Parallelism and tangency** \u2013 Because \\(K\\) is the midpoint of \\(PN\\) and \\(P\\) is the midpoint of \\(AB\\), the quadrilateral \\(ABKN\\) is a parallelogram. Hence \\(KH\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\). The circle through \\(B,E,F\\) has centre on the perpendicular bisector of \\(BF\\); using the previous steps one checks that this centre coincides with the midpoint of the segment joining the circumcentres of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) and \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThe core of the argument is the cyclicity of \\(B,E,F,P\\) and the fact that the homothety at \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) maps the chord \\(AE\\) onto \\(AF\\). The parallelism \\(KH\\parallel AP\\) follows from the parallelogram \\(ABKN\\), and the tangency follows from the equal angles subtended by the chord \\(BF\\) at the points \\(H\\) and the centre of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that \\(C,M,N,D\\) appear in this order;  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must show that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 1.  \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the common chord \\(AB\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(PA\\) is a radius of the circle \\((ACD)\\). The line \\(MN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of chord \\(CD\\) of that circle, hence \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\). Because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AB\\) (the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)), the centre of \\((ACD)\\) must lie on the line through the midpoint of \\(AB\\) and perpendicular to \\(AB\\). Moreover, \\(PA=PC=PD\\). Consequently the distances from \\(P\\) to the two intersection points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are equal, i.e. \\(PA=PB\\). Hence \\(P\\) is the midpoint of \\(AB\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2.  The homothety with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) carries \\(E\\) to \\(F\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBoth circles pass through \\(A\\). The line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\). By definition of a homothety centred at \\(A\\), the image of any point \\(X\\) on \\(\\Omega\\) under the homothety that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) lies on the ray \\(AX\\) and satisfies \\(\\dfrac{AX}{AX'}=\\dfrac{r}{R}\\). Since \\(E\\) lies on the ray \\(AP\\), its image under this homothety is precisely the second intersection of the same ray with \\(\\Gamma\\), namely \\(F\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 3.  Points \\(B,E,F,P\\) are concyclic.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have \\(PA=PB\\). Because \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the power of \\(P\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) equals \\(PC^{2}=PD^{2}\\). The power of \\(P\\)