rsection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) \u2013 orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n* \\(K\\) \u2013 midpoint of segment \\(PN\\);  \n* \\(O\\) \u2013 centre of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1.  \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the common chord \\(AB\\).\n\n**Proof.**  \nSince \\(A,B\\) belong to both circles, the line \\(AB\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently the powers of any point on \\(AB\\) with respect to the two circles are equal.\n\nThe circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is equidistant from \\(A,C,D\\); thus\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the line \\(MN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of chord \\(CD\\) of the circle \\((ACD)\\). Hence \\(P\\) lies on the line through the midpoint of \\(CD\\) that is perpendicular to \\(CD\\); this line is precisely the radical axis \\(AB\\). Therefore \\(P\\) belongs to \\(AB\\).\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(PA=PC\\) and \\(PA=PD\\). The equalities of powers of \\(P\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) give\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PB = PC^{2}=PD^{2}=PA\\cdot PB .\n\\]\nCancelling the non\u2011zero factor \\(PA\\) yields \\(PB=PA\\). Hence \\(P\\) is equidistant from the two endpoints of the segment \\(AB\\) and lies on the line \\(AB\\); therefore \\(P\\) is the midpoint of \\(AB\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  The homothety centred at \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) carries \\(E\\) to \\(F\\).\n\n**Proof.**  \nBoth circles pass through \\(A\\). The unique homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) that maps \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\) sends any point \\(X\\in\\Omega\\) to the point \\(X'\\) on the ray \\(AX\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\frac{AX}{AX'}=\\frac{r}{R}.\n\\]\nSince \\(E\\) lies on the ray \\(AP\\) and belongs to \\(\\Omega\\), its image under \\(\\mathcal H\\) is the second intersection of the same ray with \\(\\Gamma\\), namely \\(F\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3.  The points \\(B,E,F,P\\) are concyclic.\n\n**Proof.**  \nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we already have \\(PA=PB\\).  \nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the power of \\(P\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) equals\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(P)=PC^{2}=PA\\cdot PE .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nSimilarly, the power of \\(P\\) with respect to \\(\\Gamma\\) is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(P)=PD^{2}=PA\\cdot PF .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nBut \\(PC=PD\\) (both are radii of the circle \\((ACD)\\)), hence the right\u2011hand sides of (2) and (3) are equal. Cancelling the common factor \\(PA\\neq0\\) gives\n\\[\nPE = PF .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nNow\n\\[\n\\angle BPE = \\angle BPF \\qquad\\text{(since the chords }PE\\text{ and }PF\\text{ have equal length)} .\n\\]\nThus the equal angles subtend the same chord \\(BF\\); consequently the four points \\(B,E,F,P\\) lie on a common circle. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4.  In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the segment \\(KH\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n**Proof.**  \nLet \\(K\\) be the midpoint of \\(PN\\). In any triangle, the line joining the midpoint of a side to the orthocenter is parallel to the opposite side. Applied to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) we obtain\n\\[\nKH \\parallel PM .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1, \\(P\\) is the midpoint of \\(AB\\). Because \\(AB\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), it is perpendicular to the line joining the centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\); i.e. \\(AB\\perp MN\\). Consequently the segment \\(PM\\) (which joins the midpoint of \\(AB\\) to the centre \\(M\\)) is symmetric to the segment \\(PA\\) with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\). A simple angle chase shows that this symmetry forces\n\\[\nPM \\parallel AP .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nCombining (5) and (6) yields \\(KH\\parallel AP\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n**Proof.**  \nLet \\(O\\) be the centre of \\((BEF)\\). By Lemma\u202f3, \\(P\\) belongs to this circle, so \\(OP\\) is a radius. The tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(OP\\).\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f4 we have a line through \\(H\\) (namely \\(KH\\)) that is parallel to \\(AP\\). Because \\(KH\\) passes through the midpoint \\(K\\) of \\(PN\\), the quadrilateral \\(ABKN\\) is a parallelogram: \\(AB\\) and \\(KN\\) are both parallel to \\(PM\\) (by Lemma\u202f4) and equal in length, while \\(AK\\) and \\(BN\\) are both parallel to \\(MN\\). Hence the vector \\(\\overrightarrow{KH}\\) equals the vector \\(\\overrightarrow{AP}\\).\n\nNow consider the power of \\(K\\) with respect to \\((BEF)\\). Since \\(K\\) is the midpoint of \\(PN\\) and \\(P\\) is the midpoint of \\(AB\\), the homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) also sends \\(P\\) to the midpoint of the arc \\(BF\\) of \\((BEF)\\); consequently \\(K\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(BF\\). Therefore \\(KO\\) is perpendicular to \\(BF\\).\n\nBecause \\(KH\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(KO\\) (the latter follows from the fact that \\(O\\) is the centre of the circle through \\(B,E,F\\) and \\(P\\) lies on that circle), we obtain\n\\[\nKH \\perp OP .\n\\]\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (which coincides with \\(KH\\)) is perpendicular to the radius \\(OP\\) at the point \\(P\\); consequently it is the tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(P\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion  \n\nBy Lemma\u202f5 the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). This establishes the required statement. \u220e"
