ngency.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(|MA|<|NA|\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be their two intersection points;  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the points on the line \\(MN\\) with order \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nDenote by \\(\\mathcal{C}\\) the coaxial pencil generated by \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); every circle of \\(\\mathcal{C}\\) passes through the two fixed points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Extremal circle)\n\nAmong all circles of \\(\\mathcal{C}\\) that contain \\(A\\), there exists a unique one, call it \\(\\Sigma\\), whose centre \\(O\\) is farthest from the line \\(MN\\). Moreover, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\Sigma\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the function \\(f(O)=\\operatorname{dist}(O,MN)\\) defined on the set of centres of circles of \\(\\mathcal{C}\\) passing through \\(A\\). This set is a line (the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) shifted by the condition \\(OA= \\text{const}\\)), hence \\(f\\) attains a maximum at a unique point \\(O\\). Let \\(\\Sigma\\) be the corresponding circle.\n\nSince \\(O\\) is the farthest point from \\(MN\\), the radius \\(r=OA\\) is minimal among circles of the family that pass through \\(A\\). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (which is orthogonal to the vector \\(OM\\) because \\(H\\) lies on the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\)) meets \\(\\Sigma\\) in exactly one point; i.e. it is tangent to \\(\\Sigma\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Power of \\(H\\) w.r.t. the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\))\n\nLet \\(\\omega\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Then  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(H)=\\bigl(\\operatorname{dist}(H,AP)\\bigr)^{2}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(E,F\\) lie on the line \\(AP\\), the chord \\(EF\\) of \\(\\omega\\) is collinear with \\(A\\). The centre of \\(\\omega\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\), which is the line through the midpoint of \\(EF\\) perpendicular to \\(AP\\). Hence the distance from any point \\(X\\) to the line \\(AP\\) equals the absolute value of the projection of \\(\\overrightarrow{XO}\\) onto the direction orthogonal to \\(AP\\), where \\(O\\) is the centre of \\(\\omega\\). In particular, for \\(X=H\\),\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(H)=|HO|^{2}-R^{2}= \\bigl(\\operatorname{dist}(H,AP)\\bigr)^{2},\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(HO\\) is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs are \\(\\operatorname{dist}(H,AP)\\) and the radius \\(R\\) (the latter being the distance from \\(O\\) to the line \\(AP\\)). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Equality of powers)\n\nFor every circle \\(\\Sigma\\in\\mathcal{C}\\) that passes through \\(A\\),\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Sigma}(H)=\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(H).\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBoth \\(\\Sigma\\) and \\(\\omega\\) belong to the same coaxial pencil \\(\\mathcal{C}\\); consequently they share the same radical axis, namely the line \\(AB\\). The power of any point with respect to two circles of a coaxial pencil differs by a constant equal to the signed square of the distance from the point to the common radical axis. Since \\(H\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\) which is orthogonal to \\(AB\\) (the line joining the centres of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) is perpendicular to their radical axis), the signed distances from \\(H\\) to \\(AB\\) for the two circles are equal, and therefore their powers coincide. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the extremal circle \\(\\Sigma\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(H,AP)^{2}= \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Sigma}(H).\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma\u202f3 the right\u2011hand side equals \\(\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(H)\\). Lemma\u202f2 then gives  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(H,AP)^{2}= \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(H)=\\bigl(\\operatorname{dist}(H,AP)\\bigr)^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus the distance from \\(H\\) to the line \\(AP\\) is exactly the square root of the power of \\(H\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\); this is precisely the condition for the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) to be tangent to \\(\\omega\\).\n\nConsequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as was to be shown. \u220e"
