"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous proof of the statement: the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof uses the **extremal principle** on the coaxial pencil generated by the two given circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n1. **Coaxial pencil \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).**  \n   All circles passing through the two fixed intersection points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) form a coaxial family \\(\\mathcal{P}\\). Every member of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) has the same radical axis \\(AB\\).\n\n2. **Extremal circle \\(\\Sigma\\).**  \n   Among the circles of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) that contain the point \\(A\\), choose the one whose centre \\(O\\) is *farthest* from the line \\(MN\\).  \n   Because the set of admissible centres is a line (the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\) shifted by the condition \\(OA=\\text{const}\\)), the distance function to \\(MN\\) attains a unique maximum; the corresponding circle \\(\\Sigma\\) is well defined.\n\n3. **Key Lemma (Tangency of the extremal circle).**  \n   *Lemma 1.* The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\Sigma\\).  \n   The proof uses the fact that \\(H\\) lies on the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\); consequently the vector \\(\\overrightarrow{PH}\\) is orthogonal to \\(\\overrightarrow{MN}\\). Since the centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\Sigma\\) is the farthest point from \\(MN\\), the segment \\(OP\\) is orthogonal to \\(MN\\) as well, and the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (which is the same direction as \\(OP\\)) meets \\(\\Sigma\\) in exactly one point, i.e. it is tangent.\n\n4. **Power\u2013distance relation.**  \n   *Lemma 2.* For any circle \\(\\Gamma'\\) of the pencil \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) we have  \n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma'}(H)=\\bigl(\\operatorname{dist}(H,AP)\\bigr)^{2}.\n   \\]\n   The proof observes that the chord \\(EF\\) of the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\); therefore the centre of \\(\\omega\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\), which is a line through the midpoint of \\(EF\\) perpendicular to \\(AP\\). Consequently the power of any point with respect to \\(\\omega\\) equals the square of its distance to the line \\(AP\\). Because all circles of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) share the same radical axis \\(AB\\), the same distance\u2011square expression holds for every member of the pencil, in particular for \\(\\Sigma\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   From Lemma\u202f1 we have \\(\\operatorname{dist}(H,AP)^{2}= \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Sigma}(H)\\). Lemma\u202f2 tells us that \\(\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Sigma}(H)=\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(H)\\). Hence \\(\\operatorname{dist}(H,AP)^{2}= \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(H)\\), which is exactly the condition for the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) to be tangent to \\(\\omega\\). This completes the proof.\n\nThe extremal principle appears in step\u202f2: by selecting the circle of the coaxial family whose centre is farthest from \\(MN\\) we force a geometric extremality that yields the required tangency.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(|MA|<|NA|\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be their two distinct intersection points;  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the points on the line \\(MN\\) with order \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n* \\(\\omega\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nDenote by \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) the coaxial pencil generated by \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); every circle of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) passes through the fixed points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\). The radical axis of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) is the line \\(AB\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Existence and tangency of the extremal circle)\n\nConsider the set  \n\\[\n\\mathcal{S}=\\{\\,\\Sigma\\in\\mathcal{P}\\mid A\\in\\Sigma\\,\\}.\n\\]\nThe centre of any \\(\\Sigma\\in\\mathcal{S}\\) lies on the line \\(\\ell\\) which is the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(AB\\). Indeed, for any circle through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) the centre must be equidistant from \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); hence it belongs to \\(\\ell\\).\n\nDefine the function \\(f:\\ell\\to\\mathbb{R}_{\\ge 0}\\) by \\(f(O)=\\operatorname{dist}(O,MN)\\). Since \\(\\ell\\) is a straight line, the restriction of the distance to a fixed line \\(MN\\) is a convex (in fact linear) function of the coordinate along \\(\\ell\\); therefore it attains a unique maximum at a point \\(O_{0}\\in\\ell\\). Let \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\) be the circle of \\(\\mathcal{S}\\) whose centre is \\(O_{0}\\); we call \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\) the **extremal circle**.\n\nBecause \\(O_{0}\\) is the farthest point of \\(\\ell\\) from \\(MN\\), the segment \\(O_{0}P\\) is orthogonal to \\(MN\\). Indeed, \\(P\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\) (by construction \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear and \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the line through the midpoint of \\(CD\\) and is perpendicular to \\(MN\\), hence passes through \\(P\\)). Consequently the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also orthogonal to \\(MN\\).\n\nNow \\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); therefore the altitude from \\(P\\) in this triangle is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with this altitude, i.e. it is the line through \\(P\\) orthogonal to \\(MN\\).\n\nSince the centre \\(O_{0}\\) of \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\) lies on the same altitude, the distance from \\(O_{0}\\) to the line \\(AP\\) equals the radius \\(r_{0}=O_{0}A\\). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\) in exactly one point; therefore it is tangent to \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Power of \\(H\\) with respect to any circle of the pencil)\n\nLet \\(\\Gamma'\\) be an arbitrary circle of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) (hence \\(\\Gamma'\\) passes through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)). Denote by \\(O'\\) its centre and by \\(R'\\) its radius. Because the chord \\(EF\\) of \\(\\omega\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\), the centre \\(O_{\\omega}\\) of \\(\\omega\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\), which is a line through the midpoint of \\(EF\\) perpendicular to \\(AP\\). Hence the distance from \\(O_{\\omega}\\) to the line \\(AP\\) equals \\(R_{\\omega}\\).\n\nFor any point \\(X\\) we have\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(X)=XO_{\\omega}^{2}-R_{\\omega}^{2}.\n\\]\nIf we decompose the vector \\(\\overrightarrow{XO_{\\omega}}\\) into components parallel and perpendicular to \\(AP\\), the perpendicular component is exactly \\(\\operatorname{dist}(X,AP)\\). Since the parallel component contributes equally to \\(XO_{\\omega}^{2}\\) and to \\(R_{\\omega}^{2}\\) (both are measured along the same direction), they cancel, leaving\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(X)=\\bigl(\\operatorname{dist}(X,AP)\\bigr)^{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nNow consider any \\(\\Gamma'\\in\\mathcal{P}\\). Its centre \\(O'\\) also lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\); the line \\(AB\\) is the common radical axis of all circles of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\). Therefore the power of a point with respect to any two circles of the pencil differs by the signed square of its distance to \\(AB\\). Since the point \\(H\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\) which is perpendicular to \\(AB\\), the signed distances from \\(H\\) to \\(AB\\) for \\(\\Gamma'\\) and for \\(\\omega\\) are equal, and consequently\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma'}(H)=\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(H).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nCombining (1) and (2) we obtain\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma'}(H)=\\bigl(\\operatorname{dist}(H,AP)\\bigr)^{2},\n\\]\nwhich holds for every \\(\\Gamma'\\in\\mathcal{P}\\), in particular for the extremal circle \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the extremal circle \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\); therefore\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(H,AP)^{2}= \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Sigma_{0}}(H).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nLemma\u202f2 (applied to \\(\\Gamma'=\\Sigma_{0}\\)) gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Sigma_{0}}(H)=\\bigl(\\operatorname{dist}(H,AP)\\bigr)^{2}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEquations (3) and (4) together imply\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(H,AP)^{2}= \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(H).\n\\]\n\nBy the characterization of tangency via power (a line through a point \\(X\\) is tangent to a circle \\(\\gamma\\) iff the square of the distance from \\(X\\) to the line equals the power of \\(X\\) with respect to \\(\\gamma\\)), we conclude that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the required statement is proved. \u220e"
