h two distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), its centre must be equidistant from \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); therefore it lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\). This is correct.\n\n2. **Claim:** \u201cDefine the function \\(f:\\ell\\to\\mathbb{R}_{\\ge 0}\\) by \\(f(O)=\\operatorname{dist}(O,MN)\\). \u2026 it attains a unique maximum at a point \\(O_{0}\\in\\ell\\).\u201d  \n   *Analysis:* The distance from a moving point on a line \\(\\ell\\) to another fixed line \\(MN\\) is indeed a linear (hence convex) function of the coordinate along \\(\\ell\\). Therefore a maximum exists at one of the endpoints of the (possibly infinite) interval of \\(\\ell\\). However, \\(\\ell\\) is an infinite line, so the distance function has **no maximum** (it grows without bound in one direction). The claim of a unique maximum is false unless additional constraints (e.g., restricting to circles intersecting both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)) are imposed, which are not stated. **Critical Error.**\n\n3. **Conclusion from the (non\u2011existent) maximum:** \u201cBecause \\(O_{0}\\) is the farthest point of \\(\\ell\\) from \\(MN\\), the segment \\(O_{0}P\\) is orthogonal to \\(MN\\).\u201d  \n   *Analysis:* Even if a farthest point existed, the orthogonality would be between the line joining that point to the foot of the perpendicular from \\(\\ell\\) to \\(MN\\), not necessarily the point \\(P\\). The argument implicitly assumes \\(P\\) lies on \\(MN\\) (see next point). Since that assumption is false, this step is invalid. **Critical Error.**\n\n4. **Key false assertion:** \u201c\\(P\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\) (by construction \u2026)\u201d.  \n   *Analysis:* \\(P\\) is defined as the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). The points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on \\(MN\\), but there is no reason for the circumcenter of \\(A, C, D\\) to be on \\(MN\\). In general the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is **parallel** to \\(MN\\) (since \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are collinear with \\(M,N\\)), not perpendicular. Hence the claim that the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) is false. Consequently \\(P\\) is not on \\(MN\\). This invalidates the remainder of Lemma\u202f1. **Critical Error.**\n\n5. **Derived statement:** \u201cConsequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with this altitude, i.e. it is the line through \\(P\\) orthogonal to \\(MN\\).\u201d  \n   *Analysis:* Because the previous step is false, the identification of the line through \\(H\\) with the altitude from \\(P\\) is unjustified. **Critical Error.**\n\n6. **Final claim of Lemma\u202f1:** \u201cthe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\) in exactly one point; therefore it is tangent to \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\).\u201d  \n   *Analysis:* Even if the extremal circle \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\) existed, the tangency argument relies on the orthogonality established earlier, which is invalid. Hence the conclusion of Lemma\u202f1 is unsupported. **Critical Error.**\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f2  \n\n1. **Power formula introduction:** \u201cFor any point \\(X\\) we have \\(\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(X)=XO_{\\omega}^{2}-R_{\\omega}^{2}\\).\u201d  \n   *Justified.* This is the definition of power of a point with respect to a circle.\n\n2. **Decomposition claim leading to (1):** The solution attempts to express the power solely via the perpendicular distance to the line \\(AP\\).  \n   *Analysis:* The vector decomposition argument is flawed. The parallel component of \\(\\overrightarrow{XO_{\\omega}}\\) does **not** cancel with any part of \\(R_{\\omega}^{2}\\); the radius is a scalar, not a vector component. Consequently the equality \\(\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(X)=\\bigl(\\operatorname{dist}(X,AP)\\bigr)^{2}\\) is generally false. **Critical Error.**\n\n3. **Statement about powers in a coaxial pencil:** \u201cthe power of a point with respect to any two circles of the pencil differs by the signed square of its distance to \\(AB\\).\