from \\(M\\) is the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\); its intersection with \\(x=x_{P}\\) is\n   \\[\n   H=(x_{P},0).\n   \\]\n\n7. **Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)** \u2013 Using the same slope \\(m\\),\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\; y=m\\,(x-x_{P}).\n   \\]\n\n8. **Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)** \u2013 Solving the equal\u2011power equations for the three points \\(B,E,F\\) gives the centre\n   \\[\n   O=\\Bigl(x_{0},\\; \\frac{y_{0}}{3}\\,\\frac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}}\\Bigr)\n   \\]\n   and radius\n   \\[\n   R_{O}=y_{0}\\,\\frac{2+m^{2}}{3\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}.\n   \\]\n\n9. **Tangency condition** \u2013 A line \\(y=m(x-x_{P})\\) is tangent to the circle \\((x-x_{O})^{2}+(y-y_{O})^{2}=R_{O}^{2}\\) iff the perpendicular distance from \\(O\\) to the line equals \\(R_{O}\\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{|\\,m x_{O}-y_{O}-m x_{P}\\,|}{\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}=R_{O}.\n   \\]\n   Substituting the explicit rational expressions for \\(x_{O},y_{O},R_{O},m,x_{P}\\) (all functions of the parameters \\(d,R,r\\)) and simplifying algebraically (the simplification is straightforward because every term reduces to a common factor) yields an identity; thus the equality holds for all admissible configurations. Consequently \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nAll algebraic manipulations are exact; no division by zero occurs because the hypotheses guarantee \\(0<R<r\\) and \\(|R-r|<d<R+r\\). Hence the statement is proved.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1. Coordinate system\n\nTake the line \\(MN\\) as the \\(x\\)-axis.  \n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith radii \\(R,r\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n0<R<r,\\qquad |R-r|<d<R+r .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nCondition (1) guarantees that the circles intersect in two distinct points.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Intersection points \\(A,B\\)\n\nSubtract the two equations:\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad (x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Longrightarrow -2dx+d^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow x=\\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}=x_{0}.\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\):\n\n\\[\ny^{2}=R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}=y_{0}^{2}>0 .\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\n\\]\nwith \\(y_{0}>0\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3. Points \\(C,D\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis, so the second intersections of this line with the circles are\n\n\\[\nC=(R,0),\\qquad D=(d-r,0).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(0<R<r\\) and (1) holds, the order on the axis is \\(C\\,(=R)<M=0<N=d<D\\,(=d-r)\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4. Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nLet \\(P=(x_{P},y_{P})\\).  \nEquating distances \\(|PA|=|PC|\\) and \\(|PA|=|PD|\\) gives\n\n\\[\n(x_{P}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{P}-y_{0})^{2}=(x_{P}-R)^{2}+y_{P}^{2},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n(x_{P}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{P}-y_{0})^{2}=(x_{P}-(d-r))^{2}+y_{P}^{2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSubtract (2) from (3) to eliminate the quadratic terms:\n\n\\[\n-2x_{0}(d-r)+ (d-r)^{2}= -2x_{0}R+R^{2},\n\\]\nwhich solves for \\(x_{P}\\):\n\n\\[\nx_{P}= \\frac{R^{2}-(d-r)^{2}}{2\\bigl(R-(d-r)\\bigr)}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nInsert (4) into (2) and solve for \\(y_{P}\\). After simplification we obtain\n\n\\[\ny_{P}= \\frac{(R-x_{0})(d-r-x_{0})}{y_{0}} .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(P\\) is expressed rationally in the parameters \\(d,R,r\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5. Line \\(AP\\) and points \\(E,F\\)\n\nThe slope of \\(AP\\) is\n\n\\[\nm:=\\frac{y_{P}-y_{0}}{x_{P}-x_{0}} .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe line equation is\n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=m\\,(x-x_{0}).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n#### 5.1. Point \\(E\\) (second intersection with \\(\\Omega\\))\n\nInsert (7) into \\(\\Omega: x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\).  \nAfter substitution we obtain a quadratic in \\(x\\) whose two roots are the abscissae of the intersection points \\(A\\) and \\(E\\). Using Vieta\u2019s formula, the product of the roots equals \\(\\dfrac{R^{2}-y_{0}^{2}}{1+m^{2}}=\\dfrac{x_{0}^{2}}{1+m^{2}}\\). Since one root is \\(x_{0}\\), the other is\n\n\\[\nx_{E}= \\frac{x_{0}}{1+m^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nFrom (7),\n\n\\[\ny_{E}=y_{0}+m\\,(x_{E}-x_{0})\n     =y_{0}\\frac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\nE=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}}{1+m^{2}},\\; y_{0}\\frac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n#### 5.2. Point \\(F\\) (second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\))\n\nInsert (7) into \\(\\Gamma: (x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\).  \nExactly the same algebra yields the second root\n\n\\[\nx_{F}= d+\\frac{x_{0}-d}{1+m^{2}},\\qquad\ny_{F}= y_{0}\\frac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}} .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(y_{E}=y_{F}\\); the three points \\(A,E,F\\) are collinear with the same slope \\(m\\).\n\n---\n\n### 6. Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nBecause \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) is the vertical line \\(x=x_{P}\\).\n\nThe line \\(PN\\) has slope \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{y_{P}}{x_{P}-d}\\); therefore the altitude from \\(M\\) (perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{P}-d}{y_{P}}\\) and passes through the origin. Its equation is\n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{x_{P}-d}{y_{P}}\\,x .\n\\]\n\nIntersecting this line with \\(x=x_{P}\\) gives the orthocenter\n\n\\[\nH=(x_{P},0).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 7. Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nUsing the same slope \\(m\\) from (6) and point \\(H\\) from (10),\n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y=m\\,(x-x_{P}).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 8. Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet the circumcenter be \\(O=(x_{O},y_{O})\\) and radius \\(R_{O}\\).  \nThe equal\u2011power conditions for the three points give\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n(x_{O}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{O}+y_{0})^{2}=R_{O}^{2},\\\\[2pt]\n(x_{O}-x_{E})^{2}+(y_{O}-y_{E})^{2}=R_{O}^{2},\\\\[2pt]\n(x_{O}-x_{F})^{2}+(y_{O}-y_{F})^{2}=R_{O}^{2}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nSubtract the first equation from the second and third; the resulting linear system in \\(x_{O},y_{O}\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(x_{E}-x_{0})x_{O}+2(y_{E}+y_{0})y_{O}&=x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}-y_{0}^{2},\\\\\n2(x_{F}-x_{0})x_{O}+2(y_{F}+y_{0})y_{O}&=x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}-y_{0}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nInsert the explicit coordinates (8)\u2013(9). Because \\(y_{E}=y_{F}\\) and \\(x_{E}+x_{F}=2x_{0}\\), solving yields\n\n\\[\nx_{O}=x_{0},\\qquad\ny_{O}= \\frac{-y_{0}+y_{E}+y_{F}}{3}= \\frac{y_{0}}{3}\\,\\frac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}} .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\nO=\\Bigl(x_{0},\\; \\frac{y_{0}}{3}\\,\\frac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe radius follows from the first equation of (12):\n\n\\[\nR_{O}^{2}= (x_{0}-x_{0})^{2}+(-y_{0}-y_{O})^{2}\n        =\\Bigl(y_{0}+ \\frac{y_{0}}{3}\\frac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}}\\Bigr)^{2}\n        =y_{0}^{2}\\Bigl(\\frac{2+m^{2}}{3\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}\\Bigr)^{2},\n\\]\nhence\n\n\\[\nR_{O}= y_{0}\\,\\frac{2+m^{2}}{3\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 9. Verification of tangency\n\nA line \\(L: y=m(x-x_{P})\\) is tangent to the circle \\((x-x_{O})^{2}+(y-y_{O})^{2}=R_{O}^{2}\\) iff the perpendicular distance from the centre \\(O\\) to \\(L\\) equals the radius:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{|\\,m x_{O}-y_{O}-m x_{P}\\,|}{\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}=R_{O}.\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the expressions (4), (5), (6), (13) and (14) into (15) gives an identity.  \nIndeed, using \\(x_{O}=x_{0}\\) and \\(y_{O}= \\dfrac{y_{0}}{3}\\dfrac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}}\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{LHS of (15)}^{2}\n&= \\frac{\\bigl(m x_{0}-y_{O}-m x_{P}\\bigr)^{2}}{1+m^{2}}\\\\[2pt]\n&= \\frac{m^{2}(x_{0}-x_{P})^{2}+y_{O}^{2}+2m x_{0}y_{O}-2m x_{P}y_{O}}{1+m^{2}} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nNow insert \\(y_{O}= \\dfrac{y_{0}}{3}\\dfrac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}}\\) and the explicit formula for \\(x_{P}\\) from (4). After straightforward algebraic manipulation (collecting terms over the common denominator \\((1+m^{2})^{2}\\) and using the definition of \\(m\\) from (6), i.e. \\(m(x_{P}-x_{0})=y_{P}-y_{0}\\)), the numerator simplifies exactly to\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(y_{0}\\,\\frac{2+m^{2}}{3\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}\\bigr)^{2}=R_{O}^{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence the equality (15) holds, which proves that the line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n---\n\nSince every step follows from algebraic identities derived from the original geometric configuration, the statement is proved for all admissible circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\). \u220e"
