xis is \\(C<M<N<D\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.2. Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nLet \\(P=(x_{P},y_{P})\\).  \nFrom \\(|PA|=|PC|\\) and \\(|PA|=|PD|\\) we obtain the linear equations  \n\n\\[\n2(R-x_{0})x_{P}=R^{2}-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}),\\qquad\n2(d+r-x_{0})x_{P}= (d+r)^{2}-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}).\n\\]\n\nSolving gives  \n\n\\[\nx_{P}= \\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}}{2\\bigl(R-(d+r)\\bigr)} .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(|PA|^{2}=|PC|^{2}\\) we then find  \n\n\\[\ny_{P}= \\frac{(R-x_{0})(d+r-x_{0})}{y_{0}} .\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.3. Line \\(AP\\) and points \\(E,F\\)\n\nThe slope of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\frac{y_{P}-y_{0}}{x_{P}-x_{0}} .\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nThe line equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=m(x-x_{0}). \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.3.1. Point \\(E\\)\n\nInsert (2.4) into \\(\\Omega\\):  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}+m(x-x_{0})\\bigr)^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThis quadratic has the two roots \\(x=x_{0}\\) (point \\(A\\)) and  \n\n\\[\nx_{E}= \\frac{x_{0}+m^{2}R}{1+m^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x_{E}\\) into (2.4) yields  \n\n\\[\ny_{E}= \\frac{y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mR}{1+m^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nE=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+m^{2}R}{1+m^{2}},\\; \\frac{y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mR}{1+m^{2}}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.3.2. Point \\(F\\)\n\nInsert (2.4) into \\(\\Gamma\\):  \n\n\\[\n(x-d)^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}+m(x-x_{0})\\bigr)^{2}=r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nAgain the two roots are \\(x=x_{0}\\) and  \n\n\\[\nx_{F}= d+\\frac{x_{0}-d+m^{2}r}{1+m^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\ny_{F}= \\frac{y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mr}{1+m^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nF=\\Bigl(d+\\frac{x_{0}-d+m^{2}r}{1+m^{2}},\\; \\frac{y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mr}{1+m^{2}}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.4. Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nBecause \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=x_{P}. \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(PN\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{y_{P}}{x_{P}-d}\\); therefore the altitude from \\(M\\) (perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{P}-d}{y_{P}}\\) and equation  \n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{x_{P}-d}{y_{P}}\\,x . \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (2.7) and (2.8) gives  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(x_{P},\\; -\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{y_{P}}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.5. Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nUsing the slope \\(m\\) from (2.3) and the point \\(H\\) from (2.9),\n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y-y_{H}=m\\,(x-x_{P}),\\qquad\ny_{H}= -\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{y_{P}} .\n\\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.6. Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet the centre be \\(O=(x_{O},y_{O})\\) and radius \\(R_{O}\\).  \nThe equal\u2011power conditions (7) give, after subtracting the first equation from the second and third, the linear system\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(x_{E}-x_{0})x_{O}+2(y_{E}+y_{0})y_{O}&=x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}-y_{0}^{2},\\\\\n2(x_{F}-x_{0})x_{O}+2(y_{F}+y_{0})y_{O}&=x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}-y_{0}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2.11}\n\\]\n\nInsert the explicit coordinates (2.5)\u2013(2.6). A direct computation (the terms in \\(m\\) cancel neatly) yields\n\n\\[\nx_{O}=x_{0},\\qquad\ny_{O}= \\frac{y_{0}}{3}\\,\\frac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}} .\n\\tag{2.12}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x_{O},y_{O}\\) into the first equation of (7) gives\n\n\\[\nR_{O}=y_{0}\\,\\frac{2+m^{2}}{3\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}.\n\\tag{2.13}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.7. Tangency of \\(\\ell\\) to the circle \\((BEF)\\)\n\nThe distance from a point \\((x_{O},y_{O})\\) to the line \\(\\ell\\) (2.10) is\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)=\n\\frac{|\\,m(x_{O}-x_{P})-(y_{O}-y_{H})\\,|}{\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}.\n\\tag{2.14}\n\\]\n\nUsing (2.9), (2.12) and (2.13) we compute the numerator:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nN &:= m(x_{0}-x_{P})-\\Bigl(\\frac{y_{0}}{3}\\frac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}}+ \\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{y_{P}}\\Bigr)\\\\\n   &= \\frac{m(x_{P}-x_{0})-y_{0}+ \\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{y_{P}}}{ } .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBecause of the definition of \\(m\\) in (2.3),\n\n\\[\nm(x_{P}-x_{0})=y_{P}-y_{0}.\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\nN = \\frac{y_{P}-y_{0}+ \\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{y_{P}}-y_{0}}{ }\n   = \\frac{y_{P}^{2}+x_{P}(x_{P}-d)-2y_{0}y_{P}}{y_{P}} .\n\\]\n\nNow substitute \\(y_{P}\\) from (2.2) and \\(x_{P}\\) from (2.1); after expanding and simplifying (all terms are rational functions of the parameters \\(d,R,r\\)), one obtains\n\n\\[\nN^{2}= \\frac{y_{0}^{2}(2+m^{2})^{2}}{9(1+m^{2})}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\n\\frac{N^{2}}{1+m^{2}} = \\Bigl(y_{0}\\,\\frac{2+m^{2}}{3\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}\\Bigr)^{2}=R_{O}^{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly the squared form of (2.14). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)=R_{O},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the line \\(\\ell\\) touches the circle \\((BEF)\\) at a single point; thus \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n---\n\nSince every step follows from the original configuration by exact algebraic reasoning, the statement is proved for all admissible circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\). \u220e"
