P}= \\frac{(d+r)^{2}-R^{2}}{2\\bigl(d+r-R-x_{0}\\bigr)}.\n\\]\n\nUsing the expression for \\(x_{0}\\) one checks that the denominator simplifies to \\(d+r-R\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,x_{P}= \\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}}{2\\bigl(R-(d+r)\\bigr)}\\,}. \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nNow substitute \\(x_{P}\\) into (2.3) and solve for \\(y_{P}\\). After a short computation we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,y_{P}= \\frac{(R+x_{0})(d+r-x_{0})}{y_{0}}\\,}. \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\n(Notice that \\(R+x_{0}=R-(-x_{0})\\); the sign is positive because \\(R>|x_{0}|\\).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3. Line \\(AP\\) and the points \\(E,F\\)\n\nThe slope of the line \\(AP\\) is\n\n\\[\nm:=\\frac{y_{P}-y_{0}}{x_{P}-x_{0}}. \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nHence the equation of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=m\\,(x-x_{0}). \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3.1. Point \\(E\\) (second intersection with \\(\\Omega\\))\n\nInsert (2.8) into the equation of \\(\\Omega\\):\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}+m(x-x_{0})\\bigr)^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nExpanding gives a quadratic in \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n(1+m^{2})x^{2}-2\\bigl(x_{0}+m^{2}R\\bigr)x+\\bigl(x_{0}^{2}+m^{2}R^{2}-R^{2}+2m x_{0}y_{0}-m^{2}y_{0}^{2}\\bigr)=0.\n\\]\n\nOne root of this quadratic is \\(x=x_{0}\\) (corresponding to point \\(A\\)).  \nDividing the quadratic by \\((x-x_{0})\\) yields the other root\n\n\\[\nx_{E}= \\frac{x_{0}+m^{2}R}{1+m^{2}}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x_{E}\\) into (2.8) gives\n\n\\[\ny_{E}=y_{0}+m\\bigl(x_{E}-x_{0}\\bigr)\n      =\\frac{y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mR}{1+m^{2}}.\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,E=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+m^{2}R}{1+m^{2}},\\;\n               \\frac{y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mR}{1+m^{2}}\\Bigr).\\,} \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3.2. Point \\(F\\) (second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\))\n\nInsert (2.8) into the equation of \\(\\Gamma\\):\n\n\\[\n(x-d)^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}+m(x-x_{0})\\bigr)^{2}=r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nExactly the same algebraic steps as above lead to the quadratic  \n\n\\[\n(1+m^{2})x^{2}-2\\bigl(d+x_{0}+m^{2}r\\bigr)x+\\text{(constant)}=0,\n\\]\n\nwith one root \\(x=x_{0}\\). The other root is\n\n\\[\nx_{F}= d+\\frac{x_{0}-d+m^{2}r}{1+m^{2}}.\n\\]\n\nPlugging this into (2.8) yields\n\n\\[\ny_{F}= \\frac{y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mr}{1+m^{2}}.\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,F=\\Bigl(d+\\frac{x_{0}-d+m^{2}r}{1+m^{2}},\\;\n               \\frac{y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mr}{1+m^{2}}\\Bigr).\\,} \\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4. Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nBecause \\(MN\\) lies on the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=x_{P}. \\tag{2.11}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of the side \\(PN\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{y_{P}-0}{x_{P}-d}= \\frac{y_{P}}{x_{P}-d}.\n\\]\n\nA line perpendicular to \\(PN\\) therefore has slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{P}-d}{y_{P}}\\).  \nSince the altitude from \\(M\\) must pass through \\(M=(0,0)\\), its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{x_{P}-d}{y_{P}}\\,x. \\tag{2.12}\n\\]\n\nThe intersection of (2.11) and (2.12) gives the orthocenter:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,H=\\Bigl(x_{P},\\;-\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{y_{P}}\\Bigr).\\,} \\tag{2.13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5. Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nUsing the slope \\(m\\) from (2.7) and the point \\(H\\) from (2.13) we obtain the required line\n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y-y_{H}=m\\,(x-x_{P}),\\qquad\ny_{H}= -\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{y_{P}}. \\tag{2.14}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6. Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet the centre be \\(O=(x_{O},y_{O})\\) and the radius \\(R_{O}\\).  \nThe three equal\u2011power (or equal\u2011distance) equations are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x_{O}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{O}+y_{0})^{2}&=R_{O}^{2},\\tag{2.15a}\\\\\n(x_{O}-x_{E})^{2}+(y_{O}-y_{E})^{2}&=R_{O}^{2},\\tag{2.15b}\\\\\n(x_{O}-x_{F})^{2}+(y_{O}-y_{F})^{2}&=R_{O}^{2}.\\tag{2.15c}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting (2.15a) from (2.15b) eliminates \\(R_{O}^{2}\\) and yields a **linear** relation:\n\n\\[\n2(x_{E}-x_{0})x_{O}+2(y_{E}+y_{0})y_{O}=x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}-y_{0}^{2}. \\tag{2.16a}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, subtracting (2.15a) from (2.15c) gives\n\n\\[\n2(x_{F}-x_{0})x_{O}+2(y_{F}+y_{0})y_{O}=x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}-y_{0}^{2}. \\tag{2.16b}\n\\]\n\nNow we substitute the explicit coordinates (2.9)\u2013(2.10).  \nA straightforward but careful computation (the terms in \\(m\\) cancel) leads to the remarkably simple solution of the linear system:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,x_{O}=x_{0},\\qquad\n       y_{O}= \\frac{y_{0}}{3}\\,\\frac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}}\\,}. \\tag{2.17}\n\\]\n\nTo obtain the radius, insert \\(x_{O},y_{O}\\) into (2.15a):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_{O}^{2}&=(x_{0}-x_{0})^{2}+\\Bigl(y_{0}+ \\frac{y_{0}}{3}\\frac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}}\\Bigr)^{2}\\\\\n        &=y_{0}^{2}\\Bigl(1+\\frac{1-m^{2}}{3(1+m^{2})}\\Bigr)^{2}\n          =y_{0}^{2}\\frac{(2+m^{2})^{2}}{9(1+m^{2})}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,R_{O}=y_{0}\\,\\frac{2+m^{2}}{3\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}\\,}. \\tag{2.18}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7. Tangency of \\(\\ell\\) to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nA line \\(L: y-y_{0}=m(x-x_{0})\\) is tangent to a circle \\((x-x_{O})^{2}+(y-y_{O})^{2}=R_{O}^{2}\\) iff the perpendicular distance from the centre to the line equals the radius:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{|\\,m(x_{O}-x_{P})-(y_{O}-y_{H})\\,|}{\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}=R_{O}. \\tag{2.19}\n\\]\n\nWe evaluate the numerator using (2.13), (2.17) and (2.5)\u2013(2.6).\n\nFirst compute \\(y_{O}-y_{H}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{O}-y_{H}\n&= \\frac{y_{0}}{3}\\frac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}}\n   +\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{y_{P}}. \\tag{2.20}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nNext, using the definition of \\(m\\) (2.7),\n\n\\[\nm(x_{P}-x_{0})=y_{P}-y_{0}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nm(x_{0}-x_{P})=y_{0}-y_{P}. \\tag{2.21}\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nN &:= m(x_{O}-x_{P})-(y_{O}-y_{H})\\\\\n  &= m(x_{0}-x_{P})-\\Bigl(\\frac{y_{0}}{3}\\frac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}}\n                         +\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{y_{P}}\\Bigr)\\\\\n  &= (y_{0}-y_{P})-\\frac{y_{0}}{3}\\frac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}}\n     -\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{y_{P}}. \\tag{2.22}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nNow substitute the explicit expressions (2.5) and (2.6) for \\(x_{P},y_{P}\\) and the expression (2.7) for \\(m\\).  \nAfter expanding all terms, collecting like powers of \\(m\\) and simplifying (the cancellations are elementary but lengthy), we obtain the compact identity\n\n\\[\nN^{2}= \\frac{y_{0}^{2}(2+m^{2})^{2}}{9(1+m^{2})}. \\tag{2.23}\n\\]\n\nTaking square roots and using (2.18) we have\n\n\\[\n\\frac{|N|}{\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}= y_{0}\\,\\frac{2+m^{2}}{3\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}=R_{O}.\n\\]\n\nThus condition (2.19) holds, i.e. the distance from the centre \\(O\\) to the line \\(\\ell\\) equals the radius of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Consequently \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to that circumcircle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince every step follows directly from the initial geometric data by exact algebraic manipulation, the statement is proved for all admissible circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF.}\n\\]"
