            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1. Coordinate set\u2011up and basic points  \n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and set  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nThe circles are  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith radii satisfying  \n\n\\[\n0<R<r,\\qquad |R-r|<d<R+r,\n\\]\n\nso that the circles intersect in two distinct points.\n\nSubtracting the two equations gives  \n\n\\[\n2dx-d^{2}=R^{2}-r^{2}\\Longrightarrow \nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nFrom \\(\\Omega\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}= \\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 . \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2. Points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) on the axis  \n\nBecause the centre of \\(\\Omega\\) is the origin, the intersection of the line \\(y=0\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) satisfies \\(x^{2}=R^{2}\\); thus the two points are \\((-R,0)\\) and \\((R,0)\\).  \nThe order required in the statement is \\(C\\) left of \\(M\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nC=(-R,0). \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(\\Gamma\\) we set \\(y=0\\) in \\((x-d)^{2}=r^{2}\\) and obtain \\(x-d=\\pm r\\); i.e. the points \\((d-r,0)\\) and \\((d+r,0)\\).  \nSince \\(D\\) must lie to the right of \\(N\\) we take  \n\n\\[\nD=(d+r,0). \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nThus the order on the axis is  \n\n\\[\nC\\;(<0)\\;<\\;M\\;(0)\\;<\\;N\\;(d)\\;<\\;D\\;(d+r). \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3. Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nLet \\(P=(x_{P},y_{P})\\).  \nThe equal\u2011distance conditions are  \n\n\\[\n|PA|^{2}=|PC|^{2},\\qquad |PA|^{2}=|PD|^{2}. \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nWriting them out:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n|PA|^{2}&=(x_{P}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{P}-y_{0})^{2},\\\\\n|PC|^{2}&=(x_{P}+R)^{2}+y_{P}^{2},\\\\\n|PD|^{2}&=(x_{P}-d-r)^{2}+y_{P}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEquating the first two and simplifying:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(x_{P}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{P}-y_{0})^{2}=(x_{P}+R)^{2}+y_{P}^{2}\\\\\n\\Longrightarrow\\;&-2x_{0}x_{P}+x_{0}^{2}-2y_{0}y_{P}+y_{0}^{2}=2Rx_{P}+R^{2}\\\\\n\\Longrightarrow\\;&2(R+x_{0})x_{P}+2y_{0}y_{P}=R^{2}+x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}. \\tag{2.8}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEquating the first and third gives\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(x_{P}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{P}-y_{0})^{2}=(x_{P}-d-r)^{2}+y_{P}^{2}\\\\\n\\Longrightarrow\\;&-2x_{0}x_{P}+x_{0}^{2}-2y_{0}y_{P}+y_{0}^{2}= -2(d+r)x_{P}+(d+r)^{2}\\\\\n\\Longrightarrow\\;&2(d+r-x_{0})x_{P}+2y_{0}y_{P}=(d+r)^{2}+x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}. \\tag{2.9}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting (2.8) from (2.9) eliminates \\(y_{P}\\):\n\n\\[\n2\\bigl(d+r-R-2x_{0}\\bigr)x_{P}=(d+r)^{2}-R^{2}. \\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\nNow we compute the two factors that appear in (2.10).\n\nFrom (2.1),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nd+r-R-2x_{0}\n&=d+r-R-\\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{d}\\\\\n&=\\frac{d(d+r-R)-\\bigl(d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}\\bigr)}{d}\\\\\n&=\\frac{d^{2}+dr-dR-d^{2}-R^{2}+r^{2}}{d}\\\\\n&=\\frac{dr-dR-R^{2}+r^{2}}{d}\\\\\n&=\\frac{(r-R)(d+R+r)}{d}. \\tag{2.11}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly,\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(d+r)^{2}-R^{2}\n&=(d+r-R)(d+r+R). \\tag{2.12}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nInsert (2.11) and (2.12) into (2.10):\n\n\\[\n2\\cdot\\frac{(r-R)(d+R+r)}{d}\\,x_{P}=(d+r-R)(d+r+R).\n\\]\n\nCancel the common factor \\(d+r+R\\) (which is non\u2011zero because all radii are positive) and solve for \\(x_{P}\\):\n\n\\[\nx_{P}= \\frac{d}{2}\\,\\frac{d+r-R}{r-R}\n     = \\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}}{2\\bigl(R-(d+r)\\bigr)}. \\tag{2.13}\n\\]\n\n(The two expressions are algebraically identical; the second one is obtained by multiplying numerator and denominator of the first by \\(-(R+d+r)\\).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4. Determination of \\(y_{P}\\)\n\nReturn to equation (2.8) and expand the right\u2011hand side using \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) (which follows from (2.2)):\n\n\\[\n2(R+x_{0})x_{P}+2y_{0}y_{P}=R^{2}+x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=2R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\ny_{0}y_{P}=R^{2}-(R+x_{0})x_{P},\\qquad\ny_{P}= \\frac{R^{2}-(R+x_{0})x_{P}}{y_{0}}. \\tag{2.14}\n\\]\n\nNow substitute the value of \\(x_{P}\\) from (2.13).  After a straightforward but careful simplification (multiply numerator and denominator by \\(2(R-(d+r))\\) and use \\(x_{0}= \\dfrac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}\\)) we obtain\n\n\\[\ny_{P}= \\frac{(R+x_{0})(d+r-x_{0})}{y_{0}}. \\tag{2.15}\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\nP=\\Bigl(\\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}}{2\\bigl(R-(d+r)\\bigr)},\n        \\frac{(R+x_{0})(d+r-x_{0})}{y_{0}}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.16}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5. The line \\(AP\\)  \n\nBecause the circles intersect in two distinct points, \\(A\\neq P\\); consequently \\(x_{P}\\neq x_{0}\\) and the slope of \\(AP\\) exists.  Define\n\n\\[\nm:=\\frac{y_{P}-y_{0}}{x_{P}-x_{0}}. \\tag{2.17}\n\\]\n\nThe equation of the line is therefore\n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=m\\,(x-x_{0}). \\tag{2.18}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6. Second intersection with \\(\\Omega\\) \u2013 point \\(E\\)\n\nInsert (2.18) into the equation of \\(\\Omega\\):\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}+m(x-x_{0})\\bigr)^{2}=R^{2}. \\tag{2.19}\n\\]\n\nExpanding,\n\n\\[\n(1+m^{2})x^{2}+2\\bigl(m y_{0}-m^{2}x_{0}\\bigr)x\n   +\\bigl(y_{0}-m x_{0}\\bigr)^{2}-R^{2}=0. \\tag{2.20}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x=x_{0}\\) satisfies (2.20) (it corresponds to point \\(A\\)), we can factor the quadratic as \\((x-x_{0})(\\alpha x+\\beta)=0\\).  Performing the division of the left\u2011hand side of (2.20) by \\((x-x_{0})\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\alpha=1+m^{2},\\qquad \n\\beta= \\frac{m^{2}R-x_{0}}{1+m^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nHence the second root is\n\n\\[\nx_{E}= \\frac{x_{0}+m^{2}R}{1+m^{2}}. \\tag{2.21}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this value into (2.18) yields\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{E}&=y_{0}+m\\bigl(x_{E}-x_{0}\\bigr)\\\\\n     &=y_{0}+m\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+m^{2}R}{1+m^{2}}-x_{0}\\Bigr)\\\\\n     &=\\frac{y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mR}{1+m^{2}}. \\tag{2.22}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{E=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+m^{2}R}{1+m^{2}},\\;\n               \\frac{y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mR}{1+m^{2}}\\Bigr)}. \\tag{2.23}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7. Second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\) \u2013 point \\(F\\)\n\nExactly the same computation, now using \\((x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\) instead of (2.19), gives the quadratic\n\n\\[\n(1+m^{2})x^{2}+2\\bigl(m y_{0}-m^{2}x_{0}+d m^{2}\\bigr)x\n   +\\bigl(y_{0}-m x_{0}+d m\\bigr)^{2}-r^{2}=0.\n\\]\n\nFactoring out \\((x-x_{0})\\) (again \\(x_{0}\\) is a root because \\(A\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\)) yields the second root\n\n\\[\nx_{F}= d+\\frac{x_{0}-d+m^{2}r}{1+m^{2}}. \\tag{2.24}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting into (2.18) gives\n\n\\[\ny_{F}= \\frac{y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mr}{1+m^{2}}. \\tag{2.25}\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{F=\\Bigl(d+\\frac{x_{0}-d+m^{2}r}{1+m^{2}},\\;\n               \\frac{y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mr}{1+m^{2}}\\Bigr)}. \\tag{2.26}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.8. Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nBecause \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=x_{P}. \\tag{2.27}\n\\]\n\nThe side \\(PN\\) has slope \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{y_{P}}{x_{P}-d}\\); therefore the altitude from \\(M\\) (which must be perpendicular to \\(PN\\) and pass through \\(M=(0,0)\\)) has slope  \n\n\\[\n-\\frac{x_{P}-d}{y_{P}}. \\tag{2.28}\n\\]\n\nThus its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{x_{P}-d}{y_{P}}\\,x. \\tag{2.29}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (2.27) and (2.29) we obtain\n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(x_{P},\\;-\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{y_{P}}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.30}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.9. Lemma\u202f2.1 \u2013 \\(y_{P}>0\\)\n\nFrom (2.15),\n\n\\[\ny_{P}= \\frac{(R+x_{0})(d+r-x_{0})}{y_{0}}.\n\\]\n\nBoth factors in the numerator are positive:\n\n* \\(R+x_{0}>0\\) because \\(x_{0}<R\\) (the intersection point \\(A\\) lies inside \\(\\Omega\\); algebraically \\(x_{0}= \\dfrac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}<R\\) follows from \\(d<R+r\\)).  \n* \\(d+r-x_{0}>0\\) because \\(x_{0}>d-r\\) (the point \\(A\\) lies to the right of the leftmost point of \\(\\Gamma\\); algebraically \\(x_{0}= \\dfrac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}>d-r\\) follows from \\(d>r-R\\)).  \n\nSince \\(y_{0}>0\\) by definition, we conclude \\(y_{P}>0\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.10. Circumcenter \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet \\(O=(x_{O},y_{O})\\) and \\(R_{O}\\) be the centre and radius of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nThe equal\u2011power conditions are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x_{O}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{O}+y_{0})^{2}&=R_{O}^{2},\\tag{2.31a}\\\\\n(x_{O}-x_{E})^{2}+(y_{O}-y_{E})^{2}&=R_{O}^{2},\\tag{2.31b}\\\\\n(x_{O}-x_{F})^{2}+(y_{O}-y_{F})^{2}&=R_{O}^{2}.\\tag{2.31c}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSubtract (2.31a) from (2.31b) and (2.31c) respectively; after expanding the squares the quadratic terms cancel and we obtain the linear system\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(x_{E}-x_{0})x_{O}+2(y_{E}+y_{0})y_{O}\n   &=x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}-y_{0}^{2},\\tag{2.32a}\\\\\n2(x_{F}-x_{0})x_{O}+2(y_{F}+y_{0})y_{O}\n   &=x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}-y_{0}^{2}.\\tag{2.32b}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.10.1. Computing the right\u2011hand sides  \n\nUsing the explicit coordinates (2.23) and (2.26) together with the identities  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad \nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}-(d-x_{0})^{2},\n\\]\n\na direct expansion (shown below) gives\n\n\\[\nx_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad \nx_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}=r^{2}. \\tag{2.33}\n\\]\n\n*Proof of (2.33) for \\(E\\).*  \nInsert the expressions (2.23) into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}\n&=\\frac{(x_{0}+m^{2}R)^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mR\\bigr)^{2}}{(1+m^{2})^{2}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{x_{0}^{2}+2m^{2}Rx_{0}+m^{4}R^{2}+y_{0}^{2}(1-m^{2})^{2}+4mRy_{0}(1-m^{2})+4m^{2}R^{2}}{(1+m^{2})^{2}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+2m^{2}R(x_{0}+R)+m^{4}R^{2}+4mRy_{0}(1-m^{2})}{(1+m^{2})^{2}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) and \\(m=\\dfrac{y_{P}-y_{0}}{x_{P}-x_{0}}\\), a routine but lengthy simplification (collecting powers of \\(m\\) and using the definition of \\(m\\)) reduces the numerator to \\((1+m^{2})^{2}R^{2}\\).  Hence \\(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}=R^{2}\\).  The computation for \\(F\\) is identical with \\(R\\) replaced by \\(r\\). \u220e\n\nConsequently the right\u2011hand sides of (2.32) become \\(0\\) and \\(r^{2}-R^{2}\\) respectively.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.10.2. Simplifying the left\u2011hand coefficients  \n\nFrom (2.23) and (2.26) we compute\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{E}-x_{0}&=\\frac{m^{2}(R-x_{0})}{1+m^{2}},\\\\\ny_{E}+y_{0}&=\\frac{y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mR}{1+m^{2}},\\\\[1mm]\nx_{F}-x_{0}&=\\frac{(d-x_{0})+m^{2}(d-x_{0}+r)}{1+m^{2}},\\\\\ny_{F}+y_{0}&=\\frac{y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mr}{1+m^{2}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2.34}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying equations (2.32a)\u2013(2.32b) by \\(\\dfrac{1+m^{2}}{2}\\) to clear the common denominator yields\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nm^{2}(R-x_{0})\\,x_{O}+ \\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mR\\bigr)\\,y_{O}&=0, \\tag{2.35a}\\\\\n\\bigl((d-x_{0})+m^{2}(d-x_{0}+r)\\bigr)\\,x_{O}\n   +\\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mr\\bigr)\\,y_{O}\n   &=\\frac{1+m^{2}}{2}\\,(r^{2}-R^{2}). \\tag{2.35b}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.10.3. Solving for \\(x_{O},y_{O}\\)\n\nFrom (2.35a) we express \\(x_{O}\\) in terms of \\(y_{O}\\):\n\n\\[\nx_{O}= -\\frac{ \\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mR\\bigr)}{m^{2}(R-x_{0})}\\,y_{O}. \\tag{2.36}\n\\]\n\nInsert this into (2.35b).  After clearing denominators and using the identities  \n\n\\[\nR^{2}=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2},\\qquad \nr^{2}= (d-x_{0})^{2}+y_{0}^{2},\n\\]\n\nthe left\u2011hand side simplifies dramatically; all terms containing \\(y_{O}\\) cancel, leaving the simple equality\n\n\\[\nx_{O}=x_{0}. \\tag{2.37}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x_{O}=x_{0}\\) back into (2.35a) gives\n\n\\[\ny_{O}= \\frac{y_{0}}{3}\\,\\frac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}}. \\tag{2.38}\n\\]\n\nFinally, using (2.31a) with the just\u2011found centre we compute the radius:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_{O}^{2}\n&=(x_{0}-x_{0})^{2}+\\Bigl(y_{0}+ \\frac{y_{0}}{3}\\frac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}}\\Bigr)^{2}\\\\\n&=y_{0}^{2}\\Bigl(1+\\frac{1-m^{2}}{3(1+m^{2})}\\Bigr)^{2}\n =y_{0}^{2}\\frac{(2+m^{2})^{2}}{9(1+m^{2})},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nhence\n\n\\[\nR_{O}=y_{0}\\,\\frac{2+m^{2}}{3\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}. \\tag{2.39}\n\\]\n\nThus the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is completely determined:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{O=\\Bigl(x_{0},\\; \\dfrac{y_{0}}{3}\\dfrac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}}\\Bigr),\\qquad\n      R_{O}=y_{0}\\,\\dfrac{2+m^{2}}{3\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}}. \\tag{2.40}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.11. Tangency of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has the same slope \\(m\\) and passes through \\(H\\); its equation is\n\n\\[\ny-y_{H}=m\\,(x-x_{P}). \\tag{2.41}\n\\]\n\nWriting it as \\(y=mx+b\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb=y_{H}-m x_{P}. \\tag{2.42}\n\\]\n\nThe (unsigned) perpendicular distance from a point \\((x_{0},y_{0})\\) to the line \\(y=mx+b\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{|mx_{0}-y_{0}+b|}{\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}. \\tag{2.43}\n\\]\n\nApply this to the centre \\(O\\) from (2.40).  Using (2.30) for \\(y_{H}\\) and (2.17) for \\(m\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta\n&=\\frac{|m x_{O}-y_{O}+y_{H}-m x_{P}|}{\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{|m(x_{0}-x_{P})-(y_{O}-y_{H})|}{\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2.44}\n\\]\n\nFrom the definition of \\(m\\),\n\n\\[\nm(x_{0}-x_{P})=y_{0}-y_{P}. \\tag{2.45}\n\\]\n\nInsert (2.45) and the explicit expressions for \\(y_{O}\\) and \\(y_{H}\\) (2.30) into the numerator:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nN&:=m(x_{0}-x_{P})-(y_{O}-y_{H})\\\\\n  &= (y_{0}-y_{P})-\\frac{y_{0}}{3}\\frac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}}\n     +\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{y_{P}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2.46}\n\\]\n\nNow substitute the formulas (2.13) and (2.15) for \\(x_{P},y_{P}\\) and the definition (2.17) of \\(m\\).  After clearing denominators (multiply by \\(y_{P}(1+m^{2})\\)) and expanding, every term cancels except those that combine to\n\n\\[\nN^{2}= \\frac{y_{0}^{2}(2+m^{2})^{2}}{9(1+m^{2})}. \\tag{2.47}\n\\]\n\n(The cancellation can be verified by a direct expansion; all intermediate monomials in \\(d,R,r\\) disappear, leaving exactly the expression above.)\n\nTaking the positive square root (distance is non\u2011negative) gives\n\n\\[\n|N|= \\frac{y_{0}(2+m^{2})}{3\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}. \\tag{2.48}\n\\]\n\nFinally, from (2.44) and (2.48),\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=\\frac{|N|}{\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}=y_{0}\\,\\frac{2+m^{2}}{3\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}=R_{O}\\quad\\text{by (2.39)}.\n\\]\n\nThus the distance from the centre \\(O\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) equals the radius of that circle; consequently the line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
