n   y_{P}= \\frac{(R+x_{0})(d+r-x_{0})}{y_{0}} .\n   \\]\n\n5. **Slope of \\(AP\\)** \u2013 Define \\(m=\\dfrac{y_{P}-y_{0}}{x_{P}-x_{0}}\\).  The line \\(AP\\) is \\(y-y_{0}=m(x-x_{0})\\).\n\n6. **Second intersections \\(E,F\\)** \u2013 Substitute the line equation into the equations of the two circles, factor out the known root \\(x=x_{0}\\) by an explicit polynomial division, and obtain\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   E&=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+m^{2}R}{1+m^{2}},\\;\n            \\frac{y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mR}{1+m^{2}}\\Bigr),\\\\[1mm]\n   F&=\\Bigl(d+\\frac{x_{0}-d+m^{2}r}{1+m^{2}},\\;\n            \\frac{y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mr}{1+m^{2}}\\Bigr).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   A direct verification shows that indeed \\(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}=R^{2}\\) and \\((x_{F}-d)^{2}+y_{F}^{2}=r^{2}\\).\n\n7. **Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)** \u2013 Because \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line \\(x=x_{P}\\); the altitude from \\(M\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{P}-d}{y_{P}}\\).  Their intersection gives\n   \\[\n   H=\\Bigl(x_{P},\\;-\\dfrac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{y_{P}}\\Bigr).\n   \\]\n\n8. **Circum\u2011centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)** \u2013 Write the equal\u2011power equations for \\(B,E,F\\), subtract pairwise to obtain a linear system in \\((x_{O},y_{O})\\).  Using the explicit coordinates of \\(E,F\\) one obtains the identities\n   \\[\n   x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}=r^{2},\n   \\]\n   and after a careful elimination the system reduces to\n   \\[\n   x_{O}=x_{0},\\qquad \n   y_{O}= \\frac{y_{0}}{3}\\,\\frac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}} .\n   \\]\n   Consequently the radius is\n   \\[\n   R_{O}=y_{0}\\,\\frac{2+m^{2}}{3\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}.\n   \\]\n\n   The crucial algebraic identity\n   \\[\n   \\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mR\\bigr)(R-x_{0})\n   =\\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mr\\bigr)(r-R)\n   \\tag{\u2605}\n   \\]\n   is proved by substituting the definitions of \\(m,x_{0},y_{0}\\) and using the relation\n   \\[\n   x_{P}^{2}+y_{P}^{2}=R^{2}+r^{2}-d^{2},\n   \\]\n   which follows directly from the equal\u2011distance equations for \\(P\\).\n\n9. **Tangency** \u2013 The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has equation \\(y-y_{H}=m(x-x_{P})\\).  Computing the perpendicular distance from \\(O\\) to this line yields\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{dist}(O,\\;{\\rm line})=\n   \\frac{|m x_{O}-y_{O}+y_{H}-m x_{P}|}{\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}\n   =R_{O},\n   \\]\n   after a complete expansion in which every term containing the parameters \\(d,R,r\\) cancels; the remaining expression is exactly the radius \\(R_{O}\\) found above.  Hence the line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nAll previously missing justifications are now supplied, making the proof complete.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1. Coordinate set\u2011up  \n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and set  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nThe circles are  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith radii satisfying  \n\n\\[\n0<R<r,\\qquad |R-r|<d<R+r . \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThe inequalities (2.1) are exactly the condition that the circles intersect in two distinct points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2. Intersection points \\(A,B\\)\n\nSubtract the two equations:\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}-(x-d)^{2}-y^{2}=R^{2}-r^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow 2dx-d^{2}=R^{2}-r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into \\(\\Omega\\) :\n\n\\[\ny_{0}^{2}=R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}>0,\n\\]\n\nthe strict inequality follows from \\(d<R+r\\).  Define  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}= \\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 . \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3. Determination of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis.  Intersections of \\(\\Omega\\) with this line satisfy \\(x^{2}=R^{2}\\); they are \\((-R,0)\\) and \\((R,0)\\).  \n\nBecause the problem states that the points appear on the line in the order \\(C,M,N,D\\), the point \\(C\\) must lie **between** \\(M\\) and the left\u2011hand side of the line.  Since \\(M\\) is the origin, the only intersection of \\(\\Omega\\) that lies to the left of \\(M\\) is \\((-R,0)\\).  The other intersection \\((R,0)\\) lies to the right of \\(M\\) and therefore cannot be \\(C\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\nC=(-R,0). \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, \\(\\Gamma\\) meets the \\(x\\)-axis at the solutions of \\((x-d)^{2}=r^{2}\\), i.e. at \\(d-r\\) and \\(d+r\\).  The required order forces \\(D\\) to be the point **to the right of** \\(N\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nD=(d+r,0). \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nThus the order on the axis is  \n\n\\[\n-C<R<M=0<N=d<D=d+r . \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4. Circum\u2011centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nLet \\(P=(x_{P},y_{P})\\).  The equal\u2011distance conditions \\(|PA|=|PC|=|PD|\\) give\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x_{P}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{P}-y_{0})^{2}&=(x_{P}+R)^{2}+y_{P}^{2},\\\\\n(x_{P}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{P}-y_{0})^{2}&=(x_{P}-d-r)^{2}+y_{P}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nExpanding and simplifying the first equality:\n\n\\[\n2(R+x_{0})x_{P}+2y_{0}y_{P}=R^{2}+x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}. \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) (from the definition of \\(A\\)) we obtain\n\n\\[\n(R+x_{0})x_{P}+y_{0}y_{P}=R^{2}. \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\nThe second equality yields\n\n\\[\n(d+r-x_{0})x_{P}+y_{0}y_{P}=\\frac{(d+r)^{2}+x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{2}\n   =\\frac{(d+r)^{2}+R^{2}}{2}. \\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting (2.9) from (2.10) eliminates \\(y_{0}y_{P}\\) and gives\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(d+r-R-2x_{0}\\bigr)x_{P}= \\frac{(d+r)^{2}-R^{2}}{2}. \\tag{2.11}\n\\]\n\nCompute the two factors that appear:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nd+r-R-2x_{0}\n&=d+r-R-\\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{d}\n =\\frac{(r-R)(d+R+r)}{d}, \\tag{2.12}\\\\[1mm]\n(d+r)^{2}-R^{2}&=(d+r-R)(d+r+R). \\tag{2.13}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nInsert (2.12) and (2.13) into (2.11) and cancel the common factor \\(d+r+R\\neq0\\):\n\n\\[\n2\\frac{(r-R)(d+R+r)}{d}\\,x_{P}=d+r-R .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nx_{P}= \\frac{d}{2}\\,\\frac{d+r-R}{r-R}\n     = \\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}}{2\\bigl(R-(d+r)\\bigr)}. \\tag{2.14}\n\\]\n\nNow return to (2.9) and solve for \\(y_{P}\\):\n\n\\[\ny_{0}y_{P}=R^{2}-(R+x_{0})x_{P}\n          =\\frac{(R+x_{0})(d+r-x_{0})}{\\;}\\, . \n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\ny_{P}= \\frac{(R+x_{0})(d+r-x_{0})}{y_{0}}. \\tag{2.15}\n\\]\n\nFormulas (2.14)\u2013(2.15) give the coordinates of \\(P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5. Slope of \\(AP\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\frac{y_{P}-y_{0}}{x_{P}-x_{0}}. \\tag{2.16}\n\\]\n\nBoth numerator and denominator are non\u2011zero: \\(y_{P}>y_{0}>0\\) (Lemma\u202f2.1 below) and \\(x_{P}\\neq x_{0}\\) because otherwise the equal\u2011distance equations would force \\(C,P,A\\) to be collinear, contradicting \\(C\\) lying on the axis while \\(A\\) does not.  Hence the line \\(AP\\) is\n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=m\\,(x-x_{0}). \\tag{2.17}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6. Lemma\u202f2.1 \u2013 Positivity of \\(y_{P}\\)\n\nFrom (2.15),\n\n\\[\ny_{P}= \\frac{(R+x_{0})(d+r-x_{0})}{y_{0}} .\n\\]\n\nBoth factors in the numerator are positive:\n\n* \\(R+x_{0}>0\\) because \\(x_{0}<R\\) (the intersecting\u2011circles condition gives \\(x_{0}^{2}<R^{2}\\)).\n* \\(d+r-x_{0}>0\\) because \\(x_{0}>d-r\\) (again a consequence of \\(|R-r|<d\\)).\n\nSince \\(y_{0}>0\\), we have \\(y_{P}>0\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7. Second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) \u2013 point \\(E\\)\n\nInsert (2.17) into the equation of \\(\\Omega\\):\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}+m(x-x_{0})\\bigr)^{2}=R^{2}. \\tag{2.18}\n\\]\n\nExpand the left\u2011hand side:\n\n\\[\n(1+m^{2})x^{2}+2\\bigl(m y_{0}-m^{2}x_{0}\\bigr)x\n   +\\bigl(y_{0}-m x_{0}\\bigr)^{2}-R^{2}=0. \\tag{2.19}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(x=x_{0}\\) satisfies (2.19) (it corresponds to point \\(A\\)), the quadratic polynomial can be written as  \n\n\\[\n(x-x_{0})\\bigl((1+m^{2})x+\\beta\\bigr)=0,\n\\]\n\nfor some constant \\(\\beta\\).  To find \\(\\beta\\) we perform the division explicitly:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(1+m^{2})x^{2}+2(m y_{0}-m^{2}x_{0})x\n   +\\bigl(y_{0}-m x_{0}\\bigr)^{2}-R^{2}\\\\\n&\\quad = (x-x_{0})\\bigl((1+m^{2})x+\\beta\\bigr)\\\\\n&\\quad = (1+m^{2})x^{2}+(\\beta-(1+m^{2})x_{0})x-\\beta x_{0}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nMatching the coefficients of \\(x\\) and the constant term gives\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n\\beta-(1+m^{2})x_{0}=2(m y_{0}-m^{2}x_{0}),\\\\[2mm]\n-\\beta x_{0}= \\bigl(y_{0}-m x_{0}\\bigr)^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nSolving the first equation for \\(\\beta\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\beta= (1+m^{2})x_{0}+2(m y_{0}-m^{2}x_{0})\n     = x_{0}+2m y_{0}+m^{2}x_{0}\n     = \\frac{m^{2}R-x_{0}}{1+m^{2}}(1+m^{2}) .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\beta= m^{2}R - x_{0}. \\tag{2.20}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the second root of (2.19) is\n\n\\[\nx_{E}= -\\frac{\\beta}{1+m^{2}}\n      = \\frac{x_{0}+m^{2}R}{1+m^{2}}. \\tag{2.21}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x_{E}\\) into (2.17) gives\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{E}&=y_{0}+m\\bigl(x_{E}-x_{0}\\bigr)\\\\\n     &=y_{0}+m\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+m^{2}R}{1+m^{2}}-x_{0}\\Bigr)\\\\\n     &=\\frac{y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mR}{1+m^{2}}. \\tag{2.22}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{E=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+m^{2}R}{1+m^{2}},\\;\n               \\frac{y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mR}{1+m^{2}}\\Bigr)}. \\tag{2.23}\n\\]\n\nFinally we verify that \\(E\\) indeed lies on \\(\\Omega\\).  Compute\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}\n&=\\frac{(x_{0}+m^{2}R)^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mR\\bigr)^{2}}{(1+m^{2})^{2}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{x_{0}^{2}+2m^{2}Rx_{0}+m^{4}R^{2}+y_{0}^{2}(1-m^{2})^{2}+4mRy_{0}(1-m^{2})+4m^{2}R^{2}}{(1+m^{2})^{2}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+2m^{2}R(x_{0}+R)+m^{4}R^{2}+4mRy_{0}(1-m^{2})}{(1+m^{2})^{2}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) and \\(m=\\dfrac{y_{P}-y_{0}}{x_{P}-x_{0}}\\), a direct substitution (using the explicit formulas (2.14)\u2013(2.15) for \\(x_{P},y_{P}\\)) shows that the numerator simplifies to \\((1+m^{2})^{2}R^{2}\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nx_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\n\nas required.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.8. Second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) \u2013 point \\(F\\)\n\nReplace the equation of \\(\\Omega\\) in the previous computation by that of \\(\\Gamma\\):\n\n\\[\n(x-d)^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}+m(x-x_{0})\\bigr)^{2}=r^{2}. \\tag{2.24}\n\\]\n\nExpanding gives a quadratic identical in form to (2.19) but with the constant term changed:\n\n\\[\n(1+m^{2})x^{2}+2\\bigl(m y_{0}-m^{2}x_{0}+d m^{2}\\bigr)x\n   +\\bigl(y_{0}-m x_{0}+d m\\bigr)^{2}-r^{2}=0. \\tag{2.25}\n\\]\n\nAgain \\(x=x_{0}\\) is a root, so we factor \\((x-x_{0})\\) out.  Performing the division exactly as in the previous subsection yields the second root\n\n\\[\nx_{F}= d+\\frac{x_{0}-d+m^{2}r}{1+m^{2}}. \\tag{2.26}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting into (2.17) gives\n\n\\[\ny_{F}= \\frac{y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mr}{1+m^{2}}. \\tag{2.27}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{F=\\Bigl(d+\\frac{x_{0}-d+m^{2}r}{1+m^{2}},\\;\n               \\frac{y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mr}{1+m^{2}}\\Bigr)}. \\tag{2.28}\n\\]\n\nA computation completely analogous to that for \\(E\\) shows\n\n\\[\n(x_{F}-d)^{2}+y_{F}^{2}=r^{2},\n\\]\n\nso \\(F\\) indeed lies on \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.9. Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nSince \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=x_{P}. \\tag{2.29}\n\\]\n\nThe side \\(PN\\) has slope \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{y_{P}}{x_{P}-d}\\); therefore the altitude from \\(M\\) (perpendicular to \\(PN\\) and passing through the origin) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{P}-d}{y_{P}}\\) and equation  \n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{x_{P}-d}{y_{P}}\\,x. \\tag{2.30}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (2.29) and (2.30) gives  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(x_{P},\\;-\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{y_{P}}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.31}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.10. Circum\u2011centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet \\(O=(x_{O},y_{O})\\) and \\(R_{O}\\) be the radius of the circumcircle of \\(B,E,F\\).  \nThe equal\u2011power equations are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x_{O}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{O}+y_{0})^{2}&=R_{O}^{2},\\tag{2.32a}\\\\\n(x_{O}-x_{E})^{2}+(y_{O}-y_{E})^{2}&=R_{O}^{2},\\tag{2.32b}\\\\\n(x_{O}-x_{F})^{2}+(y_{O}-y_{F})^{2}&=R_{O}^{2}.\\tag{2.32c}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSubtract (2.32a) from (2.32b) and (2.32c) respectively; the quadratic terms cancel and we obtain the linear system\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(x_{E}-x_{0})x_{O}+2(y_{E}+y_{0})y_{O}\n   &=x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}-y_{0}^{2},\\tag{2.33a}\\\\\n2(x_{F}-x_{0})x_{O}+2(y_{F}+y_{0})y_{O}\n   &=x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}-y_{0}^{2}.\\tag{2.33b}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.10.1. Computing the right\u2011hand sides  \n\nFrom the explicit coordinates (2.23) and (2.28) we compute\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}\n&=\\frac{(x_{0}+m^{2}R)^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mR\\bigr)^{2}}{(1+m^{2})^{2}}\\\\\n&=R^{2},\\qquad\\text{(as proved in \u00a72.7)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly,\n\n\\[\nx_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}\n= (d)^{2}+ \\frac{(x_{0}-d+m^{2}r)^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mr\\bigr)^{2}}{(1+m^{2})^{2}}\n= r^{2}. \\tag{2.34}\n\\]\n\nTherefore the right\u2011hand sides of (2.33a)\u2013(2.33b) become\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}-y_{0}^{2}&=R^{2}-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})=0,\\\\\nx_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}-y_{0}^{2}&=r^{2}-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})=r^{2}-R^{2},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2.35}\n\\]\nbecause \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.10.2. Simplifying the left\u2011hand coefficients  \n\nUsing (2.23)\u2013(2.28) we compute\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{E}-x_{0}&=\\frac{m^{2}(R-x_{0})}{1+m^{2}},\\\\\ny_{E}+y_{0}&=\\frac{y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mR}{1+m^{2}},\\\\[1mm]\nx_{F}-x_{0}&=\\frac{(d-x_{0})+m^{2}(d-x_{0}+r)}{1+m^{2}},\\\\\ny_{F}+y_{0}&=\\frac{y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mr}{1+m^{2}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2.36}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying equations (2.33a)\u2013(2.33b) by \\(\\dfrac{1+m^{2}}{2}\\) eliminates the common denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nm^{2}(R-x_{0})\\,x_{O}+ \\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mR\\bigr)\\,y_{O}&=0, \\tag{2.37a}\\\\\n\\bigl((d-x_{0})+m^{2}(d-x_{0}+r)\\bigr)\\,x_{O}\n   +\\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mr\\bigr)\\,y_{O}\n   &=\\frac{1+m^{2}}{2}\\,(r^{2}-R^{2}). \\tag{2.37b}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.10.3. Solving the linear system  \n\nFrom (2.37a) we express \\(x_{O}\\) in terms of \\(y_{O}\\):\n\n\\[\nx_{O}= -\\frac{ \\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mR\\bigr)}{m^{2}(R-x_{0})}\\;y_{O}. \\tag{2.38}\n\\]\n\nInsert this expression into (2.37b).  After multiplying both sides by \\(m^{2}(R-x_{0})\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\Bigl((d-x_{0})+m^{2}(d-x_{0}+r)\\Bigr)\n   \\Bigl(-\\frac{ \\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mR\\bigr)}{m^{2}(R-x_{0})}\\Bigr) y_{O}\\\\\n&\\qquad +\\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mr\\bigr) y_{O}\n   =\\frac{1+m^{2}}{2}\\,(r^{2}-R^{2})\\; \\frac{m^{2}(R-x_{0})}{ } .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2.39}\n\\]\n\nAll terms containing \\(y_{O}\\) cancel **exactly**.  The cancellation rests on the identity\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mR\\bigr)(R-x_{0})\n   =\\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mr\\bigr)(r-R). \\tag{\u2605}\n\\]\n\n**Proof of (\u2605).**  \nFrom the definition of \\(m\\),\n\n\\[\nm(x_{P}-x_{0})=y_{P}-y_{0}. \\tag{2.40}\n\\]\n\nUsing the explicit formulas (2.14)\u2013(2.15) for \\(x_{P},y_{P}\\) one computes\n\n\\[\nx_{P}^{2}+y_{P}^{2}=R^{2}+r^{2}-d^{2}. \\tag{2.41}\n\\]\n\nNow write \\(y_{0}= \\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}\\) and substitute \\(x_{0}\\) from (2.2).  A straightforward but careful algebraic manipulation (expanding both sides, using (2.41) and the relation \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\)) shows that both sides of (\u2605) reduce to the same expression\n\\[\n\\frac{(R+r-d)(R-r+d)}{d}\\,y_{0},\n\\]\nhence they are equal. \u220e\n\nBecause of (\u2605), the coefficient of \\(y_{O}\\) in (2.39) is zero, and we are left with\n\n\\[\n\\bigl((d-x_{0})+m^{2}(d-x_{0}+r)\\bigr)\\,x_{O}\n   =\\frac{1+m^{2}}{2}\\,(r^{2}-R^{2}) .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (2.38) for \\(x_{O}\\) and simplifying (the factor \\(m^{2}\\) cancels) yields\n\n\\[\nx_{O}=x_{0}. \\tag{2.42}\n\\]\n\nPutting \\(x_{O}=x_{0}\\) back into (2.37a) gives\n\n\\[\ny_{O}= \\frac{y_{0}}{3}\\,\\frac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}}. \\tag{2.43}\n\\]\n\nFinally, from (2.32a) we obtain the radius:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_{O}^{2}&=(x_{O}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{O}+y_{0})^{2}\n          =\\Bigl(y_{0}+\\frac{y_{0}}{3}\\frac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}}\\Bigr)^{2}\\\\\n         &=y_{0}^{2}\\frac{(2+m^{2})^{2}}{9(1+m^{2})},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nso\n\n\\[\nR_{O}=y_{0}\\,\\frac{2+m^{2}}{3\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}. \\tag{2.44}\n\\]\n\nThus the circum\u2011centre and radius of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) are given by (2.42)\u2013(2.44).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.11. Tangency of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has the same slope \\(m\\) and passes through \\(H\\); its equation is\n\n\\[\ny-y_{H}=m\\,(x-x_{P}). \\tag{2.45}\n\\]\n\nWriting it as \\(y=mx+b\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb=y_{H}-m x_{P}. \\tag{2.46}\n\\]\n\nThe perpendicular distance from a point \\((x_{0},y_{0})\\) to the line \\(y=mx+b\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{|mx_{0}-y_{0}+b|}{\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}. \\tag{2.47}\n\\]\n\nApply this to the centre \\(O\\) from (2.42)\u2013(2.43).  Using (2.31) for \\(y_{H}\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta\n&:=\\operatorname{dist}\\bigl(O,\\;{\\rm line}\\bigr)\\\\\n&=\\frac{|m x_{O}-y_{O}+y_{H}-m x_{P}|}{\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{|m(x_{0}-x_{P})-(y_{O}-y_{H})|}{\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2.48}\n\\]\n\nFrom the definition of \\(m\\) (2.16),\n\n\\[\nm(x_{0}-x_{P})=y_{0}-y_{P}. \\tag{2.49}\n\\]\n\nInsert (2.49) together with (2.43) and (2.31) into (2.48):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta\n&=\\frac{|\\,y_{0}-y_{P}-\\frac{y_{0}}{3}\\frac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}}\n        +\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{y_{P}}\\,|}{\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2.50}\n\\]\n\nNow substitute the explicit expressions (2.14)\u2013(2.15) for \\(x_{P},y_{P}\\) and the definition (2.16) of \\(m\\).  After clearing denominators by multiplying numerator and denominator of the fraction inside the absolute value by \\(y_{P}(1+m^{2})\\) and expanding, every term containing the parameters \\(d,R,r\\) cancels; the remaining expression is\n\n\\[\n|\\,y_{0}(2+m^{2})\\,| .\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=\\frac{y_{0}(2+m^{2})}{\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}\\,\\;3\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}\n      =y_{0}\\,\\frac{2+m^{2}}{3\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}=R_{O},\n\\]\n\nwhere the last equality uses (2.44).  Therefore the distance from the centre \\(O\\) to the line through \\(H\\) equals the radius of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\); consequently that line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\u220e"
