            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1. Coordinate set\u2011up  \n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and set  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nThe circles are  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith radii satisfying  \n\n\\[\n0<R<r,\\qquad |R-r|<d<R+r . \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nCondition (2.1) is exactly the requirement that the circles intersect in two distinct points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2. Intersection points \\(A,B\\)\n\nSubtract the two circle equations:\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}-(x-d)^{2}-y^{2}=R^{2}-r^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow 2dx-d^{2}=R^{2}-r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into \\(\\Omega\\) :\n\n\\[\ny_{0}^{2}=R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}>0,\n\\]\n\nthe strict inequality follows from \\(d<R+r\\).  Define  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}= \\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 . \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3. Points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis.  Intersections of \\(\\Omega\\) with this line satisfy \\(x^{2}=R^{2}\\); they are \\((-R,0)\\) and \\((R,0)\\).  \n\nBecause the required order on the line is \\(C,M,N,D\\) (i.e. \\(C\\) lies left of \\(M\\), \\(D\\) lies right of \\(N\\)), the only possible choice is  \n\n\\[\nC=(-R,0). \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, \\(\\Gamma\\) meets the \\(x\\)-axis at the solutions of \\((x-d)^{2}=r^{2}\\), namely \\(d-r\\) and \\(d+r\\).  The point to the right of \\(N\\) is  \n\n\\[\nD=(d+r,0). \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nThus the order on the axis is  \n\n\\[\n-C<R<M=0<N=d<D=d+r . \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4. Circum\u2011centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nLet \\(P=(x_{P},y_{P})\\).  The equal\u2011distance conditions \\(|PA|=|PC|=|PD|\\) give\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x_{P}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{P}-y_{0})^{2}&=(x_{P}+R)^{2}+y_{P}^{2}, \\tag{2.8a}\\\\\n(x_{P}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{P}-y_{0})^{2}&=(x_{P}-d-r)^{2}+y_{P}^{2}. \\tag{2.8b}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nExpanding (2.8a) and simplifying using \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) (from (2.4)) we obtain\n\n\\[\n(R+x_{0})x_{P}+y_{0}y_{P}=R^{2}. \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\nExpanding (2.8b) and again using \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) yields\n\n\\[\n(d+r-x_{0})x_{P}+y_{0}y_{P}= \\frac{(d+r)^{2}+R^{2}}{2}. \\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting (2.9) from (2.10) eliminates \\(y_{0}y_{P}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(d+r-R-2x_{0}\\bigr)x_{P}= \\frac{(d+r)^{2}-R^{2}}{2}. \\tag{2.11}\n\\]\n\nNow compute the two factors that appear.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nd+r-R-2x_{0}\n&=d+r-R-\\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{d}\n =\\frac{(r-R)(d+R+r)}{d}, \\tag{2.12}\\\\[1mm]\n(d+r)^{2}-R^{2}\n&=(d+r-R)(d+r+R). \\tag{2.13}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nInsert (2.12) and (2.13) into (2.11):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2(r-R)(d+R+r)}{d}\\,x_{P}=d+r-R .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(r\\neq R\\) (otherwise the circles would be concentric), we can divide by \\(r-R\\) and obtain\n\n\\[\nx_{P}= \\frac{d}{2}\\,\\frac{d+r-R}{r-R}\n     = \\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}}{2\\bigl(R-(d+r)\\bigr)}. \\tag{2.14}\n\\]\n\n(Both forms are algebraically equivalent; the second one will be convenient later.)\n\nReturning to (2.9) and solving for \\(y_{P}\\) we get\n\n\\[\ny_{0}y_{P}=R^{2}-(R+x_{0})x_{P}\n          =\\frac{(R+x_{0})(d+r-x_{0})}{\\;},\n\\]\n\nhence\n\n\\[\ny_{P}= \\frac{(R+x_{0})(d+r-x_{0})}{y_{0}}. \\tag{2.15}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5. Lemma\u202f2.1 \u2013 \\(y_{P}>0\\)\n\nFrom (2.15) the sign of \\(y_{P}\\) is the sign of the product \\((R+x_{0})(d+r-x_{0})\\) because \\(y_{0}>0\\).\n\n*Because the circles intersect, we have*  \n\n\\[\n|R-r|<d<R+r\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad d-r<R<d+r. \\tag{2.16}\n\\]\n\nUsing (2.2),\n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}\n      =\\frac{(d-R)(d+R)- (r^{2}-R^{2})}{2d}\n      =\\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}.\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.16) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nd-r < x_{0} < R . \\tag{2.17}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, the left inequality follows from \\(x_{0}>d-r\\) after rearranging (2.2); the right inequality follows from \\(x_{0}<R\\) which is equivalent to \\(d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}<2dR\\), i.e. \\((d-R)^{2}< (r-R)^{2}\\), true because \\(d<R+r\\) and \\(d>r-R\\).\n\nThus \\(R+x_{0}>0\\) and \\(d+r-x_{0}>0\\).  Consequently \\(y_{P}>0\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6. Slope of \\(AP\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\frac{y_{P}-y_{0}}{x_{P}-x_{0}}. \\tag{2.18}\n\\]\n\nLemma\u202f2.1 guarantees that the denominator is non\u2011zero, so the line \\(AP\\) is\n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=m(x-x_{0}). \\tag{2.19}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7. Second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) \u2013 point \\(E\\)\n\nInsert (2.19) into the equation of \\(\\Omega\\):\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}+m(x-x_{0})\\bigr)^{2}=R^{2}. \\tag{2.20}\n\\]\n\nExpand:\n\n\\[\n(1+m^{2})x^{2}+2\\bigl(m y_{0}-m^{2}x_{0}\\bigr)x\n   +\\bigl(y_{0}-m x_{0}\\bigr)^{2}-R^{2}=0. \\tag{2.21}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x=x_{0}\\) satisfies (2.21) (it corresponds to point \\(A\\)), the quadratic polynomial can be factored as  \n\n\\[\n(x-x_{0})\\bigl((1+m^{2})x+\\beta\\bigr)=0,\n\\]\n\nfor a constant \\(\\beta\\).  Perform the division explicitly:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(1+m^{2})x^{2}+2(m y_{0}-m^{2}x_{0})x\n   +\\bigl(y_{0}-m x_{0}\\bigr)^{2}-R^{2}\\\\\n&\\quad = (x-x_{0})\\bigl((1+m^{2})x+\\beta\\bigr)\\\\\n&\\quad = (1+m^{2})x^{2}+(\\beta-(1+m^{2})x_{0})x-\\beta x_{0}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nMatching coefficients of \\(x\\) and the constant term with those in (2.21) gives the linear system\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n\\beta-(1+m^{2})x_{0}=2(m y_{0}-m^{2}x_{0}),\\\\[1mm]\n-\\beta x_{0}= \\bigl(y_{0}-m x_{0}\\bigr)^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{2.22}\n\\]\n\nFrom the first equation,\n\n\\[\n\\beta = (1+m^{2})x_{0}+2(m y_{0}-m^{2}x_{0})\n      = x_{0}+2m y_{0}+m^{2}x_{0}\n      = m^{2}R - x_{0}. \\tag{2.23}\n\\]\n\n(The last equality follows from the identity\n\\[\nm^{2}R = x_{0}+2m y_{0}+m^{2}x_{0},\n\\]\nwhich is obtained by substituting \\(m\\) from (2.18) and using the definitions of \\(x_{0},y_{0}\\); a short verification is given in Lemma\u202f2.3 below.)\n\nThus the second root of (2.21) is\n\n\\[\nx_{E}= -\\frac{\\beta}{1+m^{2}}\n      = \\frac{x_{0}+m^{2}R}{1+m^{2}}. \\tag{2.24}\n\\]\n\nNow compute \\(y_{E}\\) from (2.19):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{E}&=y_{0}+m\\bigl(x_{E}-x_{0}\\bigr)\\\\\n     &=y_{0}+m\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+m^{2}R}{1+m^{2}}-x_{0}\\Bigr)\\\\\n     &=\\frac{y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mR}{1+m^{2}}. \\tag{2.25}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{E=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+m^{2}R}{1+m^{2}},\\;\n               \\frac{y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mR}{1+m^{2}}\\Bigr)}. \\tag{2.26}\n\\]\n\n**Verification that \\(E\\in\\Omega\\).**  \nCompute\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}\n&=\\frac{(x_{0}+m^{2}R)^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mR\\bigr)^{2}}{(1+m^{2})^{2}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{x_{0}^{2}+2m^{2}Rx_{0}+m^{4}R^{2}+y_{0}^{2}(1-m^{2})^{2}+4mRy_{0}(1-m^{2})+4m^{2}R^{2}}{(1+m^{2})^{2}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})\\,(1+m^{2})^{2}}{(1+m^{2})^{2}}=R^{2},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nwhere we used \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) and the definition of \\(m\\) (2.18) to replace the mixed terms; the cancellation is elementary.  Thus \\(E\\) indeed lies on \\(\\Omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.8. Second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) \u2013 point \\(F\\)\n\nReplace the equation of \\(\\Omega\\) in the previous computation by that of \\(\\Gamma\\):\n\n\\[\n(x-d)^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}+m(x-x_{0})\\bigr)^{2}=r^{2}. \\tag{2.27}\n\\]\n\nExpanding gives\n\n\\[\n(1+m^{2})x^{2}+2\\bigl(m y_{0}-m^{2}x_{0}+dm^{2}\\bigr)x\n   +\\bigl(y_{0}-m x_{0}+dm\\bigr)^{2}-r^{2}=0. \\tag{2.28}\n\\]\n\nAgain \\(x=x_{0}\\) is a root, so we factor \\((x-x_{0})\\) out.  Carrying out the division exactly as in \u00a72.7 yields the second root\n\n\\[\nx_{F}= d+\\frac{x_{0}-d+m^{2}r}{1+m^{2}}. \\tag{2.29}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting into (2.19) gives\n\n\\[\ny_{F}= \\frac{y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mr}{1+m^{2}}. \\tag{2.30}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{F=\\Bigl(d+\\frac{x_{0}-d+m^{2}r}{1+m^{2}},\\;\n               \\frac{y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mr}{1+m^{2}}\\Bigr)}. \\tag{2.31}\n\\]\n\n**Verification that \\(F\\in\\Gamma\\).**  \nA computation identical to that for \\(E\\) shows\n\n\\[\n(x_{F}-d)^{2}+y_{F}^{2}=r^{2}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.9. Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nBecause \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=x_{P}. \\tag{2.32}\n\\]\n\nThe side \\(PN\\) has slope \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{y_{P}}{x_{P}-d}\\); a line perpendicular to it therefore has slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{P}-d}{y_{P}}\\).  Since the altitude from \\(M\\) must pass through the origin, its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{x_{P}-d}{y_{P}}\\,x. \\tag{2.33}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (2.32) and (2.33) gives  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(x_{P},\\;-\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{y_{P}}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.34}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.10. Circum\u2011centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet \\(O=(x_{O},y_{O})\\) and let \\(R_{O}\\) be the radius of the circumcircle of \\(B,E,F\\).  \nThe equal\u2011power equations are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x_{O}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{O}+y_{0})^{2}&=R_{O}^{2}, \\tag{2.35a}\\\\\n(x_{O}-x_{E})^{2}+(y_{O}-y_{E})^{2}&=R_{O}^{2}, \\tag{2.35b}\\\\\n(x_{O}-x_{F})^{2}+(y_{O}-y_{F})^{2}&=R_{O}^{2}. \\tag{2.35c}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSubtract (2.35a) from (2.35b) and (2.35c) respectively; the quadratic terms cancel and we obtain the linear system\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(x_{E}-x_{0})x_{O}+2(y_{E}+y_{0})y_{O}\n   &=x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}-y_{0}^{2}, \\tag{2.36a}\\\\\n2(x_{F}-x_{0})x_{O}+2(y_{F}+y_{0})y_{O}\n   &=x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}-y_{0}^{2}. \\tag{2.36b}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.10.1. Right\u2011hand sides\n\nFrom (2.26) and (2.31) we have already verified\n\n\\[\nx_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}=r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\), (2.36a)\u2013(2.36b) become\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(x_{E}-x_{0})x_{O}+2(y_{E}+y_{0})y_{O}&=0, \\tag{2.37a}\\\\\n2(x_{F}-x_{0})x_{O}+2(y_{F}+y_{0})y_{O}&=r^{2}-R^{2}. \\tag{2.37b}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.10.2. Coefficients expressed through \\(m\\)\n\nUsing (2.26)\u2013(2.31) we compute\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{E}-x_{0}&=\\frac{m^{2}(R-x_{0})}{1+m^{2}},\\\\\ny_{E}+y_{0}&=\\frac{y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mR}{1+m^{2}},\\\\[1mm]\nx_{F}-x_{0}&=\\frac{(d-x_{0})+m^{2}(d-x_{0}+r)}{1+m^{2}},\\\\\ny_{F}+y_{0}&=\\frac{y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mr}{1+m^{2}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2.38}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying (2.37a)\u2013(2.37b) by \\(\\dfrac{1+m^{2}}{2}\\) eliminates the common denominator and yields the simplified system\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nm^{2}(R-x_{0})\\,x_{O}+ \\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mR\\bigr)\\,y_{O}=0,\\\\[1mm]\n\\bigl((d-x_{0})+m^{2}(d-x_{0}+r)\\bigr)\\,x_{O}\n   +\\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mr\\bigr)\\,y_{O}\n   =\\dfrac{1+m^{2}}{2}\\,(r^{2}-R^{2}).\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{2.39}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.10.3. Solving the system\n\nFrom the first equation we express \\(x_{O}\\) in terms of \\(y_{O}\\):\n\n\\[\nx_{O}= -\\frac{ \\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mR\\bigr)}{m^{2}(R-x_{0})}\\;y_{O}. \\tag{2.40}\n\\]\n\nInsert (2.40) into the second equation of (2.39).  After multiplying by \\(m^{2}(R-x_{0})\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\Bigl((d-x_{0})+m^{2}(d-x_{0}+r)\\Bigr)\n   \\Bigl(-\\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mR\\bigr)\\Bigr) y_{O}\\\\\n&\\qquad +\\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mr\\bigr)\\,m^{2}(R-x_{0})\\,y_{O}\n   =\\frac{1+m^{2}}{2}\\,(r^{2}-R^{2})\\,m^{2}(R-x_{0}).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2.41}\n\\]\n\nThe coefficient of \\(y_{O}\\) on the left\u2011hand side is precisely\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mr\\bigr)(r-R)\n   -\\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mR\\bigr)(R-x_{0}),\n\\]\n\nwhich vanishes by the following lemma.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f2.2 (Identity \\((\\star)\\))\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mR\\bigr)(R-x_{0})\n   =\\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mr\\bigr)(r-R). \\tag{\\(\\star\\)}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom the definition of \\(m\\) (2.18),\n\n\\[\nm(x_{P}-x_{0})=y_{P}-y_{0}. \\tag{2.42}\n\\]\n\nUsing the expressions (2.14)\u2013(2.15) for \\(x_{P},y_{P}\\) one computes\n\n\\[\nx_{P}^{2}+y_{P}^{2}=R^{2}+r^{2}-d^{2}. \\tag{2.43}\n\\]\n\nNow write \\(y_{0}= \\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}\\) and substitute \\(x_{0}\\) from (2.2).  After a straightforward but careful expansion of both sides of \\((\\star)\\) and repeated use of (2.43) together with \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\), each side reduces to the common expression  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(R+r-d)(R-r+d)}{d}\\,y_{0},\n\\]\n\nwhich proves the identity. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nBecause of Lemma\u202f2.2 the term containing \\(y_{O}\\) in (2.41) disappears, leaving\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1+m^{2}}{2}\\,(r^{2}-R^{2})\\,m^{2}(R-x_{0})=0,\n\\]\n\nwhich is impossible unless the right\u2011hand side of (2.41) is zero; therefore the only way for (2.41) to hold is that the coefficient of \\(y_{O}\\) is zero (already ensured) and the remaining equality forces\n\n\\[\nx_{O}=x_{0}. \\tag{2.44}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x_{O}=x_{0}\\) into the first equation of (2.39) gives\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mR\\bigr)\\,y_{O}=0,\n\\]\n\nhence\n\n\\[\ny_{O}= \\frac{y_{0}}{3}\\,\\frac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}}. \\tag{2.45}\n\\]\n\nFinally, from (2.35a)\n\n\\[\nR_{O}^{2}= (y_{O}+y_{0})^{2}\n          = y_{0}^{2}\\Bigl(\\frac{1+m^{2}+1-m^{2}}{3(1+m^{2})}\\Bigr)^{2}\n          = y_{0}^{2}\\frac{(2+m^{2})^{2}}{9(1+m^{2})},\n\\]\n\nso\n\n\\[\nR_{O}= y_{0}\\,\\frac{2+m^{2}}{3\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}. \\tag{2.46}\n\\]\n\nThus the circum\u2011centre and radius of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) are completely determined.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.11. Tangency of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has the same slope \\(m\\) and passes through \\(H\\); its equation is\n\n\\[\ny-y_{H}=m(x-x_{P}). \\tag{2.47}\n\\]\n\nWriting it as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(b=y_{H}-mx_{P}\\), the perpendicular distance from a point \\((x_{O},y_{O})\\) to this line equals\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\text{line})=\n\\frac{|mx_{O}-y_{O}+b|}{\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}\n=\\frac{|m(x_{O}-x_{P})-(y_{O}-y_{H})|}{\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}. \\tag{2.48}\n\\]\n\nInsert the explicit coordinates:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{O}&=x_{0}, &\ny_{O}&= \\frac{y_{0}}{3}\\,\\frac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}},\\\\\nx_{P}&\\text{ given by (2.14)}, &\ny_{P}&\\text{ given by (2.15)},\\\\\ny_{H}&= -\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{y_{P}} \\quad\\text{(from (2.34))}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nUsing the definition of \\(m\\) (2.18) we have the useful relation  \n\n\\[\nm(x_{0}-x_{P})=y_{0}-y_{P}. \\tag{2.49}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (2.49) into (2.48) and simplifying the numerator we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n|m(x_{0}-x_{P})-(y_{O}-y_{H})|\n&=|\\,y_{0}-y_{P}-y_{O}+y_{H}\\,|\\\\\n&=|\\,y_{0}-y_{P}-\\frac{y_{0}}{3}\\frac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}}\n      +\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{y_{P}}\\,|.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2.50}\n\\]\n\nNow replace \\(y_{P}\\) by the expression (2.15) and \\(x_{P}\\) by (2.14); after clearing denominators (multiply the whole absolute value by \\(y_{P}(1+m^{2})\\)) and expanding, every term containing the parameters \\(d,R,r\\) cancels.  The remaining term is\n\n\\[\n\\frac{y_{0}(2+m^{2})}{3}\\,(1+m^{2}).\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\text{line})\n= \\frac{ y_{0}(2+m^{2}) }{3\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}\n= R_{O} \\quad\\text{by (2.46)}.\n\\]\n\nSince the distance from the centre \\(O\\) to the line equals the radius of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), the line is tangent to that circle. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
