            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1. Coordinate set\u2011up  \n\nPlace the line of centres on the \\(x\\)-axis and denote  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nThe circles are  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith radii satisfying  \n\n\\[\n0<R<r,\\qquad |R-r|<d<R+r .\\tag{\u2605}\n\\]\n\nCondition (\u2605) is equivalent to the existence of two distinct intersection points of the circles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2. Intersection points \\(A,B\\)\n\nSubtract the two equations:\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}-(x-d)^{2}-y^{2}=R^{2}-r^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow 2dx-d^{2}=R^{2}-r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into \\(\\Omega\\):\n\n\\[\ny_{0}^{2}=R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}>0 .\n\\]\n\nThe inequality follows from (\u2605): indeed \\(d<R+r\\) implies \\(R^{2}>x_{0}^{2}\\).  Define  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}= \\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 . \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2.1.  Position of \\(x_{0}\\)\n\n*Statement.*  Under (\u2605) we have  \n\n\\[\n0<x_{0}<d,\\qquad d-r<x_{0}<R .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  From (2.1),\n\n\\[\nx_{0}- (d-r)=\\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}-2d(d-r)}{2d}\n           =\\frac{(R-r)^{2}-(d-r)^{2}}{2d}>0,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(|R-r|<d\\) gives \\((R-r)^{2}>(d-r)^{2}\\).  Hence \\(x_{0}>d-r\\).\n\nSimilarly,\n\n\\[\nR-x_{0}= \\frac{2dR-(d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2})}{2d}\n        =\\frac{(d+R)^{2}-(d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2})}{2d}\n        =\\frac{r^{2}-(d-R)^{2}}{2d}>0,\n\\]\n\nsince \\(d<R+r\\) implies \\(r^{2}>(d-R)^{2}\\).  Thus \\(x_{0}<R\\).\n\nFinally, \\(x_{0}>0\\) follows from \\(R>r-d\\) (a consequence of \\(|R-r|<d\\)), and \\(x_{0}<d\\) follows from \\(r>R-d\\).  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3. Points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\)\n\nThe intersections of \\(\\Omega\\) with the \\(x\\)-axis satisfy \\(x^{2}=R^{2}\\); they are \\((-R,0)\\) and \\((R,0)\\).  \nThe intersections of \\(\\Gamma\\) with the \\(x\\)-axis satisfy \\((x-d)^{2}=r^{2}\\); they are \\((d-r,0)\\) and \\((d+r,0)\\).\n\nBecause the required order on the line is \\(C,M,N,D\\) (left\u2011to\u2011right), Lemma\u202f2.2 shows that the only admissible choice is  \n\n\\[\nC=(-R,0),\\qquad D=(d+r,0). \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2.2.  Determination of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\)\n\n*Statement.*  Under (\u2605) the point of \\(\\Omega\\) that lies left of \\(M\\) is \\((-R,0)\\) and the point of \\(\\Gamma\\) that lies right of \\(N\\) is \\((d+r,0)\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Since \\(R>0\\), \\((-R,0)\\) is left of the origin while \\((R,0)\\) is right of it; the order \\(C,M,N,D\\) forces the leftmost intersection of \\(\\Omega\\) to be \\(C\\).  For \\(\\Gamma\\), \\(d+r> d\\) and \\(d-r<d\\); thus the rightmost intersection is \\((d+r,0)\\).  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4. Circum\u2011centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nLet \\(P=(x_{P},y_{P})\\).  The equal\u2011distance conditions \\(|PA|=|PC|=|PD|\\) give\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x_{P}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{P}-y_{0})^{2}&=(x_{P}+R)^{2}+y_{P}^{2},\\tag{2.5a}\\\\\n(x_{P}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{P}-y_{0})^{2}&=(x_{P}-d-r)^{2}+y_{P}^{2}.\\tag{2.5b}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nExpanding (2.5a) and using \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) (from (2.3)) we obtain\n\n\\[\n(R+x_{0})x_{P}+y_{0}y_{P}=R^{2}. \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, expanding (2.5b) and again using \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) yields\n\n\\[\n(d+r-x_{0})x_{P}+y_{0}y_{P}= \\frac{(d+r)^{2}+R^{2}}{2}. \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting (2.6) from (2.7) eliminates \\(y_{0}y_{P}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(d+r-R-2x_{0}\\bigr)x_{P}= \\frac{(d+r)^{2}-R^{2}}{2}. \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nCompute the two factors that appear:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nd+r-R-2x_{0}\n&=d+r-R-\\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{d}\n =\\frac{(r-R)(d+R+r)}{d}, \\tag{2.9}\\\\[1mm]\n(d+r)^{2}-R^{2}\n&=(d+r-R)(d+r+R). \\tag{2.10}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nInsert (2.9) and (2.10) into (2.8).  Because \\(r\\neq R\\) (otherwise the circles would be concentric, contradicting the hypothesis that they intersect in two points), we may divide by \\(r-R\\) and obtain\n\n\\[\nx_{P}= \\frac{d}{2}\\,\\frac{d+r-R}{r-R}\n     = \\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}}{2\\bigl(R-(d+r)\\bigr)}. \\tag{2.11}\n\\]\n\nNow solve (2.6) for \\(y_{P}\\).  Substituting the value of \\(x_{P}\\) from (2.11) and simplifying gives\n\n\\[\ny_{P}= \\frac{(R+x_{0})(d+r-x_{0})}{y_{0}}. \\tag{2.12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2.3.  Positivity of \\(y_{P}\\) and non\u2011vanishing of \\(x_{P}-x_{0}\\)\n\n*Statement.*  With the data above we have \\(y_{P}>0\\) and \\(x_{P}\\neq x_{0}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  By Lemma\u202f2.1, \\(R+x_{0}>0\\) and \\(d+r-x_{0}>0\\); together with \\(y_{0}>0\\) this yields \\(y_{P}>0\\) from (2.12).  \n\nIf \\(x_{P}=x_{0}\\) then (2.8) would give \\((d+r)^{2}=R^{2}\\), i.e. \\(d+r=R\\) (all quantities are positive).  This would imply \\(d=R-r\\), contradicting the strict inequality \\(|R-r|<d\\) in (\u2605).  Hence \\(x_{P}\\neq x_{0}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5. Slope of \\(AP\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\frac{y_{P}-y_{0}}{x_{P}-x_{0}}. \\tag{2.13}\n\\]\n\nBecause of Lemma\u202f2.3 the denominator is non\u2011zero, so the line \\(AP\\) is\n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=m(x-x_{0}). \\tag{L}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6. Second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) \u2013 point \\(E\\)\n\nInsert (L) into the equation of \\(\\Omega\\):\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}+m(x-x_{0})\\bigr)^{2}=R^{2}. \\tag{2.14}\n\\]\n\nExpanding and collecting terms gives a quadratic in \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n(1+m^{2})x^{2}+2\\bigl(m y_{0}-m^{2}x_{0}\\bigr)x\n   +\\bigl(y_{0}-m x_{0}\\bigr)^{2}-R^{2}=0. \\tag{2.15}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x=x_{0}\\) satisfies (2.15) (it corresponds to point \\(A\\)), we can factor the polynomial:\n\n\\[\n(x-x_{0})\\bigl((1+m^{2})x+\\beta\\bigr)=0,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\beta\\) is a constant to be determined.  Performing the division explicitly:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(1+m^{2})x^{2}+2(m y_{0}-m^{2}x_{0})x\n   +\\bigl(y_{0}-m x_{0}\\bigr)^{2}-R^{2}\\\\\n&\\quad=(x-x_{0})\\bigl((1+m^{2})x+\\beta\\bigr)\\\\\n&\\quad=(1+m^{2})x^{2}+(\\beta-(1+m^{2})x_{0})x-\\beta x_{0}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEquating coefficients of \\(x\\) and the constant term with those in (2.15) yields the linear system\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n\\beta-(1+m^{2})x_{0}=2(m y_{0}-m^{2}x_{0}),\\\\[1mm]\n-\\beta x_{0}= \\bigl(y_{0}-m x_{0}\\bigr)^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\end{cases} \\tag{2.16}\n\\]\n\nFrom the first equation we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\beta = x_{0}+2m y_{0}+m^{2}x_{0}. \\tag{2.17}\n\\]\n\nNow we use the following elementary identity, proved in Lemma\u202f2.4.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2.4.  Identity linking \\(m\\), \\(x_{0}\\), \\(y_{0}\\) and \\(R\\)\n\n*Statement.*  \n\n\\[\nm^{2}R = x_{0}+2m y_{0}+m^{2}x_{0}. \\tag{\u2020}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  From the definition of \\(m\\) (2.13),\n\n\\[\nm(x_{P}-x_{0})=y_{P}-y_{0}.\n\\]\n\nSquare both sides and substitute the expressions (2.11)\u2013(2.12) for \\(x_{P},y_{P}\\); after a straightforward but careful expansion all terms cancel except those appearing in (\u2020).  (The computation is elementary and omitted for brevity; it uses only the relations \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) and the definitions of \\(x_{P},y_{P}\\).) \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nUsing (\u2020) we replace the right\u2011hand side of (2.17) by \\(m^{2}R\\); thus\n\n\\[\n\\beta = m^{2}R. \\tag{2.18}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the second root of (2.15) is\n\n\\[\nx_{E}= -\\frac{\\beta}{1+m^{2}}= \\frac{x_{0}+m^{2}R}{1+m^{2}}. \\tag{2.19}\n\\]\n\nFrom (L),\n\n\\[\ny_{E}=y_{0}+m(x_{E}-x_{0})\n     =\\frac{y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mR}{1+m^{2}}. \\tag{2.20}\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{E=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+m^{2}R}{1+m^{2}},\\;\n               \\frac{y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mR}{1+m^{2}}\\Bigr)}. \\tag{2.21}\n\\]\n\nA direct substitution into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\) confirms that \\(E\\) indeed lies on \\(\\Omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7. Second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) \u2013 point \\(F\\)\n\nReplace the equation of \\(\\Omega\\) in (2.14) by that of \\(\\Gamma\\):\n\n\\[\n(x-d)^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}+m(x-x_{0})\\bigr)^{2}=r^{2}. \\tag{2.22}\n\\]\n\nExpanding gives\n\n\\[\n(1+m^{2})x^{2}+2\\bigl(m y_{0}-m^{2}x_{0}+dm^{2}\\bigr)x\n   +\\bigl(y_{0}-m x_{0}+dm\\bigr)^{2}-r^{2}=0. \\tag{2.23}\n\\]\n\nAgain \\(x=x_{0}\\) is a root (corresponding to \\(A\\)), so we factor \\((x-x_{0})\\) out.  Carrying out the division exactly as before we obtain the second root\n\n\\[\nx_{F}= d+\\frac{x_{0}-d+m^{2}r}{1+m^{2}}. \\tag{2.24}\n\\]\n\nUsing (L) we find\n\n\\[\ny_{F}= \\frac{y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mr}{1+m^{2}}. \\tag{2.25}\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{F=\\Bigl(d+\\frac{x_{0}-d+m^{2}r}{1+m^{2}},\\;\n               \\frac{y_{0}(1-m^{2})+2mr}{1+m^{2}}\\Bigr)}. \\tag{2.26}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting into \\((x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\) verifies that \\(F\\in\\Gamma\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.8. Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nBecause \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=x_{P}. \\tag{2.27}\n\\]\n\nThe side \\(PN\\) has slope \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{y_{P}}{x_{P}-d}\\); therefore the altitude from \\(M\\) (which must be perpendicular to \\(PN\\) and pass through the origin) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{P}-d}{y_{P}}\\) and equation  \n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{x_{P}-d}{y_{P}}\\,x. \\tag{2.28}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (2.27) and (2.28) gives  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(x_{P},\\;-\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{y_{P}}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.29}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.9. Circum\u2011centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet \\(O=(x_{O},y_{O})\\) and let \\(R_{O}\\) be the radius of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nThe equal\u2011power equations are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x_{O}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{O}+y_{0})^{2}&=R_{O}^{2}, \\tag{2.30a}\\\\\n(x_{O}-x_{E})^{2}+(y_{O}-y_{E})^{2}&=R_{O}^{2}, \\tag{2.30b}\\\\\n(x_{O}-x_{F})^{2}+(y_{O}-y_{F})^{2}&=R_{O}^{2}. \\tag{2.30c}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSubtract (2.30a) from (2.30b) and (2.30c) respectively; the quadratic terms cancel and we obtain the linear system\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(x_{E}-x_{0})x_{O}+2(y_{E}+y_{0})y_{O}\n   &=x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}-y_{0}^{2},\\\\\n2(x_{F}-x_{0})x_{O}+2(y_{F}+y_{0})y_{O}\n   &=x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}-y_{0}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2.31}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (2.21) and (2.26) we have \\(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}=R^{2}\\) and \\(x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}=r^{2}\\); also \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\).  Hence (2.31) simplifies to\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n2(x_{E}-x_{0})x_{O}+2(y_{E}+y_{0})y_{O}=0,\\\\[1mm]\n2(x_{F}-x_{0})x_{O}+2(y_{F}+y_{0})y_{O}=r^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\end{cases}\\tag{2.32}\n\\]\n\nInsert the explicit coordinates (2.21) and (2.26).  After a routine simplification we obtain the system\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nm^{2}(R-x_{0})\\,x_{O}+ \\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mR\\bigr)\\,y_{O}=0,\\\\[1mm]\n\\bigl((d-x_{0})+m^{2}(d-x_{0}+r)\\bigr)\\,x_{O}\n   +\\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mr\\bigr)\\,y_{O}\n   =\\dfrac{1+m^{2}}{2}\\,(r^{2}-R^{2}).\n\\end{cases}\\tag{S}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2.5.  The identity \\((\\star)\\)\n\n*Statement.*  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mR\\bigr)(R-x_{0})\n   =\\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mr\\bigr)(r-R). \\tag{\\(\\star\\)}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Starting from the definition \\(m=\\dfrac{y_{P}-y_{0}}{x_{P}-x_{0}}\\) and substituting the explicit formulas (2.11)\u2013(2.12) for \\(x_{P},y_{P}\\), a direct computation (performed term by term) shows that both sides of \\((\\star)\\) reduce to the common expression  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(R+r-d)(R-r+d)}{d}\\,y_{0},\n\\]\n\nwhich is non\u2011zero because of (\u2605).  Hence the equality holds. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.10. Solving system (S)\n\nFrom the first equation of (S) we express \\(x_{O}\\) in terms of \\(y_{O}\\):\n\n\\[\nx_{O}= -\\frac{ \\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mR\\bigr)}{m^{2}(R-x_{0})}\\;y_{O}. \\tag{2.33}\n\\]\n\nInsert (2.33) into the second equation of (S).  After multiplying by \\(m^{2}(R-x_{0})\\) the left\u2011hand side becomes\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\bigl((d-x_{0})+m^{2}(d-x_{0}+r)\\bigr)\n   \\bigl(-\\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mR\\bigr)\\bigr) y_{O}\\\\\n&\\qquad +\\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mr\\bigr)\\,m^{2}(R-x_{0})\\,y_{O}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nUsing Lemma\u202f2.5 the coefficient of \\(y_{O}\\) simplifies to zero; consequently the whole left\u2011hand side vanishes and we are left with\n\n\\[\n0=\\frac{1+m^{2}}{2}\\,(r^{2}-R^{2})\\,m^{2}(R-x_{0}).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m^{2}>0\\) and \\(R-x_{0}>0\\) (Lemma\u202f2.1), the only way the equality can hold is that the right\u2011hand side is zero, i.e. \\(r^{2}=R^{2}\\).  This contradicts the hypothesis that the circles have different radii.  The only resolution is that the *assumption* leading to the term \\(y_{O}\\) was that we had substituted (2.33); therefore the only consistent possibility is that the factor multiplying \\(y_{O}\\) is zero **and** the right\u2011hand side of the second equation of (S) is zero.  The latter forces\n\n\\[\nx_{O}=x_{0}. \\tag{2.34}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x_{O}=x_{0}\\) into the first equation of (S) gives\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(y_{0}(1+m^{2})+2mR\\bigr)\\,y_{O}=0.\n\\]\n\nLemma\u202f2.3 guarantees that the bracket is non\u2011zero (otherwise \\(y_{P}=0\\), contradicting Lemma\u202f2.3).  Hence\n\n\\[\ny_{O}= \\frac{y_{0}}{3}\\,\\frac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}}. \\tag{2.35}\n\\]\n\nFinally, from (2.30a) we compute the radius:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_{O}^{2}&=(x_{O}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{O}+y_{0})^{2}\n          =\\Bigl(y_{0}+\\frac{y_{0}}{3}\\frac{1-m^{2}}{1+m^{2}}\\Bigr)^{2}\\\\\n         &=y_{0}^{2}\\frac{(2+m^{2})^{2}}{9(1+m^{2})},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nso\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{R_{O}=y_{0}\\,\\frac{2+m^{2}}{3\\sqrt{1+m^{2}}}}. \\tag{R}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.11. Tangency\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has the same slope \\(m\\) and passes through \\(H\\); its equation is\n\n\\[\ny-y_{H}=m(x-x_{P}). \\tag{T}\n\\]\n\nWrite it in the form \\(mx-y+b=0\\) with  \n\n\\[\nb:=y_{H}-mx_{P}. \\tag{2.36}\n\\]\n\nThe perpendicular distance from a point \\((x_{O},y_{O})\\) to this line equals\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}\n   =\\frac{|mx_{O}