or each \\(k\\ge 0\\), let \\(X_{k+2}\\) be the second intersection of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle A X_k X_{k+1}\\) with the line \\(MN\\).\n\nWe claim that for every \\(k\\ge 0\\)\n\\[\n\\angle X_k A X_{k+1}= \\angle BEF .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n**Base case \\(k=0\\).**  \nThe circle \\((A X_0 X_1)=(A M N)\\) is precisely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle AMN\\).  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\) and \\(C,D\\) lie on \\(MN\\), the line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\).  \nConsequently \\(\\angle M A N\\) equals the angle subtended by chord \\(MN\\) at the point \\(A\\), which is exactly \\(\\angle BEF\\) because \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the second intersections of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nThus (4) holds for \\(k=0\\).\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume (4) holds for some \\(k\\ge 0\\); i.e. \\(\\angle X_k A X_{k+1}= \\angle BEF\\).  \nConsider the circle \\(\\gamma_k=(A X_k X_{k+1})\\).  \nBy construction, \\(X_{k+2}\\) lies on \\(\\gamma_k\\) and on the line \\(MN\\).  \nHence the quadrilateral \\(A X_k X_{k+1} X_{k+2}\\) is cyclic.  \nFrom the cyclicity we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle X_{k+1} A X_{k+2}= \\angle X_k A X_{k+1}.\n\\]\nApplying the induction hypothesis to the right\u2011hand side yields\n\\[\n\\angle X_{k+1} A X_{k+2}= \\angle BEF .\n\\]\nThus (4) holds for \\(k+1\\), completing the induction.\n\nTherefore Lemma\u202f2 is proved.\n\n---\n\n### Completion of the Proof\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(H)=\\overline{HT}^{\\,2},\n\\]\nwhere \\(T\\) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(H\\) onto the line \\(L\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nA classical characterization of tangency states that a line through a point \\(X\\) is tangent to a circle \\(\\omega\\) iff the distance from \\(X\\) to the line equals the square root of the power of \\(X\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\).  \nHence the equality above is equivalent to the statement that \\(L\\)