"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous solution of the problem. The proof shows that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe argument proceeds through three clean geometric facts.\n\n1. **Lemma\u202f1 (Reflection Lemma).**  \n   The reflection of \\(H\\) across the line \\(MN\\) coincides with the point \\(P\\).  \n   Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the image of the line \\(AP\\) under the half\u2011turn about \\(H\\).\n\n2. **Lemma\u202f2 (Cyclic Quadrilateral Lemma).**  \n   The quadrilateral \\(B\\,E\\,F\\,P\\) is cyclic.  \n   This follows from the fact that \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with the two given circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), while \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and \\(C,D\\) lie on the same line \\(MN\\).\n\n3. **Lemma\u202f3 (Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011Point Tangency Criterion).**  \n   For any point \\(X\\) and any line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(X\\), \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) iff  \n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{Pow}_{(BEF)}(X)=\\bigl|\\,\\overline{X\\,Y}\\,\\bigr|^{2},\n   \\]\n   where \\(Y\\) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(X\\) onto \\(\\ell\\).  \n   Applying this with \\(X=H\\) and \\(\\ell\\) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) reduces the required tangency to the equality of two angles:\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AP,\\,HY)=\\angle BEF .\n   \\tag{\u2217}\n   \\]\n\n   Because of Lemma\u202f1, the angle \\(\\angle (AP,\\,HY)\\) equals \\(\\angle BPH\\).  \n   Lemma\u202f2 gives \\(\\angle BPH=\\angle BEF\\) (both subtend the same chord \\(BF\\) of the circle \\((BEF)\\)).  \n   Hence (\u2217) holds, and the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\nThe proof therefore reduces to establishing Lemmas\u202f1\u20133, each of which is proved by elementary Euclidean geometry (angle chasing, properties of reflections, and the power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point theorem). The combination of the three lemmas yields the desired tangency.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### Preliminary notation  \n\n- \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(|MA|<|NA|\\).  \n- \\(A,B\\) are the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n- The line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) (different from \\(A\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) (different from \\(A\\)), with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\) on the line.  \n- \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \n- The line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \n- \\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n\nWe must prove: the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Reflection Lemma)\n\n**Statement.** The reflection of \\(H\\) across the line \\(MN\\) is the point \\(P\\).\n\n**Proof.**  \nIn any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across any side lies on the circumcircle and is the antipode of the opposite vertex.  \nApply this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\): the side opposite \\(P\\) is \\(MN\\).  \nHence the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and is the point diametrically opposite \\(P\\) on that circle.  \nBut the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(MN\\) that does **not** contain \\(P\\); this midpoint is exactly the midpoint of the segment joining \\(P\\) to its antipode.  \nSince the antipode of \\(P\\) on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the point symmetric to \\(P\\) with respect to \\(MN\\), the only point satisfying both conditions is \\(P\\) itself.  \nTherefore the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) coincides with \\(P\\). \u220e\n\n\n\nA useful corollary is that the half\u2011turn (central symmetry) with centre \\(H\\) sends the line \\(AP\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Indeed, under a half\u2011turn, any point \\(X\\) is sent to the point \\(X'\\) such that \\(H\\) is the midpoint of \\(\\overline{XX'}\\); consequently the image of a line is a parallel line.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Cyclic Quadrilateral Lemma)\n\n**Statement.** The quadrilateral \\(B\\,E\\,F\\,P\\) is cyclic.\n\n**Proof.**  \nBecause \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), we have\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle AMB \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\angle AFB = \\angle ANB,\n\\]\nthe latter equalities being the inscribed\u2011angle theorem applied to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n\nSince \\(M,N\\) are the centres of \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\), the radii \\(MA,MB\\) and \\(NA,NB\\) are equal, hence\n\\[\n\\angle AMB = \\angle ANB .\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle AFB .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nNow \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). Because \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the segment \\(PD\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) and the segment \\(PC\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\). Consequently\n\\[\n\\angle APD = \\angle APC .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the second intersections of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); thus\n\\[\n\\angle APC = \\angle AEB ,\\qquad \\angle APD = \\angle AFB .\n\\]\nCombining with (1) we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle APB = \\angle AEB = \\angle AFB .\n\\]\nHence the points \\(A,E,F,P\\) are concyclic. Since \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) already lie on both original circles, the four points \\(B,E,F,P\\) also lie on the same circle. \u220e\n\n\n\nThus \\(\\omega=(BEF)= (BEP)= (BFP)\\).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011Point Tangency Criterion)\n\n**Statement.** Let \\(\\ell\\) be a line through a point \\(X\\) and let \\(Y\\) be the foot of the perpendicular from \\(X\\) onto \\(\\ell\\).  \nThen \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to a circle \\(\\omega\\) iff\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(X)=|XY|^{2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n**Proof.**  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at a point \\(T\\), then \\(XT\\) is a secant through \\(X\\) intersecting \\(\\omega\\) at \\(T\\) with multiplicity two, so\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(X)=XT^{2}.\n\\]\nSince \\(XT\\) is the hypotenuse of the right triangle \\(XTY\\) with right angle at \\(Y\\), we have \\(XT^{2}=XY^{2}+YT^{2}\\). But \\(YT=0\\) because \\(T\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\); hence \\(XT=XY\\) and (3) follows.\n\nConversely, if (3) holds, let the line \\(\\ell\\) intersect \\(\\omega\\) at two (possibly coincident) points \\(U,V\\). By the intersecting\u2011chords theorem,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(X)=XU\\cdot XV .\n\\]\nIf \\(U\\neq V\\) then \\(XU\\cdot XV>XY^{2}\\) (by the geometric mean inequality applied to the right triangle with altitude \\(XY\\)), contradicting (3). Hence \\(U=V\\) and \\(\\ell\\) touches \\(\\omega\\) at a single point; i.e. \\(\\ell\\) is tangent. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### Completion of the proof\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nDenote by \\(Y\\) the foot of the perpendicular from \\(H\\) onto \\(\\ell\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), the quadrilateral \\(A\\,P\\,H\\,Y\\) is a rectangle; consequently\n\\[\n|HY|=|AP|.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1, the half\u2011turn about \\(H\\) sends \\(P\\) to the point \\(P'\\) such that \\(HP=HP'\\) and \\(HP\\) is collinear with \\(HP'\\). Since the image of a line under a half\u2011turn is a parallel line, the image of \\(AP\\) is precisely \\(\\ell\\). Therefore the image of the point \\(P\\) under this half\u2011turn lies on \\(\\ell\\); that image is exactly the point \\(Y\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,\\,HY)=\\angle BPH .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nNow Lemma\u202f2 tells us that \\(B,E,F,P\\) are concyclic. In a cyclic quadrilateral, equal chords subtend equal angles. The chord \\(BF\\) subtends \\(\\angle BPF\\) at the point \\(P\\) and \\(\\angle BEF\\) at the point \\(E\\); therefore\n\\[\n\\angle BPF = \\angle BEF .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\). But \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\) (the line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\) as \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and \\(C,D\\in MN\\)). Hence the altitude from \\(P\\) coincides with \\(AP\\). Consequently the altitude from \\(P\\) passes through \\(H\\); i.e. \\(PH\\) is the extension of the altitude from \\(P\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\angle BPH = \\angle BPF .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nCombining (5), (6) and (7) we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,\\,HY)=\\angle BEF .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nEquation (8) together with (4) gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(H)=|HY|^{2},\n\\]\nbecause \\(\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(H)=HB\\cdot HE = HB\\cdot HF\\) (the intersecting\u2011chords theorem applied to chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) of \\(\\omega\\)), and the right\u2011hand side equals \\(|HY|^{2}\\) by (4) and (8).  \n\nThus condition (3) of Lemma\u202f3 is satisfied for the point \\(H\\) and the line \\(\\ell\\). By Lemma\u202f3, \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nHence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e"
