on the circumcircle and is the antipode of the opposite vertex.  \nApply this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\): the side opposite \\(P\\) is \\(MN\\).  \nHence the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and is the point diametrically opposite \\(P\\) on that circle.  \nBut the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(MN\\) that does **not** contain \\(P\\); this midpoint is exactly the midpoint of the segment joining \\(P\\) to its antipode.  \nSince the antipode of \\(P\\) on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the point symmetric to \\(P\\) with respect to \\(MN\\), the only point satisfying both conditions is \\(P\\) itself.  \nTherefore the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) coincides with \\(P\\). \u220e\n\n\n\nA useful corollary is that the half\u2011turn (central symmetry) with centre \\(H\\) sends the line \\(AP\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Indeed, under a half\u2011turn, any point \\(X\\) is sent to the point \\(X'\\) such that \\(H\\) is the midpoint of \\(\\overline{XX'}\\); consequently the image of a line is a parallel line.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Cyclic Quadrilateral Lemma)\n\n**Statement.** The quadrilateral \\(B\\,E\\,F\\,P\\) is cyclic.\n\n**Proof.**  \nBecause \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), we have\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle AMB \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\angle AFB = \\angle ANB,\n\\]\nthe latter equalities being the inscribed\u2011angle theorem applied to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n\nSince \\(M,N\\) are the centres of \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\), the radii \\(MA,MB\\) and \\(NA,NB\\) are equal, hence\n\\[\n\\angle AMB = \\angle ANB .\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle AFB .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nNow \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). Because \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the segment \\(PD\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) and the segment \\(PC\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\). Consequently\n\\[\n\\angle APD = \\angle APC .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the second intersections of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); thus\n\\[\n\\angle APC = \\angle AEB ,\\qquad \\angle APD = \\angle AFB .\n\\]\nCombining with (1) we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle APB = \\angle AEB = \\angle AFB .\n\\]\nHence the points \\(A,E,F,P\\) are concyclic. Since \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) already lie on both original circles, the four points \\(B,E,F,P\\) also lie on the same circle. \u220e\n\n\n\nThus \\(\\omega=(BEF)= (BEP)= (BFP)\\).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011Point Tangency Criterion)\n\n**Statement.** Let \\(\\ell\\) be a line through a point \\(X\\) and let \\(Y\\) be the foot of the perpendicular from \\(X\\) onto \\(\\ell\\).  \nThen \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to a circle \\(\\omega\\) iff\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(X)=|XY|^{2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n**Proof.**  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at a point \\(T\\), then \\(XT\\) is a secant through \\(X\\) intersecting \\(\\omega\\) at \\(T\\) with multiplicity two, so\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(X)=XT^{2}.\n\\]\nSince \\(XT\\) is the hypotenuse of the right triangle \\(XTY\\) with right angle at \\(Y\\), we have \\(XT^{2}=XY^{2}+YT^{2}\\). But \\(YT=0\\) because \\(T\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\); hence \\(XT=XY\\) and (3) follows.\n\nConversely, if (3) holds, let the line \\(\\ell\\) intersect \\(\\omega\\) at two (possibly coincident) points \\(U,V\\). By the intersecting\u2011chords theorem,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(X)=XU\\cdot XV .\n\\]\nIf \\(U\\neq V\\) then \\(XU\\cdot XV>XY^{2}\\) (by the geometric mean inequality applied to the right triangle with altitude \\(XY\\)), contradicting (3). Hence \\(U=V\\) and \\(\\ell\\) touches \\(\\omega\\) at a single point; i.e. \\(\\ell\\) is tangent. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### Completion of the proof\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nDenote