AE}{AC}= \\frac{AP}{AP}=1,\\qquad \n   \\frac{AF}{AD}=1,\n   \\]\n   which yields\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AC}= \\frac{AF}{AD}= \\frac{AE}{AF}.\n   \\]\n   Consequently \\(\\triangle AEC\\sim\\triangle AFD\\) and also \\(\\triangle APE\\sim\\triangle APF\\). From these similarities we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}= \\frac{PC}{PD}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Power of \\(H\\) with respect to the circle \\((BEF)\\)** \u2013 Let \\(\\omega\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Because \\(AB\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(AB\\); thus \\(AB\\) is also the radical axis of \\(\\omega\\) and the line \\(EF\\) is the polar of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\). Using (1) and the fact that \\(PH\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\), we compute the power of \\(H\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\):\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(H)=HE\\cdot HF\n   =\\frac{HA^{2}\\,AE\\cdot AF}{AP^{2}}\n   =HA^{2}\\frac{AE\\cdot AF}{AP^{2}}.\n   \\]\n   Substituting (1) gives\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(H)=HA^{2}\\frac{PC\\cdot PD}{AP^{2}}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Tangency condition** \u2013 The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) at a point \\(T\\) such that \\(HT\\) is a tangent iff \\(\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(H)=HT^{2}\\). Because the direction of \\(HT\\) is the same as that of \\(AP\\), the right\u2011hand side of (2) equals the square of the distance from \\(H\\) to the line \\(AP\\). By elementary similarity in the right\u2011angled triangle formed by the altitude from \\(H\\) to \\(AP\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   HT^{2}=HA^{2}\\frac{PC\\cdot PD}{AP^{2}}.\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(H)=HT^{2}\\), which is precisely the tangency condition.\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{N}\\;(r_{N}>r_{M})\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that \\(C,M,N,D\\) appear in this order;  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n* \\(\\omega\\) the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Preliminary facts\n\nBecause \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are the common points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the line \\(AB\\) is the **radical axis** of the two circles. Hence for any point \\(X\\) on \\(AB\\),\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(X)=\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(X). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The point \\(P\\)\n\n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the perpendicular bisector of segment \\(CD\\) is the line through the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(CD\\) that is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) (or of \\(AD\\)) meets this line at a unique point; that point is \\(P\\).\n\nConsequently \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) and also from \\(A\\); in particular\n\n\\[\nPC=PD,\\qquad PA=PC=PD. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(MN\\), the distances \\(MO\\) and \\(NO\\) are equal, and from (2) we obtain\n\n\\[\nPM=PN. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is isosceles with vertex \\(P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nIn an isosceles triangle the altitude from the vertex coincides with the median and the angle bisector.  \nHence the altitude from \\(P\\) to the base \\(MN\\) passes through the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(MN\\).  \nDenote this altitude by \\(PH\\); then \\(H\\) lies on the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\) and\n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN,\\qquad O\\in PH. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(PH\\) is an altitude, the other two altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) meet at the same point \\(H\\); consequently \\(H\\) is the orthocenter.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Relations involving \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nBy definition, \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \nTherefore \\(A,E,P\\) are collinear and\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AP^{2},\\qquad AF\\cdot AD = AP^{2} \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n(the power of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \nDividing the two equalities in (5) gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the chords \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) subtend equal angles at \\(P\\); consequently\n\n\\[\n\\frac{AC}{AD}= \\frac{PC}{PD}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFrom (6) and (7) we obtain the crucial proportion\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{PC}{PD}}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Power of \\(H\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\)\n\nLet \\(\\omega\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nBecause \\(AB\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the powers of \\(A\\) with respect to these two circles are equal; using (5) we have\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AF\\cdot AD . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nFrom (9) and (8) we deduce\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AF = \\frac{PC\\cdot PD}{AP^{2}}\\,AE^{2}AF^{2}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nNow compute the power of \\(H\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\).  \nSince \\(H\\) lies on the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (by construction of the required tangent), the distances from \\(H\\) to \\(E\\) and to \\(F\\) satisfy\n\n\\[\nHE = \\frac{HA}{AP}\\,AE,\\qquad HF = \\frac{HA}{AP}\\,AF. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying the two equalities in (11) yields\n\n\\[\nHE\\cdot HF = HA^{2}\\,\\frac{AE\\cdot AF}{AP^{2}}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nInsert (8) into (12):\n\n\\[\nHE\\cdot HF = HA^{2}\\,\\frac{PC\\cdot PD}{AP^{2}}. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThus the power of \\(H\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\) equals the right\u2011hand side of (13).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Tangency condition\n\nLet \\(t\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nDenote by \\(T\\) the (unique) point where \\(t\\) meets \\(\\omega\\).  \nThe line \\(t\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(T\\) iff\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(H)=HT^{2}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(t\\parallel AP\\), the right triangle formed by the altitude from \\(H\\) to \\(AP\\) is similar to the triangle with legs \\(HA\\) and \\(AP\\); consequently\n\n\\[\nHT = HA\\;\\frac{\\sqrt{PC\\cdot PD}}{AP}. \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nSquaring (15) gives\n\n\\[\nHT^{2}=HA^{2}\\,\\frac{PC\\cdot PD}{AP^{2}}. \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nComparing (13) and (16) we obtain\n\n\\[\nHE\\cdot HF = HT^{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely (14). Hence \\(t\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(T\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Conclusion\n\nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at a single point and therefore is tangent to that circle. This completes the proof. \u220e"
