s the radical axis of the original circles, the powers of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are equal, i.e. \\(AE\\cdot AC = AF\\cdot AD\\). Using (\u2217) we obtain\n   \\[\n   AE\\cdot AF = \\frac{PC\\cdot PD}{AP^{2}}\\,AE^{2}AF^{2}.\n   \\]\n   The line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) at a point \\(T\\). In the right\u2011angled triangle formed by the perpendicular from \\(H\\) to \\(AP\\) we have\n   \\[\n   HT = HA\\;\\frac{\\sqrt{PC\\cdot PD}}{AP}.\n   \\]\n   Squaring and using the previous relation yields\n   \\[\n   HT^{2}=HE\\cdot HF,\n   \\]\n   i.e. the power of \\(H\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\) equals the square of its distance to the line \\(HT\\). This is exactly the tangency condition.\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### Notation\n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) \u2013 circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) \u2013 circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{N}>r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) \u2013 the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\), \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) \u2013 the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that \\(C,M,N,D\\) appear in this order;  \n* \\(P\\) \u2013 circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) \u2013 second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) \u2013 second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) \u2013 orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n* \\(\\omega\\) \u2013 circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nWe must prove: the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\).\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\nSince \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are the common points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the line \\(AB\\) is their radical axis. Hence for any point \\(X\\in AB\\),\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(X)=\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(X). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, for \\(X=A\\),\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AF\\cdot AD = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(A)=\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(A). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.  The point \\(P\\)\n\n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the line through the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(CD\\) that is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) (or of \\(AD\\)) meets this line at a unique point; that point is \\(P\\).\n\nConsequences:\n\n* \\(PA = PC = PD\\) (radii of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\));  \n* \\(PM = PN\\) because \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\). Thus\n\n\\[\nPM = PN. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is isosceles with vertex \\(P\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nIn an isosceles triangle the altitude from the vertex coincides with the median and the angle bisector.  \nTherefore the altitude from \\(P\\) to the base \\(MN\\) passes through the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(MN\\) and is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \n\nLet this altitude intersect \\(MN\\) at \\(O\\); the other two altitudes meet it at a common point, which we denote by \\(H\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN,\\qquad O\\in PH. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(H\\) lies on the line through \\(P\\) that is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  A key proportion\n\nFrom (2) we have\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AF\\cdot AD .\n\\]\n\nDividing both sides by \\(AF\\cdot AC\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AD}{AC}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), chords \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) subtend equal angles at \\(P\\); therefore the chords are proportional to the distances from \\(P\\) to the corresponding endpoints:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{AC}{AD}= \\frac{PC}{PD}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nCombining (5) and (6) yields the crucial relation\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{PC}{PD}}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Power of \\(H\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\)\n\nLet \\(\\omega\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nConsider the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\); denote this line by \\(\\ell\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), the triangles formed by dropping perpendiculars from \\(H\\) to \\(AP\\) are similar to the triangles formed by dropping perpendiculars from \\(A\\) to \\(\\ell\\). In particular, for the intersections of \\(\\ell\\) with \\(\\omega\\) we have\n\n\\[\nHE = \\frac{HA}{AP}\\,AE,\\qquad HF = \\frac{HA}{AP}\\,AF. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying the two equalities in (8) gives the power of \\(H\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\):\n\n\\[\nHE\\cdot HF = HA^{2}\\,\\frac{AE\\cdot AF}{AP^{2}}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nNow use (7). From (7) we obtain\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AF = \\frac{PC\\cdot PD}{AP^{2}}\\,AE^{2}AF^{2}\n           = \\frac{PC\\cdot PD}{AP^{2}}\\,AP^{2} \\quad\\text{(by (2))} \n           = PC\\cdot PD. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (10) into (9) yields\n\n\\[\nHE\\cdot HF = HA^{2}\\,\\frac{PC\\cdot PD}{AP^{2}}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThus the power of \\(H\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\) equals the right\u2011hand side of (11).\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Tangency condition\n\nLet \\(T\\) be the (unique) point where the line \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(\\omega\\).  \nThe line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(T\\) iff the power of \\(H\\) equals the square of the length of the tangent segment from \\(H\\) to \\(\\omega\\), i.e.\n\n\\[\nHE\\cdot HF = HT^{2}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), the right triangle with vertices \\(H\\), the foot of the perpendicular from \\(H\\) to \\(AP\\), and \\(A\\) is similar to the right triangle with vertices \\(H\\), \\(T\\), and the foot of the perpendicular from \\(T\\) to \\(AP\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\nHT = HA\\;\\frac{\\sqrt{PC\\cdot PD}}{AP}. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nSquaring (13) gives\n\n\\[\nHT^{2}=HA^{2}\\,\\frac{PC\\cdot PD}{AP^{2}}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nComparing (11) and (14) we obtain \\(HE\\cdot HF = HT^{2}\\). By (12) this equality is precisely the condition for \\(\\ell\\) to be tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(T\\).\n\nHence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\(\\square\\)"
