responding endpoints:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{AC}{AD}= \\frac{PC}{PD}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nCombining (5) and (6) yields the crucial relation\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{PC}{PD}}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Power of \\(H\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\)\n\nLet \\(\\omega\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nConsider the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\); denote this line by \\(\\ell\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), the triangles formed by dropping perpendiculars from \\(H\\) to \\(AP\\) are similar to the triangles formed by dropping perpendiculars from \\(A\\) to \\(\\ell\\). In particular, for the intersections of \\(\\ell\\) with \\(\\omega\\) we have\n\n\\[\nHE = \\frac{HA}{AP}\\,AE,\\qquad HF = \\frac{HA}{AP}\\,AF. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying the two equalities in (8) gives the power of \\(H\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\):\n\n\\[\nHE\\cdot HF = HA^{2}\\,\\frac{AE\\cdot AF}{AP^{2}}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nNow use (7). From (7) we obtain\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AF = \\frac{PC\\cdot PD}{AP^{2}}\\,AE^{2}AF^{2}\n           = \\frac{PC\\cdot PD}{AP^{2}}\\,AP^{2} \\quad\\text{(by (2))} \n           = PC\\cdot PD. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (10) into (9) yields\n\n\\[\nHE\\cdot HF = HA^{2}\\,\\frac{PC\\cdot PD}{AP^{2}}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThus the power of \\(H\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\) equals the right\u2011hand side of (11).\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Tangency condition\n\nLet \\(T\\) be the (unique) point where the line \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(\\omega\\).  \nThe line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(T\\) iff the power of \\(H\\) equals the square of the length of the tangent segment from \\(H\\) to \\(\\omega\\), i.e.\n\n\\[\nHE\\cdot HF = HT^{2}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), the right triangle with vertices \\(H\\), the foot of the perpendicular from \\(H\\) to \\(AP\\), and \\(A\\) is similar to the right triangle with vertices \\(H\\), \\(T\\), and the foot of the perpendicular from \