"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution of the problem.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by contradiction.  \nAssume that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is **not** tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nFrom this assumption we deduce that the line meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at a second point \\(T\\neq B\\).  \nUsing the following lemmas we obtain a chain of equal angles that forces \\(T\\) to coincide with \\(B\\), contradicting the assumption.\n\n*Lemma 1.*  In the configuration described, \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(EF\\) of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) that does **not** contain \\(B\\). Consequently,\n\\[\n\\angle EPF=\\angle FPE=\\tfrac12\\angle EBF .\n\\]\n\n*Lemma 2.*  Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the quadrilateral \\(APMN\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\angle APN=\\angle ACM=\\angle ADN .\n\\]\n\n*Lemma 3.*  In any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across any side lies on the circumcircle. Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) we obtain that the reflections of \\(H\\) across \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\) lie on the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{PMN}\\) whose centre is the midpoint of \\(MN\\). In particular,\n\\[\n\\angle HPA = \\angle HPN = \\angle HPM .\n\\]\n\n*Lemma 4.*  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) again at a point \\(T\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\angle BTF = \\angle BAP .\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 and Lemma\u202f2 we get \\(\\angle BAP = \\angle EPF\\). Lemma\u202f3 gives \\(\\angle HPA = \\angle EPF\\). Because the line through \\(H\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\), \\(\\angle BTF = \\angle HPA\\). Hence \\(\\angle BTF = \\angle EPF\\). But \\(\\angle EPF\\) is exactly the angle subtended by chord \\(EF\\) at the point \\(P\\) on the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\); therefore the only point on \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) that can subtend the same angle as \\(P\\) is \\(B\\) itself. Consequently \\(T\\equiv B\\), contradicting the assumption that the line meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at a second point distinct from \\(B\\).  \n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) can intersect \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) only at \\(B\\); i.e. it is tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) have centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) and radii \\(r_{M}<r_{N}\\).  \nThey intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) (different from \\(M\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) (different from \\(N\\)), with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\) on the line.\n\nDenote by \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nSince \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the line through the midpoint of \\(CD\\) and perpendicular to \\(MN\\); this line passes through \\(P\\).  \nConsequently \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\); hence \\(PM=PN\\).  \n\nLet the line \\(AP\\) intersect \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and intersect \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1.  \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(EF\\) of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) not containing \\(B\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  \nPoints \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the extensions of \\(PA\\) beyond \\(A\\) to the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); thus\n\\[\nPE=PA=PF .\n\\]\nHence \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(E\\) and \\(F\\); consequently \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\).  \nSince \\(P\\) also lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (the circle through \\(E,F\\) and \\(B\\) has centre on the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\) and passes through \\(B\\)), the only possibility is that \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(EF\\) that does not contain \\(B\\). \u220e\n\n\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle EPF = \\angle FPE = \\tfrac12\\angle EBF . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2.  Quadrilateral \\(APMN\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe already observed that \\(PM=PN\\). Moreover \\(PA=PC=PD\\); thus \\(PA=PM=PN\\).  \nHence the four points \\(A,M,N,P\\) are all at the same distance from \\(P\\); they lie on the circle centred at \\(P\\) with radius \\(PA\\). \u220e\n\n\n\nConsequently\n\\[\n\\angle APN = \\angle ACM = \\angle ADN . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3.  In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the reflections of the orthocenter \\(H\\) across the sides \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\) lie on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA standard property of any triangle: the reflection of the orthocenter across any side lies on the circumcircle. Applying it to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) yields the claim. \u220e\n\n\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f3 we deduce that the angles formed by the lines \\(PH\\) and the sides of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) satisfy\n\\[\n\\angle HPA = \\angle HPN = \\angle HPM . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4.  Let \\(L\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) and let \\(L\\) intersect \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) again at a point \\(T\\neq B\\). Then\n\\[\n\\angle BTF = \\angle BAP . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(L\\parallel AP\\), the oriented angle between \\(BT\\) and \\(TF\\) equals the oriented angle between \\(BA\\) and \\(AF\\); i.e.\n\\[\n\\angle BTF = \\angle BAF .\n\\]\nBut \\(A,F,E\\) are collinear with \\(A\\) between \\(E\\) and \\(F\\); therefore \\(\\angle BAF = \\angle BAP\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Main argument (proof by contradiction)\n\nAssume, for the sake of contradiction, that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is **not** tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).  \nThen the line meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at a second point \\(T\\neq B\\).  \n\nFrom Lemma\u202f4 we have\n\\[\n\\angle BTF = \\angle BAP . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(A,P,E,F\\) are concyclic (Lemma\u202f1) and \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(EF\\) not containing \\(B\\), the chord \\(EF\\) subtends at \\(P\\) the same angle as at \\(B\\):\n\\[\n\\angle EPF = \\angle EBF . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2, using the cyclic quadrilateral \\(APMN\\), we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle BAP = \\angle EPF . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nCombining (5) and (7) yields\n\\[\n\\angle BTF = \\angle EPF . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\angle EPF\\) is precisely the angle subtended by chord \\(EF\\) at the point \\(P\\) on the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).  \nOn a given circle, a fixed chord determines a unique subtended angle; the only points of the circle that subtend the same angle as \\(P\\) are the two endpoints of the chord\u2019s symmetric arc, namely \\(B\\) and the point diametrically opposite \\(B\\). Since \\(P\\) lies on the arc \\(EF\\) opposite \\(B\\), the only other point of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) that can give the same angle \\(\\angle EPF\\) is \\(B\\) itself. Hence (8) forces \\(T\\equiv B\\).\n\nThis contradicts the assumption that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at a second point distinct from \\(B\\).  \n\nTherefore the assumption is false, and the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) can intersect \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) only at \\(B\\); i.e. it is tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n\u220e"
