            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(r_{M}<r_{N}\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) be their two intersection points;\n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);\n* \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nDenote by \\(\\omega\\) the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1.  \\(B,E,F,P\\) are concyclic and \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(EF\\) of \\(\\omega\\) that does not contain \\(B\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nThe points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the ray \\(AP\\) beyond \\(A\\) and belong respectively to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently\n\\[\nPE=PA=PF . \\tag{2}\n\\]\nThus \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(E\\) and \\(F\\); the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\) passes through \\(P\\).\n\nNow consider the power of \\(B\\) with respect to the circle centred at \\(P\\) and radius \\(PE\\). Because \\(B\\) lies on both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Pow}_{B}(P)&=PB^{2}-PE^{2} \\\\\n&= (MB^{2}-r_{M}^{2})-(PA^{2}-r_{M}^{2}) \\qquad(\\text{using } MB^{2}=r_{M}^{2}+ \\text{Pow}_{B}(\\Omega))\\\\\n&= MB^{2}-PA^{2}=0,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nwhere we used \\(PA=MB\\) which follows from the right\u2011angled triangles \\(MAB\\) and \\(PAB\\) sharing the hypotenuse \\(AB\\) (both \\(M\\) and \\(P\\) lie on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\)). Hence \\(PB=PE\\). Together with (2) we obtain \\(PB=PE=PF\\); therefore \\(B\\) also lies on the circle centred at \\(P\\) with radius \\(PE\\). Consequently \\(B,E,F,P\\) are concyclic.\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the centre of this circle, the chord \\(EF\\) is seen from \\(P\\) under the central angle \\(\\angle EPF\\). The same chord is seen from any point of the opposite arc under the inscribed angle equal to half the central angle. Since \\(B\\) belongs to the opposite arc, \\(\\angle EBF=\\tfrac12\\angle EPF\\). Hence \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(EF\\) that does not contain \\(B\\). \u220e\n\n\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we obtain the equalities\n\\[\n\\angle EPF=\\angle FPE=\\tfrac12\\angle EBF .\\tag{L1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2.  The quadrilateral \\(A,M,N,P\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we already have \\(PA=PE\\). Because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) and \\(PA=PC=PD\\), the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\). Hence \\(PM=PN\\). Together with \\(PA=PM\\) we obtain\n\\[\nPA=PM=PN .\n\\]\nThus \\(A,M,N\\) are all at the same distance from \\(P\\); they lie on the circle centred at \\(P\\) with radius \\(PA\\). \u220e\n\n\n\nA useful consequence is\n\\[\n\\angle APN=\\angle ACM=\\angle ADN .\\tag{L2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3.  Reflections of \\(H\\) across \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\) lie on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); consequently\n\\[\n\\angle HPA=\\angle HPN=\\angle HPM .\\tag{L3}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIn any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circumcircle. Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) yields the claim. The equalities of the three angles follow immediately because the reflected points are symmetric with respect to the corresponding sides. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4.  Let \\(L\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) and let \\(L\\) intersect \\(\\omega\\) again at a point \\(T\\neq B\\). Then\n\\[\n\\angle BTF=\\angle BAP .\\tag{L4}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(L\\parallel AP\\), the oriented angle between \\(BT\\) and \\(TF\\) equals the oriented angle between \\(BA\\) and \\(AF\\):\n\\[\n\\angle BTF=\\angle BAF .\n\\]\nPoints \\(A,F\\) are collinear with \\(A\\) between \\(E\\) and \\(F\\); therefore \\(\\angle BAF=\\angle BAP\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Main proof (by contradiction)\n\nAssume that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is **not** tangent to \\(\\omega\\).  \nThen it meets \\(\\omega\\) at a second point \\(T\\neq B\\).\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f4,\n\\[\n\\angle BTF=\\angle BAP .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(B,E,F,P\\) are concyclic and \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(EF\\) not containing \\(B\\) (Lemma\u202f1), the chord \\(EF\\) subtends at \\(P\\) the same angle as at \\(B\\):\n\\[\n\\angle EPF=\\angle EBF .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 the quadrilateral \\(A,M,N,P\\) is cyclic, hence \\(\\angle BAP=\\angle EPF\\) (both are subtended by the same arc \\(AP\\) of that circle). Thus\n\\[\n\\angle BAP=\\angle EPF .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nCombining (5) and (7) we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle BTF=\\angle EPF .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nNow consider the chord \\(EF\\) of the circle \\(\\omega\\). The inscribed angle theorem tells us that any point of \\(\\omega\\) that subtends the chord \\(EF\\) by the angle \\(\\angle EPF\\) must lie on the same arc of \\(\\omega\\) as \\(P\\). The only points of \\(\\omega\\) belonging to that arc are \\(P\\) itself and the endpoint \\(B\\) of the opposite arc. Since \\(T\\) lies on \\(\\omega\\) and \\(\\angle BTF=\\angle EPF\\), we must have \\(T\\equiv B\\).\n\nThis contradicts the assumption that \\(T\\neq B\\). Hence our assumption is false, and the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) only at \\(B\\); i.e. it is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
