rted concyclicity).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f2  \n\n1. **Claim:** \u201cFrom Lemma\u202f1 we already have \\(PA=PE\\).\u201d  \n   *Invalid* because Lemma\u202f1\u2019s claim \\(PA=PE\\) was not justified.\n\n2. **Symmetry argument:** \u201cBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) and \\(PA=PC=PD\\), the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\). Hence \\(PM=PN\\).\u201d  \n   This reasoning is **flawed**: equality of distances from \\(P\\) to \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) does not imply symmetry of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) about the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\); it only says \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\). No link to \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) is established. This is a **Critical Error**.\n\n3. **Conclusion \u201c\\(PA=PM=PN\\)\u201d** follows from the previous false statements, making the claim that \\(A,M,N,P\\) are concyclic with centre \\(P\\) **unfounded** \u2013 a **Critical Error**.\n\n*Result for Lemma\u202f2:* Critical Error; the lemma is false.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f3  \n\n1. **Statement of a known fact:** \u201cIn any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circumcircle.\u201d  \n   This is true, but the solution does **not** define the reflected points nor show how the angle equalities \\(\\angle HPA=\\angle HPN=\\angle HPM\\) follow. The step is therefore a **Justification Gap**.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f4  \n\n1. **Angle chase using parallelism:** The claim \\(\\angle BTF=\\angle BAF\\) because \\(L\\parallel AP\\) is not rigorously justified. One must verify the orientation of points and that \\(T\\) indeed lies on the extension of \\(BF\\). The argument is a **Justification Gap**.\n\n---\n\n### Main Proof  \n\n1. **Assumption of contradiction:** Acceptable as a proof strategy.\n\n2. **Use of Lemma\u202f4:** Relies on the unproved angle equality from Lemma\u202f4 \u2013 a **Justification Gap** propagates here.\n\n3. **Use of Lemma\u202f1 (arc\u20