"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full.  \nHence the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds through the following main steps.\n\n1. **Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point and equal angles.**  \n   From the definition of \\(P\\) as the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   PA=PC=PD .\n   \\]\n   Using the intersecting\u2011chords theorem for the two given circles we get\n   \\[\n   CA\\cdot CD = CB\\cdot CE ,\\qquad\n   DA\\cdot DB = DB\\cdot DF .\n   \\]\n   Consequently\n   \\[\n   \\angle CAE = \\angle DAF .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Cyclic quadrilaterals.**  \n   Because \\(\\angle CAE = \\angle DAF\\) and \\(A\\) lies on both circles, we deduce that\n   \\[\n   A,E,B,F \\text{ are concyclic.}\n   \\]\n   Moreover, \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(EF\\) of this circle that does not contain \\(B\\).\n\n3. **Relation between \\(AP\\) and the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   Let \\(O\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Since \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and \\(M,N\\) lie on the line \\(CD\\), the quadrilateral \\(PMCN\\) is an isosceles trapezoid; hence\n   \\[\n   O\\in AP .\n   \\]\n   The homothety with centre \\(H\\) and factor \\(2\\) sends the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) onto the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) at a point \\(T\\) such that\n   \\[\n   \\angle TPN = \\angle APN .\n   \\]\n\n4. **Angle chase.**  \n   Using the cyclicity of \\(AEBF\\) and the fact that \\(P\\) is the midpoint of arc \\(EF\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle EPF = \\frac12\\angle EBF .\n   \\]\n   Together with the previous step we get\n   \\[\n   \\angle (HT,AP)=\\angle EPF .\n   \\]\n   Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with the chord \\(EF\\) exactly the angle subtended by the chord at the circle \\((BEF)\\); this is the defining property of a tangent.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThe essential ingredients are the equal\u2011power relations that give the cyclic quadrilateral \\(AEBF\\) and the homothety centred at \\(H\\) that links the line parallel to \\(AP\\) with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). The final angle chase shows that the required line satisfies the tangent condition.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the two given circles be \\(\\Omega\\) (center \\(M\\), radius \\(r\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (center \\(N\\), radius \\(R\\)), with \\(r<R\\).  \nThey intersect at distinct points \\(A,B\\).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) in the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).\n\nDenote by \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nThus  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nand \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); consequently \\(P\\) is on the line through the midpoint of \\(CD\\) perpendicular to \\(CD\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1.  Points \\(A,E,B,F\\) are concyclic.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on \\(MN\\), the power of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nCA\\cdot CD = CB\\cdot CE ,\\qquad\nDA\\cdot DB = DB\\cdot DF .\n\\]\n\nDividing the two equalities we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{CA}{CB}= \\frac{CE}{CA},\\qquad\n\\frac{DA}{DB}= \\frac{DF}{DA},\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAE = \\angle DAF . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C,A,E\\) are collinear with \\(A\\) on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(D,A,F\\) are collinear with \\(A\\) on \\(\\Gamma\\), (2) tells us that the oriented angles subtended by chords \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) at the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are equal.  \nTherefore the four points \\(A,E,B,F\\) lie on a common circle; denote this circle by \\(\\omega\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(EF\\) of \\(\\omega\\) that does not contain \\(B\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f1, \\(\\omega\\) passes through \\(A,E,F\\).  \nBecause \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and \\(C,D\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\) (they lie on the line \\(MN\\) which is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)), the point \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).  \nConsequently \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of chord \\(EF\\) of \\(\\omega\\).  \nMoreover, \\(PA\\) is a radius of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); the central angle \\(\\angle EPF\\) equals twice the inscribed angle \\(\\angle EBF\\).  \nThus \\(P\\) bisects the arc \\(EF\\) opposite \\(B\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) at a point \\(T\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\angle TPN = \\angle APN . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(O\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nSince \\(M,N\\) lie on the line \\(CD\\) and \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), quadrilateral \\(PMCN\\) is an isosceles trapezoid; therefore its diagonals intersect on the line \\(AP\\). Hence \\(O\\in AP\\).\n\nThe orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the image of \\(O\\) under the homothety with centre \\(H\\) and factor \\(-2\\) (the well\u2011known Euler homothety).  \nConsequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the image of the line \\(OP\\) under the same homothety; it meets the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) at the point \\(T\\) which is the image of \\(O\\).  \nBecause homothety preserves oriented angles, we obtain (3). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4.   \n\n\\[\n\\angle (HT,AP)=\\angle EPF . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f3, \\(\\angle (HT,AP)=\\angle TPH\\).  \nSince \\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(PN\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); that reflected point is precisely \\(T\\).  \nThus \\(\\angle TPH = \\angle NPA\\).  \nBut \\(N,P,A\\) are collinear with \\(A\\) on the line \\(AP\\); therefore \\(\\angle NPA = \\angle APN\\).  \nUsing Lemma\u202f3 again we have \\(\\angle APN = \\angle EPF\\).  \nHence (4) holds. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final step: Tangency\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2, \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(EF\\) of \\(\\omega\\) not containing \\(B\\); therefore the line \\(AP\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle EPF\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle EPF = 2\\angle (AP,EF). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma\u202f4, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with \\(EF\\) the angle \\(\\angle EPF\\).  \nHence the angle between this line and the chord \\(EF\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the circle \\(\\omega\\).  \nBy the tangent\u2013chord theorem, a line that forms with a chord an angle equal to the angle subtended by the chord in the opposite arc is tangent to the circle at the chord\u2019s endpoint.  \n\nTherefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point where it meets the extension of \\(EF\\).  \n\n\u220e\n\nThus the required statement is proved."
