 lengths of the two chords through \\(X\\).  \nApplying this to point \\(A\\) for the two circles we obtain\n\n\\[\nCA\\cdot CD = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(A)=\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(A)=DA\\cdot DB .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, for point \\(B\\),\n\n\\[\nCB\\cdot CD = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(B)=\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(B)=DB\\cdot DB .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nDividing (1) by (2) gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{CA}{CB}= \\frac{CE}{CA},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{DA}{DB}= \\frac{DF}{DA},\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n\\[\n\\angle CAE = \\angle DAF . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C,A,E\\) are collinear and \\(D,A,F\\) are collinear, (3) states that the oriented angles subtended by chords \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) at the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are equal.  \nConsequently the four points \\(A,E,B,F\\) lie on a common circle; denote this circle by \\(\\omega\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2.  \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(EF\\) of \\(\\omega\\) that does not contain \\(B\\).\n\n**Proof.**  \nFrom the definition of \\(P\\) we have\n\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the segment \\(CD\\) is symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\) (the line through the centre of \\(\\omega\\) and the midpoint of \\(EF\\)).  \nEquation (4) together with Lemma\u202f1 implies that \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(E\\) and \\(F\\); hence \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of chord \\(EF\\) of \\(\\omega\\).\n\nMoreover, in \\(\\triangle ACD\\) the segment \\(AP\\) is a radius of its circumcircle, so the central angle subtended by chord \\(EF\\) at \\(P\\) equals twice the inscribed angle subtended by the same chord at any point of \\(\\omega\\).  In particular,\n\n\\[\n\\angle EPF = 2\\angle EBF . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\angle EPF\\) is exactly the angle subtended by arc \\(EF\\) at the centre of \\(\\omega\\); consequently \\(P\\) bisects that arc.  \nSince \\(B\\) lies on \\(\\omega\\) but not on the minor arc \\(EF\\) (otherwise \\(AB\\) would be a diameter of both original circles, contradicting the hypothesis that the radii are distinct), the bisected arc is precisely the one **not** containing \\(B\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n*The remaining part of the original problem \u2013 namely, showing that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) \u2013 reduces to an angle chase that uses Lemma\u202f2 together with standard properties of the Euler line and the homothety centred at the orthocenter.  This final angle chase has not been completed here.*\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
