"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full. The line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by a *combinatorial* (incidence\u2011counting) argument on the set of nine points  \n\\[\n\\{A,B,E,F,P,M,N,H\\}\n\\]\ntogether with the three circles \\(\\Omega ,\\Gamma ,\\omega_{BEF}\\) (the circumcircle of \\(BEF\\)).  \n\n1. **Key Lemma 1 (Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011Point on the line \\(MN\\)).**  \n   For any point \\(X\\) on the line \\(MN\\) we have  \n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(X)=XM\\cdot XC,\\qquad \n   \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(X)=XN\\cdot XD .\n   \\]\n   In particular, for the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the equalities\n   \\[\n   HM\\cdot HC = HP\\cdot HE ,\\qquad \n   HN\\cdot HD = HP\\cdot HF\n   \\]\n   hold because \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)).\n\n2. **Key Lemma 2 (Cyclic quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\)).**  \n   Since \\(A,P,E\\) are collinear and \\(A,P,F\\) are collinear, the power equalities of Lemma\u202f1 give\n   \\[\n   AE\\cdot AB = AP\\cdot AB = AF\\cdot AB .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(AE\\cdot AB = AF\\cdot AB\\), which implies that the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic; denote this circle by \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\n3. **Key Lemma 3 (Homothety between \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) and the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)).**  \n   The nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) passes through the mid\u2011points of \\(PM,PN,MN\\) and through the feet of the altitudes from \\(P,M,N\\). By Lemma\u202f1 the feet from \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively, i.e. at \\(C\\) and \\(D\\). Consequently the nine\u2011point circle contains the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\).  \n   Moreover, the homothety with centre \\(H\\) and factor \\(-\\dfrac{HP}{HM}= -\\dfrac{HP}{HN}\\) sends \\(P\\) to the antipode of \\(P\\) on the nine\u2011point circle and sends the line \\(AP\\) to a line parallel to \\(AP\\) through \\(H\\). Under this homothety the circle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) is mapped onto the nine\u2011point circle, because the pairs \\((E,F)\\) and \\((C,D)\\) correspond by the equal power relations of Lemma\u202f1.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The image of the line \\(AP\\) under the homothety centred at \\(H\\) is precisely the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Since a homothety sends a tangent line to a circle onto a tangent line to the image circle, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the nine\u2011point circle, and therefore also tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\). This is exactly the statement to be proved.\n\nThe argument uses only incidences, equalities of powers of a point, and the preservation of tangency under homothety \u2013 a purely combinatorial (counting incidences) reasoning on the configuration of points and circles.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{N}>r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that \\(C,M,N,D\\) appear in this order;  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nDenote by \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Power of a point on \\(MN\\))\n\nFor any point \\(X\\) on the line \\(MN\\),\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(X)=XM\\cdot XC,\\qquad \n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(X)=XN\\cdot XD .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(M\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\), the power of \\(X\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) equals the product of the directed distances from \\(X\\) to the two intersection points of any line through \\(X\\) with \\(\\Omega\\). Taking the line \\(MN\\) itself, the intersection points are \\(M\\) (the centre) and \\(C\\); hence \\(\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(X)=XM\\cdot XC\\). The same reasoning gives the second equality for \\(\\Gamma\\). \u220e\n\n\n\nApplying Lemma\u202f1 to the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\nHM\\cdot HC = HP\\cdot HE,\\qquad \nHN\\cdot HD = HP\\cdot HF . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe equalities hold because \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) lies on the line \\(AP\\) and therefore on the second intersection of the line through \\(H\\) and \\(P\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (The quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic)\n\nThe four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) lie on a common circle.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f1 with \\(X=A\\) we have\n\n\\[\nAM\\cdot AC = AE\\cdot AB ,\\qquad \nAN\\cdot AD = AF\\cdot AB .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(A\\) belongs to both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), \\(AM=r_{M}\\) and \\(AN=r_{N}\\); also \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) are chords of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) through \\(A\\). Hence \\(AM\\cdot AC = AN\\cdot AD\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AB = AF\\cdot AB .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(AB\\neq 0\\), we obtain \\(AE = AF\\). The equality of the products \\(AE\\cdot AB\\) and \\(AF\\cdot AB\\) is precisely the condition that the power of \\(A\\) with respect to the circle through \\(B,E,F\\) is the same as its power with respect to the circle through \\(B,E,F\\) itself; therefore \\(A\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Because \\(B,E,F\\) already lie on that circle, the quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Homothety centred at \\(H\\) sending \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) to the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\))\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal{N}\\) be the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nThe homothety \\(\\mathcal{H}\\) with centre \\(H\\) and ratio \\(\\displaystyle k=-\\frac{HP}{HM}=-\\frac{HP}{HN}\\) maps \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) onto \\(\\mathcal{N}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **Images of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).**  \n   From (1) we have \\(HP\\cdot HE = HM\\cdot HC\\). Dividing by \\(HM\\) gives\n   \\[\n   \\frac{HE}{HM}= \\frac{HC}{HP}= -k .\n   \\]\n   Hence the point \\(E\\) is sent by \\(\\mathcal{H}\\) to the point \\(C\\) (the unique point on the ray \\(HE\\) such that \\(\\overrightarrow{HE}= -k\\,\\overrightarrow{HC}\\)). Similarly, \\(F\\) is sent to \\(D\\).\n\n2. **Images of \\(P,M,N\\).**  \n   By definition of the ratio, \\(\\mathcal{H}\\) sends \\(P\\) to the point \\(P'\\) satisfying \\(\\overrightarrow{HP'} = -k\\,\\overrightarrow{HP}= \\overrightarrow{HM}\\); thus \\(P'\\) coincides with \\(M\\). Analogously, \\(P\\) is sent to \\(N\\). Consequently the three vertices \\(P,M,N\\) are permuted among themselves under \\(\\mathcal{H}\\).\n\n3. **Preservation of the nine\u2011point configuration.**  \n   The nine\u2011point circle \\(\\mathcal{N}\\) is uniquely determined by the six points: the mid\u2011points of the sides of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and the feet of its three altitudes. Because \\(\\mathcal{H}\\) sends each vertex of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) to another vertex, it also sends each midpoint of a side to the midpoint of the corresponding side, and each altitude foot to the altitude foot of the image triangle. In particular, the altitude foot from \\(M\\) (which lies on \\(\\Omega\\)) is \\(C\\); the altitude foot from \\(N\\) (which lies on \\(\\Gamma\\)) is \\(D\\). Hence \\(\\mathcal{H}\\) carries the set \\(\\{E,F,P\\}\\) onto the set \\(\\{C,D,M\\}\\), all of which belong to \\(\\mathcal{N}\\).\n\nSince a homothety is determined by the images of three non\u2011collinear points, the image of the whole circle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) under \\(\\mathcal{H}\\) must be the unique circle passing through the images of three non\u2011collinear points of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\); those images are \\(C,D\\) and the midpoint of \\(EF\\), which lies on \\(\\mathcal{N}\\). Therefore the image circle is precisely \\(\\mathcal{N}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Tangency is preserved by the homothety \\(\\mathcal{H}\\))\n\nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to a circle \\(\\gamma\\) at a point \\(T\\), then the image line \\(\\mathcal{H}(\\ell)\\) is tangent to the image circle \\(\\mathcal{H}(\\gamma)\\) at the image point \\(\\mathcal{H}(T)\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA homothety maps circles to circles (or lines) and preserves angles. At a point of tangency the angle between the line and the circle is \\(0^\\circ\\); this angle is invariant under similarity transformations, hence the image line is tangent to the image circle at the image point. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final step (Proof of the required statement)\n\nConsider the line \\(\\ell = AP\\). By construction \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at the point \\(A\\) and is tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at \\(A\\) because \\(A\\) is a common point of the two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) and the power equalities of Lemma\u202f2 give \\(AE=AF\\); consequently the chord \\(EF\\) is symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\), making \\(AP\\) the external angle bisector of \\(\\angle EAF\\) and therefore the tangent at \\(A\\) to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\nApply the homothety \\(\\mathcal{H}\\) of Lemma\u202f3, centred at \\(H\\), to the line \\(\\ell\\). By Lemma\u202f4 the image line \\(\\mathcal{H}(\\ell)\\) is tangent to the image circle \\(\\mathcal{H}(\\omega_{BEF})=\\mathcal{N}\\) at the image point \\(\\mathcal{H}(A)\\). Because the ratio of \\(\\mathcal{H}\\) is \\(-\\dfrac{HP}{HM}\\), the image of a line through \\(P\\) is a line through \\(H\\) parallel to the original line. Hence \\(\\mathcal{H}(\\ell)\\) is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal{N}\\) contains the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) (the feet of the altitudes from \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\)), and \\(\\mathcal{N}\\) coincides with the image of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\mathcal{N}\\). Because \\(\\mathcal{N}\\) and \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) are homothetic with centre \\(H\\), tangency to one implies tangency to the other. Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as required. \u220e"
