\triangle PMN\\).*  \nThe altitude from \\(M\\) to \\(PN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\); similarly the altitude from \\(N\\) meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\). Hence \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the feet of two altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and therefore belong to its nine\u2011point circle \\(\\mathcal N\\).\n\n*Step\u202f4 \u2013 Sketch of the homothety.*  \nConsider the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(H\\) and ratio  \n\\[\nk=-\\frac{HP}{HM}=-\\frac{HP}{HN}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nEquation (1) shows that \\(\\mathcal H\\) sends \\(E\\) to \\(C\\) and \\(F\\) to \\(D\\). Moreover \\(\\mathcal H\\) sends the line \\(AP\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Because a homothety maps a circle to a circle and preserves tangency, the image of the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) of \\(B,E,F\\) under \\(\\mathcal H\\) is a circle passing through \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); by the uniqueness of the nine\u2011point circle this image must be \\(\\mathcal N\\). Consequently the image of the tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at \\(A\\) \u2013 which is the line \\(AP\\) \u2013 is a line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) and tangent to \\(\\mathcal N\\). Since \\(\\mathcal N\\) is the homothetic image of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\), the same line is also tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\nThe only missing piece is a rigorous verification that \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at \\(A\\); this follows from the equal power relations proved in Step\u202f2 together with the fact that \\(A\\) is the only common point of the two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) lying on \\(AP\\). A complete write\u2011up of this verification, together with a careful sign check for the ratio \\(k\\), would close the proof.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution (proved parts)**  \n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{N}>r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that \\(C,M,N,D\\) appear in this order;  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n* \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\);  \n* \\(\\mathcal N\\) the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Power of a point on the line \\(MN\\))\n\nFor any point \\(X\\) on the line \\(MN\\),\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(X)=XM\\cdot XC,\\qquad \n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(X)=XN\\cdot XD .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) at the points \\(M\\) (the centre) and \\(C\\); by definition of power,\n\\(\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(X)=XM\\cdot XC\\). The same reasoning with \\(\\Gamma\\) gives the second equality. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Power equalities for the orthocenter)\n\nThe orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) satisfies\n\n\\[\nHM\\cdot HC = HP\\cdot HE ,\\qquad \nHN\\cdot HD = HP\\cdot HF .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nApply (4) to \\(X=H\\). Because \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) lies on the line \\(AP\\) and is the second intersection of that line with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), we have \\(\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(H)=HM\\cdot HC = HP\\cdot HE\\) and similarly for \\(\\Gamma\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Cyclicity of \\(A,B,E,F\\))\n\nThe points \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic.\n\n*Proof.*  \nApply (4) with \\(X=A\\). Since \\(A\\) belongs to both circles,\n\\[\nAM\\cdot AC = AE\\cdot AB ,\\qquad \nAN\\cdot AD = AF\\cdot AB .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nBecause \\(AM=AN\\) (both equal the respective radii) and \\(AC=AD\\) (both are chords of the two circles through the common point \\(A\\)), the right\u2011hand sides of (6) are equal, giving \\(AE\\cdot AB = AF\\cdot AB\\). As \\(AB\\neq0\\), we obtain \\(AE=AF\\). Equality of the two chords \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) implies that \\(A\\) has equal power with respect to the circle through \\(B,E,F\\); consequently \\(A\\) lies on that circle, i.e. \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Nine\u2011point circle contains \\(C\\) and \\(D\\))\n\nThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the feet of the altitudes from \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); hence they belong to the nine\u2011point circle \\(\\mathcal N\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy construction, \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and on the line \\(MN\\). Since \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(PC\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\); thus \\(C\\) is the foot of the altitude from \\(M\\) to \\(PN\\). An analogous argument shows that \\(D\\) is the foot of the altitude from \\(N\\). The nine\u2011point circle of a triangle passes through the three altitude feet, so \\(C,D\\in\\mathcal N\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5 (Homothety sending \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) to \\(\\mathcal N\\))\n\nDefine the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(H\\) and ratio  \n\n\\[\nk=-\\frac{HP}{HM}=-\\frac{HP}{HN}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThen \\(\\mathcal H\\) maps the circle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) onto the nine\u2011point circle \\(\\mathcal N\\).\n\n*Proof Sketch.*  \n\n1. From (5) we have \\(HM\\cdot HC = HP\\cdot HE\\). Dividing by \\(HM\\) gives \\(\\dfrac{HE}{HM}= -k\\,\\dfrac{HC}{HP}\\), i.e. the directed segment \\(HE\\) is the image of \\(HC\\) under the homothety