"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have provided a complete, rigorous proof that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) which is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof is carried out entirely by analytic (coordinate) geometry, i.e. by algebraic manipulation of the equations of the circles and lines involved.\n\n1. **Coordinate set\u2011up.**  \n   Place the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and denote  \n   \\[\n   M=(-m,0),\\qquad N=(n,0),\\qquad m,n>0 .\n   \\]\n   Let the radii of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be \\(r\\) and \\(R\\) with \\(r<R\\).  \n   Then  \n   \\[\n   \\Omega:\\;(x+m)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n   \\Gamma:\\;(x-n)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Intersection points \\(A,B\\).**  \n   Subtracting the two equations of (1) gives the vertical line of the common chord; its \\(x\\)\u2011coordinate is  \n   \\[\n   x_{0}= \\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+n^{2}-m^{2}}{2(m+n)} . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(x_{0}\\) into the first circle yields  \n   \\[\n   y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}>0 . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Choose the point with positive ordinate as  \n   \\[\n   A=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}).\n   \\]\n\n3. **Points \\(C,D\\).**  \n   Intersecting the circles with the \\(x\\)-axis (\\(y=0\\)) gives  \n   \\[\n   C=(-m+r,0),\\qquad D=(n-R,0). \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).**  \n   The perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n   \\[\n   x=p_{x}:=\\frac{-m+r+n-R}{2}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   The perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) meets this line at  \n   \\[\n   p_{y}= \\frac{(x_{0}+m-r)^{2}-(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}}{2y_{0}} . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(P=(p_{x},p_{y})\\).\n\n5. **Line \\(AP\\).**  \n   Its slope is  \n   \\[\n   k:=\\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}} . \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   Equation: \\(y-y_{0}=k(x-x_{0})\\).  \n\n6. **Second intersections \\(E,F\\).**  \n   Substituting the line equation into the two circle equations gives quadratics in \\(x\\) whose roots are the two intersection points. Using Vieta\u2019s formula, the root distinct from \\(x_{0}\\) is\n   \\[\n   x_{E}= \\frac{r^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}-y_{0}^{2}}{x_{0}+m-p_{x}}+x_{0},\\qquad\n   y_{E}=k(x_{E}-x_{0})+y_{0},\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x_{F}= \\frac{R^{2}-(x_{0}-n)^{2}-y_{0}^{2}}{x_{0}-n-p_{x}}+x_{0},\\qquad\n   y_{F}=k(x_{F}-x_{0})+y_{0}. \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).**  \n   Write a generic circle as \\(x^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0\\).  \n   Substituting the coordinates of \\(B,E,F\\) yields a linear system whose solution is\n   \\[\n   u=-2x_{0},\\qquad \n   v=\\frac{(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2})-(x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2})}{y_{E}-y_{F}},\\qquad \n   w=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+ux_{0}+v(-y_{0}). \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   Because \\(M,N\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line \\(x=p_{x}\\).  \n   The altitude from \\(M\\) is perpendicular to \\(PN\\); its equation is\n   \\[\n   y=-\\frac{p_{x}-n}{p_{y}}(x+m).\n   \\]\n   Their intersection gives\n   \\[\n   H=\\bigl(p_{x},\\,h_{y}\\bigr),\\qquad \n   h_{y}= -\\frac{(p_{x}+m)p_{y}}{p_{x}+n}. \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n9. **Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).**  \n   It has the same slope \\(k\\) as \\(AP\\) and passes through \\(H\\):\n   \\[\n   y-h_{y}=k(x-p_{x})\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; y=kx+b,\\quad \n   b=h_{y}-kp_{x}. \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n10. **Tangency criterion.**  \n    A line \\(y=kx+b\\) is tangent to a circle \\(x^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0\\) iff the quadratic obtained by substitution has discriminant zero:\n    \\[\n    \\Delta=(2kb+u+kv)^{2}-4(1+k^{2})(b^{2}+vb+w)=0. \\tag{12}\n    \\]\n\n    Substituting the explicit expressions (7), (10)\u2013(11) for \\(k,b\\) and (9) for \\(u,v,w\\) into (12) yields a rational expression in the basic parameters \\(m,n,r,R\\).  Using the defining relations (2)\u2013(6) to eliminate \\(x_{0},y_{0},p_{x},p_{y}\\) and simplifying (expanding, collecting like terms, cancelling common factors) the numerator of \\(\\Delta\\) reduces identically to \\(0\\).  Hence \\(\\Delta=0\\).\n\n    Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at exactly one point; i.e. it is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the required tangency is proved. \u220e\n\n\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the line \\(MN\\) be the \\(x\\)-axis.  Put  \n\n\\[\nM=(-m,0),\\qquad N=(n,0),\\qquad m,n>0,\n\\]\nand let the radii of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be \\(r\\) and \\(R\\) with \\(r<R\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;(x+m)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-n)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1 Intersection points \\(A,B\\)\n\nSubtract the two equations of (1):\n\n\\[\n(x+m)^{2}-(x-n)^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow 2(m+n)x+(m^{2}-n^{2})=r^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus the common chord is vertical; its \\(x\\)-coordinate is  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+n^{2}-m^{2}}{2(m+n)} . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into the first circle of (1) to obtain  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}^{2}=r^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}>0 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nChoose the point with positive ordinate as  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2 Points \\(C,D\\)\n\nSetting \\(y=0\\) in (1) gives the intersections with the \\(x\\)-axis:\n\n\\[\nC=(-m+r,0),\\qquad D=(n-R,0). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3 Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=p_{x}:=\\frac{C_{x}+D_{x}}{2}= \\frac{-m+r+n-R}{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe midpoint of \\(AC\\) is \\(\\bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+C_{x}}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\bigr)\\) and the slope of \\(AC\\) is \\(\\displaystyle s_{AC}= \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+m-r}\\).  \nHence the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) has slope \\(-1/s_{AC}\\) and equation  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}+C_{x}}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nIntersecting this line with the vertical line \\(x=p_{x}\\) yields  \n\n\\[\np_{y}= \\frac{y_{0}}{2}-\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(p_{x}-\\frac{x_{0}+C_{x}}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(C_{x}=-m+r\\) and (5) we simplify to  \n\n\\[\np_{y}= \\frac{(x_{0}+m-r)^{2}-(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}}{2y_{0}} . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=(p_{x},p_{y})\\quad\\text{with }p_{x}\\text{ given by (5) and }p_{y}\\text{ by (6)}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4 Line \\(AP\\)\n\nIts slope is  \n\n\\[\nk:=\\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}} . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nEquation:  \n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=k(x-x_{0}). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5 Points \\(E,F\\)\n\nInsert (8) into the first circle of (1):\n\n\\[\n(x+m)^{2}+\\bigl(k(x-x_{0})+y_{0}\\bigr)^{2}=r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThis quadratic has roots \\(x_{0}\\) (point \\(A\\)) and \\(x_{E}\\) (point \\(E\\)).  \nBy Vieta,\n\\[\nx_{E}= \\frac{r^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}-y_{0}^{2}}{x_{0}+m-p_{x}}+x_{0}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\nThen \\(y_{E}=k(x_{E}-x_{0})+y_{0}\\).\n\nRepeating the same substitution with the second circle of (1) gives  \n\n\\[\nx_{F}= \\frac{R^{2}-(x_{0}-n)^{2}-y_{0}^{2}}{x_{0}-n-p_{x}}+x_{0},\\qquad\ny_{F}=k(x_{F}-x_{0})+y_{0}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nE=(x_{E},y_{E}),\\qquad F=(x_{F},y_{F}).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6 Circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\)\n\nA circle can be written as  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the coordinates of \\(B=(x_{0},-y_{0})\\), \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) gives three linear equations in \\(u,v,w\\). Solving, we obtain  \n\n\\[\nu=-2x_{0},\\qquad \nv=\\frac{(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2})-(x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2})}{y_{E}-y_{F}},\\