ag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(A,E,F\\) are collinear, \\(\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle ABE\\).  \n   By the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) we have the correspondence  \n\n   \\[\n   C\\longleftrightarrow D,\\qquad\n   B\\in\\Omega\\cap\\Gamma\\longleftrightarrow B,\\qquad\n   E=\\mathcal H_{A}(C),\\;F=\\mathcal H_{A}(D).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore \\(\\triangle BCD\\) and \\(\\triangle BEF\\) are homothetic with centre \\(A\\); corresponding angles are equal:\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle ABE=\\angle ABC=\\angle BFC . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); consequently \\(CD\\) is a diameter of the circle \\((ACD)\\). Hence \\(\\angle CAD=90^{\\circ}\\). Because \\(B\\) lies on both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), quadrilateral \\(BCAD\\) is cyclic with a right angle at \\(A\\); thus \\(\\angle BFC=\\angle BFE\\) (the latter is the angle subtended by chord \\(BF\\) in the circle \\((BEF)\\)). Combining with (6) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle ABE=\\angle BFE . \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Together with \\(\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle ABE\\) we get (4).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Equality (4) is precisely the tangent criterion: a line through a point is tangent to a circle iff the angle it makes with a chord equals the angle subtended by that chord at the opposite point of the circle. Hence \\(\\ell\\) (the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(R_{\\Omega}<R_{\\Gamma}\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) the intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)) that lies between \\(M\\) and \\(N\\);\n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Homothety centred at \\(A\\)\n\nSince the centres \\(M,N\\) of the two circles lie on the same line, the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) also sends every point of \\(\\Omega\\) to the point of \\(\\Gamma\\) lying on the same ray from \\(A\\). In particular\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H_{A}(\\Omega)=\\Gamma . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe circumcircle \\((ACD)\\) has centre \\(P\\). Because \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) maps the three points \\(A,C,D\\) to themselves, it maps \\((ACD)\\) onto the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle AFD\\); consequently the centre of the image is again \\(P\\). Hence the line \\(AP\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) and we have\n\n\\[\nAP\\parallel AC\\parallel AD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Position of the orthocenter \\(H\\)\n\nConsider \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Its circumcenter is \\(P\\) (by construction \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and \\(M,N\\) lie on the line \\(CD\\)). Because \\(A\\) belongs to both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), it also belongs to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); thus \\(A\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nA well\u2011known property of a triangle states: *the Simson line of a point on the circumcircle is the line through the orthocenter parallel to the line joining the point with the circumcenter*. Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) with the point \\(A\\) we obtain that the Simson line of \\(A\\) is the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Denote this line by \\(\\ell\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\ell\\text{ is the Simson line of }A\\text{ w.r.t. }\\triangle PMN}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nand, in particular,\n\n\\[\n\\ell\\parallel AP . \\tag{3'}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Relation between \\(\\triangle BCD\\) and \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nFrom (1) the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) sends the triangle \\(BCD\\) to the triangle \\(BEF\\) (the vertex \\(B\\) is fixed because it belongs to both circles). Therefore the two triangles are similar and corresponding angles are equal:\n\n\\[\n\\angle ABC = \\angle BFC ,\\qquad\n\\angle ACB = \\angle BCF ,\\qquad\n\\angle ABD = \\angle BFE . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIn particular,\n\n\\[\n\\angle ABE = \\angle ABC = \\angle BFC . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. A right angle on \\(CD\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); consequently \\(CD\\) is a diameter of the circle \\((ACD)\\) and\n\n\\[\n\\angle CAD = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(B\\) lies on both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\) is cyclic (it is the union of the two intersecting circles). In a cyclic quadrilateral the angle subtended by a diameter equals a right angle, so from (6) we also have\n\n\\[\n\\angle CBD = \\angle BFD = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus points \\(B,F,D\\) are concyclic with centre on the line \\(AP\\); consequently the angle subtended by chord \\(BF\\) at point \\(E\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at point \\(D\\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle BFE = \\angle BFD . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nCombining (5), (7) and (8) yields\n\n\\[\n\\angle ABE = \\angle BFC = \\angle BFD = \\angle BFE . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Tangency criterion\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(A,E,F\\) are collinear, the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and the chord \\(BE\\) equals\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle ABE . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nFrom (9) we have \\(\\angle ABE = \\angle BFE\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nEquality (11) is exactly the tangent condition: a line through a point is tangent to a circle iff the angle it makes with a chord equals the angle subtended by that chord at the opposite point of the circle. Therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\nSince \\(\\ell\\) is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), the required statement is proved. \u220e"
