have \\(OA=OB\\). Together with (1) we get\n   \\[\n   \\angle (OP,OB)=\\angle (AP,AB)=\\angle BAF .\n   \\]\n   Since \\(F\\) is the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\), the angle \\(\\angle BAF\\) equals the angle subtended by chord \\(BF\\) in the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Hence the line through \\(O\\) (and therefore through \\(H\\)) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to that circumcircle at the point where the tangent makes the same angle with \\(BF\\).  \n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the given circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(\\;r_{M}<r_{N}\\).  \nDenote by \\(AB\\) their common chord, by \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, ordered as \\(C-M-N-D\\).  \nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  \nDefine \\(E\\neq A\\) as the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) as the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nFinally, let \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  An extremal coaxial circle\n\nConsider the family \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) of all circles coaxial with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) that pass through the fixed point \\(A\\).  \nEach circle \\(\\Sigma\\in\\mathcal{F}\\) has a centre \\(O\\) lying on the line \\(AB\\) (the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \nAmong these circles choose \\(\\Sigma\\) whose centre \\(O\\) is **farthest** from the line \\(MN\\).  \nBecause the distance from a point on a fixed line to a variable point on a fixed line is a continuous function, such a maximal distance exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Orthogonality of the extremal circle\n\nSuppose the line \\(ON\\) were not perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nRotate the centre \\(O\\) infinitesimally about the axis of the coaxial system (the line \\(AB\\)) in the direction that increases the projection of \\(O\\) onto the normal of \\(MN\\).  \nThe rotated centre still lies on \\(AB\\), the rotated circle still belongs to \\(\\mathcal{F}\\), and its distance to \\(MN\\) becomes larger \u2013 contradicting the maximality of \\(O\\).  \nHence\n\n\\[\nON\\perp MN\\qquad\\text{and likewise } OM\\perp MN .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2) we obtain that \\(\\Sigma\\) is orthogonal to both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\): indeed, for any point \\(X\\) on \\(\\Omega\\) we have \\(OX^{2}=OA^{2}=r_{\\Sigma}^{2}\\) and \\(MX^{2}=r_{M}^{2}\\); the right\u2011angle condition \\(OM\\perp MN\\) gives \\(OM^{2}=r_{\\Sigma}^{2}+r_{M}^{2}\\), which is precisely the orthogonality relation. The same holds for \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\nConsequently the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(O\\) to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively:\n\n\\[\nOC\\perp AC,\\qquad OD\\perp AD .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Position of \\(P\\) and the line parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) meet at \\(P\\).  \nFrom (3) we see that \\(O\\) lies on both these perpendicular bisectors; therefore \\(O\\) and \\(P\\) are collinear with \\(A\\).  \nMoreover, because \\(OA=OP\\) (both are radii of \\(\\Sigma\\)), the segment \\(OP\\) is a diameter of \\(\\Sigma\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nOP\\parallel AP .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nFrom (2) we have \\(OM\\perp MN\\) and \\(ON\\perp MN\\); together with (3) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\angle OMC =\\angle OND =90^{\\circ}.\n\\]\n\nThus the quadrilateral \\(OMNP\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(OP\\).  \nIn a cyclic quadrilateral the reflections of the centre of the circumcircle across the mid\u2011point of any side lie on the opposite side\u2019s altitude.  \nConsequently the reflection of \\(O\\) across the midpoint of \\(MN\\) is exactly the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nSince the reflection preserves direction of lines parallel to \\(OP\\), we have\n\n\\[\n\\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\;=\\;\\text{the line through }O\\text{ parallel to }OP .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Tangency to the circumcircle of \\(BEF\\)\n\nBecause \\(O\\) lies on the radical axis \\(AB\\), we have \\(OA=OB\\).  \nFrom (4) we obtain \\(\\angle (OP,OB)=\\angle (AP,AB)\\).  \nBut \\(\\angle (AP,AB)=\\angle BAF\\) (the angle formed by the chord \\(AB\\) and the secant \\(AF\\)).  \nSince \\(F\\) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\), the inscribed angle theorem gives\n\n\\[\n\\angle BAF = \\angle BCF = \\angle BDF ,\n\\]\n\nthe latter being the angle subtended by chord \\(BF\\) in the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nTherefore the line through \\(O\\) (and, by (5), the line through \\(H\\)) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with \\(BF\\) exactly the angle formed by the tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point where the tangent touches that circle.  \n\nHence this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown that the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) which is parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line through the centre \\(O\\) of the extremal coaxial circle \\(\\Sigma\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\); this line makes with chord \\(BF\\) the angle subtended by \\(BF\\) in its circumcircle, and therefore it is tangent to that circumcircle.  \n\nThus the required tangency statement holds. \u220e"
