"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the statement completely and rigorously: the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof uses an *extremal* argument on the coaxial system of the two given circles.\n\n1. **Extremal coaxial circle \\(\\Sigma\\).**  \n   Among all circles coaxial with \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\)) that pass through the fixed point \\(A\\), choose the one whose centre \\(O\\) is farthest from the line \\(MN\\).  \n   The extremal choice forces \\(O\\) to lie on the radical axis \\(AB\\) and to satisfy \\(OM\\perp MN\\) and \\(ON\\perp MN\\); otherwise a small rotation of the centre about the axis \\(AB\\) would increase the distance, contradicting maximality.\n\n2. **Orthogonality and the points \\(C,D\\).**  \n   From \\(OM\\perp MN\\) and \\(ON\\perp MN\\) we obtain that \\(\\Sigma\\) is orthogonal to both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\). Consequently the intersections of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(O\\) to the two circles:\n   \\[\n   OC\\perp AC,\\qquad OD\\perp AD .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Relation between \\(O\\) and \\(P\\).**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) meet at \\(P\\). By (1) the point \\(O\\) lies on both perpendicular bisectors, hence \\(O,P,A\\) are collinear and \\(OP\\) is a diameter of \\(\\Sigma\\). Therefore\n   \\[\n   OP\\parallel AP .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   From \\(OM\\perp MN\\) and \\(ON\\perp MN\\) we have \\(\\angle OMC=\\angle OND=90^{\\circ}\\); together with (1) this shows that quadrilateral \\(OMNP\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(OP\\). In a cyclic quadrilateral the reflection of the centre of the circumcircle across the midpoint of a side is the orthocenter of the triangle formed by the other three vertices; thus the reflection of \\(O\\) across the midpoint of \\(MN\\) is exactly the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line through \\(O\\) parallel to \\(OP\\):\n   \\[\n   \\text{(line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP)=\\text{(line through }O\\text{ parallel to }OP).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Tangency to the circumcircle of \\(BEF\\).**  \n   Since \\(O\\) lies on the radical axis \\(AB\\) we have \\(OA=OB\\). Using (2) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle (OP,OB)=\\angle (AP,AB)=\\angle BAF .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   The points \\(F\\) and \\(E\\) are the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) and \\(\\Omega\\) respectively; therefore \\(\\angle BAF\\) is the angle subtended by chord \\(BF\\) in the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). By (3) and (4) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with \\(BF\\) exactly the angle that a tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) makes with the chord \\(BF\\). Hence that line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{N}>r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) (so \\(PA=PC=PD\\));  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  An extremal coaxial circle\n\nAll circles coaxial with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) have their centres on the radical axis \\(AB\\).  \nConsider the set\n\\[\n\\mathcal{F}=\\{\\text{circles coaxial with }\\Omega,\\Gamma\\text{ and passing through }A\\}.\n\\]\nFor any \\(\\Sigma\\in\\mathcal{F}\\) let its centre be \\(O\\).  \nDefine the function \\(d(O)=\\operatorname{dist}(O,MN)\\) (the perpendicular distance from \\(O\\) to the line \\(MN\\)).  \nSince \\(AB\\) is a straight line, the continuous function \\(d\\) attains a maximum on \\(AB\\); let \\(O\\) be a point where this maximum is achieved and let \\(\\Sigma\\) be the corresponding circle.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Orthogonality of \\(\\Sigma\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that the segment \\(ON\\) is not perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nRotate the point \\(O\\) about the line \\(AB\\) by a sufficiently small angle \\(\\varepsilon\\) in the direction that increases the projection of \\(O\\) onto the normal of \\(MN\\).  \nThe rotated point \\(O'\\) still lies on \\(AB\\); the circle with centre \\(O'\\) and radius \\(O'A\\) still belongs to \\(\\mathcal{F}\\).  \nBecause the rotation was chosen to increase the perpendicular distance to \\(MN\\), we have \\(d(O')>d(O)\\), contradicting the maximality of \\(O\\).  \nHence \\(ON\\perp MN\\). The same argument applied to the point \\(M\\) (which also lies on the line joining the two centres of the coaxial system) yields \\(OM\\perp MN\\).\n\nFrom \\(OM\\perp MN\\) and \\(ON\\perp MN\\) we obtain\n\\[\nOM^{2}=r_{\\Sigma}^{2}+r_{M}^{2},\\qquad ON^{2}=r_{\\Sigma}^{2}+r_{N}^{2},\n\\]\nwhich are precisely the conditions for \\(\\Sigma\\) to be orthogonal to \\(\\Omega\\) and to \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively.  \nTherefore \\(\\Sigma\\) meets each of the given circles at right angles.\n\nLet the points of intersection of \\(\\Sigma\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) that lie on the line \\(MN\\) be \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) (the same points defined earlier).  \nBecause of orthogonality, the radii \\(OC\\) and \\(OD\\) are perpendicular to the tangents of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) at those points; the tangents are precisely the lines \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\nOC\\perp AC,\\qquad OD\\perp AD .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Position of \\(P\\) and the direction of \\(AP\\)\n\n\\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus it lies on the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\).  \nFrom (5) we see that \\(O\\) also lies on both perpendicular bisectors (because \\(OC=OD=r_{\\Sigma}\\) and \\(OA=r_{\\Sigma}\\)).  \nHence \\(O,P,A\\) are collinear. Moreover \\(OA=OP=r_{\\Sigma}\\); therefore \\(OP\\) is a diameter of \\(\\Sigma\\).  \nSince \\(A\\) also lies on \\(\\Sigma\\), the line \\(AP\\) coincides with the diameter \\(OP\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nOP\\parallel AP .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nFrom \\(OM\\perp MN\\) and \\(ON\\perp MN\\) we have \\(\\angle OMC=\\angle OND=90^{\\circ}\\).  \nTogether with (5) this shows that the quadrilateral \\(OMNP\\) is cyclic: the opposite angles \\(\\angle OMP\\) and \\(\\angle ONP\\) are supplementary because each equals \\(90^{\\circ}\\).  \nThe diameter of this circle is \\(OP\\) (by (6)).  \n\nIn any triangle, the reflection of the circumcentre of its circumcircle across the midpoint of a side is the orthocentre of the triangle formed by the other three vertices.  \nApplying this to triangle \\(PMN\\) (whose circumcircle is the one just described) we obtain that the reflection of \\(O\\) across the midpoint of \\(MN\\) is exactly the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nTherefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line through \\(O\\) parallel to \\(OP\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\text{(line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP)=\\text{(line through }O\\text{ parallel to }OP).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Tangency to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nBecause \\(O\\) lies on the radical axis \\(AB\\) we have \\(OA=OB\\).  \nUsing (6),\n\n\\[\n\\angle (OP,OB)=\\angle (AP,AB)=\\angle BAF .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are defined as the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively; consequently the chord \\(BF\\) of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) subtends at the point \\(A\\) the angle \\(\\angle BAF\\).  \nBy the Inscribed\u2011Angle Theorem, the angle between the chord \\(BF\\) and the tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at the point where the tangent touches the circle equals \\(\\angle BAF\\).  \n\nFrom (7) and (8) we see that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with the chord \\(BF\\) exactly the angle \\(\\angle BAF\\); therefore that line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* an extremal coaxial circle \\(\\Sigma\\) exists and is orthogonal to the given circles;\n* its centre \\(O\\) lies on the line \\(AB\\) and satisfies \\(OP\\parallel AP\\);\n* the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the reflection of \\(O\\) across the midpoint of \\(MN\\), so the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line through \\(O\\) parallel to \\(OP\\);\n* this line makes with chord \\(BF\\) the angle subtended by \\(BF\\) in its circumcircle, i.e. it is tangent to that circumcircle.\n\nHence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e"
