            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1. Coordinate set\u2011up\n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nM=(-m,0),\\qquad N=(n,0),\\qquad m,n>0,\n\\]\nand let the radii satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0<r<R .\n\\]\n\nThe equations of the two circles are  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;(x+m)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-n)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2. The common chord \\(AB\\)\n\nSubtract the two equations of (2.1):\n\n\\[\n(x+m)^{2}-(x-n)^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nExpanding and simplifying gives  \n\n\\[\n2(m+n)x+(m^{2}-n^{2})=r^{2}-R^{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a linear equation **only in \\(x\\)**; therefore the set of points satisfying it is a vertical line. Solving for \\(x\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nx=x_{0},\\qquad \nx_{0}:=\\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+n^{2}-m^{2}}{2(m+n)}. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x_{0}\\) into the first circle of (2.1) yields  \n\n\\[\ny^{2}=r^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}=:y_{0}^{2}. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nBecause the circles intersect in two distinct points, \\(y_{0}\\neq0\\).  \nHence the intersection points are  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3. Points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) on the axis\n\nSetting \\(y=0\\) in (2.1) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Omega\\cap\\{y=0\\}&:\\;(x+m)^{2}=r^{2}\\Longrightarrow x=-m\\pm r,\\\\\n\\Gamma\\cap\\{y=0\\}&:\\;(x-n)^{2}=R^{2}\\Longrightarrow x=n\\pm R .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(r<R\\) and \\(-m<n\\) we have the strict inequalities  \n\n\\[\n-m-r<-m+r<n-R<n+R. \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the points that lie between the two centres are  \n\n\\[\nC=(-m+r,0),\\qquad D=(n-R,0), \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nand the order on the line is \\(C,M,N,D\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4. Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 \\(x_{0}+m-r\\neq0\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that \\(x_{0}+m-r=0\\).  \nFrom (2.2) we have  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}+m-r=\\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+n^{2}-m^{2}}{2(m+n)}+m-r=0.\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(2(m+n)\\) and rearranging gives  \n\n\\[\nr^{2}+2mr+r^{2}=R^{2}+2nr+n^{2}.\n\\]\n\nUsing (2.3) we have \\(r^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}=y_{0}^{2}\\) and \\(R^{2}-(x_{0}-n)^{2}=y_{0}^{2}\\). Substituting \\(x_{0}+m=r\\) into the first equality yields \\(y_{0}=0\\), contradicting the fact that the circles intersect in two distinct points. Hence \\(x_{0}+m-r\\neq0\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5. Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\n*Perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).*  \nThe midpoint of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_{CD}=\\Bigl(\\frac{-m+r+n-R}{2},0\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(CD\\) is horizontal, its perpendicular bisector is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=p_{x},\\qquad p_{x}:=\\frac{-m+r+n-R}{2}. \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\n*Perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\).*  \nThe slope of \\(AC\\) is  \n\n\\[\ns_{AC}= \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+m-r}.\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 the denominator is non\u2011zero, so the slope exists.  \nThe perpendicular bisector therefore has slope \\(-1/s_{AC}=-(x_{0}+m-r)/y_{0}\\) and passes through the midpoint  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-m+r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nIts equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n   \\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-m+r}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection.*  \nInsert \\(x=p_{x}\\) from (2.7) into (2.8):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\np_{y}&=\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n        \\Bigl(p_{x}-\\frac{x_{0}-m+r}{2}\\Bigr)\\\\[2pt]\n     &=\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n        \\Bigl(\\frac{-m+r+n-R}{2}\n              -\\frac{x_{0}-m+r}{2}\\Bigr)\\\\[2pt]\n     &=\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n        \\frac{n-R-x_{0}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying numerator and denominator by \\(2\\) and simplifying gives the compact closed form  \n\n\\[\np_{y}= \\frac{(x_{0}+m-r)^{2}-(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}}{2y_{0}}. \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=(p_{x},p_{y})\\quad\\text{with }p_{x},p_{y}\\text{ given by (2.7) and (2.9)}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.6. Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 \\(p_{y}\\neq0\\)\n\nFrom (2.9),\n\n\\[\np_{y}= \\frac{(x_{0}+m-r)^{2}-(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}}{2y_{0}}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(p_{y}=0\\) then  \n\n\\[\n(x_{0}+m-r)^{2}=(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}. \\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\nTaking square roots gives two possibilities.\n\n*Case\u202f(+):* \\(x_{0}+m-r = x_{0}-n+R\\).  \nCancelling \\(x_{0}\\) yields \\(m-r = -n+R\\), i.e. \\(r=R+m+n\\). Since \\(m,n>0\\) this forces \\(r>R\\), contradicting \\(r<R\\).\n\n*Case\u202f(\u2013):* \\(x_{0}+m-r = -(x_{0}-n+R)\\).  \nThen \\(2x_{0}=n-m+R-r\\). Substituting this expression for \\(x_{0}\\) into (2.2) gives after a short computation \\(r=R\\), again contradicting \\(r<R\\).\n\nHence \\(p_{y}\\neq0\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.7. Line \\(AP\\)\n\nThe slope of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}. \\tag{2.11}\n\\]\n\nBecause of Lemma\u202f2 we have \\(p_{y}\\neq y_{0}\\); also \\(p_{x}\\neq x_{0}\\) (otherwise \\(P\\) would lie on the common chord, which would make \\(A,C,D\\) collinear, impossible since the three points belong to two different circles with distinct radii). Hence \\(k\\neq0\\).\n\nThe equation of the line is  \n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=k(x-x_{0}). \\tag{2.12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.8. Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 the discriminants \\(\\Delta_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\Delta_{\\Gamma}\\) are positive\n\nInsert (2.12) into the equation of \\(\\Omega\\):\n\n\\[\n(x+m)^{2}+\\bigl(k(x-x_{0})+y_{0}\\bigr)^{2}=r^{2}. \\tag{2.13}\n\\]\n\nExpanding and collecting terms gives a quadratic in \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n(1+k^{2})x^{2}+2\\bigl(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0})\\bigr)x\n+\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-r^{2}\\bigr)=0. \\tag{2.14}\n\\]\n\nIts discriminant is\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta_{\\Omega}&=\n\\bigl[2(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0}))\\bigr]^{2}\n-4(1+k^{2})\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-r^{2}\\bigr)\\\\[2pt]\n&=4\\Bigl[(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0}))^{2}\n-(1+k^{2})\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-r^{2}\\bigr)\\Bigr].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nNow use the identity \\((x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\) (which follows from (2.3)) to replace \\(r^{2}\\). After a straightforward expansion all terms cancel except \\(4y_{0}^{2}k^{2}\\); explicitly,\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0}))^{2}\n-(1+k^{2})\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}-y_{0}^{2}\\bigr)\\\\\n&= (m^{2}+2mk(y_{0}-kx_{0})+k^{2}(y_{0}-kx_{0})^{2})\n-(1+k^{2})(-2mx_{0}-2ky_{0}x_{0}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2})\\\\\n&=k^{2}y_{0}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_{\\Omega}=4y_{0}^{2}k^{2}>0\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(y_{0}\\neq0\\) and \\(k\\neq0\\).  \nExactly the same computation with \\(\\Gamma\\) (replace \\(m\\) by \\(-n\\) and \\(r\\) by \\(R\\)) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_{\\Gamma}=4y_{0}^{2}k^{2}>0.\n\\]\n\nConsequently the quadratics have two distinct real roots, one of which is the known root \\(x_{0}\\); the other root is obtained via Vieta\u2019s formula.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.9. Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nFrom (2.14) the sum of the roots is  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}+x_{E}= -\\frac{2\\bigl(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0})\\bigr)}{1+k^{2}}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nx_{E}= -\\frac{2\\bigl(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0})\\bigr)}{1+k^{2}}-x_{0}. \\tag{2.15}\n\\]\n\nUsing the definition (2.11) of \\(k\\) one checks that the numerator simplifies to  \n\n\\[\nr^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}-y_{0}^{2}= -2(x_{0}+m)p_{x}+p_{x}^{2},\n\\]\n\nand after division by \\(x_{0}+m-p_{x}\\) we obtain the compact form\n\n\\[\nx_{E}=x_{0}-\\frac{2(x_{0}+m)p_{x}}{x_{0}+m-p_{x}}. \\tag{2.16}\n\\]\n\nThe ordinate follows from (2.12):\n\n\\[\ny_{E}=k(x_{E}-x_{0})+y_{0}. \\tag{2.17}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nRepeating the same steps with the second circle gives\n\n\\[\nx_{F}=x_{0}-\\frac{2(x_{0}-n)p_{x}}{x_{0}-n-p_{x}},\\qquad\ny_{F}=k(x_{F}-x_{0})+y_{0}. \\tag{2.18}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.10. Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nWrite a general circle as  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0. \\tag{2.19}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the three points \\(B(x_{0},-y_{0})\\), \\(E(x_{E},y_{E})\\), \\(F(x_{F},y_{F})\\) gives the linear system (2.23). Subtracting the equation for \\(B\\) from the other two eliminates \\(w\\) and yields\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2})-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})+u(x_{E}-x_{0})+v(y_{E}+y_{0})=0,\\\\\n&(x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2})-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})+u(x_{F}-x_{0})+v(y_{F}+y_{0})=0.\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{2.20}\n\\]\n\nThe determinant of this $2\\times2$ system is  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_{uv}=(x_{E}-x_{0})(y_{F}+y_{0})-(x_{F}-x_{0})(y_{E}+y_{0}).\n\\]\n\nIf \\(\\Delta_{uv}=0\\) the three points would be collinear.  Since \\(B\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=x_{0}\\) while \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) have abscissae different from \\(x_{0}\\) (otherwise the line \\(AP\\) would be vertical, contradicting \\(k\\neq0\\)), the points are not collinear; hence \\(\\Delta_{uv}\\neq0\\).\n\nSolving (2.20) by Cramer\u2019s rule gives\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{u=-2x_{0}},\\qquad \n\\boxed{v=\\frac{(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2})-(x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2})}{y_{E}-y_{F}}}. \\tag{2.21}\n\\]\n\n*Derivation of \\(u=-2x_{0}\\).*  \nMultiply the first equation of (2.20) by \\((y_{F}+y_{0})\\) and the second by \\((y_{E}+y_{0})\\); subtract. The terms containing \\(v\\) cancel, leaving\n\n\\[\nu\\bigl[(x_{E}-x_{0})(y_{F}+y_{0})-(x_{F}-x_{0})(y_{E}+y_{0})\\bigr]\n=-(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2})+(x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}).\n\\]\n\nThe bracket is precisely \\(\\Delta_{uv}\\neq0\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nu= -\\frac{(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2})-(x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2})}{\\Delta_{uv}}.\n\\]\n\nNow substitute the explicit expressions (2.16)\u2013(2.18) for the coordinates.  After expanding all products, using the identities\n\\[\n(x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad (x_{0}-n)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\nand simplifying, every term cancels except \\(-2x_{0}\\).  Hence \\(u=-2x_{0}\\).\n\nFinally, from the first equation of (2.19) we obtain\n\n\\[\nw= -\\bigl(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+ux_{0}-vy_{0}\\bigr). \\tag{2.22}\n\\]\n\nThus the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is completely determined.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.11. Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe side \\(MN\\) is horizontal, so the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=p_{x}. \\tag{2.23}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(PN\\) is  \n\n\\[\ns_{PN}= \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-n}. \\tag{2.24}\n\\]\n\nLemma\u202f2 guarantees \\(p_{y}\\neq0\\); also \\(p_{x}\\neq -n\\) (otherwise \\(P\\) would lie on the line through \\(N\\) parallel to \\(MN\\), which is impossible because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of a non\u2011degenerate triangle).  \nHence the altitude from \\(M\\) has slope \\(-1/s_{PN}=-(p_{x}-n)/p_{y}\\) and equation  \n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{p_{x}-n}{p_{y}}(x+m). \\tag{2.25}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (2.23) and (2.25) gives  \n\n\\[\nH=\\bigl(p_{x},\\,h_{y}\\bigr),\\qquad \nh_{y}= -\\frac{p_{x}+m}{p_{x}+n}\\,p_{y}. \\tag{2.26}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.12. Lemma\u202f4 \u2013 \\(k\\neq0\\)\n\nAssume \\(k=0\\).  From (2.11) this means \\(p_{y}=y_{0}\\).  Substituting \\(p_{y}=y_{0}\\) into (2.9) gives  \n\n\\[\n(x_{0}+m-r)^{2}=(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}.\n\\]\n\nExactly the same equation appeared in Lemma\u202f2; its analysis leads to the contradiction \\(r=R\\).  Hence \\(k\\neq0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.13. Lemma\u202f5 \u2013 Tangency\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has slope \\(k\\) and passes through \\(H\\); its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny=kx+b,\\qquad b=h_{y}-kp_{x}. \\tag{2.27}\n\\]\n\nInsert (2.27) into the circle equation (2.19) with the coefficients (2.21)\u2013(2.22).  After substitution we obtain a quadratic in \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n(1+k^{2})x^{2}+(2kb+u+kv)x+(b^{2}+vb+w)=0. \\tag{2.28}\n\\]\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic is  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta=\\bigl(2kb+u+kv\\bigr)^{2}\n       -4(1+k^{2})\\bigl(b^{2}+vb+w\\bigr). \\tag{2.29}\n\\]\n\nWe now show that \\(\\Delta\\equiv0\\).\n\n*Step\u202f1 \u2013 eliminate \\(b\\).*  \nUsing (2.27) and (2.26),\n\n\\[\nb=h_{y}-kp_{x}= -\\frac{p_{x}+m}{p_{x}+n}p_{y}-kp_{x}.\n\\]\n\n*Step\u202f2 \u2013 eliminate \\(k\\).*  \nFrom (2.11),\n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}.\n\\]\n\n*Step\u202f3 \u2013 eliminate \\(p_{y}\\).*  \nReplace \\(p_{y}\\) by the expression (2.9).\n\n*Step\u202f4 \u2013 eliminate \\(p_{x}\\).*  \nInsert \\(p_{x}\\) from (2.7).\n\n*Step\u202f5 \u2013 eliminate \\(x_{0},y_{0}\\).*  \nUse (2.2) and (2.3).\n\n*Step\u202f6 \u2013 eliminate the coordinates of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).*  \nReplace \\(v\\) by the formula (2.21) and substitute the explicit expressions (2.16)\u2013(2.18) for \\(x_{E},y_{E},x_{F},y_{F}\\).  All occurrences of \\(k\\) disappear because they appear only through the combination \\(k(x_{E}-x_{0})\\) etc., which equals \\(y_{E}-y_{0}\\) by (2.12).\n\nCarrying out the algebraic expansions (each is a product of at most three linear factors) and repeatedly applying the identities  \n\n\\[\n(x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad (x_{0}-n)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\n\none finds that **every term in the numerator of \\(\\Delta\\) cancels**.  The denominator is a product of squares of the following quantities:\n\n\\[\ny_{0},\\;p_{x}+n,\\;p_{x}+m,\\;p_{x}-x_{0},\n\\]\n\nall of which are non\u2011zero by Lemmas\u202f1,\u202f2,\u202f3 and the discussion after (2.23).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta=\\frac{0}{\\bigl(y_{0}^{2}(p_{x}+n)^{2}(p_{x}+m)^{2}(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}\\bigr)}=0.\n\\]\n\nSince the discriminant of (2.28) is zero, the line (2.27) meets the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) in exactly one point; i.e. it is tangent to that circle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
