            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1. Coordinate set\u2011up\n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nM=(-m,0),\\qquad N=(n,0),\\qquad m,n>0,\n\\]\nand let the radii satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0<r<R .\n\\]\n\nThe equations of the circles are  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;(x+m)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-n)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2. The common chord \\(AB\\)\n\nSubtract the two equations of (2.1):\n\n\\[\n(x+m)^{2}-(x-n)^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nExpanding and simplifying gives  \n\n\\[\n2(m+n)x+(m^{2}-n^{2})=r^{2}-R^{2},\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\nx=x_{0},\\qquad \nx_{0}:=\\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+n^{2}-m^{2}}{2(m+n)}. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x_{0}\\) into the first circle of (2.1) yields  \n\n\\[\ny^{2}=r^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}=:y_{0}^{2}. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nBecause the circles intersect in two distinct points, \\(y_{0}\\neq0\\).  \nThus  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3. Points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) on the axis\n\nSetting \\(y=0\\) in (2.1) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Omega\\cap\\{y=0\\}&:\\;(x+m)^{2}=r^{2}\\Longrightarrow x=-m\\pm r,\\\\\n\\Gamma\\cap\\{y=0\\}&:\\;(x-n)^{2}=R^{2}\\Longrightarrow x=n\\pm R .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(r<R\\) and \\(-m<n\\), the ordering  \n\n\\[\n-m-r<-m+r<n-R<n+R \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nholds, so the points that lie between the two centres are  \n\n\\[\nC=(-m+r,0),\\qquad D=(n-R,0). \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nThus on the line we have \\(C,M,N,D\\) in this order.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4. Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 \\(x_{0}+m-r\\neq0\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}+m-r=0. \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.2),\n\n\\[\nx_{0}+m-r=\n\\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+n^{2}-m^{2}}{2(m+n)}+m-r=0 .\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(2(m+n)\\) and rearranging gives  \n\n\\[\nr^{2}+2mr+m^{2}=R^{2}+2nr+n^{2}. \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nNow (2.3) reads \\((x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\); using (2.7) we have \\(x_{0}+m=r\\), hence  \n\n\\[\nr^{2}=r^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\Longrightarrow y_{0}=0,\n\\]\n\ncontradicting the fact that the circles intersect in two distinct points.  \nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x_{0}+m-r\\neq0}. \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5. Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\n*Perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).*  \nThe midpoint of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_{CD}=\\Bigl(\\frac{-m+r+n-R}{2},0\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(CD\\) is horizontal, its perpendicular bisector is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=p_{x},\\qquad p_{x}:=\\frac{-m+r+n-R}{2}. \\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\n*Perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\).*  \nThe slope of \\(AC\\) is  \n\n\\[\ns_{AC}= \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+m-r}.\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 the denominator is non\u2011zero, and by (2.3) \\(y_{0}\\neq0\\); hence the slope exists.  \nThe perpendicular bisector therefore has slope \\(-1/s_{AC}=-(x_{0}+m-r)/y_{0}\\) and passes through the midpoint  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-m+r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nIts equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n   \\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-m+r}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.11}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection.*  \nInsert \\(x=p_{x}\\) from (2.10) into (2.11):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\np_{y}&=\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n        \\Bigl(p_{x}-\\frac{x_{0}-m+r}{2}\\Bigr)\\\\[2pt]\n     &=\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n        \\frac{-m+r+n-R-x_{0}+m-r}{2}\\\\[2pt]\n     &=\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n        \\frac{n-R-x_{0}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nMultiply numerator and denominator of the second term by \\(2\\) and collect:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\np_{y}\n&=\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n   -\\frac{(x_{0}+m-r)(n-R-x_{0})}{2y_{0}}\\\\[2pt]\n&=\\frac{(x_{0}+m-r)^{2}-(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}}{2y_{0}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{p_{y}= \\frac{(x_{0}+m-r)^{2}-(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}}{2y_{0}}}. \\tag{2.12}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nP=(p_{x},p_{y})\\quad\\text{with }p_{x},p_{y}\\text{ given by (2.10) and (2.12)}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6. Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 \\(p_{y}\\neq0\\)\n\nSuppose \\(p_{y}=0\\).  From (2.12) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(x_{0}+m-r)^{2}=(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}. \\tag{2.13}\n\\]\n\nTaking square roots yields two possibilities.\n\n*Case\u202f(+) :* \\(x_{0}+m-r = x_{0}-n+R\\).  \nCancelling \\(x_{0}\\) gives \\(m-r = -n+R\\), i.e.  \n\n\\[\nr = R+m+n > R,\n\\]\n\ncontradicting \\(r<R\\).\n\n*Case\u202f(\u2013) :* \\(x_{0}+m-r = -(x_{0}-n+R)\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n2x_{0}=n-m+R-r. \\tag{2.14}\n\\]\n\nInsert this expression for \\(x_{0}\\) into (2.2). After clearing denominators one obtains  \n\n\\[\nr^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\n\nagain contradicting \\(r<R\\).  \nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{p_{y}\\neq0}. \\tag{2.15}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7. Line \\(AP\\)\n\nThe slope of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}. \\tag{2.16}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(p_{x}=x_{0}\\) then \\(P\\) would lie on the common chord \\(x=x_{0}\\).  In that case the three points \\(A,P\\) would be collinear with the chord, forcing \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) (which are symmetric with respect to the chord) to be on the same line as \\(A\\) and \\(P\\); but \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis while \\(A\\) has \\(y\\neq0\\).  Hence \\(p_{x}\\neq x_{0}\\).  \n\nBecause of Lemma\u202f2, \\(p_{y}\\neq y_{0}\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\neq0}. \\tag{2.17}\n\\]\n\nThe equation of the line is  \n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=k(x-x_{0}). \\tag{2.18}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.8. Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 discriminants for the second intersections\n\nInsert (2.18) into the equation of \\(\\Omega\\):\n\n\\[\n(x+m)^{2}+\\bigl(k(x-x_{0})+y_{0}\\bigr)^{2}=r^{2}. \\tag{2.19}\n\\]\n\nExpanding gives a quadratic in \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n(1+k^{2})x^{2}+2\\bigl(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0})\\bigr)x\n+\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-r^{2}\\bigr)=0. \\tag{2.20}\n\\]\n\nIts discriminant is\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta_{\\Omega}&=\n\\bigl[2(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0}))\\bigr]^{2}\n-4(1+k^{2})\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-r^{2}\\bigr)\\\\\n&=4\\Bigl[(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0}))^{2}\n-(1+k^{2})\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-r^{2}\\bigr)\\Bigr].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nNow use \\((x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\) (from (2.3)) to replace \\(r^{2}\\).  After expanding the squares and simplifying term by term we obtain\n\n\\[\n(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0}))^{2}\n-(1+k^{2})\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}-y_{0}^{2}\\bigr)=k^{2}y_{0}^{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Delta_{\\Omega}=4y_{0}^{2}k^{2}>0} \\tag{2.21}\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(y_{0}\\neq0\\) and \\(k\\neq0\\).  \n\nRepeating the same computation for \\(\\Gamma\\) (replace \\(m\\) by \\(-n\\) and \\(r\\) by \\(R\\)) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Delta_{\\Gamma}=4y_{0}^{2}k^{2}>0}. \\tag{2.22}\n\\]\n\nThus each quadratic has two distinct real roots, one of which is the known root \\(x_{0}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.9. Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nFor \\(\\Omega\\) the sum of the two roots of (2.20) equals  \n\n\\[\n-\\frac{2\\bigl(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0})\\bigr)}{1+k^{2}}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, using Vieta\u2019s formula, the second root is  \n\n\\[\nx_{E}= -\\frac{2\\bigl(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0})\\bigr)}{1+k^{2}}-x_{0}. \\tag{2.23}\n\\]\n\nNow substitute \\(k\\) from (2.16).  Write  \n\n\\[\nk(p_{x}-x_{0})=p_{y}-y_{0}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk=\\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}.\n\\]\n\nA short computation gives  \n\n\\[\nm+k(y_{0}-kx_{0})\n= \\frac{-(x_{0}+m)p_{x}+p_{x}^{2}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}.\n\\]\n\nInsert this into (2.23) and simplify:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{E}\n&= -\\frac{2\\bigl(-(x_{0}+m)p_{x}+p_{x}^{2}\\bigr)}{(1+k^{2})(p_{x}-x_{0})}-x_{0}\\\\\n&= x_{0}-\\frac{2(x_{0}+m)p_{x}}{x_{0}+m-p_{x}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x_{E}=x_{0}-\\frac{2(x_{0}+m)p_{x}}{x_{0}+m-p_{x}}}. \\tag{2.24}\n\\]\n\nThe ordinate follows from the line equation (2.18):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{y_{E}=k(x_{E}-x_{0})+y_{0}}. \\tag{2.25}\n\\]\n\nExactly the same reasoning for \\(\\Gamma\\) (replace \\(m\\) by \\(-n\\)) yields\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x_{F}=x_{0}-\\frac{2(x_{0}-n)p_{x}}{x_{0}-n-p_{x}}},\\qquad\n\\boxed{y_{F}=k(x_{F}-x_{0})+y_{0}}. \\tag{2.26}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.10. Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nWrite a general circle as  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0. \\tag{2.27}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the three points \\(B(x_{0},-y_{0})\\), \\(E(x_{E},y_{E})\\), \\(F(x_{F},y_{F})\\) gives the linear system\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}+u x_{E}+v y_{E}+w=0,\\\\[2pt]\nx_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}+u x_{F}+v y_{F}+w=0,\\\\[2pt]\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u x_{0}-v y_{0}+w=0.\n\\end{cases} \\tag{2.28}\n\\]\n\nSubtract the third equation from the first two; the unknown \\(w\\) disappears:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2})-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})+u(x_{E}-x_{0})+v(y_{E}+y_{0})=0,\\\\\n&(x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2})-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})+u(x_{F}-x_{0})+v(y_{F}+y_{0})=0.\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{2.29}\n\\]\n\nDenote  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_{uv}:=(x_{E}-x_{0})(y_{F}+y_{0})-(x_{F}-x_{0})(y_{E}+y_{0}).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\neq0\\) the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) have abscissae different from \\(x_{0}\\); also \\(y_{E}+y_{0}\\neq y_{F}+y_{0}\\). Hence \\(\\Delta_{uv}\\neq0\\) and the system (2.29) has a unique solution.\n\n**Coefficient \\(u\\).**  \nMultiply the first equation of (2.29) by \\((y_{F}+y_{0})\\), the second by \\((y_{E}+y_{0})\\) and subtract; the terms containing \\(v\\) cancel, giving\n\n\\[\nu=\n-\\frac{(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2})-(x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2})}{\\Delta_{uv}}. \\tag{2.30}\n\\]\n\nNow insert the explicit expressions (2.24)\u2013(2.26) for the coordinates.  After expanding all squares, collecting like terms and repeatedly using the identities  \n\n\\[\n(x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad (x_{0}-n)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\n\nthe numerator simplifies to \\(-2x_{0}\\Delta_{uv}\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{u=-2x_{0}}. \\tag{2.31}\n\\]\n\n**Coefficient \\(v\\).**  \nEliminate \\(u\\) from the first equation of (2.29) and solve for \\(v\\):\n\n\\[\nv=\n\\frac{(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2})-(x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2})}{y_{E}-y_{F}}. \\tag{2.32}\n\\]\n\nCarrying out the same algebraic reductions as above (the denominator \\(y_{E}-y_{F}=k(x_{E}-x_{F})\\neq0\\) because \\(k\\neq0\\) and \\(x_{E}\\neq x_{F}\\)) yields\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{v=\\frac{R^{2}-r^{2}}{y_{0}}}. \\tag{2.33}\n\\]\n\n**Constant term \\(w\\).**  \nFinally, substitute \\(u\\) and \\(v\\) into the third equation of (2.28):\n\n\\[\nw= -\\bigl(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u x_{0}-v y_{0}\\bigr)\n   =