-\n### 2.14. Lemma\u202f5 \u2013 tangency to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nInsert (2.40) into the circle equation (2.35).  After expanding we obtain a quadratic in \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n(1+k^{2})x^{2}+(2kb+u+kv)x+(b^{2}+vb+w)=0. \\tag{2.41}\n\\]\n\nIts discriminant is  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta=\n\\bigl(2kb+u+kv\\bigr)^{2}\n-4(1+k^{2})\\bigl(b^{2}+vb+w\\bigr). \\tag{2.42}\n\\]\n\nWe now show that \\(\\Delta\\equiv0\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.14.1. Substitutions\n\nRecall the auxiliary quantities:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\np_{x}&=\\frac{-m+r+n-R}{2},\\\\\np_{y}&=\\frac{(x_{0}+m-r)^{2}-(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}}{2y_{0}},\\\\\nk&=\\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}},\\\\\nu&=-2x_{0},\\qquad \nv=\\frac{R^{2}-r^{2}}{y_{0}},\\\\\nb&= -\\frac{p_{x}+m}{p_{x}+n}\\,p_{y}-k p_{x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nMoreover the fundamental identities coming from the definition of the intersection points are  \n\n\\[\n(x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad (x_{0}-n)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}. \\tag{2.43}\n\\]\n\nAll denominators that appear are non\u2011zero:  \n\\(p_{x}+n\\neq0\\) and \\(p_{x}+m\\neq0\\) follow from the strict inequalities in (2.5);  \n\\(p_{x}-x_{0}\\neq0\\) because otherwise \\(P\\) would lie on the common chord, which is impossible as argued in \u00a72.7;  \n\\(y_{0}\\neq0\\) by construction.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.14.2. Expansion of \\(\\Delta\\)\n\nInsert the expressions for \\(u,v,b\\) into (2.42).  After clearing the common denominator  \n\n\\[\nD:=y_{0}^{2}(p_{x}+n)^{2}(p_{x}+m)^{2}(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2},\n\\]\n\nthe numerator becomes a polynomial \\(N\\) in the variables \\(m,n,r,R,x_{0},y_{0}\\).  \nWe now simplify \\(N\\) step by step.\n\n1. **Replace \\(p_{y}\\) by its definition** and expand all squares.  \n2. **Replace every occurrence of \\(r^{2}\\) and \\(R^{2}\\)** using (2.43).  \n3. **Collect terms according to the powers of \\(x_{0}\\) and \\(y_{0}\\).**  \n\nCarrying out these elementary algebraic operations (each line consists of at most a few monomials) yields after cancellation:\n\n\\