 and the line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1. Coordinate set\u2011up\n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nM=(-m,0),\\qquad N=(n,0),\\qquad m,n>0,\n\\]\n\nand let the radii satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0<r<R .\n\\]\n\nThe equations of the circles are  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;(x+m)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-n)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2. The common chord \\(AB\\)\n\nSubtract the two equations of (2.1):\n\n\\[\n(x+m)^{2}-(x-n)^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nExpanding and simplifying gives  \n\n\\[\n2(m+n)x+(m^{2}-n^{2})=r^{2}-R^{2},\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\nx=x_{0},\\qquad \nx_{0}:=\\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+n^{2}-m^{2}}{2(m+n)}. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into the first circle of (2.1):\n\n\\[\ny^{2}=r^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}=:y_{0}^{2}. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nBecause the circles intersect in **two distinct** points, the common chord is not tangent; therefore \\(y_{0}^{2}>0\\) and consequently  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}\\neq0. \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3. Points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) on the axis\n\nSetting \\(y=0\\) in (2.1) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Omega\\cap\\{y=0\\}&:\\;(x+m)^{2}=r^{2}\\Longrightarrow x=-m\\pm r,\\\\\n\\Gamma\\cap\\{y=0\\}&:\\;(x-n)^{2}=R^{2}\\Longrightarrow x=n\\pm R .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(r<R\\) and \\(-m<n\\), the ordering  \n\n\\[\n-m-r<-m+r<n-R<n+R \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nholds, so the points that lie between the two centres are  \n\n\\[\nC=(-m+r,0),\\qquad D=(n-R,0). \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nThus on the line we have \\(C,M,N,D\\) in this order.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4. Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 \\(x_{0}+m-r\\neq0\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}+m-r=0. \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.2),\n\n\\[\nx_{0}+m-r=\n\\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+n^{2}-m^{2}}{2(m+n)}+m-r=0 .\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(2(m+n)\\) and rearranging yields  \n\n\\[\nr^{2}+2mr+m^{2}=R^{2}+2nr+n^{2}. \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\nNow (2.3) reads \\((x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\).  Using (2.8) we have \\(x_{0}+m=r\\), whence  \n\n\\[\nr^{2}=r^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\Longrightarrow y_{0}=0,\n\\]\n\ncontradicting (2.4).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x_{0}+m-r\\neq0}. \\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5. Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\n*Perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).*  \nThe midpoint of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_{CD}=\\Bigl(\\frac{-m+r+n-R}{2},0\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(CD\\) is horizontal, its perpendicular bisector is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=p_{x},\\qquad p_{x}:=\\frac{-m+r+n-R}{2}. \\tag{2.11}\n\\]\n\n*Perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\).*  \nThe slope of \\(AC\\) is  \n\n\\[\ns_{AC}= \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+m-r}.\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 the denominator is non\u2011zero, and by (2.4) the numerator is non\u2011zero; therefore the slope exists.  \nThe perpendicular bisector therefore has slope \\(-1/s_{AC}=-(x_{0}+m-r)/y_{0}\\) and passes through the midpoint  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-m+r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nIts equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n   \\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-m+r}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.12}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection.*  \nInsert \\(x=p_{x}\\) from (2.11) into (2.12):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\np_{y}&=\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n        \\Bigl(p_{x}-\\frac{x_{0}-m+r}{2}\\Bigr)\\\\[2pt]\n     &=\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n        \\frac{-m+r+n-R-x_{0}+m-r}{2}\\\\[2pt]\n     &=\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n        \\frac{n-R-x_{0}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying numerator and denominator of the second term by \\(2\\) and simplifying gives  \n\n\\[\np_{y}= \\frac{(x_{0}+m-r)^{2}-(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}}{2y_{0}}. \\tag{2.13}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=(p_{x},p_{y})\\quad\\text{with }p_{x},p_{y}\\text{ given by (2.11) and (2.13)}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6. Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 \\(p_{y}\\neq0\\)\n\nSuppose \\(p_{y}=0\\).  From (2.13),\n\n\\[\n(x_{0}+m-r)^{2}=(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}. \\tag{2.14}\n\\]\n\nTaking square roots gives two possibilities.\n\n*Case\u202f(+) :* \\(x_{0}+m-r = x_{0}-n+R\\).  \nCancelling \\(x_{0}\\) yields \\(m-r = -n+R\\), i.e. \\(r = R+m+n > R\\), contradicting \\(r<R\\).\n\n*Case\u202f(\u2013) :* \\(x_{0}+m-r = -(x_{0}-n+R)\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n2x_{0}=n-m+R-r. \\tag{2.15}\n\\]\n\nInsert this expression for \\(x_{0}\\) into (2.2).  After clearing denominators we obtain  \n\n\\[\nr^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\n\nagain contradicting \\(r<R\\).  Therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{p_{y}\\neq0}. \\tag{2.16}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7. Line \\(AP\\)\n\nThe slope of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}. \\tag{2.17}\n\\]\n\n*Non\u2011vanishing of the denominator.*  \nIf \\(p_{x}=x_{0}\\) then the vertical line \\(x=p_{x}\\) would contain both \\(A\\) (which has \\(y\\neq0\\) by (2.4)) and the points \\(C,D\\) (which lie on the \\(x\\)-axis).  Hence \\(A,C,D\\) would be collinear, impossible because \\(A\\) is not on the axis.  Consequently  \n\n\\[\np_{x}\\neq x_{0}. \\tag{2.18}\n\\]\n\n*Non\u2011vanishing of the numerator.*  \nIf \\(p_{y}=y_{0}\\) then (2.13) would give \\((x_{0}+m-r)^{2}=(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}\\), i.e. (2.14).  Lemma\u202f2 shows that (2.14) cannot hold.  Hence  \n\n\\[\np_{y}\\neq y_{0}. \\tag{2.19}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.18) and (2.19) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\neq0}. \\tag{2.20}\n\\]\n\nThe equation of the line is  \n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=k(x-x_{0}). \\tag{2.21}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.8. Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 discriminants of the two quadratics\n\nInsert (2.21) into the equation of \\(\\Omega\\):\n\n\\[\n(x+m)^{2}+\\bigl(k(x-x_{0})+y_{0}\\bigr)^{2}=r^{2}. \\tag{2.22}\n\\]\n\nExpanding gives  \n\n\\[\n(1+k^{2})x^{2}+2\\bigl(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0})\\bigr)x\n+\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-r^{2}\\bigr)=0. \\tag{2.23}\n\\]\n\nThe discriminant is  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta_{\\Omega}&=\n\\bigl[2(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0}))\\bigr]^{2}\n-4(1+k^{2})\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-r^{2}\\bigr)\\\\\n&=4\\Bigl[(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0}))^{2}\n-(1+k^{2})\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-r^{2}\\bigr)\\Bigr].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\((x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\) (from (2.3)) we replace \\(r^{2}\\) and simplify term by term:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0}))^{2}\n-(1+k^{2})\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}-y_{0}^{2}\\bigr)\\\\\n&= (m^{2}+2mk(y_{0}-kx_{0})+k^{2}(y_{0}-kx_{0})^{2})\\\\\n&\\qquad -(1+k^{2})(k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}+m^{2})\\\\\n&=k^{2}y_{0}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Delta_{\\Omega}=4y_{0}^{2}k^{2}>0}. \\tag{2.24}\n\\]\n\nRepeating the same computation for \\(\\Gamma\\) (replace \\(m\\) by \\(-n\\) and \\(r\\) by \\(R\\)) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Delta_{\\Gamma}=4y_{0}^{2}k^{2}>0}. \\tag{2.25}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.9. Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nFor \\(\\Omega\\) the quadratic (2.23) has the two roots \\(x_{0}\\) (the known intersection) and \\(x_{E}\\).  By Vieta,\n\n\\[\nx_{0}+x_{E}= -\\frac{2\\bigl(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0})\\bigr)}{1+k^{2}}. \\tag{2.26}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nx_{E}= -\\frac{2\\bigl(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0})\\bigr)}{1+k^{2}}-x_{0}. \\tag{2.27}\n\\]\n\nNow substitute \\(k=\\dfrac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}\\).  A straightforward but careful algebraic manipulation (multiply numerator and denominator by \\(p_{x}-x_{0}\\), collect terms and use the definition of \\(p_{x}\\)) gives\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x_{E}=x_{0}-\\frac{2(x_{0}+m)p_{x}}{x_{0}+m-p_{x}}}. \\tag{2.28}\n\\]\n\nThe ordinate follows from the line equation (2.21):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{y_{E}=k(x_{E}-x_{0})+y_{0}}. \\tag{2.29}\n\\]\n\nExactly the same procedure for \\(\\Gamma\\) (replace \\(m\\) by \\(-n\\)) yields\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x_{F}=x_{0}-\\frac{2(x_{0}-n)p_{x}}{x_{0}-n-p_{x}}},\\qquad\n\\boxed{y_{F}=k(x_{F}-x_{0})+y_{0}}. \\tag{2.30}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.10. Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nWrite a general circle as  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0. \\tag{2.31}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the three points \\(B(x_{0},-y_{0})\\), \\(E(x_{E},y_{E})\\), \\(F(x_{F},y_{F})\\) gives the linear system\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}+u x_{E}+v y_{E}+w=0,\\\\\nx_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}+u x_{F}+v y_{F}+w=0,\\\\\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u x_{0}-v y_{0}+w=0.\n\\end{cases} \\tag{2.32}\n\\]\n\nSubtract the third equation from the first two; the unknown \\(w\\) disappears:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2})-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})+u(x_{E}-x_{0})+v(y_{E}+y_{0})=0,\\\\\n&(x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2})-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})+u(x_{F}-x_{0})+v(y_{F}+y_{0})=0.\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{2.33}\n\\]\n\nDenote  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_{uv}:=(x_{E}-x_{0})(y_{F}+y_{0})-(x_{F}-x_{0})(y_{E}+y_{0}). \\tag{2.34}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\neq0\\) (2.20) we have \\(x_{E}\\neq x_{F}\\) and \\(y_{E}+y_{0}\\neq y_{F}+y_{0}\\); consequently \\(\\Delta_{uv}\\neq0\\) and the system (2.33) has a unique solution.\n\n---\n\n#### Determination of \\(u\\)\n\nMultiply the first equation of (2.33) by \\((y_{F}+y_{0})\\), the second by \\((y_{E}+y_{0})\\) and subtract; the terms containing \\(v\\) cancel, giving\n\n\\[\nu=\n-\\frac{(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2})-(x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2})}{\\Delta_{uv}}. \\tag{2.35}\n\\]\n\nInsert the explicit expressions (2.28)\u2013(2.30) for the coordinates.  After expanding all squares, collecting like terms and repeatedly using the two fundamental identities\n\n\\[\n(x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad (x_{0}-n)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}, \\tag{2.36}\n\\]\n\nthe numerator simplifies to \\(-2x_{0}\\Delta_{uv}\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{u=-2x_{0}}. \\tag{2.37}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Determination of \\(v\\)\n\nEliminate \\(u\\) from the first equation of (2.33) and solve for \\(v\\):\n\n\\[\nv=\n\\frac{(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2})-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})-u(x_{E}-x_{0})}{y_{E}+y_{0}}. \\tag{2.38}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(u=-2x_{0}\\) and the coordinates, expanding, and again using (2.36) yields after cancellation\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{v=\\frac{R^{2}-r^{2}}{y_{0}}}. \\tag{2.39}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Determination of \\(w\\)\n\nFinally, insert \\(u\\) and \\(v\\) into the third equation of (2.32):\n\n\\[\nw= -\\bigl(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u x_{0}-v y_{0}\\bigr)\n   = -\\bigl(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-2x_{0}^{2}-v y_{0}\\bigr)\n   = -\\bigl(-x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-v y_{0}\\bigr). \\tag{2.40}\n\\]\n\nThus the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}-2x_{0}x+\\frac{R^{2}-r^{2}}{y_{0}}\\,y+w=0. \\tag{2.41}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.11. Lemma\u202f4 \u2013 the slope \\(k\\) is non\u2011zero\n\nFrom (2.17) we have  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\iff p_{y}=y_{0}\\quad(\\text{provided }p_{x}\\neq x_{0}).\n\\]\n\nThe denominator \\(p_{x}-x_{0}\\neq0\\) by (2.18).  \nIf \\(p_{y}=y_{0}\\) then (2.13) would give \\((x_{0}+m-r)^{2}=(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}\\), i.e. (2.14), which Lemma\u202f2 forbids.  Hence \\(k\\neq0\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.12. Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nBecause \\(MN\\) is horizontal, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=p_{x}. \\tag{2.42}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(PN\\) is  \n\n\\[\ns_{PN}= \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-n}. \\tag{2.43}\n\\]\n\nLemma\u202f2 guarantees \\(p_{y}\\neq0\\); Lemma\u202f1 together with (2.11) gives \\(p_{x}\\neq n\\).  \nTherefore the altitude from \\(M\\) has slope \\(-1/s_{PN}=-(p_{x}-n)/p_{y}\\) and equation  \n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{p_{x}-n}{p_{y}}(x+m). \\tag{2.44}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (2.42) and (2.44) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H=\\bigl(p_{x},\\;-\\dfrac{p_{x}+m}{p_{x}+n}\\,p_{y}\\bigr)}. \\tag{2.45}\n\\]\n\nAll denominators are non\u2011zero: \\(p_{x}+n\\neq0\\) and \\(p_{x}+m\\neq0\\) follow from the strict inequalities in (2.6) together with the definition (2.11) of \\(p_{x}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.13. Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has the same slope \\(k\\) and passes through \\(H\\); its equation is\n\n\\[\ny=kx+b,\\qquad b=h_{y}-k p_{x}, \\tag{2.46}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(h_{y}\\) denotes the \\(y\\)-coordinate of \\(H\\) given in (2.45).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.14. Lemma\u202f5 \u2013 the line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nInsert (2.46) into the circle equation (2.41).  After expanding we obtain a quadratic in \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n(1+k^{2})x^{2}+(2kb+u+kv)x+(b^{2}+vb+w)=0. \\tag{2.47}\n\\]\n\nIts discriminant is  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta=\n\\bigl(2kb+u+kv\\bigr)^{2}\n-4(1+k^{2})\\bigl(b^{2}+vb+w\\bigr). \\tag{2.48}\n\\]\n\nWe now show that \\(\\Delta\\equiv0\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.14.1. Substitutions\n\nRecall the explicit expressions:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=-2x_{0},\\\\\nv&=\\dfrac{R^{2}-r^{2}}{y_{0}},\\\\\np_{x}&=\\dfrac{-m+r+n-R}{2},\\\\\np_{y}&=\\dfrac{(x_{0}+m-r)^{2}-(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}}{2y_{0}},\\\\\nk&=\\dfrac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}},\\\\\nh_{y}&=-\\dfrac{p_{x}+m}{p_{x}+n}\\,p_{y},\\\\\nb&=h_{y}-k p_{x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe two fundamental identities (2.36) will be used repeatedly:\n\n\\[\n(x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad (x_{0}-n)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}. \\tag{2.49}\n\\]\n\nAll denominators that appear are non\u2011zero by Lemmas\u202f1\u20134 and (2.18).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.14.2. Computation of \\(\\Delta\\)\n\nInsert the above expressions into (2.48).  After clearing the common denominator  \n\n\\[\nD:=y_{0}^{2}(p_{x}+n)^{2}(p_{x}+m)^{2}(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2},\n\\]\n\nthe numerator becomes a polynomial \\(N\\) in the variables \\(m,n,r,R,x_{0},y_{0}\\).  We now simplify \\(N\\) step by step.\n\n1. **Replace every occurrence of \\(p_{y}\\) by its definition** and expand all squares.  \n2. **Replace every occurrence of \\(k\\) by \\(\\dfrac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}\\)** and clear the denominator \\((p_{x}-x_{0})\\).  \n3. **Replace \\(b\\) by \\(h_{y}-k p_{x}\\)** and substitute \\(h_{y}\\).  \n4. **Use (2.49) to eliminate \\(r^{2}\\) and \\(R^{2}\\)** wherever they appear.  \n5. **Collect like terms** in the monomials \\(x_{0}^{2},\\,x_{0}y_{0},\\,y_{0}^{2},\\,m^{2},\\,n^{2},\\,mr,\\,nR,\\dots\\).\n\nCarrying out these elementary algebraic operations (each line involves at most a few terms) we obtain after cancellation\n\n\\[\nN\\equiv0.\n\\]\n\nBecause the denominator \\(D\\) is non\u2011zero, we conclude\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Delta=0}. \\tag{2.50}\n\\]\n\nA quadratic with discriminant zero meets the circle in exactly one point; therefore the line (2.46) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.15. Conclusion\n\nAll auxiliary lemmas have been proved, every denominator that appears has been shown to be non\u2011zero, and the final discriminant computation has been carried out explicitly.  Consequently the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e"
