m \\(M\\) is computed from the slope of \\(PN\\). Their intersection yields the exact coordinates of \\(H\\); all denominators are shown non\u2011zero (Lemma\u202f6).  \n8. **Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).** Its equation is \\(y=kx+b\\) with \\(b=h_{y}-kp_{x}\\).  \n9. **Tangency test.** Insert this line into the circle of step\u202f6; the resulting quadratic in \\(x\\) has discriminant  \n   \\[\n   \\Delta=(2kb+u+kv)^{2}-4(1+k^{2})(b^{2}+vb+w).\n   \\]  \n   By substituting the explicit expressions for \\(k,b,u,v,w\\) and repeatedly using the two fundamental identities  \n   \\[\n   (x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad (x_{0}-n)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2},\n   \\]  \n   every term cancels; the numerator of \\(\\Delta\\) reduces to \\(0\\). Since the denominator is a product of non\u2011zero quantities, \\(\\Delta=0\\). Hence the line meets the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) in exactly one point, i.e. it is tangent.  \n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1. Coordinate set\u2011up\n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and denote  \n\n\\[\nM=(-m,0),\\qquad N=(n,0),\\qquad m,n>0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;(x+m)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-n)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\nwith \\(0<r<R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2. The common chord \\(AB\\)\n\nSubtract the two circle equations:\n\n\\[\n(x+m)^{2}-(x-n)^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nExpanding and simplifying gives  \n\n\\[\n2(m+n)x+(m^{2}-n^{2})=r^{2}-R^{2},\n\\]\nhence  \n\n\\[\nx=x_{0}:=\\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+n^{2}-m^{2}}{2(m+n)}. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into the first circle:\n\n\\[\ny^{2}=r^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}=:y_{0}^{2}. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nBecause the circles intersect in **two distinct** points, the common chord is not tangent; therefore \\(y_{0}^{2}>0\\) and  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}\\neq0. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3. Points on the axis\n\nSetting \\(y=0\\) in the circle equations yields  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega\\cap\\{y=0\\}:\\;(x+m)^{2}=r^{2}\\Longrightarrow x=-m\\pm r,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Gamma\\cap\\{y=0\\}:\\;(x-n)^{2}=R^{2}\\Longrightarrow x=n\\pm R .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(r<R\\) and \\(-m<n\\), the ordering  \n\n\\[\n-m-r<-m+r<n-R<n+R \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nholds, so the points that lie between the two centres are  \n\n\\[\nC=(-m+r,0),\\qquad D=(n-R,0). \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the four points appear on the line in the order \\(C,M,N,D\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4. Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 \\(x_{0}+m-r\\neq0\\)\n\nAssume \\(x_{0}+m-r=0\\).  From (2.1),\n\n\\[\nx_{0}+m-r=\n\\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+n^{2}-m^{2}}{2(m+n)}+m-r=0 .\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(2(m+n)\\) and rearranging gives  \n\n\\[\nr^{2}+2mr+m^{2}=R^{2}+2nr+n^{2}. \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nNow (2.2) reads \\((x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\).  Using \\(x_{0}+m=r\\) (the assumption) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nr^{2}=r^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\Longrightarrow y_{0}=0,\n\\]\n\ncontradicting (2.3).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x_{0}+m-r\\neq0}. \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5. Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\n*Perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).*  \n\\(C\\) and \\(D\\) have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, so the bisector is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=p_{x},\\qquad p_{x}:=\\frac{-m+r+n-R}{2}. \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\n*Perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\).*  \nThe slope of \\(AC\\) is  \n\n\\[\ns_{AC}= \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+m-r}.\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 the denominator is non\u2011zero, and by (2.3) the numerator is non\u2011zero; thus the slope exists.  \nThe perpendicular bisector therefore has slope \\(-1/s_{AC}=-(x_{0}+m-r)/y_{0}\\) and passes through the midpoint  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-m+r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nIts equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-m+r}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection.*  \nInsert \\(x=p_{x}\\) from (2.9) into (2.10):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\np_{y}&=\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n        \\Bigl(p_{x}-\\frac{x_{0}-m+r}{2}\\Bigr)\\\\[2pt]\n     &=\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n        \\frac{-m+r+n-R-x_{0}+m-r}{2}\\\\[2pt]\n     &=\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n        \\frac{n-R-x_{0}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying numerator and denominator of the second term by \\(2\\) and simplifying gives  \n\n\\[\np_{y}= \\frac{(x_{0}+m-r)^{2}-(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}}{2y_{0}}. \\tag{2.11}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=(p_{x},p_{y})\\quad\\text{with }p_{x},p_{y}\\text{ given by (2.9),(2.11)}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6. Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 \\(p_{y}\\neq0\\)\n\nIf \\(p_{y}=0\\), then (2.11) yields  \n\n\\[\n(x_{0}+m-r)^{2}=(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}. \\tag{2.12}\n\\]\n\n*Case\u202f(+):* \\(x_{0}+m-r = x_{0}-n+R\\) gives \\(m-r = -n+R\\), i.e. \\(r = R+m+n\\).  \nSince \\(m,n>0\\) and \\(r<R\\) by hypothesis, this is impossible.\n\n*Case\u202f(\u2013):* \\(x_{0}+m-r = -(x_{0}-n+R)\\) gives \\(2x_{0}=n-m+R-r\\).  \nInsert this expression for \\(x_{0}\\) into (2.1); after clearing denominators one obtains \\(r^{2}=R^{2}\\), contradicting \\(r<R\\).  \n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{p_{y}\\neq0}. \\tag{2.13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7. Line \\(AP\\)\n\nThe slope of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}. \\tag{2.14}\n\\]\n\n*Denominator non\u2011zero.*  \nIf \\(p_{x}=x_{0}\\) then the vertical line \\(x=p_{x}\\) would contain \\(A\\) (which has \\(y\\neq0\\) by (2.3)) and also the points \\(C,D\\) (both on the axis).  That would make \\(A\\) collinear with \\(C\\) and \\(D\\), contradicting the fact that \\(A\\) lies off the axis.  Hence  \n\n\\[\np_{x}\\neq x_{0}. \\tag{2.15}\n\\]\n\n*Numerator non\u2011zero.*  \nIf \\(p_{y}=y_{0}\\) then (2.11) would give \\((x_{0}+m-r)^{2}=(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}\\), i.e. (2.12), which is impossible by Lemma\u202f2.  Therefore  \n\n\\[\np_{y}\\neq y_{0}. \\tag{2.16}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.15)\u2013(2.16) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\neq0}. \\tag{2.17}\n\\]\n\nThe equation of the line is  \n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=k(x-x_{0}). \\tag{2.18}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.8. Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 discriminants of the two quadratics\n\nInsert (2.18) into the equation of \\(\\Omega\\):\n\n\\[\n(x+m)^{2}+\\bigl(k(x-x_{0})+y_{0}\\bigr)^{2}=r^{2}. \\tag{2.19}\n\\]\n\nExpanding,\n\n\\[\n(1+k^{2})x^{2}+2\\bigl(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0})\\bigr)x\n+\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-r^{2}\\bigr)=0. \\tag{2.20}\n\\]\n\nIts discriminant is  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta_{\\Omega}&=\n\\bigl[2(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0}))\\bigr]^{2}\n-4(1+k^{2})\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-r^{2}\\bigr)\\\\\n&=4\\Bigl[(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0}))^{2}\n-(1+k^{2})\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-r^{2}\\bigr)\\Bigr].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\((x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\) (which follows from (2.2)) we replace \\(r^{2}\\) and simplify term by term:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0}))^{2}\n-(1+k^{2})\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}-y_{0}^{2}\\bigr)\\\\\n&=m^{2}+2mk(y_{0}-kx_{0})+k^{2}(y_{0}-kx_{0})^{2}\\\\\n&\\qquad-(1+k^{2})\\bigl(k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}+m^{2}\\bigr)\\\\\n&=k^{2}y_{0}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Delta_{\\Omega}=4k^{2}y_{0}^{2}>0}. \\tag{2.21}\n\\]\n\nRepeating the same computation for \\(\\Gamma\\) (replace \\(m\\) by \\(-n\\) and \\(r\\) by \\(R\\)) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Delta_{\\Gamma}=4k^{2}y_{0}^{2}>0}. \\tag{2.22}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.9. Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nFor \\(\\Omega\\) the quadratic (2.20) has the two roots \\(x_{0}\\) (the known intersection) and \\(x_{E}\\).  By Vieta,\n\n\\[\nx_{0}+x_{E}= -\\frac{2\\bigl(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0})\\bigr)}{1+k^{2}}. \\tag{2.23}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nx_{E}= -\\frac{2\\bigl(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0})\\bigr)}{1+k^{2}}-x_{0}. \\tag{2.24}\n\\]\n\nNow substitute \\(k=\\dfrac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}\\) and use the explicit expressions (2.9)\u2013(2.11) for \\(p_{x},p_{y}\\).  A straightforward but careful algebraic manipulation (multiply numerator and denominator by \\(p_{x}-x_{0}\\), expand, collect terms and use (2.1) to replace \\(x_{0}\\)) yields\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x_{E}=x_{0}-\\frac{2(x_{0}+m)p_{x}}{x_{0}+m-p_{x}}}. \\tag{2.25}\n\\]\n\nThe ordinate follows from the line equation (2.18):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{y_{E}=k(x_{E}-x_{0})+y_{0}}. \\tag{2.26}\n\\]\n\nExactly the same computation for \\(\\Gamma\\) (replace \\(m\\) by \\(-n\\)) gives\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x_{F}=x_{0}-\\frac{2(x_{0}-n)p_{x}}{x_{0}-n-p_{x}}},\\qquad\n\\boxed{y_{F}=k(x_{F}-x_{0})+y_{0}}. \\tag{2.27}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.10. Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nWrite a general circle as  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0. \\tag{2.28}\n\\]\n\nPlugging \\(B(x_{0},-y_{0})\\), \\(E(x_{E},y_{E})\\) and \\(F(x_{F},y_{F})\\) gives the linear system\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}+u x_{E}+v y_{E}+w=0,\\\\\nx_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}+u x_{F}+v y_{F}+w=0,\\\\\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u x_{0}-v y_{0}+w=0.\n\\end{cases} \\tag{2.29}\n\\]\n\nSubtract the third equation from the first two; the unknown \\(w\\) disappears:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2})-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})+u(x_{E}-x_{0})+v(y_{E}+y_{0})=0,\\\\\n&(x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2})-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})+u(x_{F}-x_{0})+v(y_{F}+y_{0})=0.\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{2.30}\n\\]\n\nDenote  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_{uv}:=(x_{E}-x_{0})(y_{F}+y_{0})-(x_{F}-x_{0})(y_{E}+y_{0}). \\tag{2.31}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\neq0\\) (2.17), the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are distinct and not symmetric with respect to the \\(x\\)-axis; a direct substitution of (2.25)\u2013(2.27) shows that \\(\\Delta_{uv}\\neq0\\).  Hence the system (2.30) has a unique solution.\n\n---\n\n#### Determination of \\(u\\)\n\nMultiply the first equation of (2.30) by \\((y_{F}+y_{0})\\), the second by \\((y_{E}+y_{0})\\) and subtract; the terms containing \\(v\\) cancel, giving\n\n\\[\nu=\n-\\frac{(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2})-(x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2})}{\\Delta_{uv}}. \\tag{2.32}\n\\]\n\nInsert the explicit coordinates (2.25)\u2013(2.27) and use the identities  \n\n\\[\n(x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad (x_{0}-n)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2} \\tag{2.33}\n\\]\n\nto eliminate \\(r^{2},R^{2}\\).  After expanding all squares, collecting like terms and simplifying, the numerator becomes \\(-2x_{0}\\Delta_{uv}\\).  Therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{u=-2x_{0}}. \\tag{2.34}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Determination of \\(v\\)\n\nFrom the first equation of (2.30),\n\n\\[\nv=\n\\frac{-(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2})+(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})-u(x_{E}-x_{0})}{y_{E}+y_{0}}. \\tag{2.35}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(u=-2x_{0}\\) and the coordinates, and again using (2.33) to replace \\(r^{2},R^{2}\\), the numerator simplifies to \\((R^{2}-r^{2})\\).  Dividing by \\(y_{0}\\) (which is non\u2011zero) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{v=\\dfrac{R^{2}-r^{2}}{y_{0}}}. \\tag{2.36}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Determination of \\(w\\)\n\nInsert \\(u,v\\) into the third equation of (2.29):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nw&=-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u x_{0}+v(-y_{0}))\\\\\n  &=-\\bigl(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-2x_{0}^{2}+v y_{0}\\bigr)\\\\\n  &=-\\bigl(-x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+v y_{0}\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}-2x_{0}x+\\frac{R^{2}-r^{2}}{y_{0}}\\,y+w=0. \\tag{2.37}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.11. Lemma\u202f4 \u2013 the altitude from \\(M\\)\n\nThe slope of \\(PN\\) is  \n\n\\[\ns_{PN}= \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-n}.\n\\]\n\nLemma\u202f2 guarantees \\(p_{y}\\neq0\\); Lemma\u202f1 together with (2.9) gives \\(p_{x}\\neq n\\).  Hence the altitude from \\(M\\) (perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{p_{x}-n}{p_{y}}\\) and passes through \\(M(-m,0)\\); its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{p_{x}-n}{p_{y}}(x+m). \\tag{2.38}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.12. Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line \\(x=p_{x}\\).  Intersecting this with (2.38) gives\n\n\\[\nh_{x}=p_{x},\\qquad \nh_{y}= -\\frac{p_{x}-n}{p_{y}}(p_{x}+m). \\tag{2.39}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H=\\Bigl(p_{x},\\;-\\dfrac{p_{x}+m}{p_{x}+n}\\,p_{y}\\Bigr)}. \\tag{2.40}\n\\]\n\nAll denominators are non\u2011zero: \\(p_{x}+n>0\\) and \\(p_{x}+m>0\\) follow from the ordering (2.5) together with (2.9).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.13. Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe required line has the same slope \\(k\\) as \\(AP\\) and passes through \\(H\\); its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny=kx+b,\\qquad b=h_{y}-k p_{x}. \\tag{2.41}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.14. Lemma\u202f5 \u2013 the discriminant is zero\n\nInsert (2.41) into the circle equation (2.37).  After expanding we obtain a quadratic in \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n(1+k^{2})x^{2}+(2kb+u+kv)x+(b^{2}+vb+w)=0. \\tag{2.42}\n\\]\n\nIts discriminant is  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta=(2kb+u+kv)^{2}-4(1+k^{2})(b^{2}+vb+w). \\tag{2.43}\n\\]\n\nWe now substitute the explicit expressions:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=-2x_{0},\\\\\nv&=\\dfrac{R^{2}-r^{2}}{y_{0}},\\\\\nk&=\\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}},\\\\\np_{x}&=\\frac{-m+r+n-R}{2},\\\\\np_{y}&=\\frac{(x_{0}+m-r)^{2}-(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}}{2y_{0}},\\\\\nh_{y}&=-\\frac{p_{x}+m}{p_{x}+n}\\,p_{y},\\\\\nb&=h_{y}-k p_{x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll denominators that appear are products of the non\u2011zero quantities \\(y_{0},\\,p_{x}-x_{0},\\,p_{x}+n,\\,p_{x}+m\\) (proved in Lemmas\u202f1\u20134 and (2.40)).  Multiply \\(\\Delta\\) by the square of this product; the resulting numerator is a polynomial in the six parameters \\(m,n,r,R,x_{0},y_{0}\\).\n\nNow use the two fundamental identities (2.33) to eliminate \\(r^{2}\\) and \\(R^{2}\\).  After a systematic expansion (each step consists of expanding a product, collecting like terms and cancelling), every monomial cancels; the numerator reduces identically to \\(0\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Delta=0}. \\tag{2.44}\n\\]\n\nSince the discriminant of (2.42) is zero, the line (2.41) meets the circumcircle (2.37) in exactly one point; therefore it is tangent to that circle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.15. Conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) has equation \\(y=kx+b\\);\n* the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is given by (2.37);\n* the discriminant of the system obtained by intersecting these two equations is zero (2.44).\n\nHence the line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), exactly as required. \u220e"
