            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1. Coordinate set\u2011up\n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and denote  \n\n\\[\nM=(-m,0),\\qquad N=(n,0),\\qquad m,n>0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;(x+m)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-n)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\nwith \\(0<r<R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2. The common chord \\(AB\\)\n\nSubtract the two circle equations:\n\n\\[\n(x+m)^{2}-(x-n)^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nExpanding and simplifying gives  \n\n\\[\n2(m+n)x+(m^{2}-n^{2})=r^{2}-R^{2},\n\\]\nhence  \n\n\\[\nx=x_{0}:=\\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+n^{2}-m^{2}}{2(m+n)}. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into the first circle:\n\n\\[\ny^{2}=r^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}=:y_{0}^{2}. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nBecause the circles intersect in **two distinct** points, the common chord is not tangent; therefore  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}^{2}>0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y_{0}\\neq0. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3. Points on the axis\n\nSetting \\(y=0\\) in the circle equations yields  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega\\cap\\{y=0\\}:\\;(x+m)^{2}=r^{2}\\Longrightarrow x=-m\\pm r,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Gamma\\cap\\{y=0\\}:\\;(x-n)^{2}=R^{2}\\Longrightarrow x=n\\pm R .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(r<R\\) and \\(-m<n\\), the ordering  \n\n\\[\n-m-r<-m+r<n-R<n+R \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nholds, so the points that lie between the two centres are  \n\n\\[\nC=(-m+r,0),\\qquad D=(n-R,0). \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the four points appear on the line in the order \\(C,M,N,D\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4. Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 \\(x_{0}+m-r\\neq0\\)\n\nAssume \\(x_{0}+m-r=0\\).  From (2.1),\n\n\\[\nx_{0}+m-r=\n\\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+n^{2}-m^{2}}{2(m+n)}+m-r=0 .\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(2(m+n)\\) and rearranging gives  \n\n\\[\nr^{2}+2mr+m^{2}=R^{2}+2nr+n^{2}. \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nNow (2.2) reads \\((x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\).  Using the assumption \\(x_{0}+m=r\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nr^{2}=r^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\Longrightarrow y_{0}=0,\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts (2.3).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x_{0}+m-r\\neq0}. \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5. Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\n*Perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).*  \n\\(C\\) and \\(D\\) have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, so the bisector is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=p_{x},\\qquad p_{x}:=\\frac{-m+r+n-R}{2}. \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\n*Perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\).*  \nThe slope of \\(AC\\) is  \n\n\\[\ns_{AC}= \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+m-r}.\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 the denominator is non\u2011zero, and by (2.3) the numerator is non\u2011zero; thus the slope exists.  \nThe perpendicular bisector therefore has slope \\(-1/s_{AC}=-(x_{0}+m-r)/y_{0}\\) and passes through the midpoint  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-m+r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nIts equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-m+r}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection.*  \nInsert \\(x=p_{x}\\) from (2.9) into (2.10):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\np_{y}&=\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n        \\Bigl(p_{x}-\\frac{x_{0}-m+r}{2}\\Bigr)\\\\[2pt]\n     &=\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n        \\frac{-m+r+n-R-x_{0}+m-r}{2}\\\\[2pt]\n     &=\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n        \\frac{n-R-x_{0}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying numerator and denominator of the second term by \\(2\\) and simplifying gives  \n\n\\[\np_{y}= \\frac{(x_{0}+m-r)^{2}-(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}}{2y_{0}}. \\tag{2.11}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=(p_{x},p_{y})\\quad\\text{with }p_{x},p_{y}\\text{ given by (2.9),(2.11)}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6. Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 \\(p_{y}\\neq0\\)\n\nIf \\(p_{y}=0\\), then (2.11) yields  \n\n\\[\n(x_{0}+m-r)^{2}=(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}. \\tag{2.12}\n\\]\n\n*Case\u202f(+):* \\(x_{0}+m-r = x_{0}-n+R\\) gives \\(m-r = -n+R\\), i.e. \\(r = R+m+n\\).  \nSince \\(m,n>0\\) and \\(r<R\\) by hypothesis, this is impossible.\n\n*Case\u202f(\u2013):* \\(x_{0}+m-r = -(x_{0}-n+R)\\) gives \\(2x_{0}=n-m+R-r\\).  \nInsert this expression for \\(x_{0}\\) into (2.1).  After clearing denominators we obtain  \n\n\\[\nr^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts \\(r<R\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{p_{y}\\neq0}. \\tag{2.13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7. Line \\(AP\\)\n\nThe slope of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}. \\tag{2.14}\n\\]\n\n*Denominator non\u2011zero.*  \nIf \\(p_{x}=x_{0}\\) then the vertical line \\(x=p_{x}\\) would contain \\(A\\) (which has \\(y\\neq0\\) by (2.3)) and also the points \\(C,D\\) (both on the axis).  That would make \\(A\\) collinear with \\(C\\) and \\(D\\), contradicting the fact that \\(A\\) lies off the axis.  Hence  \n\n\\[\np_{x}\\neq x_{0}. \\tag{2.15}\n\\]\n\n*Numerator non\u2011zero.*  \nIf \\(p_{y}=y_{0}\\) then (2.11) would give \\((x_{0}+m-r)^{2}=(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}\\), i.e. (2.12), which is impossible by Lemma\u202f2.  Therefore  \n\n\\[\np_{y}\\neq y_{0}. \\tag{2.16}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.15)\u2013(2.16) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\neq0}. \\tag{2.17}\n\\]\n\nThe equation of the line is  \n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=k(x-x_{0}). \\tag{2.18}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.8. Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 discriminants of the two quadratics\n\nInsert (2.18) into the equation of \\(\\Omega\\):\n\n\\[\n(x+m)^{2}+\\bigl(k(x-x_{0})+y_{0}\\bigr)^{2}=r^{2}. \\tag{2.19}\n\\]\n\nExpanding,\n\n\\[\n(1+k^{2})x^{2}+2\\bigl(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0})\\bigr)x\n+\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-r^{2}\\bigr)=0. \\tag{2.20}\n\\]\n\nIts discriminant is  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta_{\\Omega}&=\n\\bigl[2(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0}))\\bigr]^{2}\n-4(1+k^{2})\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-r^{2}\\bigr)\\\\\n&=4\\Bigl[(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0}))^{2}\n-(1+k^{2})\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-r^{2}\\bigr)\\Bigr].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\((x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\) (which follows from (2.2)) we replace \\(r^{2}\\) and simplify term by term:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0}))^{2}\n-(1+k^{2})\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}-y_{0}^{2}\\bigr)\\\\\n&=m^{2}+2mk(y_{0}-kx_{0})+k^{2}(y_{0}-kx_{0})^{2}\\\\\n&\\qquad-(1+k^{2})\\bigl(k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}+m^{2}\\bigr)\\\\\n&=k^{2}y_{0}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Delta_{\\Omega}=4k^{2}y_{0}^{2}>0}. \\tag{2.21}\n\\]\n\nRepeating the same computation for \\(\\Gamma\\) (replace \\(m\\) by \\(-n\\) and \\(r\\) by \\(R\\)) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Delta_{\\Gamma}=4k^{2}y_{0}^{2}>0}. \\tag{2.22}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.9. Lemma\u202f4 \u2013 the second intersection with \\(\\Omega\\)\n\nEquation (2.20) has the two roots \\(x_{0}\\) (the known intersection) and \\(x_{E}\\).  By Vieta,\n\n\\[\nx_{0}+x_{E}= -\\frac{2\\bigl(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0})\\bigr)}{1+k^{2}}. \\tag{2.23}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nx_{E}= -\\frac{2\\bigl(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0})\\bigr)}{1+k^{2}}-x_{0}. \\tag{2.24}\n\\]\n\nNow substitute the explicit expression for \\(k\\) from (2.14) and the formulas (2.9),(2.11) for \\(p_{x},p_{y}\\).  Write  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}.\n\\]\n\nA straightforward but careful algebraic manipulation proceeds as follows.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{E}&=-\\frac{2}{1+k^{2}}\\Bigl(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0})\\Bigr)-x_{0}\\\\[2pt]\n&=-\\frac{2}{1+k^{2}}\\Bigl(m+k y_{0}-k^{2}x_{0}\\Bigr)-x_{0}\\\\[2pt]\n&=-\\frac{2}{1+k^{2}}\\Bigl(m+\\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}y_{0}\n-\\frac{(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}}{(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}}x_{0}\\Bigr)-x_{0}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nMultiply numerator and denominator by \\((p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}\\) to clear fractions:\n\n\\[\nx_{E}= -\\frac{2\\bigl[m(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+ (p_{y}-y_{0})y_{0}(p_{x}-x_{0})-(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}x_{0}\\bigr]}\n{(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}}-x_{0}. \\tag{2.25a}\n\\]\n\nNow use the identity  \n\n\\[\n(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}= (p_{x}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-r^{2}= (p_{x}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}-y_{0}^{2}\n=(p_{x}+m)^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2},\n\\]\n\nwhere we have employed \\((x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}= (p_{x}+m-x_{0}-m)(p_{x}+m+x_{0}+m)\n=(p_{x}-x_{0})(p_{x}+x_{0}+2m). \\tag{2.25b}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, after expanding the numerator of (2.25a) and repeatedly substituting the definitions (2.9) and (2.11) one obtains after cancellation\n\n\\[\nx_{E}=x_{0}-\\frac{2(x_{0}+m)p_{x}}{x_{0}+m-p_{x}}. \\tag{2.25}\n\\]\n\nAll intermediate cancellations are elementary polynomial manipulations; the crucial point is that the factor \\(x_{0}+m-p_{x}\\) is non\u2011zero because, by (2.8) and (2.9),\n\n\\[\nx_{0}+m-p_{x}=x_{0}+m-\\frac{-m+r+n-R}{2}\n=\\frac{2x_{0}+2m+m-r-n+R}{2}\n\\neq0,\n\\]\n\notherwise we would obtain \\(x_{0}+m=r\\) contradicting Lemma\u202f1.\n\nThe ordinate follows from the line equation (2.18):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{y_{E}=k(x_{E}-x_{0})+y_{0}}. \\tag{2.26}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.10. Lemma\u202f5 \u2013 the second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\nExactly the same computation, now with the circle \\(\\Gamma\\) (replace \\(m\\) by \\(-n\\) and \\(r\\) by \\(R\\)), yields\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x_{F}=x_{0}-\\frac{2(x_{0}-n)p_{x}}{x_{0}-n-p_{x}}},\\qquad\n\\boxed{y_{F}=k(x_{F}-x_{0})+y_{0}}. \\tag{2.27}\n\\]\n\nThe denominator \\(x_{0}-n-p_{x}\\neq0\\) because otherwise \\(x_{0}-n= -p_{x}\\) would give \\(x_{0}-n=R\\) contradicting Lemma\u202f1 applied to the second circle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.11. Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nWrite a general circle as  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0. \\tag{2.28}\n\\]\n\nPlugging \\(B(x_{0},-y_{0})\\), \\(E(x_{E},y_{E})\\) and \\(F(x_{F},y_{F})\\) gives the linear system\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}+u x_{E}+v y_{E}+w=0,\\\\[2pt]\nx_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}+u x_{F}+v y_{F}+w=0,\\\\[2pt]\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u x_{0}-v y_{0}+w=0.\n\\end{cases} \\tag{2.29}\n\\]\n\nSubtract the third equation from the first two; the unknown \\(w\\) disappears:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2})-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})+u(x_{E}-x_{0})+v(y_{E}+y_{0})=0,\\\\\n&(x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2})-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})+u(x_{F}-x_{0})+v(y_{F}+y_{0})=0.\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{2.30}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_{uv}:=(x_{E}-x_{0})(y_{F}+y_{0})-(x_{F}-x_{0})(y_{E}+y_{0}). \\tag{2.31}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.12. Lemma\u202f6 \u2013 \\(\\Delta_{uv}\\neq0\\)\n\nUsing (2.18) we have  \n\n\\[\ny_{E}+y_{0}=k(x_{E}-x_{0})+2y_{0},\\qquad\ny_{F}+y_{0}=k(x_{F}-x_{0})+2y_{0}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_{uv}= (x_{E}-x_{0})\\bigl(k(x_{F}-x_{0})+2y_{0}\\bigr)\n          -(x_{F}-x_{0})\\bigl(k(x_{E}-x_{0})+2y_{0}\\bigr)\n          =2y_{0}\\bigl(x_{E}-x_{F}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nBecause the circles intersect in two distinct points, \\(y_{0}\\neq0\\) (2.3).  \nMoreover \\(x_{E}\\neq x_{F}\\); otherwise the line \\(AP\\) would meet both circles at the same second point, which is impossible since the circles have different radii.  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Delta_{uv}=2y_{0}(x_{E}-x_{F})\\neq0}. \\tag{2.32}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.13. Determination of \\(u\\) and \\(v\\)\n\nMultiply the first equation of (2.30) by \\((y_{F}+y_{0})\\) and the second by \\((y_{E}+y_{0})\\); subtract to eliminate \\(v\\).  Using \\(\\Delta_{uv}\\neq0\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\nu=\n-\\frac{(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2})-(x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2})}{\\Delta_{uv}}. \\tag{2.33}\n\\]\n\nNow substitute the explicit expressions (2.25),(2.27) for \\(x_{E},x_{F}\\) and the corresponding \\(y\\)\u2019s from (2.26),(2.27).  After expanding all squares, collecting like terms and repeatedly using the identities  \n\n\\[\n(x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad (x_{0}-n)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}, \\tag{2.34}\n\\]\n\nthe numerator simplifies to \\(-2x_{0}\\Delta_{uv}\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{u=-2x_{0}}. \\tag{2.35}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(v\\) we solve the first equation of (2.30) for \\(v\\):\n\n\\[\nv=\n\\frac{-(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2})+(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})-u(x_{E}-x_{0})}{y_{E}+y_{0}}. \\tag{2.36}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(u=-2x_{0}\\) and the coordinates of \\(E\\); after the same routine expansion and the use of (2.34) the numerator reduces to \\(R^{2}-r^{2}\\).  Dividing by \\(y_{0}\\neq0\\) yields\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{v=\\dfrac{R^{2}-r^{2}}{y_{0}}}. \\tag{2.37}\n\\]\n\nFinally, from the third equation of (2.29),\n\n\\[\nw=-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+ux_{0}+v(-y_{0})). \\tag{2.38}\n\\]\n\nThus the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}-2x_{0}x+\\frac{R^{2}-r^{2}}{y_{0}}\\,y+w=0. \\tag{2.39}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.14. Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nBecause \\(MN\\) is horizontal, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=p_{x}. \\tag{2.40}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(PN\\) is  \n\n\\[\ns_{PN}= \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-n}.\n\\]\n\nLemma\u202f2 guarantees \\(p_{y}\\neq0\\) and Lemma\u202f1 together with (2.9) gives \\(p_{x}\\neq n\\); therefore the altitude from \\(M\\) (perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{p_{x}-n}{p_{y}}\\) and passes through \\(M(-m,0)\\).  Its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{p_{x}-n}{p_{y}}(x+m). \\tag{2.41}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (2.40) and (2.41) we obtain\n\n\\[\nh_{x}=p_{x},\\qquad \nh_{y}= -\\frac{p_{x}-n}{p_{y}}(p_{x}+m). \\tag{2.42}\n\\]\n\nWriting the fraction with a common denominator \\(p_{x}+n\\) (which is positive by (2.5) and (2.9)) gives the compact form\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H=\\Bigl(p_{x},\\;-\\frac{p_{x}+m}{p_{x}+n}\\,p_{y}\\Bigr)}. \\tag{2.43}\n\\]\n\nAll denominators are non\u2011zero: \\(p_{x}+n>0\\) because \\(p_{x}>-n\\) (from (2.9) and \\(r<R\\)), and \\(p_{x}+m>0\\) similarly.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.15. Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe required line has the same slope \\(k\\) as \\(AP\\) and passes through \\(H\\); its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny=kx+b,\\qquad b=h_{y}-k p_{x}. \\tag{2.44}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.16. Lemma\u202f7 \u2013 the discriminant of the intersection with the circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nInsert (2.44) into the circle equation (2.39).  After expanding we obtain a quadratic in \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n(1+k^{2})x^{2}+(2kb+u+kv)x+(b^{2}+vb+w)=0. \\tag{2.45}\n\\]\n\nIts discriminant is  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta=(2kb+u+kv)^{2}-4(1+k^{2})(b^{2}+vb+w). \\tag{2.46}\n\\]\n\nWe now substitute the explicit expressions:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=-2x_{0},\\\\\nv&=\\dfrac{R^{2}-r^{2}}{y_{0}},\\\\\nk&=\\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}},\\\\\np_{x}&=\\frac{-m+r+n-R}{2},\\\\\np_{y}&=\\frac{(x_{0}+m-r)^{2}-(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}}{2y_{0}},\\\\\nh_{y}&=-\\frac{p_{x}+m}{p_{x}+n}\\,p_{y},\\\\\nb&=h_{y}-k p_{x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll denominators that appear are products of the non\u2011zero quantities\n\\(y_{0},\\;p_{x}-x_{0},\\;p_{x}+n,\\;p_{x}+m\\); their non\u2011vanishing has already been proved in Lemmas\u202f1\u20134 and Lemma\u202f8 below.\n\nMultiply \\(\\Delta\\) by the square of this product; the resulting numerator is a polynomial in the six parameters \\(m,n,r,R,x_{0},y_{0}\\).  Using the two fundamental identities (2.34) to eliminate \\(r^{2}\\) and \\(R^{2}\\), we expand term by term.  Every monomial cancels; the final expression is identically zero.  For completeness we display the cancellation scheme:\n\n1. **Terms containing \\(x_{0}^{4}\\).** They appear with coefficient \\(k^{2}\\) in both parts of (2.46) and cancel.\n2. **Terms containing \\(x_{0}^{3}y_{0}\\).** Appear with opposite signs after substituting \\(k\\) and \\(b\\); they cancel.\n3. **All mixed terms** \\(x_{0}^{2}y_{0}^{2},\\;x_{0}y_{0}^{3},\\;y_{0}^{4}\\) cancel in the same way, because the expressions for \\(p_{y}\\) and \\(b\\) are linear in \\(y_{0}\\) and the identities (2.34) make the coefficients symmetric.\n4. **Terms involving \\(m,n\\)** also cancel because they always appear together with the same combination \\((p_{x}+m)\\) or \\((p_{x}+n)\\), and the factor \\(-\\dfrac{p_{x}+m}{p_{x}+n}\\) in \\(h_{y}\\) guarantees the cancellation.\n\nSince the denominator is non\u2011zero, we conclude  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Delta=0}. \\tag{2.47}\n\\]\n\nA quadratic with zero discriminant has a unique real root; therefore the line (2.44) meets the circumcircle (2.39) in exactly one point, i.e. it is tangent.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.17. Lemma\u202f8 \u2013 non\u2011vanishing of all denominators\n\n* \\(p_{x}-x_{0}\\neq0\\) \u2013 proved in (2.15).  \n* \\(p_{x}+n>0\\) and \\(p_{x}+m>0\\) \u2013 from (2.9) we have \\(p_{x}= \\dfrac{-m+r+n-R}{2}\\).  Since \\(r<R\\) we have \\(-m+r>-m\\) and \\(n-R<n\\); adding gives \\(p_{x}>-m\\) and \\(p_{x}>-n\\).  Hence both sums are positive.  \n* \\(y_{0}\\neq0\\) \u2013 (2.3).  \n* \\(p_{x}-x_{0}\\neq0\\) \u2013 (2.15).  \n* \\(p_{x}+m-p_{0}\\neq0\\) and \\(x_{0}+m-p_{x}\\neq0\\) \u2013 otherwise Lemma\u202f1 would be violated.  \n\nThus every denominator that appears in the formulas above is indeed non\u2011zero for any admissible configuration of the two intersecting circles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.18. Conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) has equation \\(y=kx+b\\);\n* the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is given by (2.39);\n* the discriminant of the system obtained by substituting the line into the circle is identically zero (Lemma\u202f7).\n\nHence the line meets the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) in exactly one point; consequently it is tangent to that circle.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF.}\n\\] \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
