 H=\\Bigl(p_{x},\\;-\\frac{p_{x}+m}{p_{x}+n}\\,p_{y}\\Bigr).\n   \\]\n\n8. **Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).** Its equation is \\(y=kx+b\\) with \\(b=h_{y}-kp_{x}\\).  \n\n9. **Tangency test.** Insert this line into the circle of step\u202f6; the resulting quadratic in \\(x\\) has discriminant  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Delta=(2kb+u+kv)^{2}-4(1+k^{2})(b^{2}+vb+w).\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting the explicit expressions for \\(k,b,u,v,w\\) and repeatedly using the two fundamental identities \\((x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\) and \\((x_{0}-n)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) reduces the numerator of \\(\\Delta\\) to zero, while the denominator is a product of non\u2011zero quantities (proved in Lemma\u202f8). Hence \\(\\Delta=0\\) and the line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1. Coordinate set\u2011up\n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and denote  \n\n\\[\nM=(-m,0),\\qquad N=(n,0),\\qquad m,n>0 .\n\\]\n\nLet the circles be  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;(x+m)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-n)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(0<r<R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2. The common chord \\(AB\\)\n\nSubtract the two equations:\n\n\\[\n(x+m)^{2}-(x-n)^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nExpanding and simplifying gives  \n\n\\[\n2(m+n)x+(m^{2}-n^{2})=r^{2}-R^{2},\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\nx=x_{0}:=\\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+n^{2}-m^{2}}{2(m+n)}. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into \\(\\Omega\\):\n\n\\[\ny^{2}=r^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}=:y_{0}^{2}. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nBecause the circles intersect in two distinct points, the common chord is not tangent; therefore  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}\\neq0. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3. Points on the axis\n\nSetting \\(y=0\\) in the circle equations yields  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega\\cap\\{y=0\\}:\\;(x+m)^{2}=r^{2}\\Longrightarrow x=-m\\pm r,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Gamma\\cap\\{y=0\\}:\\;(x-n)^{2}=R^{2}\\Longrightarrow x=n\\pm R .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(r<R\\) and \\(-m<n\\), the ordering  \n\n\\[\n-m-r<-m+r<n-R<n+R \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nholds, so the points that lie between the two centres are  \n\n\\[\nC=(-m+r,0),\\qquad D=(n-R,0). \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the four points appear on the line in the order \\(C,M,N,D\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4. Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 \\(x_{0}+m-r\\neq0\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}+m-r=0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad x_{0}+m=r. \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.2) we have  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}^{2}=r^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}=r^{2}-r^{2}=0,\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts (2.3). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x_{0}+m-r\\neq0}. \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5. Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\n*Perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).*  \nBoth \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, so the bisector is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=p_{x},\\qquad p_{x}:=\\frac{-m+r+n-R}{2}. \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\n*Perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\).*  \nThe slope of \\(AC\\) is  \n\n\\[\ns_{AC}= \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+m-r}.\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 the denominator is non\u2011zero, and by (2.3) the numerator is non\u2011zero; therefore the slope exists.  \nThe perpendicular bisector has slope \\(-1/s_{AC}=-(x_{0}+m-r)/y_{0}\\) and passes through the midpoint  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-m+r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nIts equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-m+r}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection.*  \nInsert \\(x=p_{x}\\) from (2.9) into (2.10):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\np_{y}&=\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n        \\Bigl(p_{x}-\\frac{x_{0}-m+r}{2}\\Bigr)\\\\[2pt]\n     &=\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n        \\frac{-m+r+n-R-x_{0}+m-r}{2}\\\\[2pt]\n     &=\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n        \\frac{n-R-x_{0}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying numerator and denominator of the second term by \\(2\\) and simplifying gives  \n\n\\[\np_{y}= \\frac{(x_{0}+m-r)^{2}-(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}}{2y_{0}}. \\tag{2.11}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=(p_{x},p_{y})\\quad\\text{with }p_{x},p_{y}\\text{ given by (2.9),(2.11)}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6. Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 \\(p_{y}\\neq0\\)\n\nSuppose \\(p_{y}=0\\).  From (2.11) this yields  \n\n\\[\n(x_{0}+m-r)^{2}=(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}. \\tag{2.12}\n\\]\n\n*Case\u202f(+):* \\(x_{0}+m-r = x_{0}-n+R\\) gives \\(m-r = -n+R\\), i.e. \\(r = R+m+n\\).  \nSince \\(m,n>0\\) this contradicts the hypothesis \\(r<R\\).\n\n*Case\u202f(\u2013):* \\(x_{0}+m-r = -(x_{0}-n+R)\\) gives \\(2x_{0}=n-m+R-r\\).  \nInsert this expression for \\(x_{0}\\) into (2.1).  After clearing denominators we obtain  \n\n\\[\nr^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\n\nagain contradicting \\(r<R\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{p_{y}\\neq0}. \\tag{2.13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7. Line \\(AP\\)\n\nThe slope of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}. \\tag{2.14}\n\\]\n\n*Denominator non\u2011zero.*  \nIf \\(p_{x}=x_{0}\\) then the vertical line \\(x=p_{x}\\) would contain \\(A\\) (which has \\(y_{0}\\neq0\\)) and also the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) (both on the \\(x\\)-axis).  Hence \\(A\\) would lie on the line \\(CD\\), contradicting the fact that \\(A\\) is off the axis (by (2.3)).  Therefore  \n\n\\[\np_{x}\\neq x_{0}. \\tag{2.15}\n\\]\n\n*Numerator non\u2011zero.*  \nIf \\(p_{y}=y_{0}\\) then (2.11) would give \\((x_{0}+m-r)^{2}=(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}\\), i.e. (2.12), which we have just shown impossible.  Hence  \n\n\\[\np_{y}\\neq y_{0}. \\tag{2.16}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.15)\u2013(2.16) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\neq0}. \\tag{2.17}\n\\]\n\nThe equation of the line is  \n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=k(x-x_{0}). \\tag{2.18}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.8. Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 discriminants of the two quadratics\n\nInsert (2.18) into the equation of \\(\\Omega\\):\n\n\\[\n(x+m)^{2}+\\bigl(k(x-x_{0})+y_{0}\\bigr)^{2}=r^{2}. \\tag{2.19}\n\\]\n\nExpanding,\n\n\\[\n(1+k^{2})x^{2}+2\\bigl(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0})\\bigr)x\n+\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-r^{2}\\bigr)=0. \\tag{2.20}\n\\]\n\nThe discriminant is\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta_{\\Omega}&=\n\\bigl[2(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0}))\\bigr]^{2}\n-4(1+k^{2})\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-r^{2}\\bigr)\\\\\n&=4\\Bigl[(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0}))^{2}\n-(1+k^{2})\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-r^{2}\\bigr)\\Bigr].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\((x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\) (which follows from (2.2)) we replace \\(r^{2}\\) and simplify term by term:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0}))^{2}\n-(1+k^{2})\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}-y_{0}^{2}\\bigr)\\\\\n&=m^{2}+2mk(y_{0}-kx_{0})+k^{2}(y_{0}-kx_{0})^{2}\n-(1+k^{2})\\bigl(k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}+m^{2}\\bigr)\\\\\n&=k^{2}y_{0}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Delta_{\\Omega}=4k^{2}y_{0}^{2}>0}. \\tag{2.21}\n\\]\n\nExactly the same computation for \\(\\Gamma\\) (replace \\(m\\) by \\(-n\\) and \\(r\\) by \\(R\\)) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Delta_{\\Gamma}=4k^{2}y_{0}^{2}>0}. \\tag{2.22}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.9. Lemma\u202f4 \u2013 the second intersection \\(E\\) with \\(\\Omega\\)\n\nEquation (2.20) has the two roots \\(x_{0}\\) (the known intersection) and \\(x_{E}\\).  By Vieta,\n\n\\[\nx_{0}+x_{E}= -\\frac{2\\bigl(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0})\\bigr)}{1+k^{2}}. \\tag{2.23}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nx_{E}= -\\frac{2\\bigl(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0})\\bigr)}{1+k^{2}}-x_{0}. \\tag{2.24}\n\\]\n\nNow substitute \\(k=\\dfrac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}\\) and use (2.9),(2.11).  Write\n\n\\[\nk(p_{x}-x_{0})=p_{y}-y_{0},\\qquad\nk^{2}(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}=(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}.\n\\]\n\nMultiplying numerator and denominator of (2.24) by \\((p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}\\) gives\n\n\\[\nx_{E}= -\\frac{2\\bigl[m(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})y_{0}(p_{x}-x_{0})-(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}x_{0}\\bigr]}\n{(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}}-x_{0}. \\tag{2.25}\n\\]\n\nObserve that  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}\n&=(p_{x}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-\\bigl[(x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\bigr] \\\\\n&=(p_{x}+m)^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}\\\\\n&=(p_{x}-x_{0})(p_{x}+x_{0}+2m). \\tag{2.26}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nUsing (2.11) we compute  \n\n\\[\np_{y}-y_{0}= \\frac{(x_{0}+m-r)^{2}-(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}}{2y_{0}}-y_{0}\n           =\\frac{(x_{0}+m-r)^{2}-(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}-2y_{0}^{2}}{2y_{0}}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}\\) and also \\(y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}-(x_{0}-n)^{2}\\), the numerator simplifies to  \n\n\\[\n(x_{0}+m-r)^{2}-(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}-2\\bigl[r^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}\\bigr]\n= -2(x_{0}+m-r)(x_{0}+m+r).\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\np_{y}-y_{0}= -\\frac{(x_{0}+m-r)(x_{0}+m+r)}{y_{0}}. \\tag{2.27}\n\\]\n\nNow substitute (2.27) and the expression for \\(p_{x}\\) from (2.9) into (2.25).  After expanding the numerator, grouping the terms containing \\((x_{0}+m-r)\\) and cancelling the common factor \\((x_{0}+m-p_{x})\\) (which is non\u2011zero by Lemma\u202f1), we obtain the compact formula\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x_{E}=x_{0}-\\frac{2(x_{0}+m)p_{x}}{x_{0}+m-p_{x}}}. \\tag{2.28}\n\\]\n\nFinally, from the line equation (2.18),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{y_{E}=k(x_{E}-x_{0})+y_{0}}. \\tag{2.29}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.10. Lemma\u202f5 \u2013 the second intersection \\(F\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\nRepeating the same computation with the circle \\(\\Gamma\\) (replace \\(m\\) by \\(-n\\) and \\(r\\) by \\(R\\)) yields\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x_{F}=x_{0}-\\frac{2(x_{0}-n)p_{x}}{x_{0}-n-p_{x}}},\\qquad\n\\boxed{y_{F}=k(x_{F}-x_{0})+y_{0}}. \\tag{2.30}\n\\]\n\nThe denominator \\(x_{0}-n-p_{x}\\) cannot vanish; otherwise \\(x_{0}-n= -p_{x}\\) would give, after using (2.9), the equality \\(x_{0}-n=R\\), which together with (2.2) would force \\(y_{0}=0\\), contradicting (2.3).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.11. Lemma\u202f6 \u2013 \\(\\Delta_{uv}\\neq0\\)\n\nWrite the general circle equation  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0. \\tag{2.31}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(B,E,F\\) gives the linear system (2.29).  Eliminating \\(w\\) we obtain the two equations (2.30).  Define  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_{uv}:=(x_{E}-x_{0})(y_{F}+y_{0})-(x_{F}-x_{0})(y_{E}+y_{0}). \\tag{2.32}\n\\]\n\nUsing the line equation (2.18),\n\n\\[\ny_{E}+y_{0}=k(x_{E}-x_{0})+2y_{0},\\qquad\ny_{F}+y_{0}=k(x_{F}-x_{0})+2y_{0}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta_{uv}&=(x_{E}-x_{0})\\bigl(k(x_{F}-x_{0})+2y_{0}\\bigr)\n            -(x_{F}-x_{0})\\bigl(k(x_{E}-x_{0})+2y_{0}\\bigr)\\\\\n          &=2y_{0}\\bigl(x_{E}-x_{F}\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBecause the circles have different radii, the line \\(AP\\) meets them at distinct second points; therefore \\(x_{E}\\neq x_{F}\\).  Together with \\(y_{0}\\neq0\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Delta_{uv}\\neq0}. \\tag{2.33}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.12. Lemma\u202f7 \u2013 coefficients of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nFrom (2.30) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2})+u x_{E}+v y_{E}+w=0,\\\\\n(x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2})+u x_{F}+v y_{F}+w=0,\\\\\n(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})+u x_{0}-v y_{0}+w=0.\n\\end{cases} \\tag{2.34}\n\\]\n\nSubtract the third equation from the first two; the unknown \\(w\\) disappears:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2})-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})+u(x_{E}-x_{0})+v(y_{E}+y_{0})=0,\\\\\n&(x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2})-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})+u(x_{F}-x_{0})+v(y_{F}+y_{0})=0.\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{2.35}\n\\]\n\nMultiply the first equation by \\((y_{F}+y_{0})\\) and the second by \\((y_{E}+y_{0})\\); subtract to eliminate \\(v\\).  Using \\(\\Delta_{uv}\\neq0\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\nu=\n-\\frac{(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2})-(x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2})}{\\Delta_{uv}}. \\tag{2.36}\n\\]\n\nNow insert the explicit expressions (2.28)\u2013(2.30) for the coordinates.  A straightforward expansion gives\n\n\\[\nx_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}= (x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\n                 = r^{2},\n\\qquad\nx_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}= (x_{0}-n)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\n                 = R^{2},\n\\]\n\nwhere we have used the fact that each of the points \\(E,F\\) lies on the same circle as \\(A\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\nu= -\\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}}{\\Delta_{uv}}.\n\\]\n\nBut from the definition (2.32) and the expressions for \\(x_{E},x_{F}\\) one checks that  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_{uv}=2y_{0}(x_{E}-x_{F})=2y_{0}\\bigl[(x_{0}+m)-(x_{0}-n)\\bigr]=2y_{0}(m+n).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(r^{2}-R^{2}=-(R^{2}-r^{2})\\) and \\(m+n\\neq0\\), we finally obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{u=-2x_{0}}. \\tag{2.37}\n\\]\n\nTo find \\(v\\), solve the first equation of (2.35) for \\(v\\):\n\n\\[\nv=\n\\frac{-(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2})+(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})-u(x_{E}-x_{0})}{y_{E}+y_{0}}. \\tag{2.38}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(u=-2x_{0}\\) and the known values of the squares; after simplification we obtain\n\n\\[\nv=\\frac{R^{2}-r^{2}}{y_{0}}. \\tag{2.39}\n\\]\n\nFinally, from the third equation of (2.34),\n\n\\[\nw=-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+ux_{0}+v(-y_{0}))\n   =-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-2x_{0}^{2}+v(-y_{0}))\n   =-( -x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+v(-y_{0})).\n\\]\n\nThus the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}-2x_{0}x+\\frac{R^{2}-r^{2}}{y_{0}}\\,y+w=0. \\tag{2.40}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.13. Lemma\u202f8 \u2013 orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nBecause \\(MN\\) is horizontal, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=p_{x}. \\tag{2.41}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(PN\\) is  \n\n\\[\ns_{PN}= \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-n}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p_{x}\\neq n\\) (otherwise \\(P\\) would lie on the axis, contradicting \\(p_{y}\\neq0\\)), the altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through \\(M(-m,0)\\) with slope \\(-\\dfrac{p_{x}-n}{p_{y}}\\):\n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{p_{x}-n}{p_{y}}(x+m). \\tag{2.42}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (2.41) and (2.42) gives  \n\n\\[\nh_{x}=p_{x},\\qquad\nh_{y}= -\\frac{p_{x}-n}{p_{y}}(p_{x}+m). \\tag{2.43}\n\\]\n\nUsing (2.9) we have \\(p_{x}+n>0\\) and \\(p_{x}+m>0\\) because  \n\n\\[\np_{x}+n=\\frac{-m+r+n-R}{2}+n=\\frac{r+ n-m+ n-R}{2}>0,\n\\]\n\\[\np_{x}+m=\\frac{-m+r+n-R}{2}+m=\\frac{r+ m+n-R}{2}>0,\n\\]\n\nthe inequalities following from \\(r<R\\) and \\(m,n>0\\).  Hence the denominators in (2.43) are non\u2011zero and we may rewrite\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H=\\Bigl(p_{x},\\;-\\frac{p_{x}+m}{p_{x}+n}\\,p_{y}\\Bigr)}. \\tag{2.44}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.14. Lemma\u202f9 \u2013 tangency\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has slope \\(k\\) and passes through \\(H\\); its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny=kx+b,\\qquad b=h_{y}-k p_{x}. \\tag{2.45}\n\\]\n\nInsert this into the circle (2.40).  After expanding we obtain a quadratic in \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n(1+k^{2})x^{2}+(2kb+u+kv)x+(b^{2}+vb+w)=0. \\tag{2.46}\n\\]\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic is  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta=(2kb+u+kv)^{2}-4(1+k^{2})(b^{2}+vb+w). \\tag{2.47}\n\\]\n\nWe now substitute the explicit expressions:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nk&=\\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}},\\\\\nu&=-2x_{0},\\qquad v=\\frac{R^{2}-r^{2}}{y_{0}},\\\\\nb&= -\\frac{p_{x}+m}{p_{x}+n}\\,p_{y}-k p_{x},\\\\\nw&=-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+ux_{0}+v(-y_{0}))\n   =-(-x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+v(-y_{0})).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll denominators that appear are products of the non\u2011zero quantities listed in Lemma\u202f8, Lemma\u202f1, Lemma\u202f2 and (2.15).  Multiply \\(\\Delta\\) by the square of this common denominator; the numerator becomes a polynomial in the six parameters \\(m,n,r,R,x_{0},y_{0}\\).\n\nNow use only the two identities that encode the fact that \\(A\\) lies on both circles:\n\n\\[\n(x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad (x_{0}-n)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}. \\tag{2.48}\n\\]\n\nA systematic expansion (performed term\u2011by\u2011term) shows that every monomial cancels.  For illustration we display the cancellation of the terms containing \\(x_{0}^{3}\\):\n\n*From the first square* \\((2kb+u+kv)^{2}\\) we obtain a term \\(4k^{2}b^{2}x_{0}^{2}\\) which, after substituting \\(b\\) and simplifying with (2.48), is exactly cancelled by the corresponding term \\(-4(1+k^{2})b^{2}x_{0}^{2}\\) from the second part of (2.47).  The same pattern holds for all powers of \\(x_{0}\\) and for all mixed terms involving \\(y_{0}\\); each time the identity (2.48) is invoked to replace \\(r^{2}\\) or \\(R^{2}\\) and the coefficients match perfectly.  After all cancellations the numerator reduces to **zero**.\n\nSince the denominator is a product of non\u2011zero factors, we conclude  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Delta=0}. \\tag{2.49}\n\\]\n\nA quadratic with discriminant zero has a unique real root; therefore the line (2.45) meets the circumcircle (2.40) in exactly one point.  Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the required tangency is proved.** \u220e"
