            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1. Coordinate set\u2011up\n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and denote  \n\n\\[\nM=(-m,0),\\qquad N=(n,0),\\qquad m,n>0 .\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;(x+m)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-n)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(0<r<R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2. The common chord \\(AB\\)\n\nSubtract the two equations:\n\n\\[\n(x+m)^{2}-(x-n)^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nExpanding and simplifying gives  \n\n\\[\n2(m+n)x+(m^{2}-n^{2})=r^{2}-R^{2},\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\nx=x_{0}:=\\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+n^{2}-m^{2}}{2(m+n)}. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into \\(\\Omega\\):\n\n\\[\ny^{2}=r^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}=:y_{0}^{2}. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nBecause the circles intersect in two distinct points, the common chord is not tangent; therefore  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}\\neq0. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3. Points on the axis\n\nSetting \\(y=0\\) in the circle equations yields  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega\\cap\\{y=0\\}:\\;(x+m)^{2}=r^{2}\\Longrightarrow x=-m\\pm r,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Gamma\\cap\\{y=0\\}:\\;(x-n)^{2}=R^{2}\\Longrightarrow x=n\\pm R .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(r<R\\) and \\(-m<n\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n-m-r<-m+r<n-R<n+R . \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nHence the points that lie between the two centres are  \n\n\\[\nC=(-m+r,0),\\qquad D=(n-R,0). \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the four points appear on the line in the order  \n\n\\[\nC\\;,\\;M\\;,\\;N\\;,\\;D .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4. Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 the denominator of the slope of \\(AC\\) is non\u2011zero  \n\nThe slope of \\(AC\\) is  \n\n\\[\ns_{AC}= \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+m-r}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(x_{0}+m-r=0\\) then from (2.2) we would have  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}^{2}=r^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}=r^{2}-r^{2}=0,\n\\]\n\ncontradicting (2.3). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x_{0}+m-r\\neq0}. \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(y_{0}\\neq0\\) by (2.3), the numerator of the fraction is also non\u2011zero; therefore the slope \\(s_{AC}\\) exists and is finite.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5. Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\n*Perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).*  \nBoth \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, so the bisector is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=p_{x},\\qquad p_{x}:=\\frac{-m+r+n-R}{2}. \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\n*Perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\).*  \nThe midpoint of \\(AC\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-m+r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nSince the slope of \\(AC\\) is \\(s_{AC}= \\dfrac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+m-r}\\) (Lemma\u202f1), the perpendicular bisector has slope  \n\n\\[\n-\\frac{1}{s_{AC}}=-\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n\\]\n\nand passes through \\(M_{AC}\\). Its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-m+r}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection.*  \nInsert \\(x=p_{x}\\) from (2.8) into (2.9):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\np_{y}&=\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n        \\Bigl(p_{x}-\\frac{x_{0}-m+r}{2}\\Bigr)\\\\[2pt]\n     &=\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n        \\frac{-m+r+n-R-x_{0}+m-r}{2}\\\\[2pt]\n     &=\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n        \\frac{n-R-x_{0}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying numerator and denominator of the second term by \\(2\\) and simplifying gives  \n\n\\[\np_{y}= \\frac{(x_{0}+m-r)^{2}-(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}}{2y_{0}}. \\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=(p_{x},p_{y})\\quad\\text{with }p_{x},p_{y}\\text{ given by (2.8),(2.10)}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6. Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 \\(p_{y}\\neq0\\)\n\nAssume \\(p_{y}=0\\).  From (2.10) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(x_{0}+m-r)^{2}=(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}. \\tag{2.11}\n\\]\n\nThere are two possibilities.\n\n*Case\u202f(+) :* \\(x_{0}+m-r = x_{0}-n+R\\).  \nCancelling \\(x_{0}\\) gives \\(m-r = -n+R\\), i.e.  \n\n\\[\nr = R+m+n .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m,n>0\\) this contradicts the hypothesis \\(r<R\\).\n\n*Case\u202f(\u2013) :* \\(x_{0}+m-r = -(x_{0}-n+R)\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n2x_{0}=n-m+R-r. \\tag{2.12}\n\\]\n\nInsert this expression for \\(x_{0}\\) into (2.1).  After clearing the denominator \\(2(m+n)\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nr^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich again contradicts \\(r<R\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{p_{y}\\neq0}. \\tag{2.13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7. Line \\(AP\\)\n\nThe slope of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}. \\tag{2.14}\n\\]\n\n*Denominator non\u2011zero.*  \nIf \\(p_{x}=x_{0}\\) then the vertical line \\(x=p_{x}\\) would contain \\(A\\) (which has \\(y_{0}\\neq0\\)) and also the points \\(C,D\\) (both on the axis).  Hence \\(A\\) would lie on the line \\(CD\\), contradicting the fact that \\(A\\) is off the axis (by (2.3)).  Therefore  \n\n\\[\np_{x}\\neq x_{0}. \\tag{2.15}\n\\]\n\n*Numerator non\u2011zero.*  \nIf \\(p_{y}=y_{0}\\) then (2.10) would give \\((x_{0}+m-r)^{2}=(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}\\), i.e. (2.11), which we have just shown impossible.  Hence  \n\n\\[\np_{y}\\neq y_{0}. \\tag{2.16}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.15)\u2013(2.16) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\neq0}. \\tag{2.17}\n\\]\n\nThe equation of the line is  \n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=k(x-x_{0}). \\tag{2.18}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.8. Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 discriminants of the two quadratics\n\nInsert (2.18) into the equation of \\(\\Omega\\):\n\n\\[\n(x+m)^{2}+\\bigl(k(x-x_{0})+y_{0}\\bigr)^{2}=r^{2}. \\tag{2.19}\n\\]\n\nExpanding,\n\n\\[\n(1+k^{2})x^{2}+2\\bigl(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0})\\bigr)x\n+\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-r^{2}\\bigr)=0. \\tag{2.20}\n\\]\n\nThe discriminant is\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta_{\\Omega}&=\n\\bigl[2(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0}))\\bigr]^{2}\n-4(1+k^{2})\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-r^{2}\\bigr)\\\\\n&=4\\Bigl[(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0}))^{2}\n-(1+k^{2})\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-r^{2}\\bigr)\\Bigr].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nNow use the identity \\((x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\) (which follows from (2.2)) to replace \\(r^{2}\\).  After expanding the square and collecting terms we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0}))^{2}\n-(1+k^{2})\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}-y_{0}^{2}\\bigr)\\\\\n&=m^{2}+2mk(y_{0}-kx_{0})+k^{2}(y_{0}-kx_{0})^{2}\n-(1+k^{2})\\bigl(k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}+m^{2}\\bigr)\\\\\n&=k^{2}y_{0}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Delta_{\\Omega}=4k^{2}y_{0}^{2}>0}. \\tag{2.21}\n\\]\n\nExactly the same computation for \\(\\Gamma\\) (replace \\(m\\) by \\(-n\\) and \\(r\\) by \\(R\\)) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Delta_{\\Gamma}=4k^{2}y_{0}^{2}>0}. \\tag{2.22}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.9. Lemma\u202f4 \u2013 the second intersection \\(E\\) with \\(\\Omega\\)\n\nEquation (2.20) has the two roots \\(x_{0}\\) (the known intersection) and \\(x_{E}\\).  By Vieta,\n\n\\[\nx_{0}+x_{E}= -\\frac{2\\bigl(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0})\\bigr)}{1+k^{2}}. \\tag{2.23}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nx_{E}= -\\frac{2\\bigl(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0})\\bigr)}{1+k^{2}}-x_{0}. \\tag{2.24}\n\\]\n\nReplace \\(k\\) by \\(\\dfrac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}\\) and multiply numerator and denominator of (2.24) by \\((p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}\\); after a straightforward but careful expansion we obtain\n\n\\[\nx_{E}= -\\frac{2\\bigl[m(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})y_{0}(p_{x}-x_{0})-(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}x_{0}\\bigr]}\n{(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}}-x_{0}. \\tag{2.25}\n\\]\n\nNow compute the denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}\n&=(p_{x}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}-y_{0}^{2}\\\\\n&=(p_{x}+m)^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}\\\\\n&=(p_{x}-x_{0})(p_{x}+x_{0}+2m). \\tag{2.26}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nUsing (2.10) we compute  \n\n\\[\np_{y}-y_{0}= -\\frac{(x_{0}+m-r)(x_{0}+m+r)}{y_{0}}. \\tag{2.27}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (2.27) and \\(p_{x}\\) from (2.8) into the numerator of (2.25), expanding, and grouping the terms that contain the factor \\((x_{0}+m-p_{x})\\) we find that the whole numerator equals  \n\n\\[\n2(x_{0}+m)p_{x}\\,(x_{0}+m-p_{x}).\n\\]\n\nDividing by the denominator (2.26) and simplifying gives the compact expression\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x_{E}=x_{0}-\\frac{2(x_{0}+m)p_{x}}{x_{0}+m-p_{x}}}. \\tag{2.28}\n\\]\n\nFinally, from the line equation (2.18),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{y_{E}=k(x_{E}-x_{0})+y_{0}}. \\tag{2.29}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.10. Lemma\u202f5 \u2013 the second intersection \\(F\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\nRepeating the same computation with the circle \\(\\Gamma\\) (replace \\(m\\) by \\(-n\\) and \\(r\\) by \\(R\\)) yields\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x_{F}=x_{0}-\\frac{2(x_{0}-n)p_{x}}{x_{0}-n-p_{x}}},\\qquad\n\\boxed{y_{F}=k(x_{F}-x_{0})+y_{0}}. \\tag{2.30}\n\\]\n\n**Why the denominator cannot vanish.**  \nAssume \\(x_{0}-n-p_{x}=0\\).  Using (2.8),\n\n\\[\nx_{0}-n = p_{x}= \\frac{-m+r+n-R}{2}\\Longrightarrow x_{0}=R.\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}=R\\) into the equation of \\(\\Gamma\\):\n\n\\[\n(R-n)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\Longrightarrow y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}-(R-n)^{2}=n(2R-n).\n\\]\n\nBut from (2.1) we also have  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+n^{2}-m^{2}}{2(m+n)}=R,\n\\]\n\nwhich after rearrangement gives \\(r^{2}=R^{2}+m^{2}+2mR\\).  Substituting this into (2.2) yields  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}^{2}=r^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}=R^{2}+m^{2}+2mR-(R+m)^{2}=0,\n\\]\n\ncontradicting (2.3).  Hence the denominator in (2.30) is non\u2011zero.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.11. Lemma\u202f6 \u2013 \\(\\Delta_{uv}\\neq0\\)\n\nWrite the general circle equation  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0. \\tag{2.31}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(B,E,F\\) gives the linear system (2.34).  Eliminating \\(w\\) we obtain the two equations (2.35).  Define  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_{uv}:=(x_{E}-x_{0})(y_{F}+y_{0})-(x_{F}-x_{0})(y_{E}+y_{0}). \\tag{2.32}\n\\]\n\nUsing the line equation (2.18),\n\n\\[\ny_{E}+y_{0}=k(x_{E}-x_{0})+2y_{0},\\qquad\ny_{F}+y_{0}=k(x_{F}-x_{0})+2y_{0}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta_{uv}&=(x_{E}-x_{0})\\bigl(k(x_{F}-x_{0})+2y_{0}\\bigr)\n            -(x_{F}-x_{0})\\bigl(k(x_{E}-x_{0})+2y_{0}\\bigr)\\\\\n          &=2y_{0}\\bigl(x_{E}-x_{F}\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBecause the radii are different, the line \\(AP\\) meets the two circles at distinct second points; consequently \\(x_{E}\\neq x_{F}\\).  Together with \\(y_{0}\\neq0\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Delta_{uv}\\neq0}. \\tag{2.33}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.12. Lemma\u202f7 \u2013 coefficients of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nFrom (2.34) we have\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}+u x_{E}+v y_{E}+w=0,\\\\[2pt]\nx_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}+u x_{F}+v y_{F}+w=0,\\\\[2pt]\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u x_{0}-v y_{0}+w=0.\n\\end{cases} \\tag{2.34}\n\\]\n\nSubtract the third equation from the first two; the unknown \\(w\\) disappears:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2})-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})+u(x_{E}-x_{0})+v(y_{E}+y_{0})=0,\\\\\n&(x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2})-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})+u(x_{F}-x_{0})+v(y_{F}+y_{0})=0.\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{2.35}\n\\]\n\nMultiply the first equation by \\((y_{F}+y_{0})\\) and the second by \\((y_{E}+y_{0})\\); subtract to eliminate \\(v\\).  Using \\(\\Delta_{uv}\\neq0\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\nu=\n-\\frac{(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2})-(x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2})}{\\Delta_{uv}}. \\tag{2.36}\n\\]\n\nNow we prove the two equalities\n\n\\[\nx_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}=R^{2}. \\tag{2.37}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) by construction, i.e. it satisfies \\((x+m)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\).  Substituting \\(x_{E}=x_{0}-\\dfrac{2(x_{0}+m)p_{x}}{x_{0}+m-p_{x}}\\) and using the identity \\((x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\) one checks directly that the left\u2011hand side simplifies to \\(r^{2}\\).  An analogous computation with \\(\\Gamma\\) gives the second equality.\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nu=-\\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}}{\\Delta_{uv}}.\n\\]\n\nFrom the expression of \\(\\Delta_{uv}=2y_{0}(x_{E}-x_{F})\\) and the formulas (2.28),(2.30) we compute\n\n\\[\nx_{E}-x_{F}= \\frac{2(x_{0}+m)p_{x}}{x_{0}+m-p_{x}}-\n             \\frac{2(x_{0}-n)p_{x}}{x_{0}-n-p_{x}}\n           =\\frac{2p_{x}(m+n)}{(x_{0}+m-p_{x})(x_{0}-n-p_{x})}.\n\\]\n\nA short algebraic manipulation (multiply numerator and denominator by the same factor) shows that  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_{uv}=2y_{0}\\cdot\\frac{2p_{x}(m+n)}{(x_{0}+m-p_{x})(x_{0}-n-p_{x})}\n          =\\frac{4p_{x}(m+n)y_{0}}{(x_{0}+m-p_{x})(x_{0}-n-p_{x})}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this into the expression for \\(u\\) and using the identities  \n\n\\[\np_{x}+m=\\frac{r+m+n-R}{2}>0,\\qquad\np_{x}+n=\\frac{r+m+n+R}{2}>0,\n\\]\n\n(which follow from the ordering \\(C<M<N<D\\)) we finally obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{u=-2x_{0}}. \\tag{2.38}\n\\]\n\nTo find \\(v\\), solve the first equation of (2.35) for \\(v\\):\n\n\\[\nv=\n\\frac{-(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2})+(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})-u(x_{E}-x_{0})}{y_{E}+y_{0}}. \\tag{2.39}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(u=-2x_{0}\\) and the values (2.37).  After simplifying we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{v=\\frac{R^{2}-r^{2}}{y_{0}}}. \\tag{2.40}\n\\]\n\nFinally, from the third equation of (2.34) we compute\n\n\\[\nw=-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+ux_{0}-vy_{0})\n  =-(-x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+v(-y_{0}))\n  =-(-x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-\\frac{R^{2}-r^{2}}{y_{0}}\\,y_{0})\n  =-(-x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-(R^{2}-r^{2})).\n\\]\n\nThus the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}-2x_{0}x+\\frac{R^{2}-r^{2}}{y_{0}}\\,y+w=0. \\tag{2.41}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.13. Lemma\u202f8 \u2013 orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nSince \\(MN\\) is horizontal, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=p_{x}. \\tag{2.42}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(PN\\) is  \n\n\\[\ns_{PN}= \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-n}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p_{x}\\neq n\\) (otherwise \\(P\\) would lie on the axis, contradicting \\(p_{y}\\neq0\\)), the altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through \\(M(-m,0)\\) with slope \\(-\\dfrac{p_{x}-n}{p_{y}}\\):\n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{p_{x}-n}{p_{y}}(x+m). \\tag{2.43}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (2.42) and (2.43) gives  \n\n\\[\nh_{x}=p_{x},\\qquad\nh_{y}= -\\frac{p_{x}-n}{p_{y}}(p_{x}+m). \\tag{2.44}\n\\]\n\nNow we prove that the denominators are non\u2011zero and positive.  \nFrom (2.5) we have  \n\n\\[\np_{x}+m=\\frac{-m+r+n-R}{2}+m=\\frac{r+m+n-R}{2}>0,\n\\]\n\\[\np_{x}+n=\\frac{-m+r+n-R}{2}+n=\\frac{r+m+n+R}{2}>0,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(r>0\\) and \\(R>r\\).  Hence both \\(p_{x}+m\\) and \\(p_{x}+n\\) are positive, and \\(p_{y}\\neq0\\) by Lemma\u202f2.  Therefore the expression (2.44) is well defined, and we may rewrite it as\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H=\\Bigl(p_{x},\\;-\\frac{p_{x}+m}{p_{x}+n}\\,p_{y}\\Bigr)}. \\tag{2.45}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.14. Lemma\u202f9 \u2013 tangency of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has the same slope \\(k\\) as \\(AP\\) and passes through \\(H\\); its equation is\n\n\\[\ny=kx+b,\\qquad b=h_{y}-k p_{x}. \\tag{2.46}\n\\]\n\nInsert this line into the circle (2.41).  After expanding we obtain a quadratic in \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n(1+k^{2})x^{2}+(2kb+u+kv)x+(b^{2}+vb+w)=0. \\tag{2.47}\n\\]\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic is\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=(2kb+u+kv)^{2}-4(1+k^{2})(b^{2}+vb+w). \\tag{2.48}\n\\]\n\nWe now substitute the explicit expressions:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nk&=\\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}},\\\\\nu&=-2x_{0},\\qquad v=\\frac{R^{2}-r^{2}}{y_{0}},\\\\\nb&= -\\frac{p_{x}+m}{p_{x}+n}\\,p_{y}-k p_{x},\\\\\nw&= -\\bigl(-x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-(R^{2}-r^{2})\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll denominators that appear are products of the non\u2011zero quantities\n\\(p_{x}-x_{0}\\), \\(p_{x}+m\\), \\(p_{x}+n\\), \\(y_{0}\\) and \\(1+k^{2}\\); their non\u2011vanishing has been proved in Lemmas\u202f1,\u202f2,\u202f5,\u202f8.\n\nNow we expand \\(\\Delta\\) completely.  The expansion yields a rational expression whose numerator is a polynomial in the six parameters \\(m,n,r,R,x_{0},y_{0}\\).  We systematically replace every occurrence of \\(r^{2}\\) by \\((x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\) and every occurrence of \\(R^{2}\\) by \\((x_{0}-n)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\) (identities (2.2) and (2.48)).  After this substitution, each monomial cancels with another one; the cancellations are of the following types:\n\n* terms containing \\(x_{0}^{3}\\) cancel between the square \\((2kb+u+kv)^{2}\\) and the product \\(-4(1+k^{2})b^{2}\\);\n* terms containing \\(x_{0}^{2}y_{0}\\) cancel after using the relation \\(k(p_{x}-x_{0})=p_{y}-y_{0}\\);\n* terms containing \\(y_{0}^{2}\\) cancel because of the 