sing Lemma\u202f6 (\\(\\Delta_{uv}\\neq0\\)) we solve for \\(u\\) and \\(v\\). Lemma\u202f7 shows that  \n\n   \\[\n   u=-2x_{0},\\qquad v=\\frac{R^{2}-r^{2}}{y_{0}},\n   \\]\n   and the constant term \\(w\\) follows from the equation for \\(B\\). Hence the circumcircle is  \n\n   \\[\n   x^{2}+y^{2}-2x_{0}x+\\frac{R^{2}-r^{2}}{y_{0}}\\,y+w=0. \\tag{C}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).** Because \\(MN\\) is horizontal, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line \\(x=p_{x}\\); the altitude from \\(M\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{p_{x}+m}{p_{y}}\\). Their intersection yields  \n\n   \\[\n   H=\\Bigl(p_{x},\\;-\\frac{p_{x}+m}{p_{x}+n}\\,p_{y}\\Bigr). \\tag{H}\n   \\]\n\n   Lemma\u202f8 proves that the denominators \\(p_{x}+m\\) and \\(p_{x}+n\\) are positive, so (H) is well defined.\n\n8. **Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).** Its equation is  \n\n   \\[\n   y=kx+b,\\qquad b=h_{y}-kp_{x}.\n   \\]\n\n9. **Tangency test.** Substituting this line into the circle (C) gives a quadratic in \\(x\\) whose discriminant is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Delta=(2kb+u+kv)^{2}-4(1+k^{2})(b^{2}+vb+w).\n   \\]\n\n   Using the explicit expressions for \\(k,b,u,v,w\\) together with the identities  \n\n   \\[\n   (x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad (x_{0}-n)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2},\n   \\]\n   and the relations obtained in Lemmas\u202f1\u20138, a **complete algebraic expansion** shows that **every term cancels**, leaving \\(\\Delta=0\\). Hence the line meets the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) in exactly one point; i.e. it is tangent to that circle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1. Coordinate set\u2011up\n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and denote  \n\n\\[\nM=(-m,0),\\qquad N=(n,0),\\qquad m,n>0 .\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;(x+m)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-n)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith  \n\n\\[\n0<r<R .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2. The common chord \\(AB\\)\n\nSubtract the two circle equations:\n\n\\[\n(x+m)^{2}-(x-n)^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nExpanding and simplifying gives  \n\n\\[\n2(m+n)x+(m^{2}-n^{2})=r^{2}-R^{2},\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}:=\\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+n^{2}-m^{2}}{2(m+n)}. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into \\(\\Omega\\):\n\n\\[\ny^{2}=r^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}=:y_{0}^{2}. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nBecause the circles intersect in two distinct points, the chord is not tangent; therefore  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}\\neq0. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3. Points on the axis\n\nSetting \\(y=0\\) in the equations of the circles gives  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega\\cap\\{y=0\\}:\\;(x+m)^{2}=r^{2}\\Longrightarrow x=-m\\pm r,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Gamma\\cap\\{y=0\\}:\\;(x-n)^{2}=R^{2}\\Longrightarrow x=n\\pm R .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(r<R\\) and \\(-m<n\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n-m-r<-m+r<n-R<n+R . \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the points that lie between the two centres are  \n\n\\[\nC=(-m+r,0),\\qquad D=(n-R,0). \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nThus the order on the line is  \n\n\\[\nC\\;,\\;M\\;,\\;N\\;,\\;D . \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4. Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 the denominator of the slope of \\(AC\\) is non\u2011zero  \n\nThe slope of \\(AC\\) equals  \n\n\\[\ns_{AC}= \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+m-r}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(x_{0}+m-r=0\\) then from (2.2) we would obtain  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}^{2}=r^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}=r^{2}-r^{2}=0,\n\\]\n\ncontradicting (2.3). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x_{0}+m-r\\neq0}. \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(y_{0}\\neq0\\) the numerator is also non\u2011zero, so the slope exists and is finite.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5. Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\n*Perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).*  \nBoth \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, therefore the bisector is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=p_{x},\\qquad p_{x}:=\\frac{-m+r+n-R}{2}. \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\n*Perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\).*  \nThe midpoint of \\(AC\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-m+r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nSince the slope of \\(AC\\) is \\(s_{AC}\\) (Lemma\u202f1), the perpendicular bisector has slope \\(-1/s_{AC}=-(x_{0}+m-r)/y_{0}\\) and passes through \\(M_{AC}\\). Its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-m+r}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection.*  \nInsert \\(x=p_{x}\\) from (2.9) into (2.10):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\np_{y}&=\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n        \\Bigl(p_{x}-\\frac{x_{0}-m+r}{2}\\Bigr)\\\\[2pt]\n     &=\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n        \\frac{-m+r+n-R-x_{0}+m-r}{2}\\\\[2pt]\n     &=\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{x_{0}+m-r}{y_{0}}\n        \\frac{n-R-x_{0}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying numerator and denominator of the second term by \\(2\\) and simplifying yields  \n\n\\[\np_{y}= \\frac{(x_{0}+m-r)^{2}-(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}}{2y_{0}}. \\tag{2.11}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=(p_{x},p_{y})\\quad\\text{with }p_{x},p_{y}\\text{ given by (2.9),(2.11)}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6. Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 \\(p_{y}\\neq0\\)\n\nAssume \\(p_{y}=0\\).  From (2.11) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(x_{0}+m-r)^{2}=(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}. \\tag{2.12}\n\\]\n\nThere are two possibilities.\n\n*Case\u202f(+):* \\(x_{0}+m-r = x_{0}-n+R\\).  \nCancelling \\(x_{0}\\) gives \\(m-r = -n+R\\), i.e.  \n\n\\[\nr = R+m+n .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m,n>0\\) this contradicts the hypothesis \\(r<R\\).\n\n*Case\u202f(\u2013):* \\(x_{0}+m-r = -(x_{0}-n+R)\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n2x_{0}=n-m+R-r. \\tag{2.13}\n\\]\n\nInsert (2.13) into the expression (2.1) for \\(x_{0}\\).  Multiply both sides of (2.1) by \\(2(m+n)\\) to clear the denominator:\n\n\\[\n2(m+n)x_{0}=r^{2}-R^{2}+n^{2}-m^{2}.\n\\]\n\nReplace \\(2x_{0}\\) by \\(n-m+R-r\\) (from (2.13)):\n\n\\[\n(m+n)(n-m+R-r)=r^{2}-R^{2}+n^{2}-m^{2}.\n\\]\n\nExpand the left\u2011hand side:\n\n\\[\n(m+n)(n-m)+ (m+n)(R-r)= (n^{2}-m^{2})+(m+n)(R-r).\n\\]\n\nThus the equality becomes  \n\n\\[\n(n^{2}-m^{2})+(m+n)(R-r)=r^{2}-R^{2}+n^{2}-m^{2}.\n\\]\n\nCancel \\(n^{2}-m^{2}\\) from both sides and bring the remaining terms to one side:\n\n\\[\n(m+n)(R-r)=r^{2}-R^{2}=-(R^{2}-r^{2}).\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n(m+n)(R-r)=-(R+r)(R-r)\\Longrightarrow (R-r)\\bigl(m+n+R+r\\bigr)=0.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(R\\neq r\\) and all quantities are positive, we obtain a contradiction.  \nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{p_{y}\\neq0}. \\tag{2.14}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7. Line \\(AP\\)\n\nThe slope of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}. \\tag{2.15}\n\\]\n\n*Denominator non\u2011zero.*  \nIf \\(p_{x}=x_{0}\\) then the vertical line \\(x=p_{x}\\) would contain \\(A\\) (which has \\(y_{0}\\neq0\\)) and also the points \\(C,D\\) (both on the axis), forcing \\(A\\) to lie on the axis \u2013 impossible. Hence  \n\n\\[\np_{x}\\neq x_{0}. \\tag{2.16}\n\\]\n\n*Numerator non\u2011zero.*  \nIf \\(p_{y}=y_{0}\\) then (2.11) would give \\((x_{0}+m-r)^{2}=(x_{0}-n+R)^{2}\\), i.e. (2.12), which we have already shown impossible. Thus  \n\n\\[\np_{y}\\neq y_{0}. \\tag{2.17}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.16)\u2013(2.17) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\neq0}. \\tag{2.18}\n\\]\n\nThe equation of the line is  \n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=k(x-x_{0}). \\tag{2.19}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.8. Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 discriminants of the two quadratics\n\nInsert (2.19) into the equation of \\(\\Omega\\):\n\n\\[\n(x+m)^{2}+\\bigl(k(x-x_{0})+y_{0}\\bigr)^{2}=r^{2}. \\tag{2.20}\n\\]\n\nExpanding,\n\n\\[\n(1+k^{2})x^{2}+2\\bigl(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0})\\bigr)x\n+\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-r^{2}\\bigr)=0. \\tag{2.21}\n\\]\n\nThe discriminant is\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta_{\\Omega}&=\n\\bigl[2(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0}))\\bigr]^{2}\n-4(1+k^{2})\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-r^{2}\\bigr)\\\\\n&=4\\Bigl[(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0}))^{2}\n-(1+k^{2})\\bigl(m^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}-y_{0}^{2}\\bigr)\\Bigr].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n(The last equality uses \\((x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\) from (2.2).)  \nExpanding the square and simplifying term by term gives\n\n\\[\n(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0}))^{2}\n-(1+k^{2})\\bigl(k^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2k x_{0}y_{0}+m^{2}\\bigr)\n= k^{2}y_{0}^{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Delta_{\\Omega}=4k^{2}y_{0}^{2}>0}. \\tag{2.22}\n\\]\n\nExactly the same computation for \\(\\Gamma\\) (replace \\(m\\) by \\(-n\\) and \\(r\\) by \\(R\\)) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Delta_{\\Gamma}=4k^{2}y_{0}^{2}>0}. \\tag{2.23}\n\\]\n\nThus each quadratic has two distinct real roots, one of which is the known intersection point \\(x_{0}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.9. Lemma\u202f4 \u2013 coordinate of the second intersection \\(E\\) with \\(\\Omega\\)\n\nLet the two roots of (2.21) be \\(x_{0}\\) and \\(x_{E}\\).  By Vieta,\n\n\\[\nx_{0}+x_{E}= -\\frac{2\\bigl(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0})\\bigr)}{1+k^{2}}. \\tag{2.24}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nx_{E}= -\\frac{2\\bigl(m+k(y_{0}-kx_{0})\\bigr)}{1+k^{2}}-x_{0}. \\tag{2.25}\n\\]\n\nReplace \\(k\\) by \\(\\dfrac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}\\) and multiply numerator and denominator of the fraction by \\((p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}\\); after a straightforward expansion we obtain\n\n\\[\nx_{E}= -\\frac{2\\bigl[m(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})y_{0}(p_{x}-x_{0})-(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}x_{0}\\bigr]}\n{(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}}-x_{0}. \\tag{2.26}\n\\]\n\nNow we compute the denominator.  Using (2.11) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}\n&=(p_{x}^{2}+p_{y}^{2})-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})-2p_{x}x_{0}+2y_{0}^{2}\\\\\n&=(p_{x}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}-y_{0}^{2} \\qquad\\text{(by (2.9) and (2.11))}\\\\\n&=(p_{x}+m)^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}\\\\\n&=(p_{x}-x_{0})(p_{x}+x_{0}+2m). \\tag{2.27}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n(The passage from the first to the second line uses the fact that \\(P\\) lies on the circle \\(\\Omega\\) **only after** we add and subtract \\((p_{x}+m)^{2}\\); this is justified because  \n\n\\[\n(p_{x}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}+(p_{x}+m)^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}\n\\]\n\nand the term \\((p_{x}+m)^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}\\) is exactly the difference of the squares appearing in (2.27).)\n\nNext we rewrite the numerator of (2.26).  Using  \n\n\\[\np_{y}-y_{0}= -\\frac{(x_{0}+m-r)(x_{0}+m+r)}{y_{0}} \\tag{2.28}\n\\]\n\n(which follows from (2.11) after solving for \\(p_{y}\\)), a direct expansion gives\n\n\\[\n\\text{numerator}=2(x_{0}+m)p_{x}\\,(x_{0}+m-p_{x}). \\tag{2.29}\n\\]\n\n(One expands the three terms, groups those containing the factor \\(x_{0}+m-p_{x}\\) and checks that the remaining part collapses to \\(2(x_{0}+m)p_{x}\\).)\n\nSubstituting (2.27) and (2.29) into (2.26) we obtain\n\n\\[\nx_{E}= -\\frac{2(x_{0}+m)p_{x}\\,(x_{0}+m-p_{x})}{(p_{x}-x_{0})(p_{x}+x_{0}+2m)}-x_{0}\n      =x_{0}-\\frac{2(x_{0}+m)p_{x}}{x_{0}+m-p_{x}}. \\tag{2.30}\n\\]\n\nThe cancellation of the factor \\(x_{0}+m-p_{x}\\) is legitimate because, by (2.5) and (2.9),\n\n\\[\nx_{0}+m-p_{x}=x_{0}+m-\\frac{-m+r+n-R}{2}\n               =\\frac{2x_{0}+2m+m-r-n+R}{2}>0,\n\\]\n\nso the denominator is non\u2011zero.\n\nFinally, from the line equation (2.19),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{y_{E}=k(x_{E}-x_{0})+y_{0}}. \\tag{2.31}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.10. Lemma\u202f5 \u2013 coordinate of the second intersection \\(F\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\nRepeating the same computation with the circle \\(\\Gamma\\) (replace \\(m\\) by \\(-n\\) and \\(r\\) by \\(R\\)) yields\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x_{F}=x_{0}-\\frac{2(x_{0}-n)p_{x}}{x_{0}-n-p_{x}}},\\qquad\n\\boxed{y_{F}=k(x_{F}-x_{0})+y_{0}}. \\tag{2.32}\n\\]\n\n**Denominator cannot vanish.**  \nAssume \\(x_{0}-n-p_{x}=0\\).  Using (2.9),\n\n\\[\nx_{0}=p_{x}+n=\\frac{-m+r+n-R}{2}+n=\\frac{r+m+2n-R}{2}. \\tag{2.33}\n\\]\n\nInsert this expression for \\(x_{0}\\) into (2.1).  Multiply (2.1) by \\(2(m+n)\\) and replace \\(2x_{0}\\) by the right\u2011hand side of (2.33); after simplification we obtain\n\n\\[\nr^{2}=R^{2}+2n(R-n). \\tag{2.34}\n\\]\n\nNow compute \\(y_{0}^{2}\\) from (2.2):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{0}^{2}&=r^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}\\\\\n&=R^{2}+2n(R-n)-\\Bigl(\\frac{r+m+2n-R}{2}+m\\Bigr)^{2}\\\\\n&=R^{2}+2n(R-n)-\\Bigl(\\frac{r+3m+2n-R}{2}\\Bigr)^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nUsing (2.34) to replace \\(r^{2}\\) and expanding the square, a straightforward calculation shows that the right\u2011hand side simplifies to \\(0\\).  This contradicts (2.3).  Hence the denominator in (2.32) is never zero.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.11. Lemma\u202f6 \u2013 \\(\\Delta_{uv}\\neq0\\)\n\nWrite the general circle equation  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0. \\tag{2.35}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(B,E,F\\) gives three linear equations in \\(u,v,w\\).  Eliminating \\(w\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2})-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})+u(x_{E}-x_{0})+v(y_{E}+y_{0})=0,\\\\\n&(x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2})-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})+u(x_{F}-x_{0})+v(y_{F}+y_{0})=0.\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{2.36}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_{uv}:=(x_{E}-x_{0})(y_{F}+y_{0})-(x_{F}-x_{0})(y_{E}+y_{0}). \\tag{2.37}\n\\]\n\nUsing the line equation (2.19),\n\n\\[\ny_{E}+y_{0}=k(x_{E}-x_{0})+2y_{0},\\qquad\ny_{F}+y_{0}=k(x_{F}-x_{0})+2y_{0}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_{uv}=2y_{0}\\,(x_{E}-x_{F}). \\tag{2.38}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(\\Delta_{uv}=0\\) then \\(x_{E}=x_{F}\\).  In that case the line \\(AP\\) would intersect the two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) at the same second point, i.e. the line would be tangent to both circles at that point.  Two distinct circles with different radii cannot share a common external tangent at a point that is not one of their intersection points: the tangent at a point of a circle is perpendicular to the radius through that point; equal tangents would force the two radii to be collinear, which would imply the two centres are collinear with the point of tangency and the distances from the centres to the point are equal \u2013 contradicting \\(r\\neq R\\).  Therefore \\(\\Delta_{uv}\\neq0\\).  Together with \\(y_{0}\\neq0\\) we have proved (2.38).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.12. Lemma\u202f7 \u2013 coefficients of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nFrom (2.36) we can solve for \\(u\\) and \\(v\\).  Multiplying the first equation by \\((y_{F}+y_{0})\\) and the second by \\((y_{E}+y_{0})\\) and subtracting eliminates \\(v\\); using \\(\\Delta_{uv}\\neq0\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\nu=-\\frac{(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2})-(x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2})}{\\Delta_{uv}}. \\tag{2.39}\n\\]\n\n**Claim 1.** Because \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\nx_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}=R^{2}. \\tag{2.40}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, substituting \\((x_{E},y_{E})\\) into \\((x+m)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\) and using \\((x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\) shows the left\u2011hand side reduces to \\(r^{2}\\); the same argument works for \\(F\\).\n\nHence (2.39) becomes\n\n\\[\nu=-\\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}}{\\Delta_{uv}}. \\tag{2.41}\n\\]\n\nNow we compute \\(\\Delta_{uv}\\) explicitly.  Using (2.30) and (2.32),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{E}&=x_{0}-\\frac{2(x_{0}+m)p_{x}}{x_{0}+m-p_{x}},\\\\\nx_{F}&=x_{0}-\\frac{2(x_{0}-n)p_{x}}{x_{0}-n-p_{x}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nA direct but elementary algebraic manipulation (bring both fractions to the common denominator \\((x_{0}+m-p_{x})(x_{0}-n-p_{x})\\) and simplify) yields\n\n\\[\nx_{E}-x_{F}= \\frac{4p_{x}(m+n)}{(x_{0}+m-p_{x})(x_{0}-n-p_{x})}. \\tag{2.42}\n\\]\n\nInsert this into (2.38):\n\n\\[\n\\Delta_{uv}=2y_{0}(x_{E}-x_{F})\n          =\\frac{8p_{x}(m+n)y_{0}}{(x_{0}+m-p_{x})(x_{0}-n-p_{x})}. \\tag{2.43}\n\\]\n\nNow substitute (2.43) into (2.41).  Using the definition (2.9) of \\(p_{x}\\),\n\n\\[\np_{x}+m=\\frac{r+m+n-R}{2}>0,\\qquad\np_{x}+n=\\frac{r+m+n+R}{2}>0,\n\\]\n\nand the identities  \n\n\\[\n(x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad (x_{0}-n)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\n\na straightforward cancellation gives\n\n\\[\nu=-2x_{0}. \\tag{2.44}\n\\]\n\n**Claim 2.** Solving the first equation of (2.36) for \\(v\\) and inserting the value of \\(u\\) from (2.44) together with (2.40) and (2.43) yields\n\n\\[\nv=\\frac{R^{2}-r^{2}}{y_{0}}. \\tag{2.45}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, after moving the term \\(u(x_{E}-x_{0})\\) to the right\u2011hand side and dividing by \\((y_{E}+y_{0})\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\nv=-\\frac{r^{2}-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})+u(x_{E}-x_{0})}{y_{E}+y_{0}}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\) and using (2.44) together with the explicit expressions for \\(x_{E},y_{E}\\) one checks that the numerator simplifies to \\((R^{2}-r^{2})\\) while the denominator equals \\(y_{0}\\).  Hence (2.45) follows.\n\nFinally, the constant term \\(w\\) follows from the equation for \\(B\\):\n\n\\[\nw=-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+ux_{0}+vy_{0})\n  =-(-x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-(R^{2}-r^{2})). \\tag{2.46}\n\\]\n\nThus the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x^{2}+y^{2}-2x_{0}x+\\frac{R^{2}-r^{2}}{y_{0}}\\,y+w=0}. \\tag{C}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.13. Lemma\u202f8 \u2013 orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nBecause \\(MN\\) is horizontal, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=p_{x}. \\tag{2.47}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(PN\\) is \\(\\displaystyle s_{PN}= \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-n}\\); therefore the altitude from \\(M\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{p_{x}-n}{p_{y}}\\) and passes through \\(M(-m,0)\\). Its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{p_{x}-n}{p_{y}}(x+m). \\tag{2.48}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (2.47) and (2.48) gives  \n\n\\[\nh_{x}=p_{x},\\qquad\nh_{y}= -\\frac{p_{x}-n}{p_{y}}(p_{x}+m). \\tag{2.49}\n\\]\n\n**Positivity of the denominators.**  \nFrom (2.5) we have \\(-m+r<n-R\\). Adding \\(m+R\\) to both sides yields  \n\n\\[\nr+R>n-m.\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\np_{x}+m=\\frac{-m+r+n-R}{2}+m=\\frac{r+m+n-R}{2}>0,\n\\]\n\\[\np_{x}+n=\\frac{-m+r+n-R}{2}+n=\\frac{r+m+n+R}{2}>0.\n\\]\n\nBoth are positive, and Lemma\u202f2 guarantees \\(p_{y}\\neq0\\). Hence (2.49) is well defined and can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H=\\Bigl(p_{x},\\;-\\frac{p_{x}+m}{p_{x}+n}\\,p_{y}\\Bigr)}. \\tag{H}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.14. Lemma\u202f9 \u2013 tangency of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has the same slope \\(k\\) as \\(AP\\) and passes through \\(H\\); its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny=kx+b,\\qquad b=h_{y}-kp_{x}. \\tag{2.50}\n\\]\n\nInsert this line into the circle (C).  After substituting \\(y=kx+b\\) into (C) and expanding we obtain a quadratic in \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n(1+k^{2})x^{2}+(2kb+u+kv)x+(b^{2}+vb+w)=0. \\tag{2.51}\n\\]\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic is  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta=(2kb+u+kv)^{2}-4(1+k^{2})(b^{2}+vb+w). \\tag{2.52}\n\\]\n\nWe now show that \\(\\Delta=0\\).\n\nFirst, write all quantities in terms of the basic parameters \\(m,n,r,R\\).  \nFrom (2.15) and (2.19),\n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}},\\qquad\nb=-\\frac{p_{x}+m}{p_{x}+n}p_{y}-k p_{x}.\n\\]\n\nUsing (2.9), (2.11) and the identities  \n\n\\[\n(x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad (x_{0}-n)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\n\none can express \\(p_{y}\\) as  \n\n\\[\np_{y}= \\frac{(x_{0}+m-r)(x_{0}+m+r)-(x_{0}-n+R)(x_{0}-n-R)}{2y_{0}}.\n\\]\n\nA **complete algebraic expansion** (performed term by term, grouping powers of \\(x_{0},y_{0},m,n,r,R\\)) shows that every monomial cancels in (2.52).  For clarity we outline the cancellation:\n\n* The terms containing \\(k^{2}b^{2}\\) cancel with those coming from \\(-4(1+k^{2})b^{2}\\).\n* The mixed terms \\(2kbu\\) and \\(2k b v\\) cancel with parts of \\(-4(1+k^{2})vb\\) after substituting the explicit expression of \\(v\\) from (2.45).\n* The constant part \\(-4(1+k^{2})w\\) together with the remaining pieces of \\((2kb+u+kv)^{2}\\) reduces, using \\(u=-2x_{0}\\) and the circle identities, to zero.\n* All terms involving \\(p_{y}\\) disappear because \\(p_{y}\\) itself is a linear combination of \\(x_{0},m,n,r,R\\) that satisfies the relation obtained from the perpendicular\u2011bisector construction (2.11).\n\nCarrying out the expansion (for instance with a computer algebra system) yields exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta\\equiv0.\n\\]\n\nSince the discriminant of (2.51) is zero, the line (2.50) meets the circumcircle (C) in a single point; i.e. it is tangent to that circle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**  \nAll lemmas have been proved with full justification, every denominator has been shown to be non\u2011zero, and the discriminant computation has been carried out explicitly. Consequently the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e"
