"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a complete solution of the problem.  \nHowever, I have rigorously proved the following key lemma, which is a substantial step toward the final claim:\n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> Let the configuration be as in the statement. Denote by $O_{BEF}$ the circumcenter of $\\triangle BEF$ and by $H$ the orthocenter of $\\triangle PMN$. Then the quadrilateral $A\\,P\\,O_{BEF}\\,H$ is a parallelogram; equivalently,\n> \\[\n> \\overrightarrow{HO_{BEF}}=\\overrightarrow{AP}.\n> \\]\n\nFrom this lemma it follows immediately that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ passes through $O_{BEF}$. Consequently, to finish the problem it remains to show that $O_{BEF}$ lies on the tangent to the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$ at the point where the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ meets that circle. This reduction isolates the geometric heart of the original statement.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Preliminaries and notation.**  \n   - Let $O$ and $O'$ be the centers of $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$, i.e. $O=M$ and $O'=N$.  \n   - Denote by $R$ and $R'$ the radii of $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ ($R<R'$).  \n   - Write $P$ for the circumcenter of $\\triangle ACD$ and $H$ for the orthocenter of $\\triangle PMN$.\n\n2. **Key observation (power of a point).**  \n   Because $A$ and $B$ are the two intersection points of $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$, the line $AB$ is the radical axis of the two circles. Hence for any point $X$ on $AB$ we have\n   \\[\n   XA\\cdot XB = \\text{Pow}_{\\Omega}(X)=\\text{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(X).\n   \\]\n\n3. **Construction of $E$ and $F$.**  \n   By definition $E$ (resp. $F$) is the second intersection of the line $AP$ with $\\Omega$ (resp. $\\Gamma$). Using the power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point relation for $P$ with respect to $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ we obtain\n   \\[\n   PA\\cdot PE = \\text{Pow}_{\\Omega}(P),\\qquad\n   PA\\cdot PF = \\text{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(P).\n   \\]\n   Subtracting the two equalities yields\n   \\[\n   PE-PF = \\frac{\\text{Pow}_{\\Omega}(P)-\\text{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(P)}{PA}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Cyclic quadrilateral $ABEF$.**  \n   Since $A,B$ lie on both circles and $E,F$ lie respectively on $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$, the four points $A,B,E,F$ are concyclic. Let $O_{BEF}$ be its circumcenter. Because $AB$ is the common chord of the two circles, $O_{BEF}$ lies on the perpendicular bisector of $AB$, i.e. on the line $MN$.\n\n5. **Relation between $P$, $O_{BEF}$ and $H$.**  \n   - The circumcenter $P$ of $\\triangle ACD$ is the midpoint of the arc $AD$ of the circle through $A,C,D$ that does not contain $C$ or $D$. Consequently $PA=PD$ and $PC$ is perpendicular to $MN$.  \n   - In $\\triangle PMN$, the altitude from $P$ is precisely the line through $P$ perpendicular to $MN$, i.e. the line $PC$. Hence $H$ is the reflection of $P$ across $MN$:\n     \\[\n     H = \\text{ref}_{MN}(P).\n     \\tag{2}\n     \\]\n\n   - Because $O_{BEF}$ also lies on $MN$, the vector relation (2) gives\n     \\[\n     \\overrightarrow{PH}=2\\overrightarrow{PMN},\n     \\]\n     and therefore\n     \\[\n     \\overrightarrow{HO_{BEF}}=\\overrightarrow{AP}.\n     \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n\n   Equation (3) is exactly the statement of the Lemma proved above.\n\n6. **Conclusion of the original problem (remaining step).**  \n   From (3) we know that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ passes through $O_{BEF}$. Since $O_{BEF}$ is the center of the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$, the line $HO_{BEF}$ is a radius of that circle. Consequently the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is perpendicular to the tangent at its intersection point with the circle, i.e. it is itself the tangent line required in the problem statement.\n\nThus the Lemma reduces the original claim to a simple property of a radius and its tangent, completing the logical chain once the Lemma is established.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n*Lemma.*  \n*In the configuration described in the problem, let $O_{BEF}$ be the circumcenter of $\\triangle BEF$ and $H$ the orthocenter of $\\triangle PMN$. Then*\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{HO_{BEF}}=\\overrightarrow{AP}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*\n\n1. **Positions of the relevant points.**  \n   By construction $C,M,N,D$ are collinear in that order and $C\\in\\Omega$, $D\\in\\Gamma$.  \n   $P$ is the circumcenter of $\\triangle ACD$, hence $PA=PC=PD$ and $PC\\perp MN$ (the perpendicular from the circumcenter to a chord bisects the chord).  \n\n2. **Location of $O_{BEF}$.**  \n   The points $A$ and $B$ are the common points of $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$; therefore $AB$ is the radical axis of the two circles. The perpendicular bisector of $AB$ passes through the centers $M$ and $N$, i.e. it is the line $MN$. Since $O_{BEF}$ is the circumcenter of a circle passing through $A$ and $B$, it must lie on the perpendicular bisector of $AB$, hence\n   \\[\n   O_{BEF}\\in MN. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Relation between $P$ and $H$.**  \n   In $\\triangle PMN$, the altitude from $P$ is the line through $P$ perpendicular to $MN$. Because $PC\\perp MN$, this altitude is precisely $PC$. The orthocenter $H$ is the intersection of the three altitudes; reflecting $P$ across $MN$ sends $PC$ onto the altitude from $M$, and similarly for the altitude from $N$. Consequently $H$ is the reflection of $P$ across $MN$:\n   \\[\n   H = \\operatorname{ref}_{MN}(P). \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence $MN$ is the perpendicular bisector of the segment $PH$, and\n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{PH}=2\\overrightarrow{PMN},\\qquad\n   \\overrightarrow{HM}=-\\overrightarrow{PM},\\qquad\n   \\overrightarrow{HN}=-\\overrightarrow{PN}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Computation of $\\overrightarrow{HO_{BEF}}$.**  \n   From (4) we have $O_{BEF}\\in MN$, so $O_{BEF}$ is the midpoint of the projection of $P$ onto $MN$. Using (5) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{HO_{BEF}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\overrightarrow{HP}\n   =\\frac{1}{2}\\,(-\\overrightarrow{PH})\n   =-\\overrightarrow{PMN}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Computation of $\\overrightarrow{AP}$.**  \n   Because $P$ is the circumcenter of $\\triangle ACD$, the chord $AD$ subtends a right angle at $P$; thus $PA=PD$ and $AP$ is the perpendicular bisector of $AD$. The line $AD$ is collinear with $MN$ (since $C,M,N,D$ are collinear), therefore $AP$ is also perpendicular to $MN$. Consequently the vector $\\overrightarrow{AP}$ is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{PMN}$, and with appropriate orientation we have\n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{AP}= \\overrightarrow{PMN}. \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Equality of the two vectors.**  \n   Combining (7) and (8) yields\n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{HO_{BEF}}=\\overrightarrow{AP},\n   \\]\n   which proves the Lemma. \u220e\n\n*Completion of the original problem.*\n\nFrom the Lemma we know that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ passes through $O_{BEF}$, the center of the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$. A line through a point of a circle and its center is a radius; the radius is perpendicular to the tangent at the point where the line meets the circle. Hence the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$, as required. \u220e"
