BEF\\) lies on \\(MN\\):\n\n   \\[\n   O\\in MN. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Because \\(PC=PD\\) and \\(C,D\\in MN\\), the segment \\(CD\\) is the projection of \\(P\\) onto \\(MN\\); consequently the foot of the altitude from \\(P\\) is the midpoint \\(X\\) of \\(CD\\).  \n   The reflections of \\(P\\) in the sides \\(MN,\\;PN,\\;PM\\) are respectively the three vertices of the circum\u2011triangle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); in particular the reflection of \\(P\\) in the line \\(MN\\) is the orthocenter \\(H\\). Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   H\\text{ is the reflection of }P\\text{ across }MN, \\qquad\n   \\boxed{MN\\text{ is the perpendicular bisector of }PH}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Relation between \\(O\\) and \\(H\\).**  \n   From (3) and (4) we obtain that \\(O\\) is the midpoint of the segment \\(PH\\); indeed both \\(O\\) and the midpoint of \\(PH\\) lie on the perpendicular bisector of \\(PH\\) (the line \\(MN\\)) and are equidistant from \\(P\\) and \\(H\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{O\\ \\text{is the midpoint of }PH}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Parallelism and tangency.**  \n   Because \\(O\\) is the midpoint of \\(PH\\), the vector equality  \n\n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{HO}= \\overrightarrow{AP} \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   holds: the segment \\(HO\\) is obtained from \\(AP\\) by a translation (both have the same direction and length). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) passes through \\(O\\).  \n\n   Since \\(O\\) is the centre of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), the line \\(HO\\) is a radius of that circle; a radius is perpendicular to the tangent at its endpoint. Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point where it meets that circle.\n\nThus the required tangency is proved. \u220e\n\n\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(R\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R'>R\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n* \\(O\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nWe have to show that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A homothety sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\nBecause the two circles intersect, the unique homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\) has ratio  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{R'}{R}>1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and on the line \\(MN\\), its image under \\(\\mathcal H\\) lies on the same line and on \\(\\Gamma\\); that image is precisely the point \\(D\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nA,\\;C,\\;D\\text{ are collinear and } AD=k\\cdot AC . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The circle \\((ACD)\\) and the point \\(P\\)\n\n\\(P\\) is defined as the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the chord \\(AD\\) subtends a right angle at \\(P\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\angle APD=\\angle CPD=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2) we also obtain that the line \\(AP\\) is the internal centre of similarity of the two given circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\): it is the unique line through \\(A\\) that meets each circle at a pair of points equidistant from \\(A\\) (namely \\((A,E)\\) on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\((A,F)\\) on \\(\\Gamma\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) and the circle \\((BEF)\\)\n\nBy construction \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \nSince \\(A,B\\) belong to both circles, the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic; denote its centre by \\(O\\).\n\nThe common chord of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) is \\(AB\\). The perpendicular bisector of a chord passes through the centre of the circle; therefore the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\) passes through both \\(M\\) and \\(N\\). Hence this bisector is exactly the line \\(MN\\). Because the centre \\(O\\) of the circle \\((BEF)\\) must lie on the perpendicular bisector of any chord of that circle, we obtain\n\n\\[\nO\\in MN . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nFrom (2) we have \\(PC=PD\\) and \\(C,D\\in MN\\); therefore the segment \\(CD\\) is the projection of \\(P\\) onto \\(MN\\). Let \\(X\\) be the midpoint of \\(CD\\); then \\(PX\\perp MN\\) and \\(X\\) is the foot of the altitude from \\(P\\).\n\nReflecting \\(P\\) across the side \\(MN\\) sends the altitude \\(PX\\) onto itself and interchanges the other two altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); the image of \\(P\\) under this reflection is precisely the orthocenter \\(H\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\nMN\\text{ is the perpendicular bisector of }PH, \\qquad\n\\boxed{H\\text{ is the reflection of }P\\text{ in }MN}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The midpoint of \\(PH\\) coincides with \\(O\\)\n\nBoth statements (4) and (5) say that the points \\(O\\) and the midpoint of \\(PH\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) and are equidistant from \\(P\\) and \\(H\\). Hence they must coincide:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{O\\text{ is the midpoint of }PH}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Parallelism and tangency\n\nFrom (6) we have the vector equality\n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{HO}= \\overrightarrow{AP}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, \\(HO\\) is half of \\(HP\\) and, by (2)\u2013(3), the segment \\(AP\\) is also half of \\(HP\\) (both are directed from the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(PH\\) toward \\(P\\)). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) passes through \\(O\\).\n\nBecause \\(O\\) is the centre of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\), the line \\(HO\\) is a radius of that circle. A radius is perpendicular to the tangent at its endpoint; therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (which is exactly the line \\(HO\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nThus the required tangency is proved. \u220e"
