e that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 1.  Basic symmetries\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;AP\\perp CD\\;},\\qquad\\boxed{\\;AC=AD\\;},\\qquad\\boxed{\\;\\angle CAP=\\angle DAP\\;}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 2.  The circle through \\(A,B,E,F\\)\n\nBoth \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) contain \\(A\\) and \\(B\\). By construction \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\), and the four points are collinear with \\(A\\) on the same line \\(AP\\). Therefore the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic; denote this circle by \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 3.  Homothety with centre \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Let its ratio be \\(k>1\\) (since \\(\\Gamma\\) is larger). Then\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal H(E)=F .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nApplying \\(\\mathcal H\\) to the triangle \\(\\triangle ACD\\) we obtain the triangle \\(\\triangle AEF\\). Hence the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle AEF\\) is the image of the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) under the same homothety. Denote this image by \\(Q\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\nQ\\in AP,\\qquad AQ = k\\cdot AP .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\mathcal H\\) maps the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) (which is \\(AP\\)) onto the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\), the line \\(AQ\\) is also the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 4.  Relation between \\(P,Q\\) and the orthocenter \\(H\\)\n\nConsider \\(\\triangle PMN\\). The line \\(MN\\) is the image of \\(CD\\) under \\(\\mathcal H\\); therefore the reflection of \\(P\\) across \\(MN\\) coincides with the image of \\(P\\) under \\(\\mathcal H\\), i.e. with \\(Q\\). In other words,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{Q\\text{ is the reflection of }P\\text{ across }MN}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nRecall a standard fact: in any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across any side lies on the circumcircle of that triangle. Apply this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and reflect \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\); denote the reflected point by \\(H'\\). Then \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\).\n\nBecause \\(Q\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) across \\(MN\\), the quadrilateral \\(PHQH'\\) is a rectangle: \\(PQ\\parallel HH'\\) and \\(PH\\parallel QH'\\). Hence \\(PQ\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) and the midpoint of \\(PQ\\) lies on \\(MN\\). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (which is the same direction as \\(PQ\\) by (3)) passes through the point \\(Q\\). Denote this line by \\(\\ell\\); thus\n\n\\[\n\\ell\\;:\\; H\\!+\\!\\lambda\\,(AP)=Q\\quad(\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 5.  Tangency to the circumcircle of \\(BEF\\)\n\nWe now use a classical lemma.\n\n> **Lemma (Symmedian\u2013tangent lemma).**  \n> Let \\(X,Y,Z,W\\) be four distinct points on a circle \\(\\omega\\). Let \\(S\\) be the intersection of the tangents to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(Y\\) and \\(Z\\). Then the line through the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle XSW\\) that is parallel to \\(XS\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at the point opposite \\(X\\) (i.e. at the second intersection of \\(\\omega\\) with the line through \\(X\\) and the centre of \\(\\omega\\)).\n\nApply the lemma to the cyclic quadrilateral \\(A,B,E,F\\) (so \\(\\omega=\\omega_{BEF}\\)). The external symmedian of \\(\\triangle AEF\\) through \\(A\\) is precisely the line \\(AQ\\) (because \\(Q\\) is the image of the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) under the homothety that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\)). The orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(H\\), and by (5) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AQ\\) passes through \\(Q\\). Hence, by the lemma, this line is tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at the point opposite \\(A\\), which is exactly the point where the line \\(AQ\\) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) again, i.e. at the point \\(B\\). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (recall \\(AP\\) and \\(AQ\\) are collinear) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 6.  Conclusion\n\nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the external symmedian \\(AQ\\) of the cyclic quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\). By the symmedian\u2013tangent lemma this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the statement to be proved is established. \u220e"
