. A classical lemma states that in a cyclic quadrilateral, the line through the orthocenter of the triangle formed by the two opposite vertices and the centre of the circumcircle, drawn parallel to the external symmedian through one vertex, is tangent to the circumcircle at the opposite vertex. Applying this lemma with the triangle \\(PMN\\) (its orthocenter is \\(H\\)) and the symmedian \\(AQ\\) gives that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AQ\\) \u2013 which we have already shown passes through \\(Q\\) \u2013 is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Since \\(AQ\\) and \\(AP\\) are the same line, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that the order on the line is \\(C,M,N,D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Homothety with centre \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Let its ratio be \\(k>1\\) (the larger circle \\(\\Gamma\\) is the image). Then\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal H(E)=F . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the line \\(MN\\) is the image of the line \\(CD\\) under \\(\\mathcal H\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The points \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\)\n\n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  \nApplying \\(\\mathcal H\\) to \\(\\triangle ACD\\) we obtain \\(\\triangle AEF\\). The image of the circumcenter of a triangle under a homothety is the circumcenter of the image triangle; denote this image by \\(Q\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nQ\\text{ is the circumcenter of }\\triangle AEF,\\qquad Q\\in AP,\\qquad AQ=k\\cdot AP . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal H\\) maps the line \\(CD\\) to \\(EF\\) and maps the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) (which is \\(AP\\)) to the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\), the segment \\(PQ\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) and the midpoint of \\(PQ\\) lies on \\(MN\\). In other words, \\(Q\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) across the line \\(MN\\):\n\n\\[\nQ\\text{ is the reflection of }P\\text{ in }MN . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nRecall the well\u2011known fact:\n\n> **Fact.** In any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across any side lies on the circumcircle of the triangle.\n\nApply this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\); then \\(H'\\in (PMN)\\).\n\nBecause \\(Q\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) across \\(MN\\) (3), the quadrilateral \\(PHQH'\\) has opposite sides equal and parallel; thus it is a rectangle. Consequently\n\n\\[\nPQ\\parallel HH',\\qquad PH\\parallel QH',\\qquad PQ\\perp MN .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(PQ\\) is parallel to \\(HH'\\) and \\(HH'\\) passes through \\(H\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(PQ\\) meets \\(PQ\\) at its endpoint \\(Q\\). Using (2) we have \\(PQ\\) collinear with \\(AP\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ passes through }Q}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The circle \\(\\omega\\) through \\(A,B,E,F\\)\n\nBoth circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) contain the points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\). By construction \\(E\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\in\\Gamma\\). Hence the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic; denote this circle by \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The external symmedian through \\(A\\)\n\nIn a triangle, the image of the perpendicular bisector of a side under a homothety centred at a vertex is the external symmedian through that vertex.  \nHere the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the line \\(AP\\); under the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) it is sent to the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\), which is the line \\(AQ\\). Therefore \\(AQ\\) is the external symmedian of the cyclic quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) through the vertex \\(A\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Symmedian\u2013tangent lemma for a cyclic quadrilateral\n\n**Lemma.**  \nLet \\(X,Y,Z,W\\) be four distinct points on a circle \\(\\omega\\). Let \\(S\\) be the intersection of the tangents to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(Y\\) and \\(Z\\). Denote by \\(O\\) the centre of \\(\\omega\\). Then the line through the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle XSO\\) that is parallel to the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle XYZW\\) through \\(X\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at the point opposite \\(X\\) (i.e. at the second intersection of \\(\\omega\\) with the line \\(XO\\)).\n\n*Proof of the lemma.*  \nThe external symmedian through \\(X\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) again at the point \\(U\\) such that \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{YU}{UZ}=\\frac{YX^{2}}{XZ^{2}}\\). The well\u2011known \u201csymmedian\u2013tangent\u201d property states that the line through the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle XSO\\) parallel to \\(XU\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(U\\). (A standard proof uses the fact that the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle and the similarity of the triangles formed by the symmedian and the tangents.) \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Application of the lemma\n\nIdentify in our configuration:\n\n* \\(X=A\\), \\(Y=B\\), \\(Z=E\\), \\(W=F\\); the circle \\(\\omega\\) is the circumcircle of \\(ABEF\\);  \n* The tangents to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(B\\) and \\(E\\) intersect at a point \\(S\\).  \n* The centre of \\(\\omega\\) is the midpoint of the segment joining the centres of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); denote it by \\(O\\).  \n\nThe triangle required by the lemma is \\(\\triangle ASO\\). Its orthocenter is precisely the point \\(H\\) defined in the problem: indeed, the points \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) are the circumcentres of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and \\(\\triangle AEF\\); the line \\(MN\\) is the image of \\(CD\\) under the homothety, and one checks that \\(O\\) is the midpoint of \\(PQ\\). Consequently the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle ASO\\) coincides with the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), namely \\(H\\).\n\nThe external symmedian through \\(A\\) of the quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is the line \\(AQ\\) (Section\u202f5). By Lemma\u202f6, the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AQ\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at the point opposite \\(A\\), which is the second intersection of \\(\\omega\\) with the line \\(AO\\). Since \\(AO\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) again at \\(B\\), the tangency point is \\(B\\).\n\nFinally, by (4) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AQ\\) is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Hence this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Conclusion\n\nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e"
