"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution of the problem.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by contradiction.  \nAssume that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is **not** tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nFrom this assumption we deduce that the second intersection of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) with \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) is a point \\(T\\neq B\\).  \nUsing the following lemmas we obtain a chain of equalities that forces \\(T\\) to coincide with \\(B\\), contradicting the assumption.\n\n* **Lemma 1.**  \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(EF\\) of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) not containing \\(B\\).  \n* **Lemma 2.**  The reflection of \\(H\\) across the line \\(MN\\) lies on the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).  \n* **Lemma 3.**  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) again at the reflection of \\(B\\) across \\(MN\\).  \n\nFrom Lemma\u202f3 the second intersection of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) with \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) must be the reflection of \\(B\\) across \\(MN\\).  \nBut Lemma\u202f2 shows that this reflection coincides with \\(B\\) itself, because \\(B\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\).  \nHence the second intersection is \\(B\\), i.e. the line is tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at \\(B\\), contradicting the initial assumption.  \nTherefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThe detailed proof below establishes the three lemmas and carries out the contradiction argument rigorously.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(\\;r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be their two intersection points;  \n* \\(C\\in \\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in \\Gamma\\) be the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that \\(C,M,N,D\\) appear in this order;  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n* \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nDenote by \\(L\\) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nWe shall prove that \\(L\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1.  \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the minor arc \\(EF\\) of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) that does not contain \\(B\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the quadrilateral \\(APCD\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\).  \nConsequently \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  \nBecause \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)) and on line \\(AP\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nPE = PA = PC,\\qquad PF = PA = PD .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(PE=PF\\); hence \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of segment \\(EF\\).  \nMoreover, \\(P\\) is on line \\(AP\\), which is the external angle bisector of \\(\\angle EPF\\) (the two equal chords \\(PE\\) and \\(PF\\) subtend equal angles at the centre).  \nTherefore \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle through \\(E,F\\) that also passes through \\(B\\); i.e. \\(P\\) is the centre of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).  \nConsequently the arc \\(EF\\) not containing \\(B\\) is symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\), and its midpoint is exactly the point where the symmetry axis meets the circle, namely \\(P\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  Let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across the line \\(MN\\). Then \\(H'\\) lies on \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIn any triangle, the reflection of the orthocentre across any side lies on the circumcircle of that triangle.  \nApply this fact to \\(\\triangle PMN\\): the reflection of \\(H\\) across side \\(MN\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nDenote this circumcircle by \\(\\Omega_{PMN}\\).  \n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the centre of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) (Lemma\u202f1), the circles \\(\\Omega_{PMN}\\) and \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) share the same centre \\(P\\) and the same radius: indeed  \n\n\\[\nPM = PN \\quad\\text{(radii of }\\Omega_{PMN}\\text{)} ,\n\\]\nand by Lemma\u202f1  \n\n\\[\nPE = PF = PB = \\text{radius of }\\omega_{BEF}.\n\\]\n\nHence \\(\\Omega_{PMN}=\\omega_{BEF}\\).  \nTherefore the reflected point \\(H'\\) belongs to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3.  The line \\(L\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) again at the reflection of \\(B\\) across \\(MN\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(L\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) passes through the centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\), the line \\(L\\) is a **diameter direction** of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\): it is parallel to a radius, hence it is a chord whose midpoint is the projection of \\(P\\) onto \\(L\\).  \n\nLet \\(B'\\) be the reflection of \\(B\\) across \\(MN\\). Because \\(MN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of segment \\(BB'\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nPB = PB' \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\angle (PB,\\,MN)=\\angle (PB',\\,MN).\n\\]\n\nThus \\(B'\\) also lies on \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).  \n\nNow consider the homothety with centre \\(H\\) that sends the line \\(AP\\) to the line \\(L\\) (they are parallel).  \nSince \\(A\\) belongs to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) (as \\(A\\) is the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), and \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A,B\\)), the image of \\(A\\) under this homothety is precisely the second intersection of \\(L\\) with \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).  \nBut the homothety sends the centre \\(P\\) to a point \\(P'\\) on \\(L\\) such that \\(HP'=HP\\); because \\(P\\) is the centre of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\), the image of the whole circle is again \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).  \nConsequently the second intersection of \\(L\\) with \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) is the image of \\(B\\) under the same homothety, i.e. the point symmetric to \\(B\\) with respect to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n\nSince \\(H\\) lies on the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is itself symmetric to \\(MN\\) with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(PB\\).  \nHence the image of \\(B\\) is exactly its reflection across \\(MN\\), namely \\(B'\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof (contradiction)\n\nAssume, for the sake of contradiction, that the line \\(L\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is **not** tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at \\(B\\).  \nThen \\(L\\) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at two distinct points: \\(B\\) and another point \\(T\\neq B\\).\n\nBy Lemma\u202f3 the second intersection must be the reflection \\(B'\\) of \\(B\\) across \\(MN\\); hence \\(T=B'\\).\n\nOn the other hand, Lemma\u202f2 tells us that the reflection \\(H'\\) of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) lies on \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).  \nSince \\(H'\\) is symmetric to \\(H\\) with respect to \\(MN\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also the line through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nConsequently \\(H'\\) also belongs to the line \\(L\\).  \n\nBecause \\(L\\) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at \\(B\\) and at \\(B'\\), the only points of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) lying on \\(L\\) are \\(B\\) and \\(B'\\).  \nThus \\(H'\\) must coincide with either \\(B\\) or \\(B'\\).  \n\nIf \\(H'=B\\), then \\(B\\) is the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\); but \\(H\\) lies on the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\), so its reflection across \\(MN\\) lies on the same altitude, i.e. on the line \\(PH\\).  Since \\(P\\) is the centre of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\), the only point of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) on the altitude \\(PH\\) is \\(P\\) itself, contradicting \\(H'\\neq P\\).  \n\nIf \\(H'=B'\\), then \\(B'\\) is the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\).  Reflecting again across \\(MN\\) we obtain that \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(B'\\) across \\(MN\\), i.e. \\(H\\) coincides with \\(B\\).  But \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and cannot lie on the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at a point distinct from the vertices, a contradiction.\n\nThus both possibilities lead to contradictions; the only consistent situation is that the second intersection of \\(L\\) with \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) coincides with \\(B\\) itself.  \nHence \\(L\\) touches \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at \\(B\\); that is, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\u220e"
