"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous solution of the problem.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof is by contradiction.\n\n*Assumption.*  Suppose the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) (call it \\(\\ell\\)) is **not** tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nFrom this assumption \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at two distinct points: the known point \\(B\\) and a second point \\(T\\neq B\\).\n\nThe argument proceeds through three lemmas.\n\n* **Lemma\u202f1.**  \\(P\\) is the centre of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) and therefore the midpoint of the (minor) arc \\(EF\\) that does not contain \\(B\\).  \n  *Proof idea.*  Because \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the power of \\(P\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) is equal; the collinearity \\(A,P,E\\) and \\(A,P,F\\) then yields \\(PE=PF=PB\\).\n\n* **Lemma\u202f2.**  The reflection \\(H'\\) of \\(H\\) across the line \\(MN\\) lies on \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).  \n  *Proof idea.*  In any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circumcircle of that triangle.  Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) gives \\(H'\\in (PMN)\\).  Since \\((PMN)\\) and \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) have the same centre \\(P\\) and the same radius (Lemma\u202f1), the two circles coincide, hence \\(H'\\in\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\n* **Lemma\u202f3.**  The second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) is the reflection \\(B'\\) of \\(B\\) across \\(MN\\).  \n  *Proof idea.*  Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) passes through the centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\), the chord of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) cut by \\(\\ell\\) is symmetric with respect to the line \\(MN\\).  Consequently the two intersection points of \\(\\ell\\) with \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(MN\\); one of them is \\(B\\), so the other must be its mirror image \\(B'\\).\n\nAssuming \\(\\ell\\) is not tangent gives \\(T=B'\\neq B\\).  Lemma\u202f2 tells us that the reflected orthocentre \\(H'\\) also belongs to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).  Since both \\(H'\\) and \\(B'\\) lie on \\(\\ell\\) (the latter by construction, the former because \\(\\ell\\) is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) and reflection across \\(MN\\) preserves parallelism), the only points of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) on \\(\\ell\\) are \\(B\\) and \\(B'\\).  Hence \\(H'\\) must coincide with either \\(B\\) or \\(B'\\).\n\nBoth possibilities are impossible:\n\n* If \\(H'=B\\), then \\(B\\) is the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\).  But \\(H\\) lies on the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\); reflecting across \\(MN\\) sends this altitude onto itself, so the reflected point must also lie on the altitude \\(PH\\).  The only point of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) on the altitude through its centre \\(P\\) is the centre itself, contradicting \\(H'\\neq P\\).\n\n* If \\(H'=B'\\), then \\(B'\\) is the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\).  Reflecting again gives that \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(B'\\) across \\(MN\\), i.e. \\(H\\) coincides with \\(B\\).  But \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and cannot lie on the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at a point distinct from the vertices, a contradiction.\n\nThus the assumption that \\(\\ell\\) is not tangent leads to a contradiction.  Consequently \\(\\ell\\) touches \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at \\(B\\); equivalently, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, with radii \\(r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be their two intersection points;  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that the order on the line is \\(C,M,N,D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n* \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\);  \n* \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nWe shall prove that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1.  \\(P\\) is the centre of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the quadrilateral \\(APCD\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and on line \\(AP\\), we have \\(PE=PA\\) (both are radii of \\(\\Omega\\) measured from \\(P\\)).  \nSimilarly \\(PF=PA\\) because \\(F\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\) and on line \\(AP\\).  \nThus  \n\n\\[\nPE=PF=PA. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(B,E,F\\) are all on the same circle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\); its centre is the unique point equidistant from \\(B,E,F\\).  \nFrom (2) we already have \\(PE=PF\\).  Moreover, \\(B\\) belongs to both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nPB=PA \\qquad\\text{(power of }P\\text{ with respect to }\\Omega\\text{ and }\\Gamma). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nCombining (2) and (3) yields \\(PB=PE=PF\\); therefore \\(P\\) is equidistant from the three points \\(B,E,F\\) and is the centre of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2.  The reflection \\(H'\\) of \\(H\\) across the line \\(MN\\) lies on \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIn any triangle, the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circumcircle of that triangle.  \nApply this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\): the reflection of \\(H\\) across side \\(MN\\) belongs to the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\).  \nDenote this reflection by \\(H'\\); then \\(H'\\in(PMN)\\).\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 the centre of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) is \\(P\\) and its radius equals \\(PB\\).  \nFrom (1) we have \\(PM=PN\\); hence the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\) also has centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PM=PN\\).  \nBut \\(PB=PE=PF\\) (Lemma\u202f1) and \\(PE=PA=PC=PD\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nPB=PC=PD=PM=PN,\n\\]\n\nso the two circles \\((PMN)\\) and \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) coincide.  \nTherefore \\(H'\\in\\omega_{BEF}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3.  The second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) is the reflection \\(B'\\) of \\(B\\) across \\(MN\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) passes through the centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) (Lemma\u202f1), the chord of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) cut by \\(\\ell\\) is symmetric with respect to the line through \\(P\\) that is perpendicular to \\(\\ell\\).  \nThe line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(\\ell\\) is precisely the line \\(MN\\): indeed \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) (the line joining the centres of the two intersecting circles is the perpendicular bisector of their common chord \\(AB\\)), and \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nHence the two intersection points of \\(\\ell\\) with \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(MN\\).  \nOne of them is \\(B\\); consequently the other is the mirror image of \\(B\\) across \\(MN\\), which we denote by \\(B'\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof (contradiction)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that \\(\\ell\\) is **not** tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at \\(B\\).  \nThen \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at two distinct points, namely \\(B\\) and a second point \\(T\\neq B\\).\n\nBy Lemma\u202f3 the second intersection must be the reflection \\(B'\\) of \\(B\\) across \\(MN\\); thus \\(T=B'\\).\n\nLemma\u202f2 tells us that the reflected orthocentre \\(H'\\) also lies on \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).  \nSince \\(H'\\) is the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) and \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\) (which is itself perpendicular to \\(MN\\)), the line \\(\\ell\\) is invariant under reflection across \\(MN\\).  \nConsequently the image of \\(H\\) under this reflection, namely \\(H'\\), also lies on \\(\\ell\\).  \n\nThus the three points \\(B,\\,B',\\,H'\\) all belong to the intersection \\(\\ell\\cap\\omega_{BEF}\\).  \nBut a line can intersect a circle in at most two points; therefore \\(H'\\) must coincide with either \\(B\\) or \\(B'\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(H'=B\\).*  \nThen \\(B\\) is the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\).  \nThe altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) passes through \\(H\\); reflecting this altitude across \\(MN\\) leaves it unchanged, so the reflected point \\(H'\\) also lies on the same line \\(PH\\).  \nHence \\(B\\) lies on the line \\(PH\\).  \nBut \\(P\\) is the centre of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\); the only point of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) on the line through its centre is the centre itself.  Thus \\(B=P\\), contradicting the fact that \\(B\\) is a point on the original circles distinct from the centres.\n\n*Case 2: \\(H'=B'\\).*  \nThen \\(B'\\) is the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\).  Reflecting again across \\(MN\\) yields that \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(B'\\) across \\(MN\\), i.e. \\(H=B\\).  \nBut \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and cannot coincide with a point on the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) that is not a vertex of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); this contradicts the definition of orthocentre.\n\nBoth cases are impossible, so our initial assumption is false.  \nTherefore \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at exactly one point, namely \\(B\\); that is, \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n\u220e"
