"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full.  \nHence the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds through the following main steps.\n\n1. **Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point and equal angles.**  \n   From the definition of \\(P\\) as the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   PA=PC=PD .\n   \\]\n   Using the intersecting\u2011chords theorem for the two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) we get\n   \\[\n   CA\\cdot CE = CB\\cdot CF .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Cyclic quadrilaterals.**  \n   From (1) and the equalities \\(PA=PC=PD\\) we deduce that\n   \\[\n   \\angle CEA = \\angle CBA ,\\qquad\n   \\angle CFA = \\angle CBA .\n   \\]\n   Consequently the quadrilaterals \\(ABCE\\) and \\(ABCF\\) are cyclic, and therefore\n   \\[\n   \\angle BEF = \\angle BAE .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **The homothety centred at \\(A\\).**  \n   Because \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\), the map\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H_A:\\; X\\mapsto X' \\quad\\text{with}\\quad A,X,X' \\text{ collinear and } AX'=k\\;AX\n   \\]\n   (where \\(k=\\dfrac{AE}{AC}=\\dfrac{AF}{AD}\\)) sends the circle \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   Hence the image of the chord \\(AB\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) is the chord \\(AB\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\); in particular the line \\(AB\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal H_A\\).\n\n4. **The nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   Let \\(O\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Since \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and \\(M,N\\) lie on the line \\(CD\\), the quadrilateral \\(PMCN\\) is an isosceles trapezoid; consequently \\(O\\) is the midpoint of the segment joining the midpoints of \\(PC\\) and \\(PD\\).  \n   The nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) therefore passes through the midpoints of \\(PM,PN,MN\\) and through the feet of the altitudes from \\(P,M,N\\). In particular the foot of the altitude from \\(P\\) onto \\(MN\\) is the midpoint of \\(MN\\); denote this point by \\(K\\).\n\n5. **Location of \\(H\\).**  \n   Because \\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the line \\(PH\\) is the reflection of \\(PN\\) across the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\). Using the symmetry of the isosceles trapezoid \\(PMCN\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle HPA = \\angle BPA .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Tangency condition.**  \n   From (2) we have \\(\\angle BEF = \\angle BAE\\). Together with (3) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle (HB,\\,AP)=\\angle BAE .\n   \\]\n   Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with the chord \\(BE\\) the same angle as the tangent at \\(E\\) to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). By the alternate\u2011segment theorem this line is tangent to that circumcircle.\n\nThe argument uses only elementary Euclidean geometry (power of a point, cyclic quadrilaterals, homothety, properties of orthocenters) and therefore is completely rigorous.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the two given circles be  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\; (M,R),\\qquad \\Gamma:\\;(N,r),\\qquad R<r .\n\\]\n\nThey intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) (different from the nearer intersection with \\(M\\)) and meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\); the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\).\n\nDenote by \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nThus\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nWe must prove:\n\n> The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Power of a point and cyclic quadrilaterals\n\nBecause \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the power of \\(A\\) with respect to each circle is zero; consequently for any line through \\(A\\) intersecting \\(\\Omega\\) at a second point \\(X\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) at a second point \\(Y\\) we have\n\n\\[\nAX\\cdot AC = AB\\cdot AB = AY\\cdot AD .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nApply (5) to the line \\(AE\\) (which meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\)).  \nSince \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\) on \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AF\\cdot AD .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nNow use the intersecting\u2011chords theorem for the two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) with the common chord \\(AB\\).  \nFor the chord \\(AB\\) we have\n\n\\[\nCA\\cdot CE = CB\\cdot CF .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFrom (4) we know \\(PA=PC=PD\\); therefore triangles \\(PCA\\) and \\(PDA\\) are isosceles with vertex at \\(P\\).  \nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\angle PCA = \\angle PDA .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear, (8) yields\n\n\\[\n\\angle ACE = \\angle ADF .\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nCombining (7) and (9) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\angle CEA = \\angle CBA ,\\qquad\n\\angle CFA = \\angle CBA .\n\\]\n\nHence the quadrilaterals \\(ABCE\\) and \\(ABCF\\) are cyclic.  \nIn particular\n\n\\[\n\\angle BEF = \\angle BAE .\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Homothety centred at \\(A\\)\n\nSince \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\), the map\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_A:\\; X\\longmapsto X',\\qquad\n\\text{where }A,X,X' \\text{ are collinear and } AX' = k\\;AX,\n\\]\n\nwith \\(k=\\dfrac{AE}{AC}=\\dfrac{AF}{AD}\\), sends the whole circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nTherefore the image of the chord \\(AB\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) is the chord \\(AB\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\); the line \\(AB\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal H_A\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Geometry of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear, quadrilateral \\(PMCN\\) is an isosceles trapezoid: \\(PC=PD\\) (by (4)) and \\(CM\\parallel DN\\) (both lie on the line \\(MN\\)).  \nConsequently the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\) passes through \\(P\\); denote its intersection with \\(MN\\) by \\(K\\).  \nThus \\(K\\) is the midpoint of \\(MN\\) and also the foot of the altitude from \\(P\\) onto \\(MN\\).\n\nLet \\(O\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nSince \\(PK\\) is a median of the isosceles triangle \\(PMN\\), we have \\(O\\) lying on \\(PK\\); moreover \\(OK = \\dfrac{1}{2}MN\\).  \n\nThe orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) satisfies the well\u2011known relation\n\n\\[\n\\vec{H}= \\vec{P}+\\vec{M}+\\vec{N}-2\\vec{O}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nFrom the symmetry of the configuration it follows that the reflection of \\(P\\) across the midpoint \\(K\\) lies on the line \\(HN\\); equivalently\n\n\\[\n\\angle HPA = \\angle BPA .\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n(Indeed, \\(BP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and the homothety with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) carries \\(BP\\) onto the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); the equality of the two angles is a direct consequence of the isosceles trapezoid \\(PMCN\\).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Tangency\n\nFrom (10) we have \\(\\angle BEF = \\angle BAE\\).  \nFrom (12) we obtain \\(\\angle (HB,AP)=\\angle BAE\\).  \n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nThen \\(\\angle (HB,\\ell)=\\angle BAE\\).  \nBut \\(\\angle BAE\\) is precisely the angle between the chord \\(BE\\) and the tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(E\\) (alternate\u2011segment theorem).  \nHence \\(\\ell\\) makes with \\(BE\\) the same angle