"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full.  \nHence the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by a sequence of elementary geometric lemmas that together give the desired tangency.\n\n1. **Lemma\u202f1 (Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point for the line \\(MN\\)).**  \n   The points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the internal points of the line \\(MN\\) cut by the two circles; consequently  \n   \\[\n   MC\\cdot MN = MA^{2}=MB^{2}=ND\\cdot MN .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lemma\u202f2 (Location of \\(P\\)).**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); therefore  \n   \\[\n   PC=PD\\quad\\text{and}\\quad AP\\perp CD .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Lemma\u202f3 (Cyclic quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\)).**  \n   By construction the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the same line \\(AP\\) and on the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively. Since \\(A,B\\) belong to both circles, the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic; denote this circle by \\(\\omega\\).\n\n4. **Lemma\u202f4 (Relation between \\(H\\) and the Simson line of \\(A\\) w.r.t. \\(\\triangle PMN\\)).**  \n   In any triangle, the orthocenter together with a point on its circumcircle determines a Simson line.  \n   Because \\(A\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (the circle with diameter \\(MN\\) passes through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)), the Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is precisely the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n5. **Lemma\u202f5 (Simson line and tangency).**  \n   For a triangle \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) and a point \\(Q\\) on its circumcircle, the Simson line of \\(Q\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of the pedal triangle of \\(Q\\).  \n   Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and