ower with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) is zero, i.e. \\(MC\\cdot MC=0\\).  \nBecause the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the power of \\(M\\) with respect to the *common* chord \\(AB\\) is\n\\[\nMA^{2}=MB^{2}=MC\\cdot MN .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nSimilarly,\n\\[\nND\\cdot NM = NA^{2}=NB^{2}=NC\\cdot NM .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2.  Position of \\(P\\).\n\n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); hence \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  \nConsequently \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); therefore\n\\[\nAP\\perp CD\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad PC=PD .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3.  The quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic.\n\nBy definition \\(E\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\in\\Gamma\\).  \nSince \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) belong to both circles, the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) lie on the same circle; denote this circle by \\(\\omega\\).  \nThus\n\\[\nA,B,E,F\\ \\text{are concyclic}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nRecall that the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) has diameter \\(MN\\).  \nIndeed, \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the centers of the two given circles, and the chord \\(AB\\) is the radical axis; the midpoint of \\(MN\\) is the center of the circle through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) orthogonal to both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nConsequently \\(A\\) lies on this circumcircle, i.e.\n\\[\n\\angle MAN = 90^{\\circ}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor any triangle, the Simson line of a point on its circumcircle passes through the orthocenter of the triangle and is parallel to the line joining the point with the circumcenter of the triangle.  \nApplying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and the point \\(A\\) (which lies on its circumcircle) gives that the Simson line of \\(A\\) is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nHence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\ \\text{the line } \\ell\\text{ through }H\\text{ with }\\ell\\parallel AP\\text{ is the Simson line of }A\\text{ w.r.t. }\\triangle PMN\\ } .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f5.  Simson line and tangency to the pedal circle.\n\nLet \\(Q\\) be a point on the circumcircle of a triangle \\(\\triangle XYZ\\).  \nDenote by \\(X',Y',Z'\\) the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(Q\\) to the sides \\(YZ,ZX,XY\\) respectively (the *pedal triangle* of \\(Q\\)).  \nA classical theorem (the Simson\u2011Line Theorem) states that the Simson line of \\(Q\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of the pedal triangle \\(\\triangle X'Y'Z'\\).  \n\nApplying this theorem to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and the point \\(A\\) yields:\n\n\\[\n\\text{The line }\\ell\\text{ (the Simson line of }A\\text{)}\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of the pedal triangle of }A\\text{ w.r.t. }\\triangle PMN .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f6.  The pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nWe compute the three feet explicitly.\n\n*Foot on \\(MN\\).*  \nSince \\(MN\\) is the line joining the centers of the two given circles, the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) is the line \\(AB\\).  \nBecause \\(A\\) lies on both circles, the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(MN\\) meets \\(MN\\) at the point \\(B\\). Hence the foot on \\(MN\\) is \\(B\\).\n\n*Foot on \\(PM\\).*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f2, \\(AP\\perp CD\\).  \nThe line \\(PM\\) is symmetric to \\(PD\\) with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); consequently the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(PM\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\). Thus the foot on \\(PM\\) is \\(E\\).\n\n*Foot on \\(PN\\).*  \nA completely analogous argument, using the symmetry with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), shows that the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(PN\\) meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\). Hence the foot on \\(PN\\) is \\(F\\).\n\nTherefore the three feet are precisely \\(B,E,F\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\ \\text{the pedal triangle of }A\\text{ w.r.t. }\\triangle PMN\\text{ is }\\triangle BEF\\ } .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f6 the circumcircle of the pedal triangle of \\(A\\) is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nLemma\u202f5 tells us that the Simson line of \\(A\\) (which, by Lemma\u202f4, is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to that circumcircle.  \n\nConsequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as required. \u220e"
