            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\) (\\(r<R\\));  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (different from the nearer point to \\(M\\));  \n* \\(D\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) (different from the nearer point to \\(N\\)); the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1.  Power of a point on \\(MN\\).\n\nBecause \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and on the line \\(MN\\), the power of \\(M\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) is zero, while its power with respect to \\(\\Gamma\\) equals \\(MN\\cdot MD\\).  \nSince \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) belong to both circles, the power of \\(M\\) with respect to the common chord \\(AB\\) is\n\n\\[\nMA^{2}=MB^{2}=MC\\cdot MN . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, using point \\(N\\),\n\n\\[\nNA^{2}=NB^{2}=ND\\cdot NM . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2.  Properties of \\(P\\).\n\n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), whence\n\n\\[\nAP\\perp CD . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3.  Cyclicity of \\(A,B,E,F\\).\n\nBy construction \\(E\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\in\\Gamma\\).  \nSince \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are the two common points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) lie on the same circle; denote it by \\(\\omega\\). Thus\n\n\\[\nA,B,E,F\\ \\text{are concyclic}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4.  The circle with diameter \\(MN\\) passes through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(MA^{2}=MC\\cdot MN\\).  \nThe right\u2011hand side is precisely the power of \\(A\\) with respect to the circle having \\(MN\\) as a diameter (the circle whose centre is the midpoint of \\(MN\\) and whose radius is \\(\\tfrac{1}{2}MN\\)).  \nHence \\(A\\) lies on that circle; the same argument applied to \\(B\\) gives \\(B\\) also lies on it.  \nTherefore\n\n\\[\n\\angle MAN = \\angle MBN = 90^{\\circ}, \\qquad\nA,B\\in\\omega_{MN},\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\omega_{MN}\\) denotes the circle with diameter \\(MN\\).  \n\nConsequently \\(\\omega_{MN}\\) is the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (its centre is the midpoint of \\(MN\\), and \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\) because \\(PA=PC=PD\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f5.  Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nFor any triangle, the Simson line of a point on its circumcircle passes through the orthocenter of the triangle and is parallel to the line joining the point with the circumcenter of the triangle.  \n\nSince \\(A\\) belongs to the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{MN}\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (Lemma\u202f4), the Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) passes through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n\nThe circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(MN\\).  \nBecause \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) (Lemma\u202f2) and \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(O\\), the line \\(AO\\) coincides with \\(AP\\).  \nHence the Simson line of \\(A\\) is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Thus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is the Simson line of }A\\text{ w.r.t. }\\triangle PMN}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f6.  Pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThe pedal triangle of a point is formed by the feet of the perpendiculars from the point to the three sides of the reference triangle.\n\n*Foot on \\(MN\\).*  \nFrom (6) we have \\(\\angle MAN=90^{\\circ}\\); therefore the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(MN\\) meets \\(MN\\) at the point \\(B\\). Hence the foot on \\(MN\\) is \\(B\\).\n\n*Foot on \\(PM\\).*  \nBecause \\(AP\\perp CD\\) (Lemma\u202f2) and \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\), the line through \\(A\\) perpendicular to \\(PM\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\), namely \\(E\\). Consequently the foot on \\(PM\\) is \\(E\\).\n\n*Foot on \\(PN\\).*  \nAn identical reasoning, using the symmetry of the configuration with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), shows that the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(PN\\) meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\), namely \\(F\\). Hence the foot on \\(PN\\) is \\(F\\).\n\nThus the three feet are precisely \\(B,E,F\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{the pedal triangle of }A\\text{ w.r.t. }\\triangle PMN\\text{ is }\\triangle BEF}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f7.  Simson line is tangent to the circumcircle of the pedal triangle.\n\n**Simson\u2011tangency theorem.**  \nLet \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) be any triangle and let \\(Q\\) be a point on its circumcircle. Denote by \\(X',Y',Z'\\) the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(Q\\) to the sides \\(YZ,ZX,XY\\) respectively. Then the Simson line of \\(Q\\) (the line \\(X'Y'Z'\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle X'Y'Z'\\).\n\nApplying this theorem to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and the point \\(A\\) (which lies on its circumcircle by Lemma\u202f4) we obtain:\n\n* the Simson line of \\(A\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of its pedal triangle;\n* by Lemma\u202f5 the Simson line is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\);\n* by Lemma\u202f6 the pedal triangle is \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nTherefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe required tangency has been established rigorously. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors