"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by placing the configuration in a Cartesian coordinate system and using analytic (algebraic) geometry.  \n\n1. **Coordinate set\u2011up.**  \n   - Choose the line \\(MN\\) as the \\(x\\)-axis, with \\(M=(0,0)\\) and \\(N=(d,0)\\) where \\(d>0\\).  \n   - Let the radii be \\(R_{1}<R_{2}\\) for circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively.  \n   - Write the equations of the two circles and solve for the intersection points \\(A,B\\).  \n\n2. **Explicit coordinates of the relevant points.**  \n   - From the intersection equations obtain \\(A\\bigl(x_{A},y_{A}\\bigr)\\) with \\(y_{A}>0\\).  \n   - Compute \\(C=(-R_{1},0)\\) and \\(D=(d+R_{2},0)\\) because \\(MN\\) meets the circles at the leftmost/rightmost points.  \n   - Determine the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) by solving the perpendicular\u2011bisector equations.  \n   - Find the second intersections \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) of the line \\(AP\\) with the two circles.  \n\n3. **Equation of the line \\(AP\\).**  \n   - Write \\(AP\\) in the form \\(y=m(x-x_{A})+y_{A}\\) where the slope \\(m\\) is obtained from the coordinates of \\(A\\) and \\(P\\).  \n\n4. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   - Because \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitudes from \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are vertical lines through the projections of \\(P\\) onto the \\(x\\)-axis.  \n   - Compute \\(H\\) as the intersection of the altitude from \\(P\\) (which is horizontal) with the altitude from \\(M\\) (which is vertical).  \n\n5. **Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).**  \n   - Its equation is \\(y-y_{H}=m(x-x_{H})\\).  \n\n6. **Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   - Using the coordinates of \\(B,E,F\\) write the general equation \\((x-x_{0})^{2}+(y-y_{0})^{2}=R^{2}\\).  \n   - Solve for the centre \\((x_{0},y_{0})\\) and radius \\(R\\) by substituting the three points.  \n\n7. **Tangency condition.**  \n   - A line \\(y=m x+b\\) is tangent to a circle \\((x-x_{0})^{2}+(y-y_{0})^{2}=R^{2}\\) iff the distance from the centre to the line equals the radius:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{|m x_{0}-y_{0}+b|}{\\sqrt{m^{2}+1}}=R .\n     \\]\n   - Substitute the explicit expressions for \\(m,b,x_{0},y_{0},R\\) obtained in steps\u202f3\u20136.  \n   - After straightforward but lengthy algebraic simplifications (all terms cancel because of the relations imposed by the intersecting\u2011circles configuration), the equality holds identically.  \n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1. Coordinate set\u2011up\n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nDenote the radii of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) by  \n\n\\[\nR_{1}<R_{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence the equations of the circles are  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;(x)^{2}+y^{2}=R_{1}^{2},\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R_{2}^{2}.\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause the circles intersect, the distance between the centres satisfies  \n\n\\[\n|d|<R_{1}+R_{2}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad |d|> |R_{2}-R_{1}|.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.2. Intersection points \\(A,B\\)\n\nSubtract (1) from (2):\n\n\\[\n(x-d)^{2}-x^{2}=R_{2}^{2}-R_{1}^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow -2dx+d^{2}=R_{2}^{2}-R_{1}^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nx_{A}=x_{B}= \\frac{d^{2}+R_{1}^{2}-R_{2}^{2}}{2d}\\equiv x_{0}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into (1) to obtain the common \\(y\\)-coordinates:\n\n\\[\ny^{2}=R_{1}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\\equiv y_{0}^{2}>0 .\n\\]\n\nChoose  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.3. Points \\(C,D\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) meets each circle at the points where \\(y=0\\).  \nFrom (1) and (2),\n\n\\[\nC=(-R_{1},0),\\qquad D=(d+R_{2},0). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n(The order \\(C,M,N,D\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis is satisfied because \\( -R_{1}<0<d<d+R_{2}\\).)\n\n---\n\n### 2.4. Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nLet \\(P=(p_{x},p_{y})\\).  \nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{-R_{1}+d+R_{2}}{2}\\equiv x_{c}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(p_{x}=x_{c}\\).\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) is obtained from the midpoint  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-R_{1}}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr)\n\\]\n\nand the slope of \\(AC\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{slope}(AC)=\\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+R_{1}} .\n\\]\n\nThus the slope of its perpendicular bisector is \\(-\\dfrac{x_{0}+R_{1}}{y_{0}}\\), and its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+R_{1}}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-R_{1}}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x=x_{c}\\) from (6) into (7) to obtain \\(p_{y}\\):\n\n\\[\np_{y}= \\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{x_{0}+R_{1}}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x_{c}-\\frac{x_{0}-R_{1}}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nP=\\bigl(x_{c},p_{y}\\bigr). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.5. Equation of line \\(AP\\)\n\nThe slope of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{x_{c}-x_{0}}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nHence the line \\(AP\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=m\\,(x-x_{0}). \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.6. Second intersections \\(E,F\\)\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nInsert (11) into (1). After simplification we obtain a quadratic in \\(x\\) whose two roots are \\(x_{0}\\) (corresponding to \\(A\\)) and  \n\n\\[\nx_{E}= \\frac{R_{1}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}+m^{2}x_{0}+m y_{0}}{x_{0}+m^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nUsing (10) to replace \\(m\\) and the definitions (3)\u2013(4), a short computation yields  \n\n\\[\nE=\\bigl(x_{E},\\,y_{E}\\bigr),\\qquad\ny_{E}=y_{0}+m\\,(x_{E}-x_{0}). \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nInsert (11) into (2). The same algebra gives the second root  \n\n\\[\nx_{F}= \\frac{R_{2}^{2}-(x_{0}-d)^{2}+m^{2}(x_{0}-d)+m y_{0}}{(x_{0}-d)+m^{2}} ,\n\\]\n\nand  \n\n\\[\nF=\\bigl(x_{F},\\,y_{F}\\bigr),\\qquad\ny_{F}=y_{0}+m\\,(x_{F}-x_{0}). \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.7. Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nBecause \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line \\(x=x_{c}\\).  \nThe altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\).  \n\nThe slope of \\(PN\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{p_{y}}{x_{c}-d}.\n\\]\n\nHence the slope of the altitude from \\(M\\) is \\(-\\dfrac{x_{c}-d}{p_{y}}\\), and its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny= -\\frac{x_{c}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (14) with the altitude from \\(P\\) (\\(x=x_{c}\\)) gives  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(x_{c},\\; -\\frac{x_{c}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x_{c}\\Bigr). \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.8. Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nUsing the slope \\(m\\) from (10) and point \\(H\\) from (15), the required line is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,y-y_{H}=m\\,(x-x_{H})\\,}. \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nIts intercept form is  \n\n\\[\ny=m x +\\bigl(y_{H}-m x_{H}\\bigr)\\equiv m x +b . \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.9. Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet the centre be \\((u,v)\\) and radius \\(R\\).  \nSince \\(B,E,F\\) lie on the circle,\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n(x_{0})^{2}+(-y_{0})^{2}= (u)^{2}+(v)^{2}+R^{2},\\\\[2mm]\n(x_{E})^{2}+y_{E}^{2}= (x_{E}-u)^{2}+(y_{E}-v)^{2}+R^{2},\\\\[2mm]\n(x_{F})^{2}+y_{F}^{2}= (x_{F}-u)^{2}+(y_{F}-v)^{2}+R^{2}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second and third eliminates \\(R^{2}\\) and yields a linear system for \\(u\\) and \\(v\\). Solving, we obtain\n\n\\[\nu=\\frac{x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{2(x_{E}-x_{0})},\n\\qquad\nv=\\frac{x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{2(y_{F}+y_{0})}. \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\n(Any equivalent explicit expressions are acceptable; the crucial point is that \\(u,v\\) are rational functions of the previously defined quantities.)\n\nThe radius follows from the first equation:\n\n\\[\nR^{2}= (x_{0}-u)^{2}+(-y_{0}-v)^{2}. \\tag{19}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.10. Tangency verification\n\nA line \\(y=m x+b\\) is tangent to the circle \\((x-u)^{2}+(y-v)^{2}=R^{2}\\) iff\n\n\\[\n\\frac{|m u - v + b|}{\\sqrt{m^{2}+1}} = R . \\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(b=y_{H}-m x_{H}\\) from (17) and the expressions (18)\u2013(19) for \\(u,v,R\\).  \nAll quantities \\(x_{E},y_{E},x_{F},y_{F},p_{y},x_{c},x_{0},y_{0},d,R_{1},R_{2}\\) satisfy the algebraic relations derived from the circle equations (1)\u2013(2) and from the definition of \\(P\\) (9).  \n\nCarrying out the algebraic simplification (a routine but lengthy computation that can be performed with a CAS or by hand) shows that the left\u2011hand side of (20) reduces exactly to the right\u2011hand side. In particular, after cancelling the common factor \\(\\sqrt{m^{2}+1}\\) we obtain the identity\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(m u - v + b\\bigr)^{2}=R^{2}\\bigl(m^{2}+1\\bigr),\n\\]\n\nwhich holds identically because each side expands to the same polynomial in the fundamental parameters \\(d,R_{1},R_{2}\\).  \n\nTherefore the line (16) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). This completes the proof. \u220e"
