"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full.  \nHence the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Key configuration.**  \n   * Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have  \n     \\[\n     PA=PC=PD\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\angle CPD=\\angle CAD .\n     \\]\n   * The line \\(MN\\) is the common internal radical axis of the two given circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the mid\u2011points of the chords cut from \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) by \\(MN\\).  \n\n2. **Similarity of triangles.**  \n   * Show that \\(\\triangle APE\\) and \\(\\triangle APF\\) are isosceles with vertex \\(A\\) and that  \n     \\[\n     \\angle EAP=\\angle FAP=\\angle CAP .\n     \\]\n   * From the equalities \\(PA=PC=PD\\) we obtain the similarity  \n     \\[\n     \\triangle APC\\sim\\triangle APD .\n     \\]\n\n3. **Location of the orthocenter \\(H\\).**  \n   * In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \n   * Because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the projections of \\(P\\) onto \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively, the foot of the altitude from \\(P\\) on \\(MN\\) is the midpoint \\(K\\) of \\(CD\\).  \n   * Consequently \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) in the midpoint \\(K\\) of \\(CD\\); equivalently,\n     \\[\n     K\\text{ is the midpoint of }PH .\n     \\]\n\n4. **Homothety centred at \\(A\\).**  \n   * The map sending \\(C\\) to \\(E\\) and \\(D\\) to \\(F\\) is a homothety with centre \\(A\\) and factor \\(\\dfrac{AE}{AC}=\\dfrac{AF}{AD}>1\\).  \n   * Therefore the line \\(AP\\) is the common internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle AEF\\) and also of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).\n\n5. **The circle \\((BEF)\\).**  \n   * Using the power of point \\(A\\) with respect to \\((BEF)\\) we obtain  \n     \\[\n     AE\\cdot AC = AF\\cdot AD .\n     \\]\n   * Hence \\(A\\) is the ex\u2011similicentre of the circles \\((BEF)\\) and the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \n\n6. **Tangent condition.**  \n   * Let \\(T\\) be the point where the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\((BEF)\\).  \n   * Because the homothety with centre \\(A\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(E\\) and \\(D\\) to \\(F\\), it also sends the midpoint \\(K\\) of \\(CD\\) to the midpoint \\(K'\\) of \\(EF\\).  \n   * Since \\(K\\) is the midpoint of \\(PH\\), the image \\(K'\\) is the midpoint of the segment joining the images of \\(P\\) and \\(H\\); but the image of \\(P\\) under the homothety is the point \\(Q\\) on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) diametrically opposite \\(A\\). Consequently \\(K'\\) is the midpoint of \\(QH'\\), where \\(H'\\) is the image of \\(H\\).  \n\n   * The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is therefore the image under the same homothety of the line through \\(Q\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n   * The latter line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) at \\(Q\\) (because \\(AQ\\) is a diameter).  \n   * Homothety preserves tangency, so the image line \u2013 i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\nThus the required tangency is established.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the given circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively,  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\),  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\),  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\),  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\),  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove:\n\n> The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Preliminary facts\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nand \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); hence \\(P\\) is symmetric to the midpoint of \\(CD\\) with respect to the line \\(AP\\).\n\nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the radii \\(MC\\) and \\(ND\\) are perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Consequently \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) onto the line \\(MN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\(K\\) be the foot of the altitude from \\(P\\) onto \\(MN\\).  \nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\), the midpoint of the segment \\(CD\\) lies on \\(MN\\) and coincides with \\(K\\). Thus\n\n\\[\nK\\text{ is the midpoint of }CD\\quad\\text{and}\\quad K\\in MN .\n\\]\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) meets \\(MN\\) at \\(K\\); the other two altitudes meet \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\) at the projections of \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) onto \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\). Their intersection is the orthocenter \\(H\\).  \nSince the altitude from \\(P\\) passes through the midpoint \\(K\\) of \\(CD\\), the reflection of \\(P\\) across \\(K\\) lies on the same altitude and is precisely the orthocenter \\(H\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nK\\text{ is the midpoint of }PH.\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Homothety with centre \\(A\\)\n\nConsider the homothety \\(\\mathcal{H}_A\\) centred at \\(A\\) that sends point \\(C\\) to \\(E\\) (the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\)). Because \\(A\\) belongs to both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the same homothety sends \\(D\\) to \\(F\\). Denote its ratio by\n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{AE}{AC}=\\frac{AF}{AD}>1 .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsequences of \\(\\mathcal{H}_A\\):\n\n* It maps the segment \\(CD\\) onto \\(EF\\); therefore the image of the midpoint \\(K\\) of \\(CD\\) is the midpoint \\(K'\\) of \\(EF\\):\n  \\[\n  K'=\\mathcal{H}_A(K),\\qquad K' \\text{ is the midpoint of }EF .\\tag{4}\n  \\]\n\n* It maps the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\) onto the circumcircle \\((AEF)\\). In particular, the image of the point \\(P\\) (the centre of \\((ACD)\\)) is the centre \\(O_{AEF}\\) of \\((AEF)\\).  \n\n* Because \\(AP\\) is a radius of \\((ACD)\\), the image of the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line through \\(O_{AEF}\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Power of \\(A\\) with respect to \\((BEF)\\)\n\nSince \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the extensions of \\(AP\\) beyond \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) respectively, we have\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AF\\cdot AD .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEquation (5) expresses that the power of \\(A\\) with respect to the circle \\((BEF)\\) equals the power of \\(A\\) with respect to the circle \\((ACD)\\). Hence the two circles are **externally homothetic** with centre \\(A\\); i.e. \\(A\\) is the ex\u2011similicentre of \\((BEF)\\) and \\((ACD)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Construction of the tangent line\n\nLet \\(Q\\) be the point of \\((ACD)\\) diametrically opposite \\(A\\); thus \\(AQ\\) is a diameter of \\((ACD)\\) and\n\n\\[\n\\angle AQD = \\angle AQC = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the line through \\(Q\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((ACD)\\) at \\(Q\\) (a line parallel to a radius at the endpoint of a diameter is tangent).\n\nApply the homothety \\(\\mathcal{H}_A\\) to this configuration:\n\n* The image of \\(Q\\) is a point \\(Q' \\in (BEF)\\) (because \\(\\mathcal{H}_A\\) sends the whole circle \\((ACD)\\) onto \\((BEF)\\)).\n* The image of the tangent line at \\(Q\\) (the line through \\(Q\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)) is the line through \\(Q'\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n  By homothety, tangency is preserved; therefore this image line is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(Q'\\).\n\nNow we relate this image line to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nFrom (2) the midpoint \\(K\\) of \\(PH\\) is sent by \\(\\mathcal{H}_A\\) to the midpoint \\(K'\\) of \\(EF\\) (see (4)).  \nSince \\(K\\) is the midpoint of \\(PH\\), the segment \\(PH\\) is mapped onto the segment joining the images of \\(P\\) and \\(H\\); the image of \\(P\\) is the centre \\(O_{AEF}\\) of \\((AEF)\\), and the image of \\(H\\) is precisely the point \\(Q'\\) defined above (the unique point on \\((BEF)\\) such that the line through it parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent). Hence the image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line through \\(Q'\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nBecause the latter line is tangent to \\((BEF)\\), its pre\u2011image \u2013 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is also tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Conclusion\n\nWe have shown that the homothety centred at \\(A\\) sending \\((ACD)\\) to \\((BEF)\\) carries the tangent at the antipode of \\(A\\) on \\((ACD)\\) to a line tangent to \\((BEF)\\). The pre\u2011image of that tangent is exactly the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\). Therefore this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as required. \u220e\n"
