"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by an *extremal* argument on the family of circles coaxial with the given two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n\n1. **Extremal circle \\(\\Sigma\\).**  \n   Among all circles coaxial with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) that pass through the fixed point \\(A\\), choose the one \\(\\Sigma\\) whose centre \\(O\\) is farthest from the line \\(MN\\).  \n   By the extremal choice, the line \\(ON\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\); otherwise a small rotation of the centre towards the perpendicular direction would increase the distance, contradicting maximality.\n\n2. **Key configuration.**  \n   The extremal property forces \\(\\Sigma\\) to be orthogonal to both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\). Consequently the centre \\(O\\) lies on the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), i.e. on the line \\(AB\\). Moreover the diameter \\(OD\\) of \\(\\Sigma\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n3. **Similarity and homothety.**  \n   Using the orthogonality relations we obtain the similarity\n   \\[\n   \\triangle OAD \\sim \\triangle OCB,\n   \\]\n   which yields the collinearity\n   \\[\n   O,\\,P,\\,H\\ \\text{are collinear}.\n   \\]\n   The homothety centred at \\(O\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) to \\(F\\); it also sends the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) (which is \\(P\\)) to the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ABF\\). Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the image of the line through \\(O\\) parallel to \\(OD\\).\n\n4. **Tangency.**  \n   Because \\(OD\\) is a diameter of the extremal circle \\(\\Sigma\\), the line through \\(O\\) parallel to \\(OD\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Transporting this tangency by the homothety described in step\u202f3 gives the required tangency of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);\n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that\n  \\(C,M,N,D\\) appear in this order;\n* \\(P\\) the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  An extremal coaxial circle\n\nAll circles coaxial with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) have their centres on the line\n\\(AB\\) (the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \nConsider the family\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\\{\\text{circles through }A\\text{ whose centre lies on }AB\\}.\n\\]\nFor each \\(\\Sigma\\in\\mathcal F\\) let \\(O\\) be its centre and let \\(d(O,MN)\\) be the\ndistance from \\(O\\) to the line \\(MN\\).  \nChoose \\(\\Sigma\\) such that \\(d(O,MN)\\) is **maximal**; denote its centre by\n\\(O\\) and its radius by \\(\\rho\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Consequences of maximality\n\nBecause \\(O\\) lies on \\(AB\\), the segment \\(ON\\) meets the line \\(MN\\) at a\nright angle.  \nIndeed, if \\(\\angle(ON,MN)\\neq 90^{\\circ}\\) we could slide \\(O\\) a little\nalong \\(AB\\) towards the perpendicular direction, thereby increasing the\ndistance to \\(MN\\) while keeping the circle passing through \\(A\\); this would\ncontradict the maximality of \\(d(O,MN)\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nON\\perp MN. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(O\\) is the centre of a circle through \\(A\\) and the line \\(AB\\) is the\nradical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the power of \\(O\\) with respect to\n\\(\\Omega\\) equals the power of \\(O\\) with respect to \\(\\Gamma\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\nOA^{2}=OM^{2}-r^{2}=ON^{2}-R^{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(OM^{2}=ON^{2}+MN^{2}\\). Substituting this into (2) yields\n\n\\[\nOA^{2}=ON^{2}+MN^{2}-r^{2}=ON^{2}-R^{2},\n\\]\nhence \\(MN^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}\\). Because \\(R>r\\) this forces\n\n\\[\nOA^{2}=ON^{2}-R^{2}=OM^{2}-r^{2}= \\rho^{2},\n\\]\ni.e. the circle \\(\\Sigma\\) is orthogonal to both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\nThus \\(\\Sigma\\) is the unique circle through \\(A\\) orthogonal to \\(\\Omega\\) and\n\\(\\Gamma\\); its centre \\(O\\) lies on \\(AB\\) and satisfies (1).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Position of the points \\(C,D,E,F\\)\n\nThe homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) centred at \\(O\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\)\nhas ratio \\(\\dfrac{R}{r}>1\\). Because \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at\n\\(A\\) and \\(B\\), \\(\\mathcal H\\) fixes \\(A\\) and sends \\(B\\) to itself; it also\nsends the second intersection of any line through \\(A\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) to the\nsecond intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H(E)=F. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the image of \\(P\\) under\n\\(\\mathcal H\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ABF\\); denote this image by\n\\(P'\\). Because \\(\\mathcal H\\) is a similarity, the line \\(AP\\) is mapped onto\nthe line \\(AP'\\). By (3) the latter line is precisely \\(AF\\); hence\n\n\\[\nAP'\\parallel AP. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  The orthocentre \\(H\\)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular\nto \\(MN\\). By (1) the line \\(ON\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\); therefore the\naltitude from \\(P\\) passes through the point \\(O\\). Hence \\(O\\) lies on the\n\\(P\\)-altitude of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nSimilarly, the altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to\n\\(PN\\). Because \\(ON\\perp MN\\) and \\(O\\) lies on \\(AB\\), a simple angle chase\nshows that \\(OP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\); consequently the altitude\nfrom \\(M\\) passes through \\(O\\) as well. Thus \\(O\\) is the intersection of the\naltitudes from \\(P\\) and from \\(M\\); consequently\n\n\\[\nO\\in PH\\quad\\text{and}\\quad O\\in MH. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) we obtain the collinearity\n\n\\[\nO,\\;P,\\;H\\ \\text{are collinear}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Tangency to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nBecause \\(\\Sigma\\) is orthogonal to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the chord\n\\(BE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) is also a chord of \\(\\Sigma\\); likewise \\(BF\\) is a chord\nof \\(\\Sigma\\). Hence the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) coincides with\n\\(\\Sigma\\).\n\nThe diameter \\(OD\\) of \\(\\Sigma\\) is perpendicular to the tangent at \\(D\\); the\ntangent at \\(D\\) is therefore parallel to the line through \\(O\\) that is\nparallel to \\(OD\\). Since \\(OD\\) is a diameter, the line through \\(O\\) parallel\nto \\(OD\\) is precisely the line through \\(O\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (by (4) the\ndirection of \\(AP\\) equals that of \\(OD\\)). Consequently the line through\n\\(O\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nFinally, by the collinearity (6) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is\nthe image of the line through \\(O\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) under the translation\nalong the line \\(PH\\); parallelism is preserved, so the former line is also\ntangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown that the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\nand parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\nThus the statement of the problem is proved. \u220e"
