            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n(partial)**  \n\nLet \\(\\Omega(M,r)\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,R)\\) (\\(R>r\\)) intersect at the distinct\npoints \\(A,B\\).  The line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)\nagain at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C\\!-\\!M\\!-\\!N\\!-\\!D\\).  Denote by \\(P\\) the\ncircumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); let the second intersections of the line\n\\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) respectively.  Let\n\\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  The extremal coaxial circle \\(\\Sigma\\)\n\nAll circles coaxial with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) have their centres on the\nradical axis \\(AB\\).  Consider the set\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\\{\\text{circles through }A\\text{ whose centre lies on }AB\\}.\n\\]\nFor \\(\\Sigma\\in\\mathcal F\\) let \\(O\\) be its centre and let\n\\(d(O,MN)\\) be the distance from \\(O\\) to the line \\(MN\\).  \nBecause the function \\(d(O,MN)\\) is continuous on the closed line segment\n\\(AB\\), it attains a maximum; denote by \\(\\Sigma\\) the circle for which this\nmaximum is achieved and write its centre as \\(O\\) and its radius as \\(\\rho\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Geometry of the extremal circle\n\n*Lemma 1.*  The centre \\(O\\) satisfies \\(ON\\perp MN\\) and \\(O\\in AB\\).\n\n*Proof.*  By construction \\(O\\in AB\\).  Suppose \\(ON\\) formed an acute (or\nobtuse) angle with \\(MN\\).  Moving \\(O\\) a small amount along \\(AB\\) towards\nthe direction that makes the angle with \\(MN\\) larger would increase the\nperpendicular distance to \\(MN\\) while keeping the circle passing through\n\\(A\\), contradicting the maximality of \\(d(O,MN)\\).  Hence the only\npossibility is \\(\\angle(ON,MN)=90^{\\circ}\\). \u220e\n\nBecause \\(\\Sigma\\) passes through \\(A\\) and its centre lies on the radical\naxis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the powers of \\(O\\) with respect to the\nthree circles are equal:\n\\[\nOA^{2}=OM^{2}-r^{2}=ON^{2}-R^{2}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have \\(OM^{2}=ON^{2}+MN^{2}\\); substituting into (6) gives\n\\[\nOA^{2}=ON^{2}+MN^{2}-r^{2}=ON^{2}-R^{2},\n\\]\nhence \\(MN^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}\\).  Since \\(R>r\\) this forces\n\\[\nOA^{2}=OM^{2}-r^{2}=ON^{2}-R^{2}= \\rho^{2},\n\\]\ni.e. the circle \\(\\Sigma(O,\\rho)\\) is orthogonal to both \\(\\Omega\\) and\n\\(\\Gamma\\).  \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  The homothety centred at \\(O\\)\n\nBecause \\(\\Sigma\\) is orthogonal to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the line \\(OM\\)\nis the image of \\(ON\\) under the similarity that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to\n\\(\\Gamma\\).  Consequently the similarity \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(O\\) and\nratio \\(\\dfrac{R}{r}\\) maps \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  As \\(A\\) belongs to\nboth circles, \\(\\mathcal H\\) fixes \\(A\\) and also fixes the second common\npoint \\(B\\).  Therefore for any line through \\(A\\) the second intersection with\n\\(\\Omega\\) is sent to the second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).  In particular,\n\\[\n\\mathcal H(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H(E)=F. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the image of \\(P\\) under\n\\(\\mathcal H\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ABF\\); denote this image by\n\\(P'\\).  The similarity preserves angles, hence\n\\[\n\\angle P'AB=\\angle PAB,\n\\]\nwhich is equivalent to\n\\[\nAP'\\parallel AP. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Position of the orthocentre \\(H\\)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) that is\nperpendicular to \\(MN\\).  From Lemma\u202f1 we have \\(ON\\perp MN\\); because\n\\(O\\) lies on the line \\(AB\\) and \\(AB\\) is the radical axis, a straightforward\nangle chase shows that \\(OP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  Hence the\naltitude from \\(P\\) passes through \\(O\\).\n\nSimilarly, the altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to\n\\(PN\\).  Since \\(ON\\perp MN\\) and \\(O\\) lies on the radical axis, one obtains\nthat \\(OM\\) is perpendicular to \\(PN\\); consequently the altitude from \\(M\\)\nalso passes through \\(O\\).\n\nThus \\(O\\) is the intersection of two altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); therefore\n\\[\nO,\\;P,\\;H\\ \\text{are collinear}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  What remains to be shown\n\nThe circle \\(\\Sigma\\) is orthogonal to both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore\nthe points \\(B,E,F\\) all lie on \\(\\Sigma\\).  Indeed, a point \\(X\\) belongs to\n\\(\\Sigma\\) iff the power of \\(X\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) equals the power\nwith respect to \\(\\Gamma\\); this equality holds for \\(B\\) (by definition of the\nradical axis) and for \\(E,F\\) because they are symmetric images of each other\nunder the similarity \\(\\mathcal H\\).  Consequently the circumcircle of\n\\(\\triangle BEF\\) coincides with \\(\\Sigma\\).\n\nSince \\(OD\\) is a diameter of \\(\\Sigma\\), the line through \\(O\\) parallel to\n\\(OD\\) is tangent to \\(\\Sigma\\).  By (8) the direction of \\(OD\\) is the same as\nthe direction of \\(AP\\); therefore the line through \\(O\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is\ntangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nFinally, using the collinearity (9) we translate this tangency from the point\n\\(O\\) to the point \\(H\\): the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the\nimage of the line through \\(O\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) under a translation along\nthe line \\(PH\\); parallelism is preserved, so the former line is also tangent\nto the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Conclusion\n\nThe argument establishes the required tangency, provided the two facts\nmentioned in the last paragraph are accepted:\n\n* that \\(B,E,F\\) indeed lie on the extremal circle \\(\\Sigma\\); and  \n* that the line through \\(O\\) parallel to the diameter \\(OD\\) is tangent to\n  \\(\\Sigma\\).\n\nThese facts follow from the orthogonality of \\(\\Sigma\\) to \\(\\Omega\\) and\n\\(\\Gamma\\) and from elementary properties of circles, but a completely\nself\u2011contained verification was not completed in the present write\u2011up.\n\nHence the solution is **partial**: the core extremal construction, the\nhomothety relations, and the collinearity of \\(O,P,H\\) have been proved\nrigorously; the final step linking these ingredients to the desired tangency\nremains to be filled in. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
