rp MN\\) and \\(O\\) lies on the radical axis, one obtains that \\(OM\\) is perpendicular to \\(PN\\); consequently the altitude from \\(M\\) also passes through \\(O\\).\u201d\n  * **Issue:** The perpendicularity \\(OM\\perp PN\\) is asserted without proof; the relationship between the radical axis and the line \\(OM\\) is not established.\n\n* **Justification Gap \u2013 Claim that \\(B,E,F\\) lie on \\(\\Sigma\\)**\n  * **Location:** \u201cConsequently the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) coincides with \\(\\Sigma\\).\u201d\n  * **Issue:** The argument that the powers of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are equal (hence they lie on \\(\\Sigma\\)) is not demonstrated; it is only sketched.\n\n* **Justification Gap \u2013 Tangency from a line through \\(O\\) parallel to \\(OD\\)**\n  * **Location:** \u201cSince \\(OD\\) is a diameter of \\(\\Sigma\\), the line through \\(O\\) parallel to \\(OD\\) is tangent to \\(\\Sigma\\).\u201d\n  * **Issue:** A line through the centre of a circle cannot be tangent to that circle; the statement is false. The intended line is the one through the *point* where the diameter meets the circle, not through the centre. This logical mistake undermines the final conclusion.\n\n* **Justification Gap \u2013 Translation from \\(O\\) to \\(H\\)**\n  * **Location:** \u201cFinally, using the collinearity (9) we translate this tangency from the point \\(O\\) to the point \\(H\\): the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the image of the line through \\(O\\) \u2026\u201d\n  * **Issue:** Translating a tangent line by a vector does not, in general, preserve tangency to the same circle. The argument that parallelism alone guarantees tangency at the new point is incorrect and lacks justification.\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Verification Log\n\nBelow the solution is reproduced in numbered blocks; each block is examined in order. Quotations are taken verbatim from the solution.\n\n---\n\n### 2.1 The extremal coaxial circle \u03a3  \n\n**Quote:**  \n\u201cBecause the function \\(d(O,MN)\\) is continuous on the closed line segment \\(AB\\), it attains a maximum; denote by \\(\\Sigma\\) the circle for which this maximum is achieved \u2026\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe continuity claim is correct: the distance from a point \\(O\\) on the line segment \\(AB\\) to a fixed line \\(MN\\) varies continuously with the coordinates of \\(O\\). The existence of a maximum follows from the extreme\u2011value theorem. This step is acceptable.\n\n---\n\n### 2.2 Geometry of the extremal circle  \n\n#### Lemma\u202f1  \n\n**Quote:**  \n\u201cSuppose \\(ON\\) formed an acute (or obtuse) angle with \\(MN\\). Moving \\(O\\) a small amount along \\(AB\\) towards the direction that makes the angle with \\(MN\\) larger would increase the perpendicular distance to \\(MN\\) while keeping the circle passing through \\(A\\), contradicting the maximality \u2026 Hence \\(\\angle(ON,MN)=90^{\\circ}\\).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Justification Gap:**  \nThe argument assumes without proof that the distance from \\(O\\) to \\(MN\\) is a strictly monotone function of the angle \\(\\angle(ON,MN)\\) when \\(O\\) moves along \\(AB\\). A rigorous proof would require parametrising \\(O\\) on \\(AB\\) (e.g., using a coordinate system) and differentiating the distance function, showing that the derivative has the sign claimed. The current reasoning is informal and does not constitute a valid proof.\n\n---\n\n#### Orthogonality computation  \n\n**Quote:**  \n\u201cBecause \\(\\Sigma\\) passes through \\(A\\) and its centre lies on the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the powers of \\(O\\) with respect to the three circles are equal:\n\\[\nOA^{2}=OM^{2}-r^{2}=ON^{2}-R^{2}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have \\(OM^{2}=ON^{2}+MN^{2}\\); substituting into (6) gives\n\\[\nOA^{2}=ON^{2}+MN^{2}-r^{2}=ON^{2}-R^{2},\n\\]\nhence \\(MN^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}\\).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error (sign mistake):**  \nStarting from \\(OA^{2}=OM^{2}-r^{2}=ON^{2}-R^{2}\\) and using \\(OM^{2}=ON^{2}+MN^{2}\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nOA^{2}=ON^{2}+MN^{2}-r^{2}=ON^{2}-R^{2}\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;MN^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince the hypothesis states \\(R>r\\), the right\u2011hand side is negative, which is impossible for a squared length. The algebraic manipulation is therefore incorrect; the correct relation should be \\(MN^{2}=R^{2}-r^{2}\\). This error invalidates the subsequent claim that \\(\\Sigma\\) is orthogonal to both circles, because the orthogonality condition relies on the (incorrect) equality \\(OA^{2}= \\rho^{2}\\) derived from the faulty relation.\n\n---\n\n**Quote (continuation):**  \n\u201cSince \\(R>r\\) this forces\n\\[\nOA^{2}=OM^{2}-r^{2}=ON^{2}-R^{2}= \\rho^{2},\n\\]\ni.e. the circle \\(\\Sigma(O,\\rho)\\) is orthogonal to both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Justification Gap:**  \nEven if the sign error were corrected, the step \u201c\\(OA^{2}= \\rho^{2}\\)\u201d is asserted without showing that the radius \\(\\rho\\) of \\(\\Sigma\\) indeed equals the distance from \\(O\\) to \\(A\\). The definition of \\(\\Sigma\\) is \u201cthe circle through \\(A\\) whose centre is \\(O\\)\u201d; therefore \\(\\rho = OA\\) by definition, but the solution does not explicitly state this, nor does it verify that the orthogonality condition \\(\\rho^{2}=OM^{2}-r^{2}=ON^{2}-R^{2}\\) holds after correcting the sign. The gap leaves the orthogonality claim unproved.\n\n---\n\n### 2.3 The homothety centred at O  \n\n**Quote:**  \n\u201cBecause \\(\\Sigma\\) is orthogonal to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the line \\(OM\\) is the image of \\(ON\\) under the similarity that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\). Consequently the similarity \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(O\\) and ratio \\(\\dfrac{R}{r}\\) maps \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Justification Gap:**  \nOrthogonality of \\(\\Sigma\\) to the two circles implies that the