tion Gap:**  \nEven if the previous tangency claim were corrected, the step that the direction of \\(OD\\) equals the direction of \\(AP\\) relies on the unproved parallelism (8). Hence this conclusion rests on multiple earlier gaps.\n\n---\n\n**Quote:**  \n\u201cFinally, using the collinearity (9) we translate this tangency from the point \\(O\\) to the point \\(H\\): the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the image of the line through \\(O\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) under a translation along the line \\(PH\\); parallelism is preserved, so the former line is also tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Justification Gap / Logical flaw:**  \nTranslating a line that is tangent to a given circle does **not** in general produce another tangent line to the same circle; the circle does not move under the translation. The argument confuses preservation of direction with preservation of tangency. Therefore this step is invalid.\n\n---\n\n### 2.6 Conclusion  \n\nThe author acknowledges that the final step is incomplete, but the gaps identified above are essential to the proof. Moreover, the critical sign error and the false statement about a line through the centre being tangent constitute fatal flaws. Consequently the solution does not constitute a valid proof of the problem."
