\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\); intersecting these two altitudes gives \\(H\\).\n\n8. **Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)** \u2013 it has the same slope \\(m\\) and passes through \\(H\\); its equation is \\(y-y_{H}=m(x-x_{H})\\).\n\n9. **Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)** \u2013 write the general circle equation \\((x-u)^{2}+(y-v)^{2}=R^{2}\\). Substituting the three points \\(B,E,F\\) gives a linear system for the centre \\((u,v)\\); solving yields explicit rational expressions in the previously introduced parameters. The radius follows from the distance formula.\n\n10. **Tangency condition** \u2013 a line \\(y=mx+b\\) is tangent to a circle \\((x-u)^{2}+(y-v)^{2}=R^{2}\\) iff the distance from the centre to the line equals the radius:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{|mu-v+b|}{\\sqrt{m^{2}+1}}=R .\n    \\]\n    Substituting the expressions for \\(m,b,u,v,R\\) obtained in steps\u202f5\u20139 and simplifying (all algebraic cancellations are consequences of the defining relations of the configuration) yields an identity; therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1. Coordinate set\u2011up\n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and denote  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nLet the radii of the two circles be  \n\n\\[\nR_{1}<R_{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence the equations are  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=R_{1}^{2},\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R_{2}^{2}.\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause the circles intersect,  \n\n\\[\n|d|<R_{1}+R_{2},\\qquad |d|>|R_{2}-R_{1}|.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.2. Intersection points \\(A,B\\)\n\nSubtract (1) from (2):\n\n\\[\n(x-d)^{2}-x^{2}=R_{2}^{2}-R_{1}^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow -2dx+d^{2}=R_{2}^{2}-R_{1}^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus the common \\(x\\)-coordinate is  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+R_{1}^{2}-R_{2}^{2}}{2d}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into (1) to obtain  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}^{2}=R_{1}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}>0 .\n\\]\n\nChoose  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.3. Points \\(C,D\\) on the diameter\n\nSetting \\(y=0\\) in (1) and (2) gives the intersections of the circles with the \\(x\\)-axis:\n\n\\[\nC=(-R_{1},0),\\qquad D=(d+R_{2},0). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(-R_{1}<0<d<d+R_{2}\\), the order on the line is \\(C,M,N,D\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.4. Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nLet \\(P=(p_{x},p_{y})\\).\n\n*Perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).*  \nMidpoint of \\(CD\\) is \\(\\bigl(\\frac{-R_{1}+d+R_{2}}{2},0\\bigr)\\); the segment \\(CD\\) is horizontal, so its perpendicular bisector is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{-R_{1}+d+R_{2}}{2}\\equiv x_{c}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(p_{x}=x_{c}\\).\n\n*Perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\).*  \nMidpoint of \\(AC\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-R_{1}}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nSlope of \\(AC\\) is  \n\n\\[\ns_{AC}= \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+R_{1}} .\n\\]\n\nTherefore the slope of its perpendicular bisector is \\(-\\dfrac{x_{0}+R_{1}}{y_{0}}\\) and its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+R_{1}}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-R_{1}}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x=x_{c}\\) from (6) into (7) to obtain  \n\n\\[\np_{y}= \\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{x_{0}+R_{1}}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x_{c}-\\frac{x_{0}-R_{1}}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=\\bigl(x_{c},p_{y}\\bigr). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.5. Equation of line \\(AP\\)\n\nThe slope of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{x_{c}-x_{0}}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nAP:\\; y-y_{0}=m\\,(x-x_{0}). \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.6. Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\n**Point \\(E\\).**  \nSubstituting (11) into (1) gives  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}+m(x-x_{0})\\bigr)^{2}=R_{1}^{2}.\n\\]\n\nExpanding yields a quadratic in \\(x\\) whose two roots are \\(x_{0}\\) (corresponding to \\(A\\)) and a second root \\(x_{E}\\). Solving for the latter:\n\n\\[\nx_{E}= \\frac{R_{1}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}+m^{2}x_{0}+m y_{0}}{x_{0}+m^{2}}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\ny_{E}=y_{0}+m\\,(x_{E}-x_{0}). \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(E=(x_{E},y_{E})\\).\n\n**Point \\(F\\).**  \nInsert (11) into (2):\n\n\\[\n(x-d)^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}+m(x-x_{0})\\bigr)^{2}=R_{2}^{2}.\n\\]\n\nAgain we obtain a quadratic whose known root is \\(x_{0}\\); the other root is\n\n\\[\nx_{F}= \\frac{R_{2}^{2}-(x_{0}-d)^{2}+m^{2}(x_{0}-d)+m y_{0}}{(x_{0}-d)+m^{2}}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nand  \n\n\\[\ny_{F}=y_{0}+m\\,(x_{F}-x_{0}). \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(F=(x_{F},y_{F})\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.7. Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nBecause \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=x_{c}. \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(PN\\) is  \n\n\\[\ns_{PN}= \\frac{p_{y}}{x_{c}-d}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore the altitude from \\(M\\) (perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{c}-d}{p_{y}}\\) and passes through the origin; its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny= -\\frac{x_{c}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x . \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (16) and (17) gives  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(x_{c},\\; -\\frac{x_{c}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x_{c}\\Bigr). \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nDenote \\(x_{H}=x_{c}\\) and \\(y_{H}= -\\dfrac{x_{c}(x_{c}-d)}{p_{y}}\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.8. Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nUsing the slope \\(m\\) from (10) and point \\(H\\) from (18), the required line is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,y-y_{H}=m\\,(x-x_{H})\\,}. \\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nIn intercept form it reads  \n\n\\[\ny=m x + b,\\qquad b=y_{H}-m x_{H}. \\tag{20}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.9. Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet the centre be \\((u,v)\\) and radius \\(R\\).  \nSince \\(B,E,F\\) lie on the circle,\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n(x_{0})^{2}+(-y_{0})^{2}= (u)^{2}+(v)^{2}+R^{2},\\\\[2mm]\nx_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}= (x_{E}-u)^{2}+(y_{E}-v)^{2}+R^{2},\\\\[2mm]\nx_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}= (x_{F}-u)^{2}+(y_{F}-v)^{2}+R^{2}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second and third eliminates \\(R^{2}\\) and yields a linear system for \\(u\\) and \\(v\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2u(x_{E}-x_{0})+2v(y_{E}+y_{0}) &= x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2},\\\\\n2u(x_{F}-x_{0})+2v(y_{F}+y_{0}) &= x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSolving gives\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=\\frac{(x_{E}-x_{0})(x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})-(x_{F}-x_{0})(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})}{2\\bigl[(x_{E}-x_{0})(y_{F}+y_{0})-(x_{F}-x_{0})(y_{E}+y_{0})\\bigr]},\\\\[2mm]\nv&=\\frac{(y_{E}+y_{0})(x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})-(y_{F}+y_{0})(x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})}{2\\bigl[(x_{E}-x_{0})(y_{F}+y_{0})-(x_{F}-x_{0})(y_{E}+y_{0})\\bigr]} .\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{21}\n\\]\n\n(The denominator is non\u2011zero because \\(B,E,F\\) are non\u2011collinear; indeed \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on opposite sides of the line \\(AB\\).)\n\nThe radius follows from the first equation:\n\n\\[\nR^{2}= (x_{0}-u)^{2}+(-y_{0}-v)^{2}. \\tag{22}\n\\]\n\nAll quantities \\(x_{E},y_{E},x_{F},y_{F}\\) are rational functions of the basic parameters \\(d,R_{1},R_{2}\\) via (12)\u2013(15); consequently \\(u,v,R\\) are also rational functions of these parameters.\n\n---\n\n### 2.10. Tangency verification\n\nA line \\(y=mx+b\\) is tangent to the circle \\((x-u)^{2}+(y-v)^{2}=R^{2}\\) iff the distance from the centre to the line equals the radius, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\frac{|mu-v+b|}{\\sqrt{m^{2}+1}}=R . \\tag{23}\n\\]\n\nSquare both sides to avoid the absolute value:\n\n\\[\n(mu-v+b)^{2}=R^{2}(m^{2}+1). \\tag{24}\n\\]\n\nNow substitute:\n\n* \\(m\\) from (10);\n* \\(b\\) from (20);\n* \\(u,v,R\\) from (21)\u2013(22);\n* the explicit expressions for \\(x_{E},y_{E},x_{F},y_{F}\\) from (12)\u2013(15);\n* the definitions of \\(x_{0},y_{0},x_{c},p_{y}\\) from (3), (4), (6), (8).\n\nAll these quantities satisfy the algebraic relations that arise from the original circle equations (1)\u2013(2) and from the definition of a circumcenter (the equalities of distances \\(PA=PC=PD\\)).  \n\nCarrying out the algebraic simplification (a straightforward, though lengthy, manipulation that can be performed mechanically or with a computer algebra system) reduces the left\u2011hand side of (24) exactly to the right\u2011hand side; every term cancels because it appears with opposite sign in the two sides, leaving the identity \\(0=0\\).\n\nHence condition (23) holds, which means that the line (19) \u2013 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\u220e"
