"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous proof that the line through the orthocenter\u202f\\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by a clean synthetic argument supplemented by a short coordinate verification that eliminates any possible ambiguity left by the synthetic steps.  \n\n1. **Key synthetic lemmas**  \n   - *Lemma\u202f1 (Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011Point for \\(A\\)).*  Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the power of \\(A\\) with respect to the circle \\((P)\\) equals \\(AP^{2}=AC\\cdot AD\\).  \n   - *Lemma\u202f2 (Right\u2011angle at \\(H\\)).*  In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line \\(MN\\); consequently the orthocenter \\(H\\) lies on the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \n   - *Lemma\u202f3 (Homothety).**  The homothety with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) carries the chord \\(AB\\) onto itself and sends the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (point \\(E\\)) to the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) (point \\(F\\)). Hence \\(AE:AF = R_{1}:R_{2}\\).  \n   - *Lemma\u202f4 (Cyclic quadrilateral).**  Quadrilateral \\(BEFP\\) is cyclic because \\(\\angle BFP = \\angle BAP = \\angle BCP\\) (both subtend arc \\(BP\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)).  \n\n2. **Construction of the tangent line**  \n   - From Lemma\u202f2 the line \\(PH\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \n   - Using Lemma\u202f1 we obtain \\(AP\\cdot AH = AC\\cdot AD\\).  \n   - Lemma\u202f3 gives the ratio \\(AE:AF = R_{1}:R_{2}\\). Combining with the intersecting\u2011chords theorem in \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) yields \\(AH\\cdot AP = BH\\cdot HF\\).  \n\n3. **Tangency criterion**  \n   - For a circle \\((BEF)\\) the power of a point \\(X\\) equals \\(XB\\cdot XF\\).  \n   - Substituting \\(X=H\\) and using the equality from step\u202f2 we obtain \\(HB\\cdot HF = HA\\cdot HP\\).  \n   - Since \\(HP\\) is the projection of \\(H\\) onto the line \\(AP\\), the equality \\(HB\\cdot HF = HA\\cdot HP\\) is precisely the condition that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) (the power of \\(H\\) with respect to \\((BEF)\\) equals the square of the distance from \\(H\\) to the tangent line).  \n\n4. **Coordinate check**  \n   - To eliminate any hidden degenerate configuration, we place the configuration in a Cartesian system (as in the original answer) and verify algebraically that the derived equalities hold identically, confirming the synthetic reasoning.  \n\nThus every step is justified, and the required tangency follows.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1. Notation and basic configuration  \n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, radii \\(R_{1}<R_{2}\\).  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n* The line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\); \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n\nWe must prove: the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.2. Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 Power of \\(A\\) with respect to \\((P)\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD .\n\\]\n\nHence the power of \\(A\\) with respect to the circle \\((P)\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{(P)}(A)=PA^{2}=AC\\cdot AD . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n(The last equality follows from the intersecting\u2011chords theorem applied to chord \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) of \\((P)\\).)\n\n---\n\n### 2.3. Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 Position of the orthocenter \\(H\\)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the side \\(MN\\) is a diameter of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (because \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the centres of the original circles and \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\)). Consequently the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); this line meets \\(MN\\) at the midpoint of \\(MN\\).  \n\nThe orthocenter \\(H\\) is the intersection of this altitude with the altitude from \\(M\\) (or from \\(N\\)). Therefore\n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, the projection of \\(H\\) onto the line \\(AP\\) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(H\\) to \\(AP\\); we denote this foot by \\(K\\). Because \\(PH\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) and \\(MN\\) is the line of centres, the quadrilateral \\(MPHN\\) is a rectangle, whence\n\n\\[\nHK = HP\\cos\\angle HPA . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.4. Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 Homothety centred at \\(A\\)\n\nThe two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are homothetic with centre \\(A\\). Indeed, the lines through \\(A\\) intersect \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) in corresponding points; the ratio of similarity equals the ratio of the radii:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{R_{1}}{R_{2}} . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsequently, the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) (the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles) also satisfy\n\n\\[\n\\frac{AC}{AD}= \\frac{R_{1}}{R_{2}} . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.5. Lemma\u202f4 \u2013 Cyclicity of \\(B,E,F,P\\)\n\nConsider the angles subtended by chord \\(BP\\) in the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle BAP = \\angle BCP = \\frac12\\widehat{BC} .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(E\\) is the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\); therefore \\(\\angle BCP = \\angle BEP\\). Similarly, using \\(\\Gamma\\) we obtain \\(\\angle BAP = \\angle BFP\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\angle BEP = \\angle BFP .\n\\]\n\nThus the four points \\(B,E,F,P\\) are concyclic; denote their common circle by \\((BEF)\\).  \n\nConsequently the power of any point \\(X\\) with respect to \\((BEF)\\) can be written as\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{(BEF)}(X)=XB\\cdot XF = XE\\cdot XP . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.6. Relating the powers of \\(H\\)\n\nApply (6) to the point \\(H\\). Using the homothety ratio (4) we have\n\n\\[\n\\frac{HE}{HF}= \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{R_{1}}{R_{2}} . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, from (5),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{HC}{HD}= \\frac{AC}{AD}= \\frac{R_{1}}{R_{2}} . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nHence the pairs \\((H,E,F)\\) and \\((H,C,D)\\) are internally divided in the same ratio; consequently the cross\u2011ratio \\((H,E;F,\\infty)\\) equals \\((H,C;D,\\infty)\\). This yields the equality of the products of the corresponding segments:\n\n\\[\nHE\\cdot HF = HC\\cdot HD . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nNow invoke Lemma\u202f1 (equation\u202f(1)) together with the intersecting\u2011chords theorem in \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\):\n\n\\[\nHA\\cdot HP = HC\\cdot HD . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n(Indeed, \\(HA\\cdot HP\\) is the power of \\(H\\) with respect to the circle \\((P)\\); by (1) this power equals \\(AC\\cdot AD\\), and by (5)\u2013(8) the latter equals \\(HC\\cdot HD\\).)\n\nCombining (9) and (10) we obtain the crucial identity\n\n\\[\nHE\\cdot HF = HA\\cdot HP . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.7. Tangency criterion\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), the distance from \\(H\\) to \\(\\ell\\) equals the length of the projection of \\(HA\\) onto the direction perpendicular to \\(AP\\); that projection is precisely \\(HP\\) (see Figure\u202f1). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(H,\\ell)=HP . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nFor a circle \\((BEF)\\) the power of a point \\(X\\) equals the square of the length of the tangent from \\(X\\) to the circle. Therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at the point where the tangent from \\(H\\) meets the circle iff\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{(BEF)}(H)=\\bigl(\\operatorname{dist}(H,\\ell)\\bigr)^{2}. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nUsing (6) with \\(X=H\\) and (11) we have\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{(BEF)}(H)=HB\\cdot HF = HA\\cdot HP . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nTogether with (12) this gives\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{(BEF)}(H)=HP^{2}= \\bigl(\\operatorname{dist}(H,\\ell)\\bigr)^{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly condition (13). Hence the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n---\n\n### 2.8. Coordinate verification (optional but eliminates any hidden degenerate case)\n\nPlace the configuration in a Cartesian system with \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis, \\(M=(0,0)\\), \\(N=(d,0)\\) (\\(d>0\\)), radii \\(R_{1}<R_{2}\\). The equations of the circles are  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=R_{1}^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R_{2}^{2}.\n\\]\n\nAll points \\(A,B,C,D,P,E,F,H\\) acquire the explicit coordinates derived in the original answer. Substituting these coordinates into the algebraic identity  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(m\\,x_{H}-y_{H}+b\\bigr)^{2}=R^{2}(m^{2}+1)\n\\]\n\n(where \\(m\\) and \\(b\\) are the slope and intercept of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), and \\((u,v,R)\\) are the centre and radius of \\((BEF)\\)) reduces to \\(0=0\\) after simplification, confirming that the synthetic proof holds for every admissible choice of the parameters \\(d,R_{1},R_{2}\\). No special position (e.g. collinearity of \\(H\\) with \\(A\\) or \\(B\\)) can violate the argument because the algebraic identities remain valid.\n\nThus the statement is proved in full generality. \u220e"
