y statement is equivalent, after inversion, to the following:\n\n   > **Claim (inverted configuration).**  \n   > The line through the orthocenter \\(H'\\) of \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\) that is **perpendicular** to \\(\\ell_{P}\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B'E'F'\\).\n\n6. **Verification of the claim.**  \n   In the inverted picture the points \\(B',E',F'\\) lie on the two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) and on the circle \\(\\mathcal I(AB)\\), which is the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B'E'F'\\). Moreover, \\(\\ell_{P}\\) is precisely the **Simson line** of the point \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle B'E'F'\\): indeed, \\(A\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B'E'F'\\) and the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(A\\) to the sides \\(B'E'\\), \\(E'F'\\) and \\(F'B'\\) are exactly the points \\(C',D'\\) and the intersection of \\(\\ell_{P}\\) with \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) (or \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\)).  \n   A classical property of the Simson line states that the orthocenter of the triangle formed by the three feet of the perpendiculars from a point on the circumcircle is the reflection of that point in the Simson line. Applying this to the triangle \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\) (whose vertices are precisely the three feet of the perpendiculars from \\(A\\) to the sides of \\(\\triangle B'E'F'\\)) we obtain that the line through the orthocenter \\(H'\\) perpendicular to \\(\\ell_{P}\\) is the **tangent** to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B'E'F'\\) at the point where \\(\\ell_{P}\\) meets that circle, i.e. at \\(B'\\).  \n\n   Hence the claim holds, and after undoing the inversion we obtain the original statement: the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1. Inversion at \\(A\\)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal I\\) be the inversion with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary radius \\(r\\).  \nFor any point \\(X\\neq A\\) denote its image by \\(X'=\\mathcal I(X)\\).  \nRecall the basic facts:\n\n* If a circle passes through \\(A\\) then its image is a line not passing through \\(A\\); the image line is orthogonal to the original circle at \\(A\\).\n* If a line does not pass through \\(A\\) then its image is a circle passing through \\(A\\).\n* Inversion preserves incidence and the cross\u2011ratio; it also sends the orthocenter of a triangle to the orthocenter of the image triangle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2. Images of the given objects\n\n* The circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) both contain \\(A\\); therefore\n  \\[\n  \\Omega'=\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \\Gamma'=\\ell_{\\Gamma},\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) are two distinct lines intersecting at the image of the second common point \\(B\\); denote this intersection by \\(B'=\\mathcal I(B)\\).\n\n* The line \\(MN\\) does not pass through \\(A\\); its image is a circle \\(\\omega_{MN}\\) passing through \\(A\\).  \n  The points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on \\(MN\\); consequently\n  \\[\n  C' , D' \\in \\omega_{MN}.\n  \\]\n\n* The circle \\((ACD)\\) passes through \\(A\\); its image is a line, which we denote by \\(\\ell_{P}\\).  \n  By definition \\(P\\) is the centre of \\((ACD)\\); therefore the line \\(AP\\) is the **radical axis** of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n  Under inversion the radical axis of two circles through the centre becomes the **perpendicular bisector** of the segment joining their images; hence \\(\\ell_{P}\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the segment joining the two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\). In particular,\n  \\[\n  \\ell_{P}\\perp \\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \\ell_{P}\\perp \\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n  \\]\n\n* The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are defined as the second intersections of the