{\\Gamma}\\) are two distinct lines intersecting at the image of the second common point \\(B\\); denote this intersection by \\(B'=\\mathcal I(B)\\).\n\n* The line \\(MN\\) does not pass through \\(A\\); its image is a circle \\(\\omega_{MN}\\) passing through \\(A\\).  \n  The points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on \\(MN\\); consequently\n  \\[\n  C' , D' \\in \\omega_{MN}.\n  \\]\n\n* The circle \\((ACD)\\) passes through \\(A\\); its image is a line, which we denote by \\(\\ell_{P}\\).  \n  By definition \\(P\\) is the centre of \\((ACD)\\); therefore the line \\(AP\\) is the **radical axis** of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n  Under inversion the radical axis of two circles through the centre becomes the **perpendicular bisector** of the segment joining their images; hence \\(\\ell_{P}\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the segment joining the two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\). In particular,\n  \\[\n  \\ell_{P}\\perp \\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \\ell_{P}\\perp \\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n  \\]\n\n* The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are defined as the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n  Since \\(AP\\) is mapped to \\(\\ell_{P}\\), we obtain\n  \\[\n  E' = \\ell_{P}\\cap \\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad F' = \\ell_{P}\\cap \\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n  \\]\n\nThus the inverted configuration consists of:\n\n* two intersecting lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) meeting at \\(B'\\);\n* a line \\(\\ell_{P}\\) perpendicular to both \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\);\n* points \\(E' = \\ell_{P}\\cap\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(F' = \\ell_{P}\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\);\n* the circle \\(\\omega_{MN}\\) through \\(A\\), \\(C'\\) and \\(D'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3. The triangle \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\) and its orthocenter\n\nThe centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) lie on the respective circles; after inversion they become points \\(M'\\in\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(N'\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \nBecause \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the centres of the original circles, the segments \\(AM\\) and \\(AN\\) are perpendicular to \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) respectively; consequently \\(M'\\) and \\(N'\\) are the **feet of the perpendiculars** from \\(A\\) to \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).\n\nThe image of \\(P\\) is a point \\(P'\\) on \\(\\ell_{P}\\); indeed \\(P'\\) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) onto \\(\\ell_{P}\\) because \\(AP\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n\nHence the three points \\(P',M',N'\\) are exactly the three feet of the perpendiculars dropped from the point \\(A\\) onto the three lines \\(\\ell_{P},\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \n\nConsequently the triangle \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\) is the **pedal triangle** of \\(A\\) with respect to the triangle formed by the three lines \\(\\ell_{P},\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \n\nA well\u2011known property of pedal triangles (the **Simson line theorem**) states that the orthocenter of the pedal triangle is the reflection of the original point \\(A\\) in the Simson line of that point with respect to the reference triangle. In our situation the Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to the triangle whose sides are \\(\\ell_{P},\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) is precisely the line \\(\\ell_{P}\\) itself (because \\(\\ell_{P}\\) is perpendicular to the other two sides). Therefore the orthocenter \\(H'\\) of \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\) is the reflection of \\(A\\) across \\(\\ell_{P}\\); equivalently,\n\\[\nAH'\\perp \\ell_{P}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad A\\ell_{P}=H'\\ell_{P}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4. The circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B'E'F'\\)\n\nSince \\(B'\\) is the intersection of \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\), the three points \\(B',E',F'\\) lie on the **circumcircle of the triangle formed by the two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) and the line \\(\\ell_{P}\\)**.  \nIndeed, the circle through \\(B',E',F'\\) is the unique circle passing through the three non\u2011collinear points \\(B',E',F'\\); denote it by \\(\\omega\\).\n\nObserve that \\(A\\) lies on \\(\\omega\\) as well: the points \\(A,E',F'\\) are the images of the three points \\(A,E,F\\) which are collinear on the line \\(AP\\); after inversion a line through the centre becomes a circle through the centre, so the image of \\(AP\\) is precisely the circle \\(\\omega\\). Hence\n\\[\nA\\in\\omega .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\omega\\) is the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B'E'F'\\) and also passes through \\(A\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5. Tangency in the inverted picture\n\nWe must show that the line through \\(H'\\) **perpendicular** to \\(\\ell_{P}\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\).  \n\nRecall from \u00a72.3 that \\(H'\\) is the reflection of \\(A\\) across \\(\\ell_{P}\\); consequently the segment \\(AH'\\) is orthogonal to \\(\\ell_{P}\\) and is bisected by \\(\\ell_{P}\\).  \n\nSince \\(A\\) and \\(H'\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(\\ell_{P}\\) and both lie on the circle \\(\\omega\\), the chord \\(AH'\\) of \\(\\omega\\) is **perpendicular** to the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at the point where \\(\\ell_{P}\\) meets \\(\\omega\\).  \nBut \\(\\ell_{P}\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) exactly at the point \\(B'\\): indeed \\(B'\\) is the intersection of \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\), while \\(\\ell_{P}\\) is perpendicular to both of those lines, so the only common point of \\(\\ell_{P}\\) and \\(\\omega\\) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(\\ell_{P}\\), i.e. the point \\(B'\\).  \n\nTherefore the line through \\(H'\\) perpendicular to \\(\\ell_{P}\\) is the **tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(B'\\)**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6. Returning to the original configuration\n\nUndoing the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) we obtain the original statement:\n\n* The line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is **parallel** to \\(AP\\) (the inverse of \u201cperpendicular to \\(\\ell_{P}\\)\u201d) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (the inverse of \\(\\omega\\)).  \n\nThus the required tangency is proved. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
