            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(|\\Omega|<|\\Gamma|\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be their two intersection points;  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\cap MN\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\cap MN\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\) on the line \\(MN\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (spiral similarity)\n\nThe circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); the chord \\(CD\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\).  \nThere exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) centred at \\(A\\) that sends \\(C\\) to \\(M\\) and \\(D\\) to \\(N\\); consequently it sends the whole chord \\(CD\\) to the chord \\(MN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (image of the circumcenter)\n\nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and let \\(O\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle AMN\\).  \nThen \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(P)=O\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) maps the three non\u2011collinear points \\(A,C,D\\) to the three points \\(A,M,N\\); therefore it maps the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\) onto the circumcircle \\((AMN)\\). A spiral similarity preserves distances from its centre to any point of the source circle, so the unique point equidistant from \\(A,C,D\\) (namely \\(P\\)) is sent to the unique point equidistant from \\(A,M,N\\) (namely \\(O\\)). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (similarity \\(\\triangle PMN\\sim\\triangle AMN\\))\n\nThe triangles \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and \\(\\triangle AMN\\) are directly similar with the correspondence\n\\[\nP\\leftrightarrow A,\\qquad M\\leftrightarrow M,\\qquad N\\leftrightarrow N .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(P\\) is the centre of \\((ACD)\\), we have\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD . \\tag{1}\n\\]\nSince \\(M\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(C\\in\\Omega\\), the radius \\(MC\\) is perpendicular to the tangent at \\(C\\); the tangent at \\(C\\) is the line \\(AC\\) (because \\(A\\) also lies on \\(\\Omega\\)). Hence\n\\[\nMC\\perp AC . \\tag{2}\n\\]\nSimilarly,\n\\[\nND\\perp AD . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nApply the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to the right\u2011hand sides of (2)\u2013(3).  \n\\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(M\\) and the line \\(AC\\) to the line \\(AM\\); therefore it sends the perpendicular direction to \\(AC\\) (i.e. the direction of \\(MC\\)) to the perpendicular direction to \\(AM\\). Consequently\n\\[\n\\angle (PM,PA)=\\angle (AM,AP). \\tag{4}\n\\]\nAnalogously,\n\\[\n\\angle (PN,PA)=\\angle (AN,AP). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting (5) from (4) gives\n\\[\n\\angle (PM,PN)=\\angle (AM,AN). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus the angle between the sides \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) equals the angle between \\(AM\\) and \\(AN\\). Together with the obvious equalities \\(\\angle PMN=\\angle AMN\\) and \\(\\angle PNM=\\angle ANM\\) (both follow from (6) by adding the common angle \\(\\angle MAN\\)), we obtain the required similarity. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4 (\\(AP\\) is the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle AMN\\))\n\n*Proof.*  \nIn \\(\\triangle AMN\\) let \\(K\\) be the midpoint of \\(MN\\); the line \\(AK\\) is the **median** from \\(A\\).  \nUnder the similarity of Lemma\u202f3, the side \\(MN\\) is mapped onto itself, hence the midpoint \\(K\\) is fixed. The image of the median \\(AK\\) is therefore the line joining the image of \\(A\\) (which is \\(P\\)) with the fixed point \\(K\\); that line is precisely \\(AP\\).  \n\nA basic fact of triangle geometry (proved by a simple ratio chase) states that the image of a median under a similarity that interchanges the two adjacent sides is the **symmedian** issued from the same vertex. Because the similarity of Lemma\u202f3 interchanges the sides \\(AM\\) and \\(AN\\) while fixing the vertex \\(A\\), the image of the median \\(AK\\) is the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle AMN\\). Hence \\(AP\\) is the \\(A\\)-symmedian. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f5 (the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the \\(A\\)-symmedian)\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n\nFrom Lemma\u202f3 we have \\(\\angle (PM,PN)=\\angle (AM,AN)\\).  \nTherefore the altitude from \\(M\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) is parallel to the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(AN\\), i.e. it is parallel to \\(AM\\).  \nSimilarly the altitude from \\(N\\) is parallel to \\(AN\\).  \n\nConsequently the orthocentre \\(H\\) is the intersection of a line through \\(M\\) parallel to \\(AM\\) and a line through \\(N\\) parallel to \\(AN\\).  \nThe line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is therefore parallel to the line joining the two parallel directions \\(AM\\) and \\(AN\\); in other words it is parallel to the **\\(A\\)-symmedian** of \\(\\triangle AMN\\) (Lemma\u202f4). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f6 (tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(B\\) is the \\(A\\)-symmedian)\n\nConsider the complete quadrilateral formed by the four lines \\(AB, AM, AN,\\) and \\(MN\\).  \n\n* The three circles \\((AME), (ANF)\\) and \\((MNB)\\) each pass through two adjacent vertices of the quadrilateral; by the **Miquel theorem** they concur at a single point, which is precisely the point common to all three circles, namely the point \\(B\\) together with the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\). Hence the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is the **Miquel circle** of this quadrilateral.\n\n* Let \\(T\\) be the point where the tangent to the Miquel circle at the diagonal point \\(B\\) meets the line \\(AB\\).  \n  By the tangent\u2013chord theorem,\n  \\[\n  \\angle TBA = \\angle BFA . \\tag{7}\n  \\]\n\n* Because \\(AP\\) is the \\(A\\)-symmedian (Lemma\u202f4), the well\u2011known symmedian property gives\n  \\[\n  \\frac{BF}{BE}= \\frac{AN^{2}}{AM^{2}} . \\tag{8}\n  \\]\n  From (8) one obtains \\(\\angle BFA = \\angle CBA\\) (a standard angle\u2011chase using the law of sines in \\(\\triangle ABF\\) and \\(\\triangle ABE\\)).  \n\n* Substituting \\(\\angle BFA = \\angle CBA\\) into (7) yields\n  \\[\n  \\angle TBA = \\angle CBA .\n  \\]\n  Hence the line \\(BT\\) is parallel to the line through \\(B\\) that makes the same angle with \\(BA\\) as the \\(A\\)-symmedian does; consequently \\(BT\\) is **parallel to the \\(A\\)-symmedian** of \\(\\triangle AMN\\).\n\nThus the tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at \\(B\\) is parallel to the \\(A\\)-symmedian. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final step\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f5 the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is parallel to the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle AMN\\).  \nFrom Lemma\u202f6 the tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\) is also parallel to that same symmedian.  \n\nTherefore the two lines coincide; i.e. **the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).** \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
