gles \\(\\angle CAM = \\angle DAN\\). No gap here.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma\u202f2 (image of the circumcenter).**  \nThe argument that a spiral similarity maps the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) onto that of \\(\\triangle AMN\\) is valid. The claim that it sends the unique point equidistant from \\(A,C,D\\) to the unique point equidistant from \\(A,M,N\\) is also correct, provided one accepts that a spiral similarity preserves distances from its centre to points on the source circle. This is a standard property, so the lemma is acceptable.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma\u202f3 (similarity \\(\\triangle PMN\\sim\\triangle AMN\\)).**  \n\n1. **Step:** \u201cApply the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to the right\u2011hand sides of (2)\u2013(3). \u2026 consequently \\(\\angle (PM,PA)=\\angle (AM,AP)\\).\u201d  \n   **Issue \u2013 Justification Gap:** The spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) is defined only on points, not on directed lines such as \\(PM\\) or \\(PA\\). To deduce an angle equality one must first show that \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the line \\(PM\\) to the line \\(AM\\) (or that it sends the direction of \\(PM\\) to that of \\(AM\\)). This requires proving that \\(P\\) maps to \\(O\\) (Lemma\u202f2) and that the image of the line through \\(P\\) and \\(M\\) is the line through \\(O\\) and \\(M\\). The solution skips these intermediate steps, leaving the angle equality unjustified.\n\n2. **Step:** \u201cSubtracting (5) from (4) gives \\(\\angle (PM,PN)=\\angle (AM,AN)\\).\u201d  \n   **Issue \u2013 Justification Gap:** The subtraction of oriented angles is not explained; one must verify that the orientations are compatible and that the resulting equality indeed follows. Moreover, the connection between these angle equalities and the similarity of the two triangles is not made explicit.\n\n3. **Step:** \u201cTogether with the obvious equalities \\(\\angle PMN=\\angle AMN\\) and \\(\\angle PNM=\\angle ANM\\) (both follow from (6) by adding the common angle \\(