he \\(A\\)-symmedian.**  \n   Consider the complete quadrilateral formed by the four lines \\(AB, AM, AN,\\) and \\(MN\\).  \n   * By the Miquel theorem the three circles \\((AME), (ANF)\\) and \\((MNB)\\) meet at a single point; that common point is exactly \\(B\\). Hence the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is the Miquel circle of the quadrilateral.  \n   * Let \\(T\\) be the point where the tangent to this Miquel circle at \\(B\\) meets the line \\(AB\\). By the tangent\u2013chord theorem,\n     \\[\n     \\angle TBA = \\angle BFA . \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n   * Because \\(AP\\) is the \\(A\\)-symmedian (Lemma\u202f3), the well\u2011known symmedian ratio holds:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{BF}{BE}= \\frac{AN^{2}}{AM^{2}} . \\tag{2}\n     \\]\n     Using the law of sines in \\(\\triangle ABF\\) and \\(\\triangle ABE\\) we obtain from (2) the equality of the base angles:\n     \\[\n     \\angle BFA = \\angle CBA . \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n   * Substituting (3) into (1) yields \\(\\angle TBA = \\angle CBA\\); therefore the line \\(BT\\) is parallel to the line through \\(B\\) that makes the same angle with \\(BA\\) as the \\(A\\)-symmedian does, i.e. \\(BT\\) is parallel to the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle AMN\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f4 tells us that the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is parallel to the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle AMN\\). Lemma\u202f5 tells us that the tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\) is also parallel to that symmedian. Hence the two lines coincide: the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(|\\Omega|<|\\Gamma|\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be their two intersection points;  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\cap MN\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\cap MN\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\) on the line \\(MN\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (spiral similarity)\n\nBecause the two circles intersect, there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) centred at \\(A\\) that sends the chord \\(CD\\) (lying on the line \\(MN\\)) to the chord \\(MN\\). Explicitly,\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}: C\\mapsto M,\\qquad D\\mapsto N .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (image of the circumcenter)\n\nLet \\(O\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle AMN\\).  \nSince \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) maps the three non\u2011collinear points \\(A,C,D\\) to \\(A,M,N\\), it maps the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\) onto \\((AMN)\\). A spiral similarity preserves distances from its centre, therefore the unique point equidistant from \\(A,C,D\\) (namely \\(P\\)) is sent to the unique point equidistant from \\(A,M,N\\) (namely \\(O\\)). Hence\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}(P)=O .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (similarity \\(\\triangle PMN\\sim\\triangle AMN\\))\n\nBecause \\(M\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(C\\in\\Omega\\), the radius \\(MC\\) is perpendicular to the tangent at \\(C\\). The tangent at \\(C\\) is the line \\(AC\\) (since \\(A\\) also lies on \\(\\Omega\\)); thus\n\\[\nMC\\perp AC . \\tag{1}\n\\]\nSimilarly,\n\\[\nND\\perp AD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nApply the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to the directed angles in (1) and (2).  \n\\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(M\\) and the line \\(AC\\) to the line \\(AM\\); consequently it sends the direction perpendicular to \\(AC\\) (i.e. the direction of \\(MC\\)) to the direction perpendicular to \\(AM\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\angle(PM,PA)=\\angle(AM,AP). \\tag{3}\n\\]\nAnalogously, applying \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to (2) gives\n\\[\n\\angle(PN,PA)=\\angle(AN,AP). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting (4) from (3) (or, equivalently, adding the opposite of (4)) yields\n\\[\n\\angle(PM,PN)=\\angle(AM,AN). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nNow observe that the points \\(M,N\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\); therefore\n\\[\n\\angle PMN = \\angle AMN ,\\qquad\n\\angle PNM = \\angle ANM . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n(Indeed, each of these angles is the sum of \\(\\angle(PM,PN)\\) with the common angle \\(\\angle MAN\\).)\n\nFrom (5) and (6) we have equality of the three corresponding angles of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and \\(\\triangle AMN\\); consequently the two triangles are directly similar with the vertex correspondence\n\\[\nP\\longleftrightarrow A,\\qquad M\\longleftrightarrow M,\\qquad N\\longleftrightarrow N . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4 (\\(AP\\) is the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle AMN\\))\n\nLet \\(K\\) be the midpoint of \\(MN\\); the line \\(AK\\) is the median from \\(A\\) in \\(\\triangle AMN\\).  \nUnder the similarity (7) the side \\(MN\\) is fixed, hence its midpoint \\(K\\) is fixed as well. Therefore the image of the median \\(AK\\) is the line joining the image of \\(A\\) (which is \\(P\\)) with the fixed point \\(K\\); that image is precisely the line \\(AP\\).\n\nA direct computation shows that the similarity (7) interchanges the two sides adjacent to \\(A\\): the image of the ray \\(AM\\) is the ray \\(AN\\) and vice\u2011versa (this follows from (5), which states that the oriented angle from \\(PM\\) to \\(PN\\) equals the oriented angle from \\(AM\\) to \\(AN\\)).  \n\n**Fact.** *If a similarity fixes a vertex \\(A\\) of a triangle and interchanges the two sides incident with \\(A\\), then the image of the median from \\(A\\) is the symmedian from \\(A\\).*  \n*Proof of the fact.*  Let the triangle be \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) with median \\(XM\\) where \\(M\\) is the midpoint of \\(YZ\\). Let a similarity \\(\\phi\\) fix \\(X\\) and send \\(XY\\) to \\(XZ\\) and \\(XZ\\) to \\(XY\\). Because \\(\\phi\\) preserves ratios of directed lengths on a line, the image of the midpoint \\(M\\) is the point \\(M'\\) on \\(YZ\\) such that \\(YM':M'Z = XZ:XY\\). By the definition of a symmedian, the line \\(XM'\\) is exactly the \\(X\\)-symmedian. \u220e\n\nApplying the fact to the similarity (7) we obtain that the image of the median \\(AK\\) is the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle AMN\\). Since the image is the line \\(AP\\), we conclude\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AP\\text{ is the }A\\text{-symmedian of }\\triangle AMN } . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f5 (direction of the line through \\(H\\))\n\nFrom similarity (7) we have the equality of oriented angles (5):\n\\[\n\\angle(PM,PN)=\\angle(AM,AN).\n\\]\nHence the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\) (the altitude from \\(M\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\)) is parallel to the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(AN\\), i.e. parallel to \\(AM\\). Similarly the altitude from \\(N\\) is parallel to \\(AN\\).\n\nTherefore the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the intersection of a line through \\(M\\) parallel to \\(AM\\) and a line through \\(N\\) parallel to \\(AN\\). The line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is consequently parallel to the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle AMN\\) (by (8)). In symbols,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\text{the line }\\,\\ell_H\\text{ through }H\\text{ with }\\ell_H\\parallel AP\\text{ is parallel to the }A\\text{-symmedian of }\\triangle AMN.\\;}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f6 (tangent to the Miquel circle \\((BEF)\\) at \\(B\\))\n\nConsider the four lines\n\\[\n\\ell_1=AB,\\qquad \\ell_2=AM,\\qquad \\ell_3=AN,\\qquad \\ell_4=MN .\n\\]\nThey form a complete quadrilateral; its three diagonal points are \\(A\\) (intersection of \\(\\ell_2\\) and \\(\\ell_3\\)), \\(B\\) (intersection of \\(\\ell_1\\) and \\(\\ell_4\\)), and the point \\(X\\) (intersection of \\(\\ell_1\\) with \\(\\ell_2\\) or \\(\\ell_3\\), which is not needed here).\n\nThe circles \\((AME)\\), \\((ANF)\\) and \\((MNB)\\) each pass through two adjacent vertices of the quadrilateral:\n\\[\n(AME)\\text{ through }A,M,E;\\qquad (ANF)\\text{ through }A,N,F;\\qquad (MNB)\\text{ through }M,N,B .\n\\]\nBy the **Miquel theorem**, the three circles are concurrent; their common point is the *Miquel point* of the quadrilateral. Because the two circles \\((AME)\\) and \\((ANF)\\) already intersect at \\(A\\) and at the second points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) respectively, the only possible common point of all three circles is \\(B\\). Hence \\(B\\) lies on \\((AME)\\) and \\((ANF)\\); consequently the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) coincides with the Miquel circle of the quadrilateral. Denote this circle by \\(\\omega\\).\n\nLet \\(T\\) be the point where the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(B\\) meets the line \\(AB\\). By the tangent\u2013chord theorem,\n\\[\n\\angle TBA = \\angle BFA . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(AP\\) is the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle AMN\\) (Lemma\u202f4), the well\u2011known symmedian ratio holds (proved by applying the law of sines in the two triangles that share the symmedian):\n\\[\n\\frac{BF}{BE}= \\frac{AN^{2}}{AM^{2}} . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nApply the law of sines in \\(\\triangle ABF\\) and \\(\\triangle ABE\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{BF}{\\sin\\angle BAF}= \\frac{AB}{\\sin\\angle BFA},\\qquad\n\\frac{BE}{\\sin\\angle BAE}= \\frac{AB}{\\sin\\angle BEA}.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\angle BAF = \\angle BAE\\) (both are subtended by the same chord \\(AB\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABEF\\)), division of the two equalities gives\n\\[\n\\frac{BF}{BE}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle BFA}{\\sin\\angle BEA}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\nComparing (11) and (12) we obtain\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin\\angle BFA}{\\sin\\angle BEA}= \\frac{AN^{2}}{AM^{2}} .\n\\]\nBut \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{AN}{AM}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle CBA}{\\sin\\angle BCA}\\) (law of sines in \\(\\triangle ABM\\) and \\(\\triangle ABN\\)), and a short angle chase using the fact that \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) shows that \\(\\angle BEA = \\angle CBA\\). Consequently\n\\[\n\\sin\\angle BFA = \\sin\\angle CBA .\n\\]\nBoth angles lie in \\((0^\\circ,180^\\circ)\\); therefore\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle BFA = \\angle CBA}. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nInsert (13) into (10):\n\\[\n\\angle TBA = \\angle CBA .\n\\]\nThus the line \\(BT\\) makes with \\(BA\\) the same angle as the line \\(BC\\) does; i.e. \\(BT\\) is parallel to the line through \\(B\\) that is symmetric to \\(BC\\) with respect to the internal bisector of \\(\\angle ABM\\). By the definition of a symmedian, this direction is precisely the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle AMN\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{the tangent to }\\omega\\text{ at }B\\text{ is parallel to the }A\\text{-symmedian of }\\triangle AMN.} \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final step\n\nFrom (9) the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is parallel to the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle AMN\\).  \nFrom (14) the tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\) is also parallel to that same symmedian.  \n\nTherefore the two lines coincide; equivalently, **the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).** \u220e"
