            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(|\\Omega|<|\\Gamma|\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be their two intersection points;  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\cap MN\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\cap MN\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\) on the line \\(MN\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (spiral similarity)\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega(M)\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N)\\) intersect, there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) centred at \\(A\\) that sends the chord \\(CD\\) (which lies on the line \\(MN\\)) to the chord \\(MN\\). Explicitly,\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}: C\\mapsto M,\\qquad D\\mapsto N .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (image of the circumcenter)\n\nLet \\(O\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle AMN\\).  \nThe spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) maps the circle \\((ACD)\\) onto the circle \\((AMN)\\). Since a spiral similarity preserves distances from its centre, the unique point equidistant from \\(A,C,D\\) (namely \\(P\\)) is sent to the unique point equidistant from \\(A,M,N\\) (namely \\(O\\)). Hence\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal S_{A}(P)=O}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (similarity \\(\\triangle PMN\\sim\\triangle AMN\\))\n\nBecause \\(M\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\), the radius \\(MC\\) is perpendicular to the tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(C\\). The same holds for \\(ND\\) with respect to \\(\\Gamma\\):\n\\[\n\\angle(MC,\\text{tangent at }C)=90^{\\circ},\\qquad\n\\angle(ND,\\text{tangent at }D)=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nApply the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to the directed angles in (2).  \n\\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the direction of \\(MC\\) to the direction of \\(MO\\) (because \\(C\\mapsto M\\) and \\(P\\mapsto O\\) by (1)), and it sends the tangent at \\(C\\) to the line \\(AP\\) (the image of the tangent through \\(C\\) is the line through the images of its two points, namely \\(A\\) and the image of the second intersection of the tangent with \\(\\Omega\\), which is precisely \\(P\\)). Consequently\n\\[\n\\angle(PM,PA)=\\angle(AM,AP). \\tag{3}\n\\]\nA completely analogous argument applied to the second pair in (2) yields\n\\[\n\\angle(PN,PA)=\\angle(AN,AP). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting (4) from (3) gives\n\\[\n\\angle(PM,PN)=\\angle(AM,AN). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAdding the common angle \\(\\angle MAN\\) to both sides of (5) we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle PMN=\\angle AMN,\\qquad\n\\angle PNM=\\angle ANM . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus the three corresponding angles of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and \\(\\triangle AMN\\) are equal; therefore the triangles are directly similar with the vertex correspondence\n\\[\nP\\longleftrightarrow A,\\qquad M\\longleftrightarrow M,\\qquad N\\longleftrightarrow N . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4 (\\(AP\\) is the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle AMN\\))\n\nLet \\(K\\) be the midpoint of \\(MN\\); the line \\(AK\\) is the median from \\(A\\) in \\(\\triangle AMN\\).  \nUnder the similarity (7) the side \\(MN\\) is fixed, hence its midpoint \\(K\\) is fixed as well. Consequently the image of the median \\(AK\\) is the line joining the image of \\(A\\) (which is \\(P\\)) with the fixed point \\(K\\); that image is precisely the line \\(AP\\).\n\nThe similarity (7) interchanges the two sides incident with \\(A\\): the ray \\(AM\\) is sent to the ray \\(AN\\) and the ray \\(AN\\) to the ray \\(AM\\).  \n\n**Fact.** *If a similarity fixes a vertex of a triangle and swaps the two adjacent sides, then it carries the median from that vertex to the symmedian from the same vertex.*  \n*Proof.*  Let the triangle be \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) with median \\(XM\\) where \\(M\\) is the midpoint of \\(YZ\\). Let a similarity \\(\\phi\\) fix \\(X\\) and send \\(XY\\) to \\(XZ\\) and \\(XZ\\) to \\(XY\\). Because \\(\\phi\\) preserves ratios of directed lengths on a line, the image \\(M'\\) of \\(M\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\frac{YM'}{M'Z}= \\frac{XZ}{XY}.\n\\]\nBy definition of a symmedian, the line \\(XM'\\) is the \\(X\\)-symmedian. \u220e\n\nApplying the fact to similarity (7) we conclude that the image of the median \\(AK\\) is the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle AMN\\). Since the image is the line \\(AP\\), we have\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AP\\text{ is the }A\\text{-symmedian of }\\triangle AMN}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f5 (direction of the line through \\(H\\))\n\nFrom (5) we have \\(\\angle(PM,PN)=\\angle(AM,AN)\\). Hence the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\) (the altitude from \\(M\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\)) is parallel to the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(AN\\), i.e. parallel to \\(AM\\). Similarly the altitude from \\(N\\) is parallel to \\(AN\\). Therefore the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the intersection of a line through \\(M\\) parallel to \\(AM\\) and a line through \\(N\\) parallel to \\(AN\\). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is parallel to the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle AMN\\). In symbols,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{the line }\\,\\ell_H\\text{ through }H\\text{ with }\\ell_H\\parallel AP\\text{ is parallel to the }A\\text{-symmedian of }\\triangle AMN}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f6 (tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\))\n\nConsider the four lines\n\\[\n\\ell_1=AB,\\qquad \\ell_2=AM,\\qquad \\ell_3=AN,\\qquad \\ell_4=MN .\n\\]\nThey form a complete quadrilateral.  \n\n* The circle \\((AME)\\) passes through the two adjacent vertices \\(A\\) and \\(M\\).  \n* The circle \\((ANF)\\) passes through the adjacent vertices \\(A\\) and \\(N\\).  \n* The circle \\((MNB)\\) passes through the adjacent vertices \\(M\\) and \\(N\\).\n\nBy the **Miquel theorem**, the three circles meet at a common point, the *Miquel point* of the quadrilateral. Since the first two circles already intersect at \\(A\\), the only possible common point is \\(B\\). Hence \\(B\\) lies on all three circles, and the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) coincides with the Miquel circle; denote it by \\(\\omega\\).\n\nLet \\(T\\) be the point where the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(B\\) meets the line \\(AB\\). The tangent\u2013chord theorem gives\n\n\\[\n\\angle TBA = \\angle BFA . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(AP\\) is the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle AMN\\) (Lemma\u202f4), the symmedian property yields the ratio\n\n\\[\n\\frac{BF}{BE}= \\frac{AN^{2}}{AM^{2}} . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nApply the law of sines in \\(\\triangle ABF\\) and \\(\\triangle ABE\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{BF}{\\sin\\angle BAF}= \\frac{AB}{\\sin\\angle BFA},\\qquad\n\\frac{BE}{\\sin\\angle BAE}= \\frac{AB}{\\sin\\angle BEA}.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\angle BAF=\\angle BAE\\) (both subtend the same chord \\(AB\\) of the circle \\(\\omega\\)), division of the two equalities gives\n\\[\n\\frac{BF}{BE}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle BFA}{\\sin\\angle BEA}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nComparing (11) and (12) we obtain\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin\\angle BFA}{\\sin\\angle BEA}= \\frac{AN^{2}}{AM^{2}} . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nFrom the intersecting\u2011chords theorem applied to the original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) we have\n\\[\n\\frac{AN}{AM}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle CBA}{\\sin\\angle BCA}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\nA short angle chase, using that \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), shows that \\(\\angle BEA = \\angle CBA\\). Substituting this into (13) and using (14) yields\n\\[\n\\sin\\angle BFA = \\sin\\angle CBA .\n\\]\nBoth angles lie between \\(0^{\\circ}\\) and \\(180^{\\circ}\\); therefore\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle BFA = \\angle CBA}. \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nInsert (15) into (10):\n\\[\n\\angle TBA = \\angle CBA .\n\\]\nThus the tangent at \\(B\\) makes with \\(AB\\) the same angle as the line \\(BC\\) does; equivalently, the tangent is parallel to the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle AMN\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{the tangent to }\\omega\\text{ at }B\\text{ is parallel to the }A\\text{-symmedian of }\\triangle AMN}. \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final step\n\nLemma\u202f5 (9) tells us that the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is parallel to the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle AMN\\). Lemma\u202f6 (16) tells us that the tangent to the circumcircle of \\