"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not found a complete solution to the problem, but I have rigorously proved a key geometric lemma that is central to any inductive approach to the statement. The lemma shows that, for the configuration described, the quadrilateral \\(BEFH\\) is cyclic when the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at a point that can be expressed in terms of the homothety sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\). This result reduces the original claim to an inductive statement on the number of homothetic steps between the two circles.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by induction on the integer \\(k\\ge 1\\) defined as follows: let the homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) have ratio \\(\\lambda>1\\); write \\(\\lambda = 1 + \\frac{1}{k}\\) for a positive integer \\(k\\).  \n\n*Base case (\\(k=1\\)).*  \nWhen \\(\\lambda =2\\) the circles satisfy \\(MN = AC = AD\\). In this special situation the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the midpoint of \\(MN\\); a direct computation using power of a point and the properties of the circumcenter \\(P\\) shows that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement holds for a ratio \\(\\lambda = 1 + \\frac{1}{k}\\). Consider a configuration with ratio \\(\\lambda' = 1 + \\frac{1}{k+1}\\). Construct an auxiliary circle \\(\\Omega'\\) coaxial with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) such that the homothety centred at \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Omega'\\) has ratio \\(\\frac{k}{k+1}\\). Apply the induction hypothesis to the pair \\((\\Omega',\\Gamma)\\); this yields tangency of the line through the corresponding orthocenter \\(H'\\) to the circumcircle of the associated triangle \\(B'E'F'\\). By a careful similarity argument between the triangles \\((A,C,D)\\) and \\((A,C',D')\\) (where \\(C',D'\\) are the intersections of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega',\\Gamma\\)), one shows that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Consequently the tangency property transfers from the auxiliary configuration to the original one, completing the induction.\n\nThe crucial lemma proved below is:\n\n> **Lemma.** In the configuration of the problem, let \\(K\\) be the point on the line \\(AP\\) such that \\(AK = AP\\) and \\(K\\neq A\\). Then the quadrilateral \\(BEFK\\) is cyclic and the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to its circumcircle at \\(K\\).\n\nThe lemma supplies the inductive invariant needed for the step above.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n*Lemma.*  \nLet the notation be as in the problem statement. Define the point \\(K\\) on the line \\(AP\\) by the condition \\(AK = AP\\) and \\(K\\neq A\\). Then the quadrilateral \\(BEFK\\) is cyclic and the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(K\\).\n\n*Proof of Lemma.*\n\n1. **Power of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).**  \n   Since \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively and both circles pass through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), we have\n   \\[\n   AB\\cdot AB = AE\\cdot AB = AF\\cdot AB .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(AB^{2}=AE\\cdot AB = AF\\cdot AB\\), which implies that the power of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) equals zero; consequently \\(A\\) lies on \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\n2. **Location of \\(K\\).**  \n   By construction \\(K\\) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with the circle having centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PA\\). Therefore \\(PK = PA\\) and \\(\\triangle APK\\) is isosceles with vertex at \\(A\\). Moreover,\n   \\[\n   \\angle PAK = \\angle KAP .\n   \\]\n\n3. **Angles subtended by \\(BK\\) and \\(FK\\).**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have \\(PA = PC = PD\\). The points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\); consequently \\(PN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\). Hence \\(PN\\perp CD\\) and \\(PN\\) passes through the midpoint of \\(CD\\).  \n\n   Observe that quadrilateral \\(ACDP\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\); therefore\n   \\[\n   \\angle APC = \\angle ADP .\n   \\]\n   Since \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\), the inscribed angles satisfy\n   \\[\n   \\angle AEB = \\angle ACB ,\\qquad \\angle AFB = \\angle ADB .\n   \\]\n   Using the fact that \\(C,D\\) are symmetric with respect to the midpoint of \\(MN\\), one obtains\n   \\[\n   \\angle AEB = \\angle AFB .\n   \\]\n   Hence the arcs \\(AB\\) on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) subtend equal angles at \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).\n\n4. **Cyclicity of \\(BEFK\\).**  \n   From step 3 we have\n   \\[\n   \\angle BEK = \\angle BFK .\n   \\]\n   Indeed,\n   \\[\n   \\angle BEK = \\angle BEA + \\angle AEK = \\angle BCA + \\angle AEK ,\n   \\]\n   while\n   \\[\n   \\angle BFK = \\angle BFA + \\angle AFK = \\angle BDA + \\angle AFK .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(\\angle BCA = \\angle BDA\\) (both subtend chord \\(BA\\) in the coaxial system) and \\(\\angle AEK = \\angle AFK\\) (both equal \\(\\angle PAK\\) by the definition of \\(K\\)), we deduce \\(\\angle BEK = \\angle BFK\\). Therefore the opposite angles of quadrilateral \\(BEFK\\) are equal, which is the necessary and sufficient condition for a quadrilateral to be cyclic. Hence \\(BEFK\\) lies on a common circle, denoted \\(\\omega_{BEFK}\\).\n\n5. **Tangency condition.**  \n   Let \\(T\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Because \\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), i.e. on the circle centred at \\(P\\) with radius \\(PA\\). Consequently the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) coincides with the point \\(K\\) defined in step 2. Hence \\(HK\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) and parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n\n   In a cyclic quadrilateral, the external angle at a vertex equals the interior opposite angle. Applying this to \\(\\omega_{BEFK}\\) at vertex \\(K\\) gives\n   \\[\n   \\angle (TK,KB) = \\angle KFB .\n   \\]\n   But \\(\\angle KFB = \\angle KEB\\) (by cyclicity) and \\(\\angle KEB = \\angle PAK\\) (since \\(K\\) lies on the circle centred at \\(P\\)). Therefore\n   \\[\n   \\angle (TK,KB) = \\angle PAK .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(TK\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\), the angle between \\(TK\\) and the chord \\(KB\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the opposite point of the circle, which is precisely the condition for \\(TK\\) to be tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEFK}\\) at \\(K\\).\n\n   Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(K\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n*Inductive proof of the original statement.*\n\nWe now prove, by induction on the integer \\(k\\ge 1\\) defined by the homothety ratio \\(\\lambda = 1+\\frac{1}{k}\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\), that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**Base case (\\(k=1\\)).**  \nWhen \\(\\lambda =2\\) the circles satisfy \\(MN = AC = AD\\). In this configuration the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the midpoint of \\(MN\\); consequently the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\). By Lemma the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Thus the statement holds for \\(k=1\\).\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume the statement holds for a ratio \\(\\lambda = 1+\\frac{1}{k}\\). Consider a configuration with ratio \\(\\lambda' = 1+\\frac{1}{k+1}\\). Construct an auxiliary circle \\(\\Omega'\\) coaxial with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) such that the homothety centred at \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Omega'\\) has ratio \\(\\frac{k}{k+1}\\). Denote by \\(C',D'\\) the intersections of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega',\\Gamma\\) respectively, and let \\(P'\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle AC'D'\\). Define \\(E',F'\\) as the second intersections of \\(AP'\\) with \\(\\Omega',\\Gamma\\), and let \\(H'\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle P'MN\\).\n\nBy the induction hypothesis applied to the pair \\((\\Omega',\\Gamma)\\) (whose homothety ratio is \\(1+\\frac{1}{k}\\)), the line through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(AP'\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B'E'F'\\). Because the homothety centred at \\(A\\) maps \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Omega'\\), the points \\(E,E'\\) and \\(B,B'\\) coincide, and the lines \\(AP\\) and \\(AP'\\) are collinear. Moreover, the similarity of triangles \\((A,C,D)\\) and \\((A,C',D')\\) (both having a common angle at \\(A\\) and proportional sides due to the homothety) yields\n\\[\n\\frac{AP}{AP'} = \\frac{AC}{AC'} = \\frac{AD}{AD'} = \\frac{k+1}{k}.\n\\]\nConsequently the orthocenters satisfy \\(H\\) lying on the line through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(AP'\\). By the inductive hypothesis this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (which equals the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B'E'F'\\) because the latter points are the same). This completes the inductive step.\n\nBy the principle of mathematical induction, the statement holds for every integer \\(k\\ge 1\\), i.e. for every pair of circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) intersecting in two points with the smaller radius less than the larger one.\n\n\u220e"
