 and \\(B\\), the chords \\(AB\\) subtend equal angles at any point on the respective circles:\n   \\[\n   \\angle AEB = \\angle ACB,\\qquad \\angle AFB = \\angle ADB .\n   \\]\u201d  \n   *This is correct: the angle subtended by a chord at the circumference of the same circle is constant.*\n\n2. **Claim:** \u201c\\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); the line \\(MN\\) is the radical axis of the two circles, hence\n   \\[\n   \\angle ACB = \\angle ADB .\n   \\]\u201d  \n   **Critical Error.** For two non\u2011concentric circles, the radical axis is **perpendicular** to the line joining the centers \\(M\\) and \\(N\\). The line \\(MN\\) is the line of centers, not the radical axis. Consequently the equality \\(\\angle ACB = \\angle ADB\\) does **not** follow, and the subsequent chain of equalities collapses.\n\n   Because this error invalidates the derivation of \\(\\angle BEK = \\angle BFK\\), all later conclusions that depend on (4) are unsupported.\n\n3. The subsequent manipulations that combine (1), (2), and (3) to obtain \\(\\angle BEK = \\angle BFK\\) therefore rest on a false premise. Hence Step\u202f2 is invalid.\n\n---\n\n**Step\u202f3 \u2013 Cyclicity of \\(B E F K\\).**  \n\nThe step uses the (incorrect) result \\(\\angle BEK = \\angle BFK\\) and asserts that equality of a pair of opposite angles implies concyclicity.  \n\n*Two issues:*  \n- The premise is false (see Step\u202f2).  \n- Even if the equality were true, the statement \u201cequality of a pair of opposite angles is equivalent to concyclicity\u201d is **not** a standard criterion; the correct condition is that the sum of opposite angles equals \\(180^\\circ\\) or that equal angles subtend the same chord.  \n\nThus Step\u202f3 contains a **Justification Gap** (the criterion is misstated) and is built on a **Critical Error** from Step\u202f2, rendering the conclusion invalid.\n\n---\n\n**Step\u202f4 \u2013 Position of the orthocenter \\