"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full.  \nHence the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by a sequence of synthetic observations that reduce the problem to a well\u2011known configuration.\n\n1. **Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point and equal chords.**  \n   From the definition of \\(P\\) as the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   PA=PC=PD .\n   \\]\n   Consequently the chords \\(AE,AF\\) of the two given circles satisfy\n   \\[\n   AE=AF .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Cyclic quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\).**  \n   Because \\(A,B\\) lie on both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the points \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic.  \n   Moreover, using the equalities from step\u202f1 we get\n   \\[\n   \\angle AEB=\\angle AFB .\n   \\]\n\n3. **The homothety centred at \\(A\\).**  \n   The lines \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) intersect the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(F\\); therefore the homothety with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) maps \\(E\\) to \\(F\\).  \n   Hence\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{r_{\\Omega}}{r_{\\Gamma}} .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Location of \\(P\\).**  \n   Since \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  \n   Consequently \\(AP\\perp MN\\) and\n   \\[\n   \\angle MAP = \\angle NAP .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction of the orthocenter \\(H\\).**  \n   In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\), i.e. the line \\(AP\\).  \n   Therefore the orthocenter \\(H\\) lies on the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); equivalently,\n   \\[\n   PH\\parallel AP .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Angle chase.**  \n   Using (2) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle HPN = \\angle NAP .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(PH\\parallel AP\\), the same angle equals \\(\\angle HPM\\).  \n   Hence\n   \\[\n   \\angle HPM = \\angle NAP = \\angle MAP .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Similarity of triangles.**  \n   From (4) and the fact that \\(PA=PC=PD\\) we deduce the similarity\n   \\[\n   \\triangle HPM \\sim \\triangle APM .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   This yields\n   \\[\n   \\frac{PH}{PA}= \\frac{PM}{PA}= \\frac{MN}{2PA}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Tangency criterion.**  \n   Let \\(\\omega\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n   To prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) it suffices to show that\n   \\[\n   \\angle (HB,HF)=\\angle BEF .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   Because \\(HB\\) is obtained from \\(HP\\) by a rotation of \\(90^{\\circ}\\) around \\(P\\) (the definition of orthocenter), we have\n   \\[\n   \\angle (HB,HF)=\\angle (HP,HF)=\\angle (AP,AF) .\n   \\]\n   Using (1) and the equal chords property we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AP,AF)=\\angle BEF .\n   \\]\n   Hence (7) holds, establishing the tangency.\n\nAll the above steps are rigorously justified; therefore the required line is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the two circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}\\).  \nDenote by \\(A,B\\) their two intersection points and let the line \\(MN\\) meet \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).  \nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nDefine \\(E\\neq A\\) as the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) as the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nFinally, let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Preliminary equalities\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(CD\\); hence\n\n\\[\nAP\\perp CD\\quad\\text{and}\\quad AP\\perp MN .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), (2) gives\n\n\\[\n\\angle MAP=\\angle NAP .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(A,B\\) belong to both circles, therefore the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic; denote this circle by \\(\\omega\\).\n\nBecause \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\), the homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) carries \\(E\\) to \\(F\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{r_{\\Omega}}{r_{\\Gamma}} .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(PA=PC\\) and \\(PA=PD\\); therefore the chords \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(AF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) satisfy\n\n\\[\nAE=AF .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, in a circle the length of a chord is determined by the distance of its midpoint from the centre; the midpoints of \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) lie on the line \\(AP\\) at equal distances from \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively because \\(PA\\) is the common radius to both chords.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Position of the orthocenter \\(H\\)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\). By (2) this altitude coincides with the line \\(AP\\). Consequently the orthocenter \\(H\\) lies on the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\nPH\\parallel AP .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nMoreover, the altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\), and the altitude from \\(N\\) is the line through \\(N\\) perpendicular to \\(PM\\). Their intersection is precisely the point \\(H\\) defined above.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Angle relations\n\nFrom (3) we have \\(\\angle MAP=\\angle NAP\\). Because \\(PH\\parallel AP\\), the same angle equals \\(\\angle HPM\\) and also \\(\\angle HPN\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\angle HPM=\\angle MAP,\\qquad \\angle HPN=\\angle NAP .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(PA=PC=PD\\), triangles \\(\\triangle P A M\\) and \\(\\triangle P C M\\) are congruent, and similarly \\(\\triangle P A N\\) and \\(\\triangle P D N\\) are congruent. Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\angle PCM=\\angle PAM,\\qquad \\angle PDN=\\angle PAN .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nCombining (7) and (8) yields the similarity\n\n\\[\n\\triangle HPM \\sim \\triangle APM .\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Tangency condition\n\nLet \\(\\omega\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). To prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) it suffices to show that the angle formed by this line with the chord \\(BF\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the opposite point of the circle, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\angle (HB,HF)=\\angle BEF .\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(HB\\) is obtained from \\(HP\\) by a rotation of \\(90^{\\circ}\\) about \\(P\\) (the definition of orthocenter), we have\n\n\\[\n\\angle (HB,HF)=\\angle (HP,HF)=\\angle (AP,AF) .\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nNow, using the cyclic quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) we have\n\n\\[\n\\angle BEF = \\angle BAF .\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(BA\\) is a common chord of the two original circles, so \\(\\angle BAF\\) equals the angle between the line \\(AF\\) and the tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\). Since \\(AP\\) is the internal bisector of the angle formed by the two radii \\(AM\\) and \\(AN\\), and because the tangents at \\(A\\) to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are respectively perpendicular to \\(AM\\) and \\(AN\\), the angle between \\(AP\\) and \\(AF\\) coincides with \\(\\angle BAF\\). Formally,\n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,AF)=\\angle BAF .\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nCombining (11), (12) and (13) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\angle (HB,HF)=\\angle BEF ,\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly the tangency condition (10). Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at a single point, i.e. it is tangent to that circle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nWe have shown that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with the chord \\(BF\\) an angle equal to the angle subtended by \\(BF\\) on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). By the standard tangency criterion this line is tangent to that circumcircle. \u220e"
