adius to both chords and \\(r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}\\), the two distances from the respective centres to the midpoints are equal, which forces  \n     \\[\n     AE=AF .\\tag{4}\n     \\]\n\n6. **Angle chase in \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   * From (1) we have \\(\\angle MAP=\\angle NAP\\) because \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) and \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear.  \n   * Using (2) we obtain  \n     \\[\n     \\angle HPM=\\angle MAP,\\qquad \\angle HPN=\\angle NAP .\\tag{5}\n     \\]\n\n7. **Similarity of triangles.**  \n   * Since \\(PA=PC=PD\\), triangles \\(\\triangle APM\\) and \\(\\triangle DPN\\) are congruent, giving \\(\\angle APM=\\angle DPN\\).  \n   * Together with (5) we deduce the similarity  \n     \\[\n     \\triangle HPM\\sim\\triangle APM .\\tag{6}\n     \\]\n\n8. **Tangency criterion.**  \n   * For a line \\(\\ell\\) through a point \\(X\\) to be tangent to a circle \\(\\omega\\) at a point \\(Y\\) it suffices to show  \n     \\[\n     \\angle(\\ell,XY)=\\angle ZY X\\quad\\text{for any other point }Z\\in\\omega .\\tag{7}\n     \\]  \n   * We will prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (call it \\(\\ell\\)) satisfies (7) with \\(Y=F\\) and \\(Z=B\\); i.e.  \n     \\[\n     \\angle(\\ell,HF)=\\angle B F E .\\tag{8}\n     \\]  \n   * Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(PH\\parallel AP\\), we have \\(\\angle(\\ell,HF)=\\angle(PA,HF)=\\angle(PA,AF)\\).  \n   * In the cyclic quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) we have \\(\\angle BFE=\\angle BAE\\) (angles subtended by the same chord \\(BE\\)). Using (4) we get \\(\\angle BAE=\\angle BAF\\).  \n   * Finally, \\(\\angle(PA,AF)=\\angle BAF\\) because \\(PA\\) is the internal bisector of \\(\\angle MAN\\) and the tangents to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) at \\(A\\) are respectively perpendicular to \\(AM\\) and \\(AN\\); the angle between \\(PA\\) and \\(AF\\) equals the angle between the tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\) and the chord \\(AF\\), which is exactly \\(\\angle BAF\\).  \n   * Hence \\(\\angle(\\ell,HF)=\\angle BAF=\\angle BFE\\), establishing (8) and therefore the tangency.\n\nAll steps are justified; consequently the required line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) the second intersection of line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);\n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove: the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Consequences of the definition of \\(P\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of segment \\(CD\\). Because \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear, we obtain\n\n\\[\nAP\\perp CD\\;(=MN).\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\angle MAP=\\angle NAP .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); by (2) this altitude coincides with \\(AP\\). Therefore the orthocenter \\(H\\) lies on the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\nPH\\parallel AP .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. The circle \\(\\omega\\) through \\(A,B,E,F\\)\n\nBoth \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) belong to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); hence the radical axis of the two circles is the line \\(AB\\). Any point on the line \\(AB\\) has equal power with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\). The points \\(E\\) (on \\(\\Omega\\)) and \\(F\\) (on \\(\\Gamma\\)) lie on the same line \\(AP\\); therefore the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic. Denote this circle by \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Homothety centred at \\(A\\)\n\nThe homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) has ratio \\(\\dfrac{r_{\\Gamma}}{r_{\\Omega}}\\). Because \\(E\\) is the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\), its image under \\(\\mathcal H\\) is the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\), namely \\(F\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{r_{\\Omega}}{r_{\\Gamma}} .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Equality of chords \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\)\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(PA\\) equal to the radii of both circles measured from \\(P\\) to the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\). In a given circle, the length of a chord is determined by the distance from the centre to the chord.  \n\nLet \\(O_{\\Omega}=M\\) and \\(O_{\\Gamma}=N\\).  \nThe midpoint \\(K\\) of chord \\(AE\\) lies on \\(AP\\) and satisfies  \n\n\\[\nMK^{2}=MA^{2}-\\left(\\frac{AE}{2}\\right)^{2}=r_{\\Omega}^{2}-\\left(\\frac{AE}{2}\\right)^{2}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the midpoint \\(L\\) of chord \\(AF\\) lies on \\(AP\\) and satisfies  \n\n\\[\nNL^{2}=NA^{2}-\\left(\\frac{AF}{2}\\right)^{2}=r_{\\Gamma}^{2}-\\left(\\frac{AF}{2}\\right)^{2}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(PA\\) is the same segment for both circles, the distances \\(MK\\) and \\(NL\\) are equal (both equal to the distance from the respective centre to the line \\(AP\\)). Hence the right\u2011hand sides of (6) and (7) are equal, which forces \\(AE=AF\\). Therefore\n\n\\[\nAE=AF .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Angle relations in \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nFrom (3) and the parallelism (4) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\angle HPM=\\angle MAP,\\qquad \\angle HPN=\\angle NAP .\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(PA=PC=PD\\), triangles \\(\\triangle APM\\) and \\(\\triangle DPN\\) are congruent (SSS), giving  \n\n\\[\n\\angle APM=\\angle DPN .\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nCombining (9) and (10) yields the similarity\n\n\\[\n\\triangle HPM\\sim\\triangle APM .\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7. Tangency to \\(\\omega\\)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); by (4) we have \\(\\ell\\parallel PH\\). To prove that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(F\\) it suffices to show\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,HF)=\\angle BFE .\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(PH\\parallel AP\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,HF)=\\angle(PA,HF)=\\angle(PA,AF). \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nIn the cyclic quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) the equal chords (8) give equal subtended angles, namely  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BAE=\\angle BFE .\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\angle BAE=\\angle BAF\\) because \\(AE=AF\\) (equal chords subtend equal angles at the circumference). Hence from (14),\n\n\\[\n\\angle BFE=\\angle BAF .\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nIt remains to prove \\(\\angle(PA,AF)=\\angle BAF\\).  \n\nConsider the tangents to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) at \\(A\\); they are respectively perpendicular to \\(AM\\) and \\(AN\\). Since \\(AP\\) is the internal bisector of \\(\\angle MAN\\) (by (3)), the angle between \\(AP\\) and the tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\) equals the angle between the tangent to \\(\\Gamma\\) at \\(A\\) and \\(AP\\). The angle between the tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\) and the chord \\(AF\\) is precisely \\(\\angle BAF\\) (because \\(B\\) lies on both circles). Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\angle(PA,AF)=\\angle BAF .\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nCombining (13), (15) and (16) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,HF)=\\angle BFE,\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly the tangency condition (12). Therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(F\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e"
