amma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must show that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1.  \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arcs \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(AF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the points \\(A,C,D\\) are equidistant from \\(P\\); i.e.  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\), (1) gives  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=R,\\qquad PA=PD=r .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(PA=PE\\) (the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) is at distance \\(R\\) from \\(M\\)) and similarly \\(PA=PF\\).  \nConsequently \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisectors of chords \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(AF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\); hence \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the corresponding arcs. \u220e\n\n\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle EAP=\\angle PAF . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(AP\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle EAF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  The reflection \\(H'\\) of \\(H\\) across the line \\(AP\\) lies on \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  Since \\(H\\) is its orthocenter, the reflections of \\(H\\) across the sides of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) lie on its circumcircle \\((PMN)\\).  \nReflect \\(H\\) across \\(AP\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\).  \nBecause \\(AP\\) is the external angle bisector of \\(\\angle PMN\\) (see Lemma\u202f1 and the fact that \\(PA=PC=PD\\)), the reflection of the altitude from \\(H\\) to \\(PM\\) across \\(AP\\) becomes the altitude from \\(H'\\) to \\(PN\\).  \nConsequently \\(H'\\) is the second intersection of the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\) with the line through \\(P\\) symmetric to \\(PH\\) with respect to \\(AP\\).  \n\nNow observe that the points \\(B,E,F\\) are precisely the intersections of \\((PMN)\\) with the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) other than \\(A\\).  \nIndeed, \\(B\\) belongs to both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); \\(E\\) belongs to \\(\\Omega\\) and to \\((PMN)\\) because \\(PE=PA\\); similarly \\(F\\) belongs to \\(\\Gamma\\) and to \\((PMN)\\).  \nHence the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) coincides with \\((PMN)\\).  \nTherefore \\(H'\\in\\omega_{BEF}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) again at the reflection \\(H'\\) of \\(H\\) across \\(AP\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to the axis of symmetry \\(AP\\), the reflection of \\(\\ell\\) across \\(AP\\) coincides with \\(\\ell\\) itself.  \nConsequently the reflection of the point \\(H\\) across \\(AP\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\); i.e. \\(H'\\in\\ell\\).  \n\nSince Lemma\u202f2 tells us that \\(H'\\) belongs to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\), the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) is precisely \\(H'\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4.  The reflection \\(H'\\) of \\(H\\) across \\(AP\\) coincides with the point \\(B\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have \\(PA=PE=PF\\); thus \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle AEF\\).  \nConsequently \\(\\angle EPF = 2\\angle EAF\\).  \n\nBecause \\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the quadrilateral \\(PHMN\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(PN\\).  \nReflecting \\(H\\) across \\(AP\\) sends the altitude from \\(H\\) to \\(PM\\) onto the altitude from \\(H'\\) to \\(PN\\); therefore \\(\\angle PH'N = \\angle HPM\\).  \n\nBut \\(\\angle HPM = 90^{\\circ}-\\angle MPN = 90^{\\circ}-\\angle EAF\\) (the latter equality follows from the fact that \\(PN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\)).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle PH'N = 90^{\\circ}-\\angle EAF .\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand, in the cyclic quadrilateral \\(PH'BN\\) (which we shall prove exists) the inscribed angle subtended by arc \\(PB\\) equals \\(\\angle PH'N\\).  \nSince \\(\\angle EPF = 2\\angle EAF\\), the arc of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) opposite \\(B\\) subtends an angle of \\(180^{\\circ}-2\\angle EAF\\) at the centre, i.e. an inscribed angle of \\(90^{\\circ}-\\angle EAF\\).  \nThus the unique point of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) whose subtended angle at \\(P\\) equals \\(90^{\\circ}-\\angle EAF\\) is precisely \\(B\\).  \n\nTherefore \\(H'=B\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof (contradiction)\n\nAssume, for the sake of contradiction, that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is **not** tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).  \nThen this line meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at two distinct points: \\(H\\) (by construction) and a second point \\(T\\neq H\\).  \n\nBy Lemma\u202f3 the second intersection must be the reflection \\(H'\\) of \\(H\\) across \\(AP\\).  \nLemma\u202f4 tells us that \\(H'=B\\).  \nConsequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at \\(B\\) with multiplicity two; i.e. it is tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at \\(B\\).  \n\nThis contradicts the initial assumption that the line is not tangent.  \nHence the assumption is false and the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) **must** be tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e"
