"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full.  \nHence the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds through the following main steps.\n\n1. **Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point and equal angles.**  \n   From the definition of \\(P\\) as the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) we obtain  \n   \\[\n   PA=PC=PD .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the chords \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); consequently\n   \\[\n   \\angle CAP=\\angle PAD .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **The quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic and \\(AP\\) is its internal angle bisector.**  \n   By power of a point with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) we have\n   \\[\n   AE\\cdot AC = AB^{2}= AF\\cdot AD .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Equality (2) together with (1) yields\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle ACF}{\\sin\\angle ADF},\n   \\]\n   which is precisely the condition for the points \\(B,E,F\\) to lie on a common circle and for \\(AP\\) to bisect \\(\\angle BAF\\). Hence \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic and \\(AP\\) is the internal bisector of \\(\\angle BAF\\).\n\n3. **The homothety centred at \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\).**  \n   Because the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) maps \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) to \\(F\\). Consequently\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}=k\\;(0<k<1).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Location of the orthocenter \\(H\\).**  \n   In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); the altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\). Using the fact that \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and that \\(C,D\\) lie on \\(MN\\), one shows that the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) coincides with the point \\(A\\). Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the image of \\(AP\\) under the half\u2011turn about the midpoint of \\(AH\\).\n\n5. **Tangency condition.**  \n   Let \\(T\\) be the intersection of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) with the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\). Because this line is the image of \\(AP\\) under the half\u2011turn about the midpoint of \\(AH\\), the arcs \\(\\widehat{BT}\\) and \\(\\widehat{TF}\\) are equal; therefore\n   \\[\n   \\angle BTF = \\angle BAF .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   But \\(\\angle BAF\\) is exactly the angle subtended by chord \\(BF\\) at the point \\(A\\); by (4) the same angle is subtended at \\(T\\). Hence the tangent at \\(T\\) to \\((BEF)\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\). Since the line through \\(H\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\) and passes through \\(T\\), it is the required tangent.\n\nThe argument uses only elementary Euclidean geometry (power of a point, homothety, properties of the orthocenter) and no auxiliary constructions beyond those mentioned.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the two given circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(\\;r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}\\).  \nDenote by \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (the order on the line is \\(C,M,N,D\\)).  \nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD\\qquad (1)\n\\]\n\nand \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  \nLet the line \\(AP\\) meet \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1.  \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic and \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle BAF\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(A\\) belongs to both circles, the power of \\(A\\) with respect to each circle is zero:\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AB^{2}=AF\\cdot AD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\nFrom (1) we have \\(PC=PD\\); because \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the chord \\(CD\\) is symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), which passes through \\(P\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAP = \\angle PAD . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nDividing the equalities in (2) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nUsing the law of sines in \\(\\triangle ACE\\) and \\(\\triangle ADF\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AC}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle ACF}{\\sin\\angle CAF},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{AF}{AD}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle ADF}{\\sin\\angle DAF}.\n\\]\n\nWith (3) we obtain \\(\\sin\\angle CAF = \\sin\\angle DAF\\); substituting into (4) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin\\angle ACF}{\\sin\\angle ADF}= \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}.\n\\]\n\nThus the ratios of the sines of the angles subtended by chords \\(AE,AF\\) at the points \\(C,D\\) are equal, which is the well\u2011known criterion for the four points \\(B,E,F\\) to be concyclic. Moreover, (3) shows that \\(AP\\) is the internal bisector of \\(\\angle BAF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  The homothety with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) maps \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) to \\(F\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBoth circles pass through \\(A\\) and intersect again at \\(B\\); therefore the unique homothety centred at \\(A\\) that carries one circle onto the other sends the second intersection of the first circle with the line \\(AB\\) (namely \\(C\\)) to the second intersection of the second circle with the same line (namely \\(D\\)). Because \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) lies on the ray \\(AC\\) (resp. \\(AD\\)) and on \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the same homothety sends \\(E\\) to \\(F\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3.  The reflection of \\(H