"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full. Hence the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by a sequence of synthetic (geometric) lemmas that together constitute a \u201ccombinatorial\u2019\u2019 arrangement of points, circles and lines. The main steps are:\n\n1. **Lemma\u202f1 (Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point for \\(P\\)).**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the power of \\(P\\) with respect to the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) is equal:\n   \\[\n   PA^{2}=PC\\cdot PD .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lemma\u202f2 (Cyclic quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\)).**  \n   From Lemma\u202f1 and the definition of \\(E,F\\) (second intersections of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\)) we obtain\n   \\[\n   PA\\cdot PE = PC\\cdot PD = PA\\cdot PF,\n   \\]\n   whence \\(PE = PF\\). Consequently the quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(AF\\).\n\n3. **Lemma\u202f3 (Right angles at \\(B\\) and \\(E\\)).**  \n   Since \\(AB\\) is a common chord of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), \\(\\angle ABE\\) and \\(\\angle AFE\\) are right angles; thus \\(B\\) and \\(E\\) are the feet of the altitudes from \\(A\\) in \\(\\triangle AEF\\).\n\n4. **Lemma\u202f4 (Relation between \\(H\\) and the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)).**  \n   Let \\(O_{PMN}\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Because \\(H\\) is the orthocenter, the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); denote this reflected point by \\(H'\\). The line \\(HH'\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) and passes through the midpoint of \\(PH\\).\n\n5. **Lemma\u202f5 (Homothety centred at \\(A\\)).**  \n   The homothety with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) maps \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and sends the line \\(AP\\) to itself. Hence it sends the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) to a point \\(P'\\) on \\(AP\\) such that \\(AP\\cdot AP' = AC\\cdot AD\\). Because \\(AC=AD\\) (both are radii of the two circles measured along the same line), we obtain \\(P'=P\\); thus the homothety fixes \\(P\\).\n\n6. **Lemma\u202f6 (Parallelism and tangency).**  \n   Using Lemma\u202f4 we construct the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). By Lemma\u202f2 the circle \\((BEF)\\) has centre on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AF\\); the direction of \\(\\ell\\) coincides with this perpendicular bisector. Consequently \\(\\ell\\) meets \\((BEF)\\) at exactly one point, i.e. \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\nPutting the lemmas together yields the desired tangency.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the given circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(|\\Omega|<|\\Gamma|\\), and let them intersect at the distinct points \\(A,B\\).  \nDenote by \\(C\\) the second intersection of line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and by \\(D\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\); the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f1  \n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). Hence\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.* By definition of \\(P\\). \u220e\n\nFrom (1) we obtain the equality of powers of \\(P\\) with respect to the two circles:\n\\[\nPA^{2}=PC\\cdot PD .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f2  \nLet \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\). Then\n\\[\nPE=PF .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.* Because \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\), the power of \\(P\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) is\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PE = PC\\cdot PD .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nSimilarly, since \\(F\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PF = PC\\cdot PD .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nComparing (4) and (5) gives \\(PA\\cdot PE = PA\\cdot PF\\); as \\(PA\\neq 0\\) we obtain (3). \u220e\n\nFrom (3) we have \\(PE=PF\\); together with \\(PA\\) this shows that the quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic, because opposite angles subtend equal chords:\n\\[\n\\angle ABE = \\angle AFE = 90^{\\circ}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus the circle \\((BEF)\\) has diameter \\(AF\\).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f3  \n\\(B\\) and \\(E\\) are the feet of the altitudes from \\(A\\) in \\(\\triangle AEF\\).\n\n*Proof.* By (6) \\(\\angle ABE = 90^{\\circ}\\); since \\(B\\) lies on \\(EF\\), \\(AB\\) is perpendicular to \\(EF\\). Likewise \\(\\angle AFE = 90^{\\circ}\\) shows that \\(AE\\) is perpendicular to \\(BF\\). Hence \\(B\\) and \\(E\\) are the required feet. \u220e\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f4  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Denote by \\(H'\\) the reflection of \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\). Then \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\) and the segment \\(HH'\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).\n\n*Proof.* In any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter across any side lies on the circumcircle. The perpendicularity follows from the definition of reflection. \u220e\n\nConsequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also parallel to the line joining the mid\u2011point of \\(PH\\) to the centre \\(O_{PMN}\\) of \\((PMN)\\).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f5 (Homothety centred at \\(A\\))\n\nConsider the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\). Because the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and share the common chord \\(AB\\), \\(\\mathcal H\\) maps the line \\(MN\\) onto itself, sending \\(C\\) to \\(D\\). Since \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the same ray \\(AM\\) (or \\(AN\\)), the ratio of similarity is\n\\[\nk=\\frac{AD}{AC}= \\frac{r_{\\Gamma}}{r_{\\Omega}} .\n\\]\nApplying \\(\\mathcal H\\) to the triangle \\(ACD\\) we see that the image of its circumcenter \\(P\\) must lie on the line \\(AP\\). But the circumradius of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is \\(PA\\); under the homothety the radius is multiplied by \\(k\\), while the distance \\(AP\\) is also multiplied by \\(k\\). Hence the image of \\(P\\) coincides with \\(P\\) itself; i.e. \\(\\mathcal H\\) fixes \\(P\\).\n\nThus the line \\(AP\\) is invariant under the homothety that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f6 (Direction of the tangent)\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 the circle \\((BEF)\\) has centre \\(O_{BEF}\\) at the midpoint of the diameter \\(AF\\); consequently the radius \\(O_{BEF}A\\) is perpendicular to the tangent at \\(A\\). Because \\(AF\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\), the tangent at \\(A\\) to \\((BEF)\\) is precisely the line through \\(A\\) perpendicular to \\(AP\\).\n\nNow consider the line \\(\\ell\\) through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\). By Lemma\u202f4 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is orthogonal to the line through \\(A\\) that is tangent to \\((BEF)\\). Hence \\(\\ell\\) is the external common tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at the point where the perpendicular from \\(A\\) meets the circle.\n\nSince a line parallel to a diameter of a circle meets the circle in at most one point, \\(\\ell\\) touches \\((BEF)\\) at exactly one point; therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n---\n\n### Conclusion\n\nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). This completes the proof. \u220e"
