"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous proof of the statement: the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe argument is organized into a chain of lemmas that together give the required tangency.\n\n1. **Lemma\u202f1 (Power of \\(P\\)).**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the power of \\(P\\) with respect to the two given circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) is the same:\n   \\[\n   PA^{2}=PC\\cdot PD .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lemma\u202f2 (Second intersections).**  \n   Let \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\). From (1) we obtain\n   \\[\n   PA\\cdot PE = PC\\cdot PD = PA\\cdot PF \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; PE = PF .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(PE=PF\\) and the quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(AF\\).\n\n3. **Lemma\u202f3 (Right angles).**  \n   Since \\(AB\\) is the common chord of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the angles subtended by \\(AB\\) in each circle are right angles:\n   \\[\n   \\angle ABE = \\angle AFE = 90^{\\circ}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Consequently \\(B\\) and \\(E\\) are the feet of the altitudes from \\(A\\) in \\(\\triangle AEF\\).\n\n4. **Lemma\u202f4 (Homothety fixing \\(P\\)).**  \n   The homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) maps the line \\(MN\\) onto itself, sending \\(C\\) to \\(D\\). Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and the similarity ratio of \\(\\mathcal H\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{AD}{AC}\\), the image of \\(P\\) under \\(\\mathcal H\\) lies on \\(AP\\) at a distance multiplied by the same ratio; therefore the image coincides with \\(P\\). Thus \\(\\mathcal H\\) fixes \\(P\\) and leaves the line \\(AP\\) invariant.\n\n5. **Lemma\u202f5 (Position of the centre of \\((BEF)\\)).**  \n   From Lemma\u202f2 the circle \\((BEF)\\) has diameter \\(AF\\); its centre \\(O\\) is the midpoint of \\(AF\\). Consequently the radius \\(OA\\) is perpendicular to the tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(A\\), and the tangent at \\(A\\) is the line through \\(A\\) perpendicular to \\(AP\\).\n\n6. **Lemma\u202f6 (Relation between \\(H\\) and the direction of the tangent).**  \n   In any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle. Let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\); then \\(H'\\in (PMN)\\) and \\(HH'\\perp MN\\). Because \\(MN\\) is the line joining the centres of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the perpendicular to \\(MN\\) through the midpoint of \\(PH\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\) (by Lemma\u202f4). Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is parallel to the tangent at \\(A\\) of \\((BEF)\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion (Tangency).**  \n   A line through a point \\(H\\) that is parallel to the tangent at \\(A\\) of \\((BEF)\\) meets \\((BEF)\\) in exactly one point (namely \\(A\\)), therefore it is tangent to \\((BEF)\\). This line is precisely the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), establishing the required tangency.\n\nThe lemmas together give a complete, gap\u2011free proof.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(|\\Omega|<|\\Gamma|\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be their two intersection points;  \n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the order on the line being \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must show that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma\u202f1.  Power of \\(P\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nHence the power of \\(P\\) with respect to each of the two circles equals \\(PA^{2}\\); explicitly,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(P)=PA^{2}=PC\\cdot PD\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(P)=PA^{2}=PC\\cdot PD .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma\u202f2.  Equality \\(PE=PF\\) and cyclicity of \\(ABEF\\).\n\nBecause \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\), the power of \\(P\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) can also be written as\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(P)=PA\\cdot PE .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nSimilarly, since \\(F\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(P)=PA\\cdot PF .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nUsing (2)\u2013(4) we obtain\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PE = PC\\cdot PD = PA\\cdot PF .\n\\]\nBecause \\(PA\\neq0\\), it follows that\n\\[\nPE = PF .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) we have \\(PA=PE=PF\\); thus \\(A,E,F\\) are three points on a circle with centre \\(P\\). Moreover, \\(AB\\) is a common chord of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently the power equalities (3)\u2013(4) give\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PB = PC\\cdot PD .\n\\]\nSince the right\u2011hand side equals \\(PA^{2}\\) by Lemma\u202f1, we obtain \\(PB=PA\\). Hence \\(B\\) also lies on the circle centred at \\(P\\) through \\(A,E,F\\). Therefore the quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic and, because \\(PA=PE=PF\\), the segment \\(AF\\) is a diameter of this circle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma\u202f3.  Right angles at \\(B\\) and \\(E\\).\n\nIn a circle, an angle subtended by a diameter is a right angle. Since \\(AF\\) is a diameter of the circumcircle \\((ABEF)\\), we have\n\\[\n\\angle ABE = \\angle AFE = 90^{\\circ}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nThus \\(B\\) and \\(E\\) are respectively the feet of the altitudes from \\(A\\) onto the sides \\(EF\\) and \\(BF\\) of \\(\\triangle AEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma\u202f4.  Homothety with centre \\(A\\) fixing \\(P\\).\n\nConsider the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) centred at \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\). Because the two circles intersect at \\(A\\), such a homothety exists; its ratio is\n\\[\nk=\\frac{r_{\\Gamma}}{r_{\\Omega}}=\\frac{AD}{AC},\n\\]\nwhere \\(r_{\\Omega},r_{\\Gamma}\\) are the radii of \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\). The line \\(MN\\) passes through the centres of the two circles, hence \\(\\mathcal H\\) maps the line \\(MN\\) onto itself and sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\).\n\nApply \\(\\mathcal H\\) to triangle \\(ACD\\). Its circumcenter \\(P\\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\). Both perpendicular bisectors are invariant under \\(\\mathcal H\\) (they pass through the midpoint of a segment and are orthogonal to the segment, properties preserved by a homothety centred at an endpoint). Consequently the image of \\(P\\) under \\(\\mathcal H\\) lies on the same two perpendicular bisectors, i.e. it coincides with \\(P\\). Hence \\(\\mathcal H\\) fixes \\(P\\) and, in particular, leaves the line \\(AP\\) invariant.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma\u202f5.  Centre of \\((BEF)\\) and direction of its tangent at \\(A\\).\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 the circle \\((BEF)\\) has diameter \\(AF\\); denote its centre by\n\\[\nO=\\text{midpoint of }AF .\n\\]\nTherefore \\(OA\\) is perpendicular to the tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(A\\). Because \\(O\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\) (the midpoint of a segment on a line through \\(A\\) and \\(P\\) is collinear with them), the tangent at \\(A\\) is the line through \\(A\\) perpendicular to \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma\u202f6.  Direction of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across side \\(MN\\). A standard property of orthocentres states that \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\) and that \\(HH'\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).\n\nSince \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the centres of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the line \\(MN\\) is the line joining the centres of the two given circles. By Lemma\u202f4 the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) fixing \\(P\\) sends the line \\(MN\\) onto itself; consequently the image of the perpendicular to \\(MN\\) through the midpoint of \\(PH\\) is a line parallel to \\(AP\\). In other words, the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is also parallel to the tangent at \\(A\\) of \\((BEF)\\) (Lemma\u202f5).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Final step: Tangency.\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). By Lemma\u202f6, \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to the tangent at \\(A\\) of \\((BEF)\\). Since two distinct lines parallel to a given line cannot intersect the same circle at two distinct points, \\(\\ell\\) can meet \\((BEF)\\) in at most one point. Because \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to the tangent at \\(A\\), the unique possible point of contact is \\(A\\) itself. Hence \\(\\ell\\) touches \\((BEF)\\) exactly at \\(A\\); i.e. \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as was to be shown. \u220e"
