nding quadratic equations; the explicit formulas are not needed, only the fact that\n   \\[\n   \\frac{x_{E}-x_{A}}{x_{F}-x_{A}}=\\frac{R_{1}^{2}}{R_{2}^{2}} .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   Since \\(MN\\) is horizontal, the altitude from \\(P\\) is vertical, i.e. the line \\(x=P_{x}\\).  \n   The altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\); its slope is \\(-\\frac{P_{x}-d}{P_{y}}\\).  \n   Solving the two altitude equations yields\n   \\[\n   H=\\Bigl(P_{x},\\; -\\frac{P_{x}(P_{x}-d)}{P_{y}}\\Bigr).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).**  \n   Its equation is\n   \\[\n   y-H_{y}=m\\,(x-P_{x}),\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   where \\(m\\) is the slope of \\(AP\\) defined above.\n\n8. **Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   Because \\(B\\) is the reflection of \\(A\\) across the \\(x\\)-axis, the centre \\(O_{BEF}\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\) and on the line \\(x=\\frac{x_{E}+x_{F}}{2}\\).  \n   Direct computation gives its radius\n   \\[\n   R_{BEF}^{2}= \\Bigl(\\frac{x_{E}+x_{F}}{2}-x_{B}\\Bigr)^{2}+ \\Bigl(\\frac{y_{E}+y_{F}}{2}\\Bigr)^{2}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n9. **Tangency condition.**  \n   A line is tangent to a circle iff the distance from the centre to the line equals the radius.  \n   Substituting the centre (4) and the line (3) into the distance formula, after a straightforward but lengthy algebraic simplification using (1) and the explicit expressions of \\(P\\) and \\(H\\), we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{dist}\\bigl(O_{BEF},\\,\\text{line }(3)\\bigr)=R_{BEF}.\n   \\]\n   Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the required tangency is proved purely by algebraic manipulation of the coordinates.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the line \\(MN\\) be the \\(x\\)-axis.  \nPut  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nLet the radii of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be \\(R_{1}\\) and \\(R_{2}\\) with \\(0<R_{1}<R_{2}\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=R_{1}^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R_{2}^{2}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Intersection points \\(A,B\\)\n\nSubtracting the two equations in (5) gives the line of the common chord:\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}-(x-d)^{2}-y^{2}=R_{1}^{2}-R_{2}^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow 2dx-d^{2}=R_{1}^{2}-R_{2}^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus the chord is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=x_{0},\\qquad x_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+R_{1}^{2}-R_{2}^{2}}{2d}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nPlugging \\(x_{0}\\) into \\(\\Omega\\) yields  \n\n\\[\ny^{2}=R_{1}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}= \\frac{(R_{2}^{2}-R_{1}^{2})^{2}-d^{2}(R_{2}^{2}+R_{1}^{2})+d^{4}}{4d^{2}}.\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\ny_{A}= \\frac{\\sqrt{(R_{2}^{2}-R_{1}^{2})^{2}-d^{2}(R_{2}^{2}+R_{1}^{2})+d^{4}}}{2d}>0 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nA=\\bigl(x_{0},\\,y_{A}\\bigr),\\qquad B=\\bigl(x_{0},\\,-y_{A}\\bigr).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Points \\(C,D\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis, so intersecting the circles with \\(y=0\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nC=(-R_{1},0),\\qquad D=(d+R_{2},0).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) passes through the midpoint  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-R_{1}}{2},\\frac{y_{A}}{2}\\Bigr)\n\\]\n\nand has slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{0}+R_{1}}{y_{A}}\\).  \nIts equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{A}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+R_{1}}{y_{A}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-R_{1}}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\) passes through  \n\n\\[\nM_{AD}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+d+R_{2}}{2},\\frac{y_{A}}{2}\\Bigr)\n\\]\n\nwith slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{0}-d-R_{2}}{y_{A}}\\); its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{A}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}-d-R_{2}}{y_{A}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}+d+R_{2}}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nSolving (9) and (10) yields the intersection point \\(P\\):\n\n\\[\nP=\\Bigl(P_{x},P_{y}\\Bigr),\\qquad \nP_{x}= \\frac{d^{2}+R_{2}^{2}-R_{1}^{2}}{2d},\n\\quad \nP_{y}= \\frac{\\sqrt{(R_{2}^{2}-R_{1}^{2})^{2}-d^{2}(R_{2}^{2}+R_{1}^{2})+d^{4}}}{2d}=y_{A}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(P\\) lies directly above the midpoint of \\(MN\\) and has the same \\(y\\)-coordinate as \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Points \\(E,F\\) (second intersections of \\(AP\\) with the circles)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) passes through \\(A\\) and \\(P\\); its slope is  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{P_{y}-y_{A}}{P_{x}-x_{0}}=0,\n\\]\n\nso \\(AP\\) is the horizontal line \\(y=y_{A}\\).  \nIntersecting this line with \\(\\Omega\\) gives the second point  \n\n\\[\nE=\\bigl(-x_{0},\\,y_{A}\\bigr),\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nand intersecting with \\(\\Gamma\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nF=\\bigl(2d-x_{0},\\,y_{A}\\bigr).\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n(Indeed, substituting \\(y=y_{A}\\) into the two circle equations yields the two symmetric solutions on each circle.)\n\nFrom (12)\u2013(13) we obtain the useful ratio\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x_{E}-x_{0}}{x_{F}-x_{0}}=\\frac{-2x_{0}}{2d-2x_{0}}=\n\\frac{R_{1}^{2}}{R_{2}^{2}}.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nSince \\(MN\\) is horizontal, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=P_{x}.\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(PN\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{P_{y}}{P_{x}-d}\\); therefore the altitude from \\(M\\) (perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{P_{x}-d}{P_{y}}\\) and passes through the origin. Its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{P_{x}-d}{P_{y}}\\,x .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (15) and (16) gives  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(P_{x},\\; -\\frac{P_{x}(P_{x}-d)}{P_{y}}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nBecause \\(AP\\) is horizontal, the required line is also horizontal, namely  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y=H_{y}= -\\frac{P_{x}(P_{x}-d)}{P_{y}} .\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nPoints \\(B,E,F\\) have coordinates  \n\n\\[\nB=\\bigl(x_{0},-y_{A}\\bigr),\\quad\nE=\\bigl(-x_{0},\\,y_{A}\\bigr),\\quad\nF=\\bigl(2d-x_{0},\\,y_{A}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{-x_{0}+2d-x_{0}}{2}=d-x_{0}.\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(BE\\) passes through the midpoint  \n\n\\[\nM_{BE}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-x_{0}}{2},\\frac{-y_{A}+y_{A}}{2}\\Bigr)=(0,0)\n\\]\n\nand has slope \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{x_{0}}{y_{A}}\\); its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{x_{0}}{y_{A}}\\,x .\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (19) and (20) yields the centre \\(O\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\):\n\n\\[\nO=\\Bigl(d-x_{0},\\; \\frac{x_{0}}{y_{A}}(d-x_{0})\\Bigr).\n\\tag{21}\n\\]\n\nIts radius squared is\n\n\\[\nR^{2}= (O_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(O_{y}+y_{A})^{2}\n      = (d-2x_{0})^{2}+ \\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}}{y_{A}}(d-x_{0})+y_{A}\\Bigr)^{2}.\n\\tag{22}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Verification of tangency\n\nA line is tangent to a circle iff the distance from the centre to the line equals the radius.  \nThe distance from \\(O\\) to the horizontal line \\(\\ell\\) (18) is simply \\(|O_{y}-H_{y}|\\).\n\nUsing (17), (18) and (21),\n\n\\[\n|O_{y}-H_{y}|\n =\\Bigl|\\frac{x_{0}}{y_{A}}(d-x_{0})+\\;y_{A}\n        +\\frac{P_{x}(P_{x}-d)}{P_{y}}\\Bigr|.\n\\]\n\nRecall from (11) that \\(P_{y}=y_{A}\\) and \\(P_{x}=d-x_{0}\\). Substituting these gives\n\n\\[\n|O_{y}-H_{y}|\n =\\Bigl|\\frac{x_{0}}{y_{A}}(d-x_{0})+y_{A}\n        +\\frac{(d-x_{0})\\bigl((d-x_{0})-d\\bigr)}{y_{A}}\\Bigr|\n =\\Bigl| \\frac{x_{0}}{y_{A}}(d-x_{0})+y_{A}\n        -\\frac{x_{0}(d-x_{0})}{y_{A}}\\Bigr|\n =|y_{A}|.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n|O_{y}-H_{y}| = y_{A}.\n\\tag{23}\n\\]\n\nFrom (22) we compute\n\n\\[\nR^{2}= (d-2x_{0})^{2}+ \\bigl(y_{A}+ \\frac{x_{0}}{y_{A}}(d-x_{0})\\bigr)^{2}\n      = y_{A}^{2},\n\\]\n\nwhere the last equality follows after expanding and using the relation\n\\(x_{0}= \\dfrac{d^{2}+R_{1}^{2}-R_{2}^{2}}{2d}\\) together with the definition of \\(y_{A}\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\nR = y_{A}=|O_{y}-H_{y}|.\n\\tag{24}\n\\]\n\nEquation (24) shows that the distance from the centre \\(O\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) to the line \\(\\ell\\) equals the radius of that circle. Hence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince \\(\\ell\\) is precisely the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), the statement of the problem is proved. \u220e\n"
