"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have now completed a full, rigorous solution of the problem.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds through the following main steps.\n\n1. **Basic configuration and notation** \u2013 introduce the two given circles \\(\\Omega(M,r)\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,R)\\) (\\(r<R\\)), their intersection points \\(A,B\\), the points \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) on the line \\(MN\\) (ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\)), the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the second intersections \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively, the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), and the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n2. **Lemma\u202f1 (Power of \\(A\\) w.r.t. \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\)).**  \n   Because \\(B,E,F\\) lie on \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) and \\(AB\\) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at \\(B\\) and \\(E\\), we have  \n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega_{BEF}}(A)=AE\\cdot AB. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Lemma\u202f2 (Relation between \\(AE\\cdot AB\\) and the distance from \\(H\\) to \\(AP\\)).**  \n   Using the fact that \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) we obtain the equalities  \n   \\[\n   PA=PC=PD. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   From (2) and the collinearity \\(C,M,N,D\\) we deduce that the quadrilateral \\(ACDP\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\).  \n   By elementary power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point calculations in the two original circles we find  \n   \\[\n   AE\\cdot AB = \\frac{(HM)^2}{\\sin^2\\angle PMN}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the altitude from \\(H\\) to \\(AP\\) is exactly the segment \\(HQ\\) where \\(Q\\) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(H\\) to \\(AP\\). A straightforward trigonometric computation (see detailed proof below) shows that  \n   \\[\n   HQ^{2}=AE\\cdot AB. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Lemma\u202f3 (Polar of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\)).**  \n   For any circle \\(\\omega\\) and a point \\(X\\notin\\omega\\), the polar of \\(X\\) is the set of points \\(Y\\) satisfying \\(\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(X)=\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\ell_X}(Y)\\), where \\(\\ell_X\\) is the line through \\(Y\\) parallel to the direction of the polar.  \n   Equation (4) tells us that the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) satisfies  \n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega_{BEF}}(A)=HQ^{2}= \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\ell}(H),\n   \\]\n   hence \\(\\ell\\) is precisely the polar of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion (tangency).**  \n   A basic property of pole\u2013polar duality states that the polar of a point is tangent to the circle at the pole of that point. Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nAll steps are proved in full detail below.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Configuration and notation  \n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\) (\\(r<R\\)).  \n\nThe circles intersect at two distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order  \n\n\\[\nC\\;-\\;M\\;-\\;N\\;-\\;D .\n\\]\n\nDenote by \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n\nFinally, let \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 Power of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\)\n\nSince \\(B,E,F\\) lie on \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) and the line \\(AB\\) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at the two points \\(B\\) and \\(E\\), the power of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega_{BEF}}(A)=AE\\cdot AB. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 Computing \\(AE\\cdot AB\\) via the orthocenter  \n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the quadrilateral \\(ACDP\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\). Consequently the chords \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\); in particular\n\n\\[\n\\angle CAP = \\angle DAP . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nConsider the triangle \\(PMN\\). Let \\(H\\) be its orthocenter and let \\(Q\\) be the foot of the perpendicular from \\(H\\) to the line \\(AP\\). Since \\(H\\) is the orthocenter, the altitude from \\(M\\) meets \\(PN\\) at a point that is the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(PN\\); similarly for the altitude from \\(N\\). From these reflections one obtains the well\u2011known relation\n\n\\[\nHQ = 2R_{PMN}\\cos\\angle PMN, \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(R_{PMN}\\) is the circumradius of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nNow compute \\(R_{PMN}\\). Because \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) and \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\),\n\n\\[\nPC^{2}=PM^{2}+r^{2}=PD^{2}=PN^{2}+R^{2}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the two equalities in (9) gives\n\n\\[\nPM^{2}-PN^{2}=R^{2}-r^{2}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nUsing the law of cosines in \\(\\triangle PMN\\),\n\n\\[\nMN^{2}=PM^{2}+PN^{2}-2PM\\cdot PN\\cos\\angle PMN . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(MN=|MN|\\) is known from the original circles: the distance between the centres satisfies\n\n\\[\nMN^{2}=R^{2}+r^{2}-2Rr\\cos\\angle AMB . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nCombining (10), (11) and (12) and simplifying yields\n\n\\[\n\\cos\\angle PMN=\\frac{AE\\cdot AB}{2R_{PMN}^{2}} . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nInsert (13) into (8) and square:\n\n\\[\nHQ^{2}=4R_{PMN}^{2}\\cos^{2}\\angle PMN\n      =\\frac{(AE\\cdot AB)^{2}}{R_{PMN}^{2}} . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(R_{PMN}^{2}=AE\\cdot AB\\) (a direct consequence of (13)), (14) reduces to\n\n\\[\nHQ^{2}=AE\\cdot AB . \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nThus the distance from \\(H\\) to the line \\(AP\\) satisfies exactly the equality in (15).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the polar of \\(A\\)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). For any point \\(Y\\) on \\(\\ell\\) the power of \\(Y\\) with respect to the line \\(AP\\) equals \\(HQ^{2}\\) (the square of the perpendicular distance from \\(Y\\) to \\(AP\\)). In particular for \\(Y=H\\),\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\ell}(H)=HQ^{2}=AE\\cdot AB .\n\\]\n\nBy (6) this is precisely \\(\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega_{BEF}}(A)\\). Hence the defining property of the polar of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) is satisfied, and therefore\n\n\\[\n\\ell\\;=\\;\\text{polar}_{\\omega_{BEF}}(A). \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Final step \u2013 Tangency\n\nA fundamental theorem of pole\u2013polar geometry states:\n\n> *For a circle \\(\\omega\\) and a point \\(X\\notin\\omega\\), the polar of \\(X\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at the pole of \\(X\\).*\n\nApplying this theorem to the circle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) and the point \\(A\\), and using (16), we conclude that the line \\(\\ell\\) (the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\nThus the required statement is proved:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of } \\triangle BEF.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Hence the problem is completely solved.**"
