ution does not establish that every point on the line through \\(H\\) satisfies this condition, only that \\(H\\) itself does. Hence the conclusion is unsupported.\n* **Location:** Final step, the quoted \u201cfundamental theorem of pole\u2013polar geometry\u201d.\n  * **Issue:** Justification Gap \u2013 the theorem is stated incorrectly (the polar of a point outside a circle is not generally tangent to the circle; rather, the polar passes through the pole of the given point, and the pole is the point of tangency of the polar to the polar of the pole). The solution does not correctly apply the theorem to the configuration at hand.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Verification Log\n\n#### 2.1 Configuration and notation\n- The description of the circles, points \\(A,B,C,D\\), and the definition of \\(P\\) as the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is correct and clear. No issues.\n\n#### 2.2 Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 Power of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\)\n- The statement that the power of \\(A\\) with respect to the circumcircle of \\(B,E,F\\) equals \\(AE\\cdot AB\\) follows from the intersecting\u2011chords theorem because the line \\(AB\\) meets the circle at \\(A\\) (outside) and at the two points \\(B\\) and \\(E\\). This is correct.\n\n#### 2.3 Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 Computing \\(AE\\cdot AB\\) via the orthocenter  \n\n1. **Claim:** \u201cthe quadrilateral \\(ACDP\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\). Consequently the chords \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\); in particular \\(\\angle CAP = \\angle DAP\\).\u201d  \n   - The first part is correct: \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), so \\(A,C,D\\) lie on a circle centred at \\(P\\). However, the symmetry of chords \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) with respect to \\(AP\\) is **not** a consequence of this fact; \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the *different* circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), not on the circle \\(ACDP\\). Hence the statement that \\(\\angle CAP = \\angle DAP\\) is