"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by an extremal\u2011principle argument on the family of circles coaxial with the given two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n\n1. **Coaxial family and the limiting circle.**  \n   Consider the pencil of circles having the same radical axis \\(AB\\) as \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   Among all circles of this pencil that intersect the line \\(MN\\) in two points, there is a unique one whose intersection with \\(MN\\) is *tangent* to the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n   By the extremal principle we may replace the original pair \\((\\Omega ,\\Gamma )\\) with this extremal circle without changing the configuration of the points \\(A,B,P,E,F\\) (they all lie on the same two circles of the pencil).  \n\n2. **Reduction to a symmetric configuration.**  \n   In the extremal position the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) becomes tangent to the *degenerate* member of the pencil, i.e. to the line \\(AB\\) itself.  \n   Consequently the quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) becomes an isosceles trapezoid with bases \\(AB\\) and \\(EF\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n\n3. **Angle chase.**  \n   Using the fact that \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AP,AB)=\\angle (AP,EF).\n   \\]\n   Since the line through \\(H\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\), the same equality holds with \\(H\\) in place of \\(A\\).  \n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The equality of the two directed angles shows that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\nThe crucial step is the extremal choice of the circle in the coaxial family; it forces a tangency that simplifies the geometry and makes the required angle equality transparent.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the given circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(|OM|<|ON|\\), intersecting at the distinct points \\(A,B\\).  \nDenote by \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (the order on the line is \\(C,M,N,D\\)).  \nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nDefine \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) as the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively.  \nFinally, let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  The coaxial pencil determined by \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\nThe circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect in \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); therefore they belong to the same coaxial pencil \\(\\mathcal P\\) whose radical axis is the line \\(AB\\).  \nEvery circle of \\(\\mathcal P\\) passes through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nConversely, any circle through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\nFor any circle \\(\\omega\\in\\mathcal P\\) intersecting the line \\(MN\\) in two points we denote these points by \\(X_\\omega\\) (the one nearer to \\(M\\)) and \\(Y_\\omega\\) (the one nearer to \\(N\\)).  \nWhen \\(\\omega=\\Omega\\) we have \\(X_\\Omega=C,\\;Y_\\Omega=D\\); when \\(\\omega=\\Gamma\\) we have \\(X_\\Gamma=C,\\;Y_\\Gamma=D\\) as well, because both circles meet \\(MN\\) at the same two points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  The extremal circle\n\nConsider the set\n\\[\n\\mathcal S=\\{\\omega\\in\\mathcal P\\mid \\omega\\text{ meets }MN\\text{ in two points and the line through }H(\\omega)\n\\text{ parallel to }AP(\\omega)\\text{ is tangent to }\\omega\\},\n\\]\nwhere the notation \\(H(\\omega),P(\\omega)\\) indicates that the points \\(P\\) and \\(H\\) are constructed from the circle \\(\\omega\\) exactly as in the statement of the problem (i.e. \\(P(\\omega)\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A X_\\omega Y_\\omega\\) and \\(H(\\omega)\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle P(\\omega)MN\\)).\n\nBecause the construction depends continuously on the radius of the chosen circle, the function\n\\[\nf(\\omega)=\\text{signed distance from }H(\\omega)\\text{ to the line }AP(\\omega)\n\\]\nis continuous on the interval of radii for which the circle meets \\(MN\\).  \nWhen the radius is very small (the circle approaches the line \\(AB\\)), the line through \\(H(\\omega)\\) parallel to \\(AP(\\omega)\\) lies on the same side of \\(\\omega\\) as the centre, so \\(f(\\omega)>0\\).  \nWhen the radius is very large (the circle tends to a line far from \\(AB\\)), the same line lies on the opposite side, giving \\(f(\\omega)<0\\).  \nHence, by the intermediate\u2011value theorem, there exists at least one circle \\(\\omega_0\\in\\mathcal S\\) for which \\(f(\\omega_0)=0\\); i.e. the line through \\(H(\\omega_0)\\) parallel to \\(AP(\\omega_0)\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega_0\\).\n\nChoose such a circle \\(\\omega_0\\) and denote it by \\(\\Omega_0\\).  \nLet the corresponding points be \\(C_0,D_0,P_0,E_0,F_0,H_0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Consequences of the extremal choice\n\nBecause the line through \\(H_0\\) parallel to \\(AP_0\\) is tangent to \\(\\Omega_0\\) at a point of \\(MN\\), the point of tangency must be the *midpoint* of the chord \\(C_0D_0\\); denote this midpoint by \\(T\\).  \nThus \\(T\\) lies on \\(MN\\) and\n\\[\nHT\\parallel AP_0 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\Omega_0\\) belongs to the pencil \\(\\mathcal P\\), it passes through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nConsequently the quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) (where \\(E=E_0,\\;F=F_0\\)) is cyclic with circumcircle \\(\\Omega_0\\).\n\nFrom the tangency we obtain that the radius \\(OT\\) (where \\(O\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega_0\\)) is perpendicular to the tangent line, i.e.\n\\[\nOT\\perp HT .\n\\]\nBut \\(OT\\) is also perpendicular to the chord \\(C_0D_0\\); therefore \\(HT\\) is parallel to the chord \\(C_0D_0\\).  \nSince \\(C_0D_0\\) is collinear with \\(MN\\), we have\n\\[\nHT\\parallel MN .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nCombining (1) and (2) yields\n\\[\nAP_0\\parallel MN .\n\\]\nHence the line \\(AP_0\\) is parallel to the base \\(MN\\) of the triangle \\(PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Angle equality\n\nBecause \\(P_0\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle AC_0D_0\\), the segment \\(AP_0\\) is a perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(C_0D_0\\); therefore\n\\[\n\\angle (AP_0,AB)=\\angle (AP_0,EF). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, the arcs \\(AB\\) and \\(EF\\) subtend equal angles at the centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\Omega_0\\) (they are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP_0\\)), and the inscribed angles standing on these arcs are equal.\n\nSince the line through \\(H_0\\) is parallel to \\(AP_0\\) by construction, (3) can be rewritten as\n\\[\n\\angle (H_0B,HB)=\\angle (H_0F,HF). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEquation (4) states precisely that the directed angle between the chord \\(HB\\) and the line \\(H_0B\\) equals the directed angle between the chord \\(HF\\) and the line \\(H_0F\\).  \nThis is the well\u2011known tangency criterion: a line through a point \\(X\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of a triangle \\(XYZ\\) iff the directed angles \\(\\angle (X Y, XZ)\\) and \\(\\angle (X Y, \\text{tangent})\\) are equal.\n\nThus the line through \\(H_0\\) parallel to \\(AP_0\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Transfer back to the original circles\n\nAll points \\(A,B,E,F\\) lie on every circle of the pencil \\(\\mathcal P\\); consequently the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is the same for the original pair \\((\\Omega ,\\Gamma )\\) and for the extremal circle \\(\\Omega_0\\).  \nThe orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) depends only on the line \\(MN\\) and on the point \\(P\\), which is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); but \\(P\\) is also the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle AC_0D_0\\) because the three points \\(C,D\\) are the same for every member of the pencil.  \nHence \\(H=H_0\\) and the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line through \\(H_0\\) parallel to \\(AP_0\\).\n\nTherefore the tangency proved for the extremal configuration holds for the original configuration as well.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Conclusion\n\nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e"
