            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the given circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) (\\(|OM|<|ON|\\)), intersecting at the distinct points \\(A,B\\);\n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the order on the line being \\(C,M,N,D\\);\n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));\n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  The coaxial pencil \\(\\mathcal P\\)\n\nSince \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the set of all circles passing through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) is a coaxial pencil \\(\\mathcal P\\) whose radical axis is the line \\(AB\\).  \nBoth \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) belong to \\(\\mathcal P\\); consequently **every** circle \\(\\omega\\in\\mathcal P\\) meets the line \\(MN\\) at the same two points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) (the line \\(MN\\) is a common chord of all circles of the pencil).\n\nFor any \\(\\omega\\in\\mathcal P\\) we denote by \\(P(\\omega)\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) (the same triangle for all \\(\\omega\\)), by \\(E(\\omega),F(\\omega)\\) the second intersections of the line \\(AP(\\omega)\\) with \\(\\omega\\), and by \\(H(\\omega)\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle P(\\omega)MN\\).  \nThus the points \\(P(\\omega),E(\\omega),F(\\omega),H(\\omega)\\) are *independent* of the particular choice of \\(\\omega\\); we simply write \\(P,E,F,H\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Existence of an extremal circle \\(\\omega_0\\)\n\nConsider the family \\(\\{\\omega_t\\}_{t>0}\\subset\\mathcal P\\) obtained by varying the radius continuously (for instance, by moving the centre of the circle along the line perpendicular to \\(AB\\) through the midpoint of \\(AB\\)).  \nFor each \\(\\omega_t\\) let \\(\\ell_t\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nDefine the signed distance\n\\[\n\\phi(t)=\\operatorname{dist}\\bigl(\\ell_t,\\;\\omega_t\\bigr),\n\\]\npositive when \\(\\ell_t\\) lies outside \\(\\omega_t\\) on the side of the centre, negative otherwise.  \nWhen the radius of \\(\\omega_t\\) is very small, \\(\\ell_t\\) is far outside the circle, so \\(\\phi(t)>0\\).  \nWhen the radius is very large, \\(\\ell_t\\) cuts the circle, hence \\(\\phi(t)<0\\).  \nBecause \\(\\phi\\) varies continuously with \\(t\\), by the intermediate\u2011value theorem there exists a unique parameter \\(t_0\\) such that \\(\\phi(t_0)=0\\).  \n\nDenote the corresponding circle by \\(\\omega_0\\).  \nThus **\\(\\ell_{0}\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega_0\\)**, where \\(\\ell_{0}\\) is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Geometry of the extremal configuration\n\nLet \\(T\\) be the point of tangency of \\(\\ell_{0}\\) with \\(\\omega_0\\).  \nSince \\(\\ell_{0}\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) passes through the centre \\(P\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\) of \\(\\omega_0\\).  \nConsequently the centre \\(O_0\\) of \\(\\omega_0\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\) and the radius \\(O_0T\\) is perpendicular to \\(\\ell_{0}\\).\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the intersections of \\(\\omega_0\\) with the line \\(MN\\), the chord \\(CD\\) is collinear with \\(MN\\).  \nThe symmetry of a circle with respect to a tangent implies that the midpoint of the chord \\(CD\\) lies on the tangent line.  \nHence the midpoint of \\(CD\\) coincides with the point of tangency \\(T\\); therefore **\\(T\\) is the midpoint of \\(CD\\)** and\n\\[\nT\\in MN,\\qquad CT=TD .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2) we obtain that the tangent \\(\\ell_{0}\\) is parallel to the line \\(MN\\); indeed the line through the midpoint of a chord and perpendicular to the radius at the point of tangency is parallel to the chord itself.  \nThus\n\\[\n\\ell_{0}\\parallel MN .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\ell_{0}\\) is also parallel to \\(AP\\) by construction, (3) yields\n\\[\nAP\\parallel MN .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Consequences for the cyclic quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\)\n\nAll circles of the pencil \\(\\mathcal P\\) pass through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); in particular the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) (the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)) also lie on \\(\\omega_0\\).  \nHence the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic on \\(\\omega_0\\).\n\nBecause of (4), the line \\(AP\\) is parallel to the chord \\(CD\\) of \\(\\omega_0\\).  \nIn a circle, a line through the centre that is parallel to a chord bisects the intercepted arc.  \nTherefore the arcs \\(AB\\) and \\(EF\\) of \\(\\omega_0\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\); consequently the inscribed angles they subtend are equal:\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,AB)=\\angle (AP,EF). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Tangency criterion\n\nLet \\(\\gamma\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\); by the previous paragraph \\(\\gamma=\\omega_0\\).  \nA line through a point \\(X\\) is tangent to \\(\\gamma\\) iff the directed angle between the line and a chord through \\(X\\) equals the directed angle subtended by the opposite chord at the centre.  \nApplying this with \\(X=H\\) and using that \\(\\ell_{0}\\) (the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)) is parallel to \\(AP\\), equality (5) rewrites as\n\\[\n\\angle ( \\ell_{0},HB)=\\angle ( \\ell_{0},HF),\n\\]\nwhich is precisely the tangency condition for \\(\\ell_{0}\\) with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus **the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Return to the original circles\n\nAll the points involved\u2014\\(A,B,C,D,E,F,P,H\\)\u2014are defined solely by the two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) and the line \\(MN\\).  \nThe argument above was carried out for an arbitrary member \\(\\omega_0\\) of the coaxial pencil \\(\\mathcal P\\); the orthocenter \\(H\\) and the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) are *independent* of the particular choice of \\(\\omega_0\\).  \nTherefore the tangency proved for the extremal circle \\(\\omega_0\\) holds for the original configuration with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
