            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(R\\);\n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r\\;(R<r)\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(C\\) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (different from \\(A\\));\n* \\(D\\) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) (different from \\(A\\));\n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);\n* \\(\\omega\\) the circle with centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PA\\);\n* \\(\\ell\\) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nWe must prove that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1  \n\\(PA=PE=PF\\); consequently the five points \\(A,C,D,E,F\\) lie on the circle \\(\\omega\\). Moreover \\(B\\) also belongs to \\(\\omega\\); hence the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) coincides with \\(\\omega\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\), the distances \\(PC\\) and \\(PD\\) equal the radii of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively.  \nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\); by definition of a circle the distance from \\(M\\) to \\(E\\) equals the radius of \\(\\Omega\\), i.e. \\(ME=MC\\).  \nFrom (1) we have \\(PA=PC\\); therefore \\(PA=PE\\) (both are radii of \\(\\Omega\\)).  \nAnalogously \\(PA=PF\\).  \n\nThus \\(A,C,D,E,F\\) are all at the same distance \\(PA\\) from \\(P\\); they lie on the circle \\(\\omega\\) with centre \\(P\\).  \n\nThe points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are the two intersections of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\). Both circles have centre \\(P\\) at distance \\(PA\\) from \\(A\\); consequently the second intersection \\(B\\) also satisfies \\(PB=PA\\) and therefore \\(B\\in\\omega\\).  \n\nHence the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is exactly \\(\\omega\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2  \nLet \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across the line \\(AP\\). Then \\(H'\\in\\omega\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIn any triangle the reflections of the orthocenter across the three sides lie on the circumcircle.  \nApply this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\). The reflections of \\(H\\) across \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) are points of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), which is \\(\\omega\\) because its centre is \\(P\\) (Lemma\u202f1).  \n\nSince \\(PM=PN\\) (both equal the radius of \\(\\omega\\)), the triangle \\(PMN\\) is isosceles with vertex \\(P\\). The line \\(AP\\) is the external angle bisector of \\(\\angle MPN\\); indeed, \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of chord \\(EF\\) of \\(\\omega\\) (Lemma\u202f1) and the chord \\(EF\\) is symmetric to the base \\(MN\\) with respect to that bisector.  \n\nReflecting the whole configuration across \\(AP\\) interchanges the sides \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) while leaving the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) invariant. Consequently the reflection of the orthocenter \\(H\\) across \\(AP\\) coincides with the reflection of \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\); the latter lies on \\(\\omega\\). Hence \\(H'\\in\\omega\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3  \nThe line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) exactly at the point \\(H'\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA line parallel to the axis of a reflection is invariant under that reflection. Since \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\), reflecting \\(H\\) across \\(AP\\) sends the line \\(\\ell\\) onto itself; therefore the reflected point \\(H'\\) also lies on \\(\\ell\\).  \n\nBy Lemma\u202f2, \\(H'\\) belongs to \\(\\omega\\).  \nBecause \\(H\\) itself does not belong to \\(\\omega\\) (otherwise the orthocenter would coincide with a point on the circumcircle, which is impossible for a non\u2011degenerate triangle), the only intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with \\(\\omega\\) is the point \\(H'\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4  \n\\(H'=B\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have \\(PA=PE=PF\\); therefore \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of chord \\(EF\\) of \\(\\omega\\).  \nConsequently the reflection across \\(AP\\) sends the chord \\(EF\\) onto the chord \\(AB\\) (the two chords are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector).  \n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the chord of \\(\\omega\\) whose endpoints are the intersections of \\(\\omega\\) with the line joining the centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\). Reflecting \\(MN\\) across \\(AP\\) yields the