tly \\(AB\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\).\n\n5. **Lemma\u202f5 \u2013 The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and its reflection \\(H'\\).**  \n   Because \\(PA=PC=PD\\) (Lemma\u202f1), the point \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); thus \\(\\omega\\) is also the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n   In any triangle the reflections of the orthocenter across the three sides lie on the circumcircle (Euler\u2011reflection property). Reflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\); denote the reflected point by \\(H'\\). Then \\(H'\\in\\omega\\).  \n\n   By Lemma\u202f4 the line \\(AB\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\); therefore the reflection across the side \\(MN\\) coincides with the reflection across the line \\(AB\\). Since \\(A,B\\) lie on the same line, reflecting across \\(AB\\) is the same as reflecting across the line through \\(A\\) that is symmetric to \\(AB\\). In particular, because \\(A\\) belongs to both \\(AB\\) and the line \\(AP\\), the reflection of any point across \\(MN\\) is also the reflection of that point across the line \\(AP\\). Hence the point \\(H'\\) obtained by reflecting \\(H\\) in \\(MN\\) is exactly the reflection of \\(H\\) in the line \\(AP\\).\n\n6. **Lemma\u202f6 \u2013 Identification of the reflected point: \\(H'=B\\).**  \n   From Lemma\u202f5 we already know that \\(H'\\) lies on \\(\\omega\\).  \n   Moreover, by construction the quadrilateral \\(AH'P\\) is an isosceles triangle with \\(PA=PH'\\) (both equal to the radius \\(R\\) of \\(\\omega\\)). Hence \\(\\angle AH'P = \\angle APH'\\).  \n\n   Because \\(AB\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\) (Lemma\u202f4), the points \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(AB\\). The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is therefore symmetric with respect to \\(AB\\) as well: the altitudes from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) and from \\(M\\) (resp. \\(N\\)) to \\(PN\\) (resp. \\(PM\\)) are reflected onto each other. Consequently the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(AB\\) must lie on the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) at the unique point that is symmetric to \\(H\\) with respect to the diameter \\(AB\\). The only such point is the second intersection of \\(\\omega\\) with the line \\(AB\\), namely \\(B\\). Hence \\(H'=B\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f3 tells us that the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is \\(\\omega\\).  \n   Lemma\u202f5 shows that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) at the reflected point \\(H'\\).  \n   Lemma\u202f6 identifies this point as \\(B\\). Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\) and is tangent there (a line through a point of a circle that is parallel to the radius through that point is tangent).  \n\n   Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as required. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(R\\);\n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r\\;(R<r)\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(C\\) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (different from \\(A\\));\n* \\(D\\) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) (different from \\(A\\));\n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);\n* \\(\\omega\\) the circle with centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PA\\);\n* \\(\\ell\\) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\);\n* \\(H'\\) the reflection of \\(H\\) across the line \\(AP\\).\n\nWe must prove t