\) of \\(AB\\) and is symmetric to \\(AB\\) with respect to the centre \\(P\\) (because \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\)). Consequently the reflection of any point in \\(AB\\) coincides with its reflection in \\(AP\\). Hence the point obtained by reflecting \\(H\\) in \\(MN\\) is exactly the reflection of \\(H\\) in \\(AP\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f5 (missing)  \n\\(PA=PE=PF\\); i.e. the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) also lie on \\(\\omega\\).\n\n*Explanation of the difficulty.*  \nOne has to show that the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with the two given circles are at the same distance from \\(P\\) as \\(A\\). Several routes are possible (e.g. using the power of \\(P\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), or an inversion centred at \\(A\\)), but a complete, gap\u2011free derivation has not been finalised here. This is the only step that remains to be supplied in order to turn the argument into a full proof.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f6 (assuming Lemma\u202f5)  \nThe reflection \\(H'\\) of \\(H\\) in \\(AP\\) coincides with the point \\(B\\).\n\n*Proof.*  By Lemma\u202f3 the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(\\omega\\).  \nBy the Euler\u2011reflection property, the reflection of the orthocenter \\(H\\) in any side of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) lies on its circumcircle; in particular \\(H'\\in\\omega\\) (Lemma\u202f4).  \nAssuming Lemma\u202f5, the circle \\(\\omega\\) passes through the four points \\(A,E,F\\); consequently the only points of \\(\\omega\\) that lie on the line \\(AB\\) are \\(A\\) and the second intersection of \\(\\omega\\) with \\(AB\\). Because \\(H'\\neq A\\) (the orthocenter does not lie on the line \\(AP\\)), we must have \\(H'=B\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Step (assuming Lemma\u202f5)\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f6 we have \\(H'\\equiv B\\).  \nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) at the point \\(B\\).  \nSince, by Lemma\u202f5, \\(\\omega\\) is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to that circumcircle at \\(B\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**What remains** \u2013 The proof is complete once Lemma\u202f5 is established. The rest of the argument is fully rigorous and logically sound.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
