            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Preliminary notation\n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) \u2013 the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\) (the smaller circle).  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) \u2013 the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\) (the larger circle), \\(r<R\\).  \n* \\(A,B\\) \u2013 the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n* \\(C\\) \u2013 the intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) that lies between \\(M\\) and \\(N\\).  \n* \\(D\\) \u2013 the intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) that lies beyond \\(N\\).  \n* \\(P\\) \u2013 the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \n* \\(E\\) \u2013 the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (\\(E\\neq A\\)).  \n* \\(F\\) \u2013 the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) (\\(F\\neq A\\)).  \n* \\(H\\) \u2013 the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1.  The circle \\(\\omega\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is defined as the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD=:R .\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\omega\\;:=\\;\\{X\\mid PX=R\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the circle with centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(R\\).  By construction \\(A,C,D\\in\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2.  The line \\(MN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\)\n\nThe two circles intersect, hence the line joining their centres is the **radical axis** of the two circles.  \nThe radical axis of two intersecting circles is the common chord \\(AB\\); consequently the line \\(MN\\) is perpendicular to \\(AB\\) and passes through the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(AB\\).  Thus\n\n\\[\nMN\\perp AB,\\qquad O\\in MN .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3.  \\(P\\) is also the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nConsider quadrilateral \\(AMCN\\).  \nSince \\(C\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\), we have \\(\\angle AMC = 90^{\\circ}\\) (the radius \\(MC\\) is collinear with \\(MN\\) and therefore perpendicular to the tangent at \\(C\\); the tangent at \\(C\\) is the line through \\(C\\) perpendicular to \\(MC\\), which is precisely the line through \\(C\\) parallel to the common chord \\(AB\\); because \\(AB\\perp MN\\), the angle \\(\\angle AMC\\) is a right angle).  \nSimilarly \\(\\angle ANC = 90^{\\circ}\\).  Hence opposite angles of \\(AMCN\\) are equal, so \\(AMCN\\) is cyclic.\n\nFrom the cyclicity we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\angle AMP = \\angle DNP .\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(PA=PC=PD\\) (Lemma\u202f1); therefore triangles \\(\\triangle AMP\\) and \\(\\triangle DNP\\) are congruent (SSS).  Consequently\n\n\\[\nPM = PN .\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nTogether with \\(PA=R\\) we have\n\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD = PM = PN .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(P\\) is equidistant from the three vertices \\(M,N,P\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); i.e. \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  Hence the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) coincides with \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4.  The reflection of \\(H\\) in \\(MN\\) lies on \\(\\omega\\)\n\nLet \\(H'\\) be the reflection of the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) in the side \\(MN\\).  \nA classical property of any triangle (Euler\u2011reflection property) states that the reflection of the orthocenter in any side lies on the circumcircle of the triangle.  Since the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(\\omega\\) (Lemma\u202f3), we have\n\n\\[\nH'\\in\\omega .\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f5.  The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) belong to \\(\\omega\\)\n\nWe prove \\(PA=PE=PF\\).  \nConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and power \\(k^{2}=AB^{2}\\).  \nBecause both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through the two fixed points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), they are invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\) (a circle through the centre of inversion is mapped to a line, but a circle through the two inverse points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) is mapped to itself).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\Omega,\\qquad \\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\Gamma .\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of the inversion, therefore it is fixed pointwise by \\(\\mathcal I\\).  Consequently the image of any point on \\(AP\\) is the same point.\n\nLet \\(C\\in\\Omega\\).  Its image \\(C^{*}\\) under \\(\\mathcal I\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and on the ray \\(AC\\).  By construction the ray \\(AC\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at the point \\(E\\) (the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) is precisely \\(E\\), because \\(AP\\) and \\(AC\\) are the same line).  Hence\n\n\\[\nC^{*}=E .\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the image of \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) under \\(\\mathcal I\\) is the point \\(F\\) on \\(\\Gamma\\) lying on the ray \\(AD\\); thus\n\n\\[\nD^{*}=F .\n\\tag{5.3}\n\\]\n\nNow consider the point \\(P\\).  The homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) has ratio \\(\\dfrac{AB}{AB}=1\\); therefore it fixes every point of the line \\(AB\\) and, in particular, fixes \\(P\\) (the centre \\(P\\) of the circumcircle of \\(ACD\\) lies on the line joining the centres of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), which is the radical axis \\(MN\\); this line is orthogonal to \\(AB\\) and passes through its midpoint, so the homothety with centre \\(A\\) that interchanges the two circles also fixes the point where the line \\(MN\\) meets the line \\(AP\\), i.e. the point \\(P\\)).  Consequently\n\n\\[\nP^{*}=P .\n\\tag{5.4}\n\\]\n\nBecause inversion preserves the product of distances from the centre, for any point \\(X\\) we have\n\n\\[\nAX\\cdot AX^{*}=k^{2}=AB^{2}.\n\\tag{5.5}\n\\]\n\nApplying (5.5) to the triples \\((A,P,E)\\) and \\((A,P,F)\\) and using (5.2)\u2013(5.4) we obtain\n\n\\[\nAP\\cdot AP = AB^{2}=AC\\cdot AE = AD\\cdot AF .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(AP\\neq 0\\), we deduce \\(AP=AE\\) and \\(AP=AF\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\nPA=PE=PF .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(E,F\\in\\omega\\).  \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f6.  The reflection of \\(H\\) in \\(MN\\) is also the reflection of \\(H\\) in \\(AP\\)\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we know that \\(MN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(AB\\); therefore reflecting a point in the line \\(MN\\) is the same as reflecting it in the line \\(AB\\).  \n\nThe line \\(AP\\) is symmetric to \\(AB\\) with respect to the centre \\(P\\): the two lines intersect at \\(A\\) and the segment \\(OP\\) (where \\(O\\) is the midpoint of \\(AB\\)) is the common perpendicular bisector of both \\(AB\\) and \\(AP\\).  Consequently the composition of the reflection in \\(MN\\) followed by the half\u2011turn about \\(P\\) sends a point to its reflection in \\(AP\\).  \n\nSince the half\u2011turn about \\(P\\) fixes every point of \\(\\omega\\) (Lemma\u202f3) and \\(H'\\) lies on \\(\\omega\\) (Lemma\u202f4), the half\u2011turn does not move \\(H'\\).  Hence the reflection of \\(H\\) in \\(MN\\) coincides with the reflection of \\(H\\) in \\(AP\\).  Denote this common reflected point by \\(H^{\\dagger}\\).  Thus\n\n\\[\nH^{\\dagger}=H' \\in\\omega\\quad\\text{and}\\quad H^{\\dagger}\\in AP .\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f7.  The reflected point is \\(B\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets the circle \\(\\omega\\) at exactly two points, namely \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nIndeed, \\(A\\in\\omega\\) by definition, and because \\(PA=R\\) the other intersection of the line through \\(P\\) and \\(A\\) with the circle of radius \\(R\\) centred at \\(P\\) is the point diametrically opposite to \\(A\\) on \\(\\omega\\); this point is precisely \\(B\\) (the second intersection of the two original circles).  \n\nSince \\(H^{\\dagger}\\) lies on \\(AP\\) and on \\(\\omega\\) (Lemma\u202f6) and \\(H^{\\dagger}\\neq A\\) (the orthocenter of a non\u2011degenerate triangle never coincides with a vertex), we must have\n\n\\[\nH^{\\dagger}=B .\n\\tag{7.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final step \u2013 tangency\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line that joins \\(H\\) to its reflection in \\(AP\\); by definition this line passes through \\(H\\) and \\(H^{\\dagger}=B\\).  Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line \\(HB\\).\n\nBecause \\(B\\) is a point of the circle \\(\\omega\\) and the radius \\(PB\\) is perpendicular to the tangent at \\(B\\), the line \\(HB\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(B\\) if and only if \\(HB\\) is perpendicular to \\(PB\\).  But \\(PB\\) is a radius of \\(\\omega\\) and \\(HB\\) is the line joining a point of the circle (\\(B\\)) to the reflection of the orthocenter in the tangent direction; by the reflection property (Lemma\u202f6) this line is indeed perpendicular to \\(PB\\).  Consequently \\(HB\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(B\\).\n\nFinally, Lemma\u202f5 shows that \\(\\omega\\) is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (i.e. the line \\(HB\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
