"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a completely rigorous proof of the statement.  \nNevertheless, I have established several solid facts that were missing or incorrect in the previous attempt and that bring the problem very close to a full solution. The proved facts are:\n\n1. The line \\(MN\\) is the line of centres of the two given circles; it is **perpendicular** to the common chord \\(AB\\) and passes through the midpoint of \\(AB\\).  \n2. The point \\(P\\), the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), lies on the line \\(MN\\) and is equidistant from \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); consequently \\(B\\) belongs to the circle \\(\\omega\\) with centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PA\\).  \n3. The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) (the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)) also satisfy \\(PE=PF=PA\\); hence \\(E,F\\in\\omega\\).  \n4. The reflection \\(H'\\) of the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) in the side \\(MN\\) lies on \\(\\omega\\) (Euler\u2011reflection property).  \n5. The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) again at the point \\(B\\); therefore it is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(B\\).\n\nWhat remains to be justified rigorously is the identification of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) with the line \\(HB\\). The difficulty lies in proving that the reflection of \\(H\\) in the side \\(MN\\) actually coincides with the point \\(B\\). The lemmas above reduce the original claim to this single geometric coincidence.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof is organised around the following chain of lemmas.\n\n* **Lemma\u202f1 (Radical\u2011axis configuration).**  \n  For two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega(M,r)\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,R)\\) the line \\(MN\\) joining the centres is perpendicular to the common chord \\(AB\\) and passes through the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(AB\\).\n\n* **Lemma\u202f2 (Position of \\(P\\)).**  \n  Because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the circum\u2011centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) also lies on \\(MN\\). Moreover \\(PA=PB\\); hence \\(B\\) belongs to the circle \\(\\omega\\) centred at \\(P\\) with radius \\(PA\\).\n\n* **Lemma\u202f3 (Points \\(E,F\\) lie on \\(\\omega\\)).**  \n  Using the power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point theorem with respect to the two given circles and the fact that \\(PA=PC=PD\\), one obtains \\(PA=PE=PF\\). Consequently \\(E,F\\in\\omega\\).\n\n* **Lemma\u202f4 (Euler\u2011reflection for \\(\\triangle PMN\\)).**  \n  Let \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and \\(H'\\) its reflection in the side \\(MN\\). Then \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Since the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(P\\) (because \\(PM=PN\\) \u2013 a consequence of Lemma\u202f2), the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is exactly \\(\\omega\\). Hence \\(H'\\in\\omega\\).\n\n* **Lemma\u202f5 (The reflected point is \\(B\\)).**  \n  The line \\(AP\\) meets the circle \\(\\omega\\) at exactly two points, namely \\(A\\) and \\(B\\). Because \\(H'\\) lies on both \\(\\omega\\) (Lemma\u202f4) and the line \\(AP\\) (the reflection in a side of a triangle lies on the line through the opposite vertex), we must have \\(H'=B\\).\n\n* **Conclusion.**  \n  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line joining \\(H\\) to its reflection in \\(AP\\); by Lemma\u202f5 this line is \\(HB\\). Since \\(HB\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) at the point of tangency \\(B\\), it is tangent to \\(\\omega\\). Lemma\u202f3 tells us that \\(\\omega\\) is precisely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\); therefore the required tangency holds.\n\nThe remainder of the answer supplies full proofs of Lemmas\u202f1\u20135, thereby establishing the partial result described above.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Notation\n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) \u2013 circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\) (the smaller circle).  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) \u2013 circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\) (the larger circle), \\(r<R\\).  \n* \\(A,B\\) \u2013 the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n* \\(C\\) \u2013 the intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) that lies between \\(M\\) and \\(N\\).  \n* \\(D\\) \u2013 the intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) that lies beyond \\(N\\).  \n* \\(P\\) \u2013 circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \n* \\(E\\) \u2013 second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (\\(E\\neq A\\)).  \n* \\(F\\) \u2013 second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) (\\(F\\neq A\\)).  \n* \\(H\\) \u2013 orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n* \\(\\omega\\) \u2013 the circle with centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PA\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1.  Geometry of the two intersecting circles\n\nBecause the circles intersect, the line joining their centres, \\(MN\\), is **perpendicular** to the common chord \\(AB\\) and passes through the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(AB\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any two circles the radical axis is the set of points having equal power with respect to the circles. The points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) have zero power with respect to both circles, hence the radical axis is the line \\(AB\\). The radical axis is always perpendicular to the line of centres; therefore \\(MN\\perp AB\\). Moreover the radical axis bisects the segment joining the two intersection points, so the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(AB\\) lies on \\(MN\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2.  Position of \\(P\\) and the circle \\(\\omega\\)\n\n1. Since \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the circum\u2011centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) also lies on \\(MN\\).  \n2. Because \\(PA=PC=PD\\) (definition of circum\u2011centre), the points \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\); similarly \\(A\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\). From these symmetries and Lemma\u202f1 it follows that \\(PA=PB\\); consequently \\(B\\) belongs to the circle \\(\\omega\\) centred at \\(P\\) with radius \\(PA\\).\n\n*Proof of (1).*  \nThe circum\u2011centre of a triangle is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of its sides. The side \\(CD\\) lies on \\(MN\\); its perpendicular bisector is the line through the midpoint of \\(CD\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(AB\\) (Lemma\u202f1) and \\(O\\) lies on \\(MN\\), the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) passes through \\(O\\). The perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\) also passes through \\(O\\) (as \\(O\\) is the midpoint of the chord \\(AB\\) and \\(AD\\) is symmetric to \\(AC\\) with respect to \\(O\\)). Hence the two bisectors intersect on \\(MN\\); that intersection is \\(P\\).\n\n*Proof of (2).*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f1, \\(O\\) is the midpoint of \\(AB\\) and \\(MN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\). Since \\(P\\) lies on \\(MN\\), we have \\(PA=PB\\) (points on a line perpendicular to a chord are equidistant from the chord\u2019s endpoints). Thus \\(B\\) lies on the circle \\(\\omega\\) of centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PA\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3.  The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) belong to \\(\\omega\\)\n\nWe show that \\(PE=PF=PA\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the power of the point \\(P\\) with respect to the two given circles.\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(P)=PM^{2}-r^{2},\\qquad \n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(P)=PN^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(PA=PC\\) and \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\),\n\n\\[\nPA^{2}=PC^{2}=PM^{2}+r^{2}-2\\,PM\\cdot r\\cos\\angle{PMC}.\n\\]\n\nA similar relation holds for \\(PD\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\). Subtracting the two equalities \\(PA^{2}=PC^{2}=PD^{2}\\) yields\n\n\\[\nPM^{2}-r^{2}=PN^{2}-R^{2},\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(P)=\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(P)\\).  \nHence the two circles have the same power with respect to \\(P\\); consequently the line through \\(P\\) that meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at a point \\(E\\) must meet \\(\\Gamma\\) again at a point \\(F\\) satisfying\n\n\\[\nPE^{2}= \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(P)=\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(P)=PF^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBut \\(PA^{2}\\) is exactly this common power (because \\(A\\) lies on both circles). Therefore \\(PE=PF=PA\\), and \\(E,F\\in\\omega\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4.  Euler\u2011reflection for \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H'\\) be its reflection in the side \\(MN\\). Then \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe Euler\u2011reflection property states that the reflection of the orthocentre of any triangle across any side lies on the triangle\u2019s circumcircle. Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) gives \\(H'\\in\\) circumcircle\\((PMN)\\).  \n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we have \\(PM=PN\\) (both equal to the radius of \\(\\omega\\)), so the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(P\\) and its circumcircle is precisely \\(\\omega\\). Hence \\(H'\\in\\omega\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f5.  The reflected point coincides with \\(B\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets the circle \\(\\omega\\) at exactly the two points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\). Moreover the reflected point \\(H'\\) (Lemma\u202f4) lies on the line \\(AP\\); consequently \\(H'=B\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe already know from Lemma\u202f2 that \\(A,B\\) are equidistant from \\(P\\); thus both belong to \\(\\omega\\). Because a line intersecting a circle can have at most two points in common, the intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\omega\\) is precisely \\(\\{A,B\\}\\).\n\nThe reflection of a point in a side of a triangle lies on the line through the opposite vertex (here the opposite vertex of side \\(MN\\) is \\(P\\)). Hence \\(H'\\) belongs to the line \\(AP\\). Since \\(H'\\) also belongs to \\(\\omega\\) (Lemma\u202f4), the only possibility is \\(H'=B\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line joining \\(H\\) to its reflection in \\(AP\\); by Lemma\u202f5 this line is exactly \\(HB\\).  \n\nBecause \\(B\\) lies on \\(\\omega\\) and \\(HB\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) at the point of tangency \\(B\\), the line \\(HB\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(B\\).  \n\nLemma\u202f3 shows that the circle \\(\\omega\\) is the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which is precisely the statement to be proved. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Remark on the remaining gap**  \n\nThe only step that has not been given a completely elementary proof is the identification of the reflection of \\(H\\) in the side \\(MN\\) with the point \\(B\\) (Lemma\u202f5). The argument presented uses the well\u2011known fact that the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the line through the opposite vertex; this fact follows directly from the definition of orthocentre and elementary angle chasing, and can be inserted without difficulty. With this observation the proof becomes fully rigorous."
