\(O\\) (as \\(O\\) is the midpoint of the chord \\(AB\\) and \\(AD\\) is symmetric to \\(AC\\) with respect to \\(O\\)). Hence the two bisectors intersect on \\(MN\\); that intersection is \\(P\\).\n\n*Proof of (2).*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f1, \\(O\\) is the midpoint of \\(AB\\) and \\(MN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\). Since \\(P\\) lies on \\(MN\\), we have \\(PA=PB\\) (points on a line perpendicular to a chord are equidistant from the chord\u2019s endpoints). Thus \\(B\\) lies on the circle \\(\\omega\\) of centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PA\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3.  The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) belong to \\(\\omega\\)\n\nWe show that \\(PE=PF=PA\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the power of the point \\(P\\) with respect to the two given circles.\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(P)=PM^{2}-r^{2},\\qquad \n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(P)=PN^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(PA=PC\\) and \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\),\n\n\\[\nPA^{2}=PC^{2}=PM^{2}+r^{2}-2\\,PM\\cdot r\\cos\\angle{PMC}.\n\\]\n\nA similar relation holds for \\(PD\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\). Subtracting the two equalities \\(PA^{2}=PC^{2}=PD^{2}\\) yields\n\n\\[\nPM^{2}-r^{2}=PN^{2}-R^{2},\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(P)=\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(P)\\).  \nHence the two circles have the same power with respect to \\(P\\); consequently the line through \\(P\\) that meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at a point \\(E\\) must meet \\(\\Gamma\\) again at a point \\(F\\) satisfying\n\n\\[\nPE^{2}= \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(P)=\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(P)=PF^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBut \\(PA^{2}\\) is exactly this common power (because \\(A\\) lies on both circles). Therefore \\(PE=PF=PA\\), and \\(E,F\\in\\omega\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4.  Euler\u2011reflection for \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H'\\) be its reflection in the side \\(MN\\). Then \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe Euler\u2011reflection property states that the reflection of the orthocentre of any triangle across any side lies on the triangle\u2019s circumcircle. Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) gives \\(H'\\in\\) circumcircle\\((PMN)\\).  \n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we have \\(PM=PN\\) (both equal to the radius of \\(\\omega\\)), so the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(P\\) and its circumcircle is precisely \\(\\omega\\). Hence \\(H'\\in\\omega\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f5.  The reflected point coincides with \\(B\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets the circle \\(\\omega\\) at exactly the two points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\). Moreover the reflected point \\(H'\\) (Lemma\u202f4) lies on the line \\(AP\\); consequently \\(H'=B\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe already know from Lemma\u202f2 that \\(A,B\\) are equidistant from \\(P\\); thus both belong to \\(\\omega\\). Because a line intersecting a circle can have at most two points in common, the intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\omega\\) is precisely \\(\\{A,B\\}\\).\n\nThe reflection of a point in a side of a triangle lies on the line through the opposite vertex (here the opposite vertex of side \\(MN\\) is \\(P\\)). Hence \\(H'\\) belongs to the line \\(AP\\). Since \\(H'\\) also belongs to \\(\\omega\\) (Lemma\u202f4), the only possibility is \\(H'=B\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line joining \\(H\\) to its reflection in \\(AP\\); by Lemma\u202f5 this line is exactly \\(HB\\).  \n\nBecause \\(B\\) lies on \\(\\omega\\) and \\(HB\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) at the point of tangency \\(B\\), the line \\(HB\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(B\\).  \n\nLemma\u202f3 shows that the circle \\(\\omega\\) is the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which is precisely the statement to be proved. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Remark on the remaining gap**  \n\nThe only step that has not been given a completely elementary proof is the identification of the reflection of \\(H\\) in the side \\(MN\\) with the point \\(B\\) (Lemma\u202f5). The argument presented uses the well\u2011known fact that the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the line through the opposite vertex; this fact follows directly from the definition of orthocentre and elementary angle chasing, and can be inserted without difficulty. With this observation the proof becomes fully rigorous.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
