---\n### Notation\n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) \u2013 circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\) (the smaller circle).  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) \u2013 circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\) (the larger circle), \\(r<R\\).  \n* \\(A,B\\) \u2013 the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n* \\(C\\) \u2013 the intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) that lies between \\(M\\) and \\(N\\).  \n* \\(D\\) \u2013 the intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) that lies beyond \\(N\\).  \n* \\(P\\) \u2013 circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \n* \\(E\\) \u2013 second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (\\(E\\neq A\\)).  \n* \\(F\\) \u2013 second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) (\\(F\\neq A\\)).  \n* \\(H\\) \u2013 orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n* \\(\\omega\\) \u2013 the circle with centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PA\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1.  Geometry of the two intersecting circles\n\nBecause the circles intersect, the line joining their centres, \\(MN\\), is **perpendicular** to the common chord \\(AB\\) and passes through the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(AB\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any two circles the radical axis is the set of points having equal power with respect to the circles. The points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) have zero power with respect to both circles, hence the radical axis is the line \\(AB\\). The radical axis is always perpendicular to the line of centres; therefore \\(MN\\perp AB\\). Moreover the radical axis bisects the segment joining the two intersection points, so the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(AB\\) lies on \\(MN\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2.  Position of \\(P\\) and the circle \\(\\omega\\)\n\n1. Since \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the circum\u2011centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) also lies on \\(MN\\).  \n2. Because \\(PA=PC=PD\\) (definition of circum\u2011centre), the points \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\); similarly \\(A\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\). From these symmetries and Lemma\u202f1 it follows that \\(PA=PB\\); consequently \\(B\\) belongs to the circle \\(\\omega\\) centred at \\(P\\) with radius \\(PA\\).\n\n*Proof of (1).*  \nThe circum\u2011centre of a triangle is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of its sides. The side \\(CD\\) lies on \\(MN\\); its perpendicular bisector is the line through the midpoint of \\(CD\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(AB\\) (Lemma\u202f1) and \\(O\\) lies on \\(MN\\), the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) passes through \\(O\\). The perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\) also passes through \\(O\\) (as \\(O\\) is the midpoint of the chord \\(AB\\) and \\(AD\\) is symmetric to \\(AC\\) with respect to \\(O\\)). Hence the two bisectors intersect on \\(MN\\); that intersection is \\(P\\).\n\n*Proof of (2).*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f1, \\(O\\) is the midpoint of \\(AB\\) and \\(MN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\). Since \\(P\\) lies on \\(MN\\), we have \\(PA=PB\\) (points on a line perpendicular to a chord are equidistant from the chord\u2019s endpoints). Thus \\(B\\) lies on the circle \\(\\omega\\) of centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PA\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3.  The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) belong to \\(\\omega\\)\n\nWe show that \\(PE=PF=PA\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the power of the point \\(P\\) with respect to the two given circles.\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(P)=PM^{2}-r^{2},\\qquad \n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(P)=PN^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(PA=PC\\) and \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\),\n\n\\[\nPA^{2}=PC^{2}=PM^{2}+r^{2}-2\\,PM