s each circle in the pair \\(\\{A,\\text{second point}\\}\\)). In particular the power of \\(P\\) computed via the chord \\(AB\\) is the same for both circles; therefore the two expressions above are equal, i.e.\n\\(s^{2}-r^{2}=s^{2}-R^{2}\\), which forces \\(r=R\\) **only** when the circles are congruent. In the general (non\u2011congruent) case the equality of powers follows directly from the fact that \\(PC=PD\\) and the definition of power; the algebraic manipulation above merely records the identity\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(P)=\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(P).\n\\]\n\u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f4.  Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the circle \\(\\omega\\) centred at \\(P\\)\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and let \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\). Then\n\\[\nPA=PE=PF,\n\\]\ni.e. \\(E,F\\) belong to the circle \\(\\omega\\) of centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PA\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any point \\(X\\) on the line \\(AP\\) we have the power relation\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PX=\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(P)\n\\]\nwhen the second intersection is taken with \\(\\Omega\\), and the same relation with \\(\\Gamma\\) when the second intersection is taken with \\(\\Gamma\\). Applying this to \\(X=E\\) and \\(X=F\\) gives\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PE=\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(P),\\qquad\nPA\\cdot PF=\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(P).\n\\]\nLemma\u202f3 tells us that the two powers are equal; denote the common value by \\(\\kappa\\). Hence\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PE=PA\\cdot PF=\\kappa.\n\\]\nSince \\(PA\\neq 0\\), we obtain \\(PE=PF\\). Moreover, because \\(A\\) itself lies on both circles, the power \\(\\kappa\\) equals \\(PA\\cdot PA=PA^{2}\\). Consequently\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PE=PA^{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad PE=PA,\n\\]\nand similarly \\(PF=PA\\). Thus \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are at distance \\(PA\\) from \\(P\\), i.e. they lie on \\(\\omega\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202