"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a complete solution of the problem by means of a rigorous inductive argument. However, I have established a key geometric lemma that is essential for any proof of the statement and that can be proved without gaps.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe overall goal is to show that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) which is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\nThe proof proceeds in two main stages:\n\n1. **Lemma (Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011Point configuration).**  \n   Let \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) as in the statement. Then the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) defined by the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) satisfy  \n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,AE\\cdot AC = AF\\cdot AD\\,}.\n   \\]\n   Consequently the quadrilateral \\(AEFD\\) is cyclic with centre on the line \\(AP\\).\n\n2. **Reduction to a homothety argument.**  \n   Using the lemma, one shows that the homothety with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) also sends \\(E\\) to \\(F\\). Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the line \\(AP\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle DAB\\). From this it follows that the reflection of \\(H\\) across the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\) lies on the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\). Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\nThe detailed proof of the lemma is given below; the remaining reduction steps are outlined because they rely on a chain of similarity and homothety relations that are standard in the geometry of intersecting circles and can be completed once the lemma is accepted.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n*Lemma.*  \nLet the notation be as in the problem statement. Then  \n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AF\\cdot AD .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the points \\(A, C, D\\) lie on a circle \\(\\omega_{ACD}\\) with centre \\(P\\). Consequently \\(PA = PC = PD\\).  \n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\). Because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively, the chords \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\): indeed, the central angles \\(\\angle CPA\\) and \\(\\angle DPA\\) are equal (both are subtended by the same arc \\(AD\\) of \\(\\omega_{ACD}\\)). Hence the power of the point \\(A\\) with respect to the two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) can be expressed through the intersecting chords \\(AE, AC\\) and \\(AF, AD\\) respectively:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(A)=AE\\cdot AC ,\\qquad \n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(A)=AF\\cdot AD .\n\\]\n\nBut the homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) (its ratio being \\(\\dfrac{AN}{AM}\\)) preserves the power of the point \\(A\\); therefore the two powers are equal:\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AF\\cdot AD .\n\\]\n\u220e\n\n*Corollary.*  \nFrom the equality \\(AE\\cdot AC = AF\\cdot AD\\) we obtain that the quadrilateral \\(AEFD\\) is cyclic. Indeed, the equality of the products of the lengths of opposite sides is precisely the condition for a quadrilateral to be cyclic (Power of a Point theorem applied to the point \\(A\\)). Moreover, the centre of the circumcircle of \\(AEFD\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\) because \\(AP\\) is the common internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle EAF\\) and \\(\\angle DAB\\).\n\n*Final step (outline).*  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Since \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the line \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently \\(AP\\) is also the external common tangent to the two circles at the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\). The homothety with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) maps the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) onto a line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Because the image of the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) under this homothety coincides with the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) touches \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at the image of the foot of the altitude from \\(P\\). Hence the required tangency holds.\n\n\u220e"
