{A}(E)=F .\\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n5. **The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   * Because \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(PN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\) and the segment \\(PM\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\).  \n   * Hence \\(PN\\perp AD\\) and \\(PM\\perp AC\\).  \n   * The altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is therefore the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); denote its foot on \\(MN\\) by \\(X\\). By definition \\(H\\) is the intersection of the three altitudes, so \\(PH\\) is the line \\(PX\\) and\n     \\[\n     PH\\perp MN .\\tag{5}\n     \\]\n\n6. **Key angle relation.**  \n   From (1) and (5) we have\n   \\[\n   \\angle (PH,AB)=\\angle (MN,AB)=90^{\\circ}.\n   \\]\n   Since \\(AB\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the line \\(AB\\) is the external common tangent to the two circles at the points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\). Consequently the angle that \\(AB\\) makes with the line \\(AP\\) equals the angle that the common internal tangent \\(AP\\) makes with the line joining the centres \\(MN\\); i.e.\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AP,MN)=\\angle (AB,AP). \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (5) and (6) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle (PH,AP)=\\angle (AB,AP). \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Because the line through \\(H\\) that is **parallel** to \\(AP\\) has the same direction as \\(AP\\), (7) tells us that this parallel line makes with \\(PH\\) exactly the same angle as the chord \\(AB\\) makes with \\(AP\\).\n\n7. **Tangency to the circumcircle of \\(BEF\\).**  \n   * From Lemma\u202f1 the points \\(E,F\\) lie on the circle \\(\\omega_{AEFD}\\) whose centre lies on \\(AP\\).  \n   * The points \\(B,E,F\\) are not collinear; denote by \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n   * Because \\(B\\) belongs to both original circles, the power of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\omega_{AEFD}\\) is\n     \\[\n     \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega_{AEFD}}(B)=BA\\cdot BB=0,\n     \\]\n     i.e. \\(B\\) lies on \\(\\omega_{AEFD}\\) as well. Hence the three circles \\(\\omega_{AEFD},\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) all pass through the common points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); consequently the three circles are coaxial and share the same radical axis \\(AB\\).\n\n   * The centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\). By the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) (4) the segment \\(BE\\) is mapped onto \\(BF\\); therefore the perpendicular bisector of \\(BE\\) is mapped onto the perpendicular bisector of \\(BF\\). Hence these two bisectors are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\); consequently the centre \\(O\\) lies on the line through the midpoint of \\(AB\\) that is **perpendicular** to \\(AP\\).\n\n   * Let \\(T\\) be the point where the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\). Because this line is parallel to \\(AP\\), the radius \\(OT\\) is perpendicular to it. Therefore\n     \\[\n     \\angle (OT,AP)=90^{\\circ}.\n     \\]\n     Using the description of \\(O\\) obtained above, the radius \\(OT\\) is also perpendicular to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at the point \\(T\\).\n\n   * In other words, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\); this is exactly the statement to be proved.\n\n\u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the two given circles be \\(\\Omega(M,r)\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,R)\\) with \\(r<R\\).  \nThey intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order\n\\[\nC\\;-\\;M\\;-\\;N\\;-\\;D .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1 Preliminary observations\n\nBecause the centres of the circles lie on the same line, the radical axis of the two circles is the common chord \\(AB\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(P\\) be the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). By definition  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2 Lemma 1 \u2013 Equality of power products\n\nFor the point \\(A\\) the power with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) equals the product of the two chords through \\(A\\):\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(A)=AE\\cdot AC .\n\\]\nSimilarly,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(A)=AF\\cdot AD .\n\\]\n\nThe homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) and ratio \\(\\dfrac{AN}{AM}\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\); a homothety preserves the power of its centre, hence the two powers are equal:\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AF\\cdot AD .\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nThus the quadrilateral \\(AEFD\\) is cyclic (Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011Point theorem). Denote its circum\u2011circle by \\(\\omega_{AEFD}\\). Because the equal chords \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\), the centre of \\(\\omega_{AEFD}\\) lies on \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3 Lemma 2 \u2013 The homothety \\( \\mathcal H_{A}\\)\n\nThe same homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) sends every point of \\(\\Omega\\) to the corresponding point of \\(\\Gamma\\); in particular  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H_{A}(E)=F .\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the triangles \\(ACE\\) and \\(ADF\\) are directly similar with centre \\(A\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4 The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segments \\(PC\\) and \\(PD\\) are radii of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively; therefore  \n\n\\[\nPC\\perp AM,\\qquad PD\\perp AN .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the centres of the circles, the lines \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) are the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nPM\\perp AC,\\qquad PN\\perp AD .\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nThe altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); let its foot on \\(MN\\) be \\(X\\). By definition of orthocenter,\n\\[\nH = P X\\quad\\text{and}\\quad PH\\perp MN .\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5 Relation between the directions of \\(AP\\) and \\(PH\\)\n\nFrom (2.1) we have \\(AB\\perp MN\\). Combining this with (2.6) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (PH,AB)=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) is the internal common tangent of the two circles at the points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); consequently the angle that \\(AP\\) makes with the line of centres \\(MN\\) equals the angle that the external common tangent \\(AB\\) makes with \\(AP\\):\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,MN)=\\angle (AB,AP). \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting (2.8) from (2.7) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (PH,AP)=\\angle (AB,AP). \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\nThus the direction of \\(PH\\) is obtained from the direction of \\(AP\\) by rotating through the same angle that the chord \\(AB\\) makes with \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6 The circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nBecause \\(B\\) belongs to both original circles, it also belongs to the circle \\(\\omega_{AEFD}\\) (the three circles are coaxial). Hence the points \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic; denote their circum\u2011circle by \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\nApply the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) (2.4) to the segment \\(BE\\); it sends \\(BE\\) onto \\(BF\\). Therefore the perpendicular bisector of \\(BE\\) is mapped onto the perpendicular bisector of \\(