            "role": "user",
            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet the two given circles be \\(\\Omega(M,r)\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,R)\\) with \\(r<R\\).  \nThey intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order\n\\[\nC\\;-\\;M\\;-\\;N\\;-\\;D .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1 Preliminary observations\n\nBecause the centres of the circles lie on the same line, the radical axis of the two circles is the common chord \\(AB\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(P\\) be the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). By definition  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2 Lemma 1 \u2013 Equality of power products\n\nFor the point \\(A\\) the power with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) equals the product of the two chords through \\(A\\):\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(A)=AE\\cdot AC .\n\\]\nSimilarly,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(A)=AF\\cdot AD .\n\\]\n\nThe homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) and ratio \\(\\dfrac{AN}{AM}\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\); a homothety preserves the power of its centre, hence the two powers are equal:\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AF\\cdot AD .\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nThus the quadrilateral \\(AEFD\\) is cyclic (Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011Point theorem). Denote its circum\u2011circle by \\(\\omega_{AEFD}\\). Because the equal chords \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\), the centre of \\(\\omega_{AEFD}\\) lies on \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3 Lemma 2 \u2013 The homothety \\( \\mathcal H_{A}\\)\n\nThe same homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) sends every point of \\(\\Omega\\) to the corresponding point of \\(\\Gamma\\); in particular  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{A}(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H_{A}(E)=F .\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the triangles \\(ACE\\) and \\(ADF\\) are directly similar with centre \\(A\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4 The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segments \\(PC\\) and \\(PD\\) are radii of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively; therefore  \n\n\\[\nPC\\perp AM,\\qquad PD\\perp AN .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the centres of the circles, the lines \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) are the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nPM\\perp AC,\\qquad PN\\perp AD .\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nThe altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); let its foot on \\(MN\\) be \\(X\\). By definition of orthocenter,\n\\[\nH = P X\\quad\\text{and}\\quad PH\\perp MN .\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5 Relation between the directions of \\(AP\\) and \\(PH\\)\n\nFrom (2.1) we have \\(AB\\perp MN\\). Combining this with (2.6) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (PH,AB)=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) is the internal common tangent of the two circles at the points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); consequently the angle that \\(AP\\) makes with the line of centres \\(MN\\) equals the angle that the external common tangent \\(AB\\) makes with \\(AP\\):\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,MN)=\\angle (AB,AP). \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting (2.8) from (2.7) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (PH,AP)=\\angle (AB,AP). \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\nThus the direction of \\(PH\\) is obtained from the direction of \\(AP\\) by rotating through the same angle that the chord \\(AB\\) makes with \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6 The circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nBecause \\(B\\) belongs to both original circles, it also belongs to the circle \\(\\omega_{AEFD}\\) (the three circles are coaxial). Hence the points \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic; denote their circum\u2011circle by \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\nApply the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_{A}\\) (2.4) to the segment \\(BE\\); it sends \\(BE\\) onto \\(BF\\). Therefore the perpendicular bisector of \\(BE\\) is mapped onto the perpendicular bisector of \\(BF\\). The two bisectors are symmetric with respect to the axis of the homothety, i.e. the line \\(AP\\). Consequently the centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) lies on the line through the midpoint of \\(AB\\) that is **perpendicular** to \\(AP\\). In particular  \n\n\\[\nAO\\perp AP .\\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7 Tangency\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Because of (2.10), the radius \\(OT\\) of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) drawn to the point of intersection \\(T=\\ell\\cap\\omega_{BEF}\\) is perpendicular to \\(\\ell\\). Hence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at \\(T\\).\n\nEquivalently, using (2.9), the angle between \\(\\ell\\) (which has the same direction as \\(AP\\)) and the chord \\(BT\\) equals the angle subtended by \\(BT\\) at the centre \\(O\\); this is precisely the condition for tangency.\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe required tangency has been proved; therefore the statement of the problem holds. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
