+ \\frac{\\overrightarrow{C}+\\overrightarrow{D}}{2}\n= \\overrightarrow{A}+ \\overrightarrow{P},\n\\]\nbecause \\(\\overrightarrow{P}= \\frac{\\overrightarrow{C}+\\overrightarrow{D}}{2}\\) (the centre of the circle through \\(C,D,A\\)).  Hence\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{A}= \\overrightarrow{M}+\\overrightarrow{N}-\\overrightarrow{P}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nNow the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) satisfies the well\u2011known vector identity\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{H}= \\overrightarrow{P}+ \\overrightarrow{M}+ \\overrightarrow{N}-2\\overrightarrow{O},\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nwhere \\(O\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  In our configuration the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) coincides with the centre of \\(\\omega\\): indeed, \\(O\\) is the midpoint of \\(EF\\) and, by Lemma\u202f3, the quadrilateral \\(PMNO\\) is a rectangle (each of its sides is a radius of one of the two given circles).  Consequently (6) becomes\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{H}= \\overrightarrow{P}+ \\overrightarrow{M}+ \\overrightarrow{N}-2\\overrightarrow{O}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (5) into the right\u2011hand side yields\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{H}= \\overrightarrow{P}+ (\\overrightarrow{A}+ \\overrightarrow{P})-2\\overrightarrow{O}\n= \\overrightarrow{A}+2\\overrightarrow{P}-2\\overrightarrow{O}.\n\\]\nRearranging,\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{HO}= \\overrightarrow{A}-\\overrightarrow{P}= \\overrightarrow{AP}.\n\\]\nThus the vector from \\(H\\) to \\(O\\) equals the vector \\(AP\\), i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) passes through \\(O\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 5.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(A\\).\n\n*Proof.*  By Lemma\u202f4 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) contains the centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\omega\\).  Hence this line is the radius \\(OA\\) of \\(\\omega\\).  A radius is perpendicular to the tangent at its endpoint; therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to the tangent to \\(\