ows from the equalities \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and the midpoint properties of \\(M,N,O\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Preliminary notation  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) \u2013 circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);\n* \\(\\Gamma\\) \u2013 circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) \u2013 the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(C\\in\\Omega,\\;D\\in\\Gamma\\) \u2013 the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\);\n* \\(P\\) \u2013 the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) \u2013 the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) \u2013 the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) \u2013 the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);\n* \\(\\omega\\) \u2013 the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\); its centre is denoted by \\(O\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Basic equalities  \n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic  \n\nBoth \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\) and on \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\).  \nHence the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) lie on the unique circle passing through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) and intersecting \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).  \nWe denote this circle by \\(\\omega\\) and its centre by \\(O\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Mid\u2011point relations for \\(M,N,O\\)\n\n*Power of a point \\(M\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\).*  \nSince \\(M\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\),\n\n\\[\nMA\\cdot ME = MB^{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(MA=ME=r\\) (both are radii of \\(\\Omega\\)), (2) gives \\(MB=r\\).  Thus \\(M\\) is the midpoint of the chord \\(AE\\) of \\(\\omega\\).\n\nAnalogously, using the power of \\(N\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\) and the fact that \\(NA=NF=R\\),\n\n\\[\nNA\\cdot NF = NB^{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad NB=R,\n\\]\n\nso \\(N\\) is the midpoint of the chord \\(AF\\) of \\(\\omega\\).\n\nConsequently the centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\omega\\) is the midpoint of the segment joining the mid\u2011points of two chords, i.e.\n\n\\[\nO\\ \\text{is the midpoint of}\\ EF . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Vector relation \\(\\displaystyle \\vec{HO}= \\vec{AP}\\)\n\nPlace the origin at the point \\(P\\).  Write vectors of the relevant points with respect to this origin.\n\nFrom (1) we have \\( \\| \\vec{C}\\| = \\| \\vec{D}\\| = \\| \\vec{A}\\| = \\rho\\) (some common length).  \nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) and \\(M,N\\) are the mid\u2011points of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) respectively, we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\vec{M}= \\frac{\\vec{A}+\\vec{C}}{2}, \\qquad\n\\vec{N}= \\frac{\\vec{A}+\\vec{D}}{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAdding the two equalities in (4) and using \\(\\vec{C}+\\vec{D}=2\\vec{P}= \\mathbf{0}\\) (because the origin is \\(P\\)) yields\n\n\\[\n\\vec{M}+\\vec{N}= \\vec{A}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) satisfies the well\u2011known vector formula\n\n\\[\n\\vec{H}= \\vec{P}+ \\vec{M}+ \\vec{N}-2\\vec{O}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n(Indeed, in any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with circumcentre \\(O\\) and orthocentre \\(H\\) one has \\(\\vec{H}= \\vec{X}+ \\vec{Y}+ \\vec{Z}-2\\vec{O}\\).)\n\nSubstituting (5) into (6) and recalling that \\(\\vec{P}= \\mathbf{0}\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\vec{H}= \\vec{A}-2\\vec{O}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nRearranging (7) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\vec{HO}= \\vec{A}= \\vec{AP}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus the vector from \\(H\\) to the centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\omega\\) is exactly the vector \\(AP\\); equivalently, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) passes through \\(O\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Tangency\n\nFrom (8) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line \\(HO\\).  \nSince \\(O\\) is the centre of \\(\\omega\\), the segment \\(OA\\) is a radius of \\(\\omega\\).  \nA radius is perpendicular to the tangent at its endpoint; therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (i.e. the line \\(HO\\)) is perpendicular to the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(A\\).\n\nConsequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is precisely the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at the point \\(A\\).  Because \\(\\omega\\) is the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as required. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**"
