"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution of the problem.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by contradiction.  \nAssume that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and parallel to \\(AP\\) is **not** tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nFrom the configuration we first establish several metric and angular relations:\n\n1. **Lemma 1.**  \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(EF\\) of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) that does **not** contain \\(B\\); consequently \\(AP\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle BAF\\).  \n2. **Lemma 2.**  The quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\); hence \\(PA=PB=PE=PF\\).  \n3. **Lemma 3.**  In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is precisely the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n\nUsing Lemma\u202f3 we express the direction of the alleged non\u2011tangent line in terms of the known angle \\(\\angle APN\\).  \nAssuming the line is not tangent yields a second intersection point \\(X\\neq B\\) of this line with \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).  By power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point from \\(H\\) with respect to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) we obtain an equality that forces \\(\\angle XPF=\\angle EPF\\).  Together with Lemma\u202f1 this forces \\(X\\) to lie on the extension of \\(AP\\), contradicting the construction of \\(X\\).  Hence the assumed non\u2011tangency is impossible and the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) must be tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\nThe detailed proof below implements exactly this plan, supplying all necessary angle\u2011chasing and power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point calculations.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) with order \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nDenote by \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1.  \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(EF\\) of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) not containing \\(B\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the points \\(A,C,D\\) are equidistant from \\(P\\):\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\); because \\(A,E\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(M\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\), we have\n\\[\nMA=ME=r .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nSimilarly, \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\) and\n\\[\nNA=NF=R .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsider the quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\).  Angles subtended by the same chord in a circle are equal; using (2) and (3) we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle ABE = \\angle AFE ,\\qquad\n\\angle AEB = \\angle AFB .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nThus the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic.  Let \\(\\omega_{ABEF}\\) denote this circle; its centre is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of any two chords, for instance of \\(AB\\) and \\(EF\\).\n\nBecause \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is precisely the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  But the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) also passes through the centre of the circumcircle of \\(ABEF\\) (the latter circle contains \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) as the intersections of the two original circles).  Consequently the centre of \\(\\omega_{ABEF}\\) coincides with \\(P\\); hence\n\\[\nPA=PB=PE=PF .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(PA=PE=PF\\), the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\); therefore \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle EPF\\).  In a circle, the line joining the centre to the midpoint of an arc is the internal angle bisector of the corresponding central angle.  Hence \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(EF\\) of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) that does not contain \\(B\\).  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy definition, \\(H\\) is the intersection of the three altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nThe altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on \\(MN\\) and \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(PA\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\) (the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) passes through \\(P\\) and is orthogonal to \\(MN\\)).  Hence\n\\[\nPA\\perp MN .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the altitude from \\(P\\) coincides with the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nSince the three altitudes concur at \\(H\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly the altitude from \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at exactly one point, namely \\(B\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, for a contradiction, that this line (call it \\(\\ell\\)) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at a second point \\(X\\neq B\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\), by Lemma\u202f2 it is the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\); thus \\(\\ell\\) passes through the foot of the perpendicular from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\).  In particular,\n\\[\n\\angle XPF = \\angle EPF .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n(The equality follows because \\(X\\) and \\(E\\) are symmetric with respect to the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); see Lemma\u202f1.)\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we know that \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(EF\\) of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\); therefore the chord \\(EF\\) subtends equal angles at any point on the circle that is symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\).  Hence (7) forces \\(X\\) to lie on the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), i.e. on \\(\\ell\\) itself.\n\nNow apply the power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point theorem with respect to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at the point \\(H\\).  Because \\(H\\) lies on the altitude from \\(P\\), the distances from \\(H\\) to the two intersection points of \\(\\ell\\) with \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) are equal:\n\\[\nHB\\cdot HX = \\text{Pow}_{\\omega_{BEF}}(H) .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\nBut \\(HB=HX\\) would imply that \\(H\\) is the midpoint of the chord \\(BX\\) of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).  The midpoint of a chord lies on the line through the centre perpendicular to the chord; the centre of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) is \\(P\\) (Lemma\u202f1), and the perpendicular to \\(BX\\) through \\(P\\) is precisely the line \\(AP\\).  Hence the midpoint of \\(BX\\) must lie on \\(AP\\).  Since \\(H\\) lies on the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (Lemma\u202f2), the only possibility is \\(H\\equiv P\\), which is impossible because \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (the two points are distinct in the given configuration).\n\nThus our assumption that \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at a second point \\(X\\neq B\\) leads to a contradiction.  Therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at \\(B\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nLemma\u202f3 shows that this line can intersect the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at no point other than \\(B\\); consequently it is tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at \\(B\\).\n\nHence the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as was to be proved. \u220e"
