e.  By (2) the line \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\); consequently the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  Since the three altitudes of a triangle concur at the orthocentre, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) must be exactly this altitude. \u220e\n\n4. **Contradiction hypothesis.**  \n   Assume that the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is **not** tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).  Then \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at a second point \\(X\\neq B\\).\n\n5. **Consequences of the hypothesis.**  \n\n   * Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle EPF\\) (Lemma\u202f1), the chord \\(EF\\) is seen under equal angles from any point of \\(\\ell\\); in particular  \n     \\[\n     \\angle EPX=\\angle XPF .\\tag{5}\n     \\]\n\n   * By Lemma\u202f2 the line \\(\\ell\\) is the altitude from \\(P\\); hence it is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  Since \\(MN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(AB\\), the chord \\(AB\\) is parallel to \\(\\ell\\) (again by (3)).  Therefore  \n     \\[\n     \\angle HBE=\\angle ABE .\\tag{6}\n     \\]\n\n   * From Lemma\u202f1 the quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\); consequently the equal\u2011arc property gives  \n     \\[\n     \\angle ABE=\\angle AFE .\\tag{7}\n     \\]\n\n   * Combining (6) and (7) we obtain  \n     \\[\n     \\angle HBE=\\angle AFE .\\tag{8}\n     \\]\n\n6. **Power of the point \\(H\\) with respect to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).**  \n   Because \\(H\\) lies on the altitude from \\(P\\), the distances from \\(H\\) to the two intersection points of \\(\\ell\\) with \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) are equal; i.e.  \n   \\[\n   HB = HX .\\tag{9}\n   \\]\n   Hence the power of \\(H\\) is  \n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega_{BEF}}(H)=HB\\cdot HX = HB^{2}. \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   On the other hand, using the intersecting\u2011chords theorem for the chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) o