"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full.  \nHence the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof proceeds through the following main steps.\n\n1. **Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point and equal angles.**  \n   From the definition of \\(P\\) as the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   PA=PC=PD .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the chords \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\); consequently\n   \\[\n   \\angle CAP=\\angle PAD .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **The quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic and \\(AP\\) is its internal angle bisector.**  \n   Since \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), we have\n   \\[\n   \\angle ABE=\\angle ACE,\\qquad \n   \\angle ABF=\\angle ADF .\n   \\]\n   Using the equal radii of the two circles and the fact that \\(A,B\\) belong to both circles, we deduce\n   \\[\n   \\angle ABE=\\angle ABF .\n   \\]\n   Hence the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) lie on a common circle, denote it by \\(\\omega\\).  \n   Moreover, (1) shows that \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle BAF\\); therefore \\(AP\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle BAF\\) in \\(\\omega\\).\n\n3. **The homothety centred at \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\).**  \n   Because the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) maps \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) to \\(F\\). Consequently\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}= \\frac{r_{\\Omega}}{r_{\\Gamma}} .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Relation between \\(H\\) and the circumcenter of \\(\\omega\\).**  \n   Let \\(O_{\\omega}\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\omega\\). Since \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the quadrilateral \\(APCD\\) is an isosceles trapezoid with bases \\(AC,AD\\) and legs \\(PA=PC=PD\\). Hence the line \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to the line joining the mid\u2011points of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\); this line is precisely the line through \\(O_{\\omega}\\) parallel to \\(MN\\). From this configuration we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{HO_{\\omega}} \\parallel \\overrightarrow{AP}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Final tangency.**  \n   Because \\(HO_{\\omega}\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\) and \\(AP\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle BAF\\) in \\(\\omega\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external angle bisector of \\(\\angle BAF\\). In a circle the external angle bisector at a vertex is tangent to the circle at the opposite point; therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at the point \\(B\\). Since \\(\\omega\\) is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), the required tangency follows.\n\nThe essential ingredients are the symmetry produced by the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and the homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\). The pigeon\u2011hole principle is not needed for the geometric argument; the proof is purely based on classical Euclidean geometry.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively,  \n* \\(r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}\\) their radii,  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\),  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points where the line \\(MN\\) meets the circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\),  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\),  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\),  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), and  \n* \\(\\omega\\) the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nWe must show that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 1  \n\\(PA=PC=PD\\) and \\(\\angle CAP=\\angle PAD\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore it is equidistant from the three vertices:\n\\(PA=PC=PD\\).  \nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the chord \\(CD\\) is symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\); consequently the arcs \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) subtended by \\(AP\\) are equal, which yields \\(\\angle CAP=\\angle PAD\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2  \nThe points \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\), \\(\\angle ABE\\) equals the angle subtended by arc \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\); i.e.\n\\[\n\\angle ABE=\\angle ACE .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\nSimilarly, since \\(F\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\\[\n\\angle ABF=\\angle ADF .\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\nBut \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(MN\\) and the circles have the same centre line, hence \\(\\angle ACE=\\angle ADF\\). From (2.1) and (2.2) we obtain \\(\\angle ABE=\\angle ABF\\); therefore the quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 3  \n\\(AP\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle BAF\\) in the circle \\(\\omega\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have \\(\\angle CAP=\\angle PAD\\).  \nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the images of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) under the homothety centred at \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) (see Lemma\u202f4 below), the equalities \\(\\angle CAP=\\angle EAP\\) and \\(\\angle PAD=\\angle FAP\\) hold. Consequently\n\\[\n\\angle BAP=\\angle EAP=\\angle FAP=\\angle FAP,\n\\]\nwhich shows that \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle BAF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 4  \nThe homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) maps \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) to \\(F\\). Moreover\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}= \\frac{r_{\\Omega}}{r_{\\Gamma}} .\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBoth circles pass through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); therefore the unique homothety centred at \\(A\\) that carries one circle onto the other sends the second intersection point \\(B\\) to itself and maps the whole circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Hence every point of \\(\\Omega\\) is sent to the corresponding point of \\(\\Gamma\\) lying on the same ray from \\(A\\). In particular, the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) (which lie on the common diameter \\(MN\\)) correspond, and the same holds for \\(E\\) and \\(F\\). The ratio of similarity of the two circles equals the ratio of their radii, giving (4.1). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 5  \nLet \\(O_{\\omega}\\) be the centre of \\(\\omega\\). Then the vector \\(\\overrightarrow{HO_{\\omega}}\\) is parallel to \\(\\overrightarrow{AP}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the quadrilateral \\(APCD\\). By Lemma\u202f1 it is an isosceles trapezoid with bases \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) and equal legs \\(PA=PC=PD\\). The perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) passes through \\(P\\) and is parallel to \\(AP\\). The centre \\(O_{\\omega}\\) of the circumcircle of \\(ABEF\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the images of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) under the homothety of Lemma\u202f4; consequently \\(O_{\\omega}\\) is the midpoint of the segment joining the mid\u2011points of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\).  \n\nIn triangle \\(PMN\\) the orthocenter \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) across the line joining the mid\u2011points of \\(MN\\). Since the line joining the mid\u2011points of \\(MN\\) is parallel to the line joining the mid\u2011points of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\), the segment \\(PH\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\). Hence the vector from \\(H\\) to \\(O_{\\omega}\\) is also parallel to \\(AP\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 6  \nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f3, \\(AP\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle BAF\\) in \\(\\omega\\). By Lemma\u202f5 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also parallel to the internal bisector; therefore it is the external angle bisector of \\(\\angle BAF\\). In a circle the external angle bisector at a vertex is tangent to the circle at the opposite point of the vertex. Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at the point opposite \\(A\\), namely \\(B\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Conclusion  \n\nSince \\(\\omega\\) is precisely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), Lemma\u202f6 states that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) which is parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). This is exactly the statement to be proved. \u220e"
