) are the images of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) under the homothety of step\u202f3, the segment \\(CD\\) is the image of \\(EF\\); therefore the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) coincides with the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\), i.e. passes through \\(O_{\\omega}\\).  \n\n   In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the orthocenter \\(H\\) satisfies\n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{MN}^{\\perp},\n   \\]\n   i.e. \\(PH\\) is the reflection of \\(PM\\) across the line through the midpoint of \\(MN\\). Since \\(PA\\) is perpendicular to the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) (by the isosceles\u2011trapezoid property of \\(APCD\\)), we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{HO_{\\omega}}\\parallel\\overrightarrow{AP}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Tangency.**  \n   From step\u202f4, \\(AP\\) is the internal bisector of \\(\\angle BAF\\) in \\(\\omega\\). By (3) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also parallel to this internal bisector; consequently it is the **external** angle bisector of \\(\\angle BAF\\). In a circle the external angle bisector at a vertex is tangent to the circle at the point opposite that vertex. Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(B\\). Because \\(\\omega\\) is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), the desired tangency follows.\n\nThe argument uses only elementary Euclidean geometry (properties of circumcenters, homothety, cyclic quadrilaterals and orthocenters) and is fully rigorous.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, radii \\(r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points where the line \\(MN\\) meets the circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n* \\(\\omega\\) the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Properties of \\(P\\))\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\angle CAP=\\angle PAD .\\tag{L1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  By definition of a circumcenter. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2 (Cyclicity of \\(A,B,E,F\\))\n\nThe points \\(A,B,E,F\\) lie on a common circle \\(\\omega\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\), the inscribed angle theorem gives\n\\[\n\\angle ABE=\\angle ACE .\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\nSimilarly, \\(F\\in\\Gamma\\) yields\n\\[\n\\angle ABF=\\angle ADF .\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the common radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore the chords \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) subtend equal angles at the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) that lie on the same line, i.e.\n\\[\n\\angle ACE=\\angle ADF .\n\\]\nCombining with (2.1) and (2.2) we obtain \\(\\angle ABE=\\angle ABF\\); hence the quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 3 (Homothety centred at \\(A\\))\n\nThere exists a homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). It satisfies\n\\[\n\\mathcal H(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H(E)=F,\\qquad \n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}= \\frac{r_{\\Omega}}{r_{\\Gamma}} .\\tag{L3}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBoth circles pass through the two distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); the unique similarity with centre \\(A\\) that maps one circle onto the other must send the whole of \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Consequently every point of \\(\\Omega\\) is mapped to the point of \\(\\Gamma\\) lying on the same ray from \\(A\\); in particular \\(C\\mapsto D\\) and \\(E\\mapsto F\\). The ratio of similarity equals the ratio of the radii, giving the stated proportion. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 4 (\\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle BAF\\) in \\(\\omega\\))\n\nIn the cyclic quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) the line \\(AP\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle BAF\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have \\(\\angle CAP=\\angle PAD\\).  \nApplying the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) of Lemma\u202f3, the direction of the ray \\(AC\\) coincides with that of \\(AE\\), and the direction of \\(AD\\) coincides with that of \\(AF\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\angle EAP=\\angle FAP .\n\\]\nSince \\(B\\) lies on the same circle as \\(E\\) and \\(F\\), the angles \\(\\angle BAP\\) and \\(\\angle EAP\\) are equal, and likewise \\(\\angle FAP=\\angle BAP\\). Therefore \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle BAF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 5 (Direction of \\(HO_{\\omega}\\))\n\nLet \\(O_{\\omega}\\) be the centre of \\(\\omega\\). Then\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{HO_{\\omega}}\\parallel\\overrightarrow{AP}. \\tag{L5}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the images of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) under the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\), the segment \\(CD\\) is parallel to \\(EF\\) and its midpoint \\(K\\) is the image of the midpoint \\(L\\) of \\(EF\\). The perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\) passes through \\(O_{\\omega}\\); after the homothety it becomes the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), which also passes through \\(O_{\\omega}\\). Hence \\(O_{\\omega}\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).\n\nIn the isosceles trapezoid \\(APCD\\) (Lemma\u202f1) the line \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\). Consequently \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to the line \\(O_{\\omega}K\\).  \n\nNow consider \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Since \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(PN\\) is symmetric to \\(PM\\) with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); therefore the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) is parallel to that perpendicular bisector. The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is obtained by intersecting the three altitudes, so the line \\(PH\\) is parallel to the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\). Hence \\(PH\\) is perpendicular to \\(AP\\).\n\nThus both vectors \\(PH\\) and \\(O_{\\omega}K\\) are parallel to the same line (the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\)), and \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to that line. Consequently the vector from \\(H\\) to \\(O_{\\omega}\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\):\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{HO_{\\omega}}=\\overrightarrow{HP}+\\overrightarrow{PO_{\\omega}}\\parallel\\overrightarrow{AP}.\n\\]\n\u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 6 (Tangency)\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f4, \\(AP\\) is the internal bisector of \\(\\angle BAF\\) in \\(\\omega\\). Lemma\u202f5 tells us that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also parallel to this internal bisector; therefore it is the **external** angle bisector of \\(\\angle BAF\\). In a circle the external angle bisector at a vertex is tangent to the circle at the point opposite that vertex. Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) touches \\(\\omega\\) at \\(B\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Final Conclusion\n\nSince \\(\\omega\\) is precisely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), Lemma\u202f6 establishes that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) which is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). This completes the proof. \u220e"
