t point requires a precise proof, which is omitted.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Verification Log\n\n---\n\n#### Preliminary Setup\nThe solution correctly restates the given configuration and introduces notation. No issues here.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f1 (Properties of \\(P\\))\n\n> \u201cSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),  \n> \\[\n> PA=PC=PD\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\angle CAP=\\angle PAD .\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Verification:* By definition of a circumcenter, \\(P\\) is equidistant from the three vertices \\(A,C,D\\); thus the equalities of distances are correct. Moreover, the radii \\(PA,PC,PD\\) are equal, so \\(\\triangle APC\\) and \\(\\triangle APD\\) are isosceles, giving \\(\\angle CAP=\\angle PAD\\). This step is valid.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f2 (Cyclicity of \\(A,B,E,F\\))\n\n> \u201cBecause \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\), the inscribed angle theorem gives  \n> \\[\n> \\angle ABE=\\angle ACE .\\tag{2.1}\n> \\]  \n> Similarly, \\(F\\in\\Gamma\\) yields  \n> \\[\n> \\angle ABF=\\angle ADF .\\tag{2.2}\n> \\]  \n> The line \\(MN\\) is the common radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore the chords \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) subtend equal angles at the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) that lie on the same line, i.e.  \n> \\[\n> \\angle ACE=\\angle ADF .\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Issue \u2013 Critical Error:*  \nFor two intersecting circles the **radical axis** is the line through their intersection points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), not the line joining the centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\). Consequently the claim that \u201cthe line \\(MN\\) is the common radical axis\u201d is false. The equality \\(\\angle ACE=\\angle ADF\\) does **not** follow from any valid geometric property; it is a direct consequence of the erroneous radical\u2011axis statement. Because this equality is essential for deducing \\(\\angle ABE=\\angle ABF\\) and hence the cyclicity of \\(A,B,E,F\\), the entire Lemma\u202f2 collapses.\n\n*Result:* The conclusion that \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic is unsupported; all subsequent lemmas that rely on this fact are built on a false premise.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f3 (Homothety centred at \\(A\\))\n\n> \u201cBoth circles pass through the two distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); the unique similarity with centre \\(A\\) that maps one circle onto the other must send the whole of \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Consequently every point of \\(\\Omega\\) is mapped to the point of \\(\\Gamma\\) lying on the same ray from \\(A\\); in particular \\(C\\mapsto D\\) and \\(E\\mapsto F\\). The ratio of similarity equals the ratio of the radii \u2026\u201d\n\n*Verification:* Two circles intersecting at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) indeed admit a homothety (a similarity) with centre \\(A\\) that sends one circle to the other; the centre is \\(A\\) because both circles share that point. The homothety maps each point of the first circle to the point of the second circle lying on the same ray from \\(A\\). Hence the statements about images of \\(C\\) and \\(E\\) are correct, and the ratio of similarity equals the ratio of radii. This lemma is valid **provided** the circles are distinct and non\u2011concentric, which holds in the problem.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f4 (AP bisects \\(\\angle BAF\\) in \\(\\omega\\))\n\n> \u201cFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have \\(\\angle CAP=\\angle PAD\\).  \n> Applying the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) \u2026 the direction of the ray \\(AC\\) coincides with that of \\(AE\\), and the direction of \\(AD\\) coincides with that of \\(AF\\). Hence  \n> \\[\n> \\angle EAP=\\angle FAP .\n> \\]  \n> Since \\(B\\) lies on the same circle as \\(E\\) and \\(F\\), the angles \\(\\angle BAP\\) and \\(\\angle EAP\\) are equal, and likewise \\(\\angle FAP=\\angle BAP\\). Therefore \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle BAF\\).\u201d\n\n*Issue \u2013 Critical Error:*  \nThe step \u201cSince \\(B\\) lies on the same circle as \\(E\\) and \\(F\\), the angles \\(\\angle BAP\\) and \\(\\angle EAP\\) are equal\u201d is invalid. Equality of