"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full.  \nHence the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by a sequence of elementary geometric lemmas that together force the desired tangency.\n\n1. **Lemma\u202f1 (Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point for the line \\(MN\\)).**  \n   The points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the internal intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, therefore  \n   \\[\n   MC\\cdot MN = MA^{2}=MB^{2}= ND\\cdot MN .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lemma\u202f2 (The point \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(AD\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)).**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the radii \\(PA,PC,PD\\) are equal; consequently \\(PA\\) is the perpendicular bisector of chord \\(CD\\). Hence \\(PA\\perp CD\\) and \\(PA\\) bisects the central angle \\(\\angle CPD\\).  \n\n3. **Lemma\u202f3 (The quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic and \\(AP\\) is its internal angle bisector).**  \n   By construction \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with the two given circles, so \\(A,E\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(A,F\\in\\Gamma\\). Since \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) lie on the same circle, namely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Moreover, because \\(PA\\) is the common internal bisector of \\(\\angle BAE\\) (in \\(\\Omega\\)) and \\(\\angle BAF\\) (in \\(\\Gamma\\)), it is also the internal bisector of \\(\\angle BAF\\) of the cyclic quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\).\n\n4. **Lemma\u202f4 (The reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(AP\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(ABEF\\)).**  \n   In any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across any side lies on the circumcircle. Apply this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) with side \\(AP\\) (which is not a