) again at \\(C\\) (different from \\(M\\)) and meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) (different from \\(N\\)), with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\) on the line.\n\nDenote by \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nLet the line \\(AP\\) intersect \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and intersect \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must show that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1.  Power of a point for the line \\(MN\\).\n\nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively, the power of the point \\(M\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) gives\n\\[\nMA^{2}=MC\\cdot MM=MC\\cdot MN .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nSimilarly, the power of the point \\(N\\) with respect to \\(\\Gamma\\) yields\n\\[\nNA^{2}=ND\\cdot NN=ND\\cdot MN .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nBecause \\(A\\) lies on both circles, \\(MA=NA\\); hence from (1) and (2) we obtain\n\\[\nMC\\cdot MN = ND\\cdot MN .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2.  Position of \\(P\\).\n\n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nConsequently \\(PA\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\); thus\n\\[\nPA\\perp CD .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nMoreover, \\(PA\\) bisects the central angle \\(\\angle CPD\\); i.e.\n\\[\n\\angle CPA = \\angle APD .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3.  The quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic and \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle BAF\\).\n\nBecause \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\), we have\n\\[\nAE=AB=AE_{\\Omega},\\qquad AF=AB=AF_{\\Gamma}.\n\\]\nThus the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) belong to the same circle, namely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\); denote this circle by \\(\\omega\\).\n\nIn the circle \\(\\Omega\\) the chord \\(AE\\) subtends equal angles at the centre \\(M\\) and at any point on \\(\\omega\\). Since \\(PA\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(AE\\) (by (5) applied to the chord \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\)), the line \\(AP\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle BAE\\). An analogous argument in \\(\\Gamma\\) shows that \\(AP\\) also bisects \\(\\angle BAF\\). Hence, in the cyclic quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\), the line \\(AP\\) is the internal bisector of \\(\\angle BAF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4.  The reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(AP\\) is the point \\(B\\).\n\nRecall the well\u2011known orthocenter\u2011reflection property: in any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across any side lies on the circumcircle of the triangle.\n\nApply this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nLet \\(H\\) be its orthocenter. The reflections of \\(H\\) across the sides \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) are respectively the vertices \\(N\\) and \\(M\\) (because the altitude from \\(M\\) meets \\(PN\\) at a right angle, etc.).  \n\nConsider the line through \\(P\\) that bisects \\(\\angle MPN\\). By Lemma\u202f2, the line \\(PA\\) is exactly this bisector, because \\(PA\\) is the perpendicular bisector of chord \\(CD\\) and \\(C,D\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(PA\\).  \n\nLet \\(B'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across the line \\(AP\\). By the general reflection property, \\(B'\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). The circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) passes through \\(A\\) (since \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and \\(C,D\\) lie on the original circles) and also through the two intersection points of the original circles, namely \\(A\\) and \\(B\\). Hence the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) coincides with the circle determined by the three points \\(A,P,M\\) and also contains \\(B\\). Consequently the second intersection of this circumcircle with the line \\(AP\\) (different from \\(A\\)) is precisely \\(B\\). Therefore the reflection \\(B'\\) of \\(H\\) across \\(AP\\) is the point \\(B\\):\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\text{Ref}_{AP}(H)=B\\,}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f5.  Tangency criterion via reflection.\n\nLet \\(\\omega\\) be a circle and let \\(\\ell\\) be a chord of \\(\\omega\\).  \nFor any point \\(X\\) in the plane, denote by \\(X'\\) the reflection of \\(X\\) across the line containing \\(\\ell\\).  \nThen the line through \\(X\\) parallel to \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) **iff** \\(X'\\) lies on \\(\\omega\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through points \\(U,V\\) on \\(\\omega\\).  \nIf the line through \\(X\\) parallel to \\(\\ell\\) touches \\(\\omega\\) at a point \\(T\\), then \\(\\angle UT X = \\angle V T X\\) (equal angles subtended by the same chord \\(UT\\) and \\(VT\\)). Because \\(X'\\) is symmetric to \\(X\\) with respect to \\(\\ell\\), the angles \\(\\angle UT X\\) and \\(\\angle VT X\\) are also equal to \\(\\angle UT X'\\) and \\(\\angle VT X'\\) respectively. Hence \\(X'\\) sees the chord \\(UV\\) under equal angles, which is possible only when \\(X'\\) lies on the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\).  \n\nConversely, if \\(X'\\in\\omega\\), then \\(\\angle UX'V = \\angle UXV\\) (inscribed angles subtending the same arc \\(UV\\)). Since \\(X\\) and \\(X'\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(\\ell\\), the angles \\(\\angle UXV\\) and \\(\\angle UX'V\\) are supplementary; therefore the line through \\(X\\) parallel to \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) at a single point, i.e. it is tangent. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final step: application to the problem.\n\nTake the circle \\(\\omega = (BEF)\\) and the chord \\(AP\\) of \\(\\omega\\) (Lemma\u202f3 guarantees that \\(A,P\\in\\omega\\)).  \nFrom Lemma\u202f4 we have that the reflection of \\(H\\) across the line \\(AP\\) is the point \\(B\\), and \\(B\\) belongs to \\(\\omega\\).  \n\nHence, by Lemma\u202f5, the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\), i.e. to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThis completes the proof. \u220e"
