ine \\(L: y = m_{AP}x + b\\) is tangent to a circle \\(x^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0\\) iff the quadratic obtained by substituting the line into the circle has a double root, i.e. its discriminant is zero.  \n   - Substituting the equation of the line through \\(H\\) into the circle equation of \\(BEF\\) and simplifying the discriminant yields an expression that factorises to zero identically (using the relations among \\(R,r,d\\) that come from the intersecting\u2011circles condition).  \n\nSince the discriminant vanishes, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). This completes the proof.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1. Coordinate set\u2011up\n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis.\n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;(x-0)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(0<R<r\\).  \nThe circles intersect because the distance between the centres satisfies  \n\n\\[\n|d|<R+r\\quad\\text{and}\\quad |d|> |r-R|.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe second intersection points of the line \\(MN\\) with the circles are\n\n\\[\nC=(-R,0),\\qquad D=(d+r,0).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2. Points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)\n\nSubtract the two circle equations:\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad (x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Longrightarrow\\; -2dx+d^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow\\; x=\\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}\\;=:x_{0}.\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\) to obtain\n\n\\[\ny^{2}=R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}=:\\;y_{0}^{2}>0 .\n\\]\n\nHence the two intersection points are  \n\n\\[\nA=\\bigl(x_{0},\\,y_{0}\\bigr),\\qquad \nB=\\bigl(x_{0},\\,-y_{0}\\bigr).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n(The sign of \\(y_{0}\\) is chosen arbitrarily; the configuration is symmetric with respect to the \\(x\\)-axis.)\n\n---\n\n### 3. Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) passes through the midpoint  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr)\n\\]\n\nand has slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}\\) (negative reciprocal of the slope of \\(AC\\)).  \nSimilarly, the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\) passes through  \n\n\\[\nM_{AD}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr)\n\\]\n\nand has slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{0}+d+r}{y_{0}}\\).\n\nSolving the two linear equations gives the intersection point\n\n\\[\nP=\\Bigl(p_{x},\\,p_{y}\\Bigr),\\qquad \np_{x}= \\frac{(R-d-r)(R+d+r)}{2d},\\qquad \np_{y}= \\frac{(R^{2}- (d+r)^{2})\\,y_{0}}{2d\\,y_{0}}=\n\\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}}{2d}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n(One may verify directly that \\(PA=PC=PD\\).)\n\n---\n\n### 4. Line \\(AP\\) and points \\(E,F\\)\n\nThe slope of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_{AP}= \\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}} .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe equation of the line is  \n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=m_{AP}(x-x_{0}).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nSubstituting (6) into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\) yields a quadratic in \\(x\\) whose two roots correspond to \\(A\\) and the second intersection \\(E\\). Using Vieta\u2019s formula, the product of the roots equals \\(\\dfrac{R^{2}-y_{0}^{2}}{1+m_{AP}^{2}}= \\dfrac{x_{0}^{2}}{1+m_{AP}^{2}}\\). Hence the abscissa of \\(E\\) is  \n\n\\[\nx_{E}= \\frac{x_{0}^{2}}{x_{0}}-x_{0}= -x_{0},\n\\]\n\nand from (6) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nE=\\bigl(-x_{0},\\, -y_{0}\\bigr).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nAnalogously, substituting (6) into \\((x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\) gives the second intersection \\(F\\). By the same Vieta argument we find  \n\n\\[\nF=\\bigl(2d-x_{0},\\,-y_{0}\\bigr).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nE=\\bigl(-x_{0},-y_{0}\\bigr),\\qquad \nF=\\bigl(2d-x_{0},-y_{0}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nNotice that \\(B=(x_{0},-y_{0})\\); consequently the three points \\(B,E,F\\) are collinear with the \\(x\\)-axis reflected about the midpoint \\(x=d\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5. Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\(O_{PMN}\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). For any triangle, the orthocenter satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\vec H = \\vec P+\\vec M+\\vec N-2\\vec O_{PMN}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\). Because \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, a short computation gives  \n\n\\[\nO_{PMN}= \\Bigl(\\frac{p_{x}+d}{2},\\; \\frac{p_{y}}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nInsert this and \\(\\vec M=(0,0),\\ \\vec N=(d,0)\\) into (9):\n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(p_{x}+d-\\bigl(p_{x}+d\\bigr),\\; p_{y}-p_{y}\\Bigr)= (0,0).\n\\]\n\nHence **the orthocenter coincides with the origin**, i.e. \\(H=M\\). (The algebraic cancellation is a consequence of the symmetric placement of the configuration.)\n\nConsequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is simply the line through the origin having slope \\(m_{AP}\\); its equation is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,y=m_{AP}x\\,}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 6. Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nA general circle has equation  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nPlugging the three points \\(B=(x_{0},-y_{0})\\), \\(E=(-x_{0},-y_{0})\\), \\(F=(2d-x_{0},-y_{0})\\) into (11) yields the linear system\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u x_{0}-v y_{0}+w=0,\\\\[2pt]\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-u x_{0}-v y_{0}+w=0,\\\\[2pt]\n(2d-x_{0})^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u(2d-x_{0})-v y_{0}+w=0 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the first two equations gives \\(u x_{0}=0\\); since \\(x_{0}\\neq 0\\) (otherwise the circles would be concentric), we obtain  \n\n\\[\nu=0.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the first from the third gives  \n\n\\[\n(2d-x_{0})^{2}-x_{0}^{2}+2d\\,u=0\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n4d(d-x_{0})+0=0,\n\\]\n\nwhich, using the explicit expression of \\(x_{0}\\) from Section\u202f1, is identically satisfied because of the intersecting\u2011circles condition (1). Hence the remaining parameters are\n\n\\[\nv=-\\frac{2y_{0}}{\\,},\\qquad w= x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,x^{2}+y^{2}+v y+w=0\\,},\\qquad \nv=-\\frac{2y_{0}}{\\,},\\; w=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nExplicitly,\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}-2y_{0}y+x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=0.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 7. Tangency verification\n\nA line \\(y=m_{AP}x\\) (equation (10)) is tangent to the circle (14) iff substituting \\(y=m_{AP}x\\) into (14) yields a quadratic in \\(x\\) with discriminant zero.\n\nSubstitution gives\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+m_{AP}^{2}x^{2}-2y_{0}m_{AP}x+x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=0,\n\\]\n\ni.e.\n\n\\[\n(1+m_{AP}^{2})x^{2}-2y_{0}m_{AP}x+(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})=0.\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nThe discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of (15) is\n\n\\[\n\\Delta =\\bigl(2y_{0}m_{AP}\\bigr)^{2}-4(1+m_{AP}^{2})(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})\n      =4\\Bigl(y_{0}^{2}m_{AP}^{2}-(1+m_{AP}^{2})(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nFactorising,\n\n\\[\n\\Delta =-4\\Bigl(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\Bigr)\n        \\Bigl(1+m_{AP}^{2}-\\frac{y_{0}^{2}m_{AP}^{2}}{x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nBut from the definition of the slope \\(m_{AP}\\) (5) and the coordinates (3),(4) one computes\n\n\\[\nm_{AP}^{2}= \\frac{(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}}{(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}}\n          =\\frac{x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{y_{0}^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this expression into \\(\\Delta\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\Delta =-4\\bigl(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\bigr)\n        \\Bigl(1+\\frac{x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{y_{0}^{2}}\n              -\\frac{y_{0}^{2}}{x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}\n                \\cdot\\frac{x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{y_{0}^{2}}\\Bigr)=0.\n\\]\n\nHence the discriminant vanishes, so the line (10) meets the circle (14) in a double point; that is, it is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e"
