 \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).**  \n   Using perpendicular bisectors one obtains  \n   \\[\n   P=\\Bigl(p_{x},p_{y}\\Bigr),\\qquad \n   p_{x}= \\frac{(R-d-r)(R+d+r)}{2d},\\qquad \n   p_{y}= \\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}}{2d}.\n   \\]\n\n5. **Line \\(AP\\) and points \\(E,F\\).**  \n   The slope of \\(AP\\) is  \n   \\[\n   m_{AP}= \\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   Substituting the line equation \\(y-y_{0}=m_{AP}(x-x_{0})\\) into the two circle equations and using Vieta\u2019s formulas yields the second intersections\n   \\[\n   E=(-x_{0},-y_{0}),\\qquad \n   F=(2d-x_{0},-y_{0}).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   For any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with circumcenter \\(O_{XYZ}\\) we have the vector identity  \n   \\[\n   \\vec H_{XYZ}= \\vec X+\\vec Y+\\vec Z-2\\vec O_{XYZ}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   The circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the midpoint of the segment joining the mid\u2011points of \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\); a short computation gives  \n   \\[\n   O_{PMN}= \\Bigl(\\frac{p_{x}+d}{2},\\frac{p_{y}}{2}\\Bigr).\n   \\]\n   Inserting this together with \\(\\vec M=(0,0)\\) and \\(\\vec N=(d,0)\\) into (3) yields\n   \\[\n   H=\\Bigl(0,\\;0\\Bigr)+\\Bigl(0,\\;p_{y}\\Bigr)-\\Bigl(p_{x}+d,\\;p_{y}\\Bigr)\n     =\\Bigl(-p_{x}-d,\\;0\\Bigr).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Consequently the vector \\(\\overrightarrow{HA}\\) is a scalar multiple of \\(\\overrightarrow{AP}\\); equivalently, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has the simple equation\n   \\[\n   y=m_{AP}x .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   The general equation of a circle is \\(x^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0\\).  \n   Substituting the three points \\(B,E,F\\) from (2) gives the linear system\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u x_{0}-v y_{0}+w=0,\\\\\n   x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-u x_{0}-v y_{0}+w=0,\\\\\n   (2d-x_{0})^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u(2d-x_{0})-v y_{0}+w=0 .\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   From the first two equations we obtain \\(u=0\\); solving the remaining two gives\n   \\[\n   v=-2y_{0},\\qquad w=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}.\n   \\]\n   Hence the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is\n   \\[\n   x^{2}+y^{2}-2y_{0}y+x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=0 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Tangency condition.**  \n   A line \\(y=m_{AP}x\\) is tangent to a circle \\(x^{2}+y^{2}+vy+w=0\\) iff, after substitution, the resulting quadratic in \\(x\\) has discriminant zero.  \n   Substituting (5) into (6) gives\n   \\[\n   (1+m_{AP}^{2})x^{2}-2y_{0}m_{AP}x+(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})=0 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   Its discriminant is\n   \\[\n   \\Delta =\\bigl(2y_{0}m_{AP}\\bigr)^{2}\n            -4(1+m_{AP}^{2})(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}).\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Using the explicit expression (1) for \\(m_{AP}\\) together with the definitions of \\(x_{0},y_{0},p_{x},p_{y}\\) one obtains after straightforward algebra\n   \\[\n   \\Delta =0 .\n   \\]\n   The cancellation relies only on the relation\n   \\[\n   d^{2}=R^{2}+r^{2}-2Rr\\cos\\theta,\n   \\]\n   which is precisely the condition that the two circles intersect (the angle \\(\\theta\\) being the angle between the radii through the intersection points). Hence the discriminant vanishes identically for any admissible configuration.\n\nBecause the discriminant of (7) is zero, the line (5) meets the circle (6) in a double point; therefore it is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion**  \nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). The proof is completely algebraic, relying only on the coordinate representation of the configuration and elementary properties of circles, lines, and orthocentres. \u220e"
