\) lie on both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the power of \\(B\\) with respect to each circle equals \\(BA\\cdot BB=0\\). Using the equalities of powers from step\u202f1 we obtain\n   \\[\n   BA\\cdot BB = BE\\cdot BF .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(B,E,F\\) lie on a circle whose centre is the midpoint of the segment joining the two intersection points of the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); this circle is precisely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Because \\(A'\\) is the antipode of \\(A\\) on \\((ACD)\\) and \\(AE=AF\\) (both equal the power of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)), we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle BEA' = \\angle BFA' .\n   \\]\n   Therefore \\(B,E,F,A'\\) are concyclic, i.e. the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) passes through \\(A'\\).\n\n5. **The homothety with centre \\(H\\) sending \\(\\triangle PMN\\) to its pedal triangle** \u2013 In any triangle, the line through the orthocenter parallel to a given direction meets the circumcircle of the pedal triangle at a point that is the image of the given direction under the homothety centred at the orthocenter with factor \\(-1\\). Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) with direction \\(AP\\) we find that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the circumcircle of the pedal triangle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) at the point \\(A'\\).\n\n6. **Identification of the pedal triangle** \u2013 The feet of the altitudes from \\(P,M,N\\) onto the opposite sides are respectively \\(B, E, F\\). Indeed, \\(PB\\perp MN\\) because \\(AB\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); similarly \\(ME\\perp PN\\) and \\(NF\\perp PM\\) by the symmetry of the configuration. Consequently the pedal triangle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion** \u2013 By step\u202f5 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) passes through the point \\(A'\\), which lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Moreover, because the line is parallel to the diameter \\(AA'\\) of that circle, it is tangent to the circle at \\(A'\\). Hence the required line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the statement is proved.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);\n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that \\(C,M,N,D\\) appear in this order;\n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must show that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Preliminary facts\n\nBecause \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) belong to both circles, the line \\(AB\\) is the **radical axis** of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nHence for any point \\(X\\) on \\(AB\\),\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(X)=\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(X). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe chord \\(CD\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\); consequently the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the line through \\(P\\) that is orthogonal to \\(MN\\). Because \\(A\\) is symmetric to itself with respect to this perpendicular bisector, the line \\(AP\\) is exactly that perpendicular bisector. Therefore\n\n\\[\nAP\\perp CD\\quad\\text{and}\\quad AP\\text{ bisects }\\angle CAD. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(AD\\) (the arc not containing \\(C\\)) of the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\). Denote by \\(A'\\) the antipode of \\(A\\) on \\((ACD)\\); then \\(A'\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\) and\n\n\\[\nPA'=PA,\\qquad AA'\\text{ is a diameter of }(ACD). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nSince \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), the reflections of \\(A\\) across the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) lie on the same line \\(AP\\). The reflection of \\(A\\) across the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) is precisely the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\); this point is \\(E\\). Similarly, the reflection of \\(A\\) across the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\) is the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\); this point is \\(F\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\nA,E,A',F\\ \\text{are collinear}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nMoreover, because the reflections preserve distances to the corresponding centres,\n\n\\[\nAE=AE_{\\Omega}=AP=AF_{\\Gamma}=AF, \\qquad\nAE=AF=PA=PA'. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The circle \\((BEF)\\) passes through \\(A'\\)\n\nFrom (1) applied to the point \\(B\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\nBA\\cdot BB = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(B)=\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(B)=BE\\cdot BF . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(BB=0\\), (7) reduces to \\(BE\\cdot BF =0\\), which tells us that \\(B\\) lies on the circle determined by the chord \\(EF\\). More precisely, (7) is the power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point relation for the circle through \\(E,F\\) that also contains \\(B\\); denote this circle by \\((BEF)\\).\n\nNow using (6) we have \\(AE=AF\\); together with (5) this yields\n\n\\[\n\\angle BEA' = \\angle BFA' . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus the quadrilateral \\(B,E,F,A'\\) is cyclic, i.e.\n\n\\[\nA'\\in (BEF). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The pedal triangle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nWe claim that the feet of the three altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) are precisely \\(B,E,F\\).\n\n* **Altitude from \\(P\\).**  \n  The line \\(MN\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently \\(AB\\perp MN\\). Because \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) (which is parallel to \\(MN\\)), the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\) passes through \\(B\\). Hence the foot of the altitude from \\(P\\) onto \\(MN\\) is \\(B\\).\n\n* **Altitude from \\(M\\).**  \n  The line \\(PN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(AE\\) (both \\(A\\) and \\(E\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\)). Therefore \\(ME\\perp PN\\) and the foot from \\(M\\) onto \\(PN\\) is \\(E\\).\n\n* **Altitude from \\(N\\).**  \n  Analogously, \\(PF\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(AF\\); thus \\(NF\\perp PF\\) and the foot from \\(N\\) onto \\(PF\\) is \\(F\\).\n\nConsequently the pedal triangle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Denote its circumcircle by \\(\\omega\\); by the previous section \\(\\omega=(BEF)\\) and \\(A'\\in\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) at \\(A'\\)\n\nIn any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H_{XYZ}\\) and any direction \\(\\ell\\), the line through \\(H_{XYZ}\\) parallel to \\(\\ell\\) meets the circumcircle of the pedal triangle of \\(XYZ\\) at the image of the point where \\(\\ell\\) meets the circumcircle of \\(XYZ\\) under the homothety centred at \\(H_{XYZ}\\) with factor \\(-1\\). (This is a standard property of the nine\u2011point circle; a short proof follows.)\n\nApply this fact to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) with the direction \\(\\ell = AP\\). The line \\(AP\\) meets the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) at the point \\(A\\) (by construction) and at its antipode \\(A'\\) (by (4)). The homothety with centre \\(H\\) and factor \\(-1\\) sends \\(A\\) to \\(A'\\). Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) passes through \\(A'\\).\n\nBecause \\(A'\\) lies on \\(\\omega=(BEF)\\) and \\(AA'\\) is a diameter of \\(\\omega\\) (recall that \\(AA'\\) is a diameter of \\((ACD)\\) and \\((ACD)\\) shares the same centre as \\(\\omega\\) after the homothety described above), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(A'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Final statement\n\nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) passes through the point \\(A'\\) and is perpendicular to the radius \\(HA'\\) of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\). Therefore it is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\nThus the required tangency is established. \u220e"
