el to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Radical axis\n\nSince \\(A,B\\) belong to both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the line \\(AB\\) is the **radical axis** of the two circles.  \nHence for any point \\(X\\) on \\(AB\\),\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(X)=\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(X). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The point \\(P\\) and the antipode \\(A'\\)\n\n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the chord \\(CD\\) is symmetric with respect to the line through \\(P\\) that is perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Consequently the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) passes through \\(P\\) and is exactly the line \\(AP\\). Thus\n\n\\[\nAP\\perp CD,\\qquad AP\\text{ bisects }\\angle CAD. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(A'\\) be the antipode of \\(A\\) on the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\). From (2) and (3) we have\n\n\\[\nA'\\in AP,\\qquad PA'=PA,\\qquad AA'\\text{ is a diameter of }(ACD). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(AC\\); therefore the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) is the reflection of \\(A\\) across that bisector, namely \\(E\\). Similarly, because \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(AD\\), the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) is the reflection of \\(A\\) across that bisector, namely \\(F\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nA,E,A',F\\ \\text{are collinear} \\quad\\text{and}\\quad AE=AF=PA=PA'. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The circle \\((BEF)\\) contains \\(A'\\)\n\nApply (1) to the point \\(B\\). Since \\(B\\) lies on both circles, its power with respect to each is zero; therefore\n\n\\[\nBA\\cdot BB = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(B)=\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(B)=BE\\cdot BF . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(BB=0\\), (6) reduces to \\(BE\\cdot BF=0\\), i.e. the points \\(B,E,F\\) are not collinear and determine a unique circle, which we denote by \\((BEF)\\).\n\nFrom (5) we have \\(AE=AF\\); together with the collinearity \\(A,E,A',F\\) this yields\n\n\\[\n\\angle BEA' = \\angle BFA'. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nEquality of the subtended angles shows that the quadrilateral \\(B,E,F,A'\\) is cyclic; consequently\n\n\\[\nA'\\in (BEF). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Pedal triangle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nWe claim that the feet of the three altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) are precisely \\(B,E,F\\).\n\n* **Altitude from \\(P\\).**  \n  The line \\(MN\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore \\(AB\\perp MN\\). Since \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) which is parallel to \\(MN\\), the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\) passes through \\(B\\). Hence the foot of the altitude from \\(P\\) onto \\(MN\\) is \\(B\\).\n\n* **Altitude from \\(M\\).**  \n  From (5) the segment \\(AE\\) is symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\); that bisector is exactly the line \\(PN\\) (because \\(PN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(AE\\) of the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\)). Consequently \\(ME\\perp PN\\) and the foot from \\(M\\) onto \\(PN\\) is \\(E\\).\n\n* **Altitude from \\(N\\).**  \n  Analogously, \\(PF\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(AF\\); thus \\(NF\\perp PF\\) and the foot from \\(N\\) onto \\(PF\\) is \\(F\\).\n\nTherefore the pedal triangle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Denote its circumcircle by \\(\\omega\\); by (8) we have \\(\\omega=(BEF)\\) and \\(A'\\in\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------