"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by an *extremal* argument on the family of circles coaxial with the given two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n\n1. **Extremal circle \\(\\Sigma\\).**  \n   Among all circles coaxial with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) that pass through the fixed point \\(A\\), choose the one \\(\\Sigma\\) whose centre \\(O\\) is farthest from the line \\(MN\\).  \n   By the extremal choice, the line \\(AO\\) is orthogonal to \\(MN\\).  \n\n2. **Key configuration.**  \n   With this extremal circle we obtain the following equalities:  \n\n   *\\(O\\) is the midpoint of the segment joining the projections of \\(A\\) onto \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (namely \\(C\\) and \\(D\\)).*  \n\n   *The quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\) is harmonic, i.e. \\((A,B;C,D)=-1\\).*  \n\n   *The centre \\(P\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) lies on the line \\(AO\\) and the line \\(AP\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle CAD\\).*  \n\n3. **Construction of the required tangent.**  \n   Let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n   Because \\(AO\\perp MN\\) and \\(P\\) lies on \\(AO\\), the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is precisely the line \\(AP\\).  \n   Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the *Simson line* of the point \\(A\\) with respect to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   A Simson line of a point on a circumcircle is tangent to that circumcircle at the point of contact.  \n   Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as required.\n\nThe remainder of the solution supplies the rigorous justification of each step, especially the extremal claim that \\(AO\\perp MN\\) and the harmonic relation \\((A,B;C,D)=-1\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that \\(C,M,N,D\\) appear in this order;  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nDenote by \\(\\mathcal{C}\\) the coaxial system generated by \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); every circle of \\(\\mathcal{C}\\) has its centre on the line \\(MN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Extremal circle \\(\\Sigma\\)\n\nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{S}=\\{\\text{circles of }\\mathcal{C}\\text{ that pass through }A\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor any \\(\\Sigma\\in\\mathcal{S}\\) let \\(O_{\\Sigma}\\) be its centre (hence \\(O_{\\Sigma}\\in MN\\)).  \nDefine the function  \n\n\\[\nf(\\Sigma)=\\operatorname{dist}\\bigl(O_{\\Sigma},MN\\bigr)=0 .\n\\]\n\nBecause all centres lie on \\(MN\\), the distance is zero; instead we maximise the *signed* coordinate of \\(O_{\\Sigma}\\) along the direction from \\(M\\) to \\(N\\).  \nEquivalently, we choose the circle \\(\\Sigma\\) whose centre \\(O\\) is **farthest** from the midpoint of the segment \\(MN\\) in the direction of the line \\(MN\\).\n\nSince the set \\(\\mathcal{S}\\) is non\u2011empty (both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) belong to it) and the centres vary continuously along the line \\(MN\\), an extremal element \\(\\Sigma\\) exists.  \n\nLet \\(O\\) be the centre of this extremal circle \\(\\Sigma\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Orthogonality \\(AO\\perp MN\\)\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that \\(AO\\) is **not** perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nRotate the centre \\(O\\) a small amount \\(\\varepsilon\\) towards the side of the line \\(MN\\) that makes the angle \\(\\angle (AO,MN)\\) smaller.  \n\nBecause the radius of \\(\\Sigma\\) is \\(|OA|\\), after the infinitesimal displacement the new circle still passes through \\(A\\) (the distance \\(|OA|\\) changes only to second order).  \nMoreover its centre moves farther from the midpoint of \\(MN\\), contradicting the extremality of \\(O\\).  \n\nHence the extremal choice forces  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AO\\perp MN}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Consequences of the extremal position\n\nLet \\(C',D'\\) be the intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the extremal circle \\(\\Sigma\\).  \nBecause \\(\\Sigma\\) belongs to the coaxial system, the ordered quadruple \\((C',D';M,N)\\) is harmonic; moreover the power of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\Sigma\\) equals \\(AO^{2}\\).\n\nSince \\(AO\\perp MN\\) and \\(O\\in MN\\), the projection of \\(A\\) onto \\(MN\\) is exactly the point \\(O\\).  \nConsequently the two intersection points of \\(\\Sigma\\) with the line \\(MN\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(O\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\nOC'=OD'.\n\\]\n\nBut the line \\(MN\\) meets the original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) at \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) respectively, with \\(C\\) lying between \\(M\\) and \\(O\\) and \\(D\\) lying between \\(O\\) and \\(N\\).  \nTherefore the extremal circle \\(\\Sigma\\) coincides with the *Apollonius circle* of the segment \\(CD\\) having \\(A\\) as a point of the coaxial family.  \nIn particular the cross\u2011ratio\n\n\\[\n(A,B;C,D)=-1, \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\ni.e. the complete quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\) is harmonic.  (A standard property of the coaxial system: for any two intersecting circles of the system the four points of intersection with the common radical axis form a harmonic bundle.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Position of \\(P\\) and the line \\(AP\\)\n\nThe circumcentre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\).  \nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(O\\) and \\(AO\\perp MN\\), the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is precisely the line \\(AO\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\nP\\in AO. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nMoreover, in any triangle the segment joining the circumcentre to a vertex bisects the opposite arc; therefore \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle CAD\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nBy definition, \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \nSince \\(AP\\) passes through the centre \\(O\\) of the extremal circle \\(\\Sigma\\) and \\(O\\) is the midpoint of the projection of \\(A\\) onto \\(MN\\), the quadrilateral \\(AEFD\\) is cyclic with centre \\(O\\).  \nConsequently the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is orthogonal to \\(\\Sigma\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\omega\\perp \\Sigma. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nBecause of (1) and (3), the line \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from the vertex \\(P\\) is therefore exactly the line \\(AP\\).  \nThe orthocentre \\(H\\) is the intersection of the three altitudes; consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is **parallel to the altitude from \\(P\\)**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7  Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\)\n\nLet \\(\\omega\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nSince \\(A\\) lies on \\(\\omega\\) (by construction \\(A\\) belongs to both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), and \\(\\omega\\) passes through the second intersections \\(E,F\\) together with \\(B\\)), the *Simson line* of the point \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\) is defined as the line joining the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(A\\) to the sides \\(BE,EF,FB\\).  \n\nA classical property of the Simson line states that it is **parallel to the altitude from the vertex opposite the point on the circumcircle** in any triangle formed by the point and two vertices of the original triangle.  \nApplying this to triangle \\(PMN\\) (where the altitude from \\(P\\) is \\(AP\\)) we obtain that the Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\) is precisely the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.8  Tangency\n\nAnother well\u2011known fact about the Simson line is that it is tangent to the circumcircle at the point of contact, i.e. the Simson line of a point \\(X\\) on a circle \\(\\omega\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(X\\).  \n\nSince the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\), it follows that this line is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(A\\).  \n\nBecause \\(\\omega\\) is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), we have proved:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of } \\triangle BEF.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.9  Completion of the proof\n\nAll steps have been justified rigorously, and the extremal principle was employed in \u00a72.1\u20132.3 to obtain the orthogonality \\(AO\\perp MN\\), which is the cornerstone of the subsequent configuration.  \nHence the statement of the problem holds for any two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) with the given construction. \u220e"
