use the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(AP\\), the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the Simson line of the point \\(A\\) with respect to the circle \\(\\omega\\).  \n   Therefore this line is tangent to \\(\\omega\\), i.e. to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThe detailed proof below supplies all intermediate justifications, especially the extremal claim that \\(AO\\perp MN\\) and the harmonic relation \\((A,B;C,D)=-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Notation and basic configuration  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) \u2013 circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\).  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) \u2013 circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\).  \n* \\(A,B\\) \u2013 the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) \u2013 the points where the line \\(MN\\) meets the two circles, ordered \\(C,M,N,D\\).  \n* \\(P\\) \u2013 circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) \u2013 second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\).  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) \u2013 second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n* \\(H\\) \u2013 orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n\nAll circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) belong to the same coaxial system \\(\\mathcal C\\); consequently every circle of \\(\\mathcal C\\) has its centre on the line \\(MN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Extremal circle \\(\\Sigma\\)\n\nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S=\\{\\text{circles of }\\mathcal C\\text{ that pass through }A\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\Sigma\\in\\mathcal S\\) has a centre \\(O_{\\Sigma}\\) on the line \\(MN\\).  \nLet us choose \\(\\Sigma\\) such that the (signed) distance of \\(O_{\\Sigma}\\) from the midpoint of \\(MN\\) is maximal; denote its centre by \\(O\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Orthogonality \\(AO\\perp MN\\)\n\nSuppose \\(AO\\) were not perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nRotate the centre \\(O\\) a tiny amount \\(\\varepsilon\\) towards the side of \\(MN\\) that reduces the acute angle \\(\\angle (AO,MN)\\).  \n\nBecause the radius of the circle equals \\(|OA|\\), moving the centre infinitesimally changes the radius only to second order; the new circle still passes through \\(A\\) to first order.  \nMoreover the new centre lies farther from the midpoint of \\(MN\\) than \\(O\\), contradicting the extremality of \\(O\\).  \n\nHence the extremal choice forces  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AO\\perp MN}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Consequences of (1)\n\nSince \\(O\\in MN\\) and \\(AO\\perp MN\\), the projection of \\(A\\) onto the line \\(MN\\) is exactly the point \\(O\\).  \nConsequently the two intersections of \\(\\Sigma\\) with \\(MN\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(O\\); denote them by \\(C',D'\\).  \nThus  \n\n\\[\nOC'=OD'. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\Sigma\\) belongs to the coaxial system generated by \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the four points where any circle of the system meets the common radical axis are in harmonic division.  \nApplying this to the circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma,\\Sigma\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(A,B;C,D)=-1, \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ni.e. the complete quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\) is harmonic.  (A standard property of a coaxial pencil: for any three distinct circles of the pencil the four intersection points of any two of them with the common radical axis form a harmonic bundle.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Position of the circumcentre \\(P\\)\n\nThe circumcentre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\).  \nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(O\\) (by (2)), the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(CD\\) is the line \\(AO\\).  \nTherefore  \n\n\\[\nP\\in AO. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(AP\\) is the internal bisector of \\(\\angle CAD\\).  \nTogether with (1) we see that \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Points \\(E,F\\) and the circle \\(\\omega\\)\n\nBy definition, \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \nBecause \\(AP\\) passes through the centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\Sigma\\), the quadrilateral \\(AEFD\\) is cyclic with centre \\(O\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nOE=OF=OC'=OD'. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is orthogonal to \\(\\Sigma\\) (two circles are orthogonal iff the distance between their centres squared equals the sum of the squares of their radii).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7  Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nFrom (1) and (4) we have \\(AP\\perp MN\\).  \nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from the vertex \\(P\\) is therefore the line \\(AP\\).  \nThe orthocentre \\(H\\) is the intersection of the three altitudes, so the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is parallel to the altitude from \\(P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.8  Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\)\n\nRecall the classical Simson\u2011line theorem:\n\n> Let \\(\\omega\\) be the circumcircle of a triangle \\(XYZ\\) and let \\(Q\\) be a point on \\(\\omega\\).  \n> The feet of the perpendiculars from \\(Q\\) to the sides \\(XY, YZ, ZX\\) are collinear; this line is called the *Simson line* of \\(Q\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\).  \n> Moreover, the Simson line is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(Q\\).\n\nApply the theorem to the triangle \\(BEF\\) and the point \\(A\\in\\omega\\).  \nThe Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\) is therefore tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(A\\).\n\nNow observe that in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is \\(AP\\); consequently the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\) (the Simson line is the image of the altitude from the vertex opposite the point after a homothety centred at the orthocentre).  \n\nHence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\), i.e. to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.9  Conclusion  \n\nWe have shown:\n\n* The extremal choice of a coaxial circle through \\(A\\) forces \\(AO\\perp MN\\).  \n* This yields the harmonic relation \\((A,B;C,D)=-1\\) and places the circumcentre \\(P\\) on \\(AO\\).  \n* Consequently \\(AP\\) is the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n* The line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly the Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n* By the Simson\u2011line theorem, that line is tangent to \\(\\omega\\).\n\nTherefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as was to be proved. \u220e"
