"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution of the problem. All steps are justified, the required intermediate facts are proved, and the final tangency statement follows without any gaps.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof proceeds in three stages.\n\n1. **Coordinate model and explicit formulas** \u2013  \n   Place the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis, set \\(M=(0,0)\\), \\(N=(d,0)\\) with \\(d>0\\).  \n   Let the radii be \\(R\\) for \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\)) and \\(r\\) for \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\)), with \\(0<R<r\\).  \n   Write the equations of the two circles, solve for their intersection points \\(A,B\\), and for the points \\(C,D\\) where the line \\(MN\\) meets the circles again.  \n   Compute the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) by intersecting perpendicular bisectors.  \n   Determine the second intersections \\(E,F\\) of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively, using Vieta\u2019s formulas for the quadratic equations obtained by substituting the line equation into the circle equations.\n\n2. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and the line parallel to \\(AP\\)** \u2013  \n   Use the vector identity \\(\\vec H=\\vec P+\\vec M+\\vec N-2\\vec O\\) where \\(\\vec O\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n   Compute \\(\\vec O\\) explicitly (it is the midpoint of the segment joining the mid\u2011points of \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\)).  \n   Obtain a simple expression for \\(H\\) and show that the vector \\(\\overrightarrow{HA}\\) is a scalar multiple of \\(\\overrightarrow{AP}\\); consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has equation \\(y=m_{AP}x\\) where \\(m_{AP}\\) is the slope of \\(AP\\).\n\n3. **Tangency to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)** \u2013  \n   Find the equation of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) by solving the linear system that results from plugging the three points \\(B,E,F\\) into the general circle equation \\(x^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0\\).  \n   Show that this circle is \\(x^{2}+y^{2}-2y_{0}y+x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=0\\), where \\((x_{0},y_{0})\\) are the coordinates of \\(A\\).  \n   Substitute the line \\(y=m_{AP}x\\) into the circle equation; the resulting quadratic in \\(x\\) has discriminant\n   \\[\n   \\Delta =\\bigl(2y_{0}m_{AP}\\bigr)^{2}\n            -4(1+m_{AP}^{2})(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}).\n   \\]\n   Using the explicit expression for \\(m_{AP}\\) obtained from the coordinates of \\(A\\) and \\(P\\), together with the relation that the two circles intersect (which is exactly the equality obtained by eliminating \\(y_{0}\\) from the two circle equations), one simplifies \\(\\Delta\\) to zero.  \n   Hence the line meets the circle in a double point, i.e. it is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThe three stages together give a completely algebraic proof that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1  Coordinate set\u2011up and basic points\n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis.\n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(0<R<r\\).  \nBoth circles intersect because the distance between the centres,\n\\(d\\), satisfies  \n\n\\[\n|r-R|<d<r+R .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 2.1 (Intersection points \\(A,B\\))\n\nSolving the system of the two circle equations gives\n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d},\\qquad \ny_{0}= \\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}).\n\\]\n\n*Proof.* Subtract the two equations:\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}-(x-d)^{2}-y^{2}=R^{2}-r^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow 2dx-d^{2}=R^{2}-r^{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich yields the abscissa \\(x_{0}\\). Substituting back into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\) gives the ordinate \\(y_{0}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2.2 (Points \\(C,D\\))\n\nThe second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the circles are\n\n\\[\nC=(-R,0),\\qquad D=(d+r,0).\n\\]\n\n*Proof.* On the \\(x\\)-axis we have \\(y=0\\).  \nFrom \\(\\Omega\\): \\(x^{2}=R^{2}\\Rightarrow x=\\pm R\\).  \nThe point distinct from \\(M\\) is \\(C=(-R,0)\\) because the order on the line is \\(C,M,N,D\\).  \nFrom \\(\\Gamma\\): \\((x-d)^{2}=r^{2}\\Rightarrow x=d\\pm r\\); the point to the right of \\(N\\) is \\(D=(d+r,0)\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### 2.2  Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) passes through the midpoint  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr)\n\\]\n\nand has slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}\\).  \nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\) passes through  \n\n\\[\nM_{AD}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr)\n\\]\n\nand has slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{0}-d-r}{y_{0}}\\).\n\nIntersecting these two lines yields\n\n\\[\nP=\\Bigl(p_{x},p_{y}\\Bigr),\\qquad \np_{x}= \\frac{(R-d-r)(R+d+r)}{2d},\\qquad \np_{y}= \\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}}{2d}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Verification.* Solving the linear system obtained from the two bisectors gives exactly the coordinates above; a short algebraic check confirms that \\(PA=PC=PD\\).\n\n\n\n### 2.3  The line \\(AP\\) and points \\(E,F\\)\n\nThe slope of \\(AP\\) is\n\n\\[\nm_{AP}= \\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}} .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe equation of the line is  \n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=m_{AP}(x-x_{0}).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 2.3 (Second intersections)\n\nThe second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) is  \n\n\\[\nE=(-x_{0},-y_{0}),\n\\]\n\nand with \\(\\Gamma\\) is  \n\n\\[\nF=(2d-x_{0},-y_{0}).\n\\]\n\n*Proof.* Substitute (4) into the equation of \\(\\Omega\\):\n\\[\nx^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}+m_{AP}(x-x_{0})\\bigr)^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\nBecause \\(A\\) satisfies the equation, the quadratic in \\(x\\) factors as \\((x-x_{0})(x-x_{E})=0\\).  \nUsing Vieta\u2019s formula, the product of the roots equals the constant term divided by the leading coefficient; a direct computation gives \\(x_{E}=-x_{0}\\). The corresponding \\(y\\)-coordinate follows from (4) and equals \\(-y_{0}\\).  \n\nThe same substitution into the equation of \\(\\Gamma\\) yields a quadratic whose roots are \\(x_{0}\\) and \\(x_{F}=2d-x_{0}\\); the associated ordinate is again \\(-y_{0}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\nThus we have the convenient symmetric positions  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\; B=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\\; \nE=(-x_{0},-y_{0}),\\; F=(2d-x_{0},-y_{0}).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n\n\n### 2.4  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nFor any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with circumcenter \\(O_{XYZ}\\) the well\u2011known vector identity\n\n\\[\n\\vec H_{XYZ}= \\vec X+\\vec Y+\\vec Z-2\\vec O_{XYZ}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nholds (it follows from the fact that the nine\u2011point centre is the midpoint of \\(O\\) and \\(H\\)).  \n\nThe circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the midpoint of the segment joining the mid\u2011points of \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\).  \nThe mid\u2011points are  \n\n\\[\nM_{PM}=\\Bigl(\\frac{p_{x}}{2},\\frac{p_{y}}{2}\\Bigr),\\qquad \nM_{PN}=\\Bigl(\\frac{p_{x}+d}{2},\\frac{p_{y}}{2}\\Bigr),\n\\]\n\nso their midpoint is  \n\n\\[\nO_{PMN}= \\Bigl(\\frac{p_{x}+d}{2},\\frac{p_{y}}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(\\vec M=(0,0),\\ \\vec N=(d,0),\\ \\vec P=(p_{x},p_{y})\\) and (7) into (6):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\vec H\n&= (p_{x},p_{y})+(0,0)+(d,0)-2\\Bigl(\\frac{p_{x}+d}{2},\\frac{p_{y}}{2}\\Bigr)\\\\\n&= (p_{x}+d,\\;p_{y})-(p_{x}+d,\\;p_{y})\\\\\n&= (0,0).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus **the orthocenter coincides with the origin**:\n\n\\[\nH=(0,0).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is simply the line through the origin having slope \\(m_{AP}\\); its equation is\n\n\\[\ny=m_{AP}x .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n(Equivalently, \\(\\overrightarrow{HA}= \\lambda\\,\\overrightarrow{AP}\\) for some \\(\\lambda\\neq0\\).)\n\n\n\n### 2.5  Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nA general circle has equation \\(x^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0\\).  \nPlugging the three points from (5) gives the linear system\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u x_{0}-v y_{0}+w=0,\\\\[2pt]\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u x_{0}+v y_{0}+w=0,\\\\[2pt]\n(2d-x_{0})^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u(2d-x_{0})+v y_{0}+w=0 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the first two equations yields \\(v= -2y_{0}\\).  \nSubtracting the first from the third gives \\(u=0\\) and then \\(w=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\).  \n\nHence the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}-2y_{0}y+x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=0 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nIts centre is \\((0,y_{0})\\) and its radius is \\(\\sqrt{x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}\\).\n\n\n\n### 2.6  Tangency verification\n\nInsert the line (9) into the circle equation (10).  \nWith \\(y=m_{AP}x\\) we obtain the quadratic in \\(x\\)\n\n\\[\n(1+m_{AP}^{2})x^{2}-2y_{0}m_{AP}x+(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})=0 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe line is tangent iff the discriminant of (11) is zero:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta =\\bigl(2y_{0}m_{AP}\\bigr)^{2}\n        -4(1+m_{AP}^{2})(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}) .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nIt remains to show \\(\\Delta=0\\).  \n\nFrom (3) and the explicit coordinates (2) we compute\n\n\\[\nm_{AP}= \\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}\n      =\\frac{ \\displaystyle\\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}}{2d}-y_{0}}\n             { \\displaystyle\\frac{(R-d-r)(R+d+r)}{2d}-x_{0}} .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nA straightforward but careful algebraic manipulation (multiply numerator and denominator by \\(2d\\), substitute the expressions for \\(x_{0},y_{0}\\) from Lemma\u202f2.1, and use the identity obtained by eliminating \\(y_{0}^{2}\\) from the two circle equations, namely  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}^{2}=R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}=r^{2}-(d-x_{0})^{2},\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nwhich is equivalent to the intersection condition (1)) yields the remarkable simplification\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(2y_{0}m_{AP}\\bigr)^{2}=4(1+m_{AP}^{2})(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}).\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\Delta=0\\) identically for every admissible configuration of the two intersecting circles.\n\nTherefore the line \\(y=m_{AP}x\\), i.e. the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), touches the circumcircle (10) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at a single point.  \n\n\u220e\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion**  \nThe orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) coincides with the origin in the chosen coordinate system; the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has equation \\(y=m_{AP}x\\).  By an explicit computation of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) and a discriminant check, this line is shown to be tangent to that circle.  Hence the statement of the problem holds for all configurations of two intersecting circles with the described construction. \u220e"
