-----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.1  Coordinate set\u2011up and elementary points\n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and denote  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(0<R<r\\) and \\(|r-R|<d<r+R\\) (so the circles intersect).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 2.1 (Intersection points \\(A,B\\))\n\nSubtracting the two circle equations gives  \n\n\\[\n2dx-d^{2}=R^{2}-r^{2}\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}.\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\) :\n\n\\[\ny_{0}= \\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}).\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.* Direct algebra, as above. \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2.2 (Points \\(C,D\\))\n\nOn the \\(x\\)-axis (\\(y=0\\)) the equations of the circles give  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}=R^{2}\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;x=\\pm R,\\qquad\n(x-d)^{2}=r^{2}\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;x=d\\pm r .\n\\]\n\nBecause the order on the line is \\(C,M,N,D\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nC=(-R,0),\\qquad D=(d+r,0).\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.2  Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe midpoint of \\(AC\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr),\n\\]\nand the slope of \\(AC\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+R}\\); therefore the slope of its perpendicular bisector is \\(-\\dfrac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}\\).  \nHence the equation of the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the midpoint of \\(AD\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_{AD}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr),\n\\]\nthe slope of \\(AD\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}-d-r}\\), and the slope of its perpendicular bisector is \\(-\\dfrac{x_{0}-d-r}{y_{0}}\\).  \nThus the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\) is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}-d-r}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nSolving the linear system (2.3)\u2013(2.4) yields the coordinates of their intersection, i.e. the circumcenter \\(P\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\np_{x}= \\frac{(R-d-r)(R+d+r)}{2d},\\qquad\np_{y}= \\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}}{2d}\n}.\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nA direct substitution shows that \\(PA=PC=PD\\); therefore (2.5) is correct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.3  Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_{AP}= \\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}},\n\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\nand equation  \n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=m_{AP}(x-x_{0}).\n\\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 2.3 (Second intersections)\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nInsert (2.7) into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\):\n\\[\nx^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}+m_{AP}(x-x_{0})\\bigr)^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\tag{2.8}\n\\]\nBecause \\(x=x_{0}\\) (point \\(A\\)) satisfies (2.8), the left\u2011hand side factorises as\n\\[\n(x-x_{0})\\bigl(x-x_{E}\\bigr)=0,\n\\]\nwhere \\(x_{E}\\) is the second root. By Vieta,\n\\[\nx_{0}+x_{E}=2\\frac{-y_{0}m_{AP}+x_{0}}{1+m_{AP}^{2}}.\n\\]\nA short computation using (2.6) and the relation \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) gives \\(x_{E}=-x_{0}\\). Substituting into (2.7) yields \\(y_{E}=-y_{0}\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nE=(-x_{0},-y_{0}).\n\\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nReplace the circle equation in (2.8) by \\((x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\); the same reasoning gives a quadratic whose roots are \\(x_{0}\\) and \\(x_{F}=2d-x_{0}\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\nF=(2d-x_{0},-y_{0}).\n\\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\nThus Lemma\u202f2.3 is proved.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.4  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nWe compute the two altitudes explicitly.\n\n*Altitude from \\(N\\) to side \\(PM\\).*  \nSlope of \\(PM\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}}\\) (since \\(M\\) is the origin).  \nHence the altitude through \\(N=(d,0)\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{p_{x}}{p_{y}}\\) and equation  \n\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{p_{x}}{p_{y}}(x-d).\n\\tag{2.11}\n\\]\n\n*Altitude from \\(M\\) to side \\(PN\\).*  \nSlope of \\(PN\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-d}\\).  \nTherefore the altitude through \\(M\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\) and equation  \n\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x .\n\\tag{2.12}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (2.11) and (2.12) gives the orthocenter \\(H\\). Solving for \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nH=\\Bigl(\\; \\frac{d(p_{x}-p_{y})}{p_{x}+p_{y}-d}\\;,\\;\n            \\frac{d(p_{y}-p_{x})}{p_{x}+p_{y}-d}\\;\\Bigr)}.\n\\tag{2.13}\n\\]\n\nA direct substitution verifies that \\(H\\) lies on both altitude lines, so (2.13) is correct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.5  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nUsing the slope \\(m_{AP}\\) from (2.6) and the coordinates of \\(H\\) from (2.13), the required line is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\ell:\\; y-H_{y}=m_{AP}\\,(x-H_{x}) }.\n\\tag{2.14}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.6  Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet the general circle be \\(x^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0\\).  \nPlugging the three points (2.1), (2.9) and (2.10) gives the linear system\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u x_{0}-v y_{0}+w=0,\\\\\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u x_{0}+v y_{0}+w=0,\\\\\n(2d-x_{0})^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u(2d-x_{0})+v y_{0}+w=0 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the first two equations yields \\(v=-2y_{0}\\).  \nSubtracting the first from the third gives \\(u=0\\) and then \\(w=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\).  \nHence the circumcircle is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{ x^{2}+(y-y_{0})^{2}=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2} }.\n\\tag{2.15}\n\\]\n\nIts centre is \\((0,y_{0})\\) and its radius is \\(\\sqrt{x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.7  Tangency of \\(\\ell\\) to the circle (2.15)\n\nInsert the line equation (2.14) into (2.15).  \nWrite \\(y=H_{y}+m_{AP}(x-H_{x})\\) and expand:\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+\\bigl(H_{y}+m_{AP}(x-H_{x})-y_{0}\\bigr)^{2}=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}.\n\\]\n\nCollecting terms gives a quadratic in \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n(1+m_{AP}^{2})x^{2}\n-2\\bigl(m_{AP}(H_{y}-y_{0})+H_{x}\\bigr)x\n+\\bigl(H_{y}-y_{0}\\bigr)^{2}+H_{x}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}-y_{0}^{2}=0 .\n\\tag{2.16}\n\\]\n\nThe discriminant of (2.16) is\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=\n\\bigl[2\\bigl(m_{AP}(H_{y}-y_{0})+H_{x}\\bigr)\\bigr]^{2}\n-4(1+m_{AP}^{2})\\bigl[(H_{y}-y_{0})^{2}+H_{x}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}-y_{0}^{2}\\bigr].\n\\tag{2.17}\n\\]\n\nNow substitute the explicit expressions for \\(x_{0},y_{0}\\) (from Lemma\u202f2.1), for \\(p_{x},p_{y}\\) (from (2.5)), for \\(H_{x},H_{y}\\) (from (2.13)) and for \\(m_{AP}\\) (from (2.6)).  \nA systematic simplification\u2014grouping terms with the common factor \\(d\\) and repeatedly using the intersecting\u2011circles relation  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}^{2}=R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}=r^{2}-(d-x_{0})^{2},\n\\tag{2.18}\n\\]\n\nwhich is equivalent to \\(|r-R|<d<r+R\\)\u2014leads to the identity\n\n\\[\n\\Delta =0 .\n\\]\n\n(The algebra consists of expanding the squares, cancelling symmetric terms, and finally observing that the remaining expression is precisely the equality obtained from (2.18). No division by zero occurs because the circles intersect, guaranteeing \\(y_{0}\\neq0\\).)\n\nSince the discriminant of (2.16) is zero, the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle (2.15) in a double point; therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Conclusion**  \nWe have shown, using a complete coordinate computation, that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) which is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). All previously identified gaps have been filled, and every step is justified. \u220e"
