 Because the discriminant of (L9) is zero, the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle (L8) in a double point; consequently \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.1  Geometry on the \\(x\\)-axis\n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and denote  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(0<R<r\\) and \\(|r-R|<d<r+R\\) (the circles intersect in two distinct points).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.2  Points \\(A,B,C,D\\)\n\nSubtracting the two circle equations gives  \n\n\\[\n2dx-d^{2}=R^{2}-r^{2}\\Longrightarrow \nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}.\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\) :\n\n\\[\ny_{0}= \\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nBecause the line \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis, the intersections of the circles with this line are obtained by setting \\(y=0\\):\n\n\\[\n\\Omega\\cap\\{y=0\\}:\\;x=\\pm R,\\qquad \n\\Gamma\\cap\\{y=0\\}:\\;x=d\\pm r .\n\\]\n\nThe order required in the statement is \\(C,M,N,D\\).  \nSince \\(R>0\\) and \\(r>0\\) we have \\(-R<C<M<N<d+r\\); the other intersection of \\(\\Gamma\\) (\\(d-r\\)) lies between \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) only when \\(r>d\\), which contradicts the hypothesis \\(r>R\\) together with \\(|r-R|<d\\). Consequently the correct points are  \n\n\\[\nC=(-R,0),\\qquad D=(d+r,0). \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.3  Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe midpoint of \\(AC\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr),\n\\]\nand the slope of \\(AC\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+R}\\); therefore the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the midpoint of \\(AD\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_{AD}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr),\n\\]\nthe slope of \\(AD\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}-d-r}\\), and its perpendicular bisector is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}-d-r}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nSolving the linear system (2.3)\u2013(2.4) gives\n\n\\[\np_{x}= \\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}}{2(d+r-R)},\\qquad \np_{y}= \\frac{R^{2}+(d+r)^{2}-2(d+r)R}{2(d+r-R)}. \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nA direct substitution of \\((p_{x},p_{y})\\) into the three distances\n\\[\nPA^{2}= (p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2},\\;\nPC^{2}= (p_{x}+R)^{2}+p_{y}^{2},\\;\nPD^{2}= (p_{x}-d-r)^{2}+p_{y}^{2}\n\\]\nshows that all three are equal; hence \\(P\\) is indeed the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.4  Second intersections \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_{AP}= \\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}} \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\nand equation  \n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=m_{AP}(x-x_{0}). \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\n**Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).**  \nInsert (2.7) into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\):\n\\[\nx^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}+m_{AP}(x-x_{0})\\bigr)^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\nExpanding yields a quadratic\n\\[\n(1+m_{AP}^{2})x^{2}-2\\bigl(m_{AP}(y_{0}-m_{AP}x_{0})+x_{0}\\bigr)x\n+\\bigl(y_{0}^{2}+m_{AP}^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2m_{AP}x_{0}y_{0}-R^{2}\\bigr)=0.\n\\]\nBecause \\(x=x_{0}\\) is a root (it corresponds to point \\(A\\)), Vieta\u2019s formula gives the second root\n\\[\nx_{E}= \\frac{R^{2}-y_{0}^{2}-m_{AP}^{2}x_{0}+2m_{AP}y_{0}}{1+m_{AP}^{2}} .\n\\]\nUsing the identity \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) (from (2.1)) the numerator simplifies to \\(-x_{0}(1+m_{AP}^{2})\\); consequently\n\\[\nx_{E}=-x_{0},\\qquad y_{E}=y_{0}+m_{AP}(x_{E}-x_{0})=-y_{0}.\n\\]\nThus  \n\n\\[\nE=(-x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\n**Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).**  \nReplace the circle equation by \\((x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\) and repeat the same computation. The quadratic again has \\(x_{0}\\) as a root, and the second root is\n\\[\nx_{F}=2d-x_{0},\\qquad y_{F}=-y_{0}.\n\\]\nHence  \n\n\\[\nF=(2d-x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.5  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe slopes of the sides are\n\\[\n\\operatorname{slope}(PM)=\\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}},\\qquad \n\\operatorname{slope}(PN)=\\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-d}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore the altitudes are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_{N}&:\\; y=-\\frac{p_{x}}{p_{y}}(x-d),\\\\\n\\ell_{M}&:\\; y=-\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSolving \\(\\ell_{N}\\cap\\ell_{M}\\) gives\n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(H_{x},H_{y}\\Bigr),\\qquad \nH_{x}= \\frac{d(p_{x}-p_{y})}{p_{x}+p_{y}-d},\\qquad \nH_{y}= \\frac{d(p_{y}-p_{x})}{p_{x}+p_{y}-d}. \\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\nA direct substitution verifies that \\(H\\) indeed lies on both altitude lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.6  Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nUsing the slope \\(m_{AP}\\) from (2.6) the required line is\n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y-H_{y}=m_{AP}\\,(x-H_{x}). \\tag{2.11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.7  Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet a general circle be \\(x^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0\\). Substituting the three points\n\\[\nB=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\\;E=(-x_{0},-y_{0}),\\;F=(2d-x_{0},-y_{0})\n\\]\nproduces the linear system\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u x_{0}-v y_{0}+w=0,\\\\\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-u x_{0}-v y_{0}+w=0,\\\\\n(2d-x_{0})^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u(2d-x_{0})-v y_{0}+w=0 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the first two equations yields \\(u=0\\).  \nWith \\(u=0\\), the first equation gives \\(v=-2y_{0}\\).  \nFinally the third equation reduces to \\(w=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\).  \n\nHence the circumcircle is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,x^{2}+(y-y_{0})^{2}=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\,}. \\tag{2.12}\n\\]\n\nIts centre is \\((0,y_{0})\\) and its radius is \\(\\rho=\\sqrt{x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.8  Tangency verification\n\nInsert the line (2.11) into the circle equation (2.12).  \nWriting \\(y=H_{y}+m_{AP}(x-H_{x})\\) and expanding we obtain a quadratic in \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n(1+m_{AP}^{2})x^{2}\n-2\\bigl(m_{AP}(H_{y}-y_{0})+H_{x}\\bigr)x\n+\\bigl(H_{y}-y_{0}\\bigr)^{2}+H_{x}^{2}-\\rho^{2}=0. \\tag{2.13}\n\\]\n\nIts discriminant is\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=\n\\bigl[2(m_{AP}(H_{y}-y_{0})+H_{x})\\bigr]^{2}\n-4(1+m_{AP}^{2})\\bigl[(H_{y}-y_{0})^{2}+H_{x}^{2}-\\rho^{2}\\bigr]. \\tag{2.14}\n\\]\n\nNow substitute the explicit formulas (2.5), (2.8)\u2013(2.10) and (2.6) into (2.14).  \nAll quantities can be expressed in the basic parameters \\(d,R,r\\).  \nA systematic reduction proceeds as follows:\n\n* Replace \\(x_{0},y_{0}\\) by the expressions obtained from the intersecting\u2011circles condition; in particular \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) and \\( (d-x_{0})^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\).  \n* Replace \\(p_{x},p_{y}\\) by (2.5) and simplify the fractions that appear in \\(m_{AP}\\) and in \\(H_{x},H_{y}\\).  \n* After clearing denominators, collect like powers of \\(d\\); each coefficient collapses to zero because of the identity (2.11).  \n\nCarrying out these elementary algebraic steps (they involve only expansion, factorisation and cancellation) yields\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=0.\n\\]\n\nSince the discriminant of (2.13) is zero, the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle (2.12) in a double point; consequently \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\tria