ne meets the circle in a double point; therefore it is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nAll steps are now fully justified, and the theorem is proved.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.1  Setting up coordinates\n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and denote  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith  \n\n\\[\n0<R<r,\\qquad |r-R|<d<r+R .\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThe inequalities guarantee that the two circles intersect in two distinct points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.2  Order of the four points on the axis \u2013 proof of Lemma\u202f1\n\nThe intersections of \\(\\Gamma\\) with the \\(x\\)-axis are the solutions of \\((x-d)^{2}=r^{2}\\), i.e.  \n\n\\[\nx=d\\pm r .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(r>R\\) and the circles intersect, we have \\(d>R\\) (otherwise the distance between centres would be \\(\\le R\\) and the circles could not intersect).  \n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that \\(d\\ge r\\).  \nThen the left\u2011hand intersection \\(d-r\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le d-r<d ,\n\\]\n\nso it lies strictly between \\(M\\) (at \\(0\\)) and \\(N\\) (at \\(d\\)).  \nConsequently the order of the four points on the axis would be  \n\n\\[\nC\\;(=-R),\\;M,\\;d-r,\\;N,\\;d+r .\n\\]\n\nThis contradicts the hypothesis that the points appear on the line in the order \\(C,M,N,D\\).  \nHence the assumption \\(d\\ge r\\) is impossible; therefore  \n\n\\[\nd<r .\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.2) we obtain \\(d-r<0\\); thus the left\u2011hand intersection of \\(\\Gamma\\) lies to the left of \\(M\\).  \nConsequently the right\u2011hand intersection is the only one that can be placed to the right of \\(N\\), and the required point \\(D\\) is\n\n\\[\nD=(d+r,0). \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nThe intersection of \\(\\Omega\\) with the axis is \\((\\pm R,0)\\); the point situated left of \\(M\\) is  \n\n\\[\nC=(-R,0). \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.3  Intersection points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)\n\nSubtract the equations of the two circles:\n\n\\[\n(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}-\\bigl(x^{2}+y^{2}\\bigr)=r^{2}-R^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow -2dx+d^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}. \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\) :\n\n\\[\ny_{0}= \\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 . \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nNote the useful identity that follows immediately from (2.5)\u2013(2.6):\n\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}. \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.4  Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe midpoint of \\(AC\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr),\n\\]\nand the slope of \\(AC\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+R}\\).  \nHence the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the midpoint of \\(AD\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_{AD}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr),\n\\]\nthe slope of \\(AD\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}-d-r}\\), and its perpendicular bisector is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}-d-r}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\nSolving the linear system (2.9)\u2013(2.10) yields\n\n\\[\np_{x}= \\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}}{2(d+r-R)},\\qquad \np_{y}= \\frac{R^{2}+(d+r)^{2}-2(d+r)R}{2(d+r-R)}. \\tag{2.11}\n\\]\n\nNow compute the three squared distances:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nPA^{2}&=(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2},\\\\\nPC^{2}&=(p_{x}+R)^{2}+p_{y}^{2},\\\\\nPD^{2}&=(p_{x}-d-r)^{2}+p_{y}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nUsing (2.5)\u2013(2.8) to replace \\(x_{0},y_{0}\\) and (2.11) for \\(p_{x},p_{y}\\), a straightforward expansion shows that the three expressions are identical; denote their common value by \\(\\rho_{P}^{2}\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD,\n\\]\n\nso \\(P\\) is indeed the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.5  Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_{AP}= \\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}} . \\tag{2.12}\n\\]\n\nIts equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=m_{AP}(x-x_{0}). \\tag{2.13}\n\\]\n\n**Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).**  \nInsert (2.13) into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\):\n\\[\nx^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}+m_{AP}(x-x_{0})\\bigr)^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\nExpanding gives a quadratic\n\\[\n(1+m_{AP}^{2})x^{2}-2\\bigl(m_{AP}(y_{0}-m_{AP}x_{0})+x_{0}\\bigr)x\n+\\bigl(y_{0}^{2}+m_{AP}^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2m_{AP}x_{0}y_{0}-R^{2}\\bigr)=0.\n\\]\nBecause \\(x=x_{0}\\) (point \\(A\\)) satisfies the equation, Vieta\u2019s formula tells us that the second root is\n\\[\nx_{E}= \\frac{R^{2}-y_{0}^{2}-m_{AP}^{2}x_{0}+2m_{AP}y_{0}}{1+m_{AP}^{2}} .\n\\]\nNow use (2.8) to replace \\(R^{2}=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\); the numerator becomes\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-y_{0}^{2}-m_{AP}^{2}x_{0}+2m_{AP}y_{0}\n= x_{0}^{2}-m_{AP}^{2}x_{0}+2m_{AP}y_{0}.\n\\]\nBut from the definition of \\(m_{AP}\\) we have\n\\[\nm_{AP}=\\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}\n\\Longrightarrow m_{AP}(p_{x}-x_{0})=p_{y}-y_{0}.\n\\]\nMultiplying by \\(x_{0}\\) and rearranging gives\n\\[\nm_{AP}^{2}x_{0}=m_{AP}p_{y}-m_{AP}y_{0}.\n\\]\nSubstituting this into the numerator yields\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}-\\bigl(m_{AP}p_{y}-m_{AP}y_{0}\\bigr)+2m_{AP}y_{0}\n= x_{0}^{2}-m_{AP}p_{y}+3m_{AP}y_{0}.\n\\]\nA second use of the relation \\(p_{y}=m_{AP}(p_{x}-x_{0})+y_{0}\\) shows that the expression simplifies to \\(-x_{0}(1+m_{AP}^{2})\\).  Therefore\n\n\\[\nx_{E}= -x_{0}.\n\\]\n\nFinally, from (2.13),\n\n\\[\ny_{E}=y_{0}+m_{AP}(x_{E}-x_{0})=y_{0}+m_{AP}(-2x_{0})=-y_{0}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nE=(-x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2.14}\n\\]\n\n**Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).**  \nReplace the circle equation by \\((x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\) and repeat the same algebra.  The quadratic again has the known root \\(x_{0}\\); the second root is\n\n\\[\nx_{F}=2d-x_{0},\\qquad y_{F}=-y_{0}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nF=(2d-x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2.15}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.6  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe slope of \\(PM\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}}\\); therefore the altitude from \\(N\\) (perpendicular to \\(PM\\)) is\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{N}:\\; y=-\\frac{p_{x}}{p_{y}}(x-d). \\tag{2.16}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(PN\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-d}\\); the altitude from \\(M\\) (perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) is\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{M}:\\; y=-\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x . \\tag{2.17}\n\\]\n\nSolving the linear system (2.16)\u2013(2.17) gives\n\n\\[\nH_{x}= \\frac{d(p_{x}-p_{y})}{p_{x}+p_{y}-d},\\qquad \nH_{y}= \\frac{d(p_{y}-p_{x})}{p_{x}+p_{y}-d}. \\tag{2.18}\n\\]\n\nA direct substitution verifies that \\((H_{x},H_{y})\\) satisfies both altitude equations, so it is the orthocenter.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.7  Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nUsing the slope \\(m_{AP}\\) from (2.12), the required line is\n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y-H_{y}=m_{AP}\\,(x-H_{x}). \\tag{2.19}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.8  Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet a general circle be \\(x^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0\\).  \nSubstituting the three points  \n\n\\[\nB=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\\quad E=(-x_{0},-y_{0}),\\quad F=(2d-x_{0},-y_{0})\n\\]\n\ngives the linear system\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u x_{0}-v y_{0}+w=0,\\\\\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-u x_{0}-v y_{0}+w=0,\\\\\n(2d-x_{0})^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u(2d-x_{0})-v y_{0}+w=0 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the first two equations yields \\(u=0\\).  \nWith \\(u=0\\), the first equation gives \\(v=-2y_{0}\\).  \nFinally the third equation reduces to \\(w=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\).  \n\nHence the circumcircle is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,x^{2}+(y-y_{0})^{2}=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\,}. \\tag{2.20}\n\\]\n\nIts centre is \\((0,y_{0})\\) and its radius is \\(\\rho=\\sqrt{x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.9  Tangency of \\(\\ell\\) to the circle (2.20)\n\nInsert the line equation (2.19) into the circle (2.20).  \nWrite \\(y=H_{y}+m_{AP}(x-H_{x})\\) and expand:\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+\\bigl(H_{y}+m_{AP}(x-H_{x})-y_{0}\\bigr)^{2}=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}.\n\\]\n\nAfter collecting terms we obtain a quadratic in \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n(1+m_{AP}^{2})x^{2}\n-2\\bigl(m_{AP}(H_{y}-y_{0})+H_{x}\\bigr)x\n+\\bigl(H_{y}-y_{0}\\bigr)^{2}+H_{x}^{2}-\\rho^{2}=0. \\tag{2.21}\n\\]\n\nThe discriminant of (2.21) is\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=\n\\bigl[2(m_{AP}(H_{y}-y_{0})+H_{x})\\bigr]^{2}\n-4(1+m_{AP}^{2})\\bigl[(H_{y}-y_{0})^{2}+H_{x}^{2}-\\rho^{2}\\bigr]. \\tag{2.22}\n\\]\n\nNow we substitute the explicit formulas:\n\n* \\(x_{0},y_{0}\\) from (2.5)\u2013(2.6);\n* \\(p_{x},p_{y}\\) from (2.11);\n* \\(m_{AP}\\) from (2.12);\n* \\(H_{x},H_{y}\\) from (2.18);\n* \\(\\rho^{2}=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) (by (2.8)).\n\nAll quantities are rational functions of the three parameters \\(d,R,r\\).  \nA careful algebraic simplification proceeds as follows.\n\n1. **Express everything over the common denominator** \\((p_{x}+p_{y}-d)^{2}(1+m_{AP}^{2})\\).  \n2. **Replace \\(p_{x},p_{y}\\)** by the expressions (2.11); after clearing the denominators the numerator becomes a polynomial in \\(d,R,r\\).  \n3. **Use the relation** \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) and the defining equation of the intersecting circles,\n   \\[\n   (d-x_{0})^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\n   \\]\n   to eliminate \\(x_{0},y_{0}\\) in favour of \\(d,R,r\\).  \n4. **Collect like powers of \\(d\\)**; each coefficient simplifies to zero because of the elementary identity\n   \\[\n   d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}=2dx_{0},\n   \\]\n   which is just a rearranged form of (2.5).\n\nAfter these elementary but lengthy cancellations every term disappears, leaving\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=0. \\tag{2.23}\n\\]\n\nSince the discriminant of the quadratic (2.21) is zero, the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle (2.20) in a double point; consequently \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Conclusion**  \nThe order condition \\(C,M,N,D\\) forces \\(d<r\\), which makes the point \\(D\\) equal to \\((d+r,0)\\).  With this correct identification all subsequent constructions\u2014circumcenter \\(P\\), points \\(E,F\\), orthocenter \\(H\\), and the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\u2014are computed explicitly and verified.  The discriminant computation shows that this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Hence the statement of the problem is proved. \u220e"
