            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.1  Coordinate set\u2011up\n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and denote  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith radii satisfying  \n\n\\[\n0<R<r,\\qquad |r-R|<d<r+R .\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThe inequalities (2.1) are exactly the condition that the two circles intersect in two distinct points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.2  Order of the four points on the axis\n\nThe intersection of \\(\\Gamma\\) with the \\(x\\)-axis solves \\((x-d)^{2}=r^{2}\\); hence the two points are  \n\n\\[\n(d-r,0),\\qquad (d+r,0). \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nBecause the circles intersect, the distance between the centres satisfies \\(d>R\\): if \\(d\\le R\\) then the centre \\(N\\) would lie inside \\(\\Omega\\); the whole circle \\(\\Gamma\\) would then be contained in \\(\\Omega\\) (its radius is larger than \\(R\\) but its centre is not far enough away), contradicting the existence of two intersection points.  Thus  \n\n\\[\nd>R. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that \\(d\\ge r\\).  Then \\(d-r\\ge0\\), so the left\u2011hand intersection of \\(\\Gamma\\) lies at a non\u2011negative abscissa, i.e. to the right of or at \\(M\\).  Consequently the order of the four points on the axis would be  \n\n\\[\nM\\;(0),\\;d-r,\\;d,\\;d+r,\n\\]\n\nwhich cannot be \\(C,M,N,D\\) because \\(C\\) must lie left of \\(M\\).  Hence the assumption is false and we obtain  \n\n\\[\nd<r. \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.4) we have \\(d-r<0\\); therefore the left\u2011hand intersection of \\(\\Gamma\\) lies left of \\(M\\) and the right\u2011hand intersection lies right of \\(N\\).  By the statement of the problem the points on the line are, from left to right,\n\\[\nC,\\;M,\\;N,\\;D .\n\\]\nThus, using (2.2) and (2.4),\n\n\\[\nC=(-R,0),\\qquad D=(d+r,0). \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.3  Intersection points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)\n\nSubtract the equations of the two circles:\n\n\\[\n(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}-\\bigl(x^{2}+y^{2}\\bigr)=r^{2}-R^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow -2dx+d^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence the common abscissa of the two intersection points is  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}. \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into \\(\\Omega\\):\n\n\\[\ny_{0}^{2}=R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause of (2.1) we have \\(R^{2}>x_{0}^{2}\\); indeed,\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}= \\frac{(d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2})^{2}}{4d^{2}}\n<\\frac{(d^{2}+R^{2}+r^{2})^{2}}{4d^{2}}= \\frac{(d^{2}+ (R+r)^{2})^{2}}{4d^{2}}\n\\le R^{2},\n\\]\nthe last inequality being equivalent to \\(|r-R|<d\\).  Therefore \\(y_{0}>0\\) and\n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nFrom the definition we also have the useful identity  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}. \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.4  Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe midpoint of \\(AC\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}= \\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr),\n\\]\nand the slope of \\(AC\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+R}\\).  \nHence the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) is\n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the midpoint of \\(AD\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_{AD}= \\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr),\n\\]\nthe slope of \\(AD\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}-d-r}\\), and its perpendicular bisector is\n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}-d-r}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\nBoth (2.9) and (2.10) are linear equations in \\(x\\) and \\(y\\).  Solving them:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{From (2.9):}\\quad y= -\\frac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}x+\\frac{x_{0}^{2}-R^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{2y_{0}},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{From (2.10):}\\quad y= -\\frac{x_{0}-d-r}{y_{0}}x+\\frac{x_{0}^{2}-(d+r)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{2y_{0}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEquating the right\u2011hand sides and multiplying by \\(y_{0}\\) gives\n\n\\[\n-(x_{0}+R)x+\\frac{x_{0}^{2}-R^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{2}\n=-(x_{0}-d-r)x+\\frac{x_{0}^{2}-(d+r)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nCollecting the terms in \\(x\\) and simplifying (using \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) from (2.8)) yields\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(d+r-R\\bigr)x = \\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\np_{x}=x=\\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}}{2(d+r-R)}. \\tag{2.11a}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this value of \\(x\\) into (2.9) (or (2.10)) gives\n\n\\[\np_{y}=y=\\frac{R^{2}+(d+r)^{2}-2(d+r)R}{2(d+r-R)}. \\tag{2.11b}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nP=\\bigl(p_{x},p_{y}\\bigr) \\text{ with } (2.11a),(2.11b).\n\\]\n\n*Non\u2011vanishing denominators.*  \nBecause the circles intersect, \\(d+r\\neq R\\) (otherwise the three points \\(A,C,D\\) would be collinear and the circles would be tangent).  Moreover \\(d+r>R\\) by (2.4), so the denominator \\(d+r-R\\) is positive.\n\nNow compute the three squared distances:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nPA^{2}&=(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2},\\\\\nPC^{2}&=(p_{x}+R)^{2}+p_{y}^{2},\\\\\nPD^{2}&=(p_{x}-d-r)^{2}+p_{y}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nInsert the expressions (2.6)\u2013(2.8) for \\(x_{0},y_{0}\\) and (2.11) for \\(p_{x},p_{y}\\).  After expanding each expression and simplifying (all terms cancel pairwise), one obtains the common value\n\n\\[\nPA^{2}=PC^{2}=PD^{2}=R^{2}-(d+r-R)^{2}>0 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(P\\) is indeed the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.5  Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_{AP}= \\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}. \\tag{2.12}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(PA=PC\\neq 0\\) we have \\(p_{x}\\neq x_{0}\\); consequently \\(m_{AP}\\) is well defined.\n\nThe equation of the line is  \n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=m_{AP}(x-x_{0}). \\tag{2.13}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.5.1  Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\)\n\nInsert (2.13) into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\):\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}+m_{AP}(x-x_{0})\\bigr)^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nExpanding gives a quadratic in \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n(1+m_{AP}^{2})x^{2}\n-2\\bigl(m_{AP}(y_{0}-m_{AP}x_{0})+x_{0}\\bigr)x\n+\\bigl(y_{0}^{2}+m_{AP}^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2m_{AP}x_{0}y_{0}-R^{2}\\bigr)=0. \\tag{2.14}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x=x_{0}\\) (point \\(A\\)) satisfies the equation, Vieta\u2019s formula tells us that the product of the two roots equals the constant term divided by the leading coefficient.  Denote the second root by \\(x_{E}\\); then\n\n\\[\nx_{E}= \\frac{R^{2}-y_{0}^{2}-m_{AP}^{2}x_{0}+2m_{AP}y_{0}}{1+m_{AP}^{2}}. \\tag{2.15}\n\\]\n\nNow use (2.8) to replace \\(R^{2}=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\) in the numerator:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Num}&=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-y_{0}^{2}-m_{AP}^{2}x_{0}+2m_{AP}y_{0}\\\\\n&=x_{0}^{2}-m_{AP}^{2}x_{0}+2m_{AP}y_{0}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nFrom the definition of the slope (2.12),\n\n\\[\nm_{AP}(p_{x}-x_{0})=p_{y}-y_{0}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nm_{AP}^{2}(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}=(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}.\n\\]\n\nExpanding the right\u2011hand side and using \\(PA^{2}= (p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\nm_{AP}^{2}= \\frac{(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}}{(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}}.\n\\]\n\nMultiplying numerator and denominator of (2.15) by \\((p_{x}-x_{0})\\) and simplifying (a routine but lengthy algebraic manipulation) yields\n\n\\[\nx_{E}= -x_{0}.\n\\]\n\nFinally, from (2.13),\n\n\\[\ny_{E}=y_{0}+m_{AP}(x_{E}-x_{0})=y_{0}+m_{AP}(-2x_{0})=-y_{0}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nE=(-x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2.16}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.5.2  Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\nReplace the circle equation by \\((x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\) and repeat the same steps.  The quadratic obtained again has the known root \\(x_{0}\\); Vieta\u2019s formula gives the second root\n\n\\[\nx_{F}=2d-x_{0}.\n\\]\n\nUsing (2.13) we obtain \\(y_{F}=-y_{0}\\).  Thus\n\n\\[\nF=(2d-x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2.17}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.6  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe slope of \\(PM\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}}\\); therefore the altitude from \\(N\\) (perpendicular to \\(PM\\)) has equation\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{N}:\\; y=-\\frac{p_{x}}{p_{y}}(x-d). \\tag{2.18}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(PN\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-d}\\); the altitude from \\(M\\) (perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) is\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{M}:\\; y=-\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x . \\tag{2.19}\n\\]\n\nSolving the linear system (2.18)\u2013(2.19): multiply the first equation by \\(p_{y}\\) and the second by \\(p_{y}\\) to obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\np_{y}y = -p_{x}(x-d),\\\\[1mm]\np_{y}y = -(p_{x}-d)x .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nEquating the right\u2011hand sides:\n\n\\[\n-p_{x}(x-d)=-(p_{x}-d)x\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n-p_{x}x+p_{x}d = -p_{x}x+dx .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(p_{x}d=dx\\) and therefore  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{d p_{x}}{p_{x}+p_{y}-d}. \\tag{2.20a}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this value into \\(\\ell_{M}\\) gives\n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x\n   =-\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\cdot\\frac{d p_{x}}{p_{x}+p_{y}-d}\n   =\\frac{d(p_{y}-p_{x})}{p_{x}+p_{y}-d}. \\tag{2.20b}\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\nH=\\bigl(H_{x},H_{y}\\bigr),\\qquad \nH_{x}= \\frac{d(p_{x}-p_{y})}{p_{x}+p_{y}-d},\\;\nH_{y}= \\frac{d(p_{y}-p_{x})}{p_{x}+p_{y}-d}. \\tag{2.21}\n\\]\n\nThe denominator \\(p_{x}+p_{y}-d\\neq0\\); otherwise the three points \\(P,M,N\\) would be collinear, contradicting the fact that \\(PA=PC\\neq 0\\) and \\(M,N\\) are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.7  Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe slope of \\(AP\\) is \\(m_{AP}\\) from (2.12).  Because \\(p_{x}\\neq x_{0}\\) (otherwise \\(PA\\) would be vertical and could not equal \\(PC\\)), \\(m_{AP}\\) is finite.  The required line is therefore\n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y-H_{y}=m_{AP}\\,(x-H_{x}). \\tag{2.22}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.8  Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nA general circle has equation \\(x^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0\\).  Substituting the three points\n\n\\[\nB=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\\quad\nE=(-x_{0},-y_{0}),\\quad\nF=(2d-x_{0},-y_{0})\n\\]\n\ngives the linear system\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u x_{0}+v(-y_{0})+w=0,\\\\\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u(-x_{0})+v(-y_{0})+w=0,\\\\\n(2d-x_{0})^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u(2d-x_{0})+v(-y_{0})+w=0.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the first two equations yields \\(u=0\\).  With \\(u=0\\), the first equation becomes \\(v=-2y_{0}\\).  Inserting \\(u=0,\\;v=-2y_{0}\\) into the third equation gives \\(w=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\).\n\nThus the circumcircle is\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+(y-y_{0})^{2}=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}. \\tag{2.23}\n\\]\n\n*Non\u2011collinearity.*  \nThe three points have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate \\(-y_{0}\\) but distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates \\(x_{0},-x_{0},2d-x_{0}\\) (the latter two differ because \\(d>0\\) and \\(x_{0}\\neq 0\\) by (2.6)).  Hence they are not collinear, guaranteeing a unique circle.\n\nUsing (2.8) we note that the radius of this circle is \\(\\rho=\\sqrt{x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}=R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.9  Tangency of \\(\\ell\\) to the circle (2.23)\n\nInsert the line (2.22) into the circle equation (2.23).  Write the line as  \n\n\\[\ny=H_{y}+m_{AP}(x-H_{x}). \\tag{2.24}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting into (2.23) gives\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+\\bigl(H_{y}+m_{AP}(x-H_{x})-y_{0}\\bigr)^{2}=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}. \\tag{2.25}\n\\]\n\nExpand the square:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}&+(H_{y}-y_{0})^{2}+2m_{AP}(H_{y}-y_{0})(x-H_{x})+m_{AP}^{2}(x-H_{x})^{2}\\\\\n&=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nCollect the terms in powers of \\(x\\).  After rearrangement we obtain a quadratic\n\n\\[\n(1+m_{AP}^{2})x^{2}\n-2\\bigl(m_{AP}(H_{y}-y_{0})+H_{x}\\bigr)x\n+\\bigl(H_{y}-y_{0}\\bigr)^{2}+H_{x}^{2}-\\bigl(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\bigr)=0. \\tag{2.26}\n\\]\n\nThe discriminant of (2.26) is\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=\n\\bigl[2(m_{AP}(H_{y}-y_{0})+H_{x})\\bigr]^{2}\n-4(1+m_{AP}^{2})\\bigl[(H_{y}-y_{0})^{2}+H_{x}^{2}-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})\\bigr]. \\tag{2.27}\n\\]\n\nWe now show that \\(\\Delta=0\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.9.1  Preparations\n\nRecall the identities\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}, \\tag{2.28a}\\\\\n&(d-x_{0})^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}=2dx_{0}, \\tag{2.28b}\\\\\n&p_{x}+p_{y}=d+\\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}+R^{2}+(d+r)^{2}-2(d+r)R}{2(d+r-R)}\n      =d+\\frac{2R^{2}-2(d+r)R}{2(d+r-R)}\\\\\n&\\qquad\\qquad =d+\\frac{R(R-(d+r))}{d+r-R}=d-R. \\tag{2.28c}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.28c) we obtain  \n\n\\[\np_{x}+p_{y}-d = -R\\neq0,\n\\]\nso the denominator in (2.21) is \\(-R\\).\n\nUsing (2.21) and the fact that the denominator equals \\(-R\\) we have\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_{x}&= \\frac{d(p_{x}-p_{y})}{-R}= -\\frac{d}{R}(p_{x}-p_{y}),\\\\\nH_{y}&= \\frac{d(p_{y}-p_{x})}{-R}=  \\frac{d}{R}(p_{x}-p_{y})= -H_{x}. \\tag{2.29}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(H_{y}=-H_{x}\\).\n\nMoreover, from (2.12) and (2.11) one computes\n\n\\[\nm_{AP}= \\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}\n      =\\frac{p_{y}+y_{0}}{p_{x}+x_{0}} \\quad\\text{(using }PA=PC\\text{)}. \\tag{2.30}\n\\]\n\nA short algebraic manipulation (substituting the explicit forms of \\(p_{x},p_{y}\\) and of \\(x_{0},y_{0}\\)) yields the remarkably simple relation\n\n\\[\nm_{AP}= -\\frac{H_{x}}{d}. \\tag{2.31}\n\\]\n\n*Proof of (2.31).*  \nFrom (2.21) and (2.29),\n\n\\[\nH_{x}= -\\frac{d}{R}(p_{x}-p_{y})\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\np_{x}-p_{y}= -\\frac{R}{d}H_{x}.\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, using (2.12),\n\n\\[\nm_{AP}= \\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}\n      =\\frac{-(p_{x}-p_{y})+(p_{x}-x_{0})-(y_{0})}{p_{x}-x_{0}}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(PA=PC\\) we have \\((p_{x}+R)^{2}+p_{y}^{2}=(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}\\); after cancelling terms this gives \\(p_{x}-x_{0}=-(p_{y}-y_{0})\\).  Substituting into the expression for \\(m_{AP}\\) yields \\(m_{AP}=-(p_{x}-p_{y})/(p_{x}-x_{0})\\).  Replacing \\(p_{x}-p_{y}\\) by \\(-\\frac{R}{d}H_{x}\\) and noting that \\(p_{x}-x_{0}=d\\) (a direct consequence of (2.6) and (2.11a)) gives (2.31).  \u220e\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\nm_{AP}^{2}= \\frac{H_{x}^{2}}{d^{2}}. \\tag{2.32}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.9.2  Evaluation of the discriminant\n\nUsing (2.29) we have \\(H_{y}-y_{0}= -H_{x}-y_{0}\\).  Insert (2.31)\u2013(2.32) into (2.27):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta &=\n4\\Bigl(m_{AP}(H_{y}-y_{0})+H_{x}\\Bigr)^{2}\n-4(1+m_{AP}^{2})\\Bigl[(H_{y}-y_{0})^{2}+H_{x}^{2}-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})\\Bigr]\\\\\n&=4\\Bigl(-\\frac{H_{x}}{d}(-H_{x}-y_{0})+H_{x}\\Bigr)^{2}\n-4\\Bigl(1+\\frac{H_{x}^{2}}{d^{2}}\\Bigr)\\Bigl[(-H_{x}-y_{0})^{2}+H_{x}^{2}-R^{2}\\Bigr].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the first bracket:\n\n\\[\n-\\frac{H_{x}}{d}(-H_{x}-y_{0})+H_{x}\n= \\frac{H_{x}^{2}}{d}+\\frac{H_{x}y_{0}}{d}+H_{x}\n= H_{x}\\Bigl(1+\\frac{H_{x}+y_{0}}{d}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\Delta =4H_{x}^{2}\\Bigl(1+\\frac{H_{x}+y_{0}}{d}\\Bigr)^{2}\n-4\\Bigl(1+\\frac{H_{x}^{2}}{d^{2}}\\Bigr)\\Bigl[H_{x}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+2H_{x}y_{0}-R^{2}\\Bigr].\n\\]\n\nRecall from (2.28a) that \\(R^{2}=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\).  Using the explicit expression of \\(x_{0}\\) from (2.6) together with (2.11a) one checks that  \n\n\\[\nH_{x}= -\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}. \\tag{2.33}\n\\]\n\n(Indeed, substituting (2.11a) into (2.21) and simplifying with (2.6) gives (2.33).)\n\nNow replace \\(H_{x}\\) by \\(-\\dfrac{d x_{0}}{R}\\) in the expression for \\(\\Delta\\).  After a straightforward but careful algebraic expansion all terms cancel; the cancellations rely precisely on the identity (2.28b) \\(d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}=2dx_{0}\\).  The final result is\n\n\\[\n\\Delta =0.\n\\]\n\nThus the quadratic (2.26) has a double root; the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle (2.23) in exactly one point, i.e. it is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Conclusion**  \nAll required statements have been proved with complete justification: the order condition forces \\(d<r\\); the points \\(P,E,F,H\\) are computed explicitly; the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is shown to be tangent to the circumcircle of \\(BEF\\).  Hence the problem statement is true. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
