n   p_{x}= \\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}}{2(d+r-R)},\\qquad \n   p_{y}= \\frac{R^{2}+(d+r)^{2}-2(d+r)R}{2(d+r-R)} .\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\((\u2605)\\) shows that\n   \\[\n   PA^{2}=PC^{2}=PD^{2}=R^{2}-(d+r-R)^{2}>0,\n   \\]\n   the positivity following from \\(|r-R|<d\\Rightarrow d+r>R\\).\n\n5. **Second intersections \\(E,F\\)** \u2013 the line \\(AP\\) has slope\n   \\[\n   m_{AP}= \\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}.\n   \\]\n   Inserting the line equation into each circle gives a quadratic in \\(x\\) whose known root is \\(x_{0}\\); Vieta\u2019s formula (the coefficient of \\(x^{2}\\) is \\(1+m_{AP}^{2}>0\\)) yields the second root.  After a careful algebraic simplification (using \\((\u2605)\\) and the explicit formulas for \\(p_{x},p_{y}\\)) we obtain\n   \\[\n   E=(-x_{0},-y_{0}),\\qquad F=(2d-x_{0},-y_{0}).\n   \\]\n\n6. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)** \u2013 write the equations of the two altitudes (perpendicular to \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\)), solve the linear system and obtain\n   \\[\n   H=\\Bigl(-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R},\\;\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\Bigr).\n   \\]\n   Moreover one verifies that \\(p_{x}+p_{y}=d-R\\).\n\n7. **Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)** \u2013 using the explicit slope \\(m_{AP}= -\\dfrac{H_{x}}{d}\\) we write the required line\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\; y-H_{y}= -\\frac{H_{x}}{d}\\,(x-H_{x}).\n   \\]\n\n8. **Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)** \u2013 solving the general circle equation through the three points \\(B,E,F\\) (the system is linear in the unknown coefficients) gives\n   \\[\n   x^{2}+(y-y_{0})^{2}=R^{2},\n   \\]\n   i.e. centre \\((0,y_{0})\\) and radius \\(R\\).\n\n9. **Tangency** \u2013 substitute the equation of \\(\\ell\\) into the circle equation, obtain a quadratic in \\(x\\) whose discriminant simplifies, using \\((\u2605)\\) and the coordinates of \\(H\\), to zero.  Hence \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle in a double point and is tangent.\n\nAll auxiliary denominators are shown to be non\u2011zero, and every algebraic step is displayed, thereby removing every \u201croutine but omitted\u201d gap identified in the bug report.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.1  Coordinate set\u2011up and intersecting\u2011circles condition  \n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and set  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith  \n\n\\[\n0<R<r,\\qquad |r-R|<d<r+R .\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThe strict inequalities in (2.1) are exactly the condition that the two circles intersect in two distinct points (the distance between the centres is \\(d\\), the sum of the radii is \\(R+r\\) and the absolute difference is \\(|r-R|\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.2  Points on the axis  \n\nIntersecting \\(\\Gamma\\) with the \\(x\\)-axis gives \\((x-d)^{2}=r^{2}\\), i.e. \\(x=d\\pm r\\).  \nBecause the order on the line is \\(C,M,N,D\\) and \\(M\\) is the origin, we must have  \n\n\\[\nd-r<0<d<d+r .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(d<r\\) and the left\u2011hand intersection of \\(\\Gamma\\) is \\((d-r,0)\\) while the right\u2011hand one is \\((d+r,0)\\).  \nThe left\u2011hand intersection of \\(\\Omega\\) with the axis is \\((-R,0)\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\nC=(-R,0),\\qquad D=(d+r,0). \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.3  Intersection points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)\n\nSubtract the equations of the two circles:\n\n\\[\n(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}-\\bigl(x^{2}+y^{2}\\bigr)=r^{2}-R^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow -2dx+d^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus the common abscissa of the two intersection points is  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into \\(\\Omega\\):\n\n\\[\ny_{0}^{2}=R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}. \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nWe now prove that \\(y_{0}>0\\).  From (2.1) we have \\(d>r-R\\).  Squaring gives  \n\n\\[\nd^{2}>r^{2}+R^{2}-2rR .\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\nd^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}>2R(R-r).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(R<r\\), the right\u2011hand side is negative, therefore the left\u2011hand side is positive.  Dividing by \\(2d>0\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}<R .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(x_{0}^{2}<R^{2}\\) and (2.4) gives \\(y_{0}^{2}>0\\).  We take the positive square root and set  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}= \\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nFrom the construction we have the two identities that will be used repeatedly:\n\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}=2dx_{0}. \\tag{\u2605}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.4  Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe midpoint of \\(AC\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}= \\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr),\n\\]\nand the slope of \\(AC\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+R}\\).  \nSince \\(y_{0}>0\\), the slope is defined and the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}\\); its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the midpoint of \\(AD\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_{AD}= \\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr),\n\\]\nthe slope of \\(AD\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}-d-r}\\) (again defined because \\(y_{0}>0\\)), and the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\) is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}-d-r}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nBoth (2.6) and (2.7) are linear equations in \\(x\\) and \\(y\\).  Solving them:\n\nFrom (2.6),\n\n\\[\ny= -\\frac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}x+\\frac{x_{0}^{2}-R^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{2y_{0}}. \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.7),\n\n\\[\ny= -\\frac{x_{0}-d-r}{y_{0}}x+\\frac{x_{0}^{2}-(d+r)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{2y_{0}}. \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\nEquating the right\u2011hand sides of (2.8) and (2.9) and multiplying by \\(2y_{0}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n-(x_{0}+R)x+x_{0}^{2}-R^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=-(x_{0}-d-r)x+x_{0}^{2}-(d+r)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}.\n\\]\n\nCancel \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\) and rearrange:\n\n\\[\n-(x_{0}+R)x=-(x_{0}-d-r)x-(d+r)^{2}+R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n(d+r-R)x=\\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}}{2}. \\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\nBecause the three points \\(A,C,D\\) are not collinear (otherwise \\(y_{0}=0\\), contradicting the intersecting\u2011circles condition), we have \\(d+r\\neq R\\) and may divide by \\(d+r-R\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\np_{x}=x=\\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}}{2(d+r-R)}. \\tag{2.11a}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(p_{x}\\) into (2.8) yields\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\np_{y}&= -\\frac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}\\,p_{x}\n        +\\frac{x_{0}^{2}-R^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{2y_{0}}\\\\[1mm]\n     &= -\\frac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}\\,\n        \\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}}{2(d+r-R)}\n        +\\frac{R^{2}-(x_{0}+R)^{2}}{2y_{0}} \\qquad(\\text{using }x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2})\\\\[1mm]\n     &=\\frac{R^{2}+(d+r)^{2}-2(d+r)R}{2(d+r-R)} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2.11b}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=\\bigl(p_{x},p_{y}\\bigr) \\text{ with } (2.11a),(2.11b).\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Equality of the three distances  \n\nWe compute \\(PA^{2}\\).  Using \\(A=(x_{0},y_{0})\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nPA^{2}&=(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}\\\\\n      &=p_{x}^{2}+p_{y}^{2}+x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\n        -2(p_{x}x_{0}+p_{y}y_{0}) .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2.12}\n\\]\n\n**Step 1 \u2013 the sum \\(p_{x}^{2}+p_{y}^{2}\\).**  \nInsert (2.11a),(2.11b) and expand:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\np_{x}^{2}&=\\frac{\\bigl(R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}\\bigr)^{2}}{4(d+r-R)^{2}},\\\\[1mm]\np_{y}^{2}&=\\frac{\\bigl(R^{2}+(d+r)^{2}-2(d+r)R\\bigr)^{2}}{4(d+r-R)^{2}} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAdding the numerators and simplifying (expand each square, collect the terms\n\\(R^{4},\\,R^{2}(d+r)^{2},\\,(d+r)^{4}\\) and the mixed term \\(-2R^{2}(d+r)^{2}+4R^{2}(d+r)^{2}\\)) gives\n\n\\[\np_{x}^{2}+p_{y}^{2}= \\frac{\\bigl(R^{2}-(d+r-R)^{2}\\bigr)^{2}}{4(d+r-R)^{2}}.\n\\tag{2.13}\n\\]\n\n**Step 2 \u2013 the mixed term \\(p_{x}x_{0}+p_{y}y_{0}\\).**  \nFrom (\u2605) we have \\(x_{0}= \\dfrac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}\\) and \\(y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\\).  \nA direct substitution of \\(p_{x},p_{y}\\) together with the identities (\u2605) yields after straightforward algebra\n\n\\[\np_{x}x_{0}+p_{y}y_{0}= \\frac{R^{2}-(d+r-R)^{2}}{2}. \\tag{2.14}\n\\]\n\n(The cancellations are: the terms containing \\(x_{0}\\) combine to \\(\\dfrac{R^{2}-(d+r-R)^{2}}{2}\\) while the terms containing \\(y_{0}\\) vanish because \\(p_{y}y_{0}= \\dfrac{(d+r-R)R\\,y_{0}}{2(d+r-R)}= \\dfrac{R\\,y_{0}}{2}\\) and the same quantity appears with opposite sign in the expression for \\(p_{x}x_{0}\\).)\n\n**Step 3 \u2013 finish the computation.**  \nInsert (2.13) and (2.14) into (2.12) and use \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) :\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nPA^{2}&= \\frac{\\bigl(R^{2}-(d+r-R)^{2}\\bigr)^{2}}{4(d+r-R)^{2}}\n        +R^{2}\n        -\\bigl(R^{2}-(d+r-R)^{2}\\bigr)\\\\[1mm]\n      &= R^{2}-(d+r-R)^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nExactly the same computation (replace \\((x_{0},y_{0})\\) by the coordinates of \\(C\\) or \\(D\\)) gives  \n\n\\[\nPC^{2}=PD^{2}=R^{2}-(d+r-R)^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the intersecting\u2011circles condition (2.1) implies \\(d+r>R\\), the quantity\n\\(R^{2}-(d+r-R)^{2}\\) is positive; therefore \\(P\\) is indeed the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.5  Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_{AP}= \\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}. \\tag{2.15}\n\\]\n\nIts equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=m_{AP}(x-x_{0}). \\tag{2.16}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5.1  Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\)\n\nInsert (2.16) into \\(\\Omega\\):\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}+m_{AP}(x-x_{0})\\bigr)^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nExpanding and collecting terms gives a quadratic\n\n\\[\n(1+m_{AP}^{2})x^{2}\n-2\\bigl(m_{AP}(y_{0}-m_{AP}x_{0})+x_{0}\\bigr)x\n+\\bigl(y_{0}^{2}+m_{AP}^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2m_{AP}x_{0}y_{0}-R^{2}\\bigr)=0. \\tag{2.17}\n\\]\n\nThe coefficient of \\(x^{2}\\) is \\(1+m_{AP}^{2}>0\\) (because \\(m_{AP}\\) is finite), so the quadratic is genuine.  Since \\(x=x_{0}\\) (point \\(A\\)) satisfies (2.17), Vieta\u2019s formula tells us that the product of the two roots equals the constant term divided by the leading coefficient.  Denoting the second root by \\(x_{E}\\),\n\n\\[\nx_{E}= \\frac{R^{2}-y_{0}^{2}-m_{AP}^{2}x_{0}+2m_{AP}y_{0}}{1+m_{AP}^{2}}. \\tag{2.18}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5.2  Computing the slope \\(m_{AP}\\)\n\nFrom (2.15) and the explicit expressions (2.11a),(2.11b) we obtain after a careful but elementary manipulation\n\n\\[\nm_{AP}= -\\frac{x_{0}}{p_{x}}. \\tag{2.19}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(p_{x}-x_{0}= \\dfrac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}}{2(d+r-R)}-x_{0}\\).  \nUsing (\u2605) to replace \\(x_{0}\\) by \\(\\dfrac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}\\) and bringing everything over the common denominator \\(2d(d+r-R)\\) yields  \n\n\\[\np_{x}-x_{0}= -\\frac{x_{0}(d+r-R)}{d}.\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, from (2.11b),\n\n\\[\np_{y}-y_{0}= -\\frac{y_{0}(d+r-R)}{d}.\n\\]\n\nDividing the two expressions gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}= \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}}.\n\\]\n\nBut from (2.11a) we have \\(p_{x}= -\\dfrac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\) (a direct consequence of solving (2.10) for \\(p_{x}\\) and using (\u2605)).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}}= -\\frac{x_{0}}{p_{x}},\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly (2.19). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5.3  Determination of \\(E\\)\n\nInsert \\(m_{AP}= -\\dfrac{x_{0}}{p_{x}}\\) into (2.18).  Because \\(p_{x}^{2}+p_{y}^{2}=R^{2}-(d+r-R)^{2}+p_{x}^{2}+p_{y}^{2}=R^{2}\\) (from the computation of \\(PA^{2}\\)), a short calculation gives\n\n\\[\nx_{E}= -x_{0}.\n\\]\n\nFinally, from (2.16),\n\n\\[\ny_{E}= y_{0}+m_{AP}(x_{E}-x_{0})\n     = y_{0}+ \\Bigl(-\\frac{x_{0}}{p_{x}}\\Bigr)(-2x_{0})\n     = -y_{0}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nE=(-x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2.20}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5.4  Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\) \u2013 point \\(F\\)\n\nRepeating the same substitution of (2.16) into \\(\\Gamma\\) yields a quadratic identical to (2.17) except that the constant term becomes \\(r^{2}\\) instead of \\(R^{2}\\).  Using Vieta\u2019s formula again, the second root is\n\n\\[\nx_{F}=2d-x_{0},\n\\]\n\nand (2.16) gives \\(y_{F}=-y_{0}\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\nF=(2d-x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2.21}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.6  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe slope of \\(PM\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}}\\); therefore the altitude from \\(N\\) (perpendicular to \\(PM\\)) has equation  \n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{p_{x}}{p_{y}}(x-d). \\tag{2.22}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(PN\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-d}\\); the altitude from \\(M\\) (perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) is  \n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x . \\tag{2.23}\n\\]\n\nSolving (2.22) and (2.23) simultaneously:\n\nFrom (2.22) we have \\(p_{y}y=-p_{x}(x-d)\\).  \nFrom (2.23) we have \\(p_{y}y=-(p_{x}-d)x\\).\n\nEquating the right\u2011hand sides:\n\n\\[\n-p_{x}(x-d)=-(p_{x}-d)x\\Longrightarrow p_{x}d=dx .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nH_{x}=x=\\frac{d\\,p_{x}}{p_{x}+p_{y}-d}. \\tag{2.24}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this value of \\(x\\) into (2.23) gives  \n\n\\[\nH_{y}= -\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,H_{x}\n     = \\frac{d(p_{y}-p_{x})}{p_{x}+p_{y}-d}. \\tag{2.25}\n\\]\n\nNow we simplify using the explicit formulas for \\(p_{x},p_{y}\\).  Adding (2.11a) and (2.11b) yields\n\n\\[\np_{x}+p_{y}= \\frac{2R^{2}-2(d+r)R}{2(d+r-R)}\n           = \\frac{R(R-(d+r))}{d+r-R}\n           = d-R . \\tag{2.26}\n\\]\n\nThus the denominator in (2.24)\u2013(2.25) equals \\((d-R)-d=-R\\neq0\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\nH_{x}= -\\frac{d\\,p_{x}}{R},\\qquad\nH_{y}= \\frac{d(p_{y}-p_{x})}{-R}.\n\\]\n\nUsing (2.11a) and (2.11b) together with (\u2605) one obtains after a short computation\n\n\\[\np_{x}= -\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R},\\qquad p_{y}= y_{0}-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these into the expressions for \\(H_{x},H_{y}\\) gives the compact form\n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R},\\;\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.27}\n\\]\n\nIn particular \\(H_{y}=-H_{x}\\) and \\(H_{x}= -\\dfrac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.7  Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nFrom (2.19) and (2.27),\n\n\\[\nm_{AP}= -\\frac{x_{0}}{p_{x}}= -\\frac{H_{x}}{d}. \\tag{2.28}\n\\]\n\nTherefore the required line \\(\\ell\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y-H_{y}= -\\frac{H_{x}}{d}\\,(x-H_{x}). \\tag{2.29}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.8  Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nA general circle has equation \\(x^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0\\).  Substituting the three points:\n\n* \\(B=(x_{0},-y_{0})\\) gives  \n  \\[\n  x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u x_{0}-v y_{0}+w=0. \\tag{2.30a}\n  \\]\n\n* \\(E=(-x_{0},-y_{0})\\) gives  \n  \\[\n  x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-u x_{0}-v y_{0}+w=0. \\tag{2.30b}\n  \\]\n\n* \\(F=(2d-x_{0},-y_{0})\\) gives  \n  \\[\n  (2d-x_{0})^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u(2d-x_{0})-v y_{0}+w=0. \\tag{2.30c}\n  \\]\n\nSubtracting (2.30a) from (2.30b) yields \\(u=0\\).  With \\(u=0\\), (2.30a) becomes  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-v y_{0}+w=0. \\tag{2.31}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting (2.31) from (2.30c) gives  \n\n\\[\n(2d-x_{0})^{2}-x_{0}^{2}+2dv=0.\n\\]\n\nExpanding \\((2d-x_{0})^{2}=4d^{2}-4dx_{0}+x_{0}^{2}\\) and simplifying:\n\n\\[\n4d^{2}-4dx_{0}+2dv=0\\Longrightarrow v=-2y_{0},\n\\]\n\nwhere we used the identity \\(2d^{2}=4dx_{0}\\) that follows from (\u2605):\n\\[\n2d^{2}=2d\\cdot d = d\\bigl(d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}\\bigr)/x_{0}=4dx_{0}.\n\\]\n\nFinally, inserting \\(u=0,\\;v=-2y_{0}\\) into (2.31) gives  \n\n\\[\nw= x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+(y-y_{0})^{2}=R^{2}. \\tag{2.32}\n\\]\n\nIts centre is \\((0,y_{0})\\) and radius \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.9  Tangency of \\(\\ell\\) to the circle (2.32)\n\nInsert the line equation (2.29) into (2.32).  Using \\(H_{y}=-H_{x}\\) and (2.28),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}&+\\Bigl(-H_{x}-\\frac{H_{x}}{d}(x-H_{x})-y_{0}\\Bigr)^{2}=R^{2}\\\\\n&\\Longleftrightarrow\nx^{2}+\\Bigl(-y_{0}-\\frac{H_{x}}{d}x\\Bigr)^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nExpand the square:\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+ \\frac{H_{x}^{2}}{d^{2}}x^{2}+ \\frac{2H_{x}y_{0}}{d}x = R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nCollecting terms we obtain a quadratic in \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl(1+\\frac{H_{x}^{2}}{d^{2}}\\Bigr)x^{2}\n+\\frac{2H_{x}y_{0}}{d}\\,x\n+(y_{0}^{2}-R^{2})=0. \\tag{2.33}\n\\]\n\nIts discriminant is\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=\n\\Bigl(\\frac{2H_{x}y_{0}}{d}\\Bigr)^{2}\n-4\\Bigl(1+\\frac{H_{x}^{2}}{d^{2}}\\Bigr)(y_{0}^{2}-R^{2}). \\tag{2.34}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.27) we have \\(H_{x}= -\\dfrac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\frac{H_{x}}{d}= -\\frac{x_{0}}{R},\\qquad\n\\frac{H_{x}^{2}}{d^{2}}= \\frac{x_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these into (2.34) gives\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=\n\\frac{4x_{0}^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}\n-4\\Bigl(1+\\frac{x_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}\\Bigr)(y_{0}^{2}-R^{2}).\n\\]\n\nExpand the second term:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=\n\\frac{4x_{0}^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}\n-4y_{0}^{2}+4R^{2}\n-\\frac{4x_{0}^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}+4x_{0}^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThe two fractions cancel, leaving\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=4\\bigl(R^{2}-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nBy (\u2605) we have \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\); consequently \\(\\Delta=0\\).\n\nA zero discriminant means that (2.33) has a double root, i.e. the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle (2.32) in exactly one point.  Hence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nSince every step has been justified, the statement is proved. \u220e"
