            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.1  Coordinate set\u2011up and intersecting\u2011circles condition  \n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and set  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith  \n\n\\[\n0<R<r,\\qquad |r-R|<d<r+R .\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThe strict inequalities in (2.1) are exactly the condition that the two circles intersect in two distinct points (the distance between the centres is \\(d\\), the sum of the radii is \\(R+r\\) and the absolute difference is \\(|r-R|\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.2  Points on the axis  \n\nIntersecting \\(\\Gamma\\) with the \\(x\\)-axis gives \\((x-d)^{2}=r^{2}\\), i.e. \\(x=d\\pm r\\).  \nBecause the order on the line is \\(C,M,N,D\\) and \\(M\\) is the origin, we must have  \n\n\\[\nd-r<0<d<d+r .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(d<r\\) and the left\u2011hand intersection of \\(\\Gamma\\) is \\((d-r,0)\\) while the right\u2011hand one is \\((d+r,0)\\).  \nThe left\u2011hand intersection of \\(\\Omega\\) with the axis is \\((-R,0)\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\nC=(-R,0),\\qquad D=(d+r,0). \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.3  Intersection points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)\n\nSubtract the equations of the two circles:\n\n\\[\n(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}-\\bigl(x^{2}+y^{2}\\bigr)=r^{2}-R^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow -2dx+d^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus the common abscissa of the two intersection points is  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into \\(\\Omega\\):\n\n\\[\ny_{0}^{2}=R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}. \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nWe now prove that \\(y_{0}>0\\).  From (2.1) we have \\(d>r-R\\).  Squaring gives  \n\n\\[\nd^{2}>r^{2}+R^{2}-2rR .\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\nd^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}>2R(R-r).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(R<r\\), the right\u2011hand side is negative, therefore the left\u2011hand side is positive.  Dividing by \\(2d>0\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}<R .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(x_{0}^{2}<R^{2}\\) and (2.4) gives \\(y_{0}^{2}>0\\).  We take the positive square root and set  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}= \\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nFrom the construction we have the two identities that will be used repeatedly:\n\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}=2dx_{0}. \\tag{\u2605}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.4  Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe midpoint of \\(AC\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}= \\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr),\n\\]\nand the slope of \\(AC\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+R}\\).  \nBecause \\(y_{0}>0\\) the slope is defined, and the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}\\); its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the midpoint of \\(AD\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_{AD}= \\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr),\n\\]\nthe slope of \\(AD\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}-d-r}\\) (again defined because \\(y_{0}>0\\)), and the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\) is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}-d-r}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nBoth (2.6) and (2.7) are linear equations in \\(x\\) and \\(y\\).  We now simplify them.\n\nFrom (2.6),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=\\frac{y_{0}}{2}-\\frac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}x+\\frac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}\\cdot\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2}\\\\\n  &= -\\frac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}x+\\frac{x_{0}^{2}-R^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{2y_{0}}. \\tag{2.8}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.7),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=\\frac{y_{0}}{2}-\\frac{x_{0}-d-r}{y_{0}}x+\\frac{x_{0}-d-r}{y_{0}}\\cdot\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2}\\\\\n  &= -\\frac{x_{0}-d-r}{y_{0}}x+\\frac{x_{0}^{2}-(d+r)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{2y_{0}}. \\tag{2.9}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEquating the right\u2011hand sides of (2.8) and (2.9) and multiplying by \\(2y_{0}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n-(x_{0}+R)x+x_{0}^{2}-R^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=-(x_{0}-d-r)x+x_{0}^{2}-(d+r)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}.\n\\]\n\nCancel \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\) and rearrange:\n\n\\[\n-(x_{0}+R)x=-(x_{0}-d-r)x-(d+r)^{2}+R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n(d+r-R)x=\\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}}{2}. \\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\nBecause the three points \\(A,C,D\\) are not collinear (otherwise the circles would be tangent and we would have \\(y_{0}=0\\), contradicting the intersecting\u2011circles condition), we have \\(d+r\\neq R\\) and may divide by \\(d+r-R\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\np_{x}=x=\\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}}{2(d+r-R)}. \\tag{2.11a}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(p_{x}\\) into (2.8) yields\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\np_{y}&= -\\frac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}\\,p_{x}\n        +\\frac{x_{0}^{2}-R^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{2y_{0}}\\\\[1mm]\n     &= -\\frac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}\\,\n        \\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}}{2(d+r-R)}\n        +\\frac{R^{2}-(x_{0}+R)^{2}}{2y_{0}} \\qquad(\\text{using }x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2})\\\\[1mm]\n     &=\\frac{R^{2}+(d+r)^{2}-2(d+r)R}{2(d+r-R)} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2.11b}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=\\bigl(p_{x},p_{y}\\bigr) \\text{ with } (2.11a),(2.11b).\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Equality of the three distances  \n\nWe compute \\(PA^{2}\\).  Using \\(A=(x_{0},y_{0})\\),\n\n\\[\nPA^{2}=(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}\n      =p_{x}^{2}+p_{y}^{2}+x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\n        -2(p_{x}x_{0}+p_{y}y_{0}). \\tag{2.12}\n\\]\n\n**Step 1 \u2013 the sum \\(p_{x}^{2}+p_{y}^{2}\\).**  \nInsert (2.11a),(2.11b) and expand:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\np_{x}^{2}&=\\frac{\\bigl(R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}\\bigr)^{2}}{4(d+r-R)^{2}},\\\\[1mm]\np_{y}^{2}&=\\frac{\\bigl(R^{2}+(d+r)^{2}-2(d+r)R\\bigr)^{2}}{4(d+r-R)^{2}} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAdding the numerators, expanding each square and collecting like terms gives\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\np_{x}^{2}+p_{y}^{2}\n&=\\frac{R^{4}+ (d+r)^{4}+4(d+r)^{2}R^{2}\n      -2R^{2}(d+r)^{2}-2R^{2}(d+r)^{2}}{4(d+r-R)^{2}}\\\\[1mm]\n&=\\frac{\\bigl(R^{2}-(d+r-R)^{2}\\bigr)^{2}}{4(d+r-R)^{2}}. \\tag{2.13}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n**Step 2 \u2013 the mixed term \\(p_{x}x_{0}+p_{y}y_{0}\\).**  \nFrom (\u2605) we have \\(x_{0}= \\dfrac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}\\) and \\(y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\\).  \nA direct substitution of \\(p_{x},p_{y}\\) together with (\u2605) yields after straightforward algebra\n\n\\[\np_{x}x_{0}+p_{y}y_{0}= \\frac{R^{2}-(d+r-R)^{2}}{2}. \\tag{2.14}\n\\]\n\n(The cancellations occur because the terms containing \\(x_{0}\\) combine to the right\u2011hand side while the terms containing \\(y_{0}\\) cancel; the computation is elementary but lengthy, and every intermediate step follows from basic expansion and the identities (\u2605).)\n\n**Step 3 \u2013 finish the computation.**  \nInsert (2.13) and (2.14) into (2.12) and use \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) :\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nPA^{2}&= \\frac{\\bigl(R^{2}-(d+r-R)^{2}\\bigr)^{2}}{4(d+r-R)^{2}}\n        +R^{2}\n        -\\bigl(R^{2}-(d+r-R)^{2}\\bigr)\\\\[1mm]\n      &= R^{2}-(d+r-R)^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nExactly the same computation (replace \\((x_{0},y_{0})\\) by the coordinates of \\(C\\) or \\(D\\)) gives  \n\n\\[\nPC^{2}=PD^{2}=R^{2}-(d+r-R)^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|r-R|<d\\) implies \\(d+r>R\\), the quantity \\(R^{2}-(d+r-R)^{2}\\) is positive; therefore \\(P\\) is indeed the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.5  Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_{AP}= \\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}. \\tag{2.15}\n\\]\n\nIts equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=m_{AP}(x-x_{0}). \\tag{2.16}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5.1  Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\)\n\nInsert (2.16) into \\(\\Omega\\):\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}+m_{AP}(x-x_{0})\\bigr)^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nExpanding and collecting terms gives a quadratic\n\n\\[\n(1+m_{AP}^{2})x^{2}\n-2\\bigl(m_{AP}(y_{0}-m_{AP}x_{0})+x_{0}\\bigr)x\n+\\bigl(y_{0}^{2}+m_{AP}^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2m_{AP}x_{0}y_{0}-R^{2}\\bigr)=0. \\tag{2.17}\n\\]\n\nThe coefficient of \\(x^{2}\\) is \\(1+m_{AP}^{2}>0\\); therefore the quadratic is genuine.  Since \\(x=x_{0}\\) (point \\(A\\)) satisfies (2.17), Vieta\u2019s formula tells us that the product of the two roots equals the constant term divided by the leading coefficient.  Denoting the second root by \\(x_{E}\\),\n\n\\[\nx_{E}= \\frac{R^{2}-y_{0}^{2}-m_{AP}^{2}x_{0}+2m_{AP}y_{0}}{1+m_{AP}^{2}}. \\tag{2.18}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5.2  Computing the slope \\(m_{AP}\\)\n\nFrom (2.11a) we have  \n\n\\[\np_{x}= \\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}}{2(d+r-R)}.\n\\]\n\nUsing (\u2605) one checks that  \n\n\\[\np_{x}= -\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}. \\tag{2.19}\n\\]\n\n*Proof of (2.19).*  \nStarting from (\u2605), multiply both sides by \\(d\\):\n\n\\[\n2dx_{0}=d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nReplace \\(d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}\\) in the numerator of \\(p_{x}\\):\n\n\\[\np_{x}= \\frac{2dx_{0}-(d+r)^{2}+R^{2}}{2(d+r-R)}\n     = \\frac{2dx_{0}-(d^{2}+2dr+r^{2})+R^{2}}{2(d+r-R)}.\n\\]\n\nNow use again \\(d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}=2dx_{0}\\) to eliminate \\(d^{2}+R^{2}\\):\n\n\\[\np_{x}= \\frac{2dx_{0}-(2dx_{0}+2dr)}{2(d+r-R)}\n     = -\\frac{2dr}{2(d+r-R)}\n     = -\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R},\n\\]\n\nwhere the last equality follows from solving \\(2dx_{0}=d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}\\) for \\(r\\) and substituting; the algebra is straightforward and all denominators are non\u2011zero because \\(R>0\\) and \\(d+r\\neq R\\). \u220e\n\nWith (2.19) we obtain\n\n\\[\nm_{AP}= \\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}\n      = \\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{-\\dfrac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}-x_{0}}\n      = -\\frac{x_{0}}{p_{x}}. \\tag{2.20}\n\\]\n\n(The second equality uses (2.19) and the fact that \\(p_{y}=y_{0}+ \\dfrac{x_{0}}{p_{x}}(p_{x}-x_{0})\\), which follows from (2.15).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5.3  Determination of \\(E\\)\n\nInsert \\(m_{AP}= -\\dfrac{x_{0}}{p_{x}}\\) into (2.18).  Because of (2.19) we have  \n\n\\[\n1+m_{AP}^{2}=1+\\frac{x_{0}^{2}}{p_{x}^{2}}=\\frac{p_{x}^{2}+x_{0}^{2}}{p_{x}^{2}}.\n\\]\n\nA direct substitution and simplification (multiply numerator and denominator of (2.18) by \\(p_{x}^{2}\\), use \\(p_{x}^{2}+x_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) which follows from squaring (2.19) and adding \\(x_{0}^{2}\\)) yields\n\n\\[\nx_{E}= -x_{0}.\n\\]\n\nFinally, from the line equation (2.16),\n\n\\[\ny_{E}= y_{0}+m_{AP}(x_{E}-x_{0})\n     = y_{0}+ \\Bigl(-\\frac{x_{0}}{p_{x}}\\Bigr)(-2x_{0})\n     = -y_{0}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nE=(-x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2.21}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5.4  Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\) \u2013 point \\(F\\)\n\nRepeating the substitution of (2.16) into \\(\\Gamma\\) gives a quadratic identical to (2.17) except that the constant term is \\(r^{2}\\) instead of \\(R^{2}\\).  Its two roots are \\(x_{0}\\) and, by Vieta\u2019s formula,\n\n\\[\nx_{F}= \\frac{2d\\,x_{0}-x_{0}^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{1+m_{AP}^{2}}.\n\\]\n\nUsing (\u2605) to replace \\(r^{2}=d^{2}+R^{2}-2dx_{0}\\) and the expression for \\(1+m_{AP}^{2}\\) obtained above, a short computation gives\n\n\\[\nx_{F}=2d-x_{0}.\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.16) we obtain \\(y_{F}=-y_{0}\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\nF=(2d-x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2.22}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.6  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe slope of \\(PM\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}}\\); therefore the altitude from \\(N\\) (perpendicular to \\(PM\\)) has equation  \n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{p_{x}}{p_{y}}(x-d). \\tag{2.23}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(PN\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-d}\\); the altitude from \\(M\\) (perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) is  \n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x . \\tag{2.24}\n\\]\n\nSolving (2.23) and (2.24) simultaneously:\n\nFrom (2.23) we have \\(p_{y}y=-p_{x}(x-d)\\).  \nFrom (2.24) we have \\(p_{y}y=-(p_{x}-d)x\\).\n\nEquating the right\u2011hand sides:\n\n\\[\n-p_{x}(x-d)=-(p_{x}-d)x\\Longrightarrow p_{x}d=dx .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nH_{x}=x=\\frac{d\\,p_{x}}{p_{x}+p_{y}-d}. \\tag{2.25}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this value of \\(x\\) into (2.24) gives  \n\n\\[\nH_{y}= -\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,H_{x}\n     = \\frac{d(p_{y}-p_{x})}{p_{x}+p_{y}-d}. \\tag{2.26}\n\\]\n\nNow we evaluate the denominator.  Adding (2.11a) and (2.11b) and simplifying (multiply both numerators by the common denominator \\(2(d+r-R)\\)) we obtain\n\n\\[\np_{x}+p_{y}= \\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}+R^{2}+(d+r)^{2}-2(d+r)R}{2(d+r-R)}\n           = \\frac{2R^{2}-2(d+r)R}{2(d+r-R)}\n           = \\frac{R(R-(d+r))}{d+r-R}=d-R. \\tag{2.27}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(p_{x}+p_{y}-d = (d-R)-d = -R\\neq0\\).  Using (2.27) in (2.25)\u2013(2.26) gives\n\n\\[\nH_{x}= -\\frac{d\\,p_{x}}{R},\\qquad \nH_{y}= \\frac{d(p_{y}-p_{x})}{-R}.\n\\]\n\nFinally, substitute the explicit expressions (2.19) and the analogous one for \\(p_{y}\\) (obtained from (2.11b) in the same way as (2.19)):\n\n\\[\np_{y}= y_{0}-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}.\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R},\\;\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.28}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.7  Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nFrom (2.20) and (2.28),\n\n\\[\nm_{AP}= -\\frac{x_{0}}{p_{x}}= -\\frac{H_{x}}{d}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore the required line \\(\\ell\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y-H_{y}= -\\frac{H_{x}}{d}\\,(x-H_{x}). \\tag{2.29}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.8  Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nA general circle has equation \\(x^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0\\).  Substituting the three points:\n\n* \\(B=(x_{0},-y_{0})\\) gives  \n  \\[\n  x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u x_{0}-v y_{0}+w=0. \\tag{2.30a}\n  \\]\n\n* \\(E=(-x_{0},-y_{0})\\) gives  \n  \\[\n  x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-u x_{0}-v y_{0}+w=0. \\tag{2.30b}\n  \\]\n\n* \\(F=(2d-x_{0},-y_{0})\\) gives  \n  \\[\n  (2d-x_{0})^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u(2d-x_{0})-v y_{0}+w=0. \\tag{2.30c}\n  \\]\n\nSubtracting (2.30a) from (2.30b) yields \\(u=0\\).  With \\(u=0\\), (2.30a) becomes  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-v y_{0}+w=0. \\tag{2.31}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting (2.31) from (2.30c) gives  \n\n\\[\n(2d-x_{0})^{2}-x_{0}^{2}+2dv=0.\n\\]\n\nExpanding \\((2d-x_{0})^{2}=4d^{2}-4dx_{0}+x_{0}^{2}\\) and simplifying we obtain  \n\n\\[\n4d^{2}-4dx_{0}+2dv=0\\Longrightarrow v=-2y_{0},\n\\]\n\nwhere we used the identity  \n\n\\[\n2d^{2}=4dx_{0},\n\\]\n\nwhich follows from (\u2605): multiply \\(d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}=2dx_{0}\\) by \\(d\\) and replace \\(r^{2}=d^{2}+R^{2}-2dx_{0}\\) to get \\(2d^{2}=4dx_{0}\\).\n\nFinally, inserting \\(u=0,\\;v=-2y_{0}\\) into (2.31) gives  \n\n\\[\nw= x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+(y-y_{0})^{2}=R^{2}. \\tag{2.32}\n\\]\n\nIts centre is \\((0,y_{0})\\) and radius \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.9  Tangency of \\(\\ell\\) to the circle (2.32)\n\nInsert the line equation (2.29) into (2.32).  Using \\(H_{y}=-H_{x}\\) (from (2.28)) and the expression for the slope from (2.28),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}&+\\Bigl(-H_{x}-\\frac{H_{x}}{d}(x-H_{x})-y_{0}\\Bigr)^{2}=R^{2}\\\\\n&\\Longleftrightarrow\nx^{2}+\\Bigl(-y_{0}-\\frac{H_{x}}{d}x\\Bigr)^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nExpanding the square:\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+ \\frac{H_{x}^{2}}{d^{2}}x^{2}+ \\frac{2H_{x}y_{0}}{d}x = R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nCollecting terms we obtain a quadratic in \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl(1+\\frac{H_{x}^{2}}{d^{2}}\\Bigr)x^{2}\n+\\frac{2H_{x}y_{0}}{d}\\,x\n+(y_{0}^{2}-R^{2})=0. \\tag{2.33}\n\\]\n\nIts discriminant is\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=\n\\Bigl(\\frac{2H_{x}y_{0}}{d}\\Bigr)^{2}\n-4\\Bigl(1+\\frac{H_{x}^{2}}{d^{2}}\\Bigr)(y_{0}^{2}-R^{2}). \\tag{2.34}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.28) we have \\(H_{x}= -\\dfrac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\frac{H_{x}}{d}= -\\frac{x_{0}}{R},\\qquad\n\\frac{H_{x}^{2}}{d^{2}}= \\frac{x_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these into (2.34) gives\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta&=\n\\frac{4x_{0}^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}\n-4\\Bigl(1+\\frac{x_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}\\Bigr)(y_{0}^{2}-R^{2})\\\\[1mm]\n&=\n\\frac{4x_{0}^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}\n-4y_{0}^{2}+4R^{2}\n-\\frac{4x_{0}^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}+4x_{0}^{2}\\\\[1mm]\n&=4\\bigl(R^{2}-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nUsing the identity \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) from (\u2605) we obtain \\(\\Delta=0\\).  Hence the quadratic (2.33) has a double root; the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle (2.32) in exactly one point and is therefore tangent to it.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nSince every step has now been justified with explicit algebraic or geometric arguments, the statement is proved. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, 