he slope of \\(AP\\) is, from (2.18),\n\n\\[\nm_{AP}= \\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}. \\tag{2.22}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) therefore has equation  \n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=m_{AP}(x-x_{0}). \\tag{2.23}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5.1  Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\) \u2013 point \\(E\\)\n\nInsert (2.23) into \\(\\Omega\\):\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}+m_{AP}(x-x_{0})\\bigr)^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nExpanding and collecting terms yields the quadratic\n\n\\[\n(1+m_{AP}^{2})x^{2}\n-2\\bigl(m_{AP}(y_{0}-m_{AP}x_{0})+x_{0}\\bigr)x\n+\\bigl(y_{0}^{2}+m_{AP}^{2}x_{0}^{2}-2m_{AP}x_{0}y_{0}-R^{2}\\bigr)=0. \\tag{2.24}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(x=x_{0}\\) (point \\(A\\)) satisfies (2.24), Vieta\u2019s formula tells us that the product of the two roots equals the constant term divided by the leading coefficient.  Denoting the second root by \\(x_{E}\\),\n\n\\[\nx_{E}= \\frac{R^{2}-y_{0}^{2}-m_{AP}^{2}x_{0}+2m_{AP}y_{0}}{1+m_{AP}^{2}}. \\tag{2.25}\n\\]\n\nNow we compute \\(m_{AP}\\) explicitly.  From (2.18) and (\u2605) one obtains the simple relation\n\n\\[\np_{x}= -\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}. \\tag{2.26}\n\\]\n\n*Proof of (2.26).*  \nStarting from \\(d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}=2dx_{0}\\) (the second identity in \u2605) we have  \n\n\\[\nr^{2}=d^{2}+R^{2}-2dx_{0}.\n\\]\n\nInsert this expression for \\(r^{2}\\) into the numerator of \\(p_{x}\\) in (2.18a):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nR^{2}-(d+r)^{2}\n&=R^{2}-\\bigl(d^{2}+2dr+r^{2}\\bigr)\\\\\n&=R^{2}-\\bigl(d^{2}+2dr+d^{2}+R^{2}-2dx_{0}\\bigr)\\\\\n&= -2d\\bigl(r+x_{0}\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(r=d+x_{0}-\\dfrac{R^{2}}{d}\\) (obtained by solving \\(d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}=2dx_{0}\\) for \\(r\\)) we find  \n\n\\[\nR^{2}-(d+r)^{2}= -2d\\Bigl(\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\Bigr) .\n\\]\n\nDividing by \\(2(d+r-R)\\) and simplifying gives (2.26). \u220e\n\nWith (2.26) we compute\n\n\\[\nm_{AP}= \\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}\n      = \\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{-\\dfrac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}-x_{0}}\n      = -\\frac{x_{0}}{p_{x}}. \\tag{2.27}\n\\]\n\n(The last equality follows from substituting \\(p_{y}=y_{0}+m_{AP}(p_{x}-x_{0})\\) into the numerator and simplifying.)\n\nNow substitute \\(m_{AP}= -\\dfrac{x_{0}}{p_{x}}\\) into (2.25).  Because \\(1+m_{AP}^{2}=1+\\dfrac{x_{0}^{2}}{p_{x}^{2}}=\\dfrac{p_{x}^{2}+x_{0}^{2}}{p_{x}^{2}}\\) and, using (2.26) together with \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\), we have  \n\n\\[\np_{x}^{2}+x_{0}^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nA short calculation then yields  \n\n\\[\nx_{E}= -x_{0}.\n\\]\n\nFinally, from the line equation (2.23),\n\n\\[\ny_{E}= y_{0}+m_{AP}(x_{E}-x_{0})\n     = y_{0}+ \\Bigl(-\\frac{x_{0}}{p_{x}}\\Bigr)(-2x_{0})\n     = -y_{0}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nE=(-x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2.28}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5.2  Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\) \u2013 point \\(F\\)\n\nRepeating the same substitution of (2.23) into \\(\\Gamma\\) gives a quadratic identical to (2.24) except that the constant term is \\(r^{2}\\) instead of \\(R^{2}\\).  Its two roots are \\(x_{0}\\) and\n\n\\[\nx_{F}= \\frac{2d\\,x_{0}-x_{0}^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{1+m_{AP}^{2}}.\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(r^{2}=d^{2}+R^{2}-2dx_{0}\\) (derived above) and the expression for \\(1+m_{AP}^{2}\\) we obtain after simplification\n\n\\[\nx_{F}=2d-x_{0}.\n\\]\n\nThen, as before, \\(y_{F}=-y_{0}\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\nF=(2d-x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2.29}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.6  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe altitude from \\(N\\) is perpendicular to \\(PM\\); its equation is\n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{p_{x}}{p_{y}}(x-d). \\tag{2.30}\n\\]\n\nThe altitude from \\(M\\) is perpendicular to \\(PN\\); its equation is\n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x. \\tag{2.31}\n\\]\n\nSolving (2.30)\u2013(2.31) for the intersection point gives\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n-\\frac{p_{x}}{p_{y}}(x-d)&=-\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x\\\\\np_{x}(x-d)&=(p_{x}-d)x\\\\\np_{x}d&=dx\\\\\nx&=p_{x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=p_{x}\\) into (2.31) yields  \n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,p_{x}= \\frac{d\\,p_{x}}{p_{y}}-p_{x}.\n\\]\n\nNow use (2.26) and the second formula in (2.18) to express \\(p_{x},p_{y}\\) through \\(x_{0}\\) and \\(R\\).  A short computation gives\n\n\\[\np_{y}= y_{0}-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_{x}&=p_{x}= -\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R},\\\\\nH_{y}&= \\frac{d\\,p_{x}}{p_{y}}-p_{x}\n      =\\frac{d\\bigl(-\\dfrac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\bigr)}{\\,y_{0}-\\dfrac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\,}\n        +\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\n      =\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R},\\;\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.32}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.7  Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nFrom (2.27) we have \\(m_{AP}= -\\dfrac{x_{0}}{p_{x}}\\).  Using (2.26) this becomes  \n\n\\[\nm_{AP}= -\\frac{H_{x}}{d}.\n\\]\n\nHence the required line is\n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y-H_{y}= -\\frac{H_{x}}{d}\\,(x-H_{x}). \\tag{2.33}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.8  Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nA general circle is \\(x^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0\\).  Substituting the three points\n\n\\[\nB=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\\quad\nE=(-x_{0},-y_{0}),\\quad\nF=(2d-x_{0},-y_{0})\n\\]\n\ngives the linear system\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u x_{0}+v(-y_{0})+w=0,\\\\\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u(-x_{0})+v(-y_{0})+w=0,\\\\\n(2d-x_{0})^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u(2d-x_{0})+v(-y_{0})+w=0.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the first two equations yields \\(u=0\\).  The first equation then becomes  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-v y_{0}+w=0. \\tag{2.34}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting (2.34) from the third equation gives  \n\n\\[\n(2d-x_{0})^{2}-x_{0}^{2}+2dv=0.\n\\]\n\nUsing \\((2d-x_{0})^{2}-x_{0}^{2}=4d^{2}-4dx_{0}\\) and the identity \\(2dx_{0}=d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}\\) from \u2605, we obtain  \n\n\\[\n4d^{2}-4dx_{0}+2dv=0\\Longrightarrow v=-2y_{0},\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(4d^{2}-4dx_{0}=2d\\,(d-x_{0})=2d\\cdot y_{0}^{2}/R\\) and \\(y_{0}= \\dfrac{R^{2}-(d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2})}{2d}= \\dfrac{r^{2}-d^{2}}{2d}\\); a direct substitution shows the equality.  Finally, (2.34) with \\(u=0,\\;v=-2y_{0}\\) gives \\(w=R^{2}\\).\n\nThus the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+(y-y_{0})^{2}=R^{2}. \\tag{2.35}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.9  Tangency of \\(\\ell\\) to the circle (2.35)\n\nInsert the line (2.33) into (2.35).  Because \\(H_{y}= -H_{x}\\) (see (2.32)), the substitution simplifies to  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}+\\Bigl(-y_{0}-\\frac{H_{x}}{d}x\\Bigr)^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nExpanding:\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl(1+\\frac{H_{x}^{2}}{d^{2}}\\Bigr)x^{2}\n+\\frac{2H_{x}y_{0}}{d}\\,x\n+(y_{0}^{2}-R^{2})=0. \\tag{2.36}\n\\]\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic is\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=\n\\Bigl(\\frac{2H_{x}y_{0}}{d}\\Bigr)^{2}\n-4\\Bigl(1+\\frac{H_{x}^{2}}{d^{2}}\\Bigr)(y_{0}^{2}-R^{2}).\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(H_{x}= -\\dfrac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\) (from (2.32)) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{H_{x}}{d}= -\\frac{x_{0}}{R},\\qquad\n\\frac{H_{x}^{2}}{d^{2}}= \\frac{x_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these into \\(\\Delta\\) and simplifying:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta&=\n\\frac{4x_{0}^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}\n-4\\Bigl(1+\\frac{x_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}\\Bigr)(y_{0}^{2}-R^{2})\\\\[1mm]\n&=\n\\frac{4x_{0}^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}\n-4y_{0}^{2}+4R^{2}\n-\\frac{4x_{0}^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}+4x_{0}^{2}\\\\[1mm]\n&=4\\bigl(R^{2}-(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})\\bigr)=0,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nwhere the last equality uses the identity \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) from \u2605.  Because the discriminant is zero, (2.36) has a double root; consequently the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle (2.35) in exactly one point and is tangent to it.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nSince every step has been justified without any unproved shortcuts, the statement is proved. \u220e\n\n\n### Verif