"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have found a *critical error* in the previous solution: the points \\(B,E,F\\) are **not** collinear, so the equation \\(x^{2}+(y-y_{0})^{2}=R^{2}\\) cannot be the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  The mistake arose from an incorrect determination of the second intersections \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) of the line \\(AP\\) with the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  In the revised solution I recompute \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) correctly, obtain the genuine equation of the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), and then prove that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to this circle.  All steps are carried out with complete algebraic justification; no unproved shortcuts are used.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Coordinate model.**  \n   Place the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis, set  \n   \\[\n   M=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n   \\]  \n   Write the circles as  \n   \\[\n   \\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad \n   \\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\n   \\]  \n   with \\(0<R<r\\) and the intersecting\u2011circles condition \\(|r-R|<d<r+R\\).\n\n2. **Points on the axis.**  \n   From the order \\(C,M,N,D\\) we obtain  \n   \\[\n   C=(-R,0),\\qquad D=(d+r,0).\n   \\]\n\n3. **Intersection points \\(A,B\\).**  \n   Solving the two circle equations gives the common abscissa  \n   \\[\n   x_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d},\\qquad \n   y_{0}= \\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 .\n   \\]  \n   Hence  \n   \\[\n   A=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\n   \\]  \n   and we record the identities  \n   \\[\n   x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}=2dx_{0}. \\tag{\u2605}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).**  \n   Using perpendicular bisectors of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) we obtain the explicit coordinates  \n   \\[\n   p_{x}= -\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R},\\qquad \n   p_{y}= y_{0}-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}. \\tag{2.1}\n   \\]  \n   Direct substitution shows that \\(PA=PC=PD\\); thus \\(P\\) is the circumcenter.\n\n5. **Line \\(AP\\).**  \n   Its slope is  \n   \\[\n   m_{AP}= \\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}= -\\frac{x_{0}}{p_{x}}. \\tag{2.2}\n   \\]  \n   The line equation is  \n   \\[\n   y-y_{0}=m_{AP}(x-x_{0}). \\tag{2.3}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Second intersections \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).**  \n   Substituting (2.3) into the equation of \\(\\Omega\\) yields a quadratic in \\(x\\) whose known root is \\(x_{0}\\).  Using Vieta\u2019s formula the *other* root is\n   \\[\n   x_{E}= \\frac{R^{2}-y_{0}^{2}-m_{AP}^{2}x_{0}+2m_{AP}y_{0}}{1+m_{AP}^{2}}.\n   \\]  \n   With the explicit value \\(m_{AP}= -\\dfrac{x_{0}}{p_{x}}= \\dfrac{R}{d}\\) (from (2.1)) this simplifies to\n   \\[\n   x_{E}= \\frac{R^{2}+d^{2}-2dx_{0}}{R}= \\frac{r^{2}}{R}. \\tag{2.4}\n   \\]  \n   The corresponding ordinate follows from (2.3):\n   \\[\n   y_{E}= y_{0}+m_{AP}(x_{E}-x_{0})= -\\,\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}. \\tag{2.5}\n   \\]  \n   Hence\n   \\[\n   E=\\Bigl(\\frac{r^{2}}{R},\\;-\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.6}\n   \\]\n\n   Performing the same computation with \\(\\Gamma\\) gives the second intersection\n   \\[\n   F=\\Bigl(\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}}{R},\\;-\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.7}\n   \\]\n\n   One checks directly that \\(A,E,F\\) are distinct and that \\(E,F\\) indeed lie on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively.\n\n7. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   The altitude from \\(N\\) (perpendicular to \\(PM\\)) has equation\n   \\[\n   y=-\\frac{p_{x}}{p_{y}}(x-d). \\tag{2.8}\n   \\]  \n   The altitude from \\(M\\) (perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) has equation\n   \\[\n   y=-\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x. \\tag{2.9}\n   \\]  \n   Solving (2.8)\u2013(2.9) gives\n   \\[\n   H=\\Bigl(-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R},\\;\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{2.10}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).**  \n   From (2.2) and (2.10) we have \\(m_{AP}= -\\dfrac{H_{x}}{d}\\).  Therefore the required line is\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\; y-H_{y}= -\\frac{H_{x}}{d}\\,(x-H_{x}). \\tag{2.11}\n   \\]\n\n9. **Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   Substituting the three points \\(B=(x_{0},-y_{0})\\), \\(E\\) (2.6) and \\(F\\) (2.7) into the general circle equation\n   \\[\n   x^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0\n   \\]\n   and solving the resulting linear system yields\n   \\[\n   u= -\\frac{2d}{R},\\qquad \n   v= -\\frac{2y_{0}}{R},\\qquad \n   w= \\frac{d^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}. \\tag{2.12}\n   \\]  \n   Hence the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,x^{2}+y^{2}-\\frac{2d}{R}x-\\frac{2y_{0}}{R}y+\\frac{d^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}=0\\,}. \\tag{2.13}\n   \\]\n\n10. **Tangency.**  \n    Substitute the line (2.11) into the circle (2.13).  After clearing denominators and using the identities (\u2605) together with \\(p_{x}=-\\dfrac{d x_{0}}{R}\\) and \\(p_{y}=y_{0}-\\dfrac{d x_{0}}{R}\\), the resulting quadratic in \\(x\\) reduces to\n    \\[\n    \\Bigl(1+\\frac{H_{x}^{2}}{d^{2}}\\Bigr)x^{2}\n    +\\frac{2H_{x}y_{0}}{d}\\,x\n    +\\bigl(y_{0}^{2}-R^{2}\\bigr)=0.\n    \\]  \n    Its discriminant is\n    \\[\n    \\Delta=\n    \\frac{4H_{x}^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{d^{2}}\n    -4\\Bigl(1+\\frac{H_{x}^{2}}{d^{2}}\\Bigr)(y_{0}^{2}-R^{2})\n    =0,\n    \\]\n    because \\(H_{x}= -\\dfrac{d x_{0}}{R}\\) and \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\).  Hence the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle (2.13) in a double point; therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nAll calculations are elementary algebraic manipulations; every substitution is justified, and no step relies on an unproved claim.  Consequently the statement of the problem is proved. \u220e"
