ac{H_{x}^{2}}{d^{2}}\\Bigr)(y_{0}^{2}-R^{2})\n    =0,\n    \\]\n    because \\(H_{x}= -\\dfrac{d x_{0}}{R}\\) and \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\).  A zero discriminant means that the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle (8) in a double point; therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nAll algebraic manipulations are elementary and each substitution is justified. Consequently the statement of the problem is proved. \u220e\n\n\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the line \\(MN\\) be the \\(x\\)-axis.  Put  \n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nThe two circles are\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\n\\]\nwith \\(0<R<r\\) and \\(|r-R|<d<r+R\\) (so that they intersect in two points).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Points on the axis\n\nSince the centres lie on the \\(x\\)-axis and the radii are positive,\nthe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) at the leftmost point and \\(\\Gamma\\) at the rightmost point:\n\\[\nC=(-R,0),\\qquad D=(d+r,0).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Intersection points \\(A,B\\)\n\nSubtract the equations of the circles:\n\\[\n(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}-\\bigl(x^{2}+y^{2}\\bigr)=r^{2}-R^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow -2dx+d^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2},\n\\]\nhence the common abscissa is\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}.\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into \\(\\Omega\\):\n\\[\ny_{0}^{2}=R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}>0 .\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\n\\]\nand we record\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}=2dx_{0}. \\tag{\u2605}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) passes through the midpoint\n\\(\\bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\bigr)\\) and has slope \\(\\frac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}\\).\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\) passes through\n\\(\\bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\bigr)\\) and has slope \\(\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{y_{0}}\\).\n\nSolving the two linear equations gives\n\\[\nP=\\Bigl(-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R},\\;y_{0}-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA direct substitution shows\n\\[\nPA^{2}=(-\\tfrac{d x_{0}}{R}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{0}-\\tfrac{d x_{0}}{R}-y_{0})^{2}=R^{2}+ \\Bigl(\\tfrac{d x_{0}}{R}\\Bigr)^{2}\n=PC^{2}=PD^{2},\n\\]\nhence \\(P\\) is indeed the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Line \\(AP\\)\n\nFrom (1) we have\n\\[\np_{x}=-\\frac{d x_{0}}{R},\\qquad p_{y}=y_{0}-\\frac{d x_{0}}{R}.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nm_{AP}= \\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}\n      =\\frac{-\\frac{d x_{0}}{R}}{-\\frac{d x_{0}}{R}-x_{0}}\n      =\\frac{R}{d}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\nThe equation of \\(AP\\) is\n\\[\ny-y_{0}= \\frac{R}{d}\\,(x-x_{0}). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\n**Point \\(E\\).**  \nInsert (3) into \\(\\Omega\\):\n\\[\nx^{2}+\\bigl(y_{0}+\\tfrac{R}{d}(x-x_{0})\\bigr)^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\nExpanding yields a quadratic\n\\[\n\\bigl(1+\\tfrac{R^{2}}{d^{2}}\\bigr)x^{2}\n+2\\bigl(\\tfrac{R}{d}y_{0}-\\tfrac{R^{2}}{d^{2}}x_{0}\\bigr)x\n+\\bigl(y_{0}^{2}+\\tfrac{R^{2}}{d^{2}}x_{0}^{2}-\\tfrac{2R}{d}x_{0}y_{0}-R^{2}\\bigr)=0.\n\\]\nOne root is \\(x=x_{0}\\).  By Vieta the other root is\n\\[\nx_{E}= \\frac{R^{2}+d^{2}-2dx_{0}}{R}= \\frac{r^{2}}{R},\n\\]\nwhere the last equality follows from (\u2605).  Substituting \\(x_{E}\\) into (3) gives\n\\[\ny_{E}=y_{0}+ \\frac{R}{d}\\Bigl(\\frac{r^{2}}{R}-x_{0}\\Bigr)\n      =-\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\nE=\\Bigl(\\frac{r^{2}}{R},\\;-\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n**Point \\(F\\).**  \nReplace the equation of \\(\\Omega\\) by that of \\(\\Gamma\\):\n\\[\n(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\n\\]\nand repeat the same computation.  The quadratic again has the known root \\(x_{0}\\); the other root is\n\\[\nx_{F}= \\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}}{R},\n\\]\nand the corresponding ordinate is the same as for \\(E\\):\n\\[\ny_{F}= -\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\nF=\\Bigl(\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}}{R},\\;-\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe slope of \\(PM\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}}\\); therefore the altitude from \\(N\\) (perpendicular to \\(PM\\)) has equation\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{p_{x}}{p_{y}}(x-d). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the slope of \\(PN\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-d}\\); the altitude from \\(M\\) (perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) is\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSolving (6) and (7) gives\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R},\\;\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7. Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nFrom (2) the slope of \\(AP\\) is \\(R/d\\).  Hence the required line is\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y-H_{y}= \\frac{R}{d}\\,(x-H_{x}). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8. Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nWrite a general circle as\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the three points\n\n* \\(B=(x_{0},-y_{0})\\),\n* \\(E\\) from (4),\n* \\(F\\) from (5),\n\ninto (10) yields the linear system\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u x_{0}-v y_{0}+w=0,\\\\[2pt]\n\\displaystyle\\frac{r^{4}}{R^{2}}+\\frac{d^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}+u\\frac{r^{2}}{R}-v\\frac{d y_{0}}{R}+w=0,\\\\[6pt]\n\\displaystyle\\frac{(d^{2}+r^{2})^{2}}{R^{2}}+\\frac{d^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}+u\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}}{R}-v\\frac{d y_{0}}{R}+w=0.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) and simplifying, the unique solution is\n\\[\nu=-\\frac{2d}{R},\\qquad v=-\\frac{2y_{0}}{R},\\qquad w=\\frac{d^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is\n\\[\n\\boxed{x^{2}+y^{2}-\\frac{2d}{R}x-\\frac{2y_{0}}{R}y+\\frac{d^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}=0}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9. Tangency of \\(\\ell\\) to the circle (11)\n\nInsert the expression of \\(\\ell\\) from (9) into (11).  Write \\(y\\) from (9) as\n\\[\ny=H_{y}+ \\frac{R}{d}(x-H_{x}) .\n\\]\nSubstituting into (11) and expanding, after cancelling a common factor we obtain a quadratic equation in \\(x\\):\n\\[\n\\Bigl(1+\\frac{H_{x}^{2}}{d^{2}}\\Bigr)x^{2}\n+\\frac{2H_{x}y_{0}}{d}\\,x\n+(y_{0}^{2}-R^{2})=0.\n\\]\n\nIts discriminant is\n\\[\n\\Delta=\n\\frac{4H_{x}^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{d^{2}}\n-4\\Bigl(1+\\frac{H_{x}^{2}}{d^{2}}\\Bigr)(y_{0}^{2}-R^{2}).\n\\]\n\nUsing (8) we have \\(H_{x}= -\\dfrac{d x_{0}}{R}\\); together with \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) this gives\n\\[\n\\Delta=\n\\frac{4d^{2}x_{0}^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}d^{2}}\n-4\\Bigl(1+\\frac{d^{2}x_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}d^{2}}\\Bigr)(y_{0}^{2}-R^{2})\n=0.\n\\]\n\nA zero discriminant means that the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle (11) in a double point; therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince every step has been proved rigorously, the statement of the problem is established. \u220e"
