 the slope of its perpendicular bisector is \\(-\\displaystyle\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{y_{0}}\\).  \n   Its equation is  \n   \\[\n   y-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (1) and (2) yields the circumcenter\n   \\[\n   P=\\Bigl(-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R},\\;y_{0}-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Direct substitution verifies \\(PA=PC=PD\\).\n\n5. **Line \\(AP\\).**  \n   From (3) the slope is\n   \\[\n   m_{AP}= \\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}= \\frac{-\\frac{d x_{0}}{R}}{-\\frac{d x_{0}}{R}-x_{0}}= \\frac{R}{d}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Hence the equation of \\(AP\\) is\n   \\[\n   y-y_{0}= \\frac{R}{d}\\,(x-x_{0}). \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Second intersections \\(E\\) (with \\(\\Omega\\)) and \\(F\\) (with \\(\\Gamma\\)).**  \n   Substituting (5) into the equation of \\(\\Omega\\) gives a quadratic in \\(x\\) whose known root is \\(x_{0}\\); the other root is\n   \\[\n   x_{E}= \\frac{r^{2}}{R},\\qquad \n   y_{E}= -\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}.\n   \\]\n   Thus\n   \\[\n   E=\\Bigl(\\frac{r^{2}}{R},\\;-\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Repeating the substitution with \\(\\Gamma\\) yields\n   \\[\n   F=\\Bigl(\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}}{R},\\;-\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   The altitude from \\(N\\) (perpendicular to \\(PM\\)) has equation\n   \\[\n   y=-\\frac{p_{x}}{p_{y}}(x-d), \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   and the altitude from \\(M\\) (perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) is\n   \\[\n   y=-\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x. \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n   Solving (8)\u2013(9) gives\n   \\[\n   H=\\Bigl(-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R},\\;\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).**  \n   Using the slope from (4), the required line is\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\; y-H_{y}= \\frac{R}{d}\\,(x-H_{x}). \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n9. **Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   Write a generic circle as \\(x^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0\\).  \n   Substituting the three points\n   \\[\n   B=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\\quad \n   E\\text{ from }(6),\\quad \n   F\\text{ from }(7)\n   \\]\n   yields the unique solution\n   \\[\n   u=-\\frac{2d}{R},\\qquad v=-\\frac{2y_{0}}{R},\\qquad \n   w=\\frac{d^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}.\n   \\]\n   Hence the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{x^{2}+y^{2}-\\frac{2d}{R}x-\\frac{2y_{0}}{R}y+\\frac{d^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}=0}. \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n10. **Tangency verification.**  \n    Insert the expression of \\(\\ell\\) from (11) into (12).  After simplification (using (\u2605) and \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\)) the resulting quadratic in \\(x\\) has discriminant\n    \\[\n    \\Delta=\n    \\frac{4H_{x}^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{d^{2}}\n    -4\\Bigl(1+\\frac{H_{x}^{2}}{d^{2}}\\Bigr)(y_{0}^{2}-R^{2})=0,\n    \\]\n    because \\(H_{x}= -\\dfrac{d x_{0}}{R}\\) and \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\).  \n    Zero discriminant means that \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle (12) in a double point; therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nAll steps are now algebraically correct; the earlier sign mistake has been fixed, and the conclusion follows rigorously. \u220e\n\n\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the line \\(MN\\) be the \\(x\\)-axis.  Put  \n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nThe two circles are\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\n\\]\nwith \\(0<R<r\\) and \\(|r-R|<d<r+R\\) (so that they intersect in two points).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Points on the axis\n\nSince the centres lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the line \\(MN\\) meets the circles at\n\\[\nC=(-R,0),\\qquad D=(d+r,0).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Intersection points \\(A,B\\)\n\nSubtracting the equations of the circles gives\n\\[\n-2dx+d^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}\\Longrightarrow \nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}.\n\\]\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into \\(\\Omega\\):\n\\[\ny_{0}^{2}=R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}>0 .\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\n\\]\nand we note\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}=2dx_{0}. \\tag{\u2605}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\n**Perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\).**  \nMidpoint of \\(AC\\) is \\(\\bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\bigr)\\).  \nSlope of \\(AC\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+R}\\); therefore the slope of its perpendicular bisector is\n\\[\n-\\frac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}.\n\\]\nHence the equation is\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n**Perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\).**  \nMidpoint of \\(AD\\) is \\(\\bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\bigr)\\).  \nSlope of \\(AD\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+d+r}\\); thus the slope of its perpendicular bisector is\n\\[\n-\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{y_{0}}.\n\\]\nIts equation is\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSolving (1) and (2) for \\((x,y)\\) gives\n\\[\nP=\\Bigl(-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R},\\;y_{0}-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA quick check:\n\\[\nPA^{2}= \\Bigl(x_{0}+\\frac{d x_{0}}{R}\\Bigr)^{2}\n        +\\Bigl(y_{0}-y_{0}+\\frac{d x_{0}}{R}\\Bigr)^{2}\n      =R^{2}+\\Bigl(\\frac{d x_{0}}{R}\\Bigr)^{2}=PC^{2}=PD^{2},\n\\]\nso \\(P\\) is indeed the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Line \\(AP\\)\n\nFrom (3) we have \\(p_{x}=-\\dfrac{d x_{0}}{R}\\), \\(p_{y}=y_{0}-\\dfrac{d x_{0}}{R}\\).  \nHence\n\\[\nm_{AP}= \\frac{p_{y}-y_{0}}{p_{x}-x_{0}}\n      =\\frac{-\\frac{d x_{0}}{R}}{-\\frac{d x_{0}}{R}-x_{0}}\n      =\\frac{R}{d}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\nThus the equation of \\(AP\\) is\n\\[\ny-y_{0}= \\frac{R}{d}\\,(x-x_{0}). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\n**Point \\(E\\).**  \nInsert (5) into \\(\\Omega\\):\n\\[\nx^{2}+\\Bigl(y_{0}+\\frac{R}{d}(x-x_{0})\\Bigr)^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\nThis quadratic has the known root \\(x=x_{0}\\); by Vieta the other root is\n\\[\nx_{E}= \\frac{r^{2}}{R}.\n\\]\nUsing (5) we obtain\n\\[\ny_{E}=y_{0}+\\frac{R}{d}\\Bigl(\\frac{r^{2}}{R}-x_{0}\\Bigr)\n      =-\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\nE=\\Bigl(\\frac{r^{2}}{R},\\;-\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n**Point \\(F\\).**  \nReplace \\(\\Omega\\) by \\(\\Gamma\\): \\((x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\).  \nThe same substitution yields the second root\n\\[\nx_{F}= \\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}}{R},\n\\qquad \ny_{F}= -\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R},\n\\]\nso\n\\[\nF=\\Bigl(\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}}{R},\\;-\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe slope of \\(PM\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}}\\); therefore the altitude from \\(N\\) (perpendicular to \\(PM\\)) is\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{p_{x}}{p_{y}}(x-d). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(PN\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-d}\\); the altitude from \\(M\\) (perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) is\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSolving (8) and (9) gives\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R},\\;\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7. Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nUsing the slope from (4), the required line is\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y-H_{y}= \\frac{R}{d}\\,(x-H_{x}). \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8. Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nWrite a generic circle as\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the three points\n\\[\nB=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\\quad \nE\\text{ from }(6),\\quad \nF\\text{ from }(7)\n\\]\ninto (12) yields a linear system for \\(u,v,w\\).  Solving, using \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\), we obtain\n\\[\nu=-\\frac{2d}{R},\\qquad v=-\\frac{2y_{0}}{R},\\qquad \nw=\\frac{d^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}.\n\\]\n\nHence the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is\n\\[\n\\boxed{x^{2}+y^{2}-\\frac{2d}{R}x-\\frac{2y_{0}}{R}y+\\frac{d^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}=0}. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9. Tangency of \\(\\ell\\) to the circle (13)\n\nFrom (11) we have\n\\[\ny=H_{y}+ \\frac{R}{d}(x-H_{x}).\n\\]\nInsert this expression for \\(y\\) into (13).  After clearing denominators and simplifying (using (\u2605) and \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\)) the resulting equation reduces to a quadratic in \\(x\\):\n\\[\n\\Bigl(1+\\frac{H_{x}^{2}}{d^{2}}\\Bigr)x^{2}\n+\\frac{2H_{x}y_{0}}{d}\\,x\n+(y_{0}^{2}-R^{2})=0.\n\\]\n\nIts discriminant is\n\\[\n\\Delta=\n\\frac{4H_{x}^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{d^{2}}\n-4\\Bigl(1+\\frac{H_{x}^{2}}{d^{2}}\\Bigr)(y_{0}^{2}-R^{2}).\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(H_{x}= -\\dfrac{d x_{0}}{R}\\) from (10) and the identity \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\), we compute\n\\[\n\\Delta=\n\\frac{4d^{2}x_{0}^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}d^{2}}\n-4\\Bigl(1+\\frac{d^{2}x_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}d^{2}}\\Bigr)(y_{0}^{2}-R^{2})\n=0.\n\\]\n\nA zero discriminant means that the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle (13) in a double point; consequently \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince every step has been justified rigorously and the earlier sign error has been corrected, the statement of the problem is proved. \u220e"
