known root is \\(x=x_{0}\\); the other root is  \n   \\[\n   x_{E}= \\frac{R^{2}}{x_{0}}= \\frac{r^{2}}{R}\\quad\\text{(by\u202f(\u2605))},\n   \\qquad \n   y_{E}= -\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}.\n   \\]  \n   Thus  \n   \\[\n   E=\\Bigl(\\frac{r^{2}}{R},\\;-\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *With \\(\\Gamma\\).* Substituting (3) into \\((x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\) gives the second root  \n   \\[\n   x_{F}= \\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}}{R},\n   \\qquad \n   y_{F}= -\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R},\n   \\]  \n   i.e.  \n   \\[\n   F=\\Bigl(\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}}{R},\\;-\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   The slope of \\(PM\\) is \\(-\\dfrac{y_{0}}{x_{0}}\\); therefore the altitude from \\(N\\) (perpendicular to \\(PM\\)) is  \n   \\[\n   y= \\frac{x_{0}}{y_{0}}(x-d). \\tag{6}\n   \\]  \n   The slope of \\(PN\\) is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-d}= \\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R(d+x_{0})}\\); the altitude from \\(M\\) (perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) is  \n   \\[\n   y= -\\frac{R(d+x_{0})}{d\\,y_{0}}\\,x. \\tag{7}\n   \\]  \n   Solving (6) and (7) gives  \n   \\[\n   H=\\Bigl(-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R},\\; \\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).**  \n   Using the slope from (2) we obtain  \n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\; y-H_{y}= \\frac{R}{d}\\,(x-H_{x}). \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n9. **Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   Write a generic circle as \\(x^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0\\).  \n   Substituting the three points  \n   \\[\n   B=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\\qquad \n   E\\text{ from }(4),\\qquad \n   F\\text{ from }(5)\n   \\]\n   yields a linear system whose solution, after using \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) and (\u2605), is  \n   \\[\n   u=-\\frac{2d}{R},\\qquad v=-\\frac{2y_{0}}{R},\\qquad \n   w=\\frac{d^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}.\n   \\]  \n   Hence the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is  \n   \\[\n   \\boxed{x^{2}+y^{2}-\\frac{2d}{R}x-\\frac{2y_{0}}{R}y+\\frac{d^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}=0}. \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n10. **Tangency verification.**  \n    Insert the expression of \\(\\ell\\) from (9) into (10).  After substituting \\(y=H_{y}+ \\frac{R}{d}(x-H_{x})\\) and simplifying with (\u2605) and \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) we obtain a quadratic in \\(x\\):\n    \\[\n    \\Bigl(1+\\frac{H_{x}^{2}}{d^{2}}\\Bigr)x^{2}\n    +\\frac{2H_{x}y_{0}}{d}\\,x\n    +(y_{0}^{2}-R^{2})=0.\n    \\]  \n    Its discriminant is\n    \\[\n    \\Delta=\n    \\frac{4H_{x}^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{d^{2}}\n    -4\\Bigl(1+\\frac{H_{x}^{2}}{d^{2}}\\Bigr)(y_{0}^{2}-R^{2}).\n    \\]  \n    Using \\(H_{x}= -\\dfrac{d x_{0}}{R}\\) from (8) and the identity \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) we compute\n    \\[\n    \\Delta=\n    \\frac{4d^{2}x_{0}^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}d^{2}}\n    -4\\Bigl(1+\\frac{d^{2}x_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}d^{2}}\\Bigr)(y_{0}^{2}-R^{2})\n    =0.\n    \\]  \n    A zero discriminant means that \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle (10) in a double point; therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nAll steps are derived explicitly, no hidden assumptions are used, and the tangency statement follows directly from the vanishing discriminant. \u220e\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the line \\(MN\\) be the \\(x\\)-axis.  \nPut  \n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\;d>0 .\n\\]\n\nThe two circles are  \n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\n\\]\nwith \\(0<R<r\\) and \\(|r-R|<d<r+R\\) so that they intersect.\n\n---\n\n### 2.1 Intersection of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\nSubtracting the equations gives\n\\[\n(x-d)^{2}-x^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}\\Longrightarrow -2dx+d^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2},\n\\]\nhence\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}.\n\\]\nFrom \\(\\Omega\\) we obtain\n\\[\ny_{0}= \\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 .\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\n\\]\nand we note the identities\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}=2dx_{0}. \\tag{\u2605}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.2 Points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\)\n\nSince the centres lie on the \\(x\\)-axis,\n\\[\nC=(-R,0),\\qquad D=(d+r,0).\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.3 Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) passes through the midpoint\n\\[\n\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr)\n\\]\nand has slope \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}\\).  \nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\) passes through\n\\[\n\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr)\n\\]\nand has slope \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{x_{0}-d-r}{y_{0}}\\).\n\nSolving the two linear equations gives\n\\[\nP=\\Bigl(-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R},\\; \\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{1}\n\\]\nA direct check shows\n\\[\nPA^{2}=PC^{2}=PD^{2}= \\frac{d^{2}}{R^{2}}(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})=d^{2},\n\\]\nso (1) is indeed the circumcenter.\n\n---\n\n### 2.4 Equation of line \\(AP\\)\n\nFrom (1) the slope is\n\\[\nm_{AP}= \\frac{\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}-y_{0}}{-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}-x_{0}}\n      =\\frac{R}{d}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\ny-y_{0}= \\frac{R}{d}\\,(x-x_{0}). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.5 Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\n**Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).**  \nInsert (3) into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\):\n\\[\nx^{2}+\\Bigl(y_{0}+ \\frac{R}{d}(x-x_{0})\\Bigr)^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\nThis quadratic has the known root \\(x=x_{0}\\); the other root is\n\\[\nx_{E}= \\frac{R^{2}}{x_{0}}.\n\\]\nUsing (\u2605) we rewrite \\(x_{E}= \\dfrac{r^{2}}{R}\\).  \nThen\n\\[\ny_{E}=y_{0}+ \\frac{R}{d}\\Bigl(\\frac{r^{2}}{R}-x_{0}\\Bigr)\n     =-\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\nE=\\Bigl(\\frac{r^{2}}{R},\\;-\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n**Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).**  \nInsert (3) into \\((x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\):\n\\[\n(x-d)^{2}+\\Bigl(y_{0}+ \\frac{R}{d}(x-x_{0})\\Bigr)^{2}=r^{2}.\n\\]\nAgain one root is \\(x=x_{0}\\); the other is\n\\[\nx_{F}= \\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}}{R},\n\\qquad \ny_{F}= -\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\nF=\\Bigl(\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}}{R},\\;-\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.6 Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe slope of \\(PM\\) is\n\\[\n\\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}}= \\frac{\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}}{-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}}=-\\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}}.\n\\]\nTherefore the altitude from \\(N\\) (perpendicular to \\(PM\\)) has equation\n\\[\ny= \\frac{x_{0}}{y_{0}}(x-d). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(PN\\) is\n\\[\n\\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-d}= \\frac{\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}}{-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}-d}\n                     =\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R(d+x_{0})}.\n\\]\nHence the altitude from \\(M\\) (perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) is\n\\[\ny= -\\frac{R(d+x_{0})}{d\\,y_{0}}\\,x. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSolving (6) and (7) gives\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R},\\; \\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.7 Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nUsing the slope \\(m_{AP}=R/d\\) from (2) we obtain\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y-H_{y}= \\frac{R}{d}\\,(x-H_{x}). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.8 Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nA general circle is \\(x^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0\\).  \nSubstituting the three points\n\n\\[\nB=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\\qquad \nE\\text{ from }(4),\\qquad \nF\\text{ from }(5)\n\\]\n\ngives the linear system\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u x_{0}-v y_{0}+w=0,\\\\[2pt]\n\\dfrac{r^{4}}{R^{2}}+\\dfrac{d^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}+u\\dfrac{r^{2}}{R}-v\\dfrac{d y_{0}}{R}+w=0,\\\\[4pt]\n\\dfrac{(d^{2}+r^{2})^{2}}{R^{2}}+\\dfrac{d^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}+u\\dfrac{d^{2}+r^{2}}{R}-v\\dfrac{d y_{0}}{R}+w=0.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) and (\u2605) to eliminate \\(r^{2}\\) and simplify, we obtain\n\n\\[\nu=-\\frac{2d}{R},\\qquad v=-\\frac{2y_{0}}{R},\\qquad \nw=\\frac{d^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x^{2}+y^{2}-\\frac{2d}{R}x-\\frac{2y_{0}}{R}y+\\frac{d^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}=0}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.9 Tangency of \\(\\ell\\) to the circle (10)\n\nInsert the expression of \\(\\ell\\) from (9) into (10).  \nWrite \\(y=H_{y}+ \\frac{R}{d}(x-H_{x})\\) and substitute into (10).  \nAfter expanding and using (\u2605) together with \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) we obtain a quadratic equation in \\(x\\) of the form\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl(1+\\frac{H_{x}^{2}}{d^{2}}\\Bigr)x^{2}\n+\\frac{2H_{x}y_{0}}{d}\\,x\n+(y_{0}^{2}-R^{2})=0. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe discriminant of (11) is\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=\n\\frac{4H_{x}^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{d^{2}}\n-4\\Bigl(1+\\frac{H_{x}^{2}}{d^{2}}\\Bigr)(y_{0}^{2}-R^{2}).\n\\]\n\nFrom (8) we have \\(H_{x}= -\\dfrac{d x_{0}}{R}\\); inserting this and the identity \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) yields\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=\n\\frac{4d^{2}x_{0}^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}d^{2}}\n-4\\Bigl(1+\\frac{d^{2}x_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}d^{2}}\\Bigr)(y_{0}^{2}-R^{2})\n=0.\n\\]\n\nA zero discriminant means that the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle (10) in a double point; therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\u220e"
