rom \\(\\Omega\\) we obtain  \n> \\[\n> y_{0}= \\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 .\n> \\]  \n> Thus  \n> \\[\n> A=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\n> \\]  \n> and we note the identities  \n> \\[\n> x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}=2dx_{0}. \\tag{\u2605}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Verification:** Algebra is correct; the derived coordinates satisfy both circle equations and the identities (\u2605) follow directly from the definitions.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\)\n\n> \u201cSince the centres lie on the \\(x\\)-axis,  \n> \\[\n> C=(-R,0),\\qquad D=(d+r,0).\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Verification:** Correct. The leftmost point of \\(\\Omega\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis is \\((-R,0)\\) and the rightmost point of \\(\\Gamma\\) is \\((d+r,0)\\). The order \\(C,M,N,D\\) holds because \\(-R<0<d<d+r\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4. Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\n> *Derivation of perpendicular bisectors and solving gives*  \n> \\[\n> P=\\Bigl(-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R},\\; \\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{1}\n> \\]  \n> *A direct check shows*  \n> \\[\n> PA^{2}=PC^{2}=PD^{2}=d^{2},\n> \\]  \n> *so (1) is indeed the circumcenter.*\n\n**Verification:** Substituting \\(P\\) into the distance formulas yields  \n\\[\nPA^{2}= \\Bigl(x_{0}+\\frac{d x_{0}}{R}\\Bigr)^{2}+\\Bigl(y_{0}-\\frac{d y_{0}}{R}\\Bigr)^{2}\n      =\\frac{d^{2}}{R^{2}}(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})=d^{2},\n\\]  \nusing \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\). Similar calculations give \\(PC^{2}=PD^{2}=d^{2}\\). Hence (1) is correct.\n\n---\n\n### 5. **Slope of line \\(AP\\)**  *(Critical Error)*  \n\n> \u201cFrom (1) the slope is  \n> \\[\n> m_{AP}= \\frac{\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}-y_{0}}{-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}-x_{0}}\n>       =\\frac{R}{d}. \\tag{2}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Issue:** The algebraic simplification is **incorrect**.\n\n*Correct computation:*  \n\\[\nm_{AP}= \\frac{ \\dfrac{d y_{0}}{R} - y_{0} }{ -\\dfrac{d x_{0}}{R} - x_{0} }\n      = \\frac{ y_{0}\\bigl(\\dfrac{d}{R}-1\\bigr) }{ -x_{0}\\bigl(\\dfrac{d}{R}+1\\bigr) }\