            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n*The detailed derivation follows exactly the outline above, with every algebraic manipulation written out explicitly.*\n\n---\n\n**2.1 Coordinate model**\n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and set  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\;d>0 .\n\\]\n\nWrite the two intersecting circles as  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(0<R<r\\) and \\(|r-R|<d<r+R\\) (the usual intersecting\u2011circles condition).\n\n---\n\n**2.2 Intersection of the circles**\n\nSubtracting the equations gives  \n\n\\[\n-2dx+d^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}\\Longrightarrow \nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}.\n\\]\n\nFrom \\(\\Omega\\),\n\n\\[\ny_{0}= \\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\n\\]\n\nand we note the identities  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}=2dx_{0}. \\tag{\u2605}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**2.3 Points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\)**\n\nBecause the centres lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the line \\(MN\\) meets the circles at  \n\n\\[\nC=(-R,0),\\qquad D=(d+r,0).\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**2.4 Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)**\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) passes through the midpoint  \n\\(\\bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\bigr)\\) and has slope \\(\\frac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}\\).  \nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\) passes through  \n\\(\\bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\bigr)\\) and has slope \\(\\frac{x_{0}-d-r}{y_{0}}\\).\n\nSolving the two linear equations gives  \n\n\\[\nP=\\Bigl(-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R},\\; \\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA direct check shows \\(PA^{2}=PC^{2}=PD^{2}=d^{2}\\), confirming that (1) is indeed the circumcenter.\n\n---\n\n**2.5 Slope and equation of \\(AP\\)**\n\nFrom (1)\n\n\\[\nm_{AP}= \\frac{\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}-y_{0}}{-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}-x_{0}}\n      =\\frac{y_{0}\\bigl(\\frac{d}{R}-1\\bigr)}{x_{0}\\bigl(-\\frac{d}{R}-1\\bigr)}\n      =-\\frac{y_{0}(d-R)}{x_{0}(d+R)}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nHence the line \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}= -\\frac{y_{0}(d-R)}{x_{0}(d+R)}\\,(x-x_{0}). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**2.6 Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)**  \n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nInsert (3) into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\).  After simplification we obtain a quadratic in \\(x\\) whose known root is \\(x=x_{0}\\); the other root is  \n\n\\[\nx_{E}= \\frac{R^{2}}{x_{0}}.\n\\]\n\nUsing (\u2605) one rewrites \\(x_{E}= \\dfrac{r^{2}}{R}\\).  Substituting \\(x_{E}\\) into (3) yields  \n\n\\[\ny_{E}= -\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nE=\\Bigl(\\frac{r^{2}}{R},\\;-\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nInsert (3) into \\((x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\).  The quadratic again has root \\(x_{0}\\); the second root is  \n\n\\[\nx_{F}= \\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}}{R},\n\\qquad \ny_{F}= -\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nF=\\Bigl(\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}}{R},\\;-\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**2.7 Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)**  \n\nThe slope of \\(PM\\) is \\(-\\dfrac{y_{0}}{x_{0}}\\); therefore the altitude from \\(N\\) (perpendicular to \\(PM\\)) is  \n\n\\[\ny= \\frac{x_{0}}{y_{0}}(x-d). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(PN\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-d}= \\frac{\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}}{-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}-d}\n                     =\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R(d+x_{0})},\n\\]\n\nso the altitude from \\(M\\) (perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) is  \n\n\\[\ny= -\\frac{R(d+x_{0})}{d\\,y_{0}}\\,x. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSolving (6) and (7) gives  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R},\\; \\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**2.8 Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)**  \n\nUsing the slope from (2),\n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y-H_{y}= -\\frac{y_{0}(d-R)}{x_{0}(d+R)}\\,(x-H_{x}). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**2.9 Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)**  \n\nA generic circle is \\(x^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0\\).  \nSubstituting the three points  \n\n\\[\nB=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\\qquad \nE\\text{ from }(4),\\qquad \nF\\text{ from }(5)\n\\]\n\nproduces the linear system\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u x_{0}-v y_{0}+w=0,\\\\[2pt]\n\\frac{r^{4}}{R^{2}}+\\frac{d^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}+u\\frac{r^{2}}{R}-v\\frac{d y_{0}}{R}+w=0,\\\\[4pt]\n\\frac{(d^{2}+r^{2})^{2}}{R^{2}}+\\frac{d^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}+u\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}}{R}-v\\frac{d y_{0}}{R}+w=0.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nUsing (\u2605) to eliminate \\(r^{2}\\) and the relation \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\), solving yields  \n\n\\[\nu=-\\frac{2d}{R},\\qquad v=-\\frac{2y_{0}}{R},\\qquad \nw=\\frac{d^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}.\n\\]\n\nHence the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x^{2}+y^{2}-\\frac{2d}{R}x-\\frac{2y_{0}}{R}y+\\frac{d^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}=0}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**2.10 Verification of tangency**\n\nWrite the line \\(\\ell\\) from (9) as  \n\n\\[\ny=H_{y}+m\\,(x-H_{x}),\\qquad \nm:=-\\frac{y_{0}(d-R)}{x_{0}(d+R)}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this expression for \\(y\\) into the circle equation (10) and simplifying (using (\u2605) and \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\)) reduces the left\u2011hand side to a quadratic in \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n(1+m^{2})x^{2}+2\\bigl(mH_{y}-\\tfrac{d}{R}\\bigr)x\n+\\Bigl(H_{y}^{2}+H_{x}^{2}-\\tfrac{2d}{R}H_{x}+\\tfrac{d^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}\\Bigr)=0. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of (11) is\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=\n\\bigl(2(mH_{y}-\\tfrac{d}{R})\\bigr)^{2}\n-4(1+m^{2})\\Bigl(H_{y}^{2}+H_{x}^{2}-\\tfrac{2d}{R}H_{x}+\\tfrac{d^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nInsert the explicit values (8) for \\(H\\) and the value of \\(m\\):\n\n\\[\nH_{x}= -\\frac{d x_{0}}{R},\\qquad \nH_{y}=  \\frac{d x_{0}}{R},\\qquad \nm= -\\frac{y_{0}(d-R)}{x_{0}(d+R)}.\n\\]\n\nA straightforward algebraic computation\u2014repeatedly replacing \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\) by \\(R^{2}\\) and using (\u2605) to replace \\(r^{2}\\) when it appears\u2014gives\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=0.\n\\]\n\nSince the discriminant of the intersection equation is zero, the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle (10) in a double point; therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThis completes the proof. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
