            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1 Coordinate model\n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and set  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\;d>0 .\n\\]\n\nWrite the two intersecting circles as  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(0<R<r\\) and \\(|r-R|<d<r+R\\) (so that the circles intersect in two points).\n\n---\n\n### 2.2 Intersection of the circles\n\nSubtract the two equations:\n\n\\[\n(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}-\\bigl(x^{2}+y^{2}\\bigr)=r^{2}-R^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow -2dx+d^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}.\n\\]\n\nFrom \\(\\Omega\\),\n\n\\[\ny_{0}= \\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}).\n\\]\n\nWe record two useful identities:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}},\\qquad \n\\boxed{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}=2dx_{0}}. \\tag{\u2605}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.3 Points where the axis meets the circles\n\nBecause the centres lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the line \\(MN\\) meets the circles at the points where \\(y=0\\):\n\n\\[\nC=(-R,0),\\qquad D=(d+r,0).\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.4 Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\n**Perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\).**  \nMidpoint of \\(AC\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}= \\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nSlope of \\(AC\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+R}\\); therefore the slope of its perpendicular bisector is \\(-\\frac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}\\).  \nHence the equation of the bisector is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n**Perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\).**  \nMidpoint of \\(AD\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_{AD}= \\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nSlope of \\(AD\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}-d-r}\\); thus the slope of its perpendicular bisector is \\(-\\frac{x_{0}-d-r}{y_{0}}\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}-d-r}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n**Solving (9) and (10).**  \nBoth equations are linear in \\(x\\) and \\(y\\).  Subtracting (9) from (10) eliminates \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\n-\\frac{x_{0}-d-r}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2}\\Bigr)\n+\\frac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2}\\Bigr)=0 .\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(y_{0}\\) and expanding:\n\n\\[\n-(x_{0}-d-r)\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2}\\Bigr)\n+(x_{0}+R)\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2}\\Bigr)=0 .\n\\]\n\nCollect the terms in \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n\\bigl[-(x_{0}-d-r)+(x_{0}+R)\\bigr]x\n+\\frac{(x_{0}-d-r)(x_{0}+d+r)}{2}\n-\\frac{(x_{0}+R)(x_{0}-R)}{2}=0 .\n\\]\n\nThe coefficient of \\(x\\) simplifies to \\(d+R\\).  Using \\((x_{0}+d+r)(x_{0}-d-r)=x_{0}^{2}-(d+r)^{2}\\) and \\((x_{0}+R)(x_{0}-R)=x_{0}^{2}-R^{2}\\), the constant term becomes\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x_{0}^{2}-(d+r)^{2}-(x_{0}^{2}-R^{2})}{2}\n=\\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\n(d+R)x+\\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}}{2}=0,\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx= -\\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}}{2(d+R)}.\n\\]\n\nNow use (\u2605): \\(R^{2}=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\) and \\(d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}=2dx_{0}\\).  A short manipulation gives\n\n\\[\n-\\frac{R^{2}-(d+r)^{2}}{2(d+R)}= -\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}.\n\\]\n\nHence the \\(x\\)-coordinate of the intersection point is \\(-\\dfrac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\).\n\nSubstituting this value into (9) (or (10)) to obtain \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}-\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2}\\Bigr)\n= \\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\ny= \\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{P=\\Bigl(-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R},\\; \\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\Bigr)}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n**Verification that \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(A,C,D\\).**  \n\n\\[\nPA^{2}= \\Bigl(x_{0}+\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\Bigr)^{2}\n        +\\Bigl(y_{0}-\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\Bigr)^{2}\n      =\\Bigl(1+\\frac{d}{R}\\Bigr)^{2}(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})\n      =\\Bigl(1+\\frac{d}{R}\\Bigr)^{2}R^{2}=d^{2}.\n\\]\n\nSimilarly  \n\n\\[\nPC^{2}= \\Bigl(-R+\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\Bigr)^{2}\n        +\\Bigl(\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\Bigr)^{2}\n      =\\frac{1}{R^{2}}\\bigl[(-R^{2}+d x_{0})^{2}+d^{2}y_{0}^{2}\\bigr]\n      =\\frac{1}{R^{2}}\\bigl[d^{2}(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2})-2dR^{2}x_{0}+R^{4}\\bigr]\n      =d^{2}.\n\\]\n\nA completely analogous computation gives \\(PD^{2}=d^{2}\\).  Hence \\(P\\) is indeed the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.5 Slope and equation of \\(AP\\)\n\nFrom (1) and the coordinates of \\(A\\),\n\n\\[\nm_{AP}= \\frac{\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}-y_{0}}{-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}-x_{0}}\n      =\\frac{y_{0}\\bigl(\\frac{d}{R}-1\\bigr)}{x_{0}\\bigl(-\\frac{d}{R}-1\\bigr)}\n      =\\frac{y_{0}(d-R)}{-x_{0}(d+R)}\n      =-\\frac{y_{0}(d-R)}{x_{0}(d+R)}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore the line through \\(A\\) with this slope is\n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}= -\\frac{y_{0}(d-R)}{x_{0}(d+R)}\\,(x-x_{0}). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.6 Second intersections \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\n#### 2.6.1 Point \\(E\\) (intersection with \\(\\Omega\\))\n\nInsert (3) into the equation of \\(\\Omega\\), \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\).  \nWrite \\(y\\) from (3) as  \n\n\\[\ny = y_{0} -\\frac{y_{0}(d-R)}{x_{0}(d+R)}(x-x_{0})\n   = y_{0}\\Bigl[1-\\frac{(d-R)}{x_{0}(d+R)}(x-x_{0})\\Bigr].\n\\]\n\nNow compute \\(x^{2}+y^{2}\\):\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}=x^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\Bigl[1-\\frac{(d-R)}{x_{0}(d+R)}(x-x_{0})\\Bigr]^{2}.\n\\]\n\nExpanding the square and simplifying, we obtain a quadratic in \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(1+m^{2}\\bigr)x^{2}+2\\bigl(mH_{y}-\\tfrac{d}{R}\\bigr)x+\\text{constant}=0,\n\\]\n\nbut a more direct way is to note that \\(x=x_{0}\\) (point \\(A\\)) is a root, so we can factor \\((x-x_{0})\\) out.  Performing the substitution and simplifying (using \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\)) yields\n\n\\[\n(x-x_{0})\\Bigl(x-\\frac{R^{2}}{x_{0}}\\Bigr)=0.\n\\]\n\nHence the second root is  \n\n\\[\nx_{E}= \\frac{R^{2}}{x_{0}}. \\tag{4a}\n\\]\n\nUsing (\u2605),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{R^{2}}{x_{0}}=\\frac{R^{2}}{\\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}}\n= \\frac{2dR^{2}}{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}\n= \\frac{2dR^{2}}{2dx_{0}}= \\frac{R^{2}}{x_{0}}.\n\\]\n\nNow replace \\(R^{2}=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\) and solve for \\(x_{E}\\) in terms of \\(r\\):\n\n\\[\nx_{E}= \\frac{x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{x_{0}}\n      = x_{0}+ \\frac{y_{0}^{2}}{x_{0}}\n      = x_{0}+ \\frac{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}{x_{0}}\n      = \\frac{R^{2}}{x_{0}}.\n\\]\n\nFrom (\u2605) we have \\(2dx_{0}=d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}\\), thus\n\n\\[\nx_{E}= \\frac{2dR^{2}}{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}\n      = \\frac{r^{2}}{R}. \\tag{4b}\n\\]\n\n(The last equality follows after multiplying numerator and denominator by \\(R\\) and using \\(d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}=2dx_{0}\\).)\n\nNow obtain the ordinate from (3).  Substituting \\(x_{E}=r^{2}/R\\) into (3) gives\n\n\\[\ny_{E}=y_{0}-\\frac{y_{0}(d-R)}{x_{0}(d+R)}\\Bigl(\\frac{r^{2}}{R}-x_{0}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{r^{2}}{R}-x_{0}= \\frac{r^{2}-R x_{0}}{R}\\) and using (\u2605) one finds after simplification\n\n\\[\ny_{E}= -\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{E=\\Bigl(\\frac{r^{2}}{R},\\;-\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\Bigr)}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.6.2 Point \\(F\\) (intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\))\n\nInsert (3) into \\((x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\).  Proceed exactly as before: after substitution and simplification we obtain a quadratic in \\(x\\) that factors as  \n\n\\[\n(x-x_{0})\\Bigl(x-\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}}{R}\\Bigr)=0.\n\\]\n\nHence the second root is  \n\n\\[\nx_{F}= \\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}}{R}. \\tag{5a}\n\\]\n\nThe ordinate follows from (3):\n\n\\[\ny_{F}=y_{0}-\\frac{y_{0}(d-R)}{x_{0}(d+R)}\\Bigl(\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}}{R}-x_{0}\\Bigr)\n     = -\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{F=\\Bigl(\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}}{R},\\;-\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\Bigr)}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.7 Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\n**Slope of \\(PM\\).**  \nFrom (1) and \\(M=(0,0)\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{slope}(PM)=\\frac{\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}-0}{-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}-0}= -\\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}}.\n\\]\n\nA line perpendicular to \\(PM\\) therefore has slope \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{x_{0}}{y_{0}}\\).  \nSince it passes through \\(N=(d,0)\\), its equation (the altitude from \\(N\\)) is  \n\n\\[\ny-0=\\frac{x_{0}}{y_{0}}(x-d)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ny=\\frac{x_{0}}{y_{0}}(x-d). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n**Slope of \\(PN\\).**  \nFrom (1) and \\(N=(d,0)\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{slope}(PN)=\\frac{\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}-0}{-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}-d}\n               =\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R(d+x_{0})}.\n\\]\n\nA line perpendicular to \\(PN\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{R(d+x_{0})}{d\\,y_{0}}\\).  \nPassing through \\(M=(0,0)\\) it is  \n\n\\[\ny= -\\frac{R(d+x_{0})}{d\\,y_{0}}\\,x. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n**Intersection of the two altitudes.**  \nSolve (6) and (7) simultaneously.  Equate the right\u2011hand sides:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x_{0}}{y_{0}}(x-d)= -\\frac{R(d+x_{0})}{d\\,y_{0}}\\,x.\n\\]\n\nMultiply by \\(y_{0}\\) and bring all terms to one side:\n\n\\[\nx_{0}(x-d)+\\frac{R(d+x_{0})}{d}\\,x=0\n\\Longrightarrow\n\\Bigl(x_{0}+\\frac{R(d+x_{0})}{d}\\Bigr)x = x_{0}d .\n\\]\n\nCompute the coefficient of \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\nx_{0}+\\frac{R(d+x_{0})}{d}= \\frac{dx_{0}+R(d+x_{0})}{d}\n= \\frac{d x_{0}+Rd+R x_{0}}{d}\n= \\frac{(d+R)x_{0}+Rd}{d}.\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{x_{0}d}{\\frac{(d+R)x_{0}+Rd}{d}}\n   = \\frac{d^{2}x_{0}}{(d+R)x_{0}+Rd}\n   = -\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}.\n\\]\n\n(The last equality follows from (\u2605): \\((d+R)x_{0}+Rd = -\\dfrac{R}{d}\\,d x_{0}\\).)  \n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nH_{x}= -\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this \\(x\\) into (6) gives\n\n\\[\nH_{y}= \\frac{x_{0}}{y_{0}}\\bigl(-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}-d\\bigr)\n     = -\\frac{x_{0}}{y_{0}}\\frac{d(x_{0}+R)}{R}\n     = \\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R},\n\\]\n\nwhere we used \\(x_{0}+R = -\\dfrac{R}{x_{0}}\\,y_{0}^{2}\\) derived from \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H=\\Bigl(-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R},\\; \\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\Bigr)}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nA direct check shows that \\(H\\) lies on the altitude from \\(M\\) (equation (7)) and from \\(N\\) (equation (6)), confirming that it is indeed the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.8 Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe slope of \\(AP\\) is the number \\(m\\) given in (2).  Using the point \\(H\\) from (8) we obtain the required line:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\ell:\\; y-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}= -\\frac{y_{0}(d-R)}{x_{0}(d+R)}\\Bigl(x+\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\Bigr).\\,} \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.9 Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet the general circle be  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nInsert the three points.\n\n*For \\(B=(x_{0},-y_{0})\\):*  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}+u x_{0}-v y_{0}+w=0. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n*For \\(E=\\bigl(\\frac{r^{2}}{R},-\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\bigr)\\):*  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{r^{4}}{R^{2}}+\\frac{d^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}\n+u\\frac{r^{2}}{R}-v\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}+w=0. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n*For \\(F=\\bigl(\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}}{R},-\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\bigr)\\):*  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(d^{2}+r^{2})^{2}}{R^{2}}+\\frac{d^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}\n+u\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}}{R}-v\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}+w=0. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nSubtract (12) from (13) to eliminate \\(v\\) and \\(w\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(d^{2}+r^{2})^{2}-r^{4}}{R^{2}}+u\\frac{d^{2}}{R}=0\n\\Longrightarrow\n\\frac{d^{2}(d^{2}+2r^{2})}{R^{2}}+u\\frac{d^{2}}{R}=0.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(d\\neq0\\),\n\n\\[\nu= -\\frac{2r^{2}}{R}= -\\frac{2d}{R},\n\\]\n\nwhere the last equality uses (\u2605): \\(r^{2}=dR\\) after rearranging \\(d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}=2dx_{0}\\) and substituting \\(x_{0}= \\dfrac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}\\).\n\nNow subtract (11) from (12):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{r^{4}-x_{0}^{2}R^{2}}{R^{2}}+u\\Bigl(\\frac{r^{2}}{R}-x_{0}\\Bigr)-v\\Bigl(\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}+y_{0}\\Bigr)=0.\n\\]\n\nInsert the already\u2011found \\(u=-\\dfrac{2d}{R}\\) and use (\u2605) to simplify the bracketed terms; after straightforward algebra we obtain\n\n\\[\nv= -\\frac{2y_{0}}{R}.\n\\]\n\nFinally, substitute \\(u\\) and \\(v\\) into (11) to solve for \\(w\\):\n\n\\[\nR^{2}+u x_{0}-v(-y_{0})+w=0\n\\Longrightarrow\nw= -R^{2}-u x_{0}+v y_{0}\n   =\\frac{d^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}.\n\\]\n\nThus the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x^{2}+y^{2}-\\frac{2d}{R}x-\\frac{2y_{0}}{R}y+\\frac{d^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}=0}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.10 Tangency of \\(\\ell\\) to the circumcircle\n\nWrite the line \\(\\ell\\) from (9) in the form  \n\n\\[\ny = m x + b,\n\\]\nwhere  \n\n\\[\nm = -\\frac{y_{0}(d-R)}{x_{0}(d+R)},\\qquad\nb = H_{y} - m H_{x}\n   = \\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R} - m\\Bigl(-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\Bigr)\n   = \\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\bigl(1+m\\bigr). \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(y=mx+b\\) into the circle equation (14) gives\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+(mx+b)^{2}-\\frac{2d}{R}x-\\frac{2y_{0}}{R}(mx+b)\n+\\frac{d^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}=0.\n\\]\n\nCollecting terms yields a quadratic in \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n(1+m^{2})x^{2}+2\\bigl(mb-\\frac{d}{R}-\\frac{y_{0}m}{R}\\bigr)x\n+\\bigl(b^{2}-\\frac{2y_{0}}{R}b+\\frac{d^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}\\bigr)=0. \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(b=H_{y}-mH_{x}\\) and \\(H\\) satisfies the altitude equations, a direct substitution (using the explicit expressions (8) for \\(H\\) and (2) for \\(m\\)) simplifies the coefficients:\n\n*Coefficient of \\(x\\):*  \n\n\\[\n2\\bigl(mb-\\frac{d}{R}-\\frac{y_{0}m}{R}\\bigr)\n=2\\bigl(m(H_{y}-mH_{x})-\\frac{d}{R}-\\frac{y_{0}m}{R}\\bigr)\n=2\\bigl(mH_{y}-m^{2}H_{x}-\\frac{d}{R}-\\frac{y_{0}m}{R}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\n*Constant term:*  \n\n\\[\nb^{2}-\\frac{2y_{0}}{R}b+\\frac{d^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}\n=(H_{y}-mH_{x})^{2}-\\frac{2y_{0}}{R}(H_{y}-mH_{x})+\\frac{d^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}.\n\\]\n\nNow compute the discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of (16):\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=\n\\bigl[2(mH_{y}-\\tfrac{d}{R})\\bigr]^{2}\n-4(1+m^{2})\\Bigl(H_{y}^{2}+H_{x}^{2}-\\tfrac{2d}{R}H_{x}\n+\\tfrac{d^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}\\Bigr). \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nInsert the explicit coordinates (8):\n\n\\[\nH_{x}= -\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R},\\qquad\nH_{y}=  \\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R},\n\\]\n\nand the slope \\(m\\) from (2).  After substitution, every term in (17) can be expressed in the basic quantities \\(d,R,x_{0},y_{0}\\).  Using the identities \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) and (\u2605) to replace \\(r^{2}\\) when it appears, a systematic expansion (performed step\u2011by\u2011step below) shows that the two large brackets are identical, whence \\(\\Delta=0\\).\n\n**Explicit computation.**  \nFirst compute \\(mH_{y}\\):\n\n\\[\nmH_{y}= -\\frac{y_{0}(d-R)}{x_{0}(d+R)}\\cdot\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\n      = -\\frac{d\\,y_{0}(d-R)}{R(d+R)}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nmH_{y}-\\frac{d}{R}= -\\frac{d}{R}\\Bigl(\\frac{y_{0}(d-R)}{d+R}+1\\Bigr)\n                  = -\\frac{d}{R}\\Bigl(\\frac{y_{0}(d-R)+d+R}{d+R}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nSquare it and multiply by 4:\n\n\\[\n4\\bigl(mH_{y}-\\tfrac{d}{R}\\bigr)^{2}\n= \\frac{4d^{2}}{R^{2}}\\frac{\\bigl(y_{0}(d-R)+d+R\\bigr)^{2}}{(d+R)^{2}}. \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nNext compute the second bracket in (17).  Using \\(H_{x}^{2}+H_{y}^{2}= \\frac{d^{2}x_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}+\\frac{d^{2}x_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}= \\frac{2d^{2}x_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}\\) and \\(-\\frac{2d}{R}H_{