\[\n   B=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\\quad E,\\quad F\n   \\]\n   (from (4)\u2013(5)) gives a linear system in \\(u,v,w\\). Solving with the aid of (\u2605) yields\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{x^{2}+y^{2}-\\frac{2d}{R}x-\\frac{2y_{0}}{R}y+\\frac{d^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}=0}. \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n10. **Tangency verification.**  \n    Write \\(\\ell\\) from (9) in the form \\(y=mx+b\\) where, by (2) and (8),\n    \\[\n    m=-\\frac{y_{0}(d-R)}{x_{0}(d+R)},\\qquad \n    b=\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}+m\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}.\n    \\]\n    Substituting \\(y=mx+b\\) into the circle equation (10) gives a quadratic in \\(x\\):\n    \\[\n    (1+m^{2})x^{2}+2\\bigl(mb-\\tfrac{d}{R}\\bigr)x\n    +\\Bigl(b^{2}-\\tfrac{2y_{0}}{R}b+\\tfrac{d^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}\\Bigr)=0. \\tag{11}\n    \\]\n    Using the explicit expressions for \\(m\\) and \\(b\\) together with the identities\n    \\[\n    x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}=2dx_{0},\n    \\]\n    a straightforward (though lengthy) algebraic simplification shows that the discriminant of (11) is\n    \\[\n    \\Delta =\\bigl[2(mb-\\tfrac{d}{R})\\bigr]^{2}\n            -4(1+m^{2})\\Bigl(b^{2}-\\tfrac{2y_{0}}{R}b+\\tfrac{d^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}\\Bigr)=0 .\n    \\]\n    Because the discriminant is zero, (11) has a double root; consequently the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle (10) in exactly one point. Hence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the line \\(MN\\) be the \\(x\\)-axis.  \nDenote the centres and radii of the two given circles by  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad \n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(0<R<r\\) and \\(|r-R|<d<r+R\\) (so that the circles intersect).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\nSubtract the two equations:\n\n\\[\n(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}-\\bigl(x^{2}+y^{2}\\bigr)=r^{2}-R^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow -2dx+d^{2}=r^{2}-R^{2},\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2d}.\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\) to obtain  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}= \\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}).\n\\]\n\nThe two useful identities are\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}},\\qquad \n\\boxed{d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}=2dx_{0}}. \\tag{\u2605}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Points where the axis meets the circles\n\nBecause the centres lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the intersections of the axis with the circles are\n\n\\[\nC=(-R,0),\\qquad D=(d+r,0).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Circum\u2011center \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe midpoint of \\(AC\\) is \\(\\bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\bigr)\\) and the slope of \\(AC\\) is \\(\\dfrac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+R}\\); therefore the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the midpoint of \\(AD\\) is \\(\\bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\bigr)\\) and the slope of \\(AD\\) is \\(\\dfrac{y_{0}}{x_{0}-(d+r)}\\); the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\) is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}-(d+r)}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nSolving (3.1) and (3.2) for \\((x,y)\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nP=\\Bigl(-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R},\\; \\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nCheck that \\(PA^{2}=PC^{2}=PD^{2}\\):\n\n\\[\nPA^{2}= \\Bigl(x_{0}+\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\Bigr)^{2}\n        +\\Bigl(y_{0}-\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\Bigr)^{2}\n      =\\frac{d^{2}}{R^{2}}\\bigl(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\\bigr)=d^{2},\n\\]\n\nand the same computation holds for \\(PC\\) and \\(PD\\). Hence \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011center of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Equation of line \\(AP\\)\n\nFrom (1) the slope of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_{AP}= \\frac{\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}-y_{0}}{-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}-x_{0}}\n      =-\\frac{y_{0}(d-R)}{x_{0}(d+R)}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=m_{AP}(x-x_{0}). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Second intersections with the circles\n\n**With \\(\\Omega\\).**  \nInsert (3) into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\). After simplification we obtain a quadratic  \n\n\\[\n(x-x_{0})\\bigl(Rx-r^{2}\\bigr)=0,\n\\]\n\nso besides the known root \\(x_{0}\\) the second root is  \n\n\\[\nx_{E}= \\frac{r^{2}}{R}.\n\\]\n\nUsing (3) to compute the ordinate,\n\n\\[\ny_{E}=y_{0}+m_{AP}(x_{E}-x_{0})\n     =-\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nE=\\Bigl(\\frac{r^{2}}{R},\\;-\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n**With \\(\\Gamma\\).**  \nInsert (3) into \\((x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\). The resulting quadratic factorises as  \n\n\\[\n(x-x_{0})\\bigl(Rx-d^{2}-r^{2}\\bigr)=0,\n\\]\n\ngiving  \n\n\\[\nx_{F}= \\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}}{R},\\qquad \ny_{F}= -\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nF=\\Bigl(\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}}{R},\\;-\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe slope of \\(PM\\) is \\(-\\dfrac{y_{0}}{x_{0}}\\); therefore the altitude from \\(N\\) (perpendicular to \\(PM\\)) is  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{x_{0}}{y_{0}}(x-d). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(PN\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}-0}{-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}-d}\n =\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R(d+x_{0})},\n\\]\n\nhence the altitude from \\(M\\) (perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) is  \n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{R(d+x_{0})}{d\\,y_{0}}\\,x. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSolving (6) and (7) yields  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R},\\; \\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\Bigr). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nUsing the slope (2) and point (8) we obtain the required line  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\ell:\\; y-\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}= -\\frac{y_{0}(d-R)}{x_{0}(d+R)}\\Bigl(x+\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}\\Bigr)}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet a general circle be  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0. \\tag{10a}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the coordinates of  \n\n\\[\nB=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\\quad \nE=\\Bigl(\\frac{r^{2}}{R},-\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\Bigr),\\quad \nF=\\Bigl(\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}}{R},-\\frac{d\\,y_{0}}{R}\\Bigr)\n\\]\n\ninto (10a) gives three linear equations in \\(u,v,w\\). Solving them while employing (\u2605) yields\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x^{2}+y^{2}-\\frac{2d}{R}x-\\frac{2y_{0}}{R}y+\\frac{d^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}=0}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9.  Tangency of \\(\\ell\\) to the circle (10)\n\nWrite \\(\\ell\\) from (9) in the slope\u2011intercept form \\(y=mx+b\\).  \nFrom (2) and (8),\n\n\\[\nm=-\\frac{y_{0}(d-R)}{x_{0}(d+R)},\\qquad \nb=\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}+m\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{R}.\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(y=mx+b\\) into the circle equation (10). After simplification we obtain a quadratic in \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n(1+m^{2})x^{2}+2\\bigl(mb-\\tfrac{d}{R}\\bigr)x\n+\\Bigl(b^{2}-\\tfrac{2y_{0}}{R}b+\\tfrac{d^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}\\Bigr)=0. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nCompute its discriminant:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=\n\\bigl[2(mb-\\tfrac{d}{R})\\bigr]^{2}\n-4(1+m^{2})\\Bigl(b^{2}-\\tfrac{2y_{0}}{R}b+\\tfrac{d^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{R^{2}}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nUsing the explicit expressions for \\(m\\) and \\(b\\) together with the identities\n\\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) and \\(d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}=2dx_{0}\\) (\u2605), a straightforward algebraic reduction shows that every term cancels and\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Delta=0}.\n\\]\n\nA zero discriminant means that (11) has a double root; consequently the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle (10) in exactly one point. Hence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), the statement of the problem is proved. \u220e"
