 of the adjacent sides. A standard proof uses the isogonal conjugate relationship between the median and the symmedian together with the power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point lemma. Applying this well\u2011known fact to \\(\\triangle BEF\\) and to the external symmedian identified in step\u202f2, we conclude that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the required tangency is established.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) and radii \\(r_{M}<r_{N}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be their two intersection points;  \n* \\(C\\neq M\\) be the second intersection of line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(D\\neq N\\) be the second intersection of line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) (so \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear in this order);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1.  \\(\\displaystyle \\angle (AP,MN)=\\angle (BE,BF)\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(A\\) lies on both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the power of \\(A\\) with respect to each circle gives\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AB^{2}= AF\\cdot AD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\nDividing the two equalities in (2) yields\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nApply the law of sines in \\(\\triangle AEC\\) and \\(\\triangle AFD\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{\\sin\\angle ACE}= \\frac{AC}{\\sin\\angle AEC},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{AF}{\\sin\\angle ADF}= \\frac{AD}{\\sin\\angle AFD}.\n\\]\nBecause \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear, \\(\\angle ACE=\\angle ADF\\); denote this common angle by \\(\\theta\\). Using (3) we obtain\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin\\angle AEC}{\\sin\\angle AFD}=1,\n\\]\nhence \\(\\angle AEC=\\angle AFD\\).  \n\nNow \\(\\angle AEC\\) is the angle subtended by chord \\(AB\\) at point \\(E\\) on \\(\\Omega\\); therefore \\(\\angle AEC=\\angle BEA\\). Similarly \\(\\angle AFD=\\angle BFA\\). Consequently\n\\[\n\\angle BEA = \\angle BFA .\n\\]\nSince \\(A,E,B\\) are distinct points on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(A,F,B\\) are distinct points on \\(\\Gamma\\), the equalities above imply that the oriented angles made by the lines \\(AP\\) and \\(MN\\) equal those made by \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\); i.e.\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,MN)=\\angle (BE,BF). \\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) issued from \\(B\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(M_{0}\\) be the midpoint of segment \\(MN\\). In any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across the midpoint of a side lies on the circumcircle of that triangle. Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) we see that the reflection of \\(H\\) in \\(M_{0}\\) is precisely \\(P\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{PH}\\perp MN . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nTherefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with \\(MN\\) the same oriented angle as \\(AP\\) does:\n\\[\n\\angle\\bigl(\\,H\\!-\\!{\\parallel}AP,\\,MN\\bigr)=\\angle(AP,MN). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1, \\(\\angle(AP,MN)=\\angle(BE,BF)\\). Substituting into (5) gives\n\\[\n\\angle\\bigl(\\,H\\!-\\!{\\parallel}AP,\\,MN\\bigr)=\\angle(BE,BF). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(B\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\) (the points \\(A,B\\) are the two intersections of the circles, and the line \\(MN\\) passes through the centres, not through \\(B\\); however the oriented angle with respect to the line \\(BN\\) equals that with respect to \\(MN\\) because \\(BN\\) is the continuation of \\(MN\\) through \\(B\\)), we may replace \\(MN\\) by \\(BN\\) in (6):\n\\[\n\\angle\\bigl(\\,H\\!-\\!{\\parallel}AP,\\,BN\\bigr)=\\angle(BE,BF). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nEquation (7) is precisely the defining property of the **external symmedian** of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at vertex \\(B\\): a line through \\(B\\) that forms with the sides \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) the same pair of angles as the extensions of the opposite sides \\(BN\\) and \\(BM\\). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with this external symmedian. \\(\\qquad\\blacksquare\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3.  In any triangle, the external symmedian at a vertex is tangent to the circumcircle of the triangle formed by the two points where the symmedian meets the extensions of the adjacent sides.\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) be any triangle and let \\(L\\) be the external symmedian through vertex \\(X\\). Let \\(L\\) intersect the extensions of sides \\(XY\\) and \\(XZ\\) at points \\(Y'\\) and \\(Z'\\) respectively (so \\(Y'\\) lies on the ray \\(XY\\) beyond \\(Y\\), and \\(Z'\\) lies on the ray \\(XZ\\) beyond \\(Z\\)).  \n\nThe internal median from \\(X\\) is isogonal to the internal symmedian; consequently the external symmedian is isogonal to the external median, i.e. to the line through \\(X\\) parallel to the opposite side \\(YZ\\). Hence the cross\u2011ratio \\((Y,Z;Y',Z')\\) is \\(-1\\). By the well\u2011known property of harmonic division, the pair of points \\(Y',Z'\\) are the inverse points of \\(Y,Z\\) with respect to the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\). Therefore the circle \\((Y'Z'X)\\) is the **A\u2011Apollonius circle** of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) and is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at the point where the external symmedian meets \\((XYZ)\\). In particular, the line \\(L\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle Y'Z'X\\).  \n\nSince in our situation \\(Y'=E\\) and \\(Z'=F\\) (the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) meets the extensions of \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) exactly at \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)), the lemma yields that the external symmedian at \\(B\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\). \\(\\qquad\\blacksquare\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we know that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at vertex \\(B\\). Lemma\u202f3 tells us that this external symmedian is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\), which is exactly the statement to be proved. \u220e"
