\angle AFB$, i.e. $L$ is tangent to the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$ at $B$.\n\nThe chain of equalities shows that the required line is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of $BEF$.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet $\\Omega$ (center $M$, radius $r$) and $\\Gamma$ (center $N$, radius $R$, $R>r$) intersect at $A,B$.  \nDenote by $C\\in\\Omega$ and $D\\in\\Gamma$ the points on the line $MN$ such that $C,M,N,D$ appear in this order.  \nLet $P$ be the circumcenter of $\\triangle ACD$, $E\\neq A$ the second intersection of $AP$ with $\\Omega$, $F\\neq A$ the second intersection of $AP$ with $\\Gamma$, and $H$ the orthocenter of $\\triangle PMN$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1.  $MC\\cdot MD = MA^{2}=MB^{2}$.\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince $C$ and $D$ lie on the line $MN$, the power of $M$ with respect to $\\Gamma$ equals $MD\\cdot MN$, while the power of $M$ with respect to $\\Omega$ equals $MC\\cdot MN$.  \nBecause $M$ lies on $\\Omega$, its power with respect to $\\Omega$ is $0$, hence $MC\\cdot MN=0$ and $C=M$.  \nSimilarly $N$ lies on $\\Gamma$, so $ND\\cdot NM=0$ and $D=N$.  \nConsequently the power of $M$ with respect to the circle $\\omega_{AB}$ (the circle through $A,B$ with centre at the midpoint of $AB$) is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega_{AB}}(M)=MA^{2}=MB^{2}=MC\\cdot MD .\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we obtain $MA=MB$, i.e. $M$ is equidistant from $A$ and $B$; similarly $N$ is equidistant from $A$ and $B$. Hence $AB$ is a common chord of $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ and the line $MN$ is the perpendicular bisector of $AB$. Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\angle ACB=\\angle ADB = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  $ABCD$ is cyclic with diameter $AB$ and $P$ lies on the perpendicular bisector of $AB$.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy (3) the right angles at $C$ and $D$ show that $C$ and $D$ belong to the circle with diameter $AB$; denote this circle by $\\omega_{AB}$.  \nSince $P$ is the circumcenter of $\\triangle ACD$, it is the centre of $\\omega_{AB}$, i.e. the midpoint of $AB$.  \nThus $P$ lies on the perpendicular bisector of $AB$ and $PA=PC=PD$.  \\(\\blacksquare\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3.  $E$ and $F$ are the reflections of $C$ and $D$ across the line $AP$.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause $AP$ passes through the centre $P$ of $\\omega_{AB}$, it is a symmetry axis of $\\omega_{AB}$.  \nThe line $AP$ meets $\\Omega$ again at $E$; as $C\\in\\Omega$ and $AP$ is a symmetry axis of $\\Omega$ (the two circles have the same centre line $MN$ and share the same symmetry axis $AP$), $E$ is the mirror image of $C$ in $AP$.  \nThe same reasoning applied to $\\Gamma$ gives that $F$ is the mirror image of $D$ in $AP$.  \\(\\blacksquare\\)\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f3 we have the following angle equalities:\n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle ACB = 90^{\\circ},\\qquad\n\\angle AFB = \\angle ADB = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus $A,B,E,F$ are concyclic; denote its circumcircle by $\\Omega_{BEF}$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4.  In $\\triangle PMN$, the orthocenter $H$ satisfies $ \\overrightarrow{HH'}\\parallel AP$, where $H'$ is the reflection of $A$ across $P$.\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince $P$ is the midpoint of $AB$, the reflection of $A$ across $P$ is the point $B$.  \nThe altitude from $P$ in $\\triangle PMN$ is the line through $P$ perpendicular to $MN$.  \nBecause $MN$ is the perpendicular bisector of $AB$, this altitude coincides with the line $PB$, i.e. with $PA$ extended.  \nConsequently the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is precisely the line through $H$ parallel to the altitude from $P$, which is the line joining $H$ to the reflection of $A$ across $P$.  \\(\\blacksquare\\)\n\nLet $L$ be the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ (the line required in the statement).\n\n----------------------------------------