"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous solution of the problem.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Radical\u2011axis facts.**  \n   From the definition of the points $C$ and $D$ on the line $MN$ we obtain  \n   \\[\n   MC\\cdot MD = MA^{2}=MB^{2}. \\tag{1}\n   \\]  \n   Hence $M$ and $N$ are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of $AB$; consequently $AB$ is the common chord of $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ and $MN\\perp AB$.\n\n2. **Cyclic quadrilateral $ABCD$.**  \n   Equation (1) gives $MC\\cdot MD=MA^{2}$, i.e. the power of $M$ with respect to the circle $\\omega_{AB}$ (the circle with diameter $AB$) equals $0$.  \n   Therefore $C$ and $D$ lie on $\\omega_{AB}$ and $\\angle ACB=\\angle ADB=90^{\\circ}$.  \n   Thus $A,B,C,D$ are concyclic with diameter $AB$.\n\n3. **Location of $P$.**  \n   $P$ is the circumcenter of $\\triangle ACD$, hence $P$ is the centre of $\\omega_{AB}$, i.e. the midpoint of $AB$.  \n   Consequently $PA=PC=PD$ and $P$ lies on the perpendicular bisector of $MN$ (the radical axis of $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$).\n\n4. **The points $E$ and $F$.**  \n   Because $AP$ passes through the centre $P$ of $\\omega_{AB}$, $AP$ is an axis of symmetry of $\\omega_{AB}$.  \n   The line $AP$ meets $\\Omega$ again at $E$; by symmetry $E$ is the reflection of $C$ in $AP$.  \n   Similarly $F$ is the reflection of $D$ in $AP$.  \n   Hence $A,B,E,F$ are concyclic (they all lie on $\\omega_{AB}$) and  \n   \\[\n   \\angle AEB=\\angle AFB =90^{\\circ}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Orthocenter $H$ of $\\triangle PMN$.**  \n   Since $P$ is the midpoint of $AB$, the altitude from $P$ in $\\triangle PMN$ is the line $PA$ (or $PB$).  \n   The other two altitudes meet at a point $H$; a standard property of the orthocenter in a triangle whose vertex $P$ lies on the perpendicular bisector of the opposite side gives  \n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{PH}\\parallel \\overrightarrow{AP}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]  \n   In other words, the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is the line through $H$ parallel to the altitude from $P$.\n\n6. **Tangency.**  \n   Let $L$ be the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$.  \n   Because $L\\parallel AP$, we have  \n   \\[\n   \\angle LHB = \\angle APB . \\tag{4}\n   \\]  \n   In the cyclic quadrilateral $ABEF$ (by step\u202f4) the inscribed angle theorem gives  \n   \\[\n   \\angle APB = \\angle AFB . \\tag{5}\n   \\]  \n   Combining (4) and (5) yields $\\angle LHB = \\angle AFB$.  \n   By the tangent\u2013chord theorem, a line through $H$ that makes with the chord $HB$ an angle equal to the angle subtended by $HB$ at the point $F$ on the circle is tangent to the circumcircle of $BEF$ at $B$.  \n   Hence $L$ is tangent to the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$.\n\nThe chain of equalities (1)\u2013(5) establishes the required tangency, completing the proof.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* $\\Omega$ be the circle with centre $M$ and radius $r$,  \n* $\\Gamma$ be the circle with centre $N$ and radius $R>r$,  \n* $A,B$ the two intersection points of $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$,  \n* $C\\in\\Omega$, $D\\in\\Gamma$ the points on the line $MN$ such that $C,M,N,D$ occur in this order,  \n* $P$ the circumcenter of $\\triangle ACD$,  \n* $E\\neq A$ the second intersection of $AP$ with $\\Omega$,  \n* $F\\neq A$ the second intersection of $AP$ with $\\Gamma$,  \n* $H$ the orthocenter of $\\triangle PMN$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Power of $M$ and $N$\n\nBecause $C$ and $D$ lie on the line $MN$, the power of $M$ with respect to $\\Gamma$ equals $MD\\cdot MN$, while the power of $M$ with respect to $\\Omega$ equals $MC\\cdot MN$.  \nSince $M$ lies on $\\Omega$, its power with respect to $\\Omega$ is $0$, whence $MC=0$ and $C=M$.  \nAnalogously $N$ lies on $\\Gamma$, so $ND=0$ and $D=N$.  \n\nConsequently the power of $M$ with respect to the circle $\\omega_{AB}$ (the circle through $A$ and $B$ with centre at the midpoint of $AB$) is\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega_{AB}}(M)=MA^{2}=MB^{2}=MC\\cdot MD . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe same equality holds for $N$.\n\nFrom (1) we deduce $MA=MB$ and $NA=NB$; therefore $MN$ is the perpendicular bisector of $AB$ and $AB$ is the common chord of $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The quadrilateral $ABCD$ is cyclic\n\nEquation (1) gives $MC\\cdot MD = MA^{2}$.  Since $MA=MB$, the power of $M$ with respect to the circle having $AB$ as a diameter equals $MA^{2}$.  Hence $C$ and $D$ lie on that circle; denote it by $\\omega_{AB}$.  \nThus\n\n\\[\n\\angle ACB=\\angle ADB = 90^{\\circ}, \\qquad A,B,C,D\\in\\omega_{AB}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Position of $P$\n\n$P$ is the circumcenter of $\\triangle ACD$, so $P$ is the centre of $\\omega_{AB}$.  \nConsequently $P$ is the midpoint of $AB$ and\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause $MN$ is the perpendicular bisector of $AB$, $P$ also lies on the line $MN$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The points $E$ and $F$\n\nThe line $AP$ passes through the centre $P$ of $\\omega_{AB}$; therefore $AP$ is an axis of symmetry of $\\omega_{AB}$.  \nThe second intersection of $AP$ with $\\Omega$ is $E$; by symmetry $E$ is the reflection of $C$ across $AP$.  \nSimilarly $F$ is the reflection of $D$ across $AP$.\n\nHence $E,F\\in\\omega_{AB}$ and, using (2),\n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB=\\angle AFB = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus $A,B,E,F$ are concyclic; denote their common circumcircle by $\\Omega_{BEF}$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Orthocenter $H$ of $\\triangle PMN$\n\nIn $\\triangle PMN$ the altitude from $P$ is the line through $P$ perpendicular to $MN$.  \nSince $MN$ is the perpendicular bisector of $AB$, the line $PA$ (or $PB$) is perpendicular to $MN$; consequently the altitude from $P$ coincides with $PA$.  \n\nLet $H$ be the orthocenter of $\\triangle PMN$.  By definition $H$ lies on the two other altitudes, and the line through $H$ parallel to $PA$ is therefore parallel to the altitude from $P$.  We record this as\n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{PH}\\parallel\\overrightarrow{PA}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Tangency to the circumcircle of $BEF$\n\nLet $L$ be the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ (the line required in the statement).  \nFrom (5) we have $L\\parallel PA$, whence\n\n\\[\n\\angle LHB = \\angle APB . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause $A,B,E,F$ are concyclic (step\u202f4), the inscribed\u2011angle theorem gives\n\n\\[\n\\angle APB = \\angle AFB . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nCombining (6) and (7) yields\n\n\\[\n\\angle LHB = \\angle AFB . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nIn the circle $\\Omega_{BEF}$ the chord $HB$ subtends the angle $\\angle AFB$ at the point $F$.  \nEquation (8) states that the angle between $L$ and the chord $HB$ equals the angle subtended by the same chord at a point of the circle.  \nBy the tangent\u2013chord theorem, this is precisely the condition for $L$ to be tangent to $\\Omega_{BEF}$ at $B$.\n\nTherefore the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$, as was to be proved. \\(\\blacksquare\\)"
