n\n### Step 4 \u2013 Quadrilateral $ABCD$ is cyclic\n\n> \u201cEquation (1) gives $MC\\cdot MD = MA^{2}$. Since $MA=MB$, the power of $M$ with respect to the circle having $AB$ as a diameter equals $MA^{2}$. Hence $C$ and $D$ lie on that circle \u2026\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** The \u201ccircle having $AB$ as a diameter\u201d is the circle with centre at the midpoint of $AB$, not $\\omega_{AB}$ defined earlier (which was already ill\u2011defined). The power argument again uses the false equality (1). No legitimate proof that $\\angle ACB = \\angle ADB = 90^\\circ$ is given.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 relies on invalid power computation.\n\n---\n\n### Step 5 \u2013 Position of $P$\n\n> \u201c$P$ is the circumcenter of $\\triangle ACD$, so $P$ is the centre of $\\omega_{AB}$. Consequently $P$ is the midpoint of $AB$ \u2026\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** Even if $C$ and $D$ were on the circle with diameter $AB$, the circumcenter of $\\triangle ACD$ would be the centre of that circle **only if** $A$, $C$, $D$ are all on that circle, which has not been established. The earlier claim that $\\omega_{AB}$ is the circle with centre at the midpoint of $AB$ is unproved. Hence the identification of $P$ with the midpoint of $AB$ is unjustified.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 6 \u2013 Claim that $P$ lies on $MN$\n\n> \u201cBecause $MN$ is the perpendicular bisector of $AB$, $P$ also lies on the line $MN$.\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** The perpendicular\u2011bisector property of $MN$ was never correctly proved (see Step\u202f2). Therefore the conclusion that $P\\in MN$ is unsupported.\n\n**Classification:** **Justification Gap** (even if we temporarily accept the previous claim, the link is not explicitly shown).\n\n---\n\n### Step 7 \u2013 Symmetry of $\\omega_{AB}$ about $AP$\n\n> \u201cThe line $AP$ passes through the centre $P$ of $\\omega_{AB}$; therefore $AP$ is an axis of symmetry of $\\omega_{AB}$.\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** For a line through