ed Solution**\n\nLet the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) have centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) and radii \\(r<R\\) respectively.  \nDenote by \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nAll points are defined as in the statement: \\(A,B\\) are the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); the line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\); \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); the line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\); \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1.  \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(EF\\) of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) not containing \\(B\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the points \\(A,C,D\\) are equidistant from \\(P\\); i.e. \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  \nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\).  \nBecause \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\), the chords \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\): the power of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) equals \\(AE\\cdot AC\\) and with respect to \\(\\Gamma\\) equals \\(AF\\cdot AD\\); but \\(AC=AD\\) (both are chords subtended by the same central angle \\(\\angle MAN\\)), hence \\(AE=AF\\).  \nConsequently the arcs \\(\\widehat{AE}\\) on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\widehat{AF}\\) on \\(\\Gamma\\) have equal lengths, which implies that the central angles \\(\\angle MAP\\) and \\(\\angle NAP\\) are equal.  \nTherefore \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle EAF\\).  \n\nNow consider the quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\).  Points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) lie on both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively, and all four are concyclic because they share the same power with respect to the radical axis \\(AB\\).  Moreover, \\(PA=PE\\) because \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle through \\(A\\) and \\(E\\) (both lie on \\(\\Omega\\) and are symmetric with respect to \\(AP\\)).  Similarly \\(PA=PF\\).  Hence \\(PA=PE=PF\\); thus \\(P\\) is the centre of the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).  Consequently \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(EF\\) that does not contain \\(B\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\) and \\(PA=PE=PF\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we already have \\(PA=PE=PF\\).  Since \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) lie on both original circles, the power of \\(B\\) with respect to the circle centred at \\(P\\) equals \\(PB^{2}=PA^{2}=PE^{2}=PF^{2}\\); hence \\(B\\) also lies on that circle.  Therefore \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic with centre \\(P\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3.  Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  Denote by \\(T\\neq B\\) the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).  Then  \n\\[\n\\angle BTP = \\angle BAP .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), the oriented angle \\(\\angle (BT,TP)\\) equals the oriented angle \\(\\angle (BA,AP)\\); i.e. \\(\\angle BTP = \\angle BAP\\).  The equality holds for the directed angles modulo \\(180^{\\circ}\\); since \\(T\\neq B\\) the equality is genuine. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4.  In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the reflection \\(H'\\) of the orthocentre \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIn any triangle, the reflection of the orthocentre across any side lies on the circumcircle (well\u2011known property).  Applying this to side \\(MN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) yields the claim. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5.  The point \\(H'\\) of Lemma\u202f4 coincides with the point \\(T\\) defined in Lemma\u202f3.\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(H'\\) is the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\), the line \\(HH'\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) and is bisected by \\(MN\\).  Because \\(H\\) lies on the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) (definition of orthocentre), the line \\(PH\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  Hence \\(PH\\) and \\(HH'\\) are the same line; consequently \\(P, H, H'\\) are collinear.  \n\nMoreover, \\(PH\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\) (by construction of \\(\\ell\\)).  Therefore the line \\(PH\\) coincides with the line \\(\\ell\\) of Lemma\u202f3.  Since \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and \\(P\\) is the centre of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) (Lemma\u202f2), the only common point of \\(\\ell\\) with \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) distinct from \\(B\\) is precisely the intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  Hence \\(H'=T\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Main contradiction\n\nAssume, for the sake of contradiction, that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is **not** tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).  \nThen the line meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at a second point \\(T\\neq B\\).  \n\nFrom Lemma\u202f3 we have  \n\\[\n\\angle BTP = \\angle BAP . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f5, \\(T\\) is the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\); consequently \\(T\\) also lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  Since \\(P\\) is the centre of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) (Lemma\u202f2), the chord \\(PT\\) subtends at the centre the same angle as the chord \\(PA\\); i.e.  \n\\[\n\\angle BPA = \\angle BPT . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBut by Lemma\u202f1, \\(AP\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle BAE\\) and, because \\(PA=PE=PF\\), also of \\(\\angle BPA\\); hence  \n\\[\n\\angle BAP = \\angle BPA . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nCombining (1), (2) and (3) yields  \n\\[\n\\angle BTP = \\angle BAP = \\angle BPA = \\angle BPT .\n\\]\nThus \\(\\angle BTP = \\angle BPT\\), which is possible only when the points \\(B,T,P\\) are collinear.  Since \\(P\\) is the centre of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\), the only point of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) collinear with \\(B\\) and \\(P\\) is \\(B\\) itself.  Hence \\(T=B\\), contradicting the assumption that the line meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at a second distinct point.\n\nTherefore the supposition is false, and the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) must be tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). This completes the proof."
