            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) and radii \\(r<R\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be their two intersection points;  \n* the line \\(MN\\) meet \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* the line \\(AP\\) meet \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n* \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1.  \\(P\\) is the centre of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively, and \\(A\\) lies on both circles.  \nBecause \\(AP\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)), the powers of \\(A\\) with respect to the two circles are equal:\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AF\\cdot AD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\nBut \\(AC = AD\\) (both are chords subtended by the same central angle \\(\\angle MAN\\)), hence from (2) we obtain \\(AE = AF\\).\n\nNow \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\).  Since \\(AE=AF\\) and \\(A\\) is symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\) in the two circles, the line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(EF\\).  Consequently\n\\[\nPE = PF . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we already have \\(PA = PC\\).  Because \\(C\\) belongs to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(E\\) also belongs to \\(\\Omega\\), the centre \\(M\\) is equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(E\\); similarly \\(N\\) is equidistant from \\(D\\) and \\(F\\).  Using (1) and (3) we obtain\n\\[\nPA = PE = PF .\n\\]\nThus \\(A,E,F\\) are all at the same distance from \\(P\\); the same distance also equals \\(PB\\) because \\(B\\) has the same power with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) as \\(A\\).  Hence \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic with centre \\(P\\); i.e. \\(P\\) is the centre of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2.  In \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) the chord \\(AP\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle BPA\\) (and of \\(\\angle BAP\\)).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(P\\) is the centre of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\), the radii \\(PA, PB, PE, PF\\) are equal.  Therefore \\(\\triangle BPA\\) is isosceles with \\(PA = PB\\); consequently the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(AB\\) is also the median and the angle bisector.  The line \\(AP\\) passes through the vertex \\(A\\) and the centre \\(P\\); hence it is the internal bisector of \\(\\angle BPA\\).  By symmetry the same line bisects \\(\\angle BAP\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3.  Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nDenote by \\(T\\neq B\\) the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).  \nThen\n\\[\n\\angle BTP = \\angle BAP . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), the corresponding angles formed with the transversal \\(BT\\) are equal:\n\\[\n\\angle (BT,TP)=\\angle (BA,AP),\n\\]\ni.e. \\(\\angle BTP = \\angle BAP\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4.  In any triangle, the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circumcircle.\n\n*Proof.*  \nStandard fact: if \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) and \\(H'\\) is the reflection of \\(H\\) across side \\(YZ\\), then \\(XH' = XH\\) and \\(\\angle XH'Y = 180^{\\circ}-\\angle XZY\\); hence \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f5.  The point \\(T\\) of Lemma\u202f3 coincides with the reflection \\(H'\\) of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  By definition of orthocentre, \\(H\\) lies on this altitude; therefore \\(PH\\perp MN\\).  \nConsequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (which, by Lemma\u202f1, is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\)) must be the same line as \\(PH\\).  Hence \\(\\ell = PH\\).\n\nReflect \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\).  By Lemma\u202f4, \\(H'\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  Because \\(P\\) is the centre of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) (Lemma\u202f1), the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) exactly at the points where the line \\(PH\\) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).  The line \\(PH\\) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at \\(B\\) (since \\(B\\) lies on both circles) and at a second point, which by definition is \\(T\\).  Therefore \\(T=H'\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the contradiction\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is **not** tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).  \nThen it meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at a second point \\(T\\neq B\\).\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f3,\n\\[\n\\angle BTP = \\angle BAP . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f5, \\(T\\) is the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\); consequently \\(T\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  Since \\(P\\) is the centre of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\), the chord \\(PT\\) subtends at the centre the same angle as the chord \\(PA\\); therefore\n\\[\n\\angle BPA = \\angle BPT . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nLemma\u202f2 tells us that \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle BPA\\); hence\n\\[\n\\angle BAP = \\angle BPA . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nCombining (5), (6) and (7) we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle BTP = \\angle BAP = \\angle BPA = \\angle BPT .\n\\]\nThus \\(\\angle BTP = \\angle BPT\\), which forces the three points \\(B,T,P\\) to be collinear.\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the centre of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\), the only point of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) that is collinear with \\(B\\) and \\(P\\) is \\(B\\) itself.  Hence collinearity implies \\(T=B\\), contradicting the assumption that \\(T\\neq B\\).\n\nTherefore the original assumption is false, and the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) must be tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
