ve \\(PB = PA\\). Power equality only says \\(PA^2 - r_{\\Omega}^2 = PB^2 - r_{\\Omega}^2\\) (or analogous), which does not imply equal distances to the same point. Therefore the conclusion that \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is unfounded.\n\n*Overall assessment of Lemma\u202f1:* The lemma rests on multiple false statements; it is a **Critical Error** that invalidates all subsequent lemmas that depend on it.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f2: \u201cIn \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) the chord \\(AP\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle BPA\\) (and of \\(\\angle BAP\\)).\u201d\n\n*Critical Error:* This lemma directly uses Lemma\u202f1 (that \\(P\\) is the center of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\)). Since Lemma\u202f1 is false, the premise that \\(PA = PB\\) is not established, and the claim that \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle BPA\\) has no justification. Hence Lemma\u202f2 is invalid.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f3: Relating \\(\\angle BTP\\) and \\(\\angle BAP\\) via parallelism.\n\nThe statement \u201cBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), the corresponding angles formed with the transversal \\(BT\\) are equal\u201d is a correct geometric fact **provided** that \\(\\ell\\) indeed meets the circle at a point \\(T\\). However, the existence and definition of \\(T\\) depend on Lemma\u202f5, which is later shown to be false. Thus while the angle\u2011chasing itself is correct, the lemma is built on an undefined configuration, making it **unsupported** in the overall proof.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f4: \u201cReflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle.\u201d\n\n*Correct:* This is a standard theorem in Euclidean geometry. No issue here.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f5: Identification of \\(T\\) with the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\).\n\n1. **Statement:** \u201cIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\). By definition of orthocentre, \\(H\\) lies on this altitude; therefore \\(PH\\perp MN\\).\