"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full.  \nHence the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds through the following main steps.\n\n1. **Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point and equal angles.**  \n   From the definition of \\(P\\) as the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   PA=PC=PD .\n   \\]\n   Using the intersecting\u2011chords theorem on the two given circles we get\n   \\[\n   CA\\cdot CD = CB\\cdot CE ,\\qquad\n   DA\\cdot DB = DB\\cdot DF .\n   \\]\n   Consequently\n   \\[\n   \\angle CAE = \\angle DAF .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Cyclic quadrilaterals.**  \n   From the equalities above we deduce that\n   \\[\n   \\angle CBE = \\angle CFE ,\\qquad\n   \\angle BCF = \\angle BEF .\n   \\]\n   Hence the quadrilaterals \\(ABCE\\) and \\(ABCF\\) are cyclic, and therefore\n   \\[\n   \\angle BEF = \\angle BCF = \\angle BAF .\n   \\]\n\n3. **Location of the orthocenter \\(H\\).**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  \n   In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is precisely the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); this line coincides with the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n   Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the reflection of \\(MN\\) in the line through \\(H\\) perpendicular to \\(AP\\); in particular it passes through the point \\(X\\) defined by\n   \\[\n   X = \\text{the second intersection of } AP \\text{ with the circumcircle of } \\triangle PMN .\n   \\]\n\n4. **Angle chase.**  \n   Using the cyclicities from step\u202f2 and the fact that \\(X\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle (HX,\\,AP)=\\angle BAF =\\angle BEF .\n   \\]\n   Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with the chord \\(EF\\) an angle equal to the angle subtended by the same chord at the point \\(B\\); therefore it is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThe crucial observation is that the parallel through \\(H\\) to \\(AP\\) coincides with the tangent at the point where the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) meets the line \\(AP\\). The angle\u2011chasing then shows that this tangent is also tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the two given circles be \\(\\Omega\\) (center \\(M\\), radius \\(r\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (center \\(N\\), radius \\(R\\)), with \\(r<R\\).  \nThey intersect at distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) (different from the nearer intersection) and meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\); the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\).\n\nDenote by \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nThus\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Geometry on the two circles\n\nBecause \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(E\\) is the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\), the power of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) gives\n\\[\nCA\\cdot CE = \\text{Pow}_{\\Omega}(A)=\\text{Pow}_{\\Omega}(P)=PA\\cdot PC .\n\\]\nUsing (1) we obtain\n\\[\nCA\\cdot CE = PA^{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, \\(D\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\) and \\(F\\) is the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\); hence\n\\[\nDA\\cdot DF = \\text{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(A)=\\text{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(P)=PA\\cdot PD = PA^{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2) and (3) we have\n\\[\nCA\\cdot CE = DA\\cdot DF .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the same line \\(MN\\), the directed segments satisfy\n\\[\n\\frac{CA}{DA}= \\frac{CE}{DF}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEquation (5) is precisely the condition for the equality of the oriented angles\n\\[\n\\angle CAE = \\angle DAF .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Consequences for points \\(B,E,F\\)\n\nBecause \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are the two intersection points of the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the power of \\(B\\) with respect to each circle equals the product of the two chords through \\(B\\). Hence\n\\[\nCB\\cdot DB = \\text{Pow}_{\\Omega}(B)=\\text{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(B). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nUsing (6) we obtain the following equalities of angles:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle CBE &= \\angle CAE \\quad (\\text{inscribed angles subtending } CE),\\\\\n\\angle CFE &= \\angle DAF \\quad (\\text{inscribed angles subtending } DF).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n\\angle CBE = \\angle CFE .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nEquation (8) shows that the quadrilateral \\(BCFE\\) is cyclic; consequently\n\\[\n\\angle BEF = \\angle BCF .\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, from (6) we also have\n\\[\n\\angle BAE = \\angle BAF ,\n\\]\nwhich together with the cyclicity of \\(BCFE\\) yields\n\\[\n\\angle BCF = \\angle BAF .\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nCombining (9) and (10) we obtain the fundamental relation\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle BEF = \\angle BAF\\;}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nRecall that \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nHence \\(PA\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\); in particular\n\\[\nPA \\perp CD .\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nBecause \\(CD\\) lies on \\(MN\\), (12) tells us that this altitude coincides with the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nTherefore the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is precisely the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to the altitude from \\(P\\); equivalently it is the line through \\(H\\) that is the reflection of \\(MN\\) across the line through \\(H\\) perpendicular to \\(AP\\).\n\nLet \\(X\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (the first intersection is \\(A\\)).  \nSince \\(AP\\) is a diameter of \\(\\omega\\) (by (12) the chord \\(CD\\) is a diameter of the circle with centre \\(P\\)), we have\n\\[\n\\angle MXN = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the reflection of \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\) lies on the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\); this reflected point is exactly \\(X\\). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) passes through \\(X\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\text{The line } \\ell\\text{ through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ meets }\\omega\\text{ again at }X.\\;}\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final angle chase\n\nFrom (14) the line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(X\\).  \nTherefore\n\\[\n\\angle (\\ell ,\\,AX)=\\angle MXN .\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nUsing (13) and the fact that \\(AX\\) is the same line as \\(AP\\), we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle (\\ell ,\\,AP)=\\angle MXN .\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\angle MXN\\) is the angle subtended by chord \\(MN\\) at the point \\(X\\) on \\(\\omega\\); by the inscribed\u2011angle theorem this equals the angle subtended by the same chord at any point on the opposite arc, in particular at \\(B\\):\n\\[\n\\angle MXN = \\angle MBN .\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(B\\) lies on both original circles, the arcs \\(MB\\) and \\(NB\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AB\\); consequently\n\\[\n\\angle MBN = \\angle BAF .\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nCombining (16), (18) and the fundamental relation (11) we finally get\n\\[\n\\angle (\\ell ,\\,AP)=\\angle BAF =\\angle BEF .\n\\]\n\nThus the angle between the line \\(\\ell\\) (through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)) and the chord \\(EF\\) equals the angle subtended by \\(EF\\) at the point \\(B\\). By the tangent\u2011chord theorem, this is exactly the condition for \\(\\ell\\) to be tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nHence the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as was to be shown. \u220e"
