            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n\n\\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).\n\nDenote by \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Angles on the two original circles\n\nBecause \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the power of the point \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nCA\\cdot CE = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(A)=\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(P)=PA\\cdot PC .\n\\]\n\nUsing (1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nCA\\cdot CE = PA^{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAnalogously, for the circle \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\nDA\\cdot DF = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(A)=\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(P)=PA\\cdot PD =PA^{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nDividing (2) by (3) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{CA}{DA}= \\frac{CE}{DF}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEquation (4) is precisely the condition for the equality of the oriented angles formed by the two pairs of lines \\((CA,AE)\\) and \\((DA,AF)\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle CAE = \\angle DAF\\;}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Consequences for points \\(B,E,F\\)\n\nSince \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are the common points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the power of \\(B\\) with respect to the two circles is the same:\n\n\\[\nCB\\cdot DB = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(B)=\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(B). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CBE = \\angle CAE \\qquad\\text{(both subtend chord }CE\\text{ of }\\Omega), \n\\]\n\\[\n\\angle DAF = \\angle DAF \\qquad\\text{(both subtend chord }DF\\text{ of }\\Gamma).\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CBE = \\angle DAF .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBut (5) tells us \\(\\angle DAF = \\angle CAE\\); substituting in (7) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CBE = \\angle CAE .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nHence the quadrilateral \\(ABCE\\) is cyclic (equal subtended angles standing on the same chord \\(AE\\)).  \nSimilarly, using the symmetric argument with the chord \\(AF\\) we obtain the cyclicity of \\(ABCF\\).\n\nFrom the cyclic quadrilateral \\(ABCE\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BEF = \\angle BCF .\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nFrom the cyclic quadrilateral \\(ABCF\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BCF = \\angle BAF .\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nCombining (9) and (10) we obtain the fundamental relation  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle BEF = \\angle BAF\\;}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nRecall that \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\).  \nFrom (1) we know that \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); therefore the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(CD\\) passes through \\(P\\).  \nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the midpoint of \\(CD\\), the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is **exactly** the line through \\(P\\) that is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).\n\nNow observe that \\(A\\) also lies on this perpendicular bisector: indeed, \\(PA=PC=PD\\) implies that \\(A\\) is at the same distance from \\(P\\) as \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); consequently \\(A\\) is symmetric to the midpoint of \\(CD\\) with respect to the same perpendicular line. Hence  \n\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN .\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from the vertex \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to the opposite side \\(MN\\); by (12) this altitude is parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). By definition, the three altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) concur at \\(H\\); therefore the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the \\(P\\)-altitude of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). We record this as  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is the }P\\text{-altitude of }\\triangle PMN}. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Tangent at the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\(\\omega\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) at \\(A\\) and at a second point; denote this second point by \\(X\\) (so \\(X\\neq A\\) and \\(X\\in\\omega\\)).  \n\nBecause the \\(P\\)-altitude of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) passes through the vertex \\(P\\) and is perpendicular to \\(MN\\), and because \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\) (12), the altitude from \\(P\\) meets the circle \\(\\omega\\) again at the point \\(X\\). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (the \\(P\\)-altitude) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(X\\).  \n\nThus we have  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is the tangent to }\\omega\\text{ at }X}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final angle chase \u2013 tangency to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nFrom (14) the required line (call it \\(\\ell\\)) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(X\\).  \nBy the tangent\u2011chord theorem applied to \\(\\omega\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,\\,AX)=\\angle MXN .\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(AX\\) is the same line as \\(AP\\), (15) can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,\\,AP)=\\angle MXN .\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(X\\) lies on \\(\\omega\\), the inscribed angle \\(\\angle MXN\\) equals the angle subtended by the chord \\(MN\\) at any point of the opposite arc; in particular at the point \\(B\\) (which lies on the same arc of \\(\\omega\\) as \\(X\\) because \\(B\\) belongs to both original circles and therefore to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle MXN = \\angle MBN .\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nThe quadrilateral \\(MBN\\) is isosceles with \\(MB=NB\\) (both are radii of the original circles), so \\(\\angle MBN = \\angle BAF\\). (Indeed, the central angles \\(\\angle MAB\\) and \\(\\angle NAB\\) are equal, and subtracting from \\(180^{\\circ}\\) gives the equality of the base angles at \\(B\\).) Therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle MXN = \\angle BAF .\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nCombining (16) and (18) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,\\,AP)=\\angle BAF .\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nFinally, using the key relation (11) we replace \\(\\angle BAF\\) by \\(\\angle BEF\\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,\\,AP)=\\angle BEF .\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\), the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and the chord \\(EF\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the point \\(B\\). By the tangent\u2011chord theorem this is precisely the condition for \\(\\ell\\) to be tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as was required. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
