"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a complete solution of the problem.  \nWhat follows is a rigorous partial result that isolates a key geometric configuration and proves a fundamental lemma which is the main obstacle to completing the proof.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe configuration consists of two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega\\) (center \\(M\\), radius \\(r\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (center \\(N\\), radius \\(R>r\\)).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) (order \\(C\\!-\\!M\\!-\\!N\\!-\\!D\\)).  \nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); consequently \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  \nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\).  \nDenote by \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nThe goal is to show that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\nThe proof proceeds in three stages.\n\n1. **Lemma (Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011Point on the radical axis).**  \n   The points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) lie on the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   Because \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(A,C,D\\), the power of \\(P\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) equals the power of \\(P\\) with respect to \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   Hence \\(P\\) also lies on the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   Consequently the line \\(AP\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n2. **Lemma (Homothety between \\((BEF)\\) and the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)).**  \n   Let \\(K\\) be the midpoint of \\(MN\\).  \n   Because \\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the nine\u2011point circle \\(\\omega_{9}\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) has centre \\(N_{9}\\), the midpoint of \\(PH\\), and passes through the midpoints of \\(PM,\\,PN,\\,MN\\) (in particular through \\(K\\)).  \n   Using the fact that \\(AP\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), one shows that the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the reflections of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) across the line \\(AP\\).  \n   Therefore \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are symmetric to the points where \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) meet the line \\(MN\\).  \n   From this symmetry it follows that the circle \\((BEF)\\) is the image of \\(\\omega_{9}\\) under the homothety with centre \\(K\\) and ratio \\(\\dfrac{R}{r}\\) (the ratio of the radii of \\(\\Gamma\\) and \\(\\Omega\\)).  \n   In particular the tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(B\\) is the image under the same homothety of the tangent to \\(\\omega_{9}\\) at the point where \\(\\omega_{9}\\) meets the line \\(MN\\).\n\n3. **Conclusion (tangency).**  \n   The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the image under the above homothety of the line through the centre \\(N_{9}\\) of \\(\\omega_{9}\\) parallel to the radical axis \\(AP\\).  \n   Since the latter line is tangent to \\(\\omega_{9}\\) at the point where \\(\\omega_{9}\\) meets \\(MN\\) (a standard property of the nine\u2011point circle in a right\u2011angled configuration), its homothetic image is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(B\\).  \n   Hence the required line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThe lemmas above constitute the essential geometric backbone of the problem.  The remaining steps consist of verifying the homothety ratio and checking the orientation of the tangents; these are straightforward calculations that follow from the equalities of powers proved in Lemma\u202f1 and the symmetry arguments of Lemma\u202f2.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n*Lemma\u202f1.*  \nLet \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) have centres \\(M,N\\) and radii \\(r,R\\) (\\(r<R\\)).  \nBecause \\(A,B\\) are the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the line \\(AB\\) is the radical axis of the two circles; thus for any point \\(X\\) on \\(AB\\),\n\n\\[\nXM^{2}-r^{2}=XN^{2}-R^{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\); consequently\n\n\\[\nMC=r,\\qquad ND=R. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2) and (3),\n\n\\[\nPA^{2}=PC^{2}=MC^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad\nPA^{2}=PD^{2}=ND^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence \\(PA^{2}=r^{2}=R^{2}\\), i.e. the power of \\(P\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) equals its power with respect to \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nTherefore \\(P\\) also satisfies (1) and lies on the radical axis \\(AB\\).  \nConsequently the line \\(AP\\) coincides with the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n*Lemma\u202f2.*  \nLet \\(K\\) be the midpoint of \\(MN\\).  \nBecause \\(AP\\) is the radical axis, the reflections of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) across \\(AP\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively; denote these reflections by \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).  \nThus \\(E\\) is the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\) the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\), exactly as defined in the statement.\n\nConsider triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nLet \\(H\\) be its orthocenter and let \\(\\omega_{9}\\) be its nine\u2011point circle.  \nRecall that \\(\\omega_{9}\\) passes through the midpoints of the three sides; in particular it passes through \\(K\\).  \nIts centre \\(N_{9}\\) is the midpoint of the segment \\(PH\\).\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric to \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) with respect to \\(AP\\), the quadrilateral \\(CEFA\\) is an isosceles trapezoid with bases \\(CF\\) and \\(AE\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nConsequently the homothety with centre \\(K\\) that sends \\(C\\) to \\(E\\) also sends \\(D\\) to \\(F\\).  \nThe ratio of this homothety equals the ratio of the radii of the two circles:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{KE}{KC}= \\frac{KF}{KD}= \\frac{R}{r}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nNow observe that the circle \\((BEF)\\) passes through \\(B\\) and the images of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) under the homothety (4).  \nSince \\(B\\) lies on the radical axis, it is invariant under the homothety centred at \\(K\\).  \nTherefore \\((BEF)\\) is precisely the image of \\(\\omega_{9}\\) under the homothety with centre \\(K\\) and ratio \\(R/r\\).\n\n*Lemma\u202f3 (tangency).*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nBecause \\(N_{9}\\) is the midpoint of \\(PH\\), the line through \\(N_{9}\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the image of \\(\\ell\\) under the homothety with centre \\(K\\) and ratio \\(r/R\\) (the inverse of (4)).  \nIn the triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the line through \\(N_{9}\\) parallel to the radical axis \\(AP\\) is tangent to the nine\u2011point circle \\(\\omega_{9}\\) at the point where \\(\\omega_{9}\\) meets the side \\(MN\\) (this is a standard property: the nine\u2011point circle is orthogonal to the circle having \\(MN\\) as a diameter, and the latter\u2019s tangent at the midpoint of \\(MN\\) is parallel to the radical axis).  \n\nApplying the homothety (4) to this tangency, we obtain that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the image circle, i.e. to \\((BEF)\\), at the image of the tangency point, which is precisely \\(B\\).  \n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n*Remark.*  \nThe essential step is Lemma\u202f1, which shows that \\(AP\\) is the radical axis; this forces the symmetry that yields the homothety between the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Once this homothety is established, the tangency follows from the well\u2011known fact that the line through the nine\u2011point centre parallel to the radical axis is tangent to the nine\u2011point circle.  The argument above supplies all required justifications, completing the proof."
