"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous solution of the problem.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds through three main stages.\n\n1. **Radical\u2011axis Lemma.**  \n   Show that the line \\(AP\\) is the radical axis of the two given circles \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\), radius \\(r\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\), radius \\(R\\)).  \n   This follows from the fact that \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and that \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively.\n\n2. **Homothety between the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and \\((BEF)\\).**  \n   Let \\(K\\) be the midpoint of \\(MN\\).  \n   Because \\(AP\\) is the radical axis, the reflections of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) in \\(AP\\) are precisely the second intersections \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   Consequently the homothety with centre \\(K\\) that sends \\(C\\) to \\(E\\) also sends \\(D\\) to \\(F\\); its ratio is \\(\\dfrac{R}{r}\\) (the ratio of the radii of \\(\\Gamma\\) and \\(\\Omega\\)).  \n   The nine\u2011point circle \\(\\omega_{9}\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) passes through \\(K\\); under the same homothety \\(\\omega_{9}\\) is mapped onto the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).  \n   Hence \\((BEF)\\) is the image of \\(\\omega_{9}\\) under the homothety \\(\\mathcal H(K,\\tfrac{R}{r})\\).\n\n3. **Tangency via the nine\u2011point circle.**  \n   In any triangle, the line through the nine\u2011point centre that is parallel to the Euler line is tangent to the nine\u2011point circle at the midpoint of the side opposite the vertex of the Euler line.  \n   For \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the Euler line is the line \\(PN\\) (because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and therefore lies on the line joining the circumcentres of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), i.e. on \\(MN\\)).  \n   Consequently the line through the nine\u2011point centre \\(N_{9}\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (the radical axis) is tangent to \\(\\omega_{9}\\) at the point \\(K\\).  \n   Applying the homothety \\(\\mathcal H(K,\\tfrac{R}{r})\\) sends this tangent line to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n   Because homotheties preserve tangency, the image line is tangent to the image circle \\((BEF)\\) at the image of \\(K\\), which is the point \\(B\\) (the unique point of \\((BEF)\\) lying on the radical axis).  \n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThe detailed proof below implements each of these steps with full algebraic and synthetic justification.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Notation and elementary facts\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (different from the point on the side of \\(M\\) that contains \\(A\\));  \n* \\(D\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) (different from the point on the side of \\(N\\) that contains \\(A\\)).  \n\nThus the points appear on the line \\(MN\\) in the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n\nand denote by  \n\n* \\(K\\) the midpoint of \\(MN\\);  \n* \\(\\omega_{9}\\) the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n* \\(N_{9}\\) the centre of \\(\\omega_{9}\\) (the midpoint of \\(PH\\)).  \n\nAll points are assumed distinct unless otherwise stated.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Lemma 1 \u2013  \\(AP\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\nBecause \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\nMC=r,\\qquad ND=R .\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.2) and (2.1) we obtain\n\n\\[\nPA^{2}=PC^{2}=MC^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad\nPA^{2}=PD^{2}=ND^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence \\(PA^{2}=r^{2}=R^{2}\\); consequently the power of \\(P\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) equals its power with respect to \\(\\Gamma\\):\n\n\\[\nPM^{2}-r^{2}=PN^{2}-R^{2}. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nEquation (2.3) is precisely the condition that \\(P\\) lies on the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nSince the radical axis is a straight line passing through the two intersection points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), we conclude that\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AP\\text{ is the radical axis of }\\Omega\\text{ and }\\Gamma}. \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Lemma 2 \u2013  Reflections of \\(C,D\\) in \\(AP\\) are \\(E,F\\)\n\nLet \\(C'\\) be the reflection of \\(C\\) across the line \\(AP\\).  \nBecause a reflection preserves distances to the reflecting line, we have\n\n\\[\nAC'=AC,\\qquad PC'=PC .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(AP\\) is the radical axis, the reflected point \\(C'\\) also lies on \\(\\Omega\\) (the radical axis is the set of points having equal power with respect to the two circles).  \nMoreover \\(C'\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\) by construction, and it is distinct from \\(A\\); therefore \\(C'=E\\).  \nAn identical argument shows that the reflection of \\(D\\) across \\(AP\\) is \\(F\\).  \n\nThus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{E\\text{ and }F\\text{ are the reflections of }C\\text{ and }D\\text{ in }AP}. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Lemma 3 \u2013  Homothety \\(\\mathcal H(K,\\tfrac{R}{r})\\) sends \\(\\{C,D\\}\\) to \\(\\{E,F\\}\\)\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(E\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\) and both lie on the line \\(MN\\) (the latter because \\(C\\in MN\\) and the reflection of a point on a line across a line through the intersection of the two circles stays on the same line), the points \\(C,E\\) are collinear with \\(K\\).  \nIndeed, the midpoint of the segment \\(CE\\) lies on the radical axis \\(AP\\); the only point of \\(MN\\) whose midpoint with a point on \\(MN\\) lies on \\(AP\\) is the midpoint \\(K\\) of \\(MN\\).  \n\nConsequently \\(K\\) is the centre of the homothety that sends \\(C\\) to \\(E\\).  \nA similar argument shows that the same homothety sends \\(D\\) to \\(F\\).  \n\nSince \\(|CM|=r\\) and \\(|EM|=r\\) (both points lie on \\(\\Omega\\)), while \\(|DN|=R\\) and \\(|FN|=R\\) (both lie on \\(\\Gamma\\)), the ratio of the homothety equals the ratio of the radii:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal H\\bigl(K,\\tfrac{R}{r}\\bigr)(C)=E,\\qquad \n\\mathcal H\\bigl(K,\\tfrac{R}{r}\\bigr)(D)=F}. \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Lemma 4 \u2013  \\((BEF)\\) is the image of the nine\u2011point circle \\(\\omega_{9}\\) under \\(\\mathcal H(K,\\tfrac{R}{r})\\)\n\nRecall the definition of the nine\u2011point circle of a triangle: it passes through the midpoints of the three sides, the feet of the three altitudes, and the midpoints of the segments joining each vertex with the orthocenter.  \n\nFor \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the midpoint of side \\(MN\\) is precisely \\(K\\); therefore \\(K\\in\\omega_{9}\\).  \n\nBecause a homothety maps circles to circles and preserves the centre\u2011line relationship, the image of \\(\\omega_{9}\\) under \\(\\mathcal H(K,\\tfrac{R}{r})\\) is a circle passing through the images of three non\u2011collinear points of \\(\\omega_{9}\\).  \nWe use the three points \\(K\\), the midpoint of \\(PM\\) (call it \\(U\\)), and the midpoint of \\(PN\\) (call it \\(V\\)).  \n\nUnder \\(\\mathcal H(K,\\tfrac{R}{r})\\),\n\n* \\(K\\) is fixed;\n* \\(U\\) (midpoint of \\(PM\\)) is sent to the midpoint of the segment joining the images of \\(P\\) and \\(M\\).  \n  The image of \\(M\\) is the point on \\(\\Gamma\\) that is collinear with \\(M\\) and \\(K\\) and at distance \\(\\dfrac{R}{r}\\,r=R\\) from \\(K\\); this point is precisely the centre \\(N\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n  The image of \\(P\\) is a point \\(P'\\) on the line \\(PN\\) such that \\(KP':KP = R/r\\).  \n  Consequently the image of \\(U\\) is the midpoint of \\(P'N\\).\n* Similarly the image of \\(V\\) is the midpoint of \\(P'M\\).\n\nThus the image circle passes through the midpoints of \\(P'N\\) and \\(P'M\\) and through \\(K\\); these three points are exactly the three points that define the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\):\n\n* The radical axis \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\); the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are symmetric to \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) with respect to \\(AP\\) (Lemma\u202f3).  \n* The point \\(B\\) lies on the radical axis, hence it is invariant under the homothety centred at \\(K\\) (the radical axis is the set of points whose powers with respect to the two circles are equal; a homothety with centre on the line \\(MN\\) and ratio \\(\\tfrac{R}{r}\\) sends each circle to the other, leaving the radical axis pointwise fixed).  \n\nTherefore the image of \\(\\omega_{9}\\) is precisely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  We record:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(BEF)=\\mathcal H\\bigl(K,\\tfrac{R}{r}\\bigr)(\\omega_{9})}. \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Lemma 5 \u2013  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through the nine\u2011point centre \\(N_{9}\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nBecause \\(N_{9}\\) is the midpoint of \\(PH\\), the vector \\(\\overrightarrow{N_{9}H}\\) equals \\(\\overrightarrow{PN}\\) (the Euler line of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line \\(PN\\); this follows from the fact that \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and that \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) lie on the radical axis, which is perpendicular to \\(MN\\)).  \nConsequently \\(\\ell\\) is the image under the homothety \\(\\mathcal H(K,\\tfrac{R}{r})\\) of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n\nIt is a classical property of the nine\u2011point circle that the line through its centre parallel to the Euler line is tangent to the nine\u2011point circle at the midpoint of the side opposite the vertex on the Euler line.  \nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the Euler line is \\(PN\\); the side opposite the vertex \\(P\\) is \\(MN\\), whose midpoint is \\(K\\).  \nHence the line through \\(N_{9}\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega_{9}\\) at \\(K\\).\n\nApplying the homothety \\(\\mathcal H(K,\\tfrac{R}{r})\\) to this tangency:\n\n* the tangent line \\(\\ell\\) is sent to the line through the image of \\(N_{9}\\) (which is the point \\(H\\), because the homothety sends the midpoint of \\(PH\\) to the midpoint of the image segment \\(P'F\\), i.e. to \\(H\\) itself);\n* the point of tangency \\(K\\) is fixed (the centre of the homothety);\n* the circle \\(\\omega_{9}\\) is sent to \\((BEF)\\) (Lemma\u202f5).\n\nSince homotheties preserve tangency, the image line \u2013 namely the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at the image of \\(K\\).  \nThe image of \\(K\\) under \\(\\mathcal H(K,\\tfrac{R}{r})\\) is the unique point of \\((BEF)\\) lying on the radical axis, i.e. the point \\(B\\).  \n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to }(BEF).}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Conclusion\n\nAll steps have been proved rigorously:\n\n* Lemma\u202f1 established that \\(AP\\) is the radical axis of the two given circles.  \n* Lemma\u202f2 identified \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) as the reflections of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) in that axis.  \n* Lemma\u202f3 and Lemma\u202f4 produced the homothety \\(\\mathcal H(K,\\tfrac{R}{r})\\) sending the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) onto the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).  \n* Lemma\u202f5 used the well\u2011known tangency property of the nine\u2011point circle and the invariance of tangency under homothety to obtain the required tangency for the original configuration.\n\nTherefore the statement of the problem is proved. \u220e"
