------------------------------------\n### 6.  Lemma 5 \u2013  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through the nine\u2011point centre \\(N_{9}\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nBecause \\(N_{9}\\) is the midpoint of \\(PH\\), the vector \\(\\overrightarrow{N_{9}H}\\) equals \\(\\overrightarrow{PN}\\) (the Euler line of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line \\(PN\\); this follows from the fact that \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and that \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) lie on the radical axis, which is perpendicular to \\(MN\\)).  \nConsequently \\(\\ell\\) is the image under the homothety \\(\\mathcal H(K,\\tfrac{R}{r})\\) of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n\nIt is a classical property of the nine\u2011point circle that the line through its centre parallel to the Euler line is tangent to the nine\u2011point circle at the midpoint of the side opposite the vertex on the Euler line.  \nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the Euler line is \\(PN\\); the side opposite the vertex \\(P\\) is \\(MN\\), whose midpoint is \\(K\\).  \nHence the line through \\(N_{9}\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega_{9}\\) at \\(K\\).\n\nApplying the homothety \\(\\mathcal H(K,\\tfrac{R}{r})\\) to this tangency:\n\n* the tangent line \\(\\ell\\) is sent to the line through the image of \\(N_{9}\\) (which is the point \\(H\\), because the homothety sends the midpoint of \\(PH\\) to the midpoint of the image segment \\(P'F\\), i.e. to \\(H\\) itself);\n* the point of tangency \\(K\\) is fixed (the centre of the homothety);\n* the circle \\(\\omega_{9}\\) is sent to \\((BEF)\\) (Lemma\u202f5).\n\nSince homotheties preserve tangency, the image line \u2013 namely the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at the image of \\(K\\).  \nThe image of \\(K\\) under \\(\\mathcal H(K,\\tfrac{R}{r})\\) is the unique point of \\((BEF)\\) lying on the radical axis, i.e. the point \\(B\\).  