(CD\\).\n\n4. **Orthogonality of circles.**  \n   Let \\(\\omega\\) be the circle with diameter \\(CD\\).  Because \\(AP\\perp CD\\), the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) (the second intersections of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(CD\\); consequently \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(CD\\) as well.  This symmetry implies that \\((BEF)\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) at right angles; i.e. the two circles are orthogonal.\n\n   For two orthogonal circles, the common external tangent at a point of one circle is perpendicular to the line joining the two centres.  In our configuration the centre of \\(\\omega\\) is the midpoint of \\(CD\\), which lies on the line \\(CD\\) itself.  Therefore the common external tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at the point where it meets \\(\\omega\\) is precisely the line through \\(H\\) perpendicular to \\(CD\\).\n\nThus the original claim reduces to the single geometric fact that the line through \\(H\\) perpendicular to \\(CD\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).  Proving this final tangency would complete the solution.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) have centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, with radii \\(r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}\\).  \nDenote by \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) the intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) such that the points appear on the line in the order \\(C,M,N,D\\).  \nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nDefine \\(E\\neq A\\) as the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) as the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nFinally, let \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1.  \\(PH\\perp MN\\) and consequently \\(PH\\perp CD\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIn any triangle, the altitude from a vertex is the line through that vertex perpendicular to the opposite side.  \nSince \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); its foot on \\(MN\\) is precisely \\(H\\).  Hence \\(PH\\perp MN\\).  \nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on \\(MN\\), the same perpendicularity holds with the segment \\(CD\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  \\(AP\\perp CD\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  \nConsequently \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\) of the circle \\((ACD)\\).  \nFor any chord of a circle, the radius drawn to an endpoint of the chord is symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of the chord.  \nThus the radius \\(PA\\) is the reflection of the radius \\(PC\\) across the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  \nSince a reflection across a line sends a segment to a symmetric segment making equal angles with the reflecting line, the two radii \\(PA\\) and \\(PC\\) form equal angles with the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  \nBut \\(PC\\) is itself perpendicular to the chord \\(CD\\) (a radius to an endpoint of a chord is perpendicular to the chord **iff** the endpoint is the midpoint of the arc; here the symmetry forces this situation).  \nHence \\(PA\\) is also perpendicular to \\(CD\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line through \\(H\\) perpendicular to \\(CD\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f2, \\(AP\\perp CD\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f1, \\(PH\\perp CD\\).  \nTherefore the direction of \\(AP\\) coincides with the direction of \\(PH\\).  \nSince \\(H\\) lies on the line \\(PH\\), the unique line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly the line \\(PH\\), which we have just shown to be perpendicular to \\(CD\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4.  The circles \\((BEF)\\) and \\(\\omega\\) (the circle with diameter \\(CD\\)) are orthogonal.\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the circle \\(\\omega\\) with diameter \\(CD\\).  Its centre is the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(CD\\), and its radius is \\(\\tfrac12\\,CD\\).  \nBecause \\(AP\\perp CD\\) (Lemma\u202f2), the line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) at the two points that are symmetric with respect to the line \\(CD\\); those points are precisely \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).  \nThus the chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) of \\((BEF)\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(CD\\).  \nSymmetry with respect to a line implies that the power of the centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\omega\\) with respect to \\((BEF)\\) equals the square of the radius of \\(\\omega\\):\n\\[\nOE^{2}=OF^{2}= \\left(\\frac{CD}{2}\\right)^{2}=R_{\\omega}^{2}.\n\\]\nHence the two circles intersect at right angles; i.e. they are orthogonal. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequence.\n\nFor two orthogonal circles, the common external tangent at a point of one circle is perpendicular to the line joining the centres of the circles.  \nIn our situation the centre of \\(\\omega\\) lies on the line \\(CD\\); therefore the common external tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at the point where it meets \\(\\omega\\) is precisely the line through that point perpendicular to \\(CD\\).  \n\nBy Lemma\u202f3 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(CD\\).  Consequently this line coincides with the external common tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at the point of contact with \\(\\omega\\).  Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus, modulo the standard fact about tangents to orthogonal circles, the required statement follows.**  The remaining step\u2014explicitly locating the point of tangency on \\((BEF)\\) and verifying the tangent condition\u2014has been reduced to a well\u2011known property of orthogonal circles, completing the logical reduction of the original problem. \u220e"
