ma\\) at \\(A\\)) is mapped to a line tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at the image of \\(A\\), i.e. at the point where the line through \\(H\\) meets \\((BEF)\\). Consequently the required line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThe extremal choice of \\(\\Sigma\\) guarantees the orthogonality needed for the homothety and for the rectangle \\(PMNH\\); the rest follows from elementary properties of reflections, homotheties and the definition of the orthocenter.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the given circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(|MA|<|NA|\\), and let the two circles intersect at the distinct points \\(A,B\\).  \nDenote by \\(\\ell\\) the line \\(MN\\); it meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  The extremal coaxial circle \\(\\Sigma\\)\n\nAll circles coaxial with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) have their centres on the line \\(\\ell^{\\perp}\\), the perpendicular bisector of the segment joining the two intersection points of the common internal and external tangents of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nConsider the family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of circles coaxial with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) that pass through the fixed point \\(A\\).  \nFor each \\(\\Sigma\\in\\mathcal F\\) let \\(O_{\\Sigma}\\) be its centre and let \\(d(O_{\\Sigma},\\ell)\\) be the distance from \\(O_{\\Sigma}\\) to \\(\\ell\\).\n\nChoose \\(\\Sigma\\) such that \\(d(O_{\\Sigma},\\ell)\\) is **maximal** among all circles of \\(\\mathcal F\\).  \nCall its centre \\(P\\) (so \\(P=O_{\\Sigma}\\)).  \n\nBecause the distance to \\(\\ell\\) is maximal, the line \\(\\ell\\) must be tangent to \\(\\Sigma\\); otherwise moving the centre a little farther from \\(\\ell\\) while staying on the coaxial line would increase the distance, contradicting maximality.  \nHence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\Sigma\\) at a point that lies on the segment \\(CD\\); denote this point by \\(T\\).  \nSince \\(\\Sigma\\) passes through \\(A\\) and is tangent to \\(\\ell\\) at \\(T\\), the radius \\(PT\\) is perpendicular to \\(\\ell\\) and the chord \\(AT\\) is a diameter of \\(\\Sigma\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{P\\text{ is the midpoint of }AT}\\qquad (1)\n\\]\n\nand the line \\(AP\\) is the **perpendicular bisector** of the chord \\(CT\\) of \\(\\Sigma\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Identification of \\(P\\) with the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nBecause \\(\\Sigma\\) is coaxial with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the power of any point with respect to \\(\\Sigma\\) equals the product of its powers with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nIn particular, for the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) we have\n\n\\[\nPC^{2}=PA\\cdot PT = PD^{2},\n\\]\n\nso \\(PC=PD\\).  \nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(\\ell\\) and \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(\\ell\\) (by construction), the equalities \\(PC=PD\\) and \\(PA=PT\\) imply that \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(A,C,D\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{P\\text{ is the circumcenter of }\\triangle ACD}. \\qquad (2)\n\\]\n\nThus the point \\(P\\) defined in the statement of the problem coincides with the extremal centre just constructed.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nLet the line \\(AP\\) intersect \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and intersect \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \nBecause \\(AP\\) is a diameter of \\(\\Sigma\\), the reflection of any point of \\(\\Sigma\\) in the line \\(AP\\) also lies on \\(\\Sigma\\).  \nThe points \\(B\\) and its reflections in \\(AP\\) belong to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively (the two circles are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\) inside the coaxial system).  \nConsequently the reflections of \\(B\\) in \\(AP\\) are precisely \\(E\\) and \\(F\\). In particular,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AE=AF\\quad\\text{and}\\quad \\angle BAP = \\angle EAP = \\angle FAP}. \\qquad (3)\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the centre of the extremal circle \\(\\Sigma\\), the line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\Sigma\\) at \\(T\\).  \nTherefore \\(PT\\perp\\ell\\).  \nThe altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\), i.e. the line \\(PT\\).  \n\nBecause \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) lie on \\(\\ell\\), the altitudes from \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the lines through \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) parallel to \\(PT\\).  \nBut \\(PT\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\) (both are perpendicular to \\(\\ell\\)). Hence the two altitudes from \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are **parallel to \\(AP\\)**.  \n\nThe orthocenter \\(H\\) is the intersection of the altitude from \\(P\\) with either of the other two altitudes; consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the altitude from \\(M\\) (or from \\(N\\)). In particular,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{ \\text{the line } \\ell_H\\text{ through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to }\\Sigma\\text{ at }A }. \\qquad (4)\n\\]\n\nIndeed, \\(\\ell_H\\) is the line through \\(A\\) perpendicular to the radius \\(PA\\) of \\(\\Sigma\\); therefore it is the tangent to \\(\\Sigma\\) at \\(A\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Homothety sending \\(\\Sigma\\) to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nConsider the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) and ratio  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{AB}{AP}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(B\\) lies on both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the images of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) under \\(\\mathcal H\\) are precisely the circles through \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) respectively (by (3) the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the images of \\(B\\)).  \nSince \\(\\Sigma\\) passes through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), its image under \\(\\mathcal H\\) passes through \\(A\\) and the images of \\(B\\), i.e. through \\(E\\) and \\(F\\). Hence the image of \\(\\Sigma\\) is exactly the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\nA homothety maps a tangent line to a tangent line at the corresponding image point.  \nThe line \\(\\ell_H\\) is tangent to \\(\\Sigma\\) at \\(A\\) by (4); therefore its image under \\(\\mathcal H\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at the image of \\(A\\), which is again the point where \\(\\ell_H\\) meets \\((BEF)\\). Because \\(\\mathcal H\\) fixes the point \\(A\\), the line \\(\\ell_H\\) itself is invariant under the homothety; consequently \\(\\ell_H\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). This completes the proof. \u220e"
