P=\\angle EAP=\\angle FAP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(\\ell\\); by (1) this line is \\(PT\\).  \nSince \\(M,N\\in\\ell\\), the altitudes from \\(M\\) and from \\(N\\) are the lines through \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) parallel to \\(PT\\).  \nBut \\(PT\\parallel AP\\) (both are perpendicular to \\(\\ell\\)); therefore the two latter altitudes are **parallel to \\(AP\\)**.\n\nThe orthocenter \\(H\\) is the intersection of the altitude from \\(P\\) with either of the other two altitudes; consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the altitude from \\(M\\) (or from \\(N\\)).  \nBecause \\(PA\\) is a radius of \\(\\Sigma\\), the line through \\(A\\) perpendicular to \\(PA\\) is the tangent to \\(\\Sigma\\) at \\(A\\).  \nSince the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) passes through \\(A\\) and is perpendicular to \\(PA\\), we have\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{the line } \\ell_H\\text{ through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is the tangent to }\\Sigma\\text{ at }A}. \\qquad (3)\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Homothety sending \\(\\Sigma\\) onto \\((BEF)\\)\n\nConsider the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) and ratio  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{AB}{AP}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(B\\) lies on both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the images of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) under \\(\\mathcal H\\) are the circles through \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) respectively (by (2) the points \\(E,F\\) are precisely the images of \\(B\\)).  \nSince \\(\\Sigma\\) passes through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), its image under \\(\\mathcal H\\) passes through \\(A\\) and the images of \\(B\\), i.e. through \\(E\\) and \\(F\\). Hence the image of \\(\\Sigma\\) is exactly the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\nA homothety maps a tangent line