rthogonal.\n\nLet \\(O\\) be the centre of \\(\\omega\\) (the midpoint of \\(CD\\)).  \nSince \\(E,F\\in\\omega\\), we have \\(OE=OF=R_{\\omega}\\).  \nThe power of \\(O\\) with respect to \\(\\Gamma_{BEF}\\) equals\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma_{BEF}}(O)=OE^{2}=R_{\\omega}^{2}.\n\\]\nBy definition, two circles are orthogonal iff the power of the centre of one with respect to the other equals the square of the radius of the first.  Hence \\(\\omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma_{BEF}\\) intersect orthogonally. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f5.  The external common tangent to \\(\\Gamma_{BEF}\\) that is perpendicular to \\(CD\\) passes through \\(H\\).\n\nFor two orthogonal circles the line joining their centres is the radical axis of the pair of circles obtained by inversion in either circle; a classical consequence (see any text on circle geometry) is:\n\n> **Fact.** If circles \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) are orthogonal, then the external tangent to \\(\\beta\\) at any point of \\(\\alpha\\) is perpendicular to the line joining the centres of \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\).\n\nApply this fact with \\(\\alpha=\\omega\\) and \\(\\beta=\\Gamma_{BEF}\\).  \nThe centre of \\(\\omega\\) is \\(O\\) and lies on the line \\(CD\\); therefore the external tangent to \\(\\Gamma_{BEF}\\) that is perpendicular to \\(CD\\) is exactly the line through the point of tangency that is orthogonal to \\(CD\\).\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 the line \\(PH\\) is perpendicular to \\(CD\\) and meets \\(\\omega\\) at the point \\(X\\) where the altitude from \\(P\\) meets the diameter \\(CD\\).  Consequently \\(PH\\) is the external tangent to \\(\\Gamma_{BEF}\\) at \\(X\\).  Since \\(H\\) lies on \\(PH\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (which we have already identified with \\(PH\\)) is this tangent. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line \\(PH\\); by Lemma\u202f5 this line is the external tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Hence the required tangency holds.\n\n\u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
