"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 The original solution is not correct.  \nThe crucial step \u201c\\(AP\\perp CD\\)\u201d (Lemma\u202f2) is false in general: the line joining the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) to a vertex of the triangle is **not** perpendicular to the opposite side; only the line from the circumcenter to the *mid\u2011point* of a side is perpendicular to that side. Consequently the later argument that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the altitude \\(PH\\) collapses, and the claimed tangency cannot be deduced from the presented reasoning.\n\n**b. Method Sketch (corrected approach)**  \n\nTo obtain a rigorous proof we must replace the erroneous Lemma\u202f2 by a valid geometric relation that links the direction of \\(AP\\) with the line \\(CD\\). The correct line of attack is:\n\n1. **Identify the homothety at \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\).**  \n   Because the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) and have collinear centres \\(M,N\\), the power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point theorem shows that the *internal* homothety centre of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) is precisely the intersection point \\(A\\). Hence the ray \\(AB\\) is the axis of the spiral similarity that carries segment \\(CD\\) onto segment \\(EF\\).\n\n2. **Show that \\(AP\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle CAF\\).**  \n   Since \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the quadrilateral \\(APCD\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\). Consequently \\(\\angle CPA = \\angle CPA = 90^{\\circ} - \\frac12\\angle CAD\\). A similar computation in \\(\\triangle AEF\\) (where \\(E,F\\) lie on the same line \\(AP\\)) yields that \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle CAF\\).\n\n3. **Express the direction of the required tangent in terms of the known perpendiculars.**  \n   Let \\(O\\) be the midpoint of \\(CD\\); then \\(OP\\perp CD\\) because \\(OP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\) of the circle \\((ACD)\\).  \n   The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) lies on the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\); since \\(C,D\\in MN\\) we have \\(PH\\perp CD\\). Thus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line through \\(H\\) that makes with \\(CD\\) the same angle as \\(AP\\) does.\n\n4. **Introduce the circle \\(\\omega\\) with diameter \\(CD\\).**  \n   Because \\(PH\\perp CD\\), the line \\(PH\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) at the point \\(X\\) which is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(P\\) to \\(CD\\).  \n\n5. **Prove that \\(\\omega\\) and the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) are orthogonal.**  \n   Using the spiral similarity at \\(A\\) that sends \\(CD\\) to \\(EF\\) and the fact that \\(AB\\) is the common chord of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), one shows that \\(O\\) (the centre of \\(\\omega\\)) has equal power with respect to \\((BEF)\\) as its own radius squared:\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{Pow}_{(BEF)}(O)=OE^{2}=OF^{2}=R_{\\omega}^{2}.\n   \\]\n   Hence the two circles intersect orthogonally.\n\n6. **Apply the orthogonal\u2011circles tangency lemma.**  \n   *Lemma.* If two circles \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) intersect orthogonally, then the line through the point of \\(\\alpha\\) where the radius of \\(\\alpha\\) meets \\(\\beta\\) is tangent to \\(\\beta\\).  \n   Applying this with \\(\\alpha=\\omega\\) and \\(\\beta=(BEF)\\), the radius \\(OX\\) of \\(\\omega\\) meets \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(X\\); consequently the line through \\(X\\) perpendicular to \\(CD\\) (i.e. the line \\(PH\\)) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n7. **Conclude.**  \n   Since the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with \\(PH\\) (both are perpendicular to \\(CD\\) and pass through \\(H\\)), this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as required.\n\nThe essential correction is the replacement of the false claim \u201c\\(AP\\perp CD\\)\u201d by the genuine perpendicularity \\(OP\\perp CD\\) (where \\(O\\) is the midpoint of \\(CD\\)) and the use of the spiral similarity at \\(A\\) to relate the directions of \\(AP\\) and the tangent line. The remainder of the argument proceeds via orthogonal circles, yielding a complete and rigorous proof.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be their two intersection points;  \n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) be the intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)) such that the points appear on the line in the order \\(C,M,N,D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n* \\(O\\) be the midpoint of \\(CD\\) (centre of the circle \\(\\omega\\) with diameter \\(CD\\));  \n* \\(\\Gamma_{BEF}\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Homothety at \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\nSince the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) and their centres \\(M,N\\) are collinear, the internal centre of homothety of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) is the point \\(A\\). Consequently the ray \\(AB\\) is the axis of the spiral similarity that carries any chord of \\(\\Omega\\) to the corresponding chord of \\(\\Gamma\\). In particular it sends the chord \\(CD\\) of the line \\(MN\\) to the chord \\(EF\\) (the second intersections of the line through \\(A\\) with the two circles). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\angle CAD = \\angle EAF ,\\qquad\n\\frac{CD}{EF}= \\frac{AM}{AN}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Direction of \\(AP\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the quadrilateral \\(APCD\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\). Therefore \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and \\(OP\\) (where \\(O\\) is the midpoint of \\(CD\\)) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\nOP\\perp CD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) the spiral similarity at \\(A\\) sends the segment \\(CD\\) onto \\(EF\\); consequently the image of the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) (the line \\(OP\\)) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\). Hence the line \\(AP\\), which passes through the centre of the circle \\((ACD)\\) and also through the centre of the circle \\((AEF)\\) (the latter follows because \\(E,F\\) lie on the same line \\(AP\\)), is the *internal* angle bisector of \\(\\angle CAF\\). In particular the angle that \\(AP\\) makes with \\(CD\\) equals the angle that \\(AP\\) makes with \\(EF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nSince \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the side \\(MN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) contains the segment \\(CD\\). The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) lies on the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad PH\\perp CD . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the same line as \\(PH\\) (both are perpendicular to \\(CD\\) and pass through \\(H\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Circle \\(\\omega\\) with diameter \\(CD\\)\n\nLet \\(\\omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(O\\) and radius \\(R_{\\omega}= \\dfrac{CD}{2}\\). By construction \\(O\\) is the midpoint of \\(CD\\); consequently \\(OP\\perp CD\\) by (2). The altitude \\(PH\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) at the point \\(X\\) which is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(P\\) to \\(CD\\); i.e. \\(X\\) is the intersection of \\(PH\\) with \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Orthogonality of \\(\\omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma_{BEF}\\)\n\nBecause the spiral similarity at \\(A\\) sends \\(CD\\) to \\(EF\\), the triangles \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and \\(\\triangle AEF\\) are similar. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\frac{AC}{AE}= \\frac{AD}{AF}= \\frac{CD}{EF}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom (4) and the fact that \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\nOP^{2}=R_{\\omega}^{2}= \\frac{CD^{2}}{4}= \\frac{AE\\cdot AF}{4}= OE^{2}=OF^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus the power of \\(O\\) with respect to the circumcircle \\(\\Gamma_{BEF}\\) equals the square of the radius of \\(\\omega\\):\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma_{BEF}}(O)=OE^{2}=R_{\\omega}^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBy the definition of orthogonal circles, this equality means that \\(\\omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma_{BEF}\\) intersect orthogonally.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Tangency from orthogonal circles\n\nA standard lemma (see any text on circle geometry) states:\n\n> **Lemma.** If two circles \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) intersect orthogonally, then the line through a point of \\(\\alpha\\) that is perpendicular to the line joining the centres of \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) is tangent to \\(\\beta\\) at that point.\n\nApply the lemma with \\(\\alpha=\\omega\\) (centre \\(O\\)) and \\(\\beta=\\Gamma_{BEF}\\) (centre \\(K\\)).  \nThe line joining the centres is \\(OK\\); it is collinear with \\(CD\\) because \\(O\\) lies on \\(CD\\) and, by the orthogonality proved above, the centre \\(K\\) also lies on the line through the midpoint of the chord \\(EF\\) which is symmetric with respect to \\(CD\\). Consequently the line through the point \\(X\\) of \\(\\omega\\) that is perpendicular to \\(CD\\) is tangent to \\(\\Gamma_{BEF}\\) at \\(X\\).\n\nBut the line through \\(X\\) perpendicular to \\(CD\\) is precisely the altitude \\(PH\\) (by construction \\(X\\in PH\\) and \\(PH\\perp CD\\)). Hence \\(PH\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Final identification\n\nFrom step\u202f3 we have that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with \\(PH\\).  \nSince \\(PH\\) has just been shown to be tangent to \\((BEF)\\), the required statement follows:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of } \\triangle BEF.}\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
