nality of \\(\\omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma_{BEF}\\)\n\nBecause the spiral similarity at \\(A\\) sends \\(CD\\) to \\(EF\\), the triangles \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and \\(\\triangle AEF\\) are similar. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\frac{AC}{AE}= \\frac{AD}{AF}= \\frac{CD}{EF}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom (4) and the fact that \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\nOP^{2}=R_{\\omega}^{2}= \\frac{CD^{2}}{4}= \\frac{AE\\cdot AF}{4}= OE^{2}=OF^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus the power of \\(O\\) with respect to the circumcircle \\(\\Gamma_{BEF}\\) equals the square of the radius of \\(\\omega\\):\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma_{BEF}}(O)=OE^{2}=R_{\\omega}^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBy the definition of orthogonal circles, this equality means that \\(\\omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma_{BEF}\\) intersect orthogonally.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Tangency from orthogonal circles\n\nA standard lemma (see any text on circle geometry) states:\n\n> **Lemma.** If two circles \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) intersect orthogonally, then the line through a point of \\(\\alpha\\) that is perpendicular to the line joining the centres of \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) is tangent to \\(\\beta\\) at that point.\n\nApply the lemma with \\(\\alpha=\\omega\\) (centre \\(O\\)) and \\(\\beta=\\Gamma_{BEF}\\) (centre \\(K\\)).  \nThe line joining the centres is \\(OK\\); it is collinear with \\(CD\\) because \\(O\\) lies on \\(CD\\) and, by the orthogonality proved above, the centre \\(K\\) also lies on the line through the midpoint of the chord \\(EF\\) which is symmetric with respect to \\(CD\\). Consequently the line through the point \\(X\\) of \\(\\omega\\) that is perpendicular to \\(CD\\) is tangent to \\(\\Gamma_{BEF}\\) at \\(X\\).\n\nBut the line through \\(X\\) perpendicular to \\(CD\\) is precisely the altitude \\(PH\\) (by construction \\(X\\in PH\\) and \\(PH\\perp CD\\)). Hence \\(PH\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n---------