            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be their two intersection points;  \n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) be the intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)) such that the points appear on the line in the order \\(C,M,N,D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n* \\(O\\) be the midpoint of \\(CD\\) (centre of the circle \\(\\omega\\) with diameter \\(CD\\));  \n* \\(\\Gamma_{BEF}\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The similarity centre at \\(A\\)\n\nFor two intersecting circles the two points of intersection are precisely the two centres of similarity.  \nIndeed, let \\(X\\) be any point on \\(\\Omega\\). The lines \\(AX\\) and \\(AB\\) meet \\(\\Gamma\\) again at points \\(Y\\) and \\(B\\) respectively; the triangles \\(AXM\\) and \\(AYN\\) are similar (they share the angle at \\(A\\) and have the ratios of corresponding sides equal to the radii). Hence the homothety that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) has centre \\(A\\). The same argument with the other intersection point gives the second centre \\(B\\).\n\nConsequently the ray \\(AB\\) is the axis of the **spiral similarity** that carries any chord of \\(\\Omega\\) onto the corresponding chord of \\(\\Gamma\\). In particular it sends the chord \\(CD\\) (the intersection of the line of centres with the two circles) onto the chord \\(EF\\) (the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with the circles). Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle (CD,AP)=\\angle (EF,AP)}\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\boxed{\\frac{CD}{EF}= \\frac{AM}{AN}}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Geometry of the triangle \\(PMN\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment joining \\(P\\) to the midpoint \\(O\\) of the chord \\(CD\\) is the perpendicular bisector of that chord; thus\n\n\\[\nOP\\perp CD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) lies on the altitude from \\(P\\) to the side \\(MN\\). Because the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) belong to the line \\(MN\\), this altitude is precisely the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(CD\\); hence\n\n\\[\nPH\\perp CD . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(\\angle (AP,CD)=\\angle (AP,EF)\\). Combining this with (3) gives\n\n\\[\n\\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ coincides with }PH. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The circle \\(\\omega\\) with diameter \\(CD\\)\n\nLet \\(\\omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(O\\) and radius \\(R_{\\omega}=CD/2\\). By construction the altitude \\(PH\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) at the foot \\(X\\) of the perpendicular from \\(P\\) to \\(CD\\); thus \\(X\\in PH\\) and \\(OX\\perp CD\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Orthogonality of \\(\\omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma_{BEF}\\)\n\nBecause the spiral similarity at \\(A\\) sends \\(CD\\) to \\(EF\\), the triangles \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and \\(\\triangle AEF\\) are directly similar; consequently\n\n\\[\n\\frac{CD}{EF}= \\frac{AC}{AE}= \\frac{AD}{AF}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIn the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\). Using the power of the point \\(O\\) with respect to this circle we obtain\n\n\\[\nOP^{2}=R_{\\omega}^{2}= \\frac{CD^{2}}{4}= \\frac{AE\\cdot AF}{4}= OE^{2}=OF^{2}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEquality (6) shows that the power of \\(O\\) with respect to the circumcircle \\(\\Gamma_{BEF}\\) equals the square of the radius of \\(\\omega\\):\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma_{BEF}}(O)=OE^{2}=R_{\\omega}^{2}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nHence the two circles \\(\\omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma_{BEF}\\) intersect **orthogonally**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Tangency from orthogonal circles\n\n**Lemma (orthogonal\u2011circles tangency).**  \nLet two circles \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) intersect orthogonally. If a radius of \\(\\alpha\\) meets \\(\\beta\\) at a point \\(X\\), then the line through \\(X\\) that is perpendicular to the common chord of the two circles is tangent to \\(\\beta\\) at \\(X\\).\n\n*Proof of the lemma.*  \nLet \\(O_{\\alpha},O_{\\beta}\\) be the centres and let \\(r_{\\alpha},r_{\\beta}\\) be the radii. Orthogonality gives \\(O_{\\alpha}X^{2}=r_{\\alpha}^{2}\\) and \\(O_{\\beta}X^{2}=r_{\\beta}^{2}\\). The right\u2011angled triangle \\(O_{\\alpha}XO_{\\beta}\\) shows that the line \\(O_{\\alpha}X\\) is perpendicular to the tangent to \\(\\beta\\) at \\(X\\); consequently the line through \\(X\\) perpendicular to the chord \\(O_{\\alpha}O_{\\beta}\\) (which is the same as the common chord of the two circles) is the required tangent. \u220e\n\nApply the lemma with \\(\\alpha=\\omega\\) (centre \\(O\\)) and \\(\\beta=\\Gamma_{BEF}\\). The radius \\(OX\\) of \\(\\omega\\) meets \\(\\Gamma_{BEF}\\) at the point \\(X\\); therefore the line through \\(X\\) perpendicular to the chord \\(CD\\) (which is the line \\(OX\\) itself) is tangent to \\(\\Gamma_{BEF}\\). Because \\(X\\) lies on the altitude \\(PH\\), we obtain\n\n\\[\nPH\\text{ is tangent to }\\Gamma_{BEF}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Final step\n\nFrom (4) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly the line \\(PH\\). By (8) this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). This is precisely the statement to be proved.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF.}\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
