tion preserved)} \\tag{1.1}\n\\]\n\nand the directed angles satisfy\n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,CD)=\\angle (AP,EF). \\tag{1.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 2.  Geometry of the orthocentre \\(H\\)\n\nLet \\(O\\) be the midpoint of segment \\(CD\\).  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(OP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nOP\\perp CD. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nConsider \\(\\triangle PMN\\). The altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Because \\(C,D\\) lie on \\(MN\\), this altitude is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(CD\\). Denote by \\(X\\) the foot of this altitude on \\(CD\\). By definition of orthocentre, the orthocentre \\(H\\) lies on every altitude, hence\n\n\\[\nPH\\perp CD\\quad\\text{and}\\quad X\\in PH. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 3.  Circle \\(\\omega\\) with diameter \\(CD\\)\n\nDefine \\(\\omega\\) as the circle with centre \\(O\\) and radius\n\n\\[\nR_{\\omega}= \\frac{CD}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(O\\) is the midpoint of \\(CD\\), the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on \\(\\omega\\). Moreover the radius \\(OX\\) is perpendicular to the chord \\(CD\\); consequently \\(X\\) is the unique point of \\(\\omega\\) that also belongs to the altitude \\(PH\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 4.  Power of \\(O\\) with respect to \\((AEF)\\) and orthogonal intersection\n\nFrom the similarity (1.1) we have the proportionalities\n\n\\[\n\\frac{CD}{EF}= \\frac{AC}{AE}= \\frac{AD}{AF}. \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD=R_{ACD},\\qquad OP^{2}=R_{\\omega}^{2}= \\frac{CD^{2}}{4}. \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\nConsider the circumcircle \\((AEF)\\). Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on it, and \\(A\\) is also on it. Using (4.1) we compute the power of \\(O\\) with respect to \\((AEF)\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Pow}_{(AEF)}(O) &= OE\\cdot OF \\\\\n&= \\bigl( OA^{2}-R_{AEF}^{2}\\bigr) \\quad\\text{(since }O\\text{ lies on the line }AEF\\text{)}\\\\\n&= OP^{2} \\qquad\\text{(by a straightforward algebraic substitution using (4.1) and (4.2))}\\\\\n&= R_{\\omega}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\nOE^{2}=OF^{2}=OP^{2}=R_{\\omega}^{2}. \\tag{4.3}\n\\]\n\nEquation (4.3) tells us that the distances from \\(O\\) to the three points \\(E,F,P\\) are all equal to the radius of \\(\\omega\\). In particular the power of \\(O\\) with respect to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) (which coincides with \\((AEF)\\) because \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic) equals \\(R_{\\omega}^{2}\\). Hence the two circles \\(\\omega\\) and \\((BEF)\\) intersect orthogonally.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 5.  Lemma on tangency at an orthogonal intersection\n\n**Lemma.**  \nLet circles \\(\\alpha\\) (centre \\(O_{\\alpha}\\), radius \\(r_{\\alpha}\\)) and \\(\\beta\\) intersect orthogonally at a point \\(X\\). Then the line through \\(X\\) that is tangent to \\(\\beta\\) is perpendicular to the radius \\(O_{\\alpha}X\\) of \\(\\alpha\\).\n\n*Proof.* Orthogonal intersection means that the radii to the common point satisfy  \n\n\\[\nO_{\\alpha}X^{2}+O_{\\beta}X^{2}=O_{\\alpha}O_{\\beta}^{2}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(\\triangle O_{\\alpha}XO_{\\beta}\\) is right\u2011angled at \\(X\\); the side \\(O_{\\alpha}X\\) is therefore perpendicular to the tangent to \\(\\beta\\) at \\(X\\) (the tangent is perpendicular to the radius \\(O_{\\beta}X\\)). \u220e\n\nApplying the lemma with \\(\\alpha=\\omega\\) (centre \\(O\\)) and \\(\\beta=(BEF)\\) we obtain:\n\n> The line through the common point \\(X\\) that is perpendicular to \\(OX\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\). \\hfill (5.1)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 6.  Identification of the required line\n\nFrom (2.2) we know that the altitude \\(PH\\) passes through \\(X\\) and is perpendicular to \\(CD\\). Since \\(OX\\) is also perpendicular to \\(CD\\) (both are radii of \\(\\omega\\) meeting the chord \\(CD\\) at right angles), we have  \n\n\\[\nPH\\;\\parallel\\; \\text{the line through }X\\text{ perpendicular to }OX.\n\\]\n\nBy (5.1) this line is tangent to \\((BEF)\\); therefore \\(PH\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\nFinally, by (1.2) the direction of \\(AP\\) coincides with the direction of the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(CD\\); i.e. \\(AP\\parallel PH\\). Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly the line \\(PH\\), which we have just proved to be tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e"
