rpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\) we compute the power of \\(O\\) with respect to \\((AEF)\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{Pow}_{(AEF)}(O)=OE\\cdot OF=OE^{2}=OP^{2}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality (6) means that the line through \\(O\\) tangent to \\((AEF)\\) is exactly the line through \\(O\\) perpendicular to \\(OP\\); that line meets \\((AEF)\\) at the two points where the circle \\(\\omega\\) meets \\((AEF)\\). One of those intersection points is \\(X\\).\n\n6. **Orthogonal\u2011intersection lemma.**  \n\n   *Lemma.* If two circles intersect orthogonally at a point \\(X\\), then the tangent to either circle at \\(X\\) is perpendicular to the radius of the other circle drawn to \\(X\\).\n\n   *Proof.* Orthogonality gives \\(O_{1}X^{2}+O_{2}X^{2}=O_{1}O_{2}^{2}\\); thus \\(\\triangle O_{1}XO_{2}\\) is right\u2011angled at \\(X\\). The tangent to the circle centred at \\(O_{2}\\) is perpendicular to \\(O_{2}X\\); by the right angle it is also perpendicular to \\(O_{1}X\\). \u220e\n\n   Applying the lemma with \\(\\alpha=\\omega\\) (centre \\(O\\)) and \\(\\beta=(AEF)\\) we obtain:\n\n   > The line through \\(X\\) perpendicular to \\(OX\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((AEF)\\). \\hfill (T)\n\n7. **From \\((AEF)\\) to \\((BEF)\\).**  \n   The quadrilateral \\(AEBF\\) is cyclic because the spiral similarity at \\(A\\) sends the chord \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) onto the chord \\(AF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) and fixes the line \\(AB\\). Hence the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle AEF\\) coincides with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Consequently the tangency in (T) also holds for \\((BEF)\\).\n\n8. **Identification of the required line.**  \n   From (3) we have \\(PH\\parallel OP\\). Since \\(OP\\perp CD\\) and \\(OX\\perp CD\\) (by definition of \\(X\\) as the foot of the altitude), the line through \\(X\\) perpendicular to \\(OX\\) coincides with the line \\(PH\\). By (T) this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n   Finally we show that