            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega(M,r)\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,R)\\) be the given circles with \\(R>r\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) their two intersection points;  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points where the line \\(MN\\) meets the circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Spiral similarity at \\(A\\)\n\nThe circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect, so there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) that carries \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nBecause \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\), \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends the chord \\(CD\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) onto the chord \\(EF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\triangle ACD\\sim\\triangle AEF ,\\qquad\n\\frac{CD}{EF}= \\frac{AC}{AE}= \\frac{AD}{AF}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The point \\(O\\) and the circle \\(\\omega\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nO:=\\text{midpoint of }CD ,\\qquad\n\\omega:=\\text{circle centred at }O\\text{ with radius }OX,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(X\\) is defined later.  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD\\quad\\text{and}\\quad OP\\perp CD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe side \\(MN\\) contains the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); therefore the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) coincides with the altitude from \\(P\\) to the line \\(CD\\).  \nDenote by  \n\n\\[\nX:=PH\\cap CD\n\\]\n\nthe foot of this altitude. Then  \n\n\\[\nPH\\perp CD,\\qquad X\\in CD . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2) and (3) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nOP\\parallel PH . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(X\\) is the unique point of \\(\\omega\\) that lies on the altitude \\(PH\\); in particular \\(OX\\perp CD\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Power of \\(O\\) with respect to \\((AEF)\\)\n\nFrom the similarity (1) we have the proportionality constant  \n\n\\[\nk:=\\frac{EF}{CD}= \\frac{AE}{AC}= \\frac{AF}{AD}>0 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(PA=PC=PD\\), the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(OP\\); thus \\(OP\\) is also the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(EF\\) of the circumcircle \\((AEF)\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nOE=OF . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nUsing (6) we compute the power of \\(O\\) with respect to \\((AEF)\\):\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{(AEF)}(O)=OE\\cdot OF=OE^{2}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(OE\\) is the distance from \\(O\\) to the centre of \\((AEF)\\) measured along the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\); this distance equals \\(OP\\) because both \\(O\\) and the centre of \\((AEF)\\) lie on the same line \\(OP\\). Therefore  \n\n\\[\nOE=OP\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\operatorname{Pow}_{(AEF)}(O)=OP^{2}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nEquality (8) means that the line through \\(O\\) tangent to \\((AEF)\\) is perpendicular to \\(OP\\); that tangent meets \\((AEF)\\) at the two points where the circle \\(\\omega\\) meets \\((AEF)\\). One of those intersection points is \\(X\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Orthogonal\u2011intersection lemma\n\n**Lemma.**  \nIf two circles intersect orthogonally at a point \\(X\\), then the tangent to either circle at \\(X\\) is perpendicular to the radius of the other circle drawn to \\(X\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet the circles have centres \\(O_{1},O_{2}\\) and radii \\(r_{1},r_{2}\\). Orthogonality gives  \n\n\\[\nO_{1}X^{2}+O_{2}X^{2}=O_{1}O_{2}^{2},\n\\]\n\nso \\(\\triangle O_{1}XO_{2}\\) is right\u2011angled at \\(X\\). The tangent to the circle centred at \\(O_{2}\\) is perpendicular to \\(O_{2}X\\); by the right angle it is also perpendicular to \\(O_{1}X\\). \u220e\n\nApplying the lemma with \\(\\alpha=\\omega\\) (centre \\(O\\)) and \\(\\beta=(AEF)\\) we obtain:\n\n> The line through the common point \\(X\\) that is perpendicular to \\(OX\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((AEF)\\). \\hfill (9)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  From \\((AEF)\\) to \\((BEF)\\)\n\nThe spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) centred at \\(A\\) sends the chord \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) onto the chord \\(AF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) and fixes the line \\(AB\\). Hence the quadrilateral \\(AEBF\\) is cyclic; consequently the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle AEF\\) coincides with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Therefore the tangency statement (9) also holds for the circle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Identification of the required line\n\nFrom (4) we have \\(PH\\parallel OP\\). Since both \\(OP\\) and