            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega(M,r)\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,R)\\) be the given circles with \\(R>r\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) their two intersection points;  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points where the line \\(MN\\) meets the circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the two circles intersect, there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nSince \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\), \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends the chord \\(CD\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) onto the chord \\(EF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\triangle ACD\\sim\\triangle AEF ,\\qquad\n\\frac{CD}{EF}= \\frac{AC}{AE}= \\frac{AD}{AF}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. The midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(CD\\) and the altitude from \\(P\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nO:=\\text{midpoint of }CD .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(OP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nOP\\perp CD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the side \\(MN\\) contains the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); consequently the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) coincides with the altitude from \\(P\\) to the line \\(CD\\). Let  \n\n\\[\nX:=PH\\cap CD\n\\]\n\nbe the foot of this altitude. Then  \n\n\\[\nPH\\perp CD,\\qquad X\\in CD . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2) and (3) we deduce  \n\n\\[\nOP\\parallel PH . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. The auxiliary circle \\(\\omega\\)\n\nLet \\(\\omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(O\\) and radius \\(OX\\;(= \\tfrac{CD}{2})\\). By construction \\(X\\) lies on \\(\\omega\\) and, because \\(PH\\perp CD\\) and \\(OP\\perp CD\\), we also have  \n\n\\[\nOX\\perp CD . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. \\(OP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\)\n\nFrom (1) set  \n\n\\[\nk:=\\frac{EF}{CD}= \\frac{AE}{AC}= \\frac{AF}{AD}>0 . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(PA=PC=PD\\), the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(OP\\); the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) preserves this symmetry, so the image chord \\(EF\\) is also symmetric with respect to \\(OP\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nOP\\text{ is the perpendicular bisector of }EF . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIn particular  \n\n\\[\nOE=OF . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Power of \\(O\\) with respect to \\((AEF)\\)\n\nLet \\(U\\) be the centre of the circumcircle \\((AEF)\\). Because the perpendicular bisector of a chord passes through the centre, (7) implies that \\(U\\) lies on the line \\(OP\\). Moreover \\(OU=OP\\). Using (8),\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{(AEF)}(O)=OE\\cdot OF=OE^{2}=OU^{2}=OP^{2}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus the line through \\(O\\) tangent to \\((AEF)\\) is perpendicular to \\(OP\\). This tangent meets \\((AEF)\\) at the two intersection points of \\(\\omega\\) with \\((AEF)\\); one of them is \\(X\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Orthogonal\u2011intersection lemma\n\n*Lemma.* If two circles intersect orthogonally at a point \\(X\\), then the tangent to either circle at \\(X\\) is perpendicular to the radius of the other circle drawn to \\(X\\).\n\n*Proof.* Let the circles have centres \\(O_{1},O_{2}\\) and radii \\(r_{1},r_{2}\\). Orthogonality gives  \n\\(O_{1}X^{2}+O_{2}X^{2}=O_{1}O_{2}^{2}\\); therefore \\(\\triangle O_{1}XO_{2}\\) is right\u2011angled at \\(X\\). The tangent to the circle centred at \\(O_{2}\\) is perpendicular to \\(O_{2}X\\); by the right angle it is also perpendicular to \\(O_{1}X\\). \u220e\n\nApplying the lemma with \\(\\alpha=\\omega\\) (centre \\(O\\)) and \\(\\beta=(AEF)\\) we obtain:\n\n> The line through the common point \\(X\\) that is perpendicular to \\(OX\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((AEF)\\). \\hfill (10)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7. From \\((AEF)\\) to \\((BEF)\\)\n\nThe spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes the line \\(AB\\) and sends the chord \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) onto the chord \\(AF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently the quadrilateral \\(AEBF\\) is cyclic. Hence the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle AEF\\) coincides with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Therefore the tangency in (10) also holds for the circle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8. The required tangent line\n\nFrom (4) we have \\(PH\\parallel OP\\). Because both \\(OP\\) and \\(OX\\) are perpendicular to \\(CD\\), the line through \\(X\\) perpendicular to \\(OX\\) coincides with the line \\(PH\\). By (10) this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nIt remains to identify this line with \u201cthe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\u201d.  \n\n* The line \\(OP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  \n* Under the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\), the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is mapped to the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\).  \n* The perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\) is precisely the line \\(AP\\) because \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle AEF\\).  \n\nThus \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends any line parallel to \\(OP\\) onto a line parallel to \\(AP\\). Since \\(PH\\parallel OP\\), its image under \\(\\mathcal S\\) is a line through the image of \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal S\\) (the similarity fixes the three vertices \\(P,M,N\\) and therefore fixes the whole triangle and its orthocenter). Hence the image of \\(H\\) is again \\(H\\). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly the line \\(PH\\).\n\nTherefore **the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)**, as was to be shown. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
