            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega(M,r)\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,R)\\) be the two circles with \\(R>r\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) their two intersection points;  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points where the line \\(MN\\) meets the circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the circles intersect, there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nBy definition  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal S(E)=F .\n\\]\n\nHence the oriented triangles \\(ACD\\) and \\(AEF\\) are similar; explicitly\n\n\\[\n\\frac{AC}{AE}= \\frac{AD}{AF}= \\frac{CD}{EF}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nMoreover \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes the line \\(AB\\) (the common chord of the two circles). Consequently the image of the chord \\(AE\\) is the chord \\(AF\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB=\\angle AFB . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nTogether with the obvious equality \\(\\angle ABE=\\angle ABE\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB+\\angle ABE=\\angle AFB+\\angle ABE,\n\\]\n\nwhich shows that the quadrilateral \\(A,E,B,F\\) is cyclic. Thus\n\n\\[\n(AEF)=(BEF). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Mid\u2011point of \\(CD\\) and the altitude from \\(P\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nO:=\\text{midpoint of }CD .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(OP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nOP\\perp CD . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the side \\(MN\\) contains the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); consequently the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) coincides with the altitude from \\(P\\) to the line \\(CD\\). Denote by  \n\n\\[\nX:=PH\\cap CD\n\\]\n\nthe foot of this altitude. Then  \n\n\\[\nPH\\perp CD,\\qquad X\\in CD . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFrom (4) and (5) we deduce  \n\n\\[\nOP\\parallel PH . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Image of the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\)\n\nThe spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends the segment \\(CD\\) to the segment \\(EF\\); therefore it sends the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) to the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\).  \nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the line \\(OP\\); denote by \\(U\\) the centre of the circumcircle \\((AEF)\\). Because the perpendicular bisector of a chord passes through the centre, the image of \\(OP\\) is precisely the line \\(AU\\).  \n\nFrom the similarity (1) we have \\(\\triangle ACD\\sim\\triangle AEF\\) with the same orientation, hence the image of the circumcentre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) under \\(\\mathcal S\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle AEF\\); i.e. \\(\\mathcal S(P)=U\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(OP)=AU . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBut the line \\(AU\\) is exactly the line \\(AP\\) (both pass through \\(A\\) and \\(U\\) lies on \\(AP\\) because \\(U\\) is the centre of the circle \\((AEF)\\) whose diameter is \\(AP\\) by construction). Hence  \n\n\\[\nAP\\ \\text{is the perpendicular bisector of }EF . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nIn particular  \n\n\\[\nAE=AF,\\qquad AP\\perp EF . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. A circle orthogonal to \\((AEF)\\)\n\nConsider the circle \\(\\omega\\) with centre \\(O\\) and radius  \n\n\\[\nOX=\\frac{CD}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBy construction \\(X\\) belongs to \\(\\omega\\) and, because \\(OX\\perp CD\\), the circles \\(\\omega\\) and \\((AEF)\\) intersect orthogonally at \\(X\\).  \n\n**Lemma (orthogonal\u2011intersection).**  \nIf two circles intersect orthogonally at a point \\(X\\), then the tangent to either circle at \\(X\\) is perpendicular to the radius of the other circle drawn to \\(X\\).\n\n*Proof.* Let the circles have centres \\(O_{1},O_{2}\\) and radii \\(r_{1},r_{2}\\). Orthogonality gives \\(O_{1}X^{2}+O_{2}X^{2}=O_{1}O_{2}^{2}\\); thus \\(\\triangle O_{1}XO_{2}\\) is right\u2011angled at \\(X\\). The tangent to the circle centred at \\(O_{2}\\) is perpendicular to \\(O_{2}X\\); by the right angle it is also perpendicular to \\(O_{1}X\\). \u220e\n\nApplying the lemma to \\(\\omega\\) (centre \\(O\\)) and \\((AEF)\\) we obtain:\n\n> The line through the common point \\(X\\) that is perpendicular to \\(OX\\) is tangent to \\((AEF)\\). \\hfill (10)\n\nBecause \\(OX\\perp CD\\) and \\(PH\\parallel OP\\parallel OX^{\\perp}\\) by (6), the line \\(PH\\) is exactly the line described in (10). Hence\n\n\\[\nPH\\ \\text{is tangent to the circumcircle }(AEF). \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. From \\((AEF)\\) to \\((BEF)\\)\n\nEquation (3) tells us that \\((AEF)=(BEF)\\). Therefore the tangency proved in (11) also holds for the circle \\((BEF)\\):\n\n\\[\nPH\\ \\text{is tangent to }(BEF). \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Identification of the required line\n\nFrom (6) we have \\(PH\\parallel OP\\). By (7) the image of any line parallel to \\(OP\\) under the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) is a line parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n\nThe similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes the three points \\(P,M,N\\): indeed, \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) lie on the axis of the similarity (the line \\(MN\\) is invariant because it is the common radical axis of the two circles), and \\(P\\) is the centre of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), which is mapped onto the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle AEF\\); the centre of the latter is precisely the image of \\(P\\). Hence \\(\\mathcal S\\) leaves the triangle \\(PMN\\) invariant and consequently its orthocenter \\(H\\) is also invariant.  \n\nTherefore the image of the line \\(PH\\) under \\(\\mathcal S\\) is the line through the (fixed) point \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\). Because the image of \\(PH\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) (by (12)), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the statement required in the problem is proved. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
