o\u202fACD is unjustified; the correct similarity is between\u202fACD and\u202fADF. Therefore (1) is not established.  \n- **Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 the similarity statement is false.\n\n### Step 4 \u2013 Angle equality \u2220AEB = \u2220AFB\n> \u201cConsequently the image of the chord\u202fAE is the chord\u202fAF; therefore \u2220AEB = \u2220AFB. (2)\u201d\n\n- **Analysis:** The argument assumes that \ud835\udcae fixes line\u202fAB and maps chord\u202fAE to\u202fAF, which has not been proved. Moreover, even if the mapping were true, the equality of those angles requires a careful argument about oriented angles under a similarity, which is omitted.  \n- **Classification:** **Justification Gap** \u2013 missing rigorous justification.\n\n### Step 5 \u2013 Cyclicity of A,E,B,F\n> \u201cTogether with the obvious equality \u2026 we obtain \u2026 quadrilateral\u202fA,E,B,F is cyclic. Thus (AEF) = (BEF). (3)\u201d\n\n- **Analysis:** The condition for four points to be cyclic is that a pair of opposite angles sum to 180\u00b0. The proof only shows \u2220AEB = \u2220AFB, which does not guarantee cyclicity; the correct condition would be \u2220AEB = \u2220AFB **and** the points are distinct and not collinear. The step is insufficient.  \n- **Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 the cyclicity claim is not justified.\n\n### Step 6 \u2013 Definition of O and perpendicular bisector\n> \u201cLet O be the midpoint of\u202fCD. Since\u202fP is the circumcentre of\u202f\u0394ACD, the segment\u202fOP is the perpendicular bisector of\u202fCD; therefore OP \u27c2 CD. (4)\u201d\n\n- **Analysis:** In a triangle, the line joining the circumcentre to the midpoint of a side is indeed perpendicular to that side. However, this property must be proved (it follows from the fact that the circumcentre is equidistant from the endpoints of the side). The solution does not provide this proof, but the statement is true.  \n- **Classification:** **Justification Gap** \u2013 the perpendicularity is asserted without proof.\n\n### Step 7 \u2013 Altitude from P to MN coincides with altitude to CD\n> \u201cIn\u202f\u0394PMN the side\u202fMN contains the points\u202fC and\u202fD; consequently the altitude from\u202fP to\u202fMN coincides with the altitude from\u202fP to the line\u202fCD. Denote X = PH \u2229 CD \u2026 (5).\u201d\n\n- **Analysis:** Since\u202fC and\u202fD lie on\u202fMN, the line\u202fMN is the same as line\u202fCD. Thus the altitude from\u202fP to\u202fMN is indeed the altitude to\u202fCD, and the foot X lies on\u202fCD. This is correct.  \n- **Classification:** **Correct**.\n\n### Step 8 \u2013 Parallelism OP \u2225 PH\n> \u201cFrom (4) and (5) we deduce OP \u2225 PH. (6)\u201d\n\n- **Analysis:** Both OP and PH are perpendicular to CD, so they are parallel. This follows logically from the previous steps.  \n- **Classification:** **Correct**, assuming (4) and (5) are valid.\n\n### Step 9 \u2013 Image of perpendicular bisector under \ud835\udcae\n> \u201cThe spiral similarity\u202f\ud835\udcae sends the segment\u202fCD to\u202fEF; therefore it sends the perpendicular bisector of\u202fCD to the perpendicular bisector of\u202fEF. \u2026 Consequently\u202f\ud835\udcae(OP)=AU. (7)\u201d\n\n- **Analysis:** A spiral similarity does not, in general, map a line that is not through its centre to another line that is the perpendicular bisector of the image segment. The perpendicular bisector of\u202fCD does **not** pass through\u202fA, so its image under \ud835\udcae is not guaranteed to be a perpendicular bisector. This claim is false.  \n- **Classification:** **Critical Error**.\n\n### Step 10 \u2013 Claim that AP is the perpendicular bisector of\u202fEF\n> \u201cBut the line\u202fAU is exactly the line\u202fAP \u2026 Hence AP is the perpendicular bisector of\u202fEF. (8)\u201d\n\n- **Analysis:** The identification of\u202fAU with\u202fAP is unsupported; the centre\u202fU of (AEF) does not necessarily lie on\u202fAP, and even if it did