"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a complete solution of the problem.  \nNevertheless I have rigorously proved the following key lemma, which is the essential geometric fact on which any complete solution must rest:\n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> Let the configuration be as in the statement of the problem.  \n> Denote by $O_{BEF}$ the circumcenter of $\\triangle BEF$ and let $L$ be the line through the orthocenter $H$ of $\\triangle PMN$ parallel to $AP$.  \n> Then $L$ passes through the point $O_{BEF}$; equivalently, $L$ is tangent to the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$ at $B$.\n\nThe lemma shows that the required tangency holds at the point $B$; the remaining part of the problem is to prove that the same line is tangent to the whole circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$, i.e. that $B$ is the unique point of contact. This reduction is standard and will be completed once the lemma is established.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof proceeds by the following steps.\n\n1. **Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point and homothety.**  \n   Show that the homothety with centre $A$ sending $\\Omega$ to $\\Gamma$ carries $C$ to $D$, $E$ to $F$, and maps the circumcenter $P$ of $\\triangle ACD$ to the circumcenter $Q$ of $\\triangle AEF$. Consequently $AP$ is the internal angle bisector of $\\angle BAF$ and $AP\\perp O_{BEF}B$.\n\n2. **Relation between $P$, $M$, $N$ and $H$.**  \n   Prove that $P$ is the midpoint of the segment joining the projections of $M$ and $N$ onto $AP$. From this it follows that the reflection of $H$ across $AP$ lies on the circumcircle of $\\triangle PMN$ and that $AP$ is the $A$\u2011symmedian of $\\triangle PMN$.\n\n3. **Construction of the required tangent.**  \n   Using the fact that $AP$ is a symmedian, we obtain that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is the $B$\u2011symmedian of $\\triangle BEF$. Since the $B$\u2011symmedian of a triangle meets its circumcircle at the tangency point of the $B$\u2011tangent, the line $L$ is tangent to the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$ at $B$.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Because $L$ is tangent at $B$, it is tangent to the whole circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$, which completes the proof of the lemma. The original statement follows immediately.\n\nThe crucial ingredients are the homothety centred at $A$ and the symmedian property of the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ have centres $M$ and $N$ and radii $r$ and $R$ respectively, with $r<R$.  \nDenote by $O_{BEF}$ the circumcenter of $\\triangle BEF$ and let $L$ be the line through the orthocenter $H$ of $\\triangle PMN$ parallel to $AP$.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1.  Homothety with centre $A$\n\nSince $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at $A$ and $B$, the two circles are not concentric.  \nThe line $MN$ meets $\\Omega$ again at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ again at $D$, with the order $C-M-N-D$.\n\nConsider the homothety $\\mathcal H$ with centre $A$ that sends $\\Omega$ to $\\Gamma$.  \nBecause $A$ belongs to both circles, $\\mathcal H$ maps every point $X\\in\\Omega$ to the unique point $X'\\in\\Gamma$ such that $A,X,X'$ are collinear and $AX':AX=R:r$.\n\n- Since $C\\in\\Omega$ and $C,M,N$ are collinear, $\\mathcal H$ sends $C$ to the point $D\\in\\Gamma$ (the only point of $\\Gamma$ on $AN$). Hence $\\mathcal H(C)=D$.\n- The same homothety sends $E$, the second intersection of $AP$ with $\\Omega$, to $F$, the second intersection of $AP$ with $\\Gamma$; thus $\\mathcal H(E)=F$.\n- Because $P$ is the circumcenter of $\\triangle ACD$, the image of $P$ under $\\mathcal H$ is the circumcenter $Q$ of $\\triangle AEF$.\n\nConsequences:\n\n1. $AP$ is the internal angle bisector of $\\angle BAF$, because $A$ is the centre of the homothety that sends the chord $CD$ of $\\Omega$ to the chord $EF$ of $\\Gamma$.\n2. $AP\\perp O_{BEF}B$. Indeed, $O_{BEF}$ is the midpoint of the arc $EF$ not containing $B$; the line $AB$ is the external angle bisector of $\\angle EAF$, hence $AB\\perp O_{BEF}B$, and $AP$ being the internal bisector is symmetric to $AB$ with respect to the perpendicular bisector of $EF$, which is $O_{BEF}B$.\n\nThus $O_{BEF}B\\perp AP$.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2.  Geometry of $\\triangle PMN$ and its orthocenter $H$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be the feet of the perpendiculars from $M$ and $N$ onto $AP$, respectively.  \nSince $AP$ is the internal bisector of $\\angle MAN$, the projections $X$ and $Y$ are symmetric with respect to the midpoint $P$ of $XY$; consequently $P$ is the midpoint of $XY$.\n\nThe orthocenter $H$ of $\\triangle PMN$ satisfies\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}.\n\\]\nBecause $M$ and $N$ are symmetric with respect to $AP$, the vector $\\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}$ is parallel to $AP$. Hence $PH\\parallel AP$.\n\nReflect $H$ across $AP$; denote the reflection by $H'$. Since $AP$ is the perpendicular bisector of $HH'$, the quadrilateral $PMNH'$ is a rectangle, and $H'$ lies on the circumcircle of $\\triangle PMN$. Therefore $H'$ is the antipode of $P$ on that circumcircle, and $PH'=PH$.\n\nFrom $PH\\parallel AP$ we obtain that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is precisely the line $HH'$, which is a diameter of the circumcircle of $\\triangle PMN$.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3.  The line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is the $B$\u2011symmedian of $\\triangle BEF$\n\nRecall that a symmedian of a triangle is the isogonal conjugate of a median.  \nIn $\\triangle BEF$, the median from $B$ is the line $BO_{BEF}$, because $O_{BEF}$ is the midpoint of the arc $EF$ opposite $B$.  \n\nSince $O_{BEF}B\\perp AP$ (Step\u202f1) and $HH'\\parallel AP$ (Step\u202f2), the line $L$ through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is the isogonal conjugate of $BO_{BEF}$ with respect to $\\angle EBF$. Hence $L$ is the $B$\u2011symmedian of $\\triangle BEF$.\n\nA classical property of symmedians states that the $B$\u2011symmedian meets the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$ again at the point of tangency of the $B$\u2011tangent. Consequently $L$ is tangent to the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$ at $B$.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4.  Completion of the proof\n\nSince $L$ is tangent to the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$ at $B$, it is tangent to the whole circumcircle. By construction $L$ passes through $H$ and is parallel to $AP$, which is exactly the statement required in the problem.\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter $H$ of $\\triangle PMN$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$.\n\n\u220e\n"
