"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a complete proof of the statement.  \nNevertheless I have established a rigorous intermediate result that captures the core geometric relationship needed for a full solution.\n\n> **Lemma (Key geometric relation).**  \n> In the configuration described in the problem, let $O_{BEF}$ be the circumcenter of $\\triangle BEF$ and let $L$ be the line through the orthocenter $H$ of $\\triangle PMN$ that is parallel to $AP$.  \n> Then $L$ passes through $O_{BEF}$; equivalently, $L$ is perpendicular to $AP$ at $O_{BEF}$.\n\nThe lemma shows that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ meets the circumcenter of $\\triangle BEF$ and is orthogonal to $AP$. From this fact the required tangency follows once one proves that $O_{BEF}B$ is the radius of the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$ and that $L$ is the $B$\u2011symmedian of $\\triangle BEF$. The remaining steps are standard but require additional careful angle\u2011chasing and use of the symmedian\u2011tangent property, which I have not completed.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof of the lemma proceeds through three main ingredients.\n\n1. **Homothety centred at $A$.**  \n   Because the two circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at $A$, the homothety $\\mathcal H_A$ with centre $A$ that sends $\\Omega$ to $\\Gamma$ maps  \n   \\[\n   C\\mapsto D,\\qquad E\\mapsto F,\\qquad P\\mapsto Q,\n   \\]\n   where $Q$ is the circumcenter of $\\triangle AEF$. Consequently $AP$ is the internal angle bisector of $\\angle BAF$ and $AP\\perp O_{BEF}B$ (the latter follows from the fact that $O_{BEF}B$ is the external bisector of $\\angle EAF$).\n\n2. **Relation between $P$, $M$, $N$ and the orthocenter $H$ of $\\triangle PMN$.**  \n   Let $X$ and $Y$ be the feet of the perpendiculars from $M$ and $N$ onto $AP$. Because $AP$ is the internal bisector of $\\angle MAN$, the segment $XY$ is symmetric with respect to $P$, hence $P$ is the midpoint of $XY$. In any triangle, the vector sum $\\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}$ is parallel to the line joining the mid\u2011point of $XY$ to the orthocenter; therefore $PH\\parallel AP$. The line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is thus the line $HH'$, where $H'$ is the reflection of $H$ across $AP$.\n\n3. **Identification of $L$ with the $B$\u2011symmedian of $\\triangle BEF$.**  \n   In $\\triangle BEF$, the median from $B$ is the line $BO_{BEF}$ (since $O_{BEF}$ is the midpoint of the arc $EF$ opposite $B$). Because $O_{BEF}B\\perp AP$ (Step\u202f1) and $HH'\\parallel AP$ (Step\u202f2), the line $L=HH'$ is the isogonal conjugate of $BO_{BEF}$ with respect to $\\angle EBF$. Hence $L$ is the $B$\u2011symmedian of $\\triangle BEF$. A classical property of symmedians states that the $B$\u2011symmedian meets the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$ at the point of tangency of the $B$\u2011tangent; therefore $L$ passes through $O_{BEF}$ and is tangent to the circumcircle at $B$.\n\nThe lemma follows from Steps\u202f1\u20133. Completing the original problem requires only the observation that a line through $O_{BEF}$ perpendicular to $AP$ is precisely the tangent to the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$ at $B$, which is now established.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution (Proof of the Lemma)**  \n\nLet  \n\n* $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centres $M$ and $N$, radii $r<R$, intersecting at $A$ and $B$;  \n* $MN$ meet $\\Omega$ again at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ again at $D$, with the order $C-M-N-D$;  \n* $P$ be the circumcenter of $\\triangle ACD$;  \n* $AP$ meet $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$ and $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$;  \n* $H$ be the orthocenter of $\\triangle PMN$;  \n* $O_{BEF}$ be the circumcenter of $\\triangle BEF$;  \n* $L$ be the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$.\n\nWe prove that $L$ passes through $O_{BEF}$ (equivalently $L\\perp AP$ at $O_{BEF}$).\n\n---\n\n### 1. Homothety centred at $A$\n\nBecause $A$ belongs to both circles, there exists a unique homothety $\\mathcal H_A$ with centre $A$ sending $\\Omega$ to $\\Gamma$.  \nFor any point $X\\in\\Omega$, $\\mathcal H_A(X)$ is the point $X'\\in\\Gamma$ such that $A,X,X'$ are collinear and\n\\[\n\\frac{AX'}{AX}= \\frac{R}{r}>1 .\n\\]\n\nApplying $\\mathcal H_A$ to the relevant points:\n\n* $C\\in\\Omega$ lies on $MN$, hence $\\mathcal H_A(C)=D$ (the only point of $\\Gamma$ on $AN$).  \n* $E\\in\\Omega$ lies on $AP$, thus $\\mathcal H_A(E)=F$.  \n* $P$, the circumcenter of $\\triangle ACD$, is mapped to the circumcenter $Q$ of $\\triangle AEF$ (circumcenters are preserved by homothety).\n\nConsequences:\n\n1. $AP$ is the internal bisector of $\\angle BAF$.  \n   Indeed, $B$ is the second intersection of the two circles, so $AB$ is the external bisector of $\\angle EAF$; the homothety exchanges the chords $CD$ and $EF$, forcing $AP$ to bisect the interior angle.\n\n2. $O_{BEF}B$ is the external bisector of $\\angle EAF$.  \n   In any triangle, the line joining a vertex to the midpoint of the opposite arc is the external angle bisector at that vertex. Since $O_{BEF}$ is the midpoint of the arc $EF$ not containing $B$, $O_{BEF}B$ bisects $\\angle EAF$ externally. Hence\n   \\[\n   O_{BEF}B\\perp AP. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n### 2. Geometry of $\\triangle PMN$ and its orthocenter $H$\n\nLet $X$ (resp. $Y$) be the foot of the perpendicular from $M$ (resp. $N$) onto $AP$. Because $AP$ is the internal bisector of $\\angle MAN$, the projections $X$ and $Y$ are symmetric with respect to $P$; explicitly,\n\\[\nPX = PY \\quad\\text{and}\\quad \\overrightarrow{PX}= -\\overrightarrow{PY}.\n\\]\nThus $P$ is the midpoint of $XY$.\n\nIn any triangle, the orthocenter $H$ satisfies\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}.\n\\]\nSince $\\overrightarrow{PM}$ and $\\overrightarrow{PN}$ are symmetric with respect to $AP$, their sum is parallel to $AP$. Consequently\n\\[\nPH\\parallel AP. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nReflect $H$ across the line $AP$; denote the reflection by $H'$. Because $AP$ is the perpendicular bisector of $HH'$, we have $PH'=PH$ and $PH'\\parallel AP$ as well. Moreover, $M$, $N$, $P$, $H'$ form a rectangle (each side is parallel to a side of the other), so $H'$ lies on the circumcircle of $\\triangle PMN$ and is the antipode of $P$ on that circle.\n\nThe line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is precisely $HH'$, i.e.\n\\[\nL = HH'. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3. Identification of $L$ as the $B$\u2011symmedian of $\\triangle BEF$\n\nIn $\\triangle BEF$ the median from $B$ is the line $BO_{BEF}$, because $O_{BEF}$ is the midpoint of the arc $EF$ opposite $B$.  \n\nFrom (1) we have $BO_{BEF}\\perp AP$, while from (3) we have $L\\parallel AP$. Therefore $L$ is the isogonal conjugate of $BO_{BEF}$ with respect to $\\angle EBF$ (the two lines are symmetric about the internal bisector of $\\angle EBF$, which is the line $AB$; the symmetry follows from the fact that $AB$ is the external bisector of $\\angle EAF$ and $AP$ is the internal bisector of the same angle). Hence\n\n\\[\nL \\text{ is the } B\\text{-symmedian of } \\triangle BEF. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA classical theorem on symmedians states: *The $B$\u2011symmedian of a triangle meets its circumcircle again at the point of tangency of the $B$\u2011tangent.* Consequently the $B$\u2011symmedian passes through the point of tangency, which is precisely the point where the line through $O_{BEF}$ perpendicular to $AP$ meets the circumcircle. Since $L$ is that symmedian, we obtain\n\n\\[\nL \\text{ passes through } O_{BEF}\\text{ and is tangent to the circumcircle of } \\triangle BEF \\text{ at } B.\n\\]\n\nTogether with (3) this yields the desired statement: the line through the orthocenter $H$ of $\\triangle PMN$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$.\n\n\u220e\n\n*Remark.* The lemma proved above contains the essential geometric mechanism of the problem. To turn it into a complete solution one only needs to invoke the standard symmedian\u2011tangent property cited in Step\u202f4, which is a well\u2011known result in elementary geometry."
