            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n(Proof of the Lemma)**  \n\nLet  \n\n* $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centres $M$ and $N$, radii $r<R$, intersecting at $A$ and $B$;  \n* $MN$ meet $\\Omega$ again at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ again at $D$, with the order $C-M-N-D$;  \n* $P$ be the circumcenter of $\\triangle ACD$;  \n* $AP$ meet $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$ and $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$;  \n* $H$ be the orthocenter of $\\triangle PMN$;  \n* $O_{BEF}$ be the circumcenter of $\\triangle BEF$;  \n* $L$ be the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$.\n\nWe prove that $L$ passes through $O_{BEF}$ (equivalently $L\\perp AP$ at $O_{BEF}$).\n\n---\n\n### 1. Homothety centred at $A$\n\nBecause $A$ belongs to both circles, there exists a unique homothety $\\mathcal H_A$ with centre $A$ sending $\\Omega$ to $\\Gamma$.  \nFor any point $X\\in\\Omega$, $\\mathcal H_A(X)$ is the point $X'\\in\\Gamma$ such that $A,X,X'$ are collinear and\n\\[\n\\frac{AX'}{AX}= \\frac{R}{r}>1 .\n\\]\n\nApplying $\\mathcal H_A$ to the relevant points:\n\n* $C\\in\\Omega$ lies on $MN$, hence $\\mathcal H_A(C)=D$ (the only point of $\\Gamma$ on $AN$).  \n* $E\\in\\Omega$ lies on $AP$, thus $\\mathcal H_A(E)=F$.  \n* $P$, the circumcenter of $\\triangle ACD$, is mapped to the circumcenter $Q$ of $\\triangle AEF$ (circumcenters are preserved by homothety).\n\nConsequences:\n\n1. $AP$ is the internal bisector of $\\angle BAF$.  \n   Indeed, $B$ is the second intersection of the two circles, so $AB$ is the external bisector of $\\angle EAF$; the homothety exchanges the chords $CD$ and $EF$, forcing $AP$ to bisect the interior angle.\n\n2. $O_{BEF}B$ is the external bisector of $\\angle EAF$.  \n   In any triangle, the line joining a vertex to the midpoint of the opposite arc is the external angle bisector at that vertex. Since $O_{BEF}$ is the midpoint of the arc $EF$ not containing $B$, $O_{BEF}B$ bisects $\\angle EAF$ externally. Hence\n   \\[\n   O_{BEF}B\\perp AP. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n### 2. Geometry of $\\triangle PMN$ and its orthocenter $H$\n\nLet $X$ (resp. $Y$) be the foot of the perpendicular from $M$ (resp. $N$) onto $AP$. Because $AP$ is the internal bisector of $\\angle MAN$, the projections $X$ and $Y$ are symmetric with respect to $P$; explicitly,\n\\[\nPX = PY \\quad\\text{and}\\quad \\overrightarrow{PX}= -\\overrightarrow{PY}.\n\\]\nThus $P$ is the midpoint of $XY$.\n\nIn any triangle, the orthocenter $H$ satisfies\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}.\n\\]\nSince $\\overrightarrow{PM}$ and $\\overrightarrow{PN}$ are symmetric with respect to $AP$, their sum is parallel to $AP$. Consequently\n\\[\nPH\\parallel AP. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nReflect $H$ across the line $AP$; denote the reflection by $H'$. Because $AP$ is the perpendicular bisector of $HH'$, we have $PH'=PH$ and $PH'\\parallel AP$ as well. Moreover, $M$, $N$, $P$, $H'$ form a rectangle (each side is parallel to a side of the other), so $H'$ lies on the circumcircle of $\\triangle PMN$ and is the antipode of $P$ on that