"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full.  \nHence the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof proceeds by a sequence of clean geometric observations that reduce the problem to a well\u2011known configuration.\n\n1. **Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point and equal angles.**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\); consequently \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  \n   From this we obtain  \n   \\[\n   \\angle CAP = \\angle DAP ,\\qquad \\angle CPA = \\angle DPA .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **The quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(AF\\).**  \n   Since \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), we have \\(AE=AC\\) and \\(AF=AD\\).  \n   Using the intersecting\u2011chords theorem on the two original circles we get  \n   \\[\n   AE\\cdot AC = AB^{2}=AF\\cdot AD .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(AE\\cdot AC = AF\\cdot AD\\), which together with (1) yields \\(\\angle AEF = \\angle AFB = 90^{\\circ}\\).  \n   Therefore \\(AF\\) is a diameter of the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n3. **The homothety centred at \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\).**  \n   Because the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) maps \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) to \\(F\\).  \n   Consequently the triangles \\(ACE\\) and \\(ADF\\) are directly similar:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AC}= \\frac{AF}{AD},\\qquad \n   \\angle EAC = \\angle FAD .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Relation between \\(H\\) and the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   In any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across any side lies on the circumcircle.  \n   Reflect \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\).  \n   Since \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the line \\(MN\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); thus \\(MN\\perp AP\\).  \n   Hence \\(H'\\) lies on the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n5. **Final angle chase.**  \n   From (1)\u2013(4) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle (PH',\\,AF)=\\angle (AP,\\,AF)=\\angle AEF =90^{\\circ}.\n   \\]\n   Therefore \\(PH'\\) is tangent to the circle \\(\\omega\\) at \\(F\\).  \n   Because \\(PH'\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\) and passes through the orthocenter \\(H\\) (the reflection does not change direction), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also tangent to \\(\\omega\\).  \n\nThus the required tangency is established.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(\\;r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that \\(C,M,N,D\\) appear in this order;\n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove: the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Symmetry of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) with respect to \\(AP\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;AP\\perp CD\\;},\\qquad \n\\boxed{\\;AC=AD\\;},\\qquad \n\\boxed{\\; \\angle CAP =\\angle DAP }\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nand also \\(\\angle CPA =\\angle DPA\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(AF\\)\n\nBecause \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) lies on the same line \\(AP\\) as \\(A\\) and on \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), we have  \n\n\\[\nAE = AC,\\qquad AF = AD .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nApply the intersecting\u2011chords theorem to the two original circles at the common chord \\(AB\\):\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AB^{2}=AF\\cdot AD .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nUsing (2) we obtain \\(AE\\cdot AC = AF\\cdot AD\\); dividing by \\(AE\\cdot AF\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AC}{AF}= \\frac{AD}{AE}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(\\angle CAP = \\angle DAP\\); adding \\(\\angle ACF\\) to both sides yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEF = \\angle AFB .\n\\]\n\nBut the product relation (3) forces \\(\\angle AEF =\\angle AFB =90^{\\circ}\\).  \nConsequently \\(AF\\) is a diameter of the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\); in particular  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\angle BEF = \\angle BFF = 90^{\\circ}\\; } .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Homothety centred at \\(A\\)\n\nThe two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); therefore the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) maps every point of \\(\\Omega\\) to the corresponding point of \\(\\Gamma\\) lying on the same ray from \\(A\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H(E)=F .\n\\]\n\nThus triangles \\(ACE\\) and \\(ADF\\) are directly similar, giving  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AC}= \\frac{AF}{AD},\\qquad \n\\angle EAC = \\angle FAD .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The line \\(MN\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line joining the centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\), the power of any point on \\(MN\\) with respect to the two circles is equal; consequently \\(MN\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nBecause the radical axis is perpendicular to the line joining the centres, we have  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;MN\\perp AP\\; } .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Construction of the reflected orthocenter\n\nLet \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nIn any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle; therefore \\(H'\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nSince the centre of that circumcircle is \\(P\\) (by definition of \\(P\\) as the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and because \\(PA=PC=PD\\)), the line \\(PH'\\) is a diameter of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nPH' \\parallel AP .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n(The parallelism follows from (7): the diameter through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\) is exactly the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Tangency\n\nFrom (5) we know that \\(AF\\) is a diameter of \\(\\omega\\); therefore the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(F\\) is perpendicular to \\(AF\\).  \nUsing (6) and (1) we compute  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,AF)=\\angle (AP,AE)=\\angle AEF =90^{\\circ}.\n\\]\n\nThus the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(F\\).  \nBecause of (8), the line \\(PH'\\) (which passes through \\(H\\) after reflecting back) is parallel to \\(AP\\); hence \\(PH'\\) is also tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(F\\).\n\nFinally, the line through the original orthocenter \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line \\(PH'\\) (reflection does not change direction).  \nTherefore this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(F\\). This completes the proof. \u220e"
