rpendicular to \\(MN\\) is the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Consequently  \n   \\[\n   PH'\\parallel AP .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Final angle chase and tangency.**  \n   From (2) we have \\(\\angle AEF=90^{\\circ}\\); therefore the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(F\\) is the line through \\(F\\) perpendicular to \\(AF\\).  \n   Using (3) and (1) we obtain \\(\\angle (AP,AF)=\\angle AEF=90^{\\circ}\\); thus the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(F\\).  \n   By (4) the line \\(PH'\\) (which passes through \\(H\\) after reflecting back) is parallel to \\(AP\\); hence \\(PH'\\) is also tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(F\\).  \n   Consequently the line through the original orthocenter \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, with radii \\(r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) the two distinct intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that the order on the line is \\(C,M,N,D\\);\n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Symmetry of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) with respect to \\(AP\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  \nThus\n\n\\[\nAP\\perp CD,\\qquad AC=AD,\\qquad \\angle CAP=\\angle DAP .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic and \\(AF\\) is a diameter of its circumcircle\n\nBecause \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and on the line \\(AP\\), we have \\(AE=AC\\); similarly \\(AF=AD\\).  \nApplying the intersecting\u2011chords theorem to the two original circles at the common chord \\(AB\\) yields\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AB^{2}=AF\\cdot AD .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nDividing (2) by \\(AE\\cdot AF\\) gives \\(\\dfrac{AC}{AF}= \\dfrac{AD}{AE}\\).  \nTogether with \\(AC=AD\\) from (1) we obtain \\(AE=AF\\).  \n\nNow compute the angle at \\(E\\) in quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\):\n\\[\n\\angle AEF = \\angle AEC = 90^{\\circ},\n\\]\nbecause \\(AE\\) is a chord of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(EC\\) is a diameter of \\(\\Omega\\) (since \\(C\\) and \\(E\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(AP\\)).  \nSimilarly \\(\\angle AFB =90^{\\circ}\\).  \nHence \\(\\angle AEF = \\angle AFB =90^{\\circ}\\); consequently the points \\(A,E,F,B\\) are concyclic and \\(AF\\) is a diameter of that circle \\(\\omega\\). In particular\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\angle AEF = 90^{\\circ}\\; } .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Homothety centred at \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\nSince the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) that maps \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\) sends every point of \\(\\Omega\\) to the point of \\(\\Gamma\\) lying on the same ray from \\(A\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal H(C)=D\\) and \\(\\mathcal H(E)=F\\).  \nTherefore triangles \\(ACE\\) and \\(ADF\\) are directly similar, giving\n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AC}= \\frac{AF}{AD},\\qquad \\angle EAC = \\angle FAD .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The line \\(MN\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\nB