            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, with radii \\(r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) the two distinct intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that the order on the line is \\(C,M,N,D\\);\n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Symmetry of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) with respect to \\(AP\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  \nThus\n\n\\[\nAP\\perp CD,\\qquad AC=AD,\\qquad \\angle CAP=\\angle DAP .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic and \\(AF\\) is a diameter of its circumcircle\n\nBecause \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and on the line \\(AP\\), we have \\(AE=AC\\); similarly \\(AF=AD\\).  \nApplying the intersecting\u2011chords theorem to the two original circles at the common chord \\(AB\\) yields\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AB^{2}=AF\\cdot AD .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nDividing (2) by \\(AE\\cdot AF\\) gives \\(\\dfrac{AC}{AF}= \\dfrac{AD}{AE}\\).  \nTogether with \\(AC=AD\\) from (1) we obtain \\(AE=AF\\).  \n\nNow compute the angle at \\(E\\) in quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\):\n\\[\n\\angle AEF = \\angle AEC = 90^{\\circ},\n\\]\nbecause \\(AE\\) is a chord of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(EC\\) is a diameter of \\(\\Omega\\) (since \\(C\\) and \\(E\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(AP\\)).  \nSimilarly \\(\\angle AFB =90^{\\circ}\\).  \nHence \\(\\angle AEF = \\angle AFB =90^{\\circ}\\); consequently the points \\(A,E,F,B\\) are concyclic and \\(AF\\) is a diameter of that circle \\(\\omega\\). In particular\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\angle AEF = 90^{\\circ}\\; } .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Homothety centred at \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\nSince the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) that maps \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\) sends every point of \\(\\Omega\\) to the point of \\(\\Gamma\\) lying on the same ray from \\(A\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal H(C)=D\\) and \\(\\mathcal H(E)=F\\).  \nTherefore triangles \\(ACE\\) and \\(ADF\\) are directly similar, giving\n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AC}= \\frac{AF}{AD},\\qquad \\angle EAC = \\angle FAD .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The line \\(MN\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\nBoth \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line joining the centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\); consequently every point of \\(MN\\) has equal power with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nHence \\(MN\\) is the radical axis of the two circles, and the radical axis is perpendicular to the line joining the centres. Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,MN\\perp AP\\, } .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Reflection of the orthocenter across \\(MN\\)\n\nLet \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across side \\(MN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nIn any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle of that triangle.  \nThus \\(H'\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and \\(PA=PC=PD\\), the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) coincides with \\(P\\).  \nTherefore the diameter of this circumcircle that is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) is the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (by (5)).  \nSince \\(H'\\) is the antipode of \\(H\\) with respect to side \\(MN\\), the segment \\(PH'\\) is exactly that diameter; hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,PH'\\parallel AP\\, } .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Tangency of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nFrom (3) we know that \\(\\angle AEF =90^{\\circ}\\).  \nBecause \\(AF\\) is a diameter of \\(\\omega\\), the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(F\\) is the line through \\(F\\) perpendicular to \\(AF\\).  \n\nUsing (1) we have \\(\\angle (AP,AF)=\\angle AEF =90^{\\circ}\\); therefore the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(AF\\) and thus coincides with the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(F\\).\n\nBy (6) the line \\(PH'\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\); consequently \\(PH'\\) is parallel to that tangent and passes through the point \\(F\\). Hence \\(PH'\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(F\\).\n\nFinally, the reflection across \\(MN\\) does not change direction, so the line through the original orthocenter \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is the same line as \\(PH'\\). Therefore this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(F\\). \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
