alse; only the midpoint of the segment of centres has equal power when the circles have equal radii, which is not assumed.\n\n* **Critical Error \u2013 Step\u202f5:** \u201cBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and \\(PA=PC=PD\\), the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) coincides with \\(P\\).\u201d  \n  * The circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the point equidistant from \\(P,M,N\\). From \\(PA=PC=PD\\) we only know that \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(A,C,D\\); there is no reason for \\(P\\) to be equidistant from \\(M\\) and \\(N\\). Hence the claim is unfounded.\n\n* **Critical Error \u2013 Step\u202f5:** \u201cThe diameter of this circumcircle that is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) is the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (by (5)).\u201d  \n  * Since the previous claim that \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is false, the existence of such a diameter and its direction are not established.\n\n* **Critical Error \u2013 Step\u202f6:** \u201cUsing (1) we have \\(\\angle (AP,AF)=\\angle AEF =90^{\\circ}\\); therefore the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(AF\\) and thus coincides with the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(F\\).\u201d  \n  * The equality \\(\\angle (AP,AF)=\\angle AEF\\) relies on the earlier (incorrect) claim that \\(\\angle AEF=90^{\\circ}\\). Because that angle was not correctly proved, the conclusion about the tangent is unsupported.\n\n* **General Gap \u2013 Homothety Claim (Step\u202f3):** \u201cSince the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the homothety with centre \\(A\\) that maps \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\) sends every point of \\(\\Omega\\) to the point of \\(\\Gamma\\) lying on the same ray from \\(A\\).\u201d  \n  * While a homothety exists, the solution never justifies that the ratio of similarity is positive (i.e., that the circles are not internally tangent) nor that the mapping indeed sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) to \\(F\\). This is a **Justification Gap** that, even if filled, would not rescue the later critical errors.\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Verification Log\n\nBelow is a step\u2011by\u2011step examination of the submitted solution. Quotations are taken verbatim from the solution; each step is followed by an analysis indicating whether it is correct, contains a justification gap, or a critical error.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f1 \u2013 \u201cSymmetry of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) with respect to \\(AP\\)\u201d\n\n> \u201cSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  \n> Thus  \n> \\[\n> AP\\perp CD,\\qquad AC=AD,\\qquad \\angle CAP=\\angle DAP .\n> \\tag{1}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nIf \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), then indeed \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\). Consequently \\(AP\\) is **not** necessarily the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) passes through the midpoint of \\(CD\\), not through \\(A\\) in general. The statement that \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is false unless \\(A\\) happens to be the midpoint of \\(CD\\), which is not given. Therefore this step contains a **Critical Error** (incorrect geometric property). Because the rest of the argument relies on (1), the logical chain is already broken.\n\n*Classification:* Critical Error \u2013 false claim about the role of \\(AP\\).\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f2 \u2013 \u201cThe quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic and \\(AF\\) is a diameter\u201d\n\n> \u201cBecause \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and on the line \\(AP\\), we have \\(AE=AC\\); similarly \\(AF=AD\\).  \n> Applying the intersecting\u2011chords theorem to the two original circles at the common chord \\(AB\\) yields  \n> \\[\n> AE\\cdot AC = AB^{2}=AF\\cdot AD .\n> \\tag{2}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- The equality \\(AE=AC\\) is **not** a consequence of \\(E\\) being on \\(\\Omega\\) and on line \\(AP\\). Both \\(AE\\) and \\(AC\\) are chords of \\(\\Omega\\) with a common endpoint \\(A\\); chords with a common endpoint are generally of different lengths. Hence the claim is false \u2192 **Critical Error**.\n\n- The \u201cintersecting\u2011chords theorem\u201d (power of a point) states that for two chords intersecting **inside** a circle, the products of the segments are equal. Here the author attempts to apply it to the two *different* circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) using the common chord \\(AB\\). The theorem does not give \\(AE\\cdot AC = AB^{2}\\); the correct relation would be \\(AE\\cdot EC = AB\\cdot BB\\) (which is meaningless) if the chords intersected inside the same circle. Thus the equation (2) is unfounded \u2192 **Critical Error**.\n\nBecause the entire conclusion that \\(AE=AF\\) and later that \\(\\angle AEF = 90^\\circ\\) depends on these false equalities, the subsequent parts of the proof collapse.\n\n*Classification:* Critical Errors \u2013 false chord equality and misuse of intersecting\u2011chords theorem.\n\n---\n\n> \u201cDividing (2) by \\(AE\\cdot AF\\) gives \\(\\dfrac{AC}{AF}= \\dfrac{AD}{AE}\\).  \n> Together with \\(AC=AD\\) from (1) we obtain \\(AE=AF\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** Even if (2) were correct, the algebraic manipulation would be valid, but since (2) is false, this deduction is meaningless. Moreover, the equality \\(AC=AD\\) itself came from the erroneous claim in Step\u202f1. Hence this line rests on false premises \u2192 **Critical Error** (propagation of earlier error).\n\n---\n\n> \u201cNow compute the angle at \\(E\\) in quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\):  \n> \\[\n> \\angle AEF = \\angle AEC = 90^{\\circ},\n> \\]  \n> because \\(AE\\) is a chord of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(EC\\) is a diameter of \\(\\Omega\\) (since \\(C\\) and \\(E\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(AP\\)).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- The claim that \\(EC\\) is a diameter of \\(\\Omega\\) hinges on the unproved (and false) symmetry 