a_{BEF}\\) at a second point \\(T\\neq B\\).  \n\nFrom Lemma\u202f3 we have\n\\[\n\\angle BTP = \\angle BAP .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(AP\\) is the internal bisector of \\(\\angle BAE\\) (Lemma\u202f1) and \\(E,F,B\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(AP\\) (Lemma\u202f2), we also have\n\\[\n\\angle BAP = \\angle BHP .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n(The last equality follows from the fact that \\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); the altitude from \\(P\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\), and \\(AP\\) is symmetric to \\(MN\\) with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), giving \\(\\angle BHP = \\angle BAP\\).)\n\nCombining (1) and (2) yields\n\\[\n\\angle BTP = \\angle BHP .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus the quadrilateral \\(BTHP\\) is cyclic (equal subtended angles standing on the same chord \\(BP\\)).  \nConsequently \\(T\\) lies on the circle with diameter \\(BP\\).  \n\nBut Lemma\u202f4 tells us that the reflection \\(H'\\) of \\(H\\) across \\(PN\\) also lies on \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).  \nSince \\(H'\\) is symmetric of \\(H\\) with respect to \\(PN\\) and \\(PN\\) is perpendicular to \\(BH\\) (property of orthocenter in \\(\\triangle PMN\\)), the points \\(B,P,H,H'\\) are concyclic with centre \\(P\\) (Lemma\u202f2).  \nHence the only points common to the circle with diameter \\(BP\\) and \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) are \\(B\\) and \\(P\\).  \n\nBecause \\(T\\neq P\\) (the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) does not pass through \\(P\\)), the only possibility is \\(T\\equiv B\\).  \nThis contradicts the assumption that the line meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at a second point distinct from \\(B\\).\n\nTherefore the supposition is false, and the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) must be tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e"
