ies on \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\). Since \\(H'\\) is symmetric of \\(H\\) with respect to the line \\(PN\\), the quadrilateral \\(BPH'H\\) is a rectangle; thus \\(H'\\) also lies on the circle with diameter \\(BP\\). Hence the two circles \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) and the circle with diameter \\(BP\\) have at least three common points: \\(B\\), \\(P\\) and \\(H'\\). Two distinct circles cannot share three non\u2011collinear points, so they must coincide. Therefore the only points common to both circles are \\(B\\) and \\(P\\).\n\nBecause \\(T\\) belongs to both circles (by construction \\(T\\in\\omega_{BEF}\\) and by (5) \\(T\\) lies on the circle with diameter \\(BP\\)), we must have \\(T\\equiv B\\) or \\(T\\equiv P\\). The line \\(\\ell\\) does not pass through \\(P\\) (it is parallel to \\(AP\\) and \\(A\\neq P\\)), so \\(T\\neq P\\). Hence \\(T\\equiv B\\), contradicting the assumption that \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at a second point distinct from \\(B\\).\n\nThe contradiction shows that the initial assumption is false. Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) only at \\(B\\); i.e. it is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e"
