            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) and radii \\(r<R\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be their two intersection points;  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n* \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1.  \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the minor arc \\(EF\\) of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the chords \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\); indeed \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  \nConsequently the two chords \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(AF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) are also symmetric with respect to \\(AP\\); therefore\n\\[\n\\angle BAP=\\angle PAE=\\angle FAP .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBoth circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through \\(B\\); hence\n\\[\nPB=PC=PD \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad PB=PE=PF .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nThus \\(B,E,F\\) are all at the same distance from \\(P\\); they lie on the circle centred at \\(P\\) with radius \\(PB\\).  \nSince \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) and lies on the internal bisector of \\(\\angle EPF\\) (by (1)), it is the midpoint of the minor arc \\(EF\\) of that circle. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2.  Quadrilateral \\(BEPF\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nEquation (2) shows that \\(PB=PE=PF\\); consequently the four points \\(B,E,F,P\\) lie on the circle with centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PB\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3.  Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) and let \\(\\ell\\) intersect \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) again at \\(T\\neq B\\).  \nThen \\(\\displaystyle\\angle BTP=\\angle BAP\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), the oriented angle between the lines \\(BT\\) and \\(TP\\) equals the oriented angle between \\(BA\\) and \\(AP\\):\n\\[\n\\angle (BT,TP)=\\angle (BA,AP)=\\angle BAP .\n\\]\nSince \\(B,T,P\\) are concyclic, the inscribed angle \\(\\angle BTP\\) equals the oriented angle \\(\\angle (BT,TP)\\). Hence \\(\\angle BTP=\\angle BAP\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4.  In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the reflection \\(H'\\) of \\(H\\) across side \\(PN\\) lies on \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any triangle, the reflection of its orthocenter across any side belongs to the circumcircle of that triangle. Apply this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\); denote by \\(\\Gamma_{PMN}\\) its circumcircle. Then \\(H'\\in\\Gamma_{PMN}\\).\n\nNow observe that \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the mid\u2011points of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) respectively (because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) and \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)). Hence \\(\\Gamma_{PMN}\\) is the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \n\nA well\u2011known property of two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) is that the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) passes through the second intersection point \\(B\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\). Consequently \\(B\\in\\Gamma_{PMN}\\).  \n\nBut Lemma\u202f2 tells us that \\(B\\) also belongs to the circle centred at \\(P\\) passing through \\(E\\) and \\(F\\), i.e. to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\). Since both circles \\(\\Gamma_{PMN}\\) and \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) have three common points \\(B,P\\) and the point symmetric of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(PN\\) (which is exactly \\(H'\\)), they must coincide. Therefore \\(H'\\in\\omega_{BEF}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof (contradiction)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is **not** tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).  \nThen \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at a second point \\(T\\neq B\\).\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f3 we have\n\\[\n\\angle BTP=\\angle BAP . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nLemma\u202f1 gives that \\(AP\\) is the internal bisector of \\(\\angle BAE\\) and \\(\\angle BAF\\); because \\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the altitude from \\(P\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) and \\(MN\\) is symmetric to \\(AP\\) with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\angle BAP=\\angle BHP . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nCombining (3) and (4) yields\n\\[\n\\angle BTP=\\angle BHP .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEquality of the two inscribed angles standing on the same chord \\(BP\\) implies that the four points \\(B,T,H,P\\) are concyclic; the circle they determine has chord \\(BP\\) as a diameter because \\(\\angle BHP\\) is a right angle (the altitude from \\(H\\) to \\(BP\\) coincides with \\(PN\\)). Consequently the circle \\((BTHP)\\) is the circle with diameter \\(BP\\).\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f4 the reflection \\(H'\\) of \\(H\\) across \\(PN\\) lies on \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\). Since \\(H'\\) is symmetric of \\(H\\) with respect to the line \\(PN\\), the quadrilateral \\(BPH'H\\) is a rectangle; thus \\(H'\\) also lies on the circle with diameter \\(BP\\). Hence the two circles \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) and the circle with diameter \\(BP\\) have at least three common points: \\(B\\), \\(P\\) and \\(H'\\). Two distinct circles cannot share three non\u2011collinear points, so they must coincide. Therefore the only points common to both circles are \\(B\\) and \\(P\\).\n\nBecause \\(T\\) belongs to both circles (by construction \\(T\\in\\omega_{BEF}\\) and by (5) \\(T\\) lies on the circle with diameter \\(BP\\)), we must have \\(T\\equiv B\\) or \\(T\\equiv P\\). The line \\(\\ell\\) does not pass through \\(P\\) (it is parallel to \\(AP\\) and \\(A\\neq P\\)), so \\(T\\neq P\\). Hence \\(T\\equiv B\\), contradicting the assumption that \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at a second point distinct from \\(B\\).\n\nThe contradiction shows that the initial assumption is false. Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) only at \\(B\\); i.e. it is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
