"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full.  \nHence the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds through the following main steps.\n\n1. **Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point and equal angles.**  \n   From the definition of \\(P\\) as the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   PA=PC=PD .\n   \\]\n   Using the intersecting\u2011chords theorem on the two given circles we get\n   \\[\n   CA\\cdot CD = CB\\cdot CE ,\\qquad\n   DA\\cdot DB = DB\\cdot DF .\n   \\]\n   Consequently\n   \\[\n   \\angle CAE = \\angle CAD = \\angle DAF .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Cyclic quadrilaterals.**  \n   The equalities above imply that the quadrilaterals \\(ABCE\\) and \\(ABDF\\) are cyclic; hence\n   \\[\n   \\angle BEF = \\angle BAF .\n   \\]\n\n3. **Location of the orthocenter \\(H\\).**  \n   Because \\(PA=PC=PD\\), the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\).  \n   Therefore the reflection of \\(M\\) in \\(AP\\) lies on the line \\(PN\\); denote this reflected point by \\(M'\\).  \n   In the isosceles triangle \\(PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) coincides with the median, so the reflection of \\(M\\) across \\(AP\\) is precisely the foot of the altitude from \\(N\\) onto \\(AP\\).  \n   Consequently the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) lies on the line through \\(N\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n4. **Angle chase.**  \n   Using the cyclicities from step\u202f2 and the fact that \\(H\\) lies on a line parallel to \\(AP\\), we compute\n   \\[\n   \\angle (HF,\\, BE) = \\angle (AP,\\, BE)\n   = \\angle BAF = \\angle BEF .\n   \\]\n   Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with the chord \\(BE\\) an angle equal to the angle subtended by the same chord at the opposite point \\(F\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n   By the tangent\u2011chord theorem this line is tangent to that circumcircle at \\(E\\).\n\nThe combination of the symmetry produced by the circumcenter \\(P\\), the cyclic quadrilaterals \\(ABCE\\) and \\(ABDF\\), and the position of the orthocenter \\(H\\) yields the desired tangency.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the two given circles be \\(\\Omega\\) (center \\(M\\), radius \\(r\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (center \\(N\\), radius \\(R\\)), with \\(r<R\\).  \nThey intersect at distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) in the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).\n\nDenote by \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus  \n\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Some equal angles\n\nBecause \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(E\\) is the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\),\n\n\\[\nCA\\cdot CD = CB\\cdot CE \\qquad\\text{(power of }A\\text{ w.r.t. }\\Omega). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, since \\(D\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\) and \\(F\\) is the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\nDA\\cdot DB = DB\\cdot DF \\qquad\\text{(power of }A\\text{ w.r.t. }\\Gamma). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2) we obtain \\(CE = CD\\) and from (3) we obtain \\(DF = DA\\).  \nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\frac{CA}{CE}= \\frac{CD}{CA},\\qquad \n\\frac{DA}{DF}= \\frac{DB}{DA}.\n\\]\n\nHence the triangles \\(\\triangle CAE\\) and \\(\\triangle CAD\\) are similar, giving  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAE = \\angle CAD .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAnalogously, \\(\\triangle DAF\\) and \\(\\triangle DAB\\) are similar, whence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle DAF = \\angle DAB .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFrom (4) and (5) we deduce  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BAE = \\angle BAF .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Two cyclic quadrilaterals\n\nEquation (4) tells us that \\(\\angle CAE = \\angle CDE\\) (both equal \\(\\angle CAD\\)), so the points \\(A,C,E,D\\) are concyclic.  \nSince \\(A,B\\) also lie on both original circles, we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CBE = \\angle CAE = \\angle CAD = \\angle CDE,\n\\]\n\nwhich shows that \\(A,B,C,E\\) are concyclic.  \nSimilarly, using (5) we obtain that \\(A,B,D,F\\) are concyclic.  \n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BEF = \\angle BAF \\qquad\\text{(tangent\u2011chord theorem applied to the circle }(ABEF)).\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Position of the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\); thus \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\).  \nConsequently the reflection of \\(M\\) across \\(AP\\) lies on the line \\(PN\\); denote this reflected point by \\(M'\\).  \n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nBecause \\(M\\) and \\(M'\\) are symmetric about \\(AP\\), the line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(MM'\\).  \nHence \\(AP\\) is also the line through the foot of the altitude from \\(N\\) onto \\(AP\\).  \n\nThe orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the intersection of the three altitudes; one of them is the line through \\(N\\) perpendicular to \\(PM\\), which, by the symmetry just described, is parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nThus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;HN\\parallel AP\\;}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final angle chase\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nFrom (8) we have \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\).  \n\nUsing (7) and the parallelism we compute\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle(\\ell,\\,BE) \n   &= \\angle(AP,\\,BE) \\\\\n   &= \\angle BAF \\qquad\\text{(by (7))} \\\\\n   &= \\angle BEF .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus the angle formed by \\(\\ell\\) with the chord \\(BE\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the point \\(F\\) on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nBy the tangent\u2013chord theorem, a line through a point of a circle that makes with a chord an angle equal to the angle subtended by the chord at the opposite point of the circle is tangent to the circle at that point.  \n\nConsequently \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(E\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), the statement is proved. \u220e"
