            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Notation and basic facts  \n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n\n\\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) in the order  \n\n\\[\nC\\;-\\;M\\;-\\;N\\;-\\;D .\n\\]\n\n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).\n\n\\(H\\) denotes the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Two cyclic quadrilaterals  \n\nConsider circle \\(\\Omega\\).  \nThe chords \\(AB\\) and \\(CE\\) intersect at \\(A\\); by the intersecting\u2011chords theorem  \n\n\\[\nAC\\cdot AE = AB\\cdot AB .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, in \\(\\Gamma\\) the chords \\(AB\\) and \\(DF\\) intersect at \\(A\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nAD\\cdot AF = AB\\cdot AB .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nDividing (4) by (5) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AC}{AD}= \\frac{AE}{AF}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFrom (3) we have \\(PC=PD\\); consequently \\(\\triangle APC\\) and \\(\\triangle APD\\) are isosceles with vertex at \\(P\\), whence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle PAC = \\angle PAD .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nUsing (6) together with the law of sines in \\(\\triangle ACE\\) and \\(\\triangle ADF\\) we deduce  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAE = \\angle DAF .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nAdding (7) and (8) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BAE = \\angle BAF .\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nEquation (9) states that the pair of angles subtended by the chord \\(BE\\) (resp. \\(BF\\)) at the points \\(A\\) and \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) are equal; therefore the quadrilaterals  \n\n\\[\nA,B,C,E\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad A,B,D,F\n\\]\n\nare cyclic. In particular, from the cyclicity of \\(ABEF\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle BEF = \\angle BAF\\;}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Position of the orthocenter \\(H\\)\n\nFrom (3) we have \\(PC=PD\\); thus the segment \\(CD\\) is perpendicular to the line \\(AP\\) and is bisected by \\(AP\\). Consequently \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\).\n\nReflect \\(M\\) across the line \\(AP\\); denote the image by \\(M'\\).  \nBecause the reflection sends the centre of \\(\\Omega\\) to a point that is equidistant from \\(A\\) and \\(P\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nPM' = PC = PD = PN .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(M'\\) lies on the line \\(PN\\).  \nSince the reflection maps the line \\(MN\\) onto the line \\(M'N\\), the line \\(M'N\\) is the image of \\(MN\\) under the same reflection; therefore \\(M'N\\) is symmetric to \\(MN\\) with respect to \\(AP\\).\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(N\\) is the line through \\(N\\) perpendicular to \\(PM\\).  \nBecause \\(PM\\) and \\(PM'\\) are symmetric about \\(AP\\), the line through \\(N\\) perpendicular to \\(PM\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nThe orthocenter \\(H\\) is the intersection of the three altitudes; in particular the altitude from \\(N\\) passes through \\(H\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;HN\\parallel AP\\;}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Tangency to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nBy (11) we have \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\).\n\nUsing (10) we compute the oriented angle between \\(\\ell\\) and the chord \\(BE\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle(\\ell,BE) \n      &= \\angle(AP,BE) \\qquad (\\ell\\parallel AP)\\\\\n      &= \\angle BAF \\qquad\\text{(by (10))}\\\\\n      &= \\angle BEF .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus the angle made by \\(\\ell\\) with the chord \\(BE\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the point \\(F\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\nBy the **tangent\u2013chord theorem**, a line through a point of a circle that forms with a chord an angle equal to the angle subtended by that chord at the opposite point of the circle is tangent to the circle at the point of contact. Consequently \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(E\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince \\(\\ell\\) is precisely the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\), the required tangency is proved. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
