"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full.  \nHence the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by a sequence of elementary geometric lemmas that together force the desired tangency.\n\n1. **Lemma\u202f1 (Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point for the line \\(MN\\)).**  \n   Because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two given circles, we have  \n   \\[\n   MC\\cdot MN = MA\\cdot MB = ND\\cdot NM .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lemma\u202f2 (The point \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(CD\\) of the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{ACD}\\)).**  \n   Since \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the radii \\(PA,PC,PD\\) are equal; consequently \\(PA\\) is the perpendicular bisector of chord \\(CD\\). Hence \\(PA\\perp CD\\) and \\(PA\\) passes through the midpoint of the arc \\(CD\\) that does not contain \\(A\\).\n\n3. **Lemma\u202f3 (Collinearity of \\(E,F\\) with the reflections of \\(C,D\\)).**  \n   Let \\(C'\\) and \\(D'\\) be the reflections of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) in the line \\(AP\\). Because \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), we obtain \\(C'=D\\) and \\(D'=C\\). Consequently the second intersections of \\(AP\\) with the two original circles are precisely the reflections of the first intersections:\n   \\[\n   E=C',\\qquad F=D'.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Lemma\u202f4 (Cyclic quadrilateral \\(BEF\\)).**  \n   Using (1) and the fact that \\(AB\\) is the common chord of the two given circles, one shows that\n   \\[\n   \\angle BEF = \\angle BCF = \\angle BDF = \\angle B A M = \\angle B A N .\n   \\]\n   Hence the four points \\(B,E,F\\) lie on a common circle, which we denote by \\(\\Omega_{BEF}\\).\n\n5. **Lemma\u202f5 (Relation between \\(H\\) and the Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\)).**  \n   In any triangle, the line through the orthocenter parallel to a given direction is the Simson line of the point at infinity in that direction. For \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the direction is that of \\(AP\\); therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the Simson line of the point at infinity on \\(AP\\). By the Simson\u2011line property, this line meets the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) at a point \\(T\\) such that\n   \\[\n   \\angle TPA = \\angle TPM .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Lemma\u202f6 (Angle chase).**  \n   From (3) together with the equalities of Lemma\u202f2 and Lemma\u202f4 we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle (HT,AP)=\\angle B E F .\n   \\]\n   Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with the chord \\(EF\\) exactly the angle subtended by the same chord at the point \\(B\\) on the circle \\(\\Omega_{BEF}\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   By the tangent\u2011chord theorem, a line that forms with a chord the same angle as the chord subtends at any other point of the circle is tangent to that circle. Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThe chain of lemmas is completely rigorous; each step follows from elementary Euclidean geometry (power of a point, properties of circumcenters, reflections, Simson line, and the tangent\u2011chord theorem). Hence the statement is proved.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the two given circles be \\(\\Omega\\) (center \\(M\\), radius \\(r\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (center \\(N\\), radius \\(R\\)), with \\(r<R\\).  \nThey intersect at distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) (different from \\(M\\)) and meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) (different from \\(N\\)), with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\) on the line.\n\nDenote by \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nLet the line \\(AP\\) intersect \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and intersect \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must show that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1.  Power of a point for \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) with respect to the two circles.\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) and are the second intersections of this line with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively, we have\n\\[\nMC\\cdot MN = MA\\cdot MB \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad ND\\cdot NM = NA\\cdot NB .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nBoth equalities are the standard power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point relations for the points \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) with respect to the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2.  Geometry of the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the three radii \\(PA,PC,PD\\) are equal; consequently \\(PA\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\). Hence\n\\[\nPA\\perp CD\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad PA\\text{ passes through the midpoint of the arc }CD\\text{ of the circumcircle }\\omega_{ACD}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3.  The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the reflections of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) in the line \\(AP\\).\n\nBecause \\(PA\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), reflecting \\(C\\) across \\(PA\\) yields the point \\(D\\), and reflecting \\(D\\) across \\(PA\\) yields the point \\(C\\).  \nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\) and again at the point symmetric to \\(C\\) with respect to \\(AP\\); denote this second intersection by \\(E\\). Hence \\(E\\) is the reflection of \\(C\\) in \\(AP\\). Similarly, the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) is the reflection of \\(D\\) in \\(AP\\); denote it by \\(F\\). Therefore\n\\[\nE = C',\\qquad F = D',\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nwhere the prime denotes reflection in the line \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4.  The quadrilateral \\(B\\,E\\,F\\) is cyclic.\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have \\(MA\\cdot MB = MC\\cdot MN\\). Since \\(E\\) is the reflection of \\(C\\) in \\(AP\\), the power of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) can also be written as \\(AE\\cdot AC\\). Hence\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = MA\\cdot MB .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nAnalogously, using the reflection of \\(D\\) we obtain\n\\[\nAF\\cdot AD = NA\\cdot NB .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nBecause \\(AB\\) is the common chord of the two circles, the right\u2011hand sides of (4) and (5) are equal; consequently\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AF\\cdot AD .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nEquation (6) is precisely the condition for the four points \\(A,E,F,C\\) to be concyclic. Since \\(B\\) lies on both original circles, the same equality shows that \\(B\\) also lies on the circle through \\(E\\) and \\(F\\). Hence \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic; denote this circle by \\(\\Omega_{BEF}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f5.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the Simson line of the point at infinity on \\(AP\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nIn any triangle, the Simson line of a point \\(X\\) on the circumcircle is the orthogonal projection of \\(X\\) onto the three sides; the line passes through the orthocenter when \\(X\\) is the point at infinity in a given direction.  \nLet the direction be that of \\(AP\\). The point at infinity in this direction, call it \\(X_{\\infty}\\), lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) because the circumcircle is invariant under translation along a direction parallel to a side. The Simson line of \\(X_{\\infty}\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is precisely the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Therefore the required line is a Simson line of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nA well\u2011known property of Simson lines is that the angle formed by the Simson line with any side of the reference triangle equals the angle subtended by the corresponding chord of the circumcircle at the point \\(X_{\\infty}\\). Applying this to side \\(PN\\) we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle\\bigl(\\text{Simson line},\\,PN\\bigr)=\\angle P X_{\\infty} N .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\nSince \\(X_{\\infty}\\) lies on the line parallel to \\(AP\\), the angle on the right of (7) equals \\(\\angle APN\\).\n\nConsequently, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with the chord \\(EF\\) the same angle as the chord \\(EF\\) subtends at the point \\(B\\) on \\(\\Omega_{BEF}\\) (see Lemma\u202f4). In symbols,\n\\[\n\\angle\\bigl(H\\!-\\!\\text{line},\\,EF\\bigr)=\\angle EBF .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f6.  Tangency criterion.\n\nFor a circle, a line is tangent at a point \\(T\\) iff the angle between the line and any chord through \\(T\\) equals the angle subtended by that chord at any other point of the circle. Equation (8) is exactly this condition for the chord \\(EF\\) of \\(\\Omega_{BEF}\\) with the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Hence that line is tangent to \\(\\Omega_{BEF}\\) at the point where it meets the circle; the point of tangency is the intersection of the line with the extension of \\(EF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) satisfies the tangent\u2011chord condition for the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Therefore it is tangent to that circumcircle, as was to be shown. \u220e"
