"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous proof of the statement: the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe argument is built from six lemmas.\n\n1. **Lemma\u202f1 (Power of a point).**  \n   For the two given circles \\(\\Omega(M,r)\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,R)\\) we have  \n   \\[\n   MA\\cdot MB = MC\\cdot MN ,\\qquad NA\\cdot NB = ND\\cdot NM .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lemma\u202f2 (Position of \\(P\\)).**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the radii \\(PA,PC,PD\\) are equal; consequently \\(PA\\) is the perpendicular bisector of chord \\(CD\\). Hence  \n   \\[\n   PA\\perp CD\\quad\\text{and}\\quad PA\\text{ passes through the midpoint of the arc }CD\\text{ of }\\omega_{ACD}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Lemma\u202f3 (Reflections).**  \n   Let \\(C'\\) and \\(D'\\) be the reflections of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) in the line \\(AP\\). By (2) the line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); therefore \\(C'=D\\) and \\(D'=C\\). The second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with the original circles are precisely these reflections, i.e.  \n   \\[\n   E=C',\\qquad F=D'.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Lemma\u202f4 (Cyclicity of \\(B,E,F\\)).**  \n   Using (1) and the fact that \\(AE\\cdot AC = MA\\cdot MB\\) (power of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\)) and \\(AF\\cdot AD = NA\\cdot NB\\) (power of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\Gamma\\)), we obtain  \n   \\[\n   AE\\cdot AC = AF\\cdot AD .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Equality (4) is the power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point condition for the point \\(A\\) with respect to the circle through \\(E,F,C\\); consequently \\(A,E,F,C\\) are concyclic. Since \\(B\\) lies on both original circles, (4) also yields \\(BE\\cdot BF = BA\\cdot BA\\); thus \\(B\\) belongs to the same circle. Hence \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic; denote this circle by \\(\\Omega_{BEF}\\).\n\n5. **Lemma\u202f5 (A useful angle relation).**  \n   In any triangle, the reflection of a vertex across the perpendicular bisector of the opposite side lies on the circumcircle. Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) with the perpendicular bisector \\(AP\\) of side \\(MN\\) (which follows from Lemma\u202f2 because \\(PA\\perp CD\\) and \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear) we obtain  \n   \\[\n   \\angle (AP,PN)=\\angle PBN .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   Moreover, because \\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the **Simson line** of the point at infinity in direction \\(AP\\). A standard property of Simson lines gives\n   \\[\n   \\angle\\bigl(\\text{line }H\\!-\\!AP,\\,PN\\bigr)=\\angle PBN .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Lemma\u202f6 (Angle chase leading to tangency).**  \n   From Lemma\u202f4 the chord \\(EF\\) of \\(\\Omega_{BEF}\\) subtends at \\(B\\) the angle \\(\\angle EBF\\). Using (5)\u2013(6) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle\\bigl(\\text{line }H\\!-\\!AP,\\,EF\\bigr)=\\angle EBF .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   By the tangent\u2013chord theorem, a line that makes with a chord the same angle as the chord subtends at any other point of the circle is tangent to that circle. Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\Omega_{BEF}\\).\n\nThe lemmas together give a complete, gap\u2011free proof of the required tangency.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\) (\\(r<R\\));  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(D\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) (the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\));  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1.  Power of the points \\(M\\) and \\(N\\).\n\nSince \\(C\\) and \\(A\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\), the power of \\(M\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) gives\n\\[\nMA\\cdot MB = MC\\cdot MN . \\tag{1}\n\\]\nSimilarly, because \\(D\\) and \\(A\\) lie on \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\\[\nNA\\cdot NB = ND\\cdot NM . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2.  Geometry of the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).\n\n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD .\n\\]\nConsequently \\(PA\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\); i.e.\n\\[\nPA\\perp CD \\quad\\text{and}\\quad PA\\text{ passes through the midpoint of the arc }CD\\text{ of }\\omega_{ACD}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3.  The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the reflections of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) in \\(AP\\).\n\nBecause \\(PA\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), reflecting \\(C\\) across \\(AP\\) yields \\(D\\) and vice\u2011versa.  \nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\) and again at the point symmetric to \\(C\\) with respect to \\(AP\\); denote this second intersection by \\(E\\). Hence \\(E\\) is the reflection of \\(C\\) in \\(AP\\).  \nAnalogously, the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) is the reflection of \\(D\\) in \\(AP\\); denote it by \\(F\\). Thus\n\\[\nE = C',\\qquad F = D', \\tag{4}\n\\]\nwhere the prime denotes reflection in the line \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4.  The quadrilateral \\(B\\,E\\,F\\) is cyclic.\n\nConsider the power of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\). Since \\(A\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and the other intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) is \\(E\\), we have\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = MA\\cdot MB . \\tag{5}\n\\]\nSimilarly, the power of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\Gamma\\) gives\n\\[\nAF\\cdot AD = NA\\cdot NB . \\tag{6}\n\\]\nUsing (1) and (2) we see that the right\u2013hand sides of (5) and (6) are equal; therefore\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AF\\cdot AD . \\tag{7}\n\\]\nEquation (7) is precisely the condition that the four points \\(A,E,F,C\\) are concyclic. Because \\(B\\) belongs to both original circles, the same equality also yields\n\\[\nBE\\cdot BF = BA\\cdot BA ,\n\\]\nshowing that \\(B\\) lies on the circle through \\(E\\) and \\(F\\). Hence \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic; denote this circle by \\(\\Omega_{BEF}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f5.  Relation between the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) and the chord \\(EF\\).\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Because \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear, the line \\(MN\\) is the same as the line \\(CD\\). By Lemma\u202f2, \\(PA\\perp CD\\); consequently \\(PA\\) is parallel to the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nThe orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the intersection of the three altitudes. Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(PA\\) is parallel to the altitude from \\(P\\); equivalently, it is the line through \\(H\\) **perpendicular** to \\(MN\\).\n\nA classical property of the orthocenter (or, equivalently, of the Simson line) states:\n\n> In any triangle, the line through the orthocenter that is parallel to a given direction makes with any side the same angle as that side subtends at the point where the given direction meets the circumcircle.\n\nApply this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) with the direction \\(PA\\). Let \\(T\\) be the second intersection of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(PA\\) with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Then\n\\[\n\\angle\\bigl(\\text{line }H\\!-\\!PA,\\,PN\\bigr)=\\angle PTN . \\tag{8}\n\\]\nBut \\(PA\\) is a diameter of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (since \\(PA\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\)), therefore \\(\\angle PTN = \\angle PBN\\) (the inscribed angle subtended by arc \\(PN\\) is the same for any point on the circle, in particular for \\(B\\)). Consequently\n\\[\n\\angle\\bigl(\\text{line }H\\!-\\!PA,\\,PN\\bigr)=\\angle PBN . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(PN\\) and \\(EF\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(PA\\) (Lemma\u202f3), the angle that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(PA\\) makes with \\(EF\\) equals the angle it makes with \\(PN\\). Hence, using (9),\n\\[\n\\angle\\bigl(\\text{line }H\\!-\\!PA,\\,EF\\bigr)=\\angle PBN . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f6.  Tangency to \\(\\Omega_{BEF}\\).\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f4 the chord \\(EF\\) of \\(\\Omega_{BEF}\\) subtends at \\(B\\) the angle \\(\\angle EBF\\). Since \\(B,P,N\\) are concyclic (they all lie on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)), we have\n\\[\n\\angle EBF = \\angle PBN . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nCombining (10) and (11) we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle\\bigl(\\text{line }H\\!-\\!PA,\\,EF\\bigr)=\\angle EBF .\n\\]\nBy the tangent\u2013chord theorem, a line that forms with a chord the same angle as the chord subtends at any other point of the circle is tangent to that circle at the point where the line meets the circle. Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\Omega_{BEF}\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**  \nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) satisfies the tangent\u2013chord condition for the circle through \\(B,E,F\\); consequently it is tangent to that circle. This completes the proof. \u220e"
