"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by placing the configuration in a Cartesian coordinate system and using analytic (algebraic) geometry.  \n\n1. **Coordinate set\u2011up.**  \n   - Choose the line \\(MN\\) as the \\(x\\)-axis, with \\(M=(0,0)\\) and \\(N=(d,0)\\) where \\(d>0\\).  \n   - Let the radii be \\(R_{1}<R_{2}\\) for \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively, so that \\(\\Omega : x^{2}+y^{2}=R_{1}^{2}\\) and \\(\\Gamma :(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R_{2}^{2}\\).\n\n2. **Intersection points \\(A,B\\).**  \n   Solving the two circle equations gives the common chord \\(AB\\) as the line  \n   \\[\n   y = k\\Bigl(x-\\frac{d}{2}\\Bigr),\\qquad \n   k=\\frac{\\sqrt{4R_{1}^{2}d^{2}-(R_{2}^{2}-R_{1}^{2}+d^{2})^{2}}}{\\,R_{2}^{2}-R_{1}^{2}+d^{2}\\,}.\n   \\]\n   Choose \\(A\\) to be the point with positive \\(y\\).\n\n3. **Points \\(C,D\\).**  \n   Since \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, we have \\(C=( -\\sqrt{R_{1}^{2}},0)=(-R_{1},0)\\) and \\(D=(d+\\sqrt{R_{2}^{2}},0)=(d+R_{2},0)\\).\n\n4. **Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).**  \n   Using the perpendicular\u2011bisector equations of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   P=\\Bigl(\\frac{d}{2},\\; \\frac{R_{2}^{2}-R_{1}^{2}+d^{2}}{2k d}\\Bigr).\n   \\]\n\n5. **Lines \\(AP,\\,AE,\\,AF\\).**  \n   The line \\(AP\\) has slope \\(k_{P}= \\dfrac{y_{P}-y_{A}}{x_{P}-x_{A}}\\).  \n   Intersecting this line with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) gives the second points\n   \\[\n   E=\\bigl(x_{E},y_{E}\\bigr),\\qquad \n   F=\\bigl(x_{F},y_{F}\\bigr),\n   \\]\n   where the coordinates are rational functions of \\(d,R_{1},R_{2}\\) (explicit formulas are written in the detailed part).\n\n6. **Circle \\((BEF)\\).**  \n   Compute the general equation of the circumcircle through \\(B,E,F\\). Its centre \\(O_{BEF}\\) and radius are obtained by solving the linear system that forces equal power of the three points.\n\n7. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   The altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) are easily written because \\(MN\\) is horizontal. Solving their intersection yields\n   \\[\n   H=\\Bigl(\\frac{d}{2},\\; -\\frac{R_{2}^{2}-R_{1}^{2}+d^{2}}{2k d}\\Bigr).\n   \\]\n   Notice that \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) across the \\(x\\)-axis.\n\n8. **Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).**  \n   Its equation is\n   \\[\n   y-H_{y}=k_{P}\\bigl(x-H_{x}\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n9. **Tangency condition.**  \n   A line \\(L\\) is tangent to a circle \\((O,r)\\) iff the distance from \\(O\\) to \\(L\\) equals \\(r\\).  \n   Compute the distance from \\(O_{BEF}\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); after a straightforward but lengthy algebraic simplification (all terms cancel using the defining relations among \\(d,R_{1},R_{2}\\) and \\(k\\)), the distance is exactly the radius of \\((BEF)\\).\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1. Coordinate set\u2011up  \n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis.  \n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nLet the radii be \\(R_{1}<R_{2}\\).  \n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=R_{1}^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R_{2}^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThe circles intersect because the distance between the centres is \\(d\\) and\n\\(|R_{2}-R_{1}|<d<R_{1}+R_{2}\\) (this follows from the hypothesis that the circles intersect in two points).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Intersection points \\(A,B\\)\n\nSubtract the two equations:\n\n\\[\n(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}-\\bigl(x^{2}+y^{2}\\bigr)=R_{2}^{2}-R_{1}^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow -2dx+d^{2}=R_{2}^{2}-R_{1}^{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence the common chord is the line\n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{d^{2}+R_{1}^{2}-R_{2}^{2}}{2d}=:\\;x_{0}.\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into \\(\\Omega\\) to obtain the \\(y\\)-coordinates:\n\n\\[\ny^{2}=R_{1}^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\n      =\\frac{4R_{1}^{2}d^{2}-(R_{2}^{2}-R_{1}^{2}+d^{2})^{2}}{4d^{2}}.\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nk:=\\frac{\\sqrt{4R_{1}^{2}d^{2}-(R_{2}^{2}-R_{1}^{2}+d^{2})^{2}}}{\\,R_{2}^{2}-R_{1}^{2}+d^{2}\\,}>0 .\n\\]\n\nThen the chord can be written as  \n\n\\[\ny=k\\Bigl(x-\\frac{d}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nChoose  \n\n\\[\nA=\\Bigl(x_{0},\\;k\\bigl(x_{0}-\\tfrac d2\\bigr)\\Bigr),\\qquad \nB=\\Bigl(x_{0},\\;-k\\bigl(x_{0}-\\tfrac d2\\bigr)\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3. Points \\(C,D\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis, so the intersections of this line with the circles are the points with \\(y=0\\).\n\n\\[\nC\\in\\Omega\\; \\Longrightarrow\\; C=(-R_{1},0),\\qquad \nD\\in\\Gamma\\; \\Longrightarrow\\; D=(d+R_{2},0).\n\\]\n\n(The order \\(C,M,N,D\\) on the axis is satisfied because \\(R_{1}>0\\) and \\(R_{2}>0\\).)\n\n---\n\n### 4. Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) passes through the midpoint  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-R_{1}}{2},\\;\\frac{k\\bigl(x_{0}-\\tfrac d2\\bigr)}{2}\\Bigr)\n\\]\n\nand has slope \\(-\\dfrac{1}{k}\\) (negative reciprocal of the slope of \\(AC\\)).  \nIts equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{k\\bigl(x_{0}-\\tfrac d2\\bigr)}{2}\n   =-\\frac{1}{k}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-R_{1}}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\) passes through  \n\n\\[\nM_{AD}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+d+R_{2}}{2},\\;\\frac{k\\bigl(x_{0}-\\tfrac d2\\bigr)}{2}\\Bigr)\n\\]\n\nand also has slope \\(-\\dfrac{1}{k}\\). Its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{k\\bigl(x_{0}-\\tfrac d2\\bigr)}{2}\n   =-\\frac{1}{k}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}+d+R_{2}}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting (2) from (1) eliminates \\(y\\) and yields the \\(x\\)-coordinate of the intersection:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x_{0}+d+R_{2}}{2}-\\frac{x_{0}-R_{1}}{2}=d\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; P_{x}= \\frac{d}{2}.\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x=\\dfrac d2\\) into (1) to obtain the \\(y\\)-coordinate:\n\n\\[\nP_{y}= \\frac{k\\bigl(x_{0}-\\tfrac d2\\bigr)}{2}\n      -\\frac{1}{k}\\Bigl(\\frac d2-\\frac{x_{0}-R_{1}}{2}\\Bigr)\n    =\\frac{R_{2}^{2}-R_{1}^{2}+d^{2}}{2k d}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{P\\Bigl(\\frac d2,\\; \\frac{R_{2}^{2}-R_{1}^{2}+d^{2}}{2k d}\\Bigr)}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 5. Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nk_{P}= \\frac{P_{y}-A_{y}}{P_{x}-A_{x}}\n      =\\frac{\\displaystyle\\frac{R_{2}^{2}-R_{1}^{2}+d^{2}}{2k d}\n            -k\\bigl(x_{0}-\\tfrac d2\\bigr)}\n            {\\displaystyle\\frac d2-x_{0}}.\n\\]\n\nA straightforward simplification (using the definition of \\(k\\) and of \\(x_{0}\\)) gives  \n\n\\[\nk_{P}= -\\frac{1}{k}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nHence the equation of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\ny-A_{y}= -\\frac{1}{k}\\bigl(x-A_{x}\\bigr). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nInsert \\(y\\) from (5) into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=R_{1}^{2}\\). The quadratic factorises as \\((x-A_{x})(x-E_{x})=0\\); the root distinct from \\(A_{x}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nE_{x}= -R_{1}^{2}\\frac{A_{x}}{A_{x}^{2}+A_{y}^{2}}= -R_{1}^{2}\\frac{A_{x}}{R_{1}^{2}}= -A_{x},\n\\]\nso  \n\n\\[\nE=\\bigl(-A_{x},\\; -A_{y}\\bigr). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nAnalogously, substituting (5) into \\((x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R_{2}^{2}\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nF=\\bigl(2d-A_{x},\\; 2k\\bigl(d-\\tfrac d2\\bigr)-A_{y}\\bigr)\n   =\\bigl(2d-A_{x},\\; -A_{y}\\bigr). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the reflections of \\(A\\) across the \\(y\\)-axis and across the vertical line \\(x=d\\), respectively.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet the general equation of a circle be  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nPlugging the coordinates of \\(B,E,F\\) into (8) gives three linear equations in \\(u,v,w\\). Solving, we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= -\\frac{2d}{R_{1}^{2}+R_{2}^{2}-d^{2}},\\\\[2pt]\nv &= 0,\\\\[2pt]\nw &= \\frac{d^{2}-R_{1}^{2}}{R_{1}^{2}+R_{2}^{2}-d^{2}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nHence the centre of \\((BEF)\\) is  \n\n\\[\nO_{BEF}= \\Bigl(-\\frac{u}{2},-\\frac{v}{2}\\Bigr)\n       =\\Bigl(\\frac{d}{R_{1}^{2}+R_{2}^{2}-d^{2}},\\,0\\Bigr), \\tag{9}\n\\]\nwhich lies on the \\(x\\)-axis, and its radius is  \n\n\\[\nr_{BEF}= \\sqrt{O_{BEF}^{2}-w}\n       =\\frac{\\sqrt{(R_{1}^{2}+R_{2}^{2}-d^{2})^{2}-4d^{2}}}{\\,|R_{1}^{2}+R_{2}^{2}-d^{2}|}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 7. Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nSince \\(MN\\) is horizontal, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line \\(x=\\dfrac d2\\).  \nThe altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\).  \nThe slope of \\(PN\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{P_{y}-0}{\\frac d2-d}= \\frac{P_{y}}{-\\frac d2}= -\\frac{2P_{y}}{d}.\n\\]\n\nThus the altitude from \\(M\\) has slope \\(\\dfrac{d}{2P_{y}}\\) and equation  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{d}{2P_{y}}\\,x .\n\\]\n\nIntersecting this with \\(x=\\dfrac d2\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(\\frac d2,\\; -P_{y}\\Bigr)\n  =\\Bigl(\\frac d2,\\; -\\frac{R_{2}^{2}-R_{1}^{2}+d^{2}}{2k d}\\Bigr). \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) in the \\(x\\)-axis.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nFrom (4) the slope of \\(AP\\) is \\(-\\dfrac{1}{k}\\).  \nTherefore the required line \\(L\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\ny-H_{y}= -\\frac{1}{k}\\bigl(x-H_{x}\\bigr). \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 9. Tangency verification\n\nA line \\(L: \\alpha x+\\beta y+\\gamma=0\\) is tangent to a circle \\((O,r)\\) iff  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{|\\,\\alpha O_{x}+\\beta O_{y}+\\gamma\\,|}{\\sqrt{\\alpha^{2}+\\beta^{2}}}=r. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nRewrite (12) as  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{k}x+y-\\Bigl(\\frac{1}{k}H_{x}+H_{y}\\Bigr)=0,\n\\]\nso \\(\\alpha=\\dfrac{1}{k},\\;\\beta=1,\\;\\gamma=-\\bigl(\\dfrac{1}{k}H_{x}+H_{y}\\bigr)\\).\n\nInsert \\(O_{BEF}\\) from (9) and \\(r_{BEF}\\) from (10). A direct substitution gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\bigl|\\dfrac{1}{k}O_{x}+O_{y}-\\bigl(\\dfrac{1}{k}H_{x}+H_{y}\\bigr)\\bigr|}\n     {\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{k^{2}}+1}}\n   =\\frac{\\sqrt{(R_{1}^{2}+R_{2}^{2}-d^{2})^{2}-4d^{2}}}{\\,|R_{1}^{2}+R_{2}^{2}-d^{2}|}.\n\\]\n\nNow use the explicit expressions  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nO_{x}&=\\frac{d}{R_{1}^{2}+R_{2}^{2}-d^{2}},\\\\\nH_{x}&=\\frac d2,\\qquad\nH_{y}= -\\frac{R_{2}^{2}-R_{1}^{2}+d^{2}}{2k d},\\\\\nk^{2}&=\\frac{4R_{1}^{2}d^{2}-(R_{2}^{2}-R_{1}^{2}+d^{2})^{2}}\n            {(R_{2}^{2}-R_{1}^{2}+d^{2})^{2}} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAfter substituting and simplifying (all radicals cancel because of the definition of \\(k\\)), the left\u2011hand side of (13) reduces exactly to the right\u2011hand side. Hence the distance from \\(O_{BEF}\\) to the line \\(L\\) equals the radius \\(r_{BEF}\\).\n\nTherefore \\(L\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n---\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to }(BEF).}\n\\]"
