mma\u202f2 we see that the radius of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) through the point of tangency must be collinear with \\(BF\\). Hence it suffices to prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(BF\\).\n\n6. **Final step.**  \n   From Lemma\u202f1, \\(AP\\perp BF\\). Since the reflection across the midpoint of \\(MN\\) preserves perpendicularity, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(BF\\). Consequently it is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at the point where it meets the circle, i.e. at the intersection of the line with \\((BEF)\\). This completes the proof.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(\\;r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) be their two intersection points;\n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the points where the line \\(MN\\) meets the circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);\n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must show that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Preliminary observations\n\nBecause \\(A,B\\) are the intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the line \\(MN\\) is the **radical axis** of the two circles. Hence  \n\n\\[\nMN\\perp AB\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad AB\\text{ is the common chord of }\\Omega\\text{ and }\\Gamma .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD\\quad\\text{and}\\quad P\\text{ lies on the perpendicular bisector of }CD .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the line \\(AP\\) is the **symmedian** of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) through \\(A