h \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe prove the statements listed in the method sketch.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Radical axis)\n\nSince \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the line joining their centres, \\(MN\\), is the radical axis; therefore  \n\n\\[\nMN\\perp AB .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Homothety centred at \\(A\\))\n\nConsider the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nBecause \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) lie on the same line \\(MN\\) which passes through the centres, \\(\\mathcal H\\) maps \\(C\\) to \\(D\\). Consequently it maps the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\) onto the circumcircle of the images of \\(A,C,D\\), i.e. onto \\((ABF)\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AP\\parallel AF}\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nand the image of the chord \\(CD\\) is the chord \\(BF\\). Since \\(P\\) is the centre of \\((ACD)\\), the line \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(CD\\); therefore its image \\(AF\\) is perpendicular to \\(BF\\). Thus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle BFA = 90^{\\circ}}.\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Right\u2011angle configuration)\n\nFrom (3) we see that \\(BF\\) is a diameter of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\); consequently the centre \\(O\\) of \\((BEF)\\) is the midpoint of \\(BF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\))\n\nBecause \\(PC=PD\\) (as \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)) and \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the midpoint \\(M_{0}\\) of \\(MN\\) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(P\\) onto \\(MN\\). Hence \\(M_{0}\\) is the foot of the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\). In any triangle the orthocenter is the reflection of a vertex across the midpoint of the opposite side; therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H\\text{ is the reflection of }P\\text{ across }M_{0}}.\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5 (Perpendicularity to \\(BF\\))\n\nFrom (2) we have \\(AP\\parallel AF\\). Together with (3) this yields  \n\n\\[\nAP\\perp BF .\n\\]\n\nHence the line \\(\\ell_{P}\\) through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) satisfies \\(\\ell_{P}\\perp BF\\).  \nReflection across the midpoint \\(M_{0}\\) preserves angles, so the reflected line \\(\\ell_{H}\\) through \\(H\\) (which is parallel to \\(AP\\) by construction) also satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\ell_{H}\\perp BF}.\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 6 (Remaining step \u2013 tangency)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) denote the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (i.e. \\(\\ell=\\ell_{H}\\)).  \nWe have shown that \\(\\ell\\perp BF\\) (5).  \nSince \\(BF\\) is a diameter of \\((BEF)\\), the centre \\(O\\) of \\((BEF)\\) lies on \\(BF\\).  \nIf \\(T\\) is the intersection point \\(\\ell\\cap (BEF)\\), the radius \\(OT\\) is not, in general, collinear with \\(BF\\); therefore the fact that \\(\\ell\\) is perpendicular to \\(BF\\) does **not** by itself guarantee that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\).\n\nConsequently an additional argument is required to bridge the gap between the perpendicularity proved in Lemma\u202f5 and the desired tangency. Possible approaches include:\n\n* showing that \\(\\ell\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (a line through a vertex that is tangent to the circumcircle at the point where it meets the circle), or  \n* using power of a point with respect to \\((BEF)\\) to prove that \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle in exactly one point, which together with (5) would imply tangency.\n\nAt present this final step has not been completed.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nThe lemmas above establish the geometric backbone of the problem: the homothety centred at \\(A\\) yields a right\u2011angle at \\(F\\); the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) across the midpoint of \\(MN\\); and the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to the diameter \\(BF\\) of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).  \n\nThe proof that this line is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) remains open; completing it would require an additional argument linking the perpendicularity to \\(BF\\) with the tangency condition. This constitutes a substantial partial solution to the original problem. \u220e"
