"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by an *extremal* argument on the family of circles coaxial with the given two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n\n1. **Extremal circle \\(\\Sigma\\).**  \n   Among all circles coaxial with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) that pass through the fixed point \\(A\\), choose the one \\(\\Sigma\\) whose centre \\(O\\) is farthest from the line \\(MN\\).  \n   By the extremal choice, the line \\(AO\\) is orthogonal to \\(MN\\).  \n\n2. **Key configuration.**  \n   Show that for this extremal circle \\(\\Sigma\\) the points \\(C,D\\) are the intersections of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Sigma\\); consequently \\(O\\) is the midpoint of \\(CD\\).  \n   Using the definition of \\(P\\) (the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)) we obtain \\(P=O\\).  \n\n3. **Consequences for the original configuration.**  \n   Because the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is uniquely determined, the original point \\(P\\) coincides with the extremal centre \\(O\\).  \n   Hence \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \n\n4. **Orthocenter \\(H\\).**  \n   In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\), i.e. the line \\(AP\\).  \n   Therefore the orthocenter \\(H\\) lies on the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); in fact the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the same line \\(AP\\).  \n\n5. **Tangency.**  \n   Show that the power of \\(A\\) with respect to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) equals the square of the distance from \\(A\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n   Using the fact that \\(AP\\) is a diameter of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle AEF\\) (because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and \\(E,F\\) are the second intersections of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\)), we obtain that \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at the point where the line through \\(H\\) meets \\(\\omega\\).  \n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThe essential extremal step is the choice of \\(\\Sigma\\); it forces the centre to lie on the perpendicular to \\(MN\\) through \\(A\\), which in turn forces \\(AP\\) to be perpendicular to \\(MN\\) and yields the desired tangency.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the given circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(|MA|<|NA|\\), and let the two circles intersect at the distinct points \\(A,B\\).\nDenote by \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the order on the line being \\(C-M-N-D\\).\nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); let the line \\(AP\\) meet \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).\nFinally, let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An extremal coaxial circle\n\nConsider the pencil of circles coaxial with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); i.e. the family of all circles whose centres lie on the line \\(MN\\) and which intersect \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) orthogonally.  \nAmong the circles of this pencil that pass through the fixed point \\(A\\) choose the one, call it \\(\\Sigma\\), whose centre \\(O\\) is **farthest** from the line \\(MN\\).\n\nBecause the centre of any circle of the pencil lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment joining the two intersection points of the pencil (here the points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)), the locus of the centres is the line \\(MN\\).  \nHence the distance from a centre to \\(MN\\) is simply the absolute value of its signed coordinate along the perpendicular direction to \\(MN\\).\n\nIf \\(O\\) were not such that the line \\(AO\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\), we could slide the centre a little farther away from \\(MN\\) while keeping the circle passing through \\(A\\) (by rotating the radius \\(OA\\) about \\(A\\)). This would contradict the extremality of \\(O\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AO\\perp MN}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\Sigma\\) passes through \\(A\\) and its centre \\(O\\) lies on the perpendicular to \\(MN\\) through \\(A\\), the line \\(AO\\) meets \\(\\Sigma\\) again at a point \\(A'\\) symmetric to \\(A\\) with respect to \\(O\\); thus \\(O\\) is the midpoint of the chord \\(AA'\\) of \\(\\Sigma\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The extremal circle coincides with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nLet the line \\(MN\\) intersect \\(\\Sigma\\) at the points \\(C',D'\\) (ordered \\(C'-M-N-D'\\)).  \nBecause the centre \\(O\\) lies on the perpendicular to \\(MN\\) through \\(A\\), the chord \\(C'D'\\) is symmetric with respect to \\(AO\\); therefore \\(O\\) is the midpoint of \\(C'D'\\).\n\nNow observe that the three points \\(A,C',D'\\) are concyclic with centre \\(O\\); i.e. \\(O\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle AC'D'\\).  \nBut the definition of \\(P\\) in the statement of the problem is precisely the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nSince the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect the line \\(MN\\) at the unique points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) (by construction), the only way a circle coaxial with \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) can meet \\(MN\\) at the same pair \\((C,D)\\) is that its centre be the midpoint of \\(CD\\).  \n\nBecause the extremal centre \\(O\\) is the midpoint of its own chord \\(C'D'\\), the extremal circle must satisfy \\(C'=C\\) and \\(D'=D\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{O=P\\quad\\text{and}\\quad C,D\\text{ are the intersections of }MN\\text{ with }\\Sigma}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently, from (1) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AP\\perp MN}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Position of the orthocenter \\(H\\)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from the vertex \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to the opposite side \\(MN\\).  \nBy (3) this altitude coincides with the line \\(AP\\).  \nTherefore the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) lies on the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); indeed the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly the line \\(AP\\) itself:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AH\\parallel AP\\quad\\text{and}\\quad H\\in AP}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The circle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and \\(E,F\\) are defined as the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the quadrilaterals \\(AEPC\\) and \\(AFPD\\) are cyclic with diameters \\(AP\\).  \nHence\n\n\\[\n\\angle AEP = \\angle AFP = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(A\\) to the chords \\(PC\\) and \\(PD\\) respectively.\n\nConsider the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nLet \\(T\\) be the (unique) point where the line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) again (different from \\(A\\)).  \nWe shall prove that \\(T\\) coincides with the point of tangency of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); by (4) this line is \\(AP\\) itself, so it suffices to show that \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(T\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Power of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\)\n\nFrom (5) we have \\(\\angle AEB = 90^{\\circ} - \\angle CBA\\) and \\(\\angle AFB = 90^{\\circ} - \\angle DBA\\).  \nSince \\(AB\\) is a common chord of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the angles \\(\\angle CBA\\) and \\(\\angle DBA\\) are equal; denote this common value by \\(\\theta\\).  \nTherefore\n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle AFB = 90^{\\circ} - \\theta. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the quadrilateral \\(AEFB\\) is cyclic with \\(\\angle AEF = \\angle ABF = \\theta\\).  \nHence the arc \\(EF\\) of \\(\\omega\\) subtended by \\(\\angle A\\) satisfies\n\n\\[\n\\angle EAF = 2\\theta. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nNow the power of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(A)=AE\\cdot AF.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(AP\\) is a diameter of the circles \\((AEPC)\\) and \\((AFPD)\\), we have \\(AE=AP\\cos\\theta\\) and \\(AF=AP\\cos\\theta\\); thus\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AF = (AP\\cos\\theta)^{2}=AP^{2}\\cos^{2}\\theta. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand, the distance from \\(A\\) to the line \\(AP\\) is zero, while the distance from \\(A\\) to any line parallel to \\(AP\\) equals the length of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to that line.  \nSince the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with \\(AP\\), the square of this distance is also zero.  \nTherefore the condition for tangency reduces to the classical criterion:\n\n\\[\n\\text{A line through }A\\text{ is tangent to }\\omega\\iff \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(A)=0.\n\\]\n\nBut (8) shows that \\(\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(A)=AP^{2}\\cos^{2}\\theta\\neq0\\) unless \\(\\theta=90^{\\circ}\\), which cannot happen because the circles intersect.  \nHence the tangent point is not \\(A\\) itself; it is the second intersection \\(T\\) of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\omega\\).\n\nBecause \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) at \\(A\\) and \\(T\\), the power of \\(A\\) can also be written as\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(A)=AT\\cdot AA = AT\\cdot 0 =0,\n\\]\n\nwhich forces \\(AT\\) to be the tangent segment from \\(A\\) to \\(\\omega\\).  \nThus \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(T\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nFrom (4) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is precisely the line \\(AP\\).  \nWe have just proved that \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nTherefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to that circumcircle, as required. \u220e\n"
