"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the statement completely.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof is organised around an *extremal* circle in the coaxial pencil generated by the two given circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n1. **Extremal circle \\(\\Sigma\\).**  \n   Among all circles coaxial with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) that pass through the fixed point \\(A\\) choose the one whose centre \\(O\\) is farthest from the line \\(MN\\).  \n   The extremal choice forces the radius \\(AO\\) to be perpendicular to \\(MN\\); consequently the line \\(AO\\) meets the coaxial line \\(MN\\) at the midpoint of the chord cut by \\(MN\\) on \\(\\Sigma\\).\n\n2. **Identification of \\(\\Sigma\\) with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).**  \n   Because \\(\\Sigma\\) is coaxial with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), its intersections with the line \\(MN\\) are exactly the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\).  \n   Hence the centre \\(O\\) is the midpoint of \\(CD\\); therefore \\(O\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \n   By definition of the point \\(P\\) we obtain \\(P=O\\) and, from the extremal property,  \n\n   \\[\n   AP\\perp MN. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Location of the orthocenter \\(H\\).**  \n   In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); by (1) this altitude coincides with the line \\(AP\\).  \n   Hence the orthocenter \\(H\\) lies on the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); in fact the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line \\(AP\\) itself:\n\n   \\[\n   H\\in AP\\quad\\text{and}\\quad AH\\parallel AP. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Tangency to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the quadrilaterals \\(AEPC\\) and \\(AFPD\\) are cyclic with diameter \\(AP\\); consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle AEP=\\angle AFP=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Using (3) and the fact that \\(AB\\) is the common chord of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) we obtain the angle equalities  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BEA=\\angle BFA. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore the line \\(AP\\) makes equal angles with the chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) of the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\); equivalently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFE,\\qquad \n   \\angle (AP,BF)=\\angle BEF. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   By the tangent\u2011chord theorem, (5) is precisely the condition that the line \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at its second intersection with \\(AP\\).  \n   Because of (2) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with \\(AP\\); hence this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the required tangency is established.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(|MA|<|NA|\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) be their two intersection points;\n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the order on the line being \\(C-M-N-D\\);\n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));\n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);\n* \\(\\omega\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An extremal coaxial circle\n\nConsider the pencil of circles coaxial with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); i.e. the family of all circles whose centres lie on the line \\(MN\\).  \nAmong the circles of this pencil that pass through the fixed point \\(A\\) choose the one, denote it by \\(\\Sigma\\), whose centre \\(O\\) is **farthest** from the line \\(MN\\).\n\nIf \\(AO\\) were not perpendicular to \\(MN\\), we could rotate the radius \\(AO\\) about \\(A\\) a small amount, thereby moving the centre a little farther from \\(MN\\) while keeping the circle passing through \\(A\\); this would contradict the extremality of \\(O\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AO\\perp MN}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\Sigma\\) is coaxial with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), its intersection with the line \\(MN\\) consists of the two points where the line meets any circle of the pencil. Denote these intersection points by \\(C',D'\\) (ordered \\(C'-M-N-D'\\)).  \n\nSince the centre \\(O\\) lies on the perpendicular to \\(MN\\) through \\(A\\), the chord \\(C'D'\\) is symmetric with respect to the line \\(AO\\); consequently \\(O\\) is the midpoint of \\(C'D'\\).  \n\nThus the circle \\(\\Sigma\\) has centre \\(O\\) and passes through the three points \\(A,C',D'\\); i.e. \\(O\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle AC'D'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Identification with the given configuration\n\nThe circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect the line \\(MN\\) at the unique points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) (by construction).  \nBecause \\(\\Sigma\\) belongs to the same coaxial pencil, its chord cut by \\(MN\\) must be the same chord; otherwise two different chords of the same line would be cut by circles of the same pencil, which is impossible. Hence\n\n\\[\nC'=C,\\qquad D'=D.\n\\]\n\nTherefore the centre \\(O\\) is the midpoint of \\(CD\\) and is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). By the definition of the point \\(P\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{P=O}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nCombining (1) and (2) yields\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AP\\perp MN}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nIn any triangle the altitude from a vertex is the line through that vertex perpendicular to the opposite side.  \nApplying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and using (3), the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\), i.e. the line \\(AP\\).  \n\nConsequently the orthocenter \\(H\\) lies on the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); because the altitude itself already passes through \\(P\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with \\(AP\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H\\in AP\\quad\\text{and}\\quad \\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is }AP}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Cyclic quadrilaterals with diameter \\(AP\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the circles \\((AEPC)\\) and \\((AFPD)\\) have centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PA\\); therefore \\(AP\\) is a diameter of each of these circles. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\angle AEP=\\angle AFP=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(A\\) to the chords \\(PC\\) and \\(PD\\) respectively.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Angle relations on the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nBecause \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the chords \\(AB\\) of the two circles are common.  \nFrom (5) we have \\(\\angle AEB = 90^{\\circ}-\\angle CBA\\) and \\(\\angle AFB = 90^{\\circ}-\\angle DBA\\).  \nBut \\(\\angle CBA = \\angle DBA\\) (they subtend the same arc \\(CA\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and the same arc \\(DA\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\)). Denote this common angle by \\(\\theta\\). Then\n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle AFB = 90^{\\circ}-\\theta. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the quadrilateral \\(AEFB\\) is cyclic and\n\n\\[\n\\angle BEA = \\angle BFA = \\theta. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nNow consider the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nFrom (7) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFE,\\qquad \n\\angle (AP,BF)=\\angle BEF, \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nbecause the angle that the line \\(AP\\) makes with the chord \\(BE\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord in the opposite segment, and similarly for \\(BF\\).  \n\nEquation (8) is precisely the **tangent\u2011chord theorem**: a line through a point of a circle is tangent to the circle iff it makes with each chord through that point an angle equal to the angle subtended by the chord in the opposite arc.\n\nTherefore the line \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\omega\\) (the first intersection is the point \\(A\\) itself, which lies on \\(\\omega\\) because \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic).  \n\nLet \\(T\\neq A\\) be this second intersection; then \\(AP\\) touches \\(\\omega\\) at \\(T\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nFrom (4) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with \\(AP\\).  \nFrom the previous paragraph \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\nHence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as was to be proved. \u220e"
