itude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) that\nis perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Since \\(MN\\) is a straight line, the foot of this\naltitude is the orthogonal projection of \\(P\\) onto \\(MN\\); denote this foot\nby \\(H'\\). By definition of the orthocenter, the altitude from \\(M\\) meets\nthe altitude from \\(P\\) at the orthocenter \\(H\\). Because the altitude from\n\\(M\\) is the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\), and \\(PN\\) is\nsymmetric with respect to \\(MN\\) to the line \\(PC\\) (both \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie\non \\(MN\\)), the intersection point is exactly the reflection of \\(P\\) across\n\\(MN\\). Hence \\(H\\) lies on \\(MN\\) and \\(MH=MP\\cos\\angle PMN\\), i.e. \\(H\\) is\nthe mirror image of \\(P\\) in \\(MN\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the antipodes of \\(A\\) on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)\nrespectively; equivalently,\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{AE}=-\\overrightarrow{AO},\\qquad\n\\overrightarrow{AF}=-\\overrightarrow{AN}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(AP\\) is a\nperpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\) of the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\).\nConsequently \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of that circle, i.e. through\nthe midpoint of the arc \\(CD\\) that does not contain \\(A\\). Because \\(C\\) and\n\\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the midpoint of arc \\(CD\\) is exactly the\nmidpoint of the segment joining the centres \\(O\\) and \\(N\\); denote this\nmidpoint by \\(M_{0}\\). Hence the line \\(AP\\) passes through \\(M_{0}\\).\n\nNow consider the intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\). The two\nintersection points are \\(A\\) and a second point \\(E\\). Since \\(O\\) is the\ncentre of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(AP\\) passes through the midpoint of the chord\n\\(CD\\), which lies on the diameter \\(ON\\), the line \\(AP\\) is orthogonal to\n\\(OA\\). Therefore \\(AE\\) is a diameter of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(E\\) is the\nantipode of \\(A\\) on \\(\\Omega\\). The same reasoning applied to the circle\n\\(\\Gamma\\) shows that \\(AF\\) is a diameter of \\(\\Gamma\\) and \\(F\\) is the\nantipode of \\(A\\) on \\(\\Gamma\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3.  Quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is an isosceles trapezoid with\n\\(AE\\parallel BF\\) and \\(AF\\parallel BE\\); consequently the points\n\\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic and the centre of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) lies on\nthe line \\(MN\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f2, \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) are diameters of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)\nrespectively; therefore \\(AE\\perp OA\\) and \\(AF\\perp NA\\). Because \\(AB\\) is\nthe common chord of the two circles, the line \\(AB\\) is orthogonal to the\nline joining the centres \\(O\\) and \\(N\\); i.e. \\(AB\\perp ON\\). Since\n\\(ON\\) is collinear with \\(MN\\), we have\n\\[\n\\angle (AB,AE)=\\angle (AB,AF)=90^{\\circ}.\n\\]\nThus \\(AE\\) is parallel to the line through \\(B\\) that is perpendicular to\n\\(AB\\); the same line is \\(BF\\). Hence \\(AE\\parallel BF\\). By symmetry we also\nobtain \\(AF\\parallel BE\\). Therefore opposite sides of quadrilateral\n\\(ABEF\\) are parallel, so it is an isosceles trapezoid. Any isosceles trapezoid\nis cyclic; consequently \\(B,E,F\\) lie on a common circle, denote it by\n\\((BEF)\\). The axis of symmetry of the trapezoid is the line through the\nmidpoints of the parallel sides, which is precisely the line \\(MN\\); hence\nthe centre \\(O_{BEF}\\) of \\((BEF)\\) belongs to \\(MN\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4.  For any line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) we have\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,\\, BE)=\\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and \\(BE\\) equals the\nangle between \\(AP\\) and \\(BE\\):\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,\\, BE)=\\angle(AP ,\\, BE). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f3, \\(BE\\parallel AF\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\angle(AP ,\\, BE)=\\angle(AP ,\\, AF). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(AF\\) is a diameter of \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore \\(\\angle(AP ,\\, AF)\\) is\nthe angle subtended by chord \\(AB\\) at the point \\(A\\) on the circle\n\\(\\Gamma\\). By the Inscribed\u2011Angle Theorem,\n\\[\n\\angle(AP ,\\, AF)=\\angle BFA .\n\\]\nSince \\(A,B,F\\) are concyclic, the inscribed angle theorem again gives\n\\[\n\\angle BFA=\\angle BEA .\n\\]\nFinally, because \\(AE\\) is a diameter of \\(\\Omega\\), the angle \\(\\angle BEA\\)\nis the angle subtended by chord \\(BE\\) at the point \\(A\\) on the circle\n\\(\\Omega\\); consequently it equals the angle subtended by the same chord at\nany point on the opposite arc, i.e.\n\\[\n\\angle BEA=\\angle BFE .\n\\]\nCombining the equalities (1)\u2013(5) yields the desired relation\n\\(\\angle(\\ell ,\\, BE)=\\angle BFE\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f4 we have \\(\\angle(\\ell ,\\, BE)=\\angle BFE\\). In a circle, a line\nthrough a point outside the circle is tangent precisely when the angle it\nmakes with a chord equals the angle subtended by that chord in the opposite\narc. Since \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic (Lemma\u202f3), the equality\n\\(\\angle(\\ell ,\\, BE)=\\angle BFE\\) tells us that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the\ncircumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(E\\) (or, equivalently, at the point\n\\(F\\); the two descriptions are symmetric).  \n\nTherefore the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that\nis parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
