ameter of \\(\\Gamma\\) and \\(F\\) is the\nantipode of \\(A\\) on \\(\\Gamma\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3.  Quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is an isosceles trapezoid with\n\\(AE\\parallel BF\\) and \\(AF\\parallel BE\\); consequently the points\n\\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic and the centre of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) lies on\nthe line \\(MN\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f2, \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) are diameters of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)\nrespectively; therefore \\(AE\\perp OA\\) and \\(AF\\perp NA\\). Because \\(AB\\) is\nthe common chord of the two circles, the line \\(AB\\) is orthogonal to the\nline joining the centres \\(O\\) and \\(N\\); i.e. \\(AB\\perp ON\\). Since\n\\(ON\\) is collinear with \\(MN\\), we have\n\\[\n\\angle (AB,AE)=\\angle (AB,AF)=90^{\\circ}.\n\\]\nThus \\(AE\\) is parallel to the line through \\(B\\) that is perpendicular to\n\\(AB\\); the same line is \\(BF\\). Hence \\(AE\\parallel BF\\). By symmetry we also\nobtain \\(AF\\parallel BE\\). Therefore opposite sides of quadrilateral\n\\(ABEF\\) are parallel, so it is an isosceles trapezoid. Any isosceles trapezoid\nis cyclic; consequently \\(B,E,F\\) lie on a common circle, denote it by\n\\((BEF)\\). The axis of symmetry of the trapezoid is the line through the\nmidpoints of the parallel sides, which is precisely the line \\(MN\\); hence\nthe centre \\(O_{BEF}\\) of \\((BEF)\\) belongs to \\(MN\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4.  For any line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) we have\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,\\, BE)=\\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and \\(BE\\) equals the\nangle between \\(AP\\) and \\(BE\\):\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,\\, BE)=\\angle(AP ,\\, BE). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f3, \\(BE\\parallel AF\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\angle(AP ,\\, BE)=\\angle(AP ,\\, AF). \\tag{2}\