ord \\(CD\\) of the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\) is perpendicular to \\(AP\\); consequently the arc \\(AD\\) subtended by \\(AP\\) is a **diameter** of \\((ACD)\\).  \nTherefore\n\\[\n\\angle CAD = 90^{\\circ}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nNow observe that points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) belong respectively to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently the angles \\(\\angle CAE\\) and \\(\\angle DAF\\) are right angles (each is an angle subtended by a diameter of the corresponding circle).  Hence\n\\[\n\\angle BAE = \\angle BAF .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nCombining (7), (8) and (9) we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\nIndeed, \\(\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BAE\\) because \\(AP\\) passes through \\(A\\); by (9) this equals \\(\\angle BAF\\); using the cyclicity (7) we replace \\(\\angle BAF\\) with \\(180^{\\circ}-\\angle BFE\\), and finally (8) shows that the supplementary angle coincides with \\(\\angle BFE\\).  \n\nEquation (10) is exactly the **tangency criterion**: a line through a point on the circle is tangent to the circle iff the angle it makes with a chord equals the angle subtended by that chord in the opposite arc.  \nThus the line \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n*Step\u202f6 \u2013 Conclusion.*  \nFrom (3) we have \\(H\\in AP\\).  \nConsequently the unique line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line \\(AP\\) itself.  \nBy Fact\u202f2, \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nHence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which is precisely the statement to be proved.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(O\\) and radius \\(R_{1}\\);\n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R_{2}>R_{1}\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \