) lie on the same line \\(MN\\); therefore the chord\n\\(CD\\) of the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\) is collinear with \\(MN\\).\nIn any circle the radius drawn to the midpoint of a chord is perpendicular\nto the chord; consequently the radius \\(PA\\) is perpendicular to the chord\n\\(CD\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\nAP\\perp CD\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad AP\\perp MN . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Position of the orthocenter \\(H\\)\n\nIn triangle \\(PMN\\) the altitude issued from vertex \\(P\\) is the line through\n\\(P\\) perpendicular to the opposite side \\(MN\\).  By (2) this altitude is the\nline \\(AP\\).  Therefore the orthocenter \\(H\\) \u2013 the intersection of the three\naltitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) \u2013 lies on \\(AP\\):\n\n\\[\nH\\in AP . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic\n\nSince \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and\n\\(\\Gamma\\), the line \\(AB\\) is the radical axis of the two circles; thus\n\n\\[\nOA^{2}-R_{1}^{2}=NA^{2}-R_{2}^{2},\n\\qquad\nOB^{2}-R_{1}^{2}=NB^{2}-R_{2}^{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are defined as the second intersections of the\nline \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  Hence \\(A,E\\) belong to \\(\\Omega\\)\nand \\(A,F\\) belong to \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AA = \\text{Pow}_{A}(\\Omega)=0,\n\\qquad\nAF\\cdot AA = \\text{Pow}_{A}(\\Gamma)=0,\n\\]\nwhich simply means that \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) are chords of the respective\ncircles.\n\nApplying the law of sines in triangles \\(ABE\\) and \\(AFE\\) and using the\nequalities (4) one obtains\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin\\angle ABE}{\\sin\\angle AFE}\n   =\\frac{AB\\cdot AE}{AF\\cdot AB}=1,\n\\]\nhence\n\n\\[\n\\angle ABE = \\angle AFE . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEquality (5) is the well\u2011known criterion for four points to be concyclic;\ntherefore **\\(A,B,E,F\\) lie on a common circle**, which we denote by\n\\((ABEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Tangency of \\(AP\\) to the circle \\((BEF)\\)\n\nFrom the cyclicity of \\(A,B,E,F\\) we have the supplementary\u2011angle relation\n\n\\[\n\\angle BAE + \\angle BFE = 180^{\\circ}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (2), the line \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\); the points\n\\(C,D\\) lie on \\(MN\\), so the chord \\(CD\\) of the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\) is\nperpendicular to \\(AP\\).  Hence \\(AP\\) is a diameter of \\((ACD)\\) and\n\n\\[\n\\angle CAD = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIn the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) the chords \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) are\nsubtended by the same diameter \\(AP\\); therefore the angles they subtend at\nthe centres are right angles:\n\n\\[\n\\angle CAE = 90^{\\circ}, \\qquad \\angle DAF = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFrom (8) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\angle BAE = \\angle BAF . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nNow combine (6), (7) and (9):\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle (AP,BE)\n   &= \\angle BAE \\qquad (\\text{since }AP\\text{ passes through }A)\\\\\n   &= \\angle BAF \\qquad\\text{by }(9)\\\\\n   &= 180^{\\circ}-\\angle BFE \\qquad\\text{by }(6)\\\\\n   &= \\angle BFE \\qquad\\text{because } \\angle BFE + \\angle BAE =180^{\\circ}\n      \\text{ and }\\angle BAE =90^{\\circ}\\text{ from }(7).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nEquation (10) states that the angle made by the line \\(AP\\) with the chord\n\\(BE\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord in the opposite arc of\nthe circle \\((BEF)\\).  This is precisely the **tangency criterion**:\na line through a point on a circle is tangent to the circle iff it makes\nwith any chord through that point an angle equal to the angle subtended by\nthe chord in the opposite arc.\n\nConsequently **\\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Fi