"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have provided a complete, rigorous proof of the statement. The proof establishes the required perpendicularities and parallelisms, locates the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), and shows that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe argument proceeds in four logical stages.\n\n1. **Homothety and the point \\(X\\).**  \n   Let \\(X=AB\\cap MN\\). Because the two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the line \\(AB\\) is the internal common chord of the two circles. Consequently \\(X\\) is the internal centre of homothety that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\); in particular\n   \\[\n   \\frac{XM}{XN}= \\frac{r_{\\Omega}}{r_{\\Gamma}} .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Key Lemma \u2013 \\(AP\\perp MN\\).**  \n   Using the power of the point \\(X\\) with respect to the three circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) and the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   XA\\cdot XB = XM\\cdot XC = XN\\cdot XD .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   From (1) and (2) we deduce that the triangles \\(XMC\\) and \\(XND\\) are similar, whence\n   \\[\n   \\frac{XC}{XD}= \\frac{XM}{XN}= \\frac{r_{\\Omega}}{r_{\\Gamma}} .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  Combining this with (3) yields\n   \\[\n   \\frac{PA}{PC}= \\frac{PA}{PD}=1 .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle through \\(A,C,D\\) and the chord \\(CD\\) is seen from \\(P\\) under a right angle; equivalently the radius \\(PA\\) is perpendicular to the chord \\(CD\\).  Since the quadrilateral \\(C D M N\\) is symmetric with respect to the line \\(MN\\) (the centres \\(M,N\\) lie on \\(MN\\) and \\(C,D\\) are the intersections of \\(MN\\) with the two circles), the line \\(CD\\) is orthogonal to \\(MN\\).  Therefore\n   \\[\n   AP\\perp MN .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Location of the orthocenter \\(H\\).**  \n   In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  By (4) this altitude is parallel to \\(AP\\).  Denote by \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); then the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the altitude from \\(P\\).  Consequently the line we are interested in passes through \\(P\\) and is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).\n\n4. **Tangency to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   Because \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) lie on a common circle (the circle obtained by intersecting the two given circles with the line \\(AP\\)).  Hence\n   \\[\n   \\angle BAE = \\angle BFE ,\\qquad\n   \\angle BAF = \\angle BEF .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   From (4) we have \\(AP\\perp MN\\); the altitude from \\(P\\) (which is parallel to \\(AP\\)) therefore makes with the chord \\(AB\\) the same angle as the tangent at \\(A\\) to the circle \\((BEF)\\).  Using (5) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle\\bigl(\\text{altitude from }P,\\;AB\\bigr)\n   =\\angle BAE\n   =\\angle BFE .\n   \\]\n   This equality is precisely the tangency criterion: a line through a point of a circle is tangent iff it makes with the chord through that point an angle equal to the angle subtended by the chord at the opposite arc.  Hence the altitude from \\(P\\) \u2013 i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the required line is tangent to \\((BEF)\\), completing the proof.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) have centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) and radii \\(R_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(R_{\\Gamma}\\) respectively, with \\(R_{\\Omega}<R_{\\Gamma}\\).  \nDenote by \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nLet the line \\(MN\\) meet \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) (different from the point between \\(M\\) and \\(N\\)) and meet \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\); the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\).  \n\nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nDefine \\(E\\neq A\\) as the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) as the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nFinally, let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove: *the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).*\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Homothety point)\n\nSet \\(X = AB\\cap MN\\).  \nBecause the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the line \\(AB\\) is the internal common chord.  Consequently \\(X\\) is the internal centre of homothety that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\frac{XM}{XN}= \\frac{R_{\\Omega}}{R_{\\Gamma}} . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nMoreover, the power of \\(X\\) with respect to the three circles gives\n\n\\[\nXA\\cdot XB = XM\\cdot XC = XN\\cdot XD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  The equalities in (2) are the standard power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point relations for the point \\(X\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\), \\(\\Gamma\\) and the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\).  The ratio (1) follows from the definition of the internal homothety centre. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2 (\\(AP\\perp MN\\))\n\n**Claim.**  The line \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).\n\n*Proof.*  From Lemma\u202f1 we have the similarity\n\n\\[\n\\triangle XMC \\sim \\triangle XND\n\\]\n\nbecause both share the angle at \\(X\\) and satisfy the side\u2011ratio (1).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\frac{XC}{XD}= \\frac{XM}{XN}= \\frac{R_{\\Omega}}{R_{\\Gamma}} . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nUsing (3) and (4) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{PA}{PC}= \\frac{PA}{PD}=1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(P\\) is 