X = AB\\cap MN\\).  \nBecause the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the line \\(AB\\) is the internal common chord.  Consequently \\(X\\) is the internal centre of homothety that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\frac{XM}{XN}= \\frac{R_{\\Omega}}{R_{\\Gamma}} . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nMoreover, the power of \\(X\\) with respect to the three circles gives\n\n\\[\nXA\\cdot XB = XM\\cdot XC = XN\\cdot XD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  The equalities in (2) are the standard power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point relations for the point \\(X\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\), \\(\\Gamma\\) and the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\).  The ratio (1) follows from the definition of the internal homothety centre. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2 (\\(AP\\perp MN\\))\n\n**Claim.**  The line \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).\n\n*Proof.*  From Lemma\u202f1 we have the similarity\n\n\\[\n\\triangle XMC \\sim \\triangle XND\n\\]\n\nbecause both share the angle at \\(X\\) and satisfy the side\u2011ratio (1).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\frac{XC}{XD}= \\frac{XM}{XN}= \\frac{R_{\\Omega}}{R_{\\Gamma}} . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nUsing (3) and (4) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{PA}{PC}= \\frac{PA}{PD}=1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(P\\) is equidistant from the endpoints \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) of the chord \\(CD\\) of the circle \\((ACD)\\); consequently \\(PA\\) is a radius drawn to the midpoint of the chord \\(CD\\).  In a circle a radius to the midpoint of a chord is perpendicular to the chord, so\n\n\\[\nPA\\perp CD . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) and the segment \\(CD\\) is symmetric with respect to \\(MN\\), the chord \\(CD\\) is itself perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  Combining this with (5) yields\n\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN .\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 3 (Location of the orthocenter)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  By Lemma\u202f2 this altitude is parallel to \\(AP\\).  Let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  Then the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the altitude from \\(P\\); in particular it passes through \\(P\\) and is perpendicular to \\(MN\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 4 (Cyclicity of \\(A,B,E,F\\))\n\nThe points \\(A,B,E,F\\) lie on a common circle.\n\n*Proof.*  Both \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are obtained by intersecting the line \\(AP\\) with the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  Hence the power of \\(A\\) with respect to the circle through \\(B,E,F\\) equals \\(AB\\cdot AB\\), and the same holds for \\(B\\).  Consequently the four points are concyclic. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 5 (Tangency criterion)\n\nFor a circle \\((BEF)\\) and a line \\(\\ell\\) through a point \\(A\\) on the circle, \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circle iff\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,AB)=\\angle BFE .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  This is the well\u2011known tangent\u2011chord theorem. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Final step \u2013 proof of the required tangency\n\nConsider the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  By Lemma\u202f3, \\(\\ell\\) is the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\); hence \\(\\ell\\perp MN\\) and \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\).\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f4 the quadrilateral \\(A,B,E,F\\) is cyclic, so\n\n\\[\n\\angle BAE = \\angle BFE ,\\qquad\n\\angle BAF = \\angle BEF . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), the angle that \\(\\ell\\) makes with the chord \\(AB\\) equals \\(\\angle BAE\\).  Using (7) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,AB)=\\angle BAE = \\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nThus condition (6) holds, and by Lemma\u202f5 the line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nSince \\(\\ell\\) is precisely the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), the statement of the problem is proved. \u220e\n\n\n\n**Therefore, the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
