ter of triangle \\(ACD\\), i.e. the centre of the circle passing through \\(A,C,D\\). In that circle, the chord is \\(CD\\) and the line \\(PA\\) indeed joins the centre to a point on the circle, but **\\(A\\) is not the midpoint of chord \\(CD\\)**; the midpoint of a chord is the foot of the perpendicular from the centre onto the chord. The statement that \u201c\\(PA\\) is a radius drawn to the midpoint of the chord \\(CD\\)\u201d is therefore false. The subsequent deduction that \\(PA\\perp CD\\) is unjustified. Moreover, the claim that \u201c\\(CD\\) is symmetric with respect to \\(MN\\)\u201d is never proved; it would require showing that \\(MN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), which has not been established. Hence this lemma contains **Justification Gaps** (and also depends on the earlier critical error).\n\n*Result:* Justification Gap \u2013 the perpendicularity claim is not rigorously established.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Location of the orthocenter)\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n\u201cIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\). By Lemma\u202f2 this altitude is parallel to \\(AP\\). \u2026 Then the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the altitude from \\(P\\); in particular it passes through \\(P\\) and is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe first sentence is correct: the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\). The statement \u201cby Lemma\u202f2 this altitude is parallel to \\(AP\\)\u201d depends on Lemma\u202f2, which we have already found to be unjustified. Therefore the conclusion that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) passes through \\(P\\) is not supported. This is a **Justification Gap** (propagation of earlier gap).\n\n*Result:* Justification Gap \u2013 relies on the unproved perpendicularity.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f4 (Cyclicity of \\(A,B,E,F\\))\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n\u201cThe points \\(A,B