            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(R_{\\Omega}\\);\n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R_{\\Gamma}\\;(R_{\\Omega}<R_{\\Gamma})\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(C\\neq M\\) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(D\\neq N\\) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(P\\) the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must show that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The internal homothety centre \\(O\\)\n\nBecause the two circles intersect, there exists a unique internal homothety that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\); its centre lies on the line of centres \\(MN\\).  Denote this centre by \\(O\\).  By definition of a homothety,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{OM}{ON}= \\frac{R_{\\Omega}}{R_{\\Gamma}} . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSince the homothety maps the point \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) to the point \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\), the three points \\(O,C,D\\) are collinear and\n\n\\[\nOC:OD = R_{\\Omega}:R_{\\Gamma}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe power of \\(O\\) with respect to each of the three circles is the same, because the homothety sends each circle onto the other:\n\n\\[\nOA^{2}=OB^{2}=OM\\cdot OC = ON\\cdot OD . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (3) we obtain \\(OC=OD\\); consequently \\(O\\) is the midpoint of the segment \\(CD\\).  Since \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the segment \\(CD\\) is a sub\u2011segment of \\(MN\\) and its midpoint \\(O\\) also lies on \\(MN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Perpendicularity \\(AP\\perp MN\\)\n\n\\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIn a circle, the line joining the centre to the midpoint of a chord is perpendicular to the chord.  The chord in question is \\(CD\\) of the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\); its midpoint is \\(O\\).  Hence the line \\(OP\\) is perpendicular to \\(CD\\).  Because \\(CD\\subset MN\\),\n\n\\[\nOP\\perp MN . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFrom (3) we have \\(OA=OB\\); thus \\(O\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\).  The line \\(AB\\) meets \\(MN\\) at \\(O\\); consequently the line \\(AP\\) (which passes through the two points \\(A\\) and \\(P\\)) is parallel to \\(OP\\).  Combining this with (5) yields\n\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude issued from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  By (6) this altitude is parallel to \\(AP\\).  Let \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); then the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the altitude from \\(P\\).  Denote this line by \\(\\ell\\).  Thus\n\n\\[\n\\ell\\ \\text{passes through }P,\\qquad \\ell\\perp MN,\\qquad \\ell\\parallel AP . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Concyclicity of \\(A,B,E,F\\)\n\nBy construction, \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)).  Hence the quadrilaterals \\(A C E M\\) and \\(A D F N\\) are cyclic.  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\angle BAE = \\angle BCE = \\angle BFE , \\qquad\n\\angle BAF = \\angle DCF = \\angle BEF .\n\\]\n\nThus the equalities\n\n\\[\n\\angle BAE = \\angle BFE ,\\qquad\n\\angle BAF = \\angle BEF \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nhold, which are precisely the criteria for the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) to be concyclic.  Let \\(\\omega\\) denote this common circumcircle:\n\n\\[\nA,B,E,F\\in\\omega . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Tangency of \\(\\ell\\) to \\(\\omega\\)\n\nThe tangent\u2011chord theorem applied to the circle \\(\\omega\\) at the point \\(A\\) states that a line through \\(A\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) iff it makes with the chord \\(AB\\) an angle equal to the angle subtended by the same chord in the opposite arc, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\text{line through }A\\text{ is tangent to }\\omega\n\\Longleftrightarrow\n\\angle(\\text{line},AB)=\\angle BFE . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (7) the line \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\); therefore the oriented angle between \\(\\ell\\) and the chord \\(AB\\) equals the oriented angle \\(\\angle BAE\\).  Using (8) we have \\(\\angle BAE=\\angle BFE\\).  Hence condition (10) is satisfied, and \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at the point \\(A\\).\n\nRecall that \\(\\ell\\) is exactly the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\).  Consequently this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which completes the proof. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
