nt on the line through a centre, the power with respect to the circle equals the product of the directed distances to the centre and to the second intersection of the line with the circle.  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(O)=OM\\cdot OC,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(O)=ON\\cdot OD .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(O\\) also lies on the radical axis \\(AB\\), its powers with respect to the two circles are equal to the square of its distance to either intersection point \\(A\\) (or \\(B\\)):\n\n\\[\nOA^{2}=OM\\cdot OC = ON\\cdot OD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 \\(AP\\perp MN\\)\n\nFrom (2) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{OM}{ON}= \\frac{OC}{OD}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nTogether with (1) this yields the proportionality\n\n\\[\n\\frac{OC}{OD}= \\frac{R_{\\Omega}}{R_{\\Gamma}} = \\frac{OM}{ON}.\n\\]\n\nThus the four points \\(M,N,O,C,D\\) are in a *harmonic division* on the line \\(MN\\); equivalently, the internal homothety centre \\(O\\) and the external homothety centre \\(O'\\) (the intersection of the external common tangents of the two circles) are symmetric with respect to the midpoint \\(M_{CD}\\) of the segment \\(CD\\).\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), it lies on the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\) of the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\); consequently \\(P\\) is the reflection of \\(O\\) in the midpoint \\(M_{CD}\\).  Hence the segment \\(OP\\) is **perpendicular** to \\(CD\\).  Since \\(C,D\\) belong to the line \\(MN\\),\n\n\\[\nOP\\perp MN . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(OP\\) joins the centre \\(O\\) of the homothety to the centre \\(P\\) of the circle \\((ACD)\\); by the definition of a homothety, the line \\(OP\\) is parallel to the line joining the corresponding points \\(A\\) on the two circles, i.e. \\(OP\\parallel AP\\).  Combining this with (4) we obtain\n\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 Position of the orthocentre \\(H\\)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude issued from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  By (5) this altitude is parallel to \\(AP\\).  Let \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); then the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the altitude from \\(P\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is exactly the line }AP.}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus the required line passes through the point \\(A\\) and is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 The quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic\n\nBy construction, \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)).  Hence\n\n\\[\nA,E,C\\in\\Omega,\\qquad A,F,D\\in\\Gamma .\n\\]\n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\n\\angle BAE = \\angle B\\,\\widehat{C}E ,\\qquad\n\\angle BAF = \\angle B\\,\\widehat{D}F . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) and \\(B\\) lies on the common chord \\(AB\\), the inscribed angles subtended by the chord \\(AB\\) in the two circles are equal:\n\n\\[\n\\angle B\\,\\widehat{C}E = \\angle B\\,\\widehat{D}F . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFrom (7) and (8) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\angle BAE = \\angle BFE ,\\qquad\n\\angle BAF = \\angle BEF .\n\\]\n\nThese equalities are precisely the criterion for the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) to be concyclic.  Hence\n\n\\[\nA,B,E,F\\ \\text{lie on a common circle } \\omega . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Lemma\u202f4 \u2013 Tangency of the line through \\(H\\)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) denote the line described in Lemma\u202f2; by (6) we have \\(\\ell = AP\\).  Apply the tangent\u2011chord theorem to the circle \\(\\omega\\) at the point \\(A\\): a line through \\(A\\) 