r of \\(O\\) with respect to the three circles is the same:\n\n\\[\nOA^{2}=OM\\cdot OC =ON\\cdot OD .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nDividing the two equalities in (2) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{OC}{OD}= \\frac{OM}{ON}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus the four points \\(C,D,M,N\\) are in harmonic division on the line \\(MN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 \\(AP\\perp MN\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), it lies on the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nOP\\perp CD .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C,D\\) belong to the line \\(MN\\), (4) yields  \n\n\\[\nOP\\perp MN .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe homothety with centre \\(O\\) sends the chord \\(AC\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to the chord \\(AD\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently the line \\(OA\\) is the *axis* of this similarity, i.e.  \n\n\\[\nOA\\parallel MN .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) and (6) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nOP\\parallel AP .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 Position of the orthocentre \\(H\\)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from the vertex \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to the opposite side \\(MN\\).  \nBy (7) this altitude is parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nThe line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) therefore coincides with the altitude from \\(P\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is the line }AP.}\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 Concyclicity of \\(A,B,E,F\\)\n\nBy construction  \n\n\\[\nE\\neq A\\in\\Omega\\cap AP ,\\qquad F\\neq A\\in\\Gamma\\cap AP .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nA,E,C\\in\\Omega ,\\qquad A,F,D\\in\\Gamma .\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) belong to both circles, the arcs \\(AB\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) subtend equal inscribed angles at any point of the respective circles; in particular  \n\n\\[\n\\angle ACB=\\angle ADB .\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nFrom (9) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB=\\angle ACB,\\qquad \\angle AFB=\\angle ADB .\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nCombining (10) and (11) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB=\\angle AFB .\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nEquality of the angles subtended by the same chord \\(AB\\) at the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) is the cyclicity criterion for the quadrilateral \\(A B E F\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\nA,B,E,F\\text{ lie on a common circle }\\omega .\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Tangency of the line through \\(H\\) to \\(\\omega\\)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) denote the line described in Lemma\u202f2; by (8) \\(\\ell=AP\\).  \nSince \\(A\\in\\ell\\) and \\(A\\in\\omega\\), to prove that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) it suffices (tangent\u2011chord theorem) to show  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,AB)=\\angle AFB .\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThe left\u2011hand side of (14) is \\(\\angle BAP\\); because \\(E\\) lies on \\(AP\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle BAP=\\angle BAE .\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nIn the cyclic quadrilateral \\(A B E F\\) the chord \\(BE\\) subtends equal angles at the points \\(A\\) and \\(F\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BAE=\\angle BFE .\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nOpposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral are supplementary, so  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BFE+\\angle BAE=180^{\\circ}. \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nFrom (16) and (17) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BAE= \\angle AFB .\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (18) into (15) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,AB)=\\angle AFB ,\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly condition (14).  \nHence, by the tangent\u2011chord theorem, \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(A\\).\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (Lemma\u202f2), the required statement follows.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF.}\n\\]"
