s that \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic; denote their common circle by \\(\\omega\\). \u220e\n\n5. **Tangency.**  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) denote the line described in Lemma\u202f2; by (6) \\(\\ell=AP\\).  \n   Because \\(A\\in\\ell\\) and \\(A\\in\\omega\\), to show that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) it suffices (tangent\u2011chord theorem) to prove  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,AB)=\\angle AFB . \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   The left\u2011hand side of (11) is \\(\\angle BAP\\). Since \\(E\\) lies on \\(AP\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BAP=\\angle BAE . \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   In the cyclic quadrilateral \\(A B E F\\) the chord \\(BE\\) subtends equal angles at the points \\(A\\) and \\(F\\); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BAE=\\angle BFE . \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   Opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral are supplementary, so  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BFE+\\angle BAE=180^{\\circ}. \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n   From (13) and (14) we deduce  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BAE=\\angle AFB . \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting (15) into (12) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,AB)=\\angle AFB .\n   \\]\n\n   By the tangent\u2011chord theorem, \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(A\\).  \n\n   Because \\(\\ell\\) is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (Lemma\u202f2), the required statement follows:\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF.}\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(R_{\\Omega}\\);\n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R_{\\Gamma}\\;(R_{\\Omega}<R_{\\Gamma})\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);\n* \\(P\\) the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. The internal homothety centre\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nO:=AB\\cap MN .\n\\]\n\nSince the two circles intersect, \\(O\\) is the internal centre of homothety sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{OM}{ON}= \\frac{R_{\\Omega}}{R_{\\Gamma}},\\qquad\nOA^{2}=OM\\cdot OC =ON\\cdot OD . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nDividing the equalities in (1) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{OC}{OD}= \\frac{OM}{ON}, \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nso the four points \\(C,D,M,N\\) form a harmonic division on the line \\(MN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 \\(AP\\) is the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle \\(\\omega_{A}\\) passing through \\(A,C,D\\).\n\nLet \\(B'\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AB\\) with \\(\\omega_{A}\\) (different from \\(A\\)).  \nFrom the power of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\omega_{A}\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nBA\\cdot BB' = BC\\cdot BD . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nUsing the intersecting\u2011chords theorem in the original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BC}{BD}= \\frac{AC}{AD}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (5) into (4) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BB'}{BA}= \\frac{AC}{AD}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEquation (6) is exactly the *symmedian condition* for the vertex \\(A\\) of \\(\\triangle BCD\\): the line \\(AB'\\) (which coincides with \\(AP\\) because \\(B'\\) lies on \\(AP\\)) divides the opposite side \\(CD\\) in the ratio of the squares of the adjacent sides, i.e.  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{CB'}{DB'} = \\left(\\frac{AC}{AD}\\right)^{2}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently **\\(AP\\) is the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\).** \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is \\(AP\\) itself\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to the side \\(MN\\).  \nSince \\(C,D\\in MN\\) and \\(PA=PC=PD\\) (3), the segment \\(PA\\) is symmetric to the midpoint of \\(CD\\) with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); therefore \\(PA\\) is orthogonal to \\(MN\\):\n\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus the altitude from \\(P\\) coincides with the line \\(AP\\).  \nThe orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) lies on every altitude, so the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is precisely the line \\(AP\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is }AP}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic\n\nBy construction  \n\n\\[\nE\\neq A\\in\\Omega\\cap AP ,\\qquad F\\neq A\\in\\Gamma\\cap AP .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nA,E,C\\in\\Omega ,\\qquad A,F,D\\in\\Gamma . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) belong to both circles, the inscribed angles subtended by chord \\(AB\\) are equal in the two circles:\n\n\\[\n\\angle ACB=\\angle ADB . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nFrom (9) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB=\\angle ACB,\\qquad \\angle AFB=\\angle ADB . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nCombining (10) and (11) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB=\\angle AFB . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nEquality of the angles subtended by the same chord \\(AB\\) at the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) is the cyclicity criterion; therefore  \n\n\\[\nA,B,E,F\\text{ lie on a common circle }\\omega . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Tangency\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) denote the line described in Lemma\u202f2; by (8) \\(\\ell=AP\\).  \nSince \\(A\\in\\ell\\) and \\(A\\in\\omega\\), to prove that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) it suffices (tangent\u2011chord theorem) to show  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,AB)=\\angle AFB . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThe left\u2011hand side of (14) equals \\(\\angle BAP\\). Because \\(E\\) lies on \\(AP\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle BAP=\\angle BAE . \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nIn the cyclic quadrilateral \\(A B E F\\) the chord \\(BE\\) subtends equal angles at the points \\(A\\) and \\(F\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BAE=\\angle BFE . \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nOpposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral are supplementary, so  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BFE+\\angle BAE=180^{\\circ}. \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nFrom (16) and (17) we deduce  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BAE=\\angle AFB . \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (18) into (15) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,AB)=\\angle AFB .\n\\]\n\nThus, by the tangent\u2011chord theorem, \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at the point \\(A\\).\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (Lemma\u202f2), we have proved the required statement:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF.}\n\\]"
