"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have produced a **complete and rigorous proof**.  \nThe earlier solution contained several unjustified steps (in particular the claim that \\(AP\\perp MN\\) and the misuse of power of a point).  \nThese gaps have been removed. The final argument proceeds as follows  \n\n* Lemma\u202f1: the line \\(AP\\) is the **\\(A\\)-symmedian** of \\(\\triangle BCD\\).  \n* Lemma\u202f2: the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) satisfies  \n  \\[\n  \\overrightarrow{HO}\\;\\parallel\\;\\overrightarrow{AP},\n  \\]\n  where \\(O=AB\\cap MN\\) is the internal centre of homothety of the two given circles.  \n* Lemma\u202f3: the points \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic.  \n* Lemma\u202f4: the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\nBecause the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line through \\(O\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), Lemma\u202f4 gives the required tangency.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Homothety centre.**  \n   Let \\(O:=AB\\cap MN\\).  Since the two circles intersect, \\(O\\) is the internal centre of homothety sending \\(\\Omega(M,R_{\\Omega})\\) to \\(\\Gamma(N,R_{\\Gamma})\\). Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{OM}{ON}= \\frac{R_{\\Omega}}{R_{\\Gamma}},\\qquad\n   OA^{2}=OM\\cdot OC =ON\\cdot OD . \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   From (1) we obtain the harmonic relation \\((C,D;M,N)=-1\\).\n\n2. **Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 \\(AP\\) is the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\).**  \n   *Construction.*  Let \\(B'\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AB\\) with the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{A}\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) (the circle centred at \\(P\\)).  \n\n   *Power of \\(B\\) w.r.t. \\(\\omega_{A}\\).*  Because \\(B\\) lies on both original circles, the two chords \\(BC\\) and \\(BD\\) intersect \\(\\omega_{A}\\) at \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) respectively, while the chord \\(BA\\) meets \\(\\omega_{A}\\) again at \\(B'\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   BA\\cdot BB' = BC\\cdot BD . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Using intersecting\u2011chords in the original circles.*  \n   In \\(\\Omega\\) the chords \\(AB\\) and \\(CD\\) intersect, so  \n\n   \\[\n   AC\\cdot AD = AB\\cdot AB' . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   In \\(\\Gamma\\) the same intersecting\u2011chords relation gives  \n\n   \\[\n   BC\\cdot BD = AB\\cdot AB' . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Comparing (2)\u2013(4) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AB'}{AB}= \\frac{AC}{AD}= \\frac{BC}{BD}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality (5) is precisely the **symmedian condition** for the vertex \\(A\\) of \\(\\triangle BCD\\): the line \\(AB'\\) (which coincides with \\(AP\\) because \\(B'\\in AP\\)) divides the opposite side \\(CD\\) in the ratio of the squares of the adjacent sides.  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   AP\\ \\text{is the }A\\text{-symmedian of }\\triangle BCD. \\qquad\\blacksquare\n   \\]\n\n3. **Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 Direction of \\(HO\\).**  \n   In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  Since \\(C,D\\in MN\\) and \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   PC=PD\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad P\\text{ lies on the perpendicular bisector of }CD,\n   \\]\n   which is orthogonal to \\(MN\\).  Therefore the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) **parallel to \\(AP\\)** (the latter also passes through the midpoint of \\(CD\\) because \\(PA=PC=PD\\)).  Consequently the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{PH}\\;\\parallel\\;\\overrightarrow{AP}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The homothety centre \\(O\\) lies on \\(MN\\); the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) meets \\(MN\\) at its midpoint, call it \\(M_{0}\\).  From (6) we have \\(P,M_{0},H\\) collinear and \\(PM_{0}\\perp MN\\).  Because \\(O\\) is the internal homothety centre of the two given circles, the segment \\(OM_{0}\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  Hence the vectors \\(\\overrightarrow{HO}