ll\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(BEF\\) at the point \\(A\\).  \n\n   Because of Lemma\u202f2, the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with \\(\\ell\\).  Consequently this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \\(\\qquad\\blacksquare\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(R_{\\Omega}\\);\n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R_{\\Gamma}\\;(R_{\\Omega}<R_{\\Gamma})\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);\n* \\(P\\) the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);\n* \\(O:=AB\\cap MN\\) (the internal centre of homothety of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 1.  Properties of the homothety centre \\(O\\)\n\nBecause the two circles intersect, the lines joining their centres and the common chord intersect at a unique point \\(O\\) which is the internal centre of homothety sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{OM}{ON}= \\frac{R_{\\Omega}}{R_{\\Gamma}},\\qquad\nOA^{2}=OM\\cdot OC =ON\\cdot OD . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we obtain the harmonic division  \n\n\\[\n(C,D;M,N)=-1 . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 2.  Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 \\(AP\\) is the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\)\n\nLet \\(B'\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AB\\) with the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{A}\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) (the circle centred at \\(P\\)).  \n\nSince \\(B\\) belongs to both original circles, the chords \\(BC\\) and \\(BD\\) intersect \\(\\omega_{A}\\) at \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) respectively, while the chord \\(BA\\) meets \\(\\omega_{A}\\) again at \\(B'\\).  Therefore the power of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\omega_{A}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nBA\\cdot BB' = BC\\cdot BD . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nApplying the intersecting\u2011chords theorem in \\(\\Omega\\) to the intersecting chords \\(AB\\) and \\(CD\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nAC\\cdot AD = AB\\cdot AB' . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nApplying the same theorem in \\(\\Gamma\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nBC\\cdot BD = AB\\cdot AB' . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFrom (3)\u2013(5) we deduce  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AB'}{AB}= \\frac{AC}{AD}= \\frac{BC}{BD}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEquality (6) is exactly the condition for the line through \\(A\\) and \\(B'\\) to be the **\\(A\\)-symmedian** of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) (the symmedian divides the opposite side in the ratio of the squares of the adjacent sides).  Because \\(B'\\) lies on \\(AP\\), we conclude  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AP\\text{ is the }A\\text{-symmedian of }\\triangle BCD}. \\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 3.  Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 Direction of \\(HO\\)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to the side \\(MN\\).  Since \\(C,D\\in MN\\) and \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nPC=PD\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad P\\text{ lies on the perpendicular bisector of }CD,\n\\]\nwhich is orthogonal to \\(MN\\).  Consequently the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) **parallel to \\(AP\\)** (the latter also passes through the midpoint of \\(CD\\) because \\(PA=PC=PD\\)).  Hence the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\