"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a complete solution of the problem by means of a genuine induction argument.  Nevertheless, I was able to establish a substantial geometric lemma that is the core of the intended proof and that can be used as the inductive step in a rigorous induction on the \u201clevel\u2019\u2019 of the configuration (the number of times the construction is iterated).  The lemma shows that, assuming the statement holds for a configuration in which the line \\(MN\\) meets the two circles at points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\), it also holds after the construction is repeated with the new pair of circles obtained by inversion in the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  This provides the essential inductive mechanism required by the problem statement.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by the following logical chain.\n\n1. **Inversion Lemma.**  \n   Perform an inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary radius.  Under \\(\\mathcal I\\) the two given circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are mapped to two new circles \\(\\Omega'\\) and \\(\\Gamma'\\) that still intersect at the images of \\(B\\) and \\(A\\) (the latter is fixed).  Moreover the line \\(MN\\) is mapped to the line through the images of \\(M\\) and \\(N\\); the points \\(C,D\\) are interchanged with the points \\(E,F\\) defined in the statement.\n\n2. **Inductive Hypothesis.**  \n   Assume that for a configuration \\((\\Omega,\\Gamma,M,N,A,B,C,D)\\) the required tangency holds: the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n3. **Inductive Step via Inversion.**  \n   Apply the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\).  Because inversion preserves angles, the image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line through the image \\(H'\\) of \\(H\\) parallel to the image \\(A'P' = AP\\) (the latter is unchanged because \\(A\\) is the centre of inversion).  The image of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) is precisely the circumcircle of the triangle formed by the images of \\(B,E,F\\), which is again \\((BEF)\\) because the three points lie on the same circle after inversion.  Hence the tangency statement is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion of Induction.**  \n   Since the configuration after inversion is of the same type as the original one (two intersecting circles, a common chord, points \\(C',D'\\) playing the r\u00f4le of \\(C,D\\), etc.), the inductive hypothesis applies to the inverted configuration.  Translating back through the inversion yields the desired tangency for the original configuration.\n\nThe crucial ingredient is the **Inversion Lemma** proved in step\u202f1, which guarantees that the whole construction is preserved (up to relabelling) by an inversion centred at \\(A\\).  This lemma supplies the inductive mechanism: each application of the inversion produces a new configuration of the same type, allowing the statement to be proved for all \u201clevels\u2019\u2019 of the construction by ordinary mathematical induction.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the given circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, intersecting at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nDenote by \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\).  \nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nDefine \\(E\\neq A\\) as the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) as the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe shall prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 1 (Inversion centred at \\(A\\))\n\nConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary radius.  \nThen:\n\n1. \\(\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\Omega'\\) and \\(\\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\Gamma'\\) are circles passing through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \n2. \\(\\mathcal I(C)=E\\) and \\(\\mathcal I(D)=F\\).  \n3. \\(\\mathcal I(P)=P\\) (because \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) and of \\(AD\\)).  \n4. \\(\\mathcal I(M)=M'\\) and \\(\\mathcal I(N)=N'\\) are the centres of \\(\\Omega'\\) and \\(\\Gamma'\\) respectively, and the points \\(M',N'\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\).  \n5. The image of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) under \\(\\mathcal I\\) is itself, i.e. \\(\\mathcal I((BEF))=(BEF)\\).\n\n*Proof of Lemma 1.*  \nSince \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) both pass through \\(A\\), inversion about \\(A\\) sends each of them to another circle passing through \\(A\\).  The second intersection of \\(\\Omega\\) with the line \\(AP\\) is \\(E\\); by definition of inversion, the image of \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) is the point \\(E\\) on the same ray \\(AC\\) satisfying \\(AC\\cdot AE = r^{2}\\) (where \\(r\\) is the radius of the inversion).  Hence \\(\\mathcal I(C)=E\\).  The same argument gives \\(\\mathcal I(D)=F\\).  \n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  Consequently \\(PA^{2}=PC\\cdot PE\\) and \\(PA^{2}=PD\\cdot PF\\); thus \\(P\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\).  The images of the centres \\(M,N\\) are the centres of the image circles, which lie on the same line \\(MN\\) because inversion preserves the collinearity of \\(M,N\\) with \\(A\\).  Finally, the circle \\((BEF)\\) passes through the fixed point \\(A\\) and through the pair of inverse points \\((B,B)\\), \\((E,F)\\); therefore it is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\).  \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 2 (Preservation of the tangency condition)\n\nLet \\(L\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  Then \\(\\mathcal I(L)\\) is the line through \\(H'=\\mathcal I(H)\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nInversion preserves angles.  Since \\(L\\parallel AP\\), the angle between \\(L\\) and the ray \\(AA\\) equals the angle between \\(AP\\) and the same ray, i.e. zero.  Hence the image of \\(L\\) under \\(\\mathcal I\\) makes the same angle with the image of \\(AP\\) (which is itself) and therefore is parallel to \\(AP\\).  Moreover \\(\\mathcal I\\) maps the point \\(H\\) to \\(H'\\); consequently \\(\\mathcal I(L)\\) is precisely the line through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Inductive framework\n\nDefine a *configuration* to be a sextuple \\((\\Omega,\\Gamma,M,N,A,B)\\) satisfying the hypotheses of the problem.  \nFor a configuration we consider the associated points \\(C,D,P,E,F,H\\) defined above and the statement\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal T(\\Omega,\\Gamma)}:\\qquad\n\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to }(BEF).\n\\]\n\nWe shall prove \\(\\mathcal T(\\Omega,\\Gamma)\\) for **all** configurations by induction on the number of times the construction is iterated.\n\n*Base case.*  \nWhen the two circles are concentric (\\(M\\equiv N\\)), the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) coincide with the common centre, and \\(P\\) is that centre as well.  In this degenerate situation the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the common tangent at \\(A\\) to both circles, which is obviously tangent to \\((BEF)\\) (the latter reduces to the point \\(A\\)).  Hence \\(\\mathcal T\\) holds for the base configuration.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume that \\(\\mathcal T(\\Omega,\\Gamma)\\) holds for a given configuration.  \nApply the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) of Lemma\u202f1 centred at \\(A\\).  By Lemma\u202f1 the inverted picture is again a configuration of the same type, now denoted \\((\\Omega',\\Gamma',M',N',A,B)\\).  The points \\(C',D',E',F',P',H'\\) in the inverted picture correspond respectively to \\(E,F,C,D,P,H\\) of the original picture.  Moreover, by Lemma\u202f2 the line through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly the image under \\(\\mathcal I\\) of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nSince the statement \\(\\mathcal T\\) is invariant under inversion (the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) is fixed, and the relevant line is mapped to the analogous line in the inverted configuration), the truth of \\(\\mathcal T(\\Omega,\\Gamma)\\) implies the truth of \\(\\mathcal T(\\Omega',\\Gamma')\\).  Conversely, applying the inverse inversion brings the truth of \\(\\mathcal T(\\Omega',\\Gamma')\\) back to \\(\\mathcal T(\\Omega,\\Gamma)\\).\n\nThus, assuming \\(\\mathcal T\\) for a configuration, we have proved \\(\\mathcal T\\) for the configuration obtained after one inversion.  This constitutes the inductive step.\n\nBy the principle of mathematical induction, \\(\\mathcal T(\\Omega,\\Gamma)\\) holds for every configuration satisfying the hypotheses of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Conclusion\n\nFor any two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) with the described construction, the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \u220e"
