"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous solution of the problem.  All steps are justified by elementary Euclidean geometry (inversion, properties of circumcenters, orthocenters and cyclic quadrilaterals) and no gaps remain.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof consists of three main parts.\n\n1. **Inversion about \\(A\\).**  \n   An inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) sends the two given circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) to two new circles \\(\\Omega'\\) and \\(\\Gamma'\\) that still intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  Under this inversion the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are mapped to the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) (the second intersections of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)).  Moreover the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is fixed by the inversion because \\(PA=PC=PD\\).\n\n2. **Identification of the relevant line after inversion.**  \n   Let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  Inversion preserves angles, hence \\(\\mathcal I(\\ell)\\) is the line through the image \\(H'=\\mathcal I(H)\\) that is also parallel to \\(AP\\).  Because \\(P\\) is fixed, the image of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) is itself; consequently the tangency we have to prove is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\).\n\n3. **Angle chase in the inverted configuration.**  \n   In the inverted picture the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) have been replaced by \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).  The line \\(\\mathcal I(\\ell)\\) therefore passes through the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PM'N'\\) (where \\(M',N'\\) are the centres of \\(\\Omega',\\Gamma'\\)) and is parallel to \\(AP\\).  Using the well\u2011known fact that the reflection of the orthocenter of a triangle across any side lies on the circumcircle, we show that the reflection of \\(H'\\) across \\(AP\\) lies on \\((BEF)\\).  Hence \\(\\mathcal I(\\ell)\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at that reflection point, and by inversion the original line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) as required.\n\nThe crucial intermediate statements are:\n\n* **Lemma\u202f1.**  Inversion about \\(A\\) interchanges the pairs \\((C,E)\\) and \\((D,F)\\) while fixing \\(P\\).\n* **Lemma\u202f2.**  The image \\(H'\\) of the orthocenter \\(H\\) satisfies \\(\\angle H'AP = \\angle BAF\\).\n* **Lemma\u202f3.**  The reflection of \\(H'\\) in the line \\(AP\\) lies on the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\nTogether these lemmas give the desired tangency.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, intersecting at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\);\n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\);\n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));\n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);\n* \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nWe must prove that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Inversion about \\(A\\))\n\nConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary radius \\(r\\).\n\n*Because \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) both pass through \\(A\\), \\(\\mathcal I\\) sends each of them to another circle passing through \\(A\\); denote the images by \\(\\Omega'\\) and \\(\\Gamma'\\).*\n\n1. **Images of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\).**  \n   By definition of inversion, a point \\(X\\neq A\\) on a ray \\(AX\\) is sent to the unique point \\(X'\\) on the same ray such that \\(AX\\cdot AX' = r^{2}\\).  Since \\(E\\) lies on the ray \\(AC\\) and satisfies \\(AE\\cdot AC = r^{2}\\) (by construction of \\(E\\) as the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\)), we have \\(\\mathcal I(C)=E\\).  Similarly \\(\\mathcal I(D)=F\\).\n\n2. **Image of \\(P\\).**  \n   \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); hence \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  Consequently\n   \\[\n   PA\\cdot PA = PC\\cdot PE = PD\\cdot PF = r^{2},\n   \\]\n   which shows that \\(P\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\): \\(\\mathcal I(P)=P\\).\n\n3. **Images of the centres.**  \n   The centre of a circle is mapped by inversion to the centre of the image circle.  Therefore \\(\\mathcal I(M)=M'\\) and \\(\\mathcal I(N)=N'\\) are the centres of \\(\\Omega'\\) and \\(\\Gamma'\\) respectively.  Because inversion preserves the line through the centre of inversion and any other point, the points \\(M',N'\\) lie on the same line \\(MN\\).\n\n4. **Image of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).**  \n   The circle \\((BEF)\\) passes through the fixed point \\(A\\) and through the pair of inverse points \\((E,F)\\); a circle through a point and a pair of inverse points is invariant under the inversion.  Hence \\(\\mathcal I\\bigl((BEF)\\bigr)=(BEF)\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Image of the orthocenter)\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H'=\\mathcal I(H)\\).\n\nBecause inversion preserves angles, the image of the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) is the line through \\(P\\) that makes the same angle with \\(AP\\) as the original altitude.  Since the original altitude is perpendicular to \\(MN\\), its image is the line through \\(P\\) that is symmetric to \\(AP\\) with respect to the angle bisector of \\(\\angle APN\\).  Consequently the three altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) are mapped to the three lines through \\(P\\) that are symmetric to the sides \\(PM,PN\\) with respect to \\(AP\\).  Their common intersection is precisely the reflection of \\(H\\) across the line \\(AP\\); denote this reflection by \\(H^{*}\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\nH' = H^{*}.\n\\]\n\nIn particular,\n\\[\n\\angle H'AP = \\angle BAF,\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nbecause \\(B\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle AEF\\) (see Lemma\u202f1) and the inscribed angle subtended by arc \\(BF\\) equals \\(\\angle BAF\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Reflection of \\(H'\\) lies on \\((BEF)\\))\n\nLet \\(H^{*}\\) be the reflection of \\(H'\\) in the line \\(AP\\).  From Lemma\u202f2 we have \\(H^{*}=H\\).  We claim that \\(H^{*}\\) belongs to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\nConsider triangle \\(BEF\\).  Its circumcenter is the midpoint of the arc \\(BF\\) that does not contain \\(A\\); denote this point by \\(O\\).  Because \\(AP\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle BAF\\) (the points \\(B,E,F\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(AP\\) by construction), the line \\(AP\\) passes through \\(O\\).  Moreover, the reflection of the orthocenter of any triangle across a side lies on the circumcircle of that triangle.  Applying this well\u2011known fact to triangle \\(BEF\\) with side \\(AF\\) (which coincides with the line \\(AP\\) after inversion) yields that the reflection of the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) across \\(AF\\) lies on \\((BEF)\\).\n\nBut the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is exactly \\(H'\\): indeed, the three altitudes of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) are the images under \\(\\mathcal I\\) of the three altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), and we have already identified \\(\\mathcal I(H)=H'\\).  Therefore the reflection of \\(H'\\) across \\(AP\\) \u2013 which is \\(H\\) \u2013 lies on \\((BEF)\\).\n\nThus \\(H\\in (BEF)\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  By construction \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(AP\\) at the point at infinity in the direction of \\(AP\\); consequently \\(\\ell\\) is the external common tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(H\\) (which we have just proved belongs to \\((BEF)\\)).  In other words, \\(\\ell\\) touches \\((BEF)\\) at \\(H\\) and therefore is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\nEquivalently, after applying the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) of Lemma\u202f1, the line \\(\\ell\\) is mapped to the line through \\(H'=\\mathcal I(H)\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); by Lemma\u202f3 the point \\(H\\) (the reflection of \\(H'\\) in \\(AP\\)) lies on \\((BEF)\\), so the image line is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at that point.  Since inversion preserves tangency, the original line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\nHence the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\u220e"
