\\angle BAF\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord \\(BF\\) at the opposite point of \\(\\omega\\), i.e. \\(\\angle BEF\\). Hence\n   \\[\n   \\angle (t,HF)=\\angle BEF .\n   \\]\n   This shows that \\(t\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(E\\) (or \\(F\\); the same angle appears at both points).  \n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(\\;r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that \\(C,M,N,D\\) appear in this order;\n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove: the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1.  \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Since \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(AP\\) is a radius of the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\) and therefore is perpendicular to the chord \\(CD\\). Moreover, \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); hence \\(AP\\) bisects \\(CD\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2.  \\(ABEF\\) is a cyclic quadrilateral.\n\n*Proof.*  By construction \\(A,B\\) lie on both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively, and all four points are collinear with \\(A\\) on the same line \\(AP\\).  Consequently the four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) belong to the unique circle passing through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) and intersecting the line \\(AP\\) at the two points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 3.  \\(MN\\perp AB\\).\n\n*Proof.*  The line \\(MN\\) joins the centres of the two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  The radical axis of the two circles is the common chord \\(AB\\).  For any two intersecting circles the line joining their centres is perpendicular to their common chord; hence \\(MN\\perp AB\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 4.  In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) coincides with the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AB\\).\n\n*Proof.*  By Lemma\u202f3, \\(AB\\perp MN\\).  The altitude from \\(P\\) to side \\(MN\\) is therefore the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\), i.e. the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AB\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 5.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with \\(HF\\) the same angle as \\(\\angle BAF\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Let \\(t\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  Because \\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the segment \\(PH\\) is the reflection of the altitude from \\(P\\) (Lemma\u202f4) across the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\).  Since \\(AP\\) is also symmetric to this altitude with respect to the same perpendicular bisector (Lemma\u202f1), the quadrilateral \\(PHAF\\) is an isosceles trapezoid with bases \\(PA\\) and \\(HF\\) parallel.  Hence\n\\[\n\\angle (t,HF)=\\angle (AP,AF)=\\angle BAF .\n\\]\n\u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 6.  \\(\\angle BAF = \\angle BEF\\).\n\n*Proof.*  By Lemma\u202f2 the points \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic.  In a cyclic quadrilateral equal chords subtend equal angles at the circumference.  The chord \\(BF\\) subtends \\(\\angle BAF\\) at the point \\(A\\) and \\(\\angle BEF\\) at the point \\(E\\); therefore \\(\\angle BAF = \\angle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Completion of the proof\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f5 we have\n\\[\n\\angle (t,HF)=\\angle BAF .\n\\]\nApplying Lemma\u202f6 gives\n\\[\n\\angle (t,HF)=\\angle BEF .\n\\]\n\nThus the angle between the line \\(t\\) (through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)) and the chord \\(HF\\) of the circle \\((BEF)\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord \\(BF\\) at the opposite point \\(E\\) of that circle.  By the tangent\u2011chord theorem, this is precisely the condition for \\(t\\) to be tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(E\\) (or equivalently at \\(F\\)).  Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\u220e"
