.\n   \\]\n   By the tangent\u2011chord theorem, a line that makes with a chord \\(HF\\) an angle equal to the angle subtended by the same chord at the opposite point of the circle is tangent to that circle. Thus \\(t\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThe proof is therefore complete.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(\\;r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that the order on the line is \\(C,M,N,D\\);\n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must show that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1.  \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), it is equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); hence \\(PC=PD\\).  \nThe segment \\(AP\\) is a radius of the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\) and therefore is perpendicular to the chord \\(CD\\).  \nThus \\(AP\\perp CD\\) and \\(AP\\) bisects \\(CD\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2.  The points \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic.\n\nBoth circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) contain \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\).  \nConsequently the unique circle passing through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) and intersecting the line \\(AP\\) at the two points distinct from \\(A\\) is precisely the circle through \\(A,B,E,F\\). Denote this circle by \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3.  In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with \\(HF\\) the same angle as \\(\\angle BAF\\).\n\n1. **Altitude from \\(P\\).**  \n   The line \\(MN\\) joins the centres of the intersecting circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore the common chord \\(AB\\) is the radical axis of the two circles and is perpendicular to \\(MN\\):\n   \\[\n   MN\\perp AB. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   Hence the altitude from \\(P\\) to side \\(MN\\) is the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AB\\).\n\n2. **Reflection across the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\).**  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be the perpendicular bisector of segment \\(MN\\).  \n   Because \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(\\ell\\), the reflection \\(\\sigma_{\\ell}\\) interchanges the two sides \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n   Consequently \\(\\sigma_{\\ell}\\) sends the altitude from \\(P\\) (which is parallel to \\(AB\\)) to a line through \\(P\\) that is symmetric to that altitude with respect to \\(\\ell\\).\n\n3. **Identification of the reflected line.**  \n   By Lemma\u202f1, \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(CD\\) and passes through the midpoint of \\(CD\\).  \n   Since \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the midpoint of \\(CD\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\).  \n   Therefore \\(AP\\) is exactly the image of the altitude from \\(P\\) under the reflection \\(\\sigma_{\\ell}\\):\n   \\[\n   \\sigma_{\\ell}(\\text{altitude from }P)=AP. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Location of \\(H\\).**  \n   The orthocenter \\(H\\) is the intersection of the three altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n   Applying \\(\\sigma_{\\ell}\\) to the altitude from \\(P\\) we obtain the line \\(PH\\) (the image of the altitude through \\(P\\) is the line joining \\(P\\) to the image of the opposite vertex, i.e. to \\(H\\)).  \n   Hence, by (2),\n   \\[\n   PH\\parallel AF. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction of the required line.**  \n   Let \\(t\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n   From (3) we have the quadrilateral \\(PHAF\\) with opposite sides parallel; therefore it is an isosceles trapezoid and\n   \\[\n   \\angle(t,HF)=\\angle(AP,AF)=\\angle BAF . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4.  \\(\\angle BAF = \\angle BEF\\).\n\nSince \\(A,B,E,F\\) lie on the same circle \\(\\omega\\) (Lemma\u202f2), the inscribed\u2011angle theorem gives that the angle subtended by chord \\(BF\\) at the points \\(A\\) and \\(E\\) are equal:\n\\[\n\\angle BAF = \\angle BEF .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nFrom (4) and Lemma\u202f4 we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle(t,HF)=\\angle BEF .\n\\]\n\nThe tangent\u2011chord theorem states that a line through a point on a circle is tangent to the circle iff the angle it makes with the chord through that point equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the opposite point of the circle.  \nHere the chord is \\(HF\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (which is \\(\\omega\\)), the line \\(t\\) passes through \\(H\\), and the angle between \\(t\\) and \\(HF\\) equals \\(\\angle BEF\\). Hence \\(t\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at the point where the chord \\(HF\\) meets the circle, i.e. at \\(E\\) (or equivalently at \\(F\\)).\n\nTherefore the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\u220e"
