es \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(\\;r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) with order \\(C-M-N-D\\);\n* \\(P\\) the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nDenote by \\((BEF)\\) the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nWe must show that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 1  \n\\(P\\) is the midpoint of the minor arc \\(EF\\) of \\((BEF)\\) that does **not** contain \\(B\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\nPE=PF,\\qquad \\angle EPF=\\angle FPE .\n\\]\n\n**Proof.**  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the points \\(A,C,D\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\): indeed \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) because \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) and lies on the line through \\(A\\) and the midpoint of \\(CD\\).  \nThe points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are defined as the second intersections of the same line \\(AP\\) with the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\). Hence \\(AP\\) is the common internal radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently the power of \\(P\\) with respect to both circles is equal:\n\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PE = PM^{2}-r_{\\Omega}^{2}=PN^{2}-r_{\\Gamma}^{2}=PA\\cdot PF .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(PA\\neq0\\), we obtain \\(PE=PF\\).  \nThus \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(E\\) and \\(F\\); together with \\(PB\\) (the common distance from \\(P\\) to the three points \\(B,E,F\\) because \\(B\\) also lies on both circles) we see that \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle through \\(B,E,F\\).  \nTherefore \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(EF\\) opposite \\(B\\), which yields \\(\\angle EPF=\\angle FPE\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2  \nQuadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\).\n\n**Proof.**  \nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we already know that \\(PE=PF\\) and \\(PB=PE\\) (since \\(B\\) belongs to both original circles, its power with respect to \\(P\\) equals that of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)). Hence \\(PA=PB=PE=PF\\); all four points are at the same distance from \\(P\\). Consequently they lie on the circle centred at \\(P\\), i.e. \\((ABEF)=(BEF)\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 3  \nLet \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across the line \\(MN\\). Then \\(H'\\) lies on \\((BEF)\\).\n\n**Proof.**  \nIn any triangle, the reflection of the orthocentre across any side lies on the circumcircle of the triangle.  \nApply this fact to \\(\\triangle PMN\\): the reflection of \\(H\\) across side \\(MN\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nBut the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(MN\\) of the circle \\((BEF)\\) (by Lemma\u202f2, \\(P\\) is the centre of \\((BEF)\\) and \\(M,N\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(AP\\)). Hence the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) coincides with \\((BEF)\\).  \nTherefore the reflected point \\(H'\\) belongs to \\((BEF)\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Main argument (proof by contradiction)\n\nAssume, for the sake of contradiction, that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the circle \\((BEF)\\) at a point \\(T\\neq B\\).  \nBecause the line is parallel to \\(AP\\), the segment \\(HT\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n\nLet \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\). By Lemma\u202f3, \\(H'\\) also lies on \\((BEF)\\).  \nSince the reflection across \\(MN\\) sends a line parallel to \\(AP\\) to another line parallel to \\(AP\\) (the direction of \\(AP\\) is orthogonal to \\(MN\\) because \\(AP\\) is the radical axis of the two original 