iff** \\(H\\) lies on the altitude, which it does not (otherwise the three altitudes would be concurrent on \\(AP\\)).  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\ell\\;:=\\;\\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(AP\\perp MN\\), the reflection of \\(\\ell\\) across \\(MN\\) is again a line parallel to \\(AP\\); this reflected line passes through \\(H'\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nH,\\;H',\\; \\text{and the two points of intersection of }\\ell\\text{ with }(BEF)\n\\text{ are collinear.}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Contradiction assumption\n\nAssume, contrary to the claim, that \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle \\((BEF)\\) at a second point \\(T\\neq B\\).  \nFrom (5) the reflected point \\(H'\\) also lies on \\(\\ell\\); therefore both \\(T\\) and \\(H'\\) belong to \\(\\ell\\) and to \\((BEF)\\).  \nA circle can intersect a straight line in at most two points, so the set of intersection points of \\(\\ell\\) with \\((BEF)\\) is exactly \\(\\{B,T\\}\\) or \\(\\{B,H'\\}\\). Since \\(B\\notin\\ell\\) (otherwise \\(AP\\) would pass through \\(B\\), contradicting the distinctness of the two original circles), we must have  \n\n\\[\nT = H'.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus the only second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with \\((BEF)\\) is the reflected orthocenter \\(H'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final angle chase\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the centre of \\((BEF)\\), for any point \\(X\\) on the circle the angle subtended by chord \\(EF\\) at \\(X\\) equals half the central angle:\n\n\\[\n\\angle EXF = \\frac12\\widehat{EF}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, using \\(X=H'\\) and \\(X=P\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle EH'F = \\angle EPF .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBut from Lemma\u202f1, \\(\\angle EPF = \\angle FPE\\).  \nSince \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) passes through the centre \\(P\\), the chord \\(EF\\) is symmetric with respect to \\(\\ell\\); consequently the equalities (8) and Lemma\u202f1 give  \n\n\\[\n\\angle EH'F = \\angle FH'E .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nEquality (9) states that the two angles formed by the chords \\(EH'\\) and \\(FH'\\) with the tangent at \\(H'\\) are equal; this is precisely the **tangent criterion**: a line through a point of a circle is tangent iff it makes equal angles with the two chords through that point.  \n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\) passes through \\(H'\\) and is parallel to \\(AP\\), (9) shows that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(H'\\).\n\nBut \\(\\ell\\) was assumed to intersect the circle at a second point \\(T\\neq H'\\); a line cannot be tangent at a point and also intersect the same circle at another distinct point. This contradiction proves that the assumption is false.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nTherefore the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at exactly one point; that point is the point of tangency. Hence the required line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e"
