e \u201cdifferentiating\u201d argument is vague and provides no rigorous justification. This is a **Critical Error**.\n\n---\n\n#### 7. Claim that \\(AP\\) is an altitude of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\n**Quote:**  \n> \u201cBecause \\(AP\\perp MN\\), the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line \\(AP\\); therefore \\(AP\\) passes through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) **iff** \\(H\\) lies on the altitude, which it does not (otherwise the three altitudes would be concurrent on \\(AP\\)).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nEven if we (incorrectly) accepted that \\(AP\\perp MN\\), the altitude from \\(P\\) would be the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); that line is indeed \\(AP\\) **only if** \\(A\\) lies on that line, which is not guaranteed. The reasoning that \u201c\\(AP\\) passes through the orthocenter iff \\(H\\) lies on the altitude\u201d is tautological and does not establish any useful fact. Since the premise (perpendicularity) is false, the whole statement collapses. This is a **Critical Error**.\n\n---\n\n#### 8. Construction of the line \\(\\ell\\) and claim about its reflection\n\n**Quote:**  \n> \u201cDefine \\(\\ell :=\\) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n> Because \\(AP\\perp MN\\), the reflection of \\(\\ell\\) across \\(MN\\) is again a line parallel to \\(AP\\); this reflected line passes through \\(H'\\). Hence  \n> \\[\n> H,\\;H',\\; \\text{and the two points of intersection of }\\ell\\text{ with }(BEF)\n> \\text{ are collinear.}\n> \\tag{5}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe claim that the reflection of a line parallel to \\(AP\\) across \\(MN\\) is again parallel to \\(AP\\) follows from the (false) assertion that \\(AP\\perp MN\\). Since that perpendicularity is not established, the statement about the reflected line is unjustified. Moreover, the collinearity conclusion (5) is asserted without proof; it does not follow from any known theorem. This is a **Critical Error**.\n\n---\n\n#### 9. Con