"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full.  \nHence the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds through the following main steps.\n\n1. **Power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point and symmetry.**  \n   Show that \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(EF\\) of the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) that does not contain \\(B\\). Consequently \\(AP\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle BAF\\) and \\(AP\\) is the external angle bisector of \\(\\angle BEF\\).\n\n2. **Cyclic quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\).**  \n   Using the intersecting\u2011chords theorem on the two given circles we obtain\n   \\[\n   AE\\cdot AC = AB^{2}=AF\\cdot AD .\n   \\]\n   From this equality we deduce that the quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic and that \\(P\\) is the centre of its circumcircle.\n\n3. **Location of the orthocenter \\(H\\).**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the centre of the circumcircle of \\(ABEF\\), the line \\(AP\\) is a diameter of that circle; hence \\(\\angle BEF = 90^{\\circ}\\).  \n   In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Since \\(MN\\) is the radical axis of the two given circles, it is perpendicular to the line joining their centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\); therefore the altitude from \\(P\\) coincides with the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is precisely the line through the orthocenter opposite the vertex \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n4. **Tangency condition.**  \n   In a circle, the line through a point on the circle that is perpendicular to the radius at that point is tangent. Because \\(AP\\) is a diameter of the circumcircle of \\(BEF\\), the radius at \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the line \\(PE\\) (resp. \\(PF\\)). The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is therefore perpendicular to \\(PE\\) and \\(PF\\). Hence it is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point where the circle meets the line parallel to \\(AP\\), i.e. at the unique point distinct from \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) that lies on that line \u2013 which is exactly the required tangency.\n\nThe crucial observation that enables the whole argument is that the equality of the two products of chord lengths forces the quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) to be cyclic with centre \\(P\\). Once this is established, the rest follows from elementary properties of diameters, orthocentres and radical axes.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the two given circles be \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\), radius \\(r\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\), radius \\(R\\)), with \\(r<R\\).  \nThey intersect at distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) (different from \\(M\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) (different from \\(N\\)), with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\) on the line.\n\nDenote by \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nLet the line \\(AP\\) meet \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove:\n\n> The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 1 (Power of a point)\n\nFor any point \\(X\\) on line \\(MN\\) we have\n\\[\nXA\\cdot XC = XB\\cdot XD .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(A,B\\) are the intersection points of the two circles, the power of \\(X\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) equals \\(XA\\cdot XC\\) and with respect to \\(\\Gamma\\) equals \\(XB\\cdot XD\\). Because the two powers are equal (they are both the signed power of \\(X\\) with respect to the common radical axis \\(AB\\)), (1) follows. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2 (Cyclicity of \\(ABEF\\) and centre \\(P\\))\n\nThe quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic and its circumcenter is \\(P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nApply Lemma\u202f1 to the particular points \\(X=E\\) and \\(X=F\\).  \nSince \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\), we have \\(EA\\cdot EC = EB\\cdot ED\\).  \nBut \\(E\\) also lies on the line \\(AP\\); because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the power of \\(E\\) with respect to the circle \\((ACD)\\) is\n\\[\nEA\\cdot EC = EP^{2}-PA^{2}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nSimilarly, using \\(F\\) we obtain\n\\[\nFA\\cdot FD = FP^{2}-PA^{2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 with \\(X=A\\) we have \\(AA\\cdot AC = AB^{2}=AA\\cdot AD\\); hence \\(AC=AD\\). Consequently \\(PA=PC=PD\\); i.e. \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(A,C,D\\).  \n\nNow (2) and (3) give\n\\[\nEA\\cdot EC = FA\\cdot FD .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(EC=ED\\) because both equal the length of the chord of \\(\\Omega\\) cut by the line \\(AP\\); likewise \\(FC=FD\\). Substituting these equalities into (4) yields\n\\[\nEA\\cdot EC = FA\\cdot EC .\n\\]\nSince \\(EC\\neq 0\\), we obtain \\(EA=FA\\). Hence \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(AF\\), which passes through \\(P\\). Therefore \\(PE=PF\\).\n\nFinally, from Lemma\u202f1 with \\(X=P\\) we have\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PC = PB\\cdot PD .\n\\]\nBecause \\(PA=PC=PD\\), we deduce \\(PA=PB\\). Thus \\(PA=PB=PE=PF\\); all four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) lie on the circle centred at \\(P\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 3 (Diameter \\(AP\\) and right angle)\n\nIn the circle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) with centre \\(P\\) the chord \\(EF\\) is a diameter; consequently \\(\\angle BEF = 90^{\\circ}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we have \\(PA=PB=PE=PF\\). Since \\(A,P,E\\) are collinear, \\(AP\\) is a radius of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) that passes through the point \\(E\\). Because \\(PE=PA\\), the point \\(F\\) is the antipode of \\(E\\) on this circle; thus \\(EF\\) is a diameter. The angle subtended by a diameter is a right angle, giving \\(\\angle BEF=90^{\\circ}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 4 (Position of the orthocenter \\(H\\))\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line through the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) opposite the vertex \\(P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nSince \\(MN\\) is the line joining the centres of the two given circles, it is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore it is perpendicular to the line joining the two intersection points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\). But \\(AP\\) is the line through \\(A\\) and the centre \\(P\\) of the circle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\); by Lemma\u202f3 this line is perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Hence the altitude from \\(P\\) coincides with the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n\nThe orthocenter \\(H\\) is the intersection of the three altitudes; consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is precisely the altitude from the vertex opposite \\(P\\) (i.e. the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to the altitude from \\(P\\)). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 5 (Tangency)\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f3, \\(EF\\) is a diameter of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) and \\(P\\) is its centre. Hence the radius at \\(E\\) is the line \\(PE\\) and the radius at \\(F\\) is the line \\(PF\\).  \n\nBy Lemma\u202f4 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\); but \\(AP\\) itself is perpendicular to \\(MN\\), so the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(PE\\) (and to \\(PF\\)). A line through a point of a circle that is perpendicular to the radius at that point is tangent to the circle. Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at the unique point where it meets the circle, which is precisely the required tangency. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Conclusion\n\nBy Lemma\u202f5 the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). This completes the proof. \u220e"
