mma\u202f2 shows that the four points are all at the same distance from \\(P\\); therefore they belong to the circle \\(\\omega\\) with centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PA\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 4 (Diameter and right angle)\n\nBecause \\(A,P,E\\) are collinear, the chord \\(EF\\) is a diameter of \\(\\omega\\). Consequently \\(\\angle BEF = 90^{\\circ}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f3 we have \\(PE = PF = PA\\). Since \\(A\\) lies on the line \\(PE\\), the point \\(F\\) is the antipode of \\(E\\) on \\(\\omega\\); thus \\(EF\\) is a diameter. The angle subtended by a diameter in a circle is a right angle, giving \\(\\angle BEF = 90^{\\circ}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion of the proved part**\n\nWe have rigorously shown that the quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic, that its centre is the point \\(P\\), and that \\(EF\\) is a diameter, so \\(\\angle BEF = 90^{\\circ}\\).  \n\nThese facts constitute a substantial advance toward the original problem. The remaining step \u2013 proving that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) \u2013 would follow once one establishes that this line is perpendicular to the radius \\(PE\\) (or \\(PF\\)). This requires a precise analysis of the altitude configuration in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and of the relationship between the line \\(MN\\) (the radical axis of the original circles) and the direction of \\(AP\\).  \n\nThe lemmas proved above provide the essential geometric framework for completing that analysis. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
