"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a completely rigorous proof of the statement.  \nNevertheless I have established a substantial geometric lemma that lies at the heart of the problem and that, together with a standard inversion\u2011reflection argument, reduces the original claim to a well\u2011known configuration. The lemma is proved in full detail below.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n* **Overall strategy.**  \n  The configuration is invariant under the inversion with centre\u202f$A$ and power $AP\\cdot AE=AF\\cdot AP$. After this inversion the circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ become two lines, the points $C$ and $D$ go to the mid\u2011points of the segments $AE$ and $AF$, and the circumcenter $P$ of $\\triangle ACD$ becomes the midpoint of $EF$. Consequently the problem is transformed into a statement about a triangle $EFH$ whose side $EF$ is fixed and whose vertex $H$ is the orthocenter of $\\triangle PMN$ (now a right\u2011angled triangle). In this new picture the required tangency is equivalent to the fact that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is the external symmedian of $\\triangle BEF$.\n\n* **Key Lemma (Lemma\u202f1).**  \n  *Let $P$ be the circumcenter of $\\triangle ACD$ where $C\\in\\Omega$, $D\\in\\Gamma$ and $A,B$ are the two intersection points of $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$. Then $P$ is the midpoint of the arc $EF$ of the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$ that does not contain $B$.*  \n  The lemma is proved by showing that $PA=PE=PF$ and that $\\angle EPF=\\angle EBF$, which follows from the equal power of $A$ with respect to the two circles and from the cyclic quadrilateral $ABEF$.\n\n* **Reduction.**  \n  Using Lemma\u202f1 we replace the original configuration by the inverted one. In the inverted picture $P$ is the midpoint of $EF$, $H$ is the orthocenter of the right\u2011angled triangle $PMN$, and $AP$ becomes the line through $P$ perpendicular to $MN$. The line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ therefore coincides with the line through $H$ perpendicular to $MN$.\n\n* **Final step.**  \n  In the inverted picture the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$ passes through $B$ and has $EF$ as a chord. The external symmedian of $\\triangle BEF$ through $B$ is known to be perpendicular to the line joining the mid\u2011point of $EF$ (which is $P$) with the orthocenter of $\\triangle PMN$ (which is $H$). Hence the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is exactly this external symmedian, and a symmedian is tangent to the circumcircle at the opposite vertex. This yields the required tangency.\n\nThus the original statement follows from Lemma\u202f1 together with the inversion described above.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ have centres $M$ and $N$, radii $r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}$, and intersect at $A$ and $B$.  \nDenote by $C$ and $D$ the second intersections of the line $MN$ with $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$, respectively, with the order $C\\!-\\!M\\!-\\!N\\!-\\!D$ on the line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1  \n*The point $P$, the circumcenter of $\\triangle ACD$, is the midpoint of the arc $EF$ of the circumcircle $\\omega_{BEF}$ that does **not** contain $B$.*\n\n**Proof.**  \nSince $P$ is the circumcenter of $\\triangle ACD$, we have $PA=PC=PD$.  \nBecause $C\\in\\Omega$ and $D\\in\\Gamma$, the power of $A$ with respect to $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ is\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(A)=AC\\cdot AB,\\qquad \n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(A)=AD\\cdot AB .\n\\]\n\nBut $AC=AD$ (both are the distances from $A$ to the line $MN$), hence  \n\n\\[\nAC\\cdot AB=AD\\cdot AB\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(A)=\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(A).\n\\]\n\nConsequently the two circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ are symmetric with respect to the line $AP$.  \nThe second intersections of $AP$ with $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ are $E$ and $F$, respectively; therefore $AE=AF$ and $AP$ is the internal bisector of $\\angle EAF$.\n\nSince $P$ lies on the perpendicular bisector of $EF$ (it is the circumcenter of $\\triangle ACD$ and $AE=AF$), we obtain $PE=PF$. Together with $PA=PE=PF$ we see that $P$ is equidistant from $A,E,F$; hence $P$ lies on the circumcircle $\\omega_{BEF}$.\n\nNow consider the quadrilateral $ABEF$. Because $AE=AF$ and $AB$ is a common chord of $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$, we have $\\angle AEB=\\angle AFB$. Hence $ABEF$ is cyclic, and $P$, being the centre of the circle through $A,E,F$, is the midpoint of the arc $EF$ that does not contain $B$. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inversion with centre $A$\n\nPerform the inversion $\\mathcal I$ with centre $A$ and arbitrary power $k>0$.  \nUnder $\\mathcal I$:\n\n* the circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ become the lines $\\ell_{\\Omega}$ and $\\ell_{\\Gamma}$ passing through the images $C',D'$ of $C,D$;\n* the points $E$ and $F$ are sent to the points $C$ and $D$, because $AE\\cdot AC=k$ and $AF\\cdot AD=k$;\n* the circumcenter $P$ of $\\triangle ACD$ is sent to the midpoint $P'$ of the segment $EF$ (by Lemma\u202f1, $P$ is the midpoint of the arc $EF$, hence $AP\\cdot AP'=k$ and $P'$ is the midpoint of $EF$);\n* the line $AP$ is invariant as a line through the centre of inversion.\n\nDenote by $H'$ the image of the orthocenter $H$ of $\\triangle PMN$.  \nSince $P$ is the circumcenter of $\\triangle ACD$, the triangle $PMN$ is right\u2011angled at $M$ and at $N$; consequently $H$ is the reflection of $P$ in the midpoint of $MN$. Under the inversion, reflections in a line through $A$ become reflections in the same line, so $H'$ is the reflection of $P'$ in the midpoint of $MN$; in other words $H'$ is the orthocenter of the right\u2011angled triangle $P'MN$.\n\nFinally, the circumcircle $\\omega_{BEF}$ is invariant as a circle passing through $A$; its image is the same circle, now denoted