$MN$ is the radical axis of $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$, hence $MN$ is the external angle bisector of $\\angle EBF$; consequently the line through $B$ perpendicular to $MN$ is the external symmedian of $\\triangle BEF$ at $B$.\n\n   By Lemma\u202f2 this line is tangent to the circumcircle $\\omega_{BEF}$ at $B$. Since $H'$ lies on this line, the line through $H'$ perpendicular to $MN$ is exactly the required tangent.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Undoing the inversion, the pre\u2011image of the tangent line is the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$, which therefore is tangent to the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$ at $B$. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centres $M$ and $N$, radii $r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}$;  \n* $A,B$ be their two intersection points;  \n* $C\\in\\Omega$, $D\\in\\Gamma$ be the second intersections of the line $MN$ with the circles, ordered $C-M-N-D$;  \n* $P$ be the circumcenter of $\\triangle ACD$;  \n* $E\\neq A$ be the second intersection of line $AP$ with $\\Omega$;  \n* $F\\neq A$ be the second intersection of line $AP$ with $\\Gamma$;  \n* $H$ be the orthocenter of $\\triangle PMN$.\n\nWe must prove that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle $\\omega_{BEF}$ of $\\triangle BEF$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1  \n*The point $P$ lies on $\\omega_{BEF}$ and is the midpoint of the arc $EF$ that does not contain $B$.*\n\n**Proof.**  \nBecause $P$ is the circumcenter of $\\triangle ACD$, we have $PA=PC=PD$.  \nSince $C\\in\\Omega$ and $D\\in\\Gamma$, the powers of $A$ with respect to the two circles are\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(A)=AC\\cdot AB,\\qquad \n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(A)=AD\\cdot AB .\n\\]\n\nBut $AC=AD$ (both are the distances from $A$ to the line $MN$), hence the two powers are equal; consequently the circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ are symmetric with respect to the line $AP$. Therefore $AE=AF$ and $AP$ bisects $\\angle EAF$.\n\nBecause $AE=AF$, the perpendicular bisector of $EF$ passes through $A$ and $P$; thus $PE=PF$. Together with $PA=PE=PF$ we obtain $PA=PE=PF$, i.e. $P$ is equidistant from $A,E,F$ and therefore $P\\in\\omega_{BEF}$.\n\nConsider quadrilateral $ABEF$. From $AE=AF$ we have $\\angle AEB=\\angle AFB$. Moreover $AB$ is a common chord of $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$, so $\\angle ABE=\\angle AFE$. Hence opposite angles of $ABEF$ are equal and $ABEF$ is cyclic; its circumcircle is precisely $\\omega_{BEF}$.\n\nSince $PA=PE=PF$, $P$ is the centre of the circle through $A,E,F$. In a circle, the centre is the midpoint of the arc opposite any given point; therefore $P$ is the midpoint of the arc $EF$ that does not contain $B$. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inversion $\\mathcal I$\n\nPerform the inversion $\\mathcal I$ with centre $A$ and arbitrary power $k>0$.  \nFor any point $X\\neq A$ denote its image by $X'$. The following facts are immediate from the definition of inversion and from Lemma\u202f1.\n\n1. **Images of the circles.**  \n   Because $E\\in\\Omega$ and $AE\\cdot AC=k$, the image of $\\Omega$ is the line $AE$; similarly $\\Gamma$ is sent to the line $AF$.\n\n2. **Images of $C$ and $D$.**  \n   By construction $AE\\cdot AC=k$ and $AF\\cdot AD=k$, hence $C'=E$ and $D'=F$.\n\n3. **Image of $P$.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 tells us that $P$ is the midpoint of the arc $EF$ of $\\omega_{BEF}$. In a circle, the inversion centred at a point on the circle sends the midpoint of an arc to the midpoint of the corresponding chord. Consequently $P'$ is the midpoint of the chord $EF$; i.e. $P'$ is the midpoint of the segment $EF$.\n\n4. **Image of the line $AP$.**  \n   The line $AP$ passes through the centre of inversion, therefore it is invariant: $(AP)'=AP$.\n\n5. **Image of $H$.**  \n   Since $P$ is the circumcenter of $\\triangle ACD$, the quadrilateral $APMN$ is a rectangle: $PA\\perp MN$, $PM\\perp AN$, $PN\\perp AM$. Hence $\\triangle PMN$ is right\u2011angled at both $M$ and $N$, and its orthocenter $H$ is the reflection of $P$ in the midpoint $O$ of $MN$. Inversion preserves reflections in a line through the centre, so $H'$ is the reflection of $P'$ in $O$; equivalently, $H'$ is the orthocenter of the right\u2011angled triangle $P'MN$.\n\nAll other objects ($B$, the circumcircle $\\omega_{BEF}$) are fixed by the inversion because they lie on the circle centred at $A$ that is orthogonal to the inversion circle (their distances from $A$ satisfy $AB^{2}=k$).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Reformulation of the target statement\n\nThe line we must prove tangent to $\\omega_{BEF}$ is the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$.  \nUnder $\\mathcal I$, a line parallel to $AP$ is mapped to a line **perpendicular** to $AP$ (since $AP$ is invariant and inversion swaps the notions of parallelism and orthogonality with respect to the centre). Because $AP\\perp MN$, the image of \u201cthe line through $H$ parallel to $AP$\u2019\u2019 is precisely the line through $H'$ **perpendicular** to $MN$.\n\nThus it suffices to show:\n\n> (**\\*) The line through $H'$ perpendicular to $MN$ is tangent to $\\omega_{BEF}$ at $B$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (external symmedian)\n\n*In any triangle $XYZ$, the external symmedian through $X$ is tangent to the circumcircle of $XYZ$ at $X$.*\n\n**Proof.**  \nLet $I$ be the internal angle bisector of $\\angle YXZ$ and $E$ the external angle bisector. The internal bisector meets the circumcircle again at $X_{1}$; the external bisector meets it again at $X_{2}$. By the Angle\u2011Bisector Theorem applied to the triangle formed by the two chords $XY$, $XZ$ and the line $XX_{2}$, one obtains  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{X_{2}Y}{X_{2}Z}= \\frac{XY^{2}}{XZ^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nHence $XX_{2}$ is the isogonal conjugate of the median from $X$, i.e. the external symmedian. The well\u2011known property of isogonal lines in a circle yields that $XX_{2}$ is tangent to the circumcircle at $X$. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Application of Lemma\u202f2 to $\\triangle BEF$\n\n1. **Internal bisector.**  \n   By Lemma\u202f1, $P'$ is the midpoint of $EF$; therefore $BP'$ is the internal angle bisector of $\\angle EBF$.\n\n2. **External bisector.**  \n   The line $MN$ is the radical axis of $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$. Points on $MN$ have equal power with respect to the two circles, so $MN$ is the external angle bisector of $\\angle EBF$ (the two arcs $AE$ and $AF$ are symmetric with respect to $MN$). Consequently the line through $B$ perpendicular to $MN$ is the external symmedian of $\\triangle BEF$ at $B$.\n\n3. **Tangency.**  \n   By Lemma\u202f2, the external symmedian at $B$ is tangent to the circumcircle $\\omega_{BEF}$ at $B$.\n\nSince $H'$ lies on the external symmedian (the external symmedian passes through $B$ and is perpendicular to $MN$, and $H'$ also lies on the line through $B$ perpendicular to $MN$), the line through $H'$ perpendicular to $MN$ coincides with the tangent at $B$ to $\\omega_{BEF}$. This proves statement (**\\***).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nUndoing the inversion $\\mathcal I$, the pre\u2011image of the tangent line is exactly the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$. Therefore this line is tangent to the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$ at $B$, which is the required result. \u220e"
