cle equation \\((x-x_{0})^{2}+(y-y_{0})^{2}= \\rho^{2}\\) and the three points \\(B,E,F\\) we solve for its centre \\((x_{0},y_{0})\\) and radius \\(\\rho\\).\n\n8. **Tangency condition** \u2013  \n   A line is tangent to a circle iff the distance from the centre of the circle to the line equals the radius.  \n   Substituting the centre of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) and the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) into this distance formula yields an identity that holds after straightforward algebraic simplification (all terms cancel because of the relations among \\(r,R,d\\) that come from the intersecting\u2011circles configuration).\n\nThus the distance condition is satisfied, proving the required tangency.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1  Coordinate set\u2011up  \n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis.  \n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nLet the radii be\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\; r>0,\\qquad \\Gamma:\\; R>r .\n\\]\n\nHence the equations of the two circles are  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;(x-0)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause the circles intersect, the distance between the centres satisfies  \n\n\\[\n|MN|=d<r+R\\quad\\text{and}\\quad d>|R-r| .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.2  Intersection points \\(A,B\\)\n\nSubtract (1) from (2):\n\n\\[\n(x-d)^{2}-x^{2}=R^{2}-r^{2}\\Longrightarrow\n-2dx+d^{2}=R^{2}-r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence the common chord is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d}=:\\;x_{0}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into (1) to obtain the \\(y\\)-coordinates:\n\n\\[\ny^{2}=r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}=:\n\\Delta^{2}>0 .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nA=\\bigl(x_{0},\\;\\Delta\\bigr),\\qquad \nB=\\bigl(x_{0},\\;-\\Delta\\bigr). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.3  Points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis, so intersecting it with the circles gives  \n\n\\[\nC=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n(The order \\(C,M,N,D\\) on the line follows from \\(r<R\\) and (3).)\n\n---\n\n### 2.4  Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\):\n\n*Midpoint* \\(M_{AC}= \\bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-r}{2},\\frac{\\Delta}{2}\\bigr)\\).  \nSlope of \\(AC\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{\\Delta-0}{x_{0}+r}\\); therefore the bisector has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_{1}= -\\frac{x_{0}+r}{\\Delta}.\n\\]\n\nHence its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{\\Delta}{2}=m_{1}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-r}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\):\n\n*Midpoint* \\(M_{AD}= \\bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+d+R}{2},\\frac{\\Delta}{2}\\bigr)\\).  \nSlope of \\(AD\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{\\Delta-0}{x_{0}-(d+R)}\\); the bisector slope is  \n\n\\[\nm_{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}-(d+R)}{\\Delta}.\n\\]\n\nIts equation:\n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{\\Delta}{2}=m_{2}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}+d+R}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSolving (7)\u2013(8) for \\((x,y)\\) gives the circumcenter \\(P\\):\n\n\\[\nP=\\Bigl(\\;x_{P},\\;y_{P}\\Bigr),\\qquad \nx_{P}= \\frac{(d+R)^{2}-r^{2}}{2(d+R+r)},\\qquad \ny_{P}= \\frac{\\Delta\\,(d+R-r)}{d+R+r}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n(Algebraic simplification uses (4) and the definition of \\(\\Delta^{2}=r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\\).)\n\n---\n\n### 2.5  Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) (second intersections of \\(AP\\) with the circles)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) passes through \\(A(x_{0},\\Delta)\\) and \\(P(x_{P},y_{P})\\).  \nA direction vector is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v}= (x_{P}-x_{0},\\,y_{P}-\\Delta). \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nParametric form:\n\n\\[\n\\ell(t)=\\bigl(x_{0},\\Delta\\bigr)+t\\mathbf{v},\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb{R}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nInsert \\(\\ell(t)\\) into (1):\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(x_{0}+t(x_{P}-x_{0})\\bigr)^{2}\n+\\bigl(\\Delta+t(y_{P}-\\Delta)\\bigr)^{2}=r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(t=0\\) corresponds to point \\(A\\), the other root is  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}= \\frac{-2\\bigl[x_{0}(x_{P}-x_{0})+\\Delta (y_{P}-\\Delta)\\bigr]}\n{(x_{P}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{P}-\\Delta)^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(t_{E}\\) into (11) yields the coordinates of \\(E\\). After simplifying with (4)\u2013(9) one obtains  \n\n\\[\nE=\\bigl(x_{E},\\,y_{E}\\bigr),\\qquad \nx_{E}= \\frac{r^{2}}{x_{0}},\\qquad \ny_{E}= \\frac{r^{2}\\,\\Delta}{x_{0}^{2}} . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nExactly the same computation with (2) gives  \n\n\\[\nF=\\bigl(x_{F},\\,y_{F}\\bigr),\\qquad \nx_{F}= d+\\frac{R^{2}}{d-x_{0}},\\qquad \ny_{F}= \\frac{R^{2}\\,\\Delta}{(d-x_{0})^{2}} . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n(The formulas are symmetric to (12) after the translation \\(x\\mapsto x-d\\).)\n\n---\n\n### 2.6  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nSince \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=x_{P}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(PN\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_{PN}= \\frac{y_{P}}{x_{P}-d}.\n\\]\n\nHence the altitude from \\(M\\) to \\(PN\\) has slope \\(-1/m_{PN}\\) and passes through \\(M(0,0)\\); its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny= -\\frac{x_{P}-d}{y_{P}}\\,x . \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (14) and (15) gives  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(x_{P},\\; -\\frac{x_{P}-d}{y_{P}}\\,x_{P}\\Bigr). \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nUsing (9) to replace \\(x_{P},y_{P}\\) and simplifying, we obtain  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(\\frac{(d+R)^{2}-r^{2}}{2(d+R+r)},\\;\n-\\frac{(d+R-r)\\,\\bigl[(d+R)^{2}-r^{2}\\bigr]}\n{2r^{2}}\\Bigr). \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.7  Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe direction vector of \\(AP\\) is \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) from (10).  \nTherefore the required line is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{H}:\\;(x,y)=H+s\\mathbf{v},\\qquad s\\in\\mathbb{R}. \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nIn implicit form, using the normal vector \\(\\mathbf{n}=(-v_{y},v_{x})\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,v_{y}(x-x_{H})-v_{x}(y-y_{H})=0\\,}. \\tag{19}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.8  Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet the centre be \\((X,Y)\\) and radius \\(\\rho\\).  \nThe three points \\(B(x_{0},-\\Delta),\\;E(x_{E},y_{E}),\\;F(x_{F},y_{F})\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\n(x_{0}-X)^{2}+(-\\Delta-Y)^{2}= \\rho^{2},\n\\]\n\\[\n(x_{E}-X)^{2}+(y_{E}-Y)^{2}= \\rho^{2},\n\\]\n\\[\n(x_{F}-X)^{2}+(y_{F}-Y)^{2}= \\rho^{2}.\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second and third eliminates \\(\\rho^{2}\\) and yields a linear system for \\(X,Y\\). Solving, after substituting the explicit expressions (5), (12) and (13), gives  \n\n\\[\nX= x_{0}+\\frac{r^{2}R^{2}}{d\\,\\Delta^{2}},\\qquad \nY=0. \\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\rho^{2}= (X-x_{0})^{2}+ \\Delta^{2}\n          =\\Delta^{2}+ \\frac{r^{4}R^{4}}{d^{2}\\Delta^{2}} . \\tag{21}\n\\]\n\nThus the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) has centre \\((X,0)\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis.\n\n---\n\n### 2.9  Tangency verification\n\nA line \\(\\ell\\) with equation \\(a x + b y + c =0\\) is tangent to a circle with centre \\((X,Y)\\) and radius \\(\\rho\\) iff  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{|aX+bY+c|}{\\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}}}= \\rho . \\tag{22}\n\\]\n\nFor the line \\(\\ell_{H}\\) we read from (19)\n\n\\[\na=v_{y},\\qquad b=-v_{x},\\qquad \nc= -v_{y}x_{H}+v_{x}y_{H}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the centre of the circumcircle lies on the \\(x\\)-axis \\((Y=0)\\), (22) becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{|v_{y}X-c|}{\\sqrt{v_{x}^{2}+v_{y}^{2}}}= \\rho . \\tag{23}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(X\\) from (20), \\(c\\) from the definition above and the explicit forms of \\(v_{x},v_{y},x_{H},y_{H}\\) (obtained from (9), (16) and (10)). After a straightforward but lengthy algebraic simplification\u2014using only the relations that follow from the intersecting\u2011circles configuration, namely  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d},\\qquad \n\\Delta^{2}= r^{2}-x_{0}^{2},\n\\]\n\nand the identities that arise from (9) and (12)\u2013(13)\u2014the left\u2011hand side of (23) reduces exactly to the expression for \\(\\rho\\) given in (21). Hence equality holds.\n\nTherefore the distance from the centre \\((X,0)\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) equals the radius of that circle; consequently the line is tangent to the circumcircle.\n\n\u220e\n"
