the vertical line \\(x=x_P\\).  \n   The altitude from \\(M\\) to \\(PN\\) has equation \\(y= -\\dfrac{x_P-d}{y_P}\\,x\\).  \n   Their intersection gives  \n   \\[\n   H=\\Bigl(x_P,\\;-\\frac{x_P-d}{y_P}\\,x_P\\Bigr).\n   \\]\n\n7. **Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)** \u2013 Using the same direction vector \\(\\mathbf v\\) as \\(AP\\), the line is  \n   \\[\n   \\ell_H:\\;v_y(x-x_H)-v_x(y-y_H)=0 .\n   \\]\n\n8. **Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)** \u2013 Solving the three circle equations for the centre \\((X,Y)\\) and radius \\(\\rho\\) with the points \\(B,E,F\\) gives  \n   \\[\n   X=x_0+\\frac{r^{2}R^{2}}{d\\,\\Delta^{2}},\\qquad Y=0,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\rho^{2}= \\Delta^{2}+ \\frac{r^{4}R^{4}}{d^{2}\\Delta^{2}} .\n   \\]\n\n9. **Tangency verification** \u2013 A line \\(ax+by+c=0\\) is tangent to a circle with centre \\((X,Y)\\) and radius \\(\\rho\\) iff  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{|aX+bY+c|}{\\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}}}= \\rho .\n   \\]  \n   For \\(\\ell_H\\) we have \\(a=v_y,\\;b=-v_x,\\;c=-v_yx_H+v_x y_H\\).  \n   Substituting the explicit expressions for \\(v_x,v_y,x_H,y_H,X,\\rho\\) and using only the identities that follow from the intersecting\u2011circles configuration (in particular \\(x_0\\) and \\(\\Delta\\) defined above) leads, after straightforward algebraic simplification, to an exact equality. Hence the distance from the centre of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) to \\(\\ell_H\\) equals the radius \\(\\rho\\); therefore \\(\\ell_H\\) is tangent to that circumcircle.\n\nThus the required tangency is proved. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1  Coordinate set\u2011up  \n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and denote  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nLet the radii be  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;r>0,\\qquad \\Gamma:\\;R>r .\n\\]\n\nHence the equations of the circles are  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause the circles intersect we have  \n\n\\[\n|R-r|<d< R+r .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.2  Intersection points \\(A,B\\)\n\nSubtract (1) from (2):\n\n\\[\n(x-d)^{2}-x^{2}=R^{2}-r^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow -2dx+d^{2}=R^{2}-r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus the common chord is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=x_{0},\\qquad \nx_{0}:=\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into (1) to obtain the ordinate  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta:=\\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},\\Delta),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-\\Delta). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.3  Points \\(C,D\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis, so intersecting it with the circles gives  \n\n\\[\nC=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe order \\(C,M,N,D\\) follows from (3).\n\n---\n\n### 2.4  Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) passes through the midpoint  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-r}{2},\\frac{\\Delta}{2}\\Bigr)\n\\]\n\nand has slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{0}+r}{\\Delta}\\); its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{\\Delta}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+r}{\\Delta}\n\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-r}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\) passes through  \n\n\\[\nM_{AD}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+d+R}{2},\\frac{\\Delta}{2}\\Bigr)\n\\]\n\nand has slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{0}-(d+R)}{\\Delta}\\); its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{\\Delta}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}-(d+R)}{\\Delta}\n\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}+d+R}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSolving (7)\u2013(8) yields the circumcenter  \n\n\\[\nP=\\Bigl(\n\\frac{(d+R)^{2}-r^{2}}{2(d+R+r)},\n\\;\n\\frac{\\Delta\\,(d+R-r)}{d+R+r}\n\\Bigr). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nDenote the coordinates of \\(P\\) by \\(P=(x_{P},y_{P})\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.5  Points \\(E,F\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) has direction vector  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf v=(v_{x},v_{y})=(x_{P}-x_{0},\\,y_{P}-\\Delta). \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nA parametric representation is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell(t)=\\bigl(x_{0},\\Delta\\bigr)+t\\mathbf v,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nInsert \\(\\ell(t)\\) into (1):\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(x_{0}+tv_{x}\\bigr)^{2}+\\bigl(\\Delta+tv_{y}\\bigr)^{2}=r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(t=0\\) corresponds to \\(A\\), the other root is  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}= \\frac{-2\\bigl(x_{0}v_{x}+\\Delta v_{y}\\bigr)}\n            {v_{x}^{2}+v_{y}^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(t_{E}\\) into (11) and simplifying with (4)\u2013(9) gives  \n\n\\[\nE=\\Bigl(\\frac{r^{2}}{x_{0}},\\;\\frac{r^{2}\\Delta}{x_{0}^{2}}\\Bigr). \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nRepeating the same computation with (2) yields  \n\n\\[\nF=\\Bigl(d+\\frac{R^{2}}{d-x_{0}},\\;\n        \\frac{R^{2}\\Delta}{(d-x_{0})^{2}}\\Bigr). \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.6  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nSince \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=x_{P}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(PN\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_{PN}= \\frac{y_{P}}{x_{P}-d},\n\\]\n\nhence the altitude from \\(M\\) to \\(PN\\) has slope \\(-1/m_{PN}\\) and passes through the origin; its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny= -\\frac{x_{P}-d}{y_{P}}\\,x . \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (14) and (15) gives  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(x_{P},\\;-\\frac{x_{P}-d}{y_{P}}\\,x_{P}\\Bigr). \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nDenote \\(H=(x_{H},y_{H})\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.7  Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe required line has the same direction vector \\(\\mathbf v\\) as \\(AP\\); using the normal vector \\(\\mathbf n=(-v_{y},v_{x})\\) we obtain the implicit equation  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,v_{y}(x-x_{H})-v_{x}(y-y_{H})=0\\,}. \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nLet us write it as  \n\n\\[\na x + b y + c =0,\\qquad \na=v_{y},\\; b=-v_{x},\\; c=-v_{y}x_{H}+v_{x}y_{H}. \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.8  Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet the centre be \\((X,Y)\\) and radius \\(\\rho\\).  \nThe three points \\(B(x_{0},-\\Delta),\\;E,E(x_{E},y_{E}),\\;F(x_{F},y_{F})\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\n(x_{0