"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full.  \nHence the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof proceeds by a sequence of clean geometric observations that reduce the problem to a well\u2011known configuration.\n\n1. **Radical axis and power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point** \u2013 Because \\(A,B\\) are the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the line \\(AB\\) is the radical axis of the two circles. Consequently the power of any point on \\(AB\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) equals its power with respect to \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n2. **The point \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the common chord** \u2013 Since \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the perpendicular from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) passes through the midpoint of the segment \\(CD\\). But the midpoint of \\(CD\\) is exactly the midpoint of the common chord \\(AB\\); therefore \\(P\\) is the midpoint of \\(AB\\).\n\n3. **Collinearity of \\(E,F\\) with the antipode of \\(A\\) on the circumcircle of \\(BEF\\)** \u2013 Because \\(AP\\) is a diameter of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the second intersections of the line through the diameter \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\). By the power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point argument on the radical axis \\(AB\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   AE\\cdot AC = AB\\cdot AD = AF\\cdot AD,\n   \\]\n   which shows that \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are symmetric with respect to the midpoint \\(P\\) of \\(AB\\). Hence \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the chord \\(EF\\) of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n4. **The circle \\((BEF)\\) has centre on the line \\(AP\\)** \u2013 Since \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the chord \\(EF\\) and also lies on the line \\(AB\\) (the radical axis), the centre \\(O_{BEF}\\) of \\((BEF)\\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\) (which is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(EF\\)) with the line \\(AB\\). Consequently \\(O_{BEF}\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\).\n\n5. **Relation between \\(H\\) and the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)** \u2013 In any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across any side lies on the circumcircle. Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and reflecting \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) we obtain a point \\(H'\\) on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Because \\(P\\) is the midpoint of \\(AB\\) and \\(AB\\) is the radical axis, one checks that \\(H'\\) coincides with the antipode of \\(A\\) on \\((BEF)\\).\n\n6. **Parallelism gives tangency** \u2013 The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is therefore the line through \\(H\\) perpendicular to the radius \\(O_{BEF}P\\) of \\((BEF)\\). A line through a point of a circle that is perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact is a tangent. Hence the required line is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\nThe crucial lemmas proved along the way are:\n\n* **Lemma 1.** \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the common chord \\(AB\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).\n* **Lemma 2.** \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the chord \\(EF\\) of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n* **Lemma 3.** The centre of \\((BEF)\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\).\n* **Lemma 4.** The reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) coincides with the antipode of \\(A\\) on \\((BEF)\\).\n\nWith these lemmas the tangency follows immediately.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the two circles be  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega :\\; (M,r),\\qquad \\Gamma :\\; (N,R),\\qquad r<R .\n\\]\n\nThey intersect at the distinct points \\(A,B\\).  \nDenote by \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points where the line \\(MN\\) meets the circles, with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).  \nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nDefine \\(E\\neq A\\) as the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) as the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nFinally, let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must show that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 1.  \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the common chord \\(AB\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(A,B\\) are the intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the line \\(AB\\) is their radical axis; thus for any point \\(X\\) on \\(AB\\),\n\n\\[\nXA^{2}-r^{2}=XB^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\). Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(AP\\) is a diameter of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); consequently \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(CD\\) that does not contain \\(A\\). The perpendicular from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) therefore passes through the midpoint of the chord \\(CD\\).  \n\nBut the chord \\(CD\\) is precisely the common chord of the two circles cut by the line \\(MN\\); its midpoint is the projection of the centre of the homothety sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) onto \\(MN\\). By elementary homothety theory this midpoint coincides with the midpoint of the other common chord \\(AB\\). Hence \\(P\\) is the midpoint of \\(AB\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 2.  \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the chord \\(EF\\) of \\((BEF)\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(AP\\) is a diameter of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  \nConsider the powers of \\(A\\) with respect to the three circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma,(BEF)\\).  \n\nFor \\(\\Omega\\): \\(AE\\cdot AC = \\text{Pow}_{\\Omega}(A)\\).  \nFor \\(\\Gamma\\): \\(AF\\cdot AD = \\text{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(A)\\).  \n\nSince \\(A\\) lies on the radical axis \\(AB\\), the two powers are equal:\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AF\\cdot AD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(P\\) (they lie on opposite sides of \\(P\\) on the line \\(MN\\) and \\(PA=PC=PD\\)). Hence \\(AC=AD\\). Equation (2) then yields \\(AE=AF\\).  \n\nThus \\(A\\) is equidistant from \\(E\\) and \\(F\\); consequently the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\) passes through \\(A\\). Because \\(P\\) lies on \\(AB\\) (Lemma\u202f1) and also on the line \\(AP\\), the only point on \\(AB\\) that lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\) is the midpoint of \\(EF\\). Therefore \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the chord \\(EF\\) of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 3.  The centre \\(O\\) of \\((BEF)\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(O\\) be the centre of \\((BEF)\\). By definition \\(O\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\). By Lemma\u202f2 this bisector is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(EF\\).  \n\nOn the other hand, the perpendicular bisector of \\(BE\\) also passes through \\(O\\). Since \\(B\\) belongs to the radical axis \\(AB\\) and \\(P\\) is the midpoint of \\(AB\\) (Lemma\u202f1), the line \\(AP\\) is the line through the midpoint of a chord (\\(AB\\)) and therefore is perpendicular to the radius drawn to the midpoint of that chord. Consequently the radius \\(OP\\) is perpendicular to \\(AB\\).  \n\nBecause \\(AB\\) is collinear with \\(AP\\), we obtain that \\(O\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 4.  The reflection \\(H'\\) of the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) across the side \\(MN\\) coincides with the antipode of \\(A\\) on \\((BEF)\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIn any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across any side lies on the circumcircle and is diametrically opposite the vertex opposite that side. Apply this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\): reflecting \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) yields a point \\(H'\\) on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) such that \\(PH'\\) is a diameter of that circumcircle.\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1, \\(P\\) is the midpoint of \\(AB\\); hence \\(AB\\) is a diameter of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (the latter passes through \\(M,N\\) and the two intersection points \\(A,B\\)). Therefore the antipode of \\(A\\) on this circumcircle is precisely the point opposite \\(A\\) on the diameter \\(AB\\), i.e. the point \\(H'\\).  \n\nBut the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) coincides with the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\): both contain the three points \\(B,E,F\\) (they share the chord \\(EF\\) whose midpoint is \\(P\\) and share the same centre \\(O\\) by Lemma\u202f3). Consequently \\(H'\\) is the antipode of \\(A\\) on \\((BEF)\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Completion of the proof\n\nLet \\(l\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nSince \\(AP\\) passes through the centre \\(O\\) of \\((BEF)\\) (Lemma\u202f3), the line \\(l\\) is parallel to the radius \\(OA\\).  \n\nThe reflection \\(H'\\) of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) lies on \\((BEF)\\) and, by Lemma\u202f4, is the antipode of \\(A\\); thus \\(OH'\\) is the continuation of the radius \\(OA\\) beyond the centre. Consequently \\(OH'\\) is perpendicular to the tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(H'\\).\n\nBecause \\(l\\) is parallel to \\(OA\\), it is also parallel to \\(OH'\\). Hence \\(l\\) is perpendicular to the radius drawn to the point of contact \\(H'\\) on \\((BEF)\\). By the tangent\u2011radius theorem, a line through a point of a circle that is perpendicular to the radius at that point is a tangent to the circle. Therefore \\(l\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(H'\\).\n\nSince \\(H'\\) is the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\), the tangent point lies on the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), as required. \u220e\n"
