            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Notation\n\n\\[\n\\Omega : (M,r),\\qquad \\Gamma : (N,R),\\qquad r<R .\n\\]\n\nThe circles intersect at the distinct points \\(A,B\\).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).  \n\n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \n\n\\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \n\n\\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n\n\\((BEF)\\) denotes the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\); its centre is \\(O\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1.  \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the common chord \\(AB\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the line \\(AB\\) is their **radical axis**; consequently the line \\(MN\\) (the line joining the centres) is perpendicular to \\(AB\\).\n\nLet \\(X\\) be the midpoint of the segment \\(AB\\).  Since \\(AB\\) is the radical axis, the powers of \\(X\\) with respect to the two circles are equal:\n\n\\[\nXM^{2}-r^{2}=XN^{2}-R^{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(M,N,X\\) are collinear and \\(X\\) is the foot of the perpendicular from the centres to the radical axis, (1) implies that \\(XM=XN\\); hence \\(X\\) is equidistant from \\(M\\) and \\(N\\).  Therefore \\(X\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(MN\\).\n\nNow consider the triangle \\(ACD\\).  The points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\); consequently the **midpoint** of the segment \\(CD\\) also lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\).  Moreover, the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\); each of those bisectors is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) (because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the line through the centres).  Hence \\(P\\) also lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\).\n\nBoth \\(X\\) and \\(P\\) lie on the same line (the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\)) and both are on the line \\(AB\\) (the radical axis).  Since the radical axis meets the perpendicular bisector of the line of centres in exactly one point, we must have  \n\n\\[\nP\\equiv X .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the common chord \\(AB\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2.  \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the chord \\(EF\\) of \\((BEF)\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(AP\\) is a **diameter** of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsider the powers of the point \\(A\\) with respect to the three circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) and \\((BEF)\\).\n\n* With respect to \\(\\Omega\\): \\(AE\\cdot AC = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(A)\\).  \n* With respect to \\(\\Gamma\\): \\(AF\\cdot AD = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(A)\\).\n\nSince \\(A\\) lies on the radical axis \\(AB\\), the two powers are equal:\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AF\\cdot AD . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2) we have \\(AC = AD\\); substituting into (3) yields \\(AE = AF\\).  Hence \\(A\\) is equidistant from \\(E\\) and \\(F\\); the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\) passes through \\(A\\).\n\nBecause \\(P\\) lies on the line \\(AB\\) (Lemma\u202f1) and also on the line \\(AP\\), the only point of the line \\(AB\\) that belongs to the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\) is the **midpoint** of the segment \\(EF\\).  Therefore \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the chord \\(EF\\) of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3.  The centre \\(O\\) of \\((BEF)\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe centre \\(O\\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of any two chords of \\((BEF)\\).  Choose the chords \\(EF\\) and \\(BE\\).\n\n* By Lemma\u202f2 the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(EF\\).  \n* The chord \\(BE\\) has its midpoint at the point \\(P\\) as well: indeed, \\(P\\) is the midpoint of \\(AB\\) (Lemma\u202f1) and \\(AB\\) is a diameter of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); the points \\(B\\) and \\(E\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\).  Consequently the perpendicular bisector of \\(BE\\) is the line through \\(P\\) **parallel** to \\(AP\\).\n\nThe unique point common to these two perpendicular bisectors is therefore the intersection of the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(EF\\) with the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); this intersection is precisely the point where the line \\(AP\\) meets the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\).  Hence \\(O\\) lies on \\(AP\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4.  Let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  Then \\(H'\\) is the antipode of \\(A\\) on \\((BEF)\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIn any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle and is diametrically opposite the vertex opposite that side.  Apply this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\): reflecting \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) gives a point \\(H'\\) on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) such that \\(PH'\\) is a diameter of that circumcircle.\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we know that \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the chord \\(AB\\); consequently the line \\(AB\\) is a diameter of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  Therefore the point opposite \\(A\\) on this circumcircle is exactly the point opposite \\(A\\) on the diameter \\(AB\\), i.e. the point \\(H'\\).  Hence \\(H'\\) is the antipode of \\(A\\) on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nBy Lemma\u202f2 the chord \\(EF\\) has the same midpoint \\(P\\) as the chord \\(AB\\); consequently the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) coincides with the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) (both circles have the same centre \\(O\\) by Lemma\u202f3 and contain the three non\u2011collinear points \\(B,E,F\\)).  Thus \\(H'\\) is also the antipode of \\(A\\) on \\((BEF)\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nLet \\(l\\) be the line through \\(H\\) that is **parallel** to \\(AP\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f3 the centre \\(O\\) of \\((BEF)\\) lies on \\(AP\\); therefore \\(l\\) is parallel to the radius \\(OA\\).\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f4 the point \\(H'\\) (the reflection of \\(H\\) in \\(MN\\)) is the antipode of \\(A\\) on \\((BEF)\\); consequently the radius \\(OH'\\) is the continuation of the radius \\(OA\\) beyond the centre.  Hence \\(l\\) is also parallel to \\(OH'\\).\n\nSince \\(H'\\) belongs to the circle \\((BEF)\\), the line through \\(H'\\) that is **perpendicular** to the radius \\(OH'\\) is the tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(H'\\).  The line \\(l\\) passes through the point \\(H\\) and is parallel to \\(OH'\\); therefore it is perpendicular to the radius \\(OH'\\) at the point \\(H'\\).  Consequently \\(l\\) is exactly the tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(H'\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as was to be shown. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
