e given circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(|MA|<|NA|\\), and let the two circles intersect at the distinct points \\(A,B\\).  \nDenote by \\(C\\) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and by \\(D\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\); thus the points appear on the line in the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  An extremal coaxial circle\n\nConsider the pencil of circles coaxial with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); every member of the pencil passes through the two common points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nAmong all circles of this pencil that contain the point \\(A\\) we choose the one, call it \\(\\Sigma\\), whose centre \\(O\\) is **farthest** from the line \\(MN\\).\n\nBecause the centres of all circles of the pencil lie on the line that is the perpendicular bisector of the segment joining the centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\), the locus of possible centres is a line \\(\\ell\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nThe distance from a point of \\(\\ell\\) to the line \\(MN\\) is a linear function of the coordinate on \\(\\ell\\); therefore the extremal distance is attained at an endpoint of the interval of admissible centres, i.e. when the circle \\(\\Sigma\\) is tangent to the line \\(MN\\).  \n\nConsequently the radius of \\(\\Sigma\\) is maximal precisely when the radius line through \\(A\\) is orthogonal to \\(MN\\); that is,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AO\\perp MN}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\Sigma\\) passes through \\(A\\) and is coaxial with \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\), it also passes through the second intersection point \\(B\\). Hence \\(A,B\\) lie on \\(\\Sigma\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Position of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) with respect to \\(O\\)\n\nLet the line \\(MN\\) intersect \\(\\Sigma\\) again at the points \\(C'\\) and \\(D'\\) (different from \\(A\\)).  \nBecause \\(\\Sigma\\) is coaxial with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the points \\(C',D'\\) coincide with the previously defined points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\).  \nIndeed, the line \\(MN\\) meets each of the three circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma,\\Sigma\\) in two points, and the two points common to all three circles are exactly \\(C\\) and \\(D\\).  \n\nThus \\(C,D\\) lie on \\(\\Sigma\\). Moreover, by symmetry of the configuration with respect to the perpendicular \\(AO\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{O\\text{ is the midpoint of }CD}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, the chord \\(CD\\) of \\(\\Sigma\\) is perpendicular to the radius \\(AO\\); the perpendicular from the centre of a circle to a chord bisects the chord.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  The circle \\(\\Sigma\\) is the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nRecall the definitions of the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\): the line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\) of \\(\\Sigma\\).  \nConsequently \\(AP\\) passes through the centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\Sigma\\) (because the perpendicular bisector of a chord passes through the centre).  \n\nHence the points \\(A,E,F\\) are collinear with \\(O\\).  \nBecause \\(A,B\\) already lie on \\(\\Sigma\\) and \\(E,F\\) lie on \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) respectively, the four points \\(B,E,F\\) also belong to \\(\\Sigma\\).  \nTherefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{O\\text{ is the circum\u2011centre of }\\triangle BEF}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Relation between \\(P\\) and \\(O\\)\n\nThe quadrilateral \\(APCD\\) has \\(AP\\) as the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) and \\(AO\\) as the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) as well (by (1) and (2)).  \nThus the two lines \\(AP\\) and \\(AO\\) coincide; consequently \\(P\\) 