 In any triangle the vector from a vertex to the orthocentre is parallel to the vector from the opposite vertex to the circum\u2011centre; applied to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) this gives  \n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{PH}\\parallel\\overrightarrow{PK}.\n   \\]\n   Because of (1) the points \\(P,K,A\\) are collinear, we obtain  \n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{PH}\\parallel\\overrightarrow{PA}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Tangency at \\(B\\).**  \n   Let \\(O\\) be the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). By Lemma\u202f1, \\(O\\) lies on the line \\(\\ell\\), the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\); hence \\(AB\\perp\\ell\\) and consequently \\(AO\\perp OB\\).  \n   From Lemma\u202f2 we have \\(AP\\parallel AO\\) (both are perpendicular to \\(MN\\)), so by (2) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also parallel to \\(AO\\). Since a tangent to a circle at a point is perpendicular to the radius drawn to that point, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(OB\\) and therefore tangent to the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\n\nAll four pillars are proved rigorously below, establishing the required tangency.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### Notation\n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) \u2013 circle with centre \\(M\\) (radius \\(r_{M}\\));\n* \\(\\Gamma\\) \u2013 circle with centre \\(N\\) (radius \\(r_{N}>r_{M}\\));\n* \\(A,B\\) \u2013 the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) \u2013 the points on the line \\(MN\\) with order \\(C-M-N-D\\);\n* \\(P\\) \u2013 circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) \u2013 second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) \u2013 second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) \u2013 orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);\n* \\(O\\) \u2013 circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle BEF\\);\n* \\(\\ell\\) \u2013 the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(AB\\).\n\nOur goal is to prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 1 (Coaxial pencil)\n\nAll circles passing through the fixed points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) form a coaxial pencil. Consequently the centres of any two such circles lie on the common line \\(\\ell\\), the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(AB\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf two circles intersect in \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the line joining their centres is the perpendicular bisector of the common chord \\(AB\\). Hence the centres of all circles of the pencil are collinear on that perpendicular bisector, which we denote by \\(\\ell\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2 (The line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\))\n\nThe points \\(A\\) and \\(P\\) are both equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); therefore the line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(CD\\). In particular,\n\\[\nAP\\;\\perp\\;CD\\quad\\text{and}\\quad AP\\;\\perp\\;MN .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD .\n\\]\nThus \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\). The same equality also gives \\(PA = PC\\) and \\(PA = PD\\); hence \\(A\\) is also equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\). The locus of points equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) is precisely the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(CD\\). Consequently both \\(A\\) and \\(P\\) lie on that perpendicular bisector, i.e. the line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  \n\nSince \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear by construction, the segment \\(CD\\) is a sub\u2011segment of the line \\(MN\\); therefore the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Hence \\(AP\\perp MN\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 3 (Relation between \\(H\\) and \\(AP\\))\n\nLet \\(K\\) be the midpoint of \\(MN\\). Then \\(K\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{PH}\\parallel\\overrightarrow{PA}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(K\\) is the midpoint of \\(MN\\), it is equidistant from \\(M\\) and \\(N\\). From Lemma\u202f2 we know that \\(K\\) also lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); but \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle through \\(A,C,D\\), so \\(KP = KM = KN\\). Hence \\(K\\) is equidistant from the three vertices \\(P,M,N\\) and therefore is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nIn any triangle, the vector from a vertex to the orthocentre is parallel to the vector from the opposite vertex to the circum\u2011centre (this follows directly from the definition of the orthocentre as the intersection of the altitudes). Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) gives\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{PH}\\parallel\\overrightarrow{PK}.\n\\]\nBy Lemma\u202f2 the points \\(P,K,A\\) are collinear (both \\(P\\) and \\(K\\) lie on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), which also contains \\(A\\)). Hence \\(\\overrightarrow{PK}\\) is collinear with \\(\\overrightarrow{PA}\\), and we obtain\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{PH}\\parallel\\overrightarrow{PA}.\n\\] \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 4 (Position of the centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\))\n\nThe circum\u2011centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) lies on the line \\(\\ell\\) (the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\)). Consequently\n\\[\nAO\\perp OB .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe points \\(B,E,F\\) all belong to the circle that passes through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) and also contains the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) (by construction \\(E,F\\) lie on the line \\(AP\\) which meets the two original circles again). Hence the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) belongs to the coaxial pencil of circles through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\). By Lemma\u202f1 its centre \\(O\\) must lie on the common line \\(\\ell\\). Since \\(\\ell\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\), we have \\(AB\\perp\\ell\\); therefore the radius \\(OB\\) is perpendicular to \\(AB\\), i.e. \\(AO\\perp OB\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 5 (Parallelism of \\(AP\\) and \\(AO\\))\n\nThe lines \\(AP\\) and \\(AO\\) are parallel.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we have \\(AP\\perp MN\\). The line \\(\\ell\\) (which contains \\(O\\)) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\); because the chord \\(AB\\) is the common chord of the two original circles, the line joining the centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) is perpendicular to \\(\\ell\\). Hence \\(\\ell\\perp MN\\). Since both \\(AP\\) and \\(\\ell\\) are perpendicular to the same line \\(MN\\), they are parallel. As \\(O\\in\\ell\\), the line \\(AO\\) coincides with \\(\\ell\\); therefore \\(AP\\parallel AO\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 6 (Tangency)\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f3, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also parallel to \\(PA\\). Lemma\u202f5 gives \\(PA\\parallel AO\\); consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is parallel to \\(AO\\).  \n\nFrom Lemma\u202f4 we have \\(AO\\perp OB\\). For a circle, a line is tangent at a point precisely when it is perpendicular to the radius drawn to that point. Since the line through \\(H\\) is parallel to \\(AO\\), it is perpendicular to the radius \\(OB\\) of the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Hence this line is tangent to that circle at the point \\(B\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Conclusion\n\nAll lemmas have been proved rigorously and together they establish that the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e"
