cond intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) \u2013 orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);\n* \\(O\\) \u2013 circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle BEF\\);\n* \\(\\ell\\) \u2013 the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(AB\\).\n\nOur aim is to prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 1 (The line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\))\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD .\n\\]\nThus \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\). The same equalities also give \\(PA = PC\\) and \\(PA = PD\\); hence \\(A\\) is also equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\).  \nThe locus of points equidistant from two fixed points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(CD\\). Consequently both \\(A\\) and \\(P\\) lie on that perpendicular bisector, i.e.\n\\[\nAP\\;\\perp\\;CD .\n\\]\n\nSince the points \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear by construction, the segment \\(CD\\) is a sub\u2011segment of the line \\(MN\\); therefore\n\\[\nAP\\;\\perp\\;MN . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 2 (The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) lies on \\(AP\\))\n\nIn any triangle the altitude from a vertex is the line through that vertex perpendicular to the opposite side.  \nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the side opposite the vertex \\(P\\) is \\(MN\\). By Lemma\u202f1 the line \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\); hence \\(AP\\) is precisely the altitude from \\(P\\).  \n\nThe orthocentre \\(H\\) is the common intersection of the three altitudes, so \\(H\\) belongs to the altitude from \\(P\\). Consequently\n\\[\nH\\in AP . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line \\(AP\\) itself.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 3 (The centre \\(O\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\))\n\nAll circles that pass through the two fixed points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) form a coaxial pencil. A standard property of a coaxial pencil is that the centres of all its circles are collinear on the common line \\(\\ell\\), the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(AB\\).  \n\nThe points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are defined as the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) passes through the two fixed points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\). Hence its centre \\(O\\) belongs to the pencil and consequently\n\\[\nO\\in\\ell .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\), we have\n\\[\nAO\\perp AB\\quad\\text{and}\\quad OB\\perp AB . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 4 (The line \\(AP\\) is parallel to \\(\\ell\\))\n\nThe line joining the centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) of the two original circles is the perpendicular bisector of the common chord \\(AB\\); therefore \\(MN\\perp\\ell\\).  \nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we already know that \\(AP\\perp MN\\). Two lines that are both perpendicular to the same line are parallel; hence\n\\[\nAP\\parallel\\ell . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(O\\in\\ell\\), (4) also yields \\(AP\\parallel AO\\).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 5 (Tangency of \\(AP\\) at \\(B\\))\n\nFrom (3) the radius \\(OB\\) is perpendicular to the chord \\(AB\\). By (4) the line \\(AP\\) is parallel to \\(AO\\); consequently \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(OB\\).  \n\nA line through a point of a circle that is perpendicular to the radius drawn to that point is the tangent to the circle at that point. Therefore \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(B\\).\n\n---\n\n### Final step\n\nBy Lemma\u202f2 the orthocentre \\(H\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\); consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly the line \\(AP\\). Lemma\u202f5 shows that this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\n\nHence the required statement is proved. \u220e"
