), we have \\(\\angle ACB = \\angle ADB\\). Therefore  \n     \\[\n     \\boxed{\\;\\angle AEB = \\angle AFB\\;}. \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n\n4. **Tangency at \\(B\\)**  \n   * From (3) we obtain \\(\\angle (AE,EB)=\\angle (AF,FB)\\). Because \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) are the same line (they are both the line \\(AP\\)), (3) can be rewritten as  \n     \\[\n     \\angle (AP,EB)=\\angle (AP,FB). \\tag{4}\n     \\]  \n   * Equation (4) states that the two angles formed by the line \\(AP\\) with the chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) are equal. By the *tangent\u2013chord theorem*, this is precisely the condition for \\(AP\\) to be tangent to the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(B\\).  \n\n5. **Conclusion**  \n   * By (2) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line \\(AP\\) itself.  \n   * By step\u202f4, \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).  \n\nHence the required statement is proved. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{N}>r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the points on the line \\(MN\\) with order \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must show that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus both \\(A\\) and \\(P\\) are equidistant from the two points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\).  \nThe set of points equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(CD\\); consequently the line \\(AP\\) is that perpendicular bisector. Because \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear, we obtain\n\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) lies on \\(AP\\)\n\nIn any triangle the altitude from a vertex is the line through that vertex perpendicular to the opposite side.  \nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the side opposite the vertex \\(P\\) is \\(MN\\); by (1) the line \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Hence \\(AP\\) is precisely the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n\nThe orthocentre \\(H\\) is the common intersection of the three altitudes, so \\(H\\) belongs to the altitude from \\(P\\). Therefore\n\n\\[\nH\\in AP . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line \\(AP\\) itself.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Equality of the angles \\(\\angle AEB\\) and \\(\\angle AFB\\)\n\nBecause \\(A,B,E\\) lie on the same circle \\(\\Omega\\), the inscribed angle theorem gives\n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle ACB , \\tag{6}\n\\]\nsince both angles subtend the chord \\(AB\\) of \\(\\Omega\\).\n\nSimilarly, \\(A,B,F\\) lie on the circle \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\angle AFB = \\angle ADB . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) we know that \\(PA=PC=PD\\); hence \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(CD\\) of the circle with centre \\(P\\) passing through \\(C\\) and \\(D\\). Consequently the chord \\(CD\\) is symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\); in particular the two base angles at \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) in the isosceles triangle \\(PCD\\) are equal:\n\n\\[\n\\angle ACB = \\angle ADB . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nCombining (6), (7) and (8) yields\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle AEB = \\angle AFB\\;}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Tangency of \\(AP\\) to the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nEquation (3) can be rewritten as\n\n\\[\n\\angle (AE,EB)=\\angle (AF,FB).\n\\]\n\nSince the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) both lie on the line \\(AP\\), we have \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) coincident with \\(AP\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,EB)=\\angle (AP,FB). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe *tangent\u2013chord theorem* states that a line through a point of a circle is tangent to the circle iff the angle it makes with one chord through that point equals the angle made with the other chord through the same point. Relation (4) is exactly this condition for the circle passing through \\(B,E,F\\). Therefore the line \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Completion of the proof\n\nFrom (2) we have \\(H\\in AP\\); consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line \\(AP\\) itself. By the result of step\u202f4, this line is tangent to the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\n\nThus the required statement is proved. \u220e"
