1)** and simplify. The terms \\(h^{2}\\) cancel, leaving  \n   \\[\n   x^{2}-r_{M}^{2}= (y+z)^{2}-r_{N}^{2}.\n   \\]\n   Using \\(r_{M}=x\\) and \\(r_{N}=z\\) (from (2)) we obtain  \n   \\[\n   x^{2}-x^{2}= (y+z)^{2}-z^{2}\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;0= y^{2}+2yz.\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(y=0\\); that is, the point where the common chord \\(AB\\) meets the line \\(MN\\) is exactly the midpoint of the segment \\(CD\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion of the lemma.**  \n   The midpoint of \\(CD\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\) and also on the common chord \\(AB\\). Therefore the chord \\(AB\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Since \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(AP\\) is the line joining \\(A\\) to the centre of the circle through \\(A,C,D\\); this centre lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), which is the line through the midpoint of \\(CD\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Consequently \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\). \u220e\n\n5. **Use of the lemma in the original argument.**  \n   * Because \\(AP\\perp MN\\), the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line \\(AP\\). Hence the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) lies on \\(AP\\); the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is simply the line \\(AP\\) itself.  \n   * Angles \\(\\angle AEB\\) and \\(\\angle AFB\\) are equal (both subtend chord \\(AB\\) in the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively, and the symmetry of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) forces the two subtended angles to coincide). Hence \\(\\angle (AP,EB)=\\angle (AP,FB)\\).  \n   * By the tangent\u2013chord theorem this equality shows that \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).  \n\n   Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (i.e. the line \\(AP\\)) is tangent to the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as required. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{N}>r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the points on the line \\(MN\\) with order \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A perpendicularity lemma\n\nDenote by \\(r_{M}=MC\\) and \\(r_{N}=ND\\) the radii of the two circles.  \nLet the directed distances on the line \\(MN\\) be\n\n\\[\nMC = x,\\qquad CN = y,\\qquad ND = z .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(MN = x+y\\) and \\(MD = x+y+z\\).  \nLet \\(h\\) be the (non\u2011negative) perpendicular distance from the common point \\(A\\) to the line \\(MN\\). Then, by the Pythagorean theorem,\n\n\\[\nMA^{2}=x^{2}+h^{2},\\qquad NA^{2}=(y+z)^{2}+h^{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(A\\) lies on both circles, the powers of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are equal:\n\n\\[\nMA^{2}-r_{M}^{2}=NA^{2}-r_{N}^{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(r_{M}=x\\) and \\(r_{N}=z\\) (from the definitions of \\(x\\) and \\(z\\)) and the expressions (1) into (2) gives\n\n\\[\n(x^{2}+h^{2})-x^{2}=((y+z)^{2}+h^{2})-z^{2}\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n0 = y^{2}+2yz .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(y=0\\). Therefore the point where the common chord \\(AB\\) meets the line \\(MN\\) coincides with the midpoint of the segment \\(CD\\).\n\nSince the midpoint of a chord lies on the line joining the centres, the chord \\(AB\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \n\nNow consider the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). Its centre is \\(P\\); because \\(PC=PD\\), \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), i.e. on the line through the midpoint of \\(CD\\) that is perpendicular to \\(MN\\). As we have just shown, the midpoint of \\(CD\\) also lies on the chord \\(AB\\). Consequently the line \\(AP\\) (which joins \\(A\\) to the centre of the circle through \\(A,C,D\\)) is exactly this perpendicular bisector, and we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,AP\\perp MN\\,}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Position of the orthocentre \\(H\\)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the side opposite the vertex \\(P\\) is \\(MN\\). By (3) the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\) is precisely the line \\(AP\\). Hence \\(AP\\) is the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\). The orthocentre \\(H\\) is the intersection of the three altitudes, so \\(H\\) lies on this altitude:\n\n\\[\nH\\in AP . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nTherefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line \\(AP\\) itself.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Equality of the angles \\(\\angle AEB\\) and \\(\\angle AFB\\)\n\nBecause \\(A,B,E\\) belong to the circle \\(\\Omega\\), the inscribed\u2011angle theorem yields\n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle ACB . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, \\(A,B,F\\) belong to the circle \\(\\Gamma\\), so\n\n\\[\n\\angle AFB = \\angle ADB . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFrom the construction of \\(P\\) we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\); thus \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(CD\\) of the circle centred at \\(P\\). Consequently the chord \\(CD\\) is symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\), which implies\n\n\\[\n\\angle ACB = \\angle ADB . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nCombining (5), (6) and (7) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle AEB = \\angle AFB\\;}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Tangency of \\(AP\\) to the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nEquation (8) can be rewritten as\n\n\\[\n\\angle (AE,EB)=\\angle (AF,FB).\n\\]\n\nSince the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) both lie on the line \\(AP\\), the rays \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) are the same line, namely \\(AP\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,EB)=\\angle (AP,FB). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe *tangent\u2013chord theorem* states that a line through a point of a circle is tangent to the circle iff the angles it makes with the two chords through that point are equal. Relation (9) is exactly this condition for the circle passing through \\(B,E,F\\). Therefore the line \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final conclusion\n\nFrom (4) the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line \\(AP\\) itself. By step\u202f4 this line is tangent to the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\nHence the statement required in the problem is proved. \u220e"
