, we have\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(CD\\). The perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the line through the midpoint of \\(CD\\) that is perpendicular to \\(MN\\). By the previous paragraph the midpoint of \\(CD\\) lies on \\(AB\\); together with (3) we obtain that the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) coincides with the line \\(AB\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nAP\\; \\text{is the line }AB\\quad\\text{and}\\quad AP\\perp MN . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the side opposite the vertex \\(P\\) is \\(MN\\). By (4) the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\) is precisely the line \\(AP\\). Therefore the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line \\(AP\\). The orthocentre \\(H\\) is the intersection of the three altitudes, so\n\n\\[\nH\\in AP . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line \\(AP\\) itself.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Equality \\(\\displaystyle\\angle ACB=\\angle ADB\\)\n\nFrom (3) we know that \\(AB\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(CD\\). Hence the reflection \\(\\sigma\\) across the line \\(AB\\) interchanges the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) while fixing every point of \\(AB\\), in particular fixing \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).\n\nUnder the reflection \\(\\sigma\\),\n\n\\[\n\\sigma(AC)=AD,\\qquad \\sigma(BC)=BD .\n\\]\n\nA reflection preserves oriented angles; therefore\n\n\\[\n\\angle ACB=\\angle \\sigma(ACB)=\\angle ADB . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Angles subtended by chord \\(AB\\) in the two given circles\n\nBecause \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(A,B,E\\) are distinct points of that circle, the inscribed\u2011angle theorem gives\n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB=\\angle ACB . \\