e{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(X)\\) are linear functions of the directed coordinate of \\(X\\) on that line; consequently the set of points for which the two powers are equal is a straight line **perpendicular** to \\(MN\\).\n\nNow any point \\(Y\\) belonging to the *radical axis* of the two circles satisfies\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(Y)=\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(Y).\n\\]\nConversely, any point with equal powers lies on the radical axis. Since the two circles intersect, the two intersection points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) clearly have equal power (both powers are zero). Hence the line \\(AB\\) is contained in the radical axis. Because the radical axis is a line perpendicular to \\(MN\\), we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AB\\perp MN}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Position of the circum\u2011centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the segment \\(CD\\) is collinear with \\(MN\\). From (1) we know that \\(AB\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\); therefore \\(AB\\) is also perpendicular to \\(CD\\).\n\nMoreover, the two circles intersect in \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); the chord \\(AB\\) is the *common chord* of the circles, and the line joining the centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) passes through the midpoint of that chord. Hence the midpoint of \\(CD\\) lies on the line \\(AB\\). Consequently the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is exactly the line \\(AB\\).\n\nSince the circum\u2011centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of the sides, we have\n\n\\[\nP\\in\\text{perp.\\,bisector of }CD = AB .\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AP\\text{ is the line }AB\\quad\\text{and}\\quad AP\\perp MN}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the side opposite the vertex \\(P\\) is \\(MN\\). By (2) the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\) is precisely the line \\(AP\\). Hence the altitude from \\(P\\) coincides with \\(AP\\). The orthocentre \\(H\\) is the common intersection of the three altitudes, so\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H\\in AP}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nTherefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line \\(AP\\) itself.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Equality \\(\\displaystyle\\angle ACB=\\angle ADB\\)\n\nFrom (1) we have that \\(AB\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(CD\\). The reflection \\(\\sigma\\) across the line \\(AB\\) therefore interchanges the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) while fixing every point of \\(AB\\), in particular fixing \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).\n\nA reflection preserves oriented angles, so applying \\(\\sigma\\) to the angle \\(\\angle ACB\\) yields\n\n\\[\n\\angle ACB=\\angle \\sigma(ACB)=\\angle ADB .\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle ACB=\\angle ADB}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Angles subtended by the chord \\(AB\\) in the two circles\n\nBecause \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(A,B,E\\) are distinct points of that circle, the inscribed\u2011angle theorem gives\n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB=\\angle ACB . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, \\(F\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\angle AFB=\\angle ADB . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nCombining (4), (5) and (6) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle AEB=\\angle AFB}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Tangency of \\(AP\\) to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nBoth points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the line \\(AP\\) (by construction). Equality (7) can be rewritten as\n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,EB)=\\angle (AP,FB). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe *tangent\u2011chord theorem* states that a line through a point of a circle is tangent to the circle iff it makes equal angles with the two chords drawn from that point. Relation (8) is exactly the condition that the line \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7  Final conclusion\n\nFrom (3) the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line \\(AP\\). By step\u202f2.6 this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). This is precisely the statement required in the problem. \u220e"
