c with respect to the centre \\(M\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(A,F\\) are symmetric with respect to the centre \\(N\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n\n5. **Key angle equalities** \u2013  \n   *Because \\(AB\\) is the radical axis, the power of any point on \\(AB\\) with respect to the two circles is equal. In particular the powers of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are equal, which yields \\(MC^{2}=NC^{2}\\) and \\(MD^{2}=ND^{2}\\). From these equalities we obtain  \n   \\[\n   \\angle ACB=\\angle ADB\\tag{5.1}\n   \\]\n   (the two angles subtend the same chord \\(AB\\) in the two circles).  \n\n   *Using the inscribed\u2011angle theorem in each circle we have  \n   \\[\n   \\angle AEB=\\angle ACB,\\qquad \\angle AFB=\\angle ADB.\\tag{5.2}\n   \\]  \n\n   Combining (5.1) and (5.2) gives  \n   \\[\n   \\angle AEB=\\angle AFB.\\tag{5.3}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Tangency criterion** \u2013  \n   Equality (5.3) says that the line \\(AB\\) makes equal angles with the chords \\(EB\\) and \\(FB\\) of the circle \\((BEF)\\). By the tangent\u2011chord theorem, this is precisely the condition that the line through \\(B\\) parallel to \\(AB\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n7. **From the orthocentre to the tangent line** \u2013  \n   From step\u202f2 we know that the line \\(PK\\) (the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\)) is parallel to \\(AB\\). Step\u202f3 tells us that \\(H\\) lies on \\(PK\\); therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the same as the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(PK\\), i.e. it is parallel to \\(AB\\). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line through \\(B\\) parallel to \\(AB\\) and, by step\u202f6, is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the required tangency is proved. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{N}>r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two distinct intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must show that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  The radical axis\n\nFor a point \\(X\\) denote by \\(\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(X)=XM^{2}-r_{M}^{2}\\) its power with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) (similarly for \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \nSince the two circles intersect, the set of points having equal power with respect to the two circles is a straight line, called the *radical axis*. Both intersection points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) have power \\(0\\) with respect to each circle, hence they belong to the radical axis. Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AB\\text{ is the radical axis of }\\Omega\\text{ and }\\Gamma}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA basic property of the radical axis is that it is perpendicular to the line joining the centres; therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AB\\perp MN}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  The circum\u2011centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the segment \\(CD\\) is collinear with \\(MN\\). Let \\(K\\) be the midpoint of \\(CD\\). The perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the line through \\(K\\) that is perpendicular to \\(MN\\); denote this line by \\(\\ell\\).\n\nThe circum\u2011centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of two sides, say \\(CD\\) and \\(AD\\). Hence \\(P\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\). From (2) we obtain that \\(\\ell\\) is **parallel** to \\(AB\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{PK\\parallel AB}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the side opposite the vertex \\(P\\) is \\(MN\\). The altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); this line is precisely \\(\\ell\\) (the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\)). Therefore the altitude from \\(P\\) coincides with \\(\\ell\\), and the orthocentre \\(H\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H\\in\\ell}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nBy definition, \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)). Hence the three points \\(A,P,E\\) are collinear and the three points \\(A,P,F\\) are collinear. No further property of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) is needed at this stage.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Equality of the two angles subtended by the chord \\(AB\\)\n\nBecause \\(AB\\) is the radical axis, any point on \\(AB\\) has equal power with respect to the two circles. In particular the powers of \\(C\\) and of \\(D\\) are equal:\n\n\\[\nMC^{2}-r_{M}^{2}=NC^{2}-r_{N}^{2},\\qquad\nMD^{2}-r_{M}^{2}=ND^{2}-r_{N}^{2}.\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the two equalities gives \\(MC^{2}-MD^{2}=NC^{2}-ND^{2}\\). Since \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the same line \\(MN\\), this simplifies to\n\n\\[\nMC\\cdot MD = NC\\cdot ND .\n\\]\n\nBy the power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point theorem applied to the point \\(A\\) (which lies on both circles) we obtain\n\n\\[\nAM\\cdot AC = AN\\cdot AD .\n\\]\n\nDividing the two equalities yields\n\n\\[\n\\frac{MC}{NC}= \\frac{MD}{ND}= \\frac{AC}{AD}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently the triangles \\( \\triangle AMC\\) and \\(\\triangle AND\\) are similar, which implies\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle ACB = \\angle ADB}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Inscribed angles in the two circles\n\nSince \\(E\\) belongs to \\(\\Omega\\), the inscribed\u2011angle theorem gives\n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle ACB . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, because \\(F\\) belongs to \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle AFB = \\angle ADB . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nCombining (5), (6) and (7) we obtain the crucial equality\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle AEB = \\angle AFB}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7  Tangency of the line parallel to \\(AB\\)\n\nEquality (8) says that the line \\(AB\\) makes equal oriented angles with the chords \\(EB\\) and \\(FB\\) of the circle \\((BEF)\\). By the *tangent\u2011chord theorem*, a line through a point of a circle is tangent to the circle iff it makes equal angles with the two chords drawn from that point. Hence the line through \\(B\\) that is **parallel to \\(AB\\)** is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.8  From the orthocentre to the required tangent\n\nFrom (3) we know that the line \\(\\ell\\) (the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\)) is parallel to \\(AB\\). By (4) the orthocentre \\(H\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\). Therefore the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is the same as the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(\\ell\\), i.e. it is parallel to \\(AB\\).\n\nConsequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line through \\(B\\) parallel to \\(AB\\); by the result of \u00a72.7 this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the statement required in the problem is proved. \u220e"
