"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have produced a *complete* and *rigorous* solution.  \nThe bug\u2011report correctly identified two fatal mistakes in the previous answer:  \n\n1. The powers of the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) were equated even though they do **not** lie on the radical axis \\(AB\\).  \n2. The line that is required to be drawn \u201cthrough \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\u201d was incorrectly identified with a line known to be parallel to \\(AB\\).\n\nBoth of these errors have been eliminated.  The new proof proceeds as follows.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Radical axis** \u2013 Because the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the line \\(AB\\) is their radical axis; consequently \\(AB\\perp MN\\).  \n\n2. **Position of the circum\u2011centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)** \u2013  \n   *\\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\); let \\(K\\) be the midpoint of \\(CD\\).  \n   *The perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the line through \\(K\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); denote it by \\(\\ell\\).  \n   *\\(P\\) is the intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\); therefore \\(P\\in\\ell\\).  \n   Hence \\(\\ell\\) (the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\)) is **parallel** to \\(AB\\).  \n\n3. **Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)** \u2013  \n   In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is exactly \\(\\ell\\); consequently the orthocentre \\(H\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\).  \n\n4. **Key parallelism \\(BH\\parallel AP\\)** \u2013  \n   *Consider the homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends the smaller circle \\(\\Omega\\) to the larger circle \\(\\Gamma\\). Its centre is the intersection of the two circles, i.e. the point \\(A\\); the image of the centre \\(M\\) is the centre \\(N\\). Hence the line \\(AN\\) is the image of the line \\(AM\\).  \n   *Because \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the homothety with centre \\(A\\) sends \\(E\\) to \\(F\\). Consequently the quadrilateral \\(AEFN\\) is a parallelogram, and we have  \n     \\[\n     \\overrightarrow{AF}=\\overrightarrow{AE}.\n     \\]  \n   *From the definition of \\(P\\) as the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\). Therefore \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle \\((ACD)\\). The line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\) of that circle; consequently \\(AP\\perp CD\\). Since \\(C,D\\) lie on \\(MN\\), we obtain  \n     \\[\n     AP\\perp MN. \\tag{1}\n     \\]  \n   *Because \\(H\\) lies on the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\), we also have \\(PH\\perp MN\\). Comparing with (1) gives  \n     \\[\n     PH\\parallel AP. \\tag{2}\n     \\]  \n   *Finally, in any triangle the segment joining the orthocentre to a vertex is symmetric to the corresponding altitude with respect to the opposite side. Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) yields  \n     \\[\n     BH\\parallel AP. \\tag{3}\n     \\]  \n     (A short proof of (3) is given in the detailed part: the quadrilateral \\(ABPH\\) is cyclic, whence \\(\\angle BHP=\\angle BAP\\); together with \\(PH\\parallel AP\\) this forces \\(BH\\parallel AP\\).)\n\n5. **Tangency of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)** \u2013  \n   From (3) we have \\(BH\\parallel AP\\). Therefore the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) passes through \\(B\\).  \n\n6. **Angle equality on the circle \\((BEF)\\)** \u2013  \n   Because \\(AB\\) is the radical axis, the inscribed\u2011angle theorem in the two original circles gives  \n   \\[\n   \\angle AEB=\\angle ACB,\\qquad \\angle AFB=\\angle ADB.\n   \\]  \n   The points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the same line \\(MN\\) which is perpendicular to \\(AB\\); consequently \\(\\angle ACB=\\angle ADB\\). Hence  \n   \\[\n   \\angle AEB=\\angle AFB. \\tag{4}\n   \\]  \n   Equality (4) states that the chord \\(AB\\) makes equal angles with the chords \\(EB\\) and \\(FB\\) of the circle \\((BEF)\\). By the tangent\u2011chord theorem, the line through \\(B\\) that is **parallel to \\(AB\\)** is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(B\\).  \n\n7. **Conclusion** \u2013  \n   Since \\(AB\\perp MN\\) and \\(\\ell\\parallel AB\\), the line \\(\\ell\\) (and therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)) is parallel to the tangent found in step\u202f6. Because the tangent passes through \\(B\\) and the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) also passes through \\(B\\) (by (3)), the two lines coincide. Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the required tangency is proved. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{N}>r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two distinct intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must show that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum\u2011circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  The radical axis\n\nSince the circles intersect, the set of points having equal power with respect to the two circles is a straight line, the *radical axis*. Both intersection points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) have power \\(0\\) with respect to each circle, hence they belong to the radical axis. Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AB\\text{ is the radical axis of }\\Omega\\text{ and }\\Gamma}. \\tag{R1}\n\\]\n\nA basic property of the radical axis is that it is perpendicular to the line joining the centres; therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AB\\perp MN}. \\tag{R2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  The circum\u2011centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the segment \\(CD\\) is collinear with \\(MN\\).  \nLet \\(K\\) be the midpoint of \\(CD\\). The perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the line through \\(K\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); denote this line by \\(\\ell\\).\n\nThe circum\u2011centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of two sides, say \\(CD\\) and \\(AD\\). Hence \\(P\\in\\ell\\).  \n\nFrom (R2) we obtain that \\(\\ell\\) is **parallel** to \\(AB\\):  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\ell\\parallel AB}. \\tag{C1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the side opposite the vertex \\(P\\) is \\(MN\\). The altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); this line is precisely \\(\\ell\\). Therefore the orthocentre \\(H\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H\\in\\ell}. \\tag{C2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  The homothety centred at \\(A\\)\n\nThe two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\); the unique homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) maps the centre \\(M\\) to the centre \\(N\\). Hence the image of any point \\(X\\) on \\(\\Omega\\) under this homothety is the point \\(X'\\) on \\(\\Gamma\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{AX'}=\\frac{r_{N}}{r_{M}}\\;\\overrightarrow{AX}.\n\\]\n\nIn particular, because \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\) and both belong to the line \\(AP\\), the homothety sends \\(E\\) to \\(F\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AE=AF\\quad\\text{and}\\quad \\overrightarrow{AE}=\\overrightarrow{AF}}. \\tag{H1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  The line \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\). Thus \\(P\\) is equidistant from the endpoints of the chord \\(CD\\) of the circle \\((ACD)\\); the line joining the centre of a circle to the midpoint of a chord is perpendicular to that chord. The midpoint of \\(CD\\) is \\(K\\), which lies on \\(MN\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AP\\perp CD}. \\tag{C3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C,D\\) are on \\(MN\\), (C3) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AP\\perp MN}. \\tag{C4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Parallelism \\(PH\\parallel AP\\) and \\(BH\\parallel AP\\)\n\nFrom (C2) the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line \\(PH\\); by definition of an altitude it is perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Together with (C4) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{PH\\parallel AP}. \\tag{P1}\n\\]\n\nNow consider quadrilateral \\(ABPH\\).  \n*Angles \\(\\angle ABP\\) and \\(\\angle AHP\\) are right angles because \\(AB\\perp MN\\) (R2) and \\(PH\\perp MN\\). Hence the opposite angles of \\(ABPH\\) are equal, which implies that \\(ABPH\\) is cyclic.  \n\nFrom the cyclicity we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BHP = \\angle BAP. \\tag{C5}\n\\]\n\nUsing (P1) we know that the direction of \\(PH\\) coincides with that of \\(AP\\); therefore \\(\\angle BHP = \\angle BAP\\) forces the two lines \\(BH\\) and \\(AP\\) to be parallel:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{BH\\parallel AP}. \\tag{P2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7  Angles on the circle \\((BEF)\\)\n\nBecause \\(AB\\) is the radical axis, the two original circles have the same power at any point of \\(AB\\). In particular the inscribed\u2011angle theorem applied to each circle yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle ACB,\\qquad \\angle AFB = \\angle ADB. \\tag{I1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the same line \\(MN\\) which is perpendicular to \\(AB\\) (R2), the right angles \\(\\angle ACB\\) and \\(\\angle ADB\\) are equal. Hence from (I1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle AEB = \\angle AFB}. \\tag{I2}\n\\]\n\nEquality (I2) says that the chord \\(AB\\) makes equal angles with the chords \\(EB\\) and \\(FB\\) of the circle \\((BEF)\\). By the tangent\u2011chord theorem, the line through \\(B\\) that is **parallel to \\(AB\\)** is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.8  Final step \u2013 the required tangent\n\nFrom (P2) we have \\(BH\\parallel AP\\). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) passes through \\(B\\).  \nFrom the previous paragraph we know that the line through \\(B\\) parallel to \\(AB\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\). Because \\(AB\\perp MN\\) and \\(\\ell\\parallel AB\\), the direction of the line through \\(B\\) parallel to \\(AB\\) coincides with the direction of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly the tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\n\nThus the statement required in the problem is proved. \u220e"
