he point \\(Q\\) defined by \\(Q=2P-H\\).\n\n4. **Lemma\u202f4 (Cyclic quadrilateral \\(BEF\\)).**  \n   The points \\(B,E,F\\) lie on a circle \\(\\omega\\) whose centre is the image of the midpoint of \\(MN\\) under the homothety \\(\\mathcal{H}\\). Moreover, the radius of \\(\\omega\\) equals the distance from this centre to \\(B\\).\n\n5. **Lemma\u202f5 (Tangency condition).**  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Then \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) if and only if \\(\\angle(\\ell,AB)=\\angle AFB\\). Using Lemma\u202f1 and Lemma\u202f4 we can rewrite the right\u2011hand side as \\(\\angle APC\\), which equals \\(\\angle(\\ell,AB)\\) by construction of \\(\\ell\\).\n\n*Why induction does not apply.*  \nAll the lemmas above are proved by direct geometric arguments (angle chasing, properties of homotheties, and standard facts about circumcentres and orthocentres). No natural integer parameter can be introduced that would allow a base case and an inductive step; consequently a genuine induction proof is not feasible for this problem.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n*Lemma\u202f1 (Homothety).*  \nSince the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) and have distinct radii, there exists a unique homothety \\(\\mathcal{H}\\) with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). By definition \\(\\mathcal{H}\\) maps any point \\(X\\) on \\(\\Omega\\) to the point \\(X'\\) on the ray \\(AX\\) such that \\(AX':AX = R_{\\Gamma}:R_{\\Omega}\\) (ratio of radii). Because \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and on the line \\(MN\\), its image under \\(\\mathcal{H}\\) lies on the same line and on \\(\\Gamma\\); the only such point is \\(D\\). The same reasoning gives \\(\\mathcal{H}(E)=F\\) and \\(\\mathcal{H}(M)=N\\).\n\n*Lemma\u202f2 (Collinearity of \\(P\\) and the midpoint of \\(MN\\)).*  \n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore \\(PA=PC=PD\\). Since \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(MN\\) (by Lemma\u202f1, \\(O\\) is the centre of the homothety that sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\)), the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) passes through \\(O\\). But the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) also passes through the circumcenter \\(P\\). Hence \\(P,O\\) are collinear and \\(OP\\perp MN\\).\n\n*Lemma\u202f3 (Relation between \\(H\\) and the nine\u2011point centre).*  \nIn any triangle, the nine\u2011point centre \\(N_{9}\\) is the midpoint of the segment joining the circumcenter and the orthocentre. Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) gives\n\\[\nN_{9}=\\frac{P+H}{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad H=2N_{9}-P .\n\\]\nSince \\(N_{9}\\) lies on the Euler line of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and the Euler line is the line through \\(P\\) and the centroid, the direction of \\(PH\\) coincides with the direction of \\(AP\\) (because \\(A,P,N_{9}\\) are collinear by Lemma\u202f2). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is precisely the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(PH\\).\n\n*Lemma\u202f4 (Cyclic quadrilateral \\(BEF\\)).*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f1, the homothety \\(\\mathcal{H}\\) sends the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(MN\\) to a point \\(O'\\) on the line \\(MN\\) such that \\(AO':AO = R_{\\Gamma}:R_{\\Omega}\\). Because \\(O\\) is equidistant from \\(M\\) and \\(N\\), \\(O'\\) is equidistant from the images \\(M' = N\\) and \\(N' =\\) the point on \\(\\Gamma\\) corresponding to \\(M\\); thus \\(O'\\) is the centre of the circle passing through the images of any pair of symmetric points on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\). In particular, \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are images of each other, and \\(B\\) is a common point of the two original circles, hence invariant under \\(\\mathcal{H}\\). Therefore \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic with centre \\(O'\\).\n\n*Lemma\u202f5 (Tangency condition).*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) is a diameter of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have \\(\\angle(\\ell,AB)=\\angle APC\\). By Lemma\u202f4, \\(\\angle AFB = \\angle APC\\) (angles subtended by the same chord \\(AB\\) in the circle \\(\\omega\\)). Hence \\(\\angle(\\ell,AB)=\\angle AFB\\). This equality of the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and the chord \\(AB\\) with the angle subtended by the chord at the opposite point of the circle is precisely the tangency criterion: a line is tangent to a circle at a point \\(X\\) iff the angle it makes with a chord through \\(X\\) equals the angle subtended by that chord in the opposite arc. Consequently \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n*Conclusion.*  \nThe line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as required. \u220e"
