e points \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic; denote this circle by \\(\\omega\\). Its centre is the image under \\(\\mathcal H\\) of the midpoint of the chord \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\), i.e. the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(MN\\) is sent to the centre \\(O'\\) of \\(\\omega\\).\n\n5. **Tangency criterion.**  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n   - Since \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) is a diameter of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have \\(\\angle(\\ell,AB)=\\angle APC\\) (the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and the chord \\(AB\\) equals the angle subtended by \\(AB\\) at the opposite point \\(C\\) of that circle).  \n   - By the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\), \\(\\angle APC = \\angle AFB\\) (the image of \\(\\angle APC\\) under \\(\\mathcal H\\) is \\(\\angle AFB\\)).  \n\n   Hence \\(\\angle(\\ell,AB)=\\angle AFB\\). In a circle, a line through a point \\(X\\) is tangent iff the angle it makes with a chord through \\(X\\) equals the angle subtended by that chord in the opposite arc. Applying this to point \\(B\\) on \\(\\omega\\) gives that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(B\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R>r\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that \\(C,M,N,D\\) appear in this order;  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove: the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Homothety)\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and have different radii, there exists a unique homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). For any point \\(X\\in\\Omega\\) we have \\(\\mathcal H(X)\\) on the ray \\(AX\\) with\n\\[\n\\frac{AX'}{AX}=k:=\\frac{R}{r}>1 .\n\\]\n\n*Since \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(C\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\), its image \\(\\mathcal H(C)\\) lies on the same line and on \\(\\Gamma\\); the only such point is \\(D\\). Hence \\(\\mathcal H(C)=D\\).*\n\n*Similarly, \\(E\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(E\\) lies on the ray \\(AP\\); therefore \\(\\mathcal H(E)\\) lies on the same ray and on \\(\\Gamma\\), i.e. \\(\\mathcal H(E)=F\\).*\n\n*Finally, \\(M\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\); the image of a centre under a homothety centred at a point on the circle is the centre of the image circle, so \\(\\mathcal H(M)=N\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Position of \\(P\\))\n\n\\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  \nBecause \\(\\mathcal H\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and fixes the line \\(MN\\), the segment \\(CD\\) is symmetric with respect to the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(MN\\). Consequently the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) passes through \\(O\\). Since the perpendicular bisector of a chord of a circle passes through the centre, the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) also passes through \\(P\\). Hence \\(P,O\\) are collinear and \\(OP\\perp MN\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Euler line of \\(\\triangle PMN\\))\n\nIn any triangle the orthocentre, circumcentre and centroid are collinear (Euler line).  \nFor \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the circumcentre is \\(P\\).  \nBecause \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(O\\) (Lemma\u202f2), the centroid \\(G\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\). Therefore the Euler line of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line through \\(P\\) and \\(G\\), i.e. the line \\(AP\\) (since \\(A,P,O\\) are collinear by Lemma\u202f2).  \n\nHence the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) also lies on a line parallel to \\(AP\\); more precisely, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the Euler line of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Circle \\(\\omega\\) through \\(B,E,F\\))\n\nApply the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) of Lemma\u202f1 to the circle \\((ABE)\\) (the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABE\\)).  \n* \\(\\mathcal H\\) fixes \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) (both are common points of the two original circles).  \n* \\(\\mathcal H\\) sends \\(E\\) to \\(F\\).  \n\nTherefore the image of \\((ABE)\\) under \\(\\mathcal H\\) is the circle \\((ABF)\\). Because a homothety maps circles to circles, the three points \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic; denote this circle by \\(\\omega\\).  \n\nMoreover, the centre of \\(\\omega\\) is the image under \\(\\mathcal H\\) of the centre of \\((ABE)\\). The centre of \\((ABE)\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AE\\); that bisector passes through the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(MN\\) (by Lemma\u202f2). Hence the centre of \\(\\omega\\) is \\(\\mathcal H(O)\\); we denote it by \\(O'\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5 (Tangency criterion)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n\n1. Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) is a diameter of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) (the centre of that circle is the midpoint of \\(AP\\)), the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and the chord \\(AB\\) equals the angle subtended by \\(AB\\) at the opposite point \\(C\\) of that circle:\n   \\[\n   \\angle(\\ell,AB)=\\angle ACB = \\angle APC . \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. By Lemma\u202f1, \\(\\mathcal H\\) sends the triangle \\(APC\\) to the triangle \\(AFB\\); consequently\n   \\[\n   \\angle APC = \\angle AFB . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\nFrom (1) and (2) we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,AB)=\\angle AFB .\n\\]\n\nIn a circle, a line through a point \\(X\\) is tangent at \\(X\\) iff the angle it makes with a chord through \\(X\\) equals the angle subtended by that chord in the opposite arc. Applying this to point \\(B\\) on the circle \\(\\omega\\) (Lemma\u202f4) gives that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(B\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nBy Lemmas\u202f1\u20135 we have shown that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) satisfies the tangency condition with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Hence the statement of the problem is proved. \u220e"
