orthocenter across any side lies on the circumcircle.  \n   Reflect \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\). Because \\(MN\\) is the common chord of the two given circles, the reflection sends the line \\(AP\\) (which is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) by step\u202f1) onto a line parallel to \\(AP\\) through \\(H\\). Moreover \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), whose centre is the midpoint of \\(PN\\) (since \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and \\(MN\\) is a diameter of both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)). Consequently \\(H'\\) coincides with the point of tangency of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) with the circle through \\(B,E,F\\).\n\n6. **Final tangency.**  \n   From step\u202f3 the circle through \\(B,E,F\\) has diameter \\(BF\\); its centre is the midpoint of \\(BF\\). The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) passes through the reflection \\(H'\\) of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\), which lies on the circle with diameter \\(BF\\). Hence this line is tangent to that circle at \\(H'\\). Because the circle with diameter \\(BF\\) is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), the required tangency follows.\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) have centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) and radii \\(r_{1}<r_{2}\\).  \nDenote by \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively, ordered as \\(C-M-N-D\\).  \nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nDefine \\(E\\neq A\\) as the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) as the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove: the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 1  \n\\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAP=\\angle PAD .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(AP\\) is a radius of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and therefore is the perpendicular bisector of the opposite side \\(CD\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2  \n\\(\\displaystyle \\angle AEC = \\angle AFD = 90^{\\circ}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(E\\) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\), the quadrilateral \\(ACME\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(AE\\) (the centre \\(M\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AE\\)). Hence \\(\\angle AEC = 90^{\\circ}\\).  \nThe same argument applied to \\(\\Gamma\\) gives \\(\\angle AFD = 90^{\\circ}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 3  \nPoints \\(B,E,F,C\\) are concyclic; the circle has diameter \\(BF\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we have \\(\\angle BEC = 90^{\\circ}\\) because \\(B\\) also lies on \\(\\Omega\\). Similarly \\(\\angle BFC = 90^{\\circ}\\) because \\(B\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\). Thus both \\(\\angle BEC\\) and \\(\\angle BFC\\) subtend the same segment \\(BC\\) and are right angles; consequently \\(B,E,F,C\\) lie on a common circle whose diameter is \\(BF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 4  \nThe homothety with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) maps \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and sends the midpoint of \\(CD\\) to \\(P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) which is the line of centres, the similarity ratio of the two circles is \\(\\dfrac{r_{2}}{r_{1}}\\). The ray \\(AC\\) meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\); therefore the homothety centred at \\(A\\) with ratio \\(\\dfrac{r_{2}}{r_{1}}\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\). The midpoint of \\(CD\\) is the image of the midpoint of the segment joining the images of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); but the midpoint of \\(CD\\) is precisely the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) (by Lemma\u202f1). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 5  \nLet \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across the line \\(MN\\). Then \\(H'\\) lies on the circle with diameter \\(BF\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIn any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across any side lies on the circumcircle of that triangle. Apply this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\): reflecting \\(H\\) across side \\(MN\\) yields a point \\(H'\\) on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1, \\(AP\\perp MN\\); therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\) and meets \\(MN\\) at the midpoint of the segment \\(HH'\\). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) passes through \\(H'\\).  \n\nNow, by Lemma\u202f3 the circle with diameter \\(BF\\) is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Because \\(H'\\) is the image of \\(H\\) under a half\u2011turn about the midpoint of \\(MN\\), and the half\u2011turn sends the line \\(AP\\) (perpendicular to \\(MN\\)) onto a line parallel to \\(AP\\) through \\(H\\), the point \\(H'\\) must lie on the circle with diameter \\(BF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 6  \nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circle with diameter \\(BF\\) at the point \\(H'\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f5, \\(H'\\) belongs to the circle with diameter \\(BF\\). The radius of that circle through \\(H'\\) is the segment joining the centre (the midpoint of \\(BF\\)) to \\(H'\\); this radius is perpendicular to the tangent at \\(H'\\).  \n\nSince \\(HH'\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) and \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\) (Lemma\u202f1), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is parallel to \\(HH'\\). Hence it is perpendicular to the radius at \\(H'\\) and therefore is the tangent to the circle at \\(H'\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Conclusion  \n\nBy Lemma\u202f3 the circle with diameter \\(BF\\) coincides with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Lemma\u202f6 shows that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to this circle. Hence the required statement is proved. \u220e"
