ng each of the above facts with explicit synthetic arguments.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) and radii \\(r_{1}<r_{2}\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) be their two intersection points;\n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);\n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));\n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 1 \u2013 \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment joining \\(P\\) to the midpoint of \\(CD\\) is a radius of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). Hence \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\); in particular  \n\n\\[\nAP\\perp CD\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad AC=AD .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAP=\\angle PAD .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 2 \u2013 Right angles at \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nBecause \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(E\\) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\), the quadrilateral \\(A C M E\\) is cyclic: \\(M\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(MA=MC=r_{1}\\); also \\(ME\\) is a radius, so \\(\\angle AEC\\) subtends a diameter of \\(\\Omega\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEC = 90^{\\circ}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe same reasoning applied to \\(\\Gamma\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AFD = 90^{\\circ}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 3 \u2013 Points \\(B,E,F,C\\) are concyclic with diameter \\(BF\\)\n\nSince \\(B\\) also belongs to both circles, from (3) we have \\(\\angle BEC = 90^{\\circ}\\) (because \\(B\\) and \\(C\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\)). From (4) we obtain \\(\\angle BFC = 90^{\\circ}\\) (because \\(B\\) and \\(F\\) lie on \\(\\Gamma\\)). Thus the four points \\(B,E,F,C\\) all subtend a right angle over the same segment \\(BF\\); therefore they are concyclic and the segment \\(BF\\) is a diameter of that circle. Consequently the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is exactly the circle with diameter \\(BF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 4 \u2013 Homothety centred at \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) has centre \\(P\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) joins the centres of the two circles; the similarity ratio of \\(\\Gamma\\) to \\(\\Omega\\) is \\(\\dfrac{r_{2}}{r_{1}}\\). The ray \\(AC\\) meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\); therefore the homothety with centre \\(A\\) and ratio \\(\\dfrac{r_{2}}{r_{1}}\\) maps \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\) and sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\).  \n\nBecause \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) (Lemma\u202f1), the midpoint of \\(CD\\) lies on \\(AP\\). The midpoint of a segment joining a point and its image under a homothety lies on the line joining the centre of the homothety to the midpoint of the original segment; hence that midpoint is precisely the centre of the homothety. Therefore the centre of the homothety is \\(P\\). In particular  \n\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 5 \u2013 The reflection \\(H'\\) of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) lies on the circle with diameter \\(BF\\)\n\n1. **Reflection property.**  \n   In any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across any side lies on the circumcircle of the triangle. Apply this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\): let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\); then \\(H'\\) belongs to the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{PMN}\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n2. **Location of \\(H'\\) relative to the line parallel to \\(AP\\).**  \n   From (5) we have \\(AP\\perp MN\\). The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is therefore also perpendicular to \\(MN\\). The unique line through \\(H\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\) meets \\(MN\\) at the midpoint of the segment \\(HH'\\); consequently that line passes through the reflected point \\(H'\\). Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) contains \\(H'\\).\n\n3. **\\(H'\\) belongs to the circle with diameter \\(BF\\).**  \n   Because \\(H'\\) lies on \\(\\omega_{PMN}\\), its power with respect to the circle \\(\\omega_{BF}\\) (the circle with diameter \\(BF\\) from Lemma\u202f3) can be computed using the right\u2011angle configuration. Observe that \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(BF\\) of \\(\\omega_{BF}\\): indeed, \\(PB=PC\\) and \\(PF=PC\\) (all equal to the radius of \\(\\Omega\\) or \\(\\Gamma\\) after the homothety), and \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(BF\\) because \\(AP\\) is that perpendicular bisector (Lemma\u202f1). Therefore the centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\omega_{BF}\\) is the midpoint of \\(BF\\) and lies on \\(AP\\).  \n\n   Since \\(O\\) lies on \\(AP\\) and \\(AP\\perp MN\\), the line \\(ON\\) is parallel to \\(MN\\). The quadrilateral \\(O H' N P\\) is a rectangle: \\(ON\\parallel HP\\) (both are parallel to \\(MN\\)) and \\(OP\\parallel H'N\\) (both are parallel to \\(AP\\)). Hence \\(OH'=ON\\). Because \\(O\\) is the centre of \\(\\omega_{BF}\\), the equality \\(OH'=ON\\) shows that \\(H'\\) is at distance equal to the radius of \\(\\omega_{BF}\\) from \\(O\\); consequently \\(H'\\) lies on \\(\\omega_{BF}\\).\n\nThus \\(H'\\) belongs to the circle with diameter \\(BF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 6 \u2013 Tangency\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f5, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) passes through the point \\(H'\\) of the circle \\(\\omega_{BF}\\). Because \\(H'\\) is the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\), the segment \\(HH'\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\). By (5) the line \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\); therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the same as the line \\(HH'\\).  \n\nAt a point of a circle, the radius drawn to that point is perpendicular to the tangent at the point. The radius of \\(\\omega_{BF}\\) through \\(H'\\) is \\(OH'\\), where \\(O\\) is the midpoint of \\(BF\\). Since \\(HH'\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) and \\(ON\\) is parallel to \\(MN\\), we have \\(HH'\\perp ON\\). But \\(ON\\) is collinear with \\(OH'\\) (both are along the line joining the centre \\(O\\) to the point \\(H'\\)). Hence \\(HH'\\) is perpendicular to the radius \\(OH'\\); consequently \\(HH'\\) is the tangent to \\(\\omega_{BF}\\) at \\(H'\\).\n\nBecause \\(\\omega_{BF}\\) is precisely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (Lemma\u202f3), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Conclusion**  \nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e"
