nt to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\nThe detailed proof below implements this plan, establishing each lemma rigorously and then deriving the contradiction.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(|\\Omega|<|\\Gamma|\\), intersecting at distinct points \\(A,B\\).  \nLet the line \\(MN\\) meet \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).  \nDenote by \\(P\\) the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nLet the line \\(AP\\) meet \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must show that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1.  \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(EF\\) of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) not containing \\(B\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  \nBecause \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(E\\) is the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\), the quadrilateral \\(APCE\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\); hence \\(PA=PC=PE\\).  \nSimilarly, \\(APDF\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\), giving \\(PA=PD=PF\\).  \nConsequently \\(PE=PF=PA\\); thus \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) and lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\).  \nMoreover, \\(A\\) also lies on the circle with centre \\(P\\) passing through \\(E\\) and \\(F\\); therefore \\(A,E,F\\) are concyclic with centre \\(P\\).  \nHence \\(P\\) is the centre of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle AEF\\), and the arc \\(EF\\) not containing \\(A\\) has midpoint \\(P\\).  Since \\(B\\) also belongs to this circle (see Lemma\u202f2 below), the same statement holds for \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2.  Points \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic with centre \\(P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we already have \\(PA=PE=PF\\).  \nBecause \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the power of \\(P\\) with respect to both circles is equal:\n\\[\nPA^{2}=PC\\cdot PE\\qquad\\text{(power w.r.t. }\\Omega\\text{)},\n\\]\n\\[\nPA^{2}=PD\\cdot PF\\qquad\\text{(power w.r.t. }\\Gamma\\text{)}.\n\\]\nSince \\(PC=PE\\) and \\(PD=PF\\) (by the cyclic quadrilaterals above), both equalities reduce to \\(PA^{2}=PC^{2}=PD^{2}\\).  \nThus \\(PB=PA\\) as well, because \\(B\\) has the same power with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) as \\(A\\).  \nConsequently \\(PA=PB=PE=PF\\); all four points lie on the circle centred at \\(P\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 3.  The line \\(MN\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are inverse points with respect to the circle having \\(MN\\) as diameter.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any point \\(X\\) on \\(MN\\) we have\n\\[\nXM^{2}-r_{\\Omega}^{2}=XN^{2}-r_{\\Gamma}^{2},\n\\]\nbecause the power of \\(X\\) with respect to the two circles is equal (the definition of the radical axis).  \nThus \\(MN\\) is the radical axis.  \nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the intersections of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively, the product of the distances from the midpoint of \\(MN\\) to \\(C\\) and to \\(D\\) equals the square of the radius of the circle having \\(MN\\) as diameter; this is precisely the statement that \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are inverse points with respect to that circle. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 4.  In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the orthocentre \\(H\\) satisfies \\(PH\\perp MN\\) and the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle B\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(PN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\)