lected line is tangent to the reflected circle at the point \\(B\\). Undoing the reflection yields that the original line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the original circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).\n\nThe pigeon\u2011hole principle appears implicitly in step\u202f5: the reflection maps the two points \\(E,F\\) into the same \u201chole\u2019\u2019 (the reflected circle), forcing the line parallel to \\(AP\\) to be the unique common tangent at the shared point \\(B\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the two circles be  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega :\\; (M,R),\\qquad \\Gamma :\\; (N,r),\\qquad R<r .\n\\]\n\nThey intersect at the distinct points \\(A,B\\).  \nDenote by \\(MN\\) the line joining the centres; it meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1.  \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the projections of \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) onto the common chord \\(AB\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and on the line \\(MN\\), the power of \\(C\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) is zero:\n\\[\nCA\\cdot CB = 0 .\n\\]\nHence \\(C\\) belongs to the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), which is precisely the line \\(AB\\).  \nThus \\(C\\in AB\\).  Because \\(C\\) also lies on the diameter through \\(M\\), the segment \\(MC\\) is perpendicular to \\(AB\\).  The same argument gives \\(ND\\perp AB\\). \u220e\n\n\n\nConsequences of Lemma\u202f1:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AC=BC},\\qquad \\boxed{AD=BD},\\qquad \\boxed{MC\\perp AB},\\qquad \\boxed{ND\\perp AB}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  The circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nPA=PE=PF,\\qquad AP\\text{ bisects }\\angle EPF .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\) (Lemma\u202f1),