-----------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  Let \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). Then  \n\n\\[\nPA=PE=PF,\\qquad AP\\text{ bisects }\\angle EPF .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause of (1) we have \\(AC=AD\\); thus \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is isosceles with vertex \\(A\\).  \nConsequently its circumcenter \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\) and also on that of \\(AC\\); hence \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  \n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\).  Since \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(PA=PC\\), the chord \\(AE\\) is symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\); therefore \\(PA=PE\\).  An identical argument with \\(\\Gamma\\) yields \\(PA=PF\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nPA=PE=PF .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause the three points \\(E,P,F\\) are equidistant from \\(A\\), the segment \\(AP\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle EPF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3.  Points \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom (1) we have \\(BC=BD\\).  Using (2),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}\n   =\\frac{BE}{BC}\\cdot\\frac{BC}{BF}\n   =\\frac{BE}{BC}\\cdot\\frac{BD}{BF}\n   =\\frac{BE}{BC}\\cdot\\frac{BC}{BF}\n   =1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(BE=BF\\); equivalently \\(\\angle BEC = \\angle CBF\\).  Since \\(C\\) lies on \\(AB\\), \\(\\angle BEC = \\angle BEA\\) and \\(\\angle CBF = \\angle DBF\\).  Therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BEA = \\angle DBF .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\angle DBF = \\angle BDF\\) because \\(D\\) lies on \\(AB\\).  Hence (3) gives \\(\\angle BEA = \\angle BDF\\).  The equality of the angles subtended by the same chord \\(BE\\) at the points \\(A\\) and \\(D\\) implies that \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic.  Removing the irrelevant point \\(A\\) we obtain that \\(B,E,F\\) lie on a common circle, denote it by \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4.  Let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Then \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) across the line \\(MN\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(MC\\perp AB\\) and \\(ND\\perp AB\\) (Lemma\u202f1), the line \\(AB\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); this line is precisely \\(PA\\) (since \\(PA\\perp MN\\) by Lemma\u202f2 and the fact that \\(AP\\) is the internal bisector of \\(\\angle EPF\\), which is symmetric with respect to \\(AB\\)).  \nConsequently the foot of this altitude is the midpoint of the segment joining the projections of \\(P\\) onto the two circles, i.e. the midpoint of \\(MN\\).  \nReflecting \\(P\\) across \\(MN\\) therefore sends the altitude from \\(P\\) onto the altitude from the reflected point; the intersection of the three reflected altitudes is exactly the orthocenter \\(H\\).  Hence \\(H\\) is the mirror image of \\(P\\) in \\(MN\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nDenote by \\(\\sigma\\) the reflection in the line \\(MN\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f4, \\(\\sigma(P)=H\\).  Because \\(\\sigma\\) fixes every point of \\(MN\\) and \\(B\\in MN\\) (Lemma\u202f1), we have \\(\\sigma(B)=B\\).  \nApply \\(\\sigma\\) to the circle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\); its image is the circle \\(\\omega_{BE'F'}\\) where  \n\n\\[\nE'=\\sigma(E),\\qquad F'=\\sigma(F).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sigma\\) is an isometry, the line \\(\\sigma(AP)\\) coincides with \\(AP\\) (the image of a line parallel to \\(AP\\) through \\(H\\) is the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), i.e. \\(AP\\) itself).  \n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2, \\(AP\\) is the internal bisector of \\(\\angle EPF\\).  \nReflection turns an internal bisector into an external bisector; therefore \\(AP\\) is the external bisector of \\(\\angle E'P'F'\\) where \\(P'=\\sigma