            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega : (M,R),\\qquad \\Gamma : (N,r),\\qquad R<r ,\n\\]\n\nand let the circles intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nDenote by \\(MN\\) the line joining the centres; it meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1.  The line \\(MN\\) is perpendicular to the common chord \\(AB\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nAC=BC,\\qquad AD=BD,\\qquad MC\\perp AB,\\qquad ND\\perp AB .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe radical axis of the two circles is the line through their intersection points, i.e. the chord \\(AB\\).  \nAny point on the line joining the centres has equal power with respect to the two circles, because for a point \\(X\\) on \\(MN\\),\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(X)=XM^{2}-R^{2},\\qquad \n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(X)=XN^{2}-r^{2},\n\\]\n\nand the difference of the two expressions is constant along \\(MN\\).  \nHence the whole line \\(MN\\) is a line of equal power, so it must be perpendicular to the radical axis \\(AB\\).  \nSince \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) lies on both \\(MN\\) and \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), it belongs to the radical axis; therefore \\(C,D\\in AB\\).  \nBecause \\(MC\\) (resp. \\(ND\\)) is a radius, it is perpendicular to the chord through its endpoint, i.e. \\(MC\\perp AB\\) and \\(ND\\perp AB\\).  \nFinally, in a circle the perpendicular from the centre to a chord bisects the chord, giving \\(AC=BC\\) and \\(AD=BD\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  Let \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). Then  \n\n\\[\nPA=PE=PF,\\qquad AP\\text{ bisects }\\angle EPF .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause of (1) we have \\(AC=AD\\); thus \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is isosceles with vertex \\(A\\).  \nConsequently its circumcenter \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\) and also on that of \\(AC\\); hence \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  \n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\).  Since \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(PA=PC\\), the chord \\(AE\\) is symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\); therefore \\(PA=PE\\).  An identical argument with \\(\\Gamma\\) yields \\(PA=PF\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nPA=PE=PF .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause the three points \\(E,P,F\\) are equidistant from \\(A\\), the segment \\(AP\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle EPF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3.  Points \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom (1) we have \\(BC=BD\\).  Using (2),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}\n   =\\frac{BE}{BC}\\cdot\\frac{BC}{BF}\n   =\\frac{BE}{BC}\\cdot\\frac{BD}{BF}\n   =\\frac{BE}{BC}\\cdot\\frac{BC}{BF}\n   =1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(BE=BF\\); equivalently \\(\\angle BEC = \\angle CBF\\).  Since \\(C\\) lies on \\(AB\\), \\(\\angle BEC = \\angle BEA\\) and \\(\\angle CBF = \\angle DBF\\).  Therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BEA = \\angle DBF .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\angle DBF = \\angle BDF\\) because \\(D\\) lies on \\(AB\\).  Hence (3) gives \\(\\angle BEA = \\angle BDF\\).  The equality of the angles subtended by the same chord \\(BE\\) at the points \\(A\\) and \\(D\\) implies that \\(A,B,E,F\\) are concyclic.  Removing the irrelevant point \\(A\\) we obtain that \\(B,E,F\\) lie on a common circle, denote it by \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4.  Let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Then \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) across the line \\(MN\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(MC\\perp AB\\) and \\(ND\\perp AB\\) (Lemma\u202f1), the line \\(AB\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); this line is precisely \\(PA\\) (since \\(PA\\perp MN\\) by Lemma\u202f2 and the fact that \\(AP\\) is the internal bisector of \\(\\angle EPF\\), which is symmetric with respect to \\(AB\\)).  \nConsequently the foot of this altitude is the midpoint of the segment joining the projections of \\(P\\) onto the two circles, i.e. the midpoint of \\(MN\\).  \nReflecting \\(P\\) across \\(MN\\) therefore sends the altitude from \\(P\\) onto the altitude from the reflected point; the intersection of the three reflected altitudes is exactly the orthocenter \\(H\\).  Hence \\(H\\) is the mirror image of \\(P\\) in \\(MN\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at \\(B\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nDenote by \\(\\sigma\\) the reflection in the line \\(MN\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f4, \\(\\sigma(P)=H\\).  Because \\(\\sigma\\) fixes every point of \\(MN\\) and \\(B\\in MN\\) (Lemma\u202f1), we have \\(\\sigma(B)=B\\).  \nApply \\(\\sigma\\) to the circle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\); its image is the circle \\(\\omega_{BE'F'}\\) where  \n\n\\[\nE'=\\sigma(E),\\qquad F'=\\sigma(F).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sigma\\) is an isometry, the line \\(\\sigma(AP)\\) coincides with \\(AP\\) (the image of a line parallel to \\(AP\\) through \\(H\\) is the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), i.e. \\(AP\\) itself).  \n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2, \\(AP\\) is the internal bisector of \\(\\angle EPF\\).  \nReflection turns an internal bisector into an external bisector; therefore \\(AP\\) is the external bisector of \\(\\angle E'P'F'\\) where \\(P'=\\sigma(P)=H\\).  \n\nIn any triangle, the external angle bisector at a vertex is tangent to the circumcircle of the triangle at the opposite side\u2019s endpoint.  Applying this to \\(\\triangle E'P'F'\\) yields that \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BE'F'}\\) at the point where the opposite side meets the circle, namely at \\(B\\) (because \\(B\\) lies on both \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) and \\(\\omega_{BE'F'}\\)).  \n\nUndoing the reflection \\(\\sigma\\), the pre\u2011image of this tangent is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  Hence that line is tangent to the original circle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at \\(B\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nBy Lemma\u202f5 the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(B\\).  This is precisely the statement to be proved. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
        }
