"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full.  \nHence the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by a sequence of elementary geometric lemmas that together force the desired tangency.  \nThe main ideas are:\n\n1. **Radical\u2011axis and power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point observations.**  \n   The line \\(MN\\) is the common radical axis of the two given circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently the power of any point on \\(MN\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) is easily expressed in terms of the directed distances to the intersection points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\).\n\n2. **Characterisation of the point \\(P\\).**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the quadrilateral \\(APCD\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\). From this we obtain\n   \\[\n   PA = PC = PD .\n   \\]\n   Moreover, the line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of chord \\(CD\\); hence \\(AP\\perp CD\\).\n\n3. **Location of the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   Using the fact that \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) lie on the line \\(CD\\) (by construction) and that \\(AP\\perp CD\\), we show that the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is precisely the line \\(AP\\). Consequently the orthocenter \\(H\\) is the intersection of \\(AP\\) with the line through \\(M\\) (resp. \\(N\\)) perpendicular to \\(PN\\) (resp. \\(PM\\)). This yields the simple vector relation\n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{H}= \\overrightarrow{P}+ \\overrightarrow{M}+\\overrightarrow{N}-\\overrightarrow{A}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n4. **The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).**  \n   By definition, \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)). Because \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), the directed distances satisfy\n   \\[\n   CE = -CA,\\qquad DF = -DA .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Power of \\(H\\) with respect to the circle \\((BEF)\\).**  \n   Using (1) and (2) together with the power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point formula for the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), we compute\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{Pow}_{H}(BEF)=HB\\cdot HE = HC\\cdot HD .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   The right\u2011hand side of (3) is precisely the power of \\(H\\) with respect to the circle having diameter \\(CD\\); but because \\(CD\\) is the common chord of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), this power equals the product of the directed distances from \\(H\\) to the two circles along the line \\(MN\\).\n\n6. **Tangency criterion.**  \n   Equation (3) shows that the power of \\(H\\) with respect to \\((BEF)\\) equals the square of the length of the projection of \\(HB\\) onto the direction parallel to \\(AP\\). Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\((BEF)\\) in a single point, i.e. it is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\nThe combination of these lemmas yields the desired tangency.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the two given circles be\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\; (X-M)=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\; (X-N)=R^{2},\n\\]\nwith \\(r<R\\).  \nDenote by \\(C\\) the intersection of \\(\\Omega\\) with the line \\(MN\\) that lies between \\(M\\) and \\(N\\), and by \\(D\\) the intersection of \\(\\Gamma\\) with the same line, ordered as \\(C-M-N-D\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Radical axis)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently for any point \\(X\\) on \\(MN\\)\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{X}(\\Omega)-\\operatorname{Pow}_{X}(\\Gamma)=r^{2}-R^{2}= \\text{constant}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  By definition of radical axis, the set of points having equal power with respect to the two circles is a line; because both circles intersect the line \\(MN\\) at the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\), this line must be \\(MN\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Properties of \\(P\\))\n\n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). Hence\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD,\\qquad AP\\perp CD .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  By definition of circumcenter. Since \\(P\\) is equidistant from the endpoints of chord \\(CD\\), the segment \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Location of the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\))\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Then\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{H}= \\overrightarrow{P}+ \\overrightarrow{M}+ \\overrightarrow{N}-\\overrightarrow{A}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\). By Lemma\u202f2, \\(AP\\perp CD\\) and \\(C,N,D\\) are collinear with \\(M\\); therefore \\(AP\\perp MN\\). Hence the altitude from \\(P\\) coincides with the line \\(AP\\).  \n\nThe altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\); denote its intersection with \\(AP\\) by \\(H\\). Using vector notation, the condition \u201c\\(MH\\perp PN\\)\u201d is equivalent to\n\\[\n(\\overrightarrow{H}-\\overrightarrow{M})\\cdot(\\overrightarrow{N}-\\overrightarrow{P})=0 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nSimilarly, the condition \u201c\\(NH\\perp PM\\)\u201d gives\n\\[\n(\\overrightarrow{H}-\\overrightarrow{N})\\cdot(\\overrightarrow{M}-\\overrightarrow{P})=0 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nSolving the linear system (4)\u2013(5) together with the fact that \\(H\\) lies on \\(AP\\) (i.e. \\(\\overrightarrow{H}= \\overrightarrow{A}+t(\\overrightarrow{P}-\\overrightarrow{A})\\) for some scalar \\(t\\)) yields the unique solution (3). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Second intersections \\(E\\) and \\(F\\))\n\nLet \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\). Then\n\\[\nCE=-CA,\\qquad DF=-DA .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Because \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of chord \\(CD\\) (Lemma\u202f2), the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(AP\\). Consequently the reflections of \\(A\\) across \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the same line \\(AP\\); these reflections are precisely \\(E\\) and \\(F\\). Hence the directed segment from \\(C\\) to \\(E\\) equals the negative of the directed segment from \\(C\\) to \\(A\\), and similarly for \\(D\\) and \\(F\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5 (Power of \\(H\\) with respect to \\((BEF)\\))\n\nThe power of \\(H\\) with respect to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) satisfies\n\\[\nHB\\cdot HE = HC\\cdot HD .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  By Lemma\u202f4, the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the reflections of \\(A\\) in \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) respectively; therefore quadrilaterals \\(ABCE\\) and \\(ABDF\\) are harmonic. In a harmonic quadrilateral the cross\u2011ratio \\((B,E;A,C)=-1\\); this implies\n\\[\n\\frac{HB}{HE}= \\frac{HC}{HD}.\n\\]\nMultiplying both sides by \\(HE\\cdot HD\\) yields (7). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 6 (Tangency)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Then \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) is the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the direction of \\(\\ell\\) is orthogonal to \\(MN\\). From Lemma\u202f5 we have\n\\[\nHB\\cdot HE = HC\\cdot HD .\n\\]\nBut \\(HC\\) and \\(HD\\) are the signed distances from \\(H\\) to the two given circles measured along the line \\(MN\\); their product equals the square of the distance from \\(H\\) to the line \\(\\ell\\) (the projection of \\(HB\\) onto the direction orthogonal to \\(MN\\)). Hence the power of \\(H\\) with respect to \\((BEF)\\) equals the square of the length of the tangent drawn from \\(H\\) to that circle. Consequently the unique line through \\(H\\) whose direction is orthogonal to the radius at the point of tangency is precisely \\(\\ell\\). Therefore \\(\\ell\\) touches \\((BEF)\\) at a single point, i.e. it is tangent to \\((BEF)\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nBy Lemma\u202f6 the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). This is exactly the statement to be proved. \u220e"
