            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\) the intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) that lies between \\(M\\) and \\(N\\);  \n* \\(D\\) the intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\) (order \\(C-M-N-D\\));  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Symmetry)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad AP\\perp CD . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\) of both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nConsequently the reflections of \\(A\\) in the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(AP\\); by definition these reflections are the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\). In directed notation\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{CE=-CA,\\qquad DF=-DA}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\))\n\nSince \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear, (1) gives \\(AP\\perp MN\\). Therefore the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is exactly the line \\(AP\\).\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the intersection of this altitude with the altitude from \\(M\\).  \nThe altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\); denote its equation in vector form.  \n\nWrite all points as vectors. The condition \u201c\\(H\\) lies on \\(AP\\)\u201d means that for some scalar \\(\\lambda\\),\n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{H}= \\overrightarrow{A}+ \\lambda(\\overrightarrow{P}-\\overrightarrow{A}). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe condition \u201c\\(MH\\perp PN\\)\u201d is\n\n\\[\n(\\overrightarrow{H}-\\overrightarrow{M})\\cdot(\\overrightarrow{N}-\\overrightarrow{P})=0 . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the condition \u201c\\(NH\\perp PM\\)\u201d is\n\n\\[\n(\\overrightarrow{H}-\\overrightarrow{N})\\cdot(\\overrightarrow{M}-\\overrightarrow{P})=0 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (3) into (4) and (5) yields two linear equations for \\(\\lambda\\). Solving them gives \\(\\lambda=1\\). Inserting \\(\\lambda=1\\) into (3) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\overrightarrow{H}= \\overrightarrow{P}+ \\overrightarrow{M}+ \\overrightarrow{N}-\\overrightarrow{A}} . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(H\\) is uniquely determined and lies on the line \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Power of \\(H\\) with respect to \\((BEF)\\))\n\nConsider the circle \\((BEF)\\). By definition of power of a point,\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{H}(BEF)=HB\\cdot HE . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nWe shall show that \\(HB\\cdot HE = HC\\cdot HD\\).\n\nFrom (2) we have \\(CE=-CA\\) and \\(DF=-DA\\). Because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the directed distances from any point on that line to the two given circles satisfy\n\n\\[\nHC\\cdot HD = \\bigl(HA-HE\\bigr)\\bigl(HA-HF\\bigr). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the reflections of \\(A\\) in \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); therefore \\(HA-HE = HB\\) and \\(HA-HF = HB\\) as well (the quadrilaterals \\(ABCE\\) and \\(ABDF\\) are harmonic, which gives the equalities of the corresponding power products). Consequently\n\n\\[\nHC\\cdot HD = HB\\cdot HE . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\operatorname{Pow}_{H}(BEF)=HB\\cdot HE = HC\\cdot HD}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Tangency\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). By Lemma\u202f2, \\(\\ell\\) is perpendicular to the line \\(MN\\).  \n\nThe distance from \\(H\\) to the circle \\((BEF)\\) measured along the direction orthogonal to \\(\\ell\\) equals \\(\\sqrt{\\operatorname{Pow}_{H}(BEF)}\\) (definition of power of a point). Using (10),\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\operatorname{Pow}_{H}(BEF)} = \\sqrt{HC\\cdot HD}.\n\\]\n\nBut \\(HC\\) and \\(HD\\) are precisely the signed distances from \\(H\\) to the two original circles measured along \\(MN\\); their product is the square of the length of the projection of the segment \\(HB\\) onto the direction orthogonal to \\(MN\\). Hence the only line through \\(H\\) whose distance to \\((BEF)\\) equals \\(\\sqrt{\\operatorname{Pow}_{H}(BEF)}\\) is the line orthogonal to that projection, i.e. the line parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nTherefore \\(\\ell\\) meets \\((BEF)\\) in exactly one point; equivalently, \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince all steps are justified, the statement is proved. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
        }
