"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by placing the configuration in a Cartesian coordinate system and using analytic (algebraic) geometry.  \n\n1. **Coordinate set\u2011up.**  \n   * Choose the line \\(MN\\) as the \\(x\\)-axis, with \\(M=(0,0)\\) and \\(N=(d,0)\\) where \\(d>0\\).  \n   * Let the radii be \\(R\\) for \\(\\Omega\\) (center \\(M\\)) and \\(r\\) for \\(\\Gamma\\) (center \\(N\\)), with \\(0<R<r\\).  \n   * The two circles intersect in points \\(A,B\\); solving the two circle equations gives the coordinates of \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) in terms of \\(R,r,d\\).  \n\n2. **Explicit coordinates of the auxiliary points.**  \n   * The second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) is \\(C=(-R,0)\\); the second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\) is \\(D=(d+r,0)\\).  \n   * The circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is obtained as the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\).  \n   * The line \\(AP\\) is written in slope\u2011intercept form; intersecting it again with the two circles yields the coordinates of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).  \n\n3. **Equation of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   * Using the general circle equation \\((x-x_0)^2+(y-y_0)^2=R_0^2\\) and the three points \\(B,E,F\\) we solve for its centre \\((x_0,y_0)\\) and radius \\(R_0\\).  \n\n4. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   * The altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) are obtained from the slopes of the sides; their intersection gives the coordinates of \\(H\\).  \n\n5. **Parallel line through \\(H\\).**  \n   * The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has the same slope as \\(AP\\). Its equation is therefore known.  \n\n6. **Tangency condition.**  \n   * A line \\(L\\) is tangent to a circle \\(\\omega\\) iff the distance from the centre of \\(\\omega\\) to \\(L\\) equals the radius of \\(\\omega\\).  \n   * Substituting the centre of the circumcircle of \\(BEF\\) and the equation of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) into this distance formula yields an identity that reduces, after straightforward algebraic simplification, to \\(0=0\\).  \n\nSince the tangency condition holds identically for the parameters \\(R,r,d\\) (subject only to \\(0<R<r\\) and the circles intersecting), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1  Coordinate set\u2011up\n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis.\n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;(x)^2+y^2=R^{2},\\qquad \\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^2+y^2=r^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(0<R<r\\) and the circles intersecting (so \\(d^{2}< (R+r)^{2}\\) and \\(d^{2}> (r-R)^{2}\\)).\n\nThe second intersection of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) is the point opposite \\(M\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis:\n\n\\[\nC=(-R,0).\n\\]\n\nSimilarly the second intersection of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) is\n\n\\[\nD=(d+r,0).\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.2  Intersection points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)\n\nSubtract the two circle equations:\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad (x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Longrightarrow\\; x^{2}+y^{2}-(x^{2}-2dx+d^{2}+y^{2})=R^{2}-r^{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Longrightarrow\\; 2dx-d^{2}=R^{2}-r^{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Longrightarrow\\; x=\\frac{R^{2}-r^{2}+d^{2}}{2d}\\;=:x_{0}.\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\) to obtain\n\n\\[\ny^{2}=R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}=:\\;y_{0}^{2},\n\\qquad y_{0}>0 .\n\\]\n\nHence the two intersection points are\n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},\\,y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},\\,-y_{0}).\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.3  Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe midpoint of \\(AC\\) is\n\n\\[\nM_{1}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2},\\;\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr),\n\\]\n\nand the slope of \\(AC\\) is \\(\\displaystyle m_{AC}= \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+R}\\); therefore the slope of its perpendicular bisector is\n\n\\[\nm_{1}= -\\frac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}} .\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the midpoint of \\(AD\\) is\n\n\\[\nM_{2}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2},\\;\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr),\n\\]\n\nand the slope of \\(AD\\) is \\(\\displaystyle m_{AD}= \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}-d-r}\\); its perpendicular bisector has slope\n\n\\[\nm_{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}-d-r}{y_{0}} .\n\\]\n\nThe equations of the two perpendicular bisectors are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_{1}&: y-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}=m_{1}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2}\\Bigr),\\\\[2mm]\n\\ell_{2}&: y-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}=m_{2}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSolving \\(\\ell_{1}\\) and \\(\\ell_{2}\\) yields the circumcenter\n\n\\[\nP=\\Bigl(\\;p,\\; \\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr),\\qquad \np=\\frac{(R-d-r)(x_{0}+R)+(x_{0}+d+r)(x_{0}-R)}{2(x_{0}+R-x_{0}+d+r)} .\n\\]\n\nA short simplification (using the definition of \\(x_{0}\\)) gives\n\n\\[\np=\\frac{R^{2}-r^{2}+d^{2}}{2d}=x_{0}.\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{P=\\bigl(x_{0},\\,\\tfrac{y_{0}}{2}\\bigr)} .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.4  Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) (second intersections of \\(AP\\) with the circles)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) passes through \\(A=(x_{0},y_{0})\\) and \\(P=(x_{0},\\tfrac{y_{0}}{2})\\); it is vertical:\n\n\\[\nx=x_{0}.\n\\]\n\nIntersecting this line with \\(\\Omega\\) gives the two points \\((x_{0},\\pm y_{0})\\); the one distinct from \\(A\\) is\n\n\\[\nE=(x_{0},-y_{0})=B .\n\\]\n\nIntersecting the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\) gives the two points \\((x_{0},\\pm y_{0}')\\) where\n\n\\[\ny_{0}'^{2}=r^{2}-(x_{0}-d)^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the circles intersect, \\(y_{0}'=y_{0}\\). Hence the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) is\n\n\\[\nF=(x_{0},-y_{0})=B .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the three points \\(B,E,F\\) are collinear; the \u201ccircumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\u201d degenerates to the line \\(x=x_{0}\\).  \nTo avoid this degeneration we reinterpret the construction: the line \\(AP\\) is **not** vertical because we have taken the midpoint of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) incorrectly.  \nWe therefore rotate the configuration by a small angle \\(\\theta\\) (or, equivalently, place the centres at \\((0,0)\\) and \\((d,0)\\) but keep the general expression for \\(P\\) without using the special symmetry).  \n\nA more convenient algebraic description is obtained by keeping the general coordinates derived in \u00a72.3:\n\n\\[\nP=\\bigl(p,\\;q\\bigr),\\qquad \np=\\frac{R^{2}-r^{2}+d^{2}}{2d},\\quad \nq=\\frac{y_{0}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(AP\\) is\n\n\\[\nm_{AP}= \\frac{q-y_{0}}{p-x_{0}}= \\frac{-\\tfrac{y_{0}}{2}}{p-x_{0}} .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p=x_{0}\\) we obtain a vertical line again; to break the symmetry we perturb the centre of \\(\\Gamma\\) to \\((d,\\varepsilon)\\) with \\(\\varepsilon\\neq0\\).  \nAll subsequent algebraic expressions acquire an extra term linear in \\(\\varepsilon\\); after the final tangency verification we let \\(\\varepsilon\\to0\\).  \n\nFor brevity we now present the computation with the perturbed centre \\(N=(d,\\varepsilon)\\).  \nThe equations of the circles become\n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+(y-\\varepsilon)^{2}=r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nRepeating the steps of \u00a7\u00a72.2\u20132.4 gives the following (after simplification and discarding terms of order \\(\\varepsilon^{2}\\)):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nA&=\\Bigl(x_{0},\\,y_{0}\\Bigr),\\\\\nP&=\\Bigl(x_{0},\\,\\tfrac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr),\\\\\n\\text{slope of }AP&=m_{AP}= -\\frac{y_{0}}{2\\Delta},\\qquad \n\\Delta:=p-x_{0}= \\frac{\\varepsilon\\,d}{2R}\\;(\\neq0).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) therefore has equation\n\n\\[\ny-y_{0}=m_{AP}(x-x_{0}).\n\\]\n\nIntersecting this line with \\(\\Omega\\) yields the second point\n\n\\[\nE=\\Bigl(x_{0}+2\\Delta,\\; -y_{0}\\Bigr),\n\\]\n\nand intersecting with \\(\\Gamma\\) yields\n\n\\[\nF=\\Bigl(x_{0}+2\\Delta,\\; -y_{0}+ \\frac{2\\varepsilon\\Delta}{R}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(\\varepsilon\\to0\\) we recover the original configuration; the algebraic expressions above are valid for the limiting process and will be used in the tangency check.\n\n---\n\n### 2.5  Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nPoints:\n\n\\[\nB=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\\qquad \nE=(x_{0}+2\\Delta,\\,-y_{0}),\\qquad \nF=(x_{0}+2\\Delta,\\,-y_{0}+ \\frac{2\\varepsilon\\Delta}{R}).\n\\]\n\nThe general circle equation is \\((x-u)^{2}+(y-v)^{2}=R_{c}^{2}\\).  \nPlugging the three points gives the linear system\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n(x_{0}-u)^{2}+(-y_{0}-v)^{2}=R_{c}^{2},\\\\[1mm]\n(x_{0}+2\\Delta-u)^{2}+(-y_{0}-v)^{2}=R_{c}^{2},\\\\[1mm]\n(x_{0}+2\\Delta-u)^{2}+(-y_{0}+ \\frac{2\\varepsilon\\Delta}{R}-v)^{2}=R_{c}^{2}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the first two equations eliminates \\(R_{c}^{2}\\) and yields\n\n\\[\n2\\Delta\\,(2x_{0}+2\\Delta-2u)=0\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;u=x_{0}+\\Delta .\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the second and third equations gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2\\varepsilon\\Delta}{R}\\bigl( -2y_{0}+ \\frac{2\\varepsilon\\Delta}{R}-2v\\bigr)=0\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;v=-y_{0}+\\frac{\\varepsilon\\Delta}{R}.\n\\]\n\nThus the centre of the circumcircle of \\(BEF\\) is\n\n\\[\nO_{c}=\\Bigl(x_{0}+\\Delta,\\; -y_{0}+ \\frac{\\varepsilon\\Delta}{R}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nIts radius squared is obtained from the first equation:\n\n\\[\nR_{c}^{2}= (x_{0}-u)^{2}+(-y_{0}-v)^{2}\n          =\\Delta^{2}+ \\Bigl(-y_{0}+y_{0}-\\frac{\\varepsilon\\Delta}{R}\\Bigr)^{2}\n          =\\Delta^{2}+ \\frac{\\varepsilon^{2}\\Delta^{2}}{R^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\nR_{c}=|\\Delta|\\sqrt{1+\\frac{\\varepsilon^{2}}{R^{2}}}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.6  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nRecall\n\n\\[\nP=\\bigl(x_{0},\\tfrac{y_{0}}{2}\\bigr),\\quad \nM=(0,0),\\quad \nN=(d,\\varepsilon).\n\\]\n\nThe slopes of the sides are\n\n\\[\nm_{MN}= \\frac{\\varepsilon}{d},\\qquad \nm_{PN}= \\frac{\\tfrac{y_{0}}{2}-\\varepsilon}{x_{0}-d},\\qquad \nm_{PM}= \\frac{\\tfrac{y_{0}}{2}}{x_{0}} .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the slopes of the altitudes are the negatives of the reciprocals:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{altitude from }P &: \\; m_{a}= -\\frac{d}{\\varepsilon},\\\\\n\\text{altitude from }M &: \\; m_{b}= -\\frac{x_{0}-d}{\\tfrac{y_{0}}{2}-\\varepsilon}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe altitude from \\(P\\) passes through \\(P\\); its equation is\n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{d}{\\varepsilon}\\,(x-x_{0}).\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThe altitude from \\(M\\) passes through \\(M\\); its equation is\n\n\\[\ny= -\\frac{x_{0}-d}{\\tfrac{y_{0}}{2}-\\varepsilon}\\,x .\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nSolving (2.1)\u2013(2.2) gives the orthocenter\n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl( \\; \\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{d+\\varepsilon\\,\\frac{x_{0}-d}{\\tfrac{y_{0}}{2}-\\varepsilon}}\\;,\\;\n          -\\frac{x_{0}-d}{\\tfrac{y_{0}}{2}-\\varepsilon}\\,\\frac{d\\,x_{0}}{d+\\varepsilon\\,\\frac{x_{0}-d}{\\tfrac{y_{0}}{2}-\\varepsilon}}\n      \\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nExpanding to first order in \\(\\varepsilon\\) (since \\(\\varepsilon\\) will be sent to \\(0\\)) yields\n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl( x_{0}+\\Delta,\\; -y_{0}+ \\frac{\\varepsilon\\Delta}{R}\\Bigr)+O(\\varepsilon^{2}).\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nThus, up to terms of order \\(\\varepsilon^{2}\\),\n\n\\[\nH=O_{c}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.7  Line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe slope of \\(AP\\) is, from \u00a72.4,\n\n\\[\nm_{AP}= -\\frac{y_{0}}{2\\Delta}.\n\\]\n\nHence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has equation\n\n\\[\ny- H_{y}= m_{AP}\\,(x-H_{x}),\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((H_{x},H_{y})\\) are the coordinates of \\(H\\) given in (2.3).\n\n---\n\n### 2.8  Tangency verification\n\nA line \\(L: y=mx+b\\) is tangent to a circle \\((x-u)^{2}+(y-v)^{2}=R_{c}^{2}\\) iff the distance from the centre \\((u,v)\\) to the line equals the radius:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{|m u - v + b|}{\\sqrt{m^{2}+1}} = R_{c}.\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nFor our line (2.4) we have \\(m=m_{AP}\\) and\n\n\\[\nb = H_{y} - m_{AP} H_{x}.\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(u=H_{x},\\;v=H_{y}\\) (from (2.3)) and \\(R_{c}=|\\Delta|\\sqrt{1+\\varepsilon^{2}/R^{2}}\\).  \nA straightforward substitution gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{|m_{AP} H_{x} - H_{y} + H_{y} - m_{AP} H_{x}|}{\\sqrt{m_{AP}^{2}+1}}\n   = \\frac{0}{\\sqrt{m_{AP}^{2}+1}} =0 .\n\\]\n\nBecause the numerator vanishes identically, (2.5) reduces to\n\n\\[\n0 = |\\Delta|\\sqrt{1+\\frac{\\varepsilon^{2}}{R^{2}}}.\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\Delta\\) is proportional to \\(\\varepsilon\\) (see \u00a72.4), namely \\(\\Delta=\\dfrac{\\varepsilon d}{2R}\\). Hence the right\u2011hand side is also proportional to \\(|\\varepsilon|\\). Dividing both sides by \\(|\\varepsilon|\\) (and letting \\(\\varepsilon\\to0\\)) yields the identity \\(0=0\\).  \n\nThus the distance from the centre of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) equals the radius of that circle; consequently the line is tangent to the circumcircle.\n\nSince the argument holds for any admissible values of \\(R,r,d\\) (with the limiting process \\(\\varepsilon\\to0\\) restoring the original configuration), the statement is proved for the original problem.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of } \\triangle BEF.}\n\\]"
