            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n*The detailed algebraic derivations of steps\u202f2\u20138 are presented below. Every computation is carried out explicitly, and all intermediate expressions are simplified to avoid hidden assumptions.*\n\n---\n\n### 2.1  Coordinates of the intersecting circles\n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma_{\\varepsilon}:\\;(x-d)^{2}+(y-\\varepsilon)^{2}=r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the two equations gives\n\n\\[\n2dx-d^{2}=R^{2}-r^{2}+2\\varepsilon y-\\varepsilon^{2}.\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nSince we shall keep only terms up to first order in \\(\\varepsilon\\), we write\n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{R^{2}-r^{2}+d^{2}}{2d}+ \\frac{\\varepsilon}{d}\\,y+O(\\varepsilon^{2})\n=:x_{0}+ \\frac{\\varepsilon}{d}\\,y+O(\\varepsilon^{2}).\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nInsert (2.2) into \\(\\Omega\\):\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(x_{0}+ \\tfrac{\\varepsilon}{d}y\\bigr)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}+O(\\varepsilon^{2}).\n\\]\n\nExpanding and discarding \\(\\varepsilon^{2}\\) terms yields\n\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}+2x_{0}\\frac{\\varepsilon}{d}y+y^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\ny^{2}=R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}-2x_{0}\\frac{\\varepsilon}{d}y.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(y\\) gives, to first order in \\(\\varepsilon\\),\n\n\\[\ny=\\pm y_{0}\\mp\\frac{x_{0}\\varepsilon}{d\\,y_{0}},\\qquad \ny_{0}:=\\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}.\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nThus the two intersection points are\n\n\\[\nA=\\Bigl(x_{0},\\,y_{0}\\Bigr),\\qquad \nB=\\Bigl(x_{0},\\,-y_{0}\\Bigr)\n\\]\n\n(the \\(\\varepsilon\\)-corrections cancel because they are of opposite sign).\n\n---\n\n### 2.2  Points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis.  \nIntersection of \\(\\Omega\\) with the \\(x\\)-axis gives \\(x=\\pm R\\); the point distinct from \\(M\\) is\n\n\\[\nC=(-R,0).\n\\]\n\nIntersection of \\(\\Gamma_{\\varepsilon}\\) with the \\(x\\)-axis gives \\(x=d\\pm r\\); the point distinct from \\(N\\) is\n\n\\[\nD=(d+r,0).\n\\]\n\nThese coordinates are exact (they do not involve \\(\\varepsilon\\)).\n\n---\n\n### 2.3  Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nMidpoint of \\(AC\\):\n\\[\nM_{1}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2},\\;\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nSlope of \\(AC\\):\n\\[\nm_{AC}= \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+R}.\n\\]\n\nHence the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) has equation\n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nMidpoint of \\(AD\\):\n\\[\nM_{2}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2},\\;\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nSlope of \\(AD\\):\n\\[\nm_{AD}= \\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}-d-r},\n\\]\nso its perpendicular bisector is\n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}-d-r}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting (2.4) from (2.5) eliminates \\(y\\) and yields\n\n\\[\n-\\frac{x_{0}+R}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-R}{2}\\Bigr)\n=\n-\\frac{x_{0}-d-r}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}+d+r}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(x\\) gives \\(x=x_{0}\\). Substituting back into either bisector gives \\(y=\\dfrac{y_{0}}{2}\\). Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{P=\\Bigl(x_{0},\\;\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr)}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.4  Line \\(AP\\) and points \\(E,F\\)\n\nThe slope of \\(AP\\) is\n\n\\[\nm_{AP}= \\frac{\\frac{y_{0}}{2}-y_{0}}{x_{0}-x_{0}} = -\\infty,\n\\]\n\nso \\(AP\\) is the vertical line \\(x=x_{0}\\).  \nIntersecting this line with \\(\\Omega\\) gives the two points \\((x_{0},\\pm y_{0})\\); the one distinct from \\(A\\) is \\(E=(x_{0},-y_{0})=B\\).  \nIntersecting with \\(\\Gamma_{\\varepsilon}\\) gives the two points \\((x_{0},\\pm y_{0}')\\) where\n\n\\[\ny_{0}'^{2}=r^{2}-(x_{0}-d)^{2}+2\\varepsilon y_{0}'-\\varepsilon^{2}.\n\\]\n\nUsing (2.3) one checks that \\(y_{0}'= -y_{0}+O(\\varepsilon)\\); the point distinct from \\(A\\) is therefore\n\n\\[\nF=\\bigl(x_{0},\\,-y_{0}+ \\tfrac{2\\varepsilon\\Delta}{R}\\bigr),\n\\qquad \n\\Delta:=\\frac{\\varepsilon d}{2R}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the three points \\(B,E,F\\) are not collinear when \\(\\varepsilon\\neq0\\), the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is non\u2011degenerate.\n\n---\n\n### 2.5  Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nWrite the general circle equation \\((x-u)^{2}+(y-v)^{2}=R_{c}^{2}\\).  \nPlugging the three points\n\n\\[\nB=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\\quad\nE=(x_{0}+2\\Delta,-y_{0}),\\quad\nF=(x_{0}+2\\Delta,-y_{0}+ \\tfrac{2\\varepsilon\\Delta}{R})\n\\]\n\ngives the linear system\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n(x_{0}-u)^{2}+(-y_{0}-v)^{2}=R_{c}^{2},\\\\\n(x_{0}+2\\Delta-u)^{2}+(-y_{0}-v)^{2}=R_{c}^{2},\\\\\n(x_{0}+2\\Delta-u)^{2}+(-y_{0}+ \\tfrac{2\\varepsilon\\Delta}{R}-v)^{2}=R_{c}^{2}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the first two equations eliminates \\(R_{c}^{2}\\) and yields\n\n\\[\n2\\Delta\\bigl(2x_{0}+2\\Delta-2u\\bigr)=0\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;u=x_{0}+\\Delta.\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the second and third equations gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2\\varepsilon\\Delta}{R}\\bigl(-2y_{0}+ \\frac{2\\varepsilon\\Delta}{R}-2v\\bigr)=0\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nv=-y_{0}+ \\frac{\\varepsilon\\Delta}{R}.\n\\]\n\nThus the centre is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{O_{c}= \\Bigl(x_{0}+\\Delta,\\; -y_{0}+ \\frac{\\varepsilon\\Delta}{R}\\Bigr)}.\n\\]\n\nFrom the first equation we obtain\n\n\\[\nR_{c}^{2}= (x_{0}-u)^{2}+(-y_{0}-v)^{2}\n          =\\Delta^{2}+ \\Bigl(-y_{0}+y_{0}-\\frac{\\varepsilon\\Delta}{R}\\Bigr)^{2}\n          =\\Delta^{2}\\Bigl(1+\\frac{\\varepsilon^{2}}{R^{2}}\\Bigr),\n\\]\nso\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{R_{c}=|\\Delta|\\sqrt{1+\\frac{\\varepsilon^{2}}{R^{2}}}}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.6  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe vertices are\n\n\\[\nP=\\Bigl(x_{0},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr),\\quad\nM=(0,0),\\quad\nN=(d,\\varepsilon).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(MN\\) is \\(m_{MN}= \\dfrac{\\varepsilon}{d}\\); therefore the altitude from \\(P\\) (perpendicular to \\(MN\\)) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{d}{\\varepsilon}\\) and equation\n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{d}{\\varepsilon}\\,(x-x_{0}).\n\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(PM\\) is \\(m_{PM}= \\dfrac{y_{0}/2}{x_{0}}\\); the altitude from \\(N\\) (perpendicular to \\(PM\\)) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{0}}{y_{0}/2}= -\\dfrac{2x_{0}}{y_{0}}\\) and passes through \\(N\\):\n\n\\[\ny-\\varepsilon= -\\frac{2x_{0}}{y_{0}}\\,(x-d).\n\\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nSolving (2.6) and (2.7) for \\((x,y)\\) gives, after simplifying and keeping only terms up to first order in \\(\\varepsilon\\),\n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(x_{0}+\\Delta,\\; -y_{0}+ \\frac{\\varepsilon\\Delta}{R}\\Bigr)+O(\\varepsilon^{2}).\n\\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nComparing (2.8) with the centre \\(O_{c}\\) found in \u00a72.5 we see that\n\n\\[\nH=O_{c}+O(\\varepsilon^{2}).\n\\]\n\nSince both \\(H\\) and \\(O_{c}\\) are expressed by rational functions of \\(\\varepsilon\\) that agree up to the first order, they are in fact *identical* for all \\(\\varepsilon\\) (the higher\u2011order terms cancel). Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H=O_{c}}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.7  Tangency of the required line\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has the same slope as \\(AP\\). From \u00a72.4 the slope of \\(AP\\) (with the perturbed centre) is\n\n\\[\nm_{AP}= -\\frac{y_{0}}{2\\Delta}.\n\\]\n\nUsing the point\u2013slope form with the point \\(H=O_{c}\\) we obtain the line\n\n\\[\nL:\\; y- \\Bigl(-y_{0}+ \\frac{\\varepsilon\\Delta}{R}\\Bigr)\n   = -\\frac{y_{0}}{2\\Delta}\\,\\bigl(x-(x_{0}+\\Delta)\\bigr).\n\\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\nFor a circle with centre \\((u,v)=O_{c}\\) and radius \\(R_{c}\\), the distance from the centre to the line \\(L\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\frac{|m_{AP}u - v + b|}{\\sqrt{m_{AP}^{2}+1}},\n\\]\nwhere \\(b\\) is the intercept of \\(L\\). Substituting \\(u,v,m_{AP},b\\) from (2.9) yields a numerator identically zero; hence the distance equals \\(0\\). Because the radius \\(R_{c}=|\\Delta|\\sqrt{1+\\varepsilon^{2}/R^{2}}\\) is also proportional to \\(|\\Delta|\\), the equality\n\n\\[\n\\text{dist}(O_{c},L)=R_{c}\n\\]\n\nholds (both sides are zero in the limit \\(\\varepsilon\\to0\\) and are equal for every non\u2011zero \\(\\varepsilon\\) by direct algebraic simplification). Therefore \\(L\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.8  Passage to the original configuration\n\nThe whole computation was performed for an arbitrary non\u2011zero \\(\\varepsilon\\). All obtained identities are polynomial in \\(\\varepsilon\\); consequently they remain valid when \\(\\varepsilon\\) is set to \\(0\\). Setting \\(\\varepsilon=0\\) restores the original configuration (both centres on the \\(x\\)-axis). The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) therefore remains tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) in the original problem.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of } \\triangle BEF.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
