te choice, and the coordinates of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are correctly obtained from the circle equations. This part is acceptable.\n\n---\n\n### 2.3  Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe perpendicular\u2011bisector calculations are standard. Solving the two linear equations indeed yields \\(x=x_{0},\\;y=y_{0}/2\\). Hence \\(P=(x_{0},y_{0}/2)\\) is correct **provided** the coordinates of \\(A,B,C,D\\) from the previous sections are accurate. No error here.\n\n---\n\n### 2.4  Line \\(AP\\) and points \\(E,F\\)\n\n> \u201cThe slope of \\(AP\\) is  \n> \\[\n> m_{AP}= \\frac{\\frac{y_{0}}{2}-y_{0}}{x_{0}-x_{0}} = -\\infty,\n> \\]  \n> so \\(AP\\) is the vertical line \\(x=x_{0}\\).\u201d\n\nThis is correct: \\(A\\) and \\(P\\) have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, so \\(AP\\) is vertical.\n\n> \u201cIntersecting this line with \\(\\Omega\\) gives the two points \\((x_{0},\\pm y_{0})\\); the one distinct from \\(A\\) is \\(E=(x_{0},-y_{0})=B\\).\u201d\n\n**Critical Error.** By definition, \\(E\\) is the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) distinct from \\(A\\). Since the two intersections are \\((x_{0},y_{0})=A\\) and \\((x_{0},-y_{0})\\), the point distinct from \\(A\\) is indeed \\((x_{0},-y_{0})\\). However, the point \\((x_{0},-y_{0})\\) is **exactly the point \\(B\\)**, because earlier the solution identified the two intersection points of the circles as \\(A=(x_{0},y_{0})\\) and \\(B=(x_{0},-y_{0})\\). Consequently \\(E\\) coincides with \\(B\\).\n\nThe problem statement requires \\(E\\neq B\\) (otherwise triangle \\(BEF\\) would be degenerate). The solution later treats \\(B,E,F\\) as three distinct points, which contradicts this identification. This inconsistency invalidates all subsequent arguments that rely on a non\u2011degenerate triangle \\(BEF\\).\n\n> \u201cIntersecting with \\(\\Gamma_{\\varepsilon}\\) gives the two points \\((x_{0},\\pm y_{0}')\\) \u2026 the point distinct from \\(A\\) is therefore  \n> \\[\n> F=\\bigl(x_{0},\