at the higher\u2011order terms vanish, nor that the expressions are indeed polynomials in \\(\\varepsilon\\). Hence the conclusion \\(H=O_{c}\\) is unjustified.\n\n---\n\n### 2.7  Tangency of the required line\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) should be vertical (since \\(AP\\) was shown to be vertical). Yet the author writes a finite slope  \n\n\\[\nm_{AP}= -\\frac{y_{0}}{2\\Delta},\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts the earlier result that \\(AP\\) is the line \\(x=x_{0}\\). This inconsistency indicates a mis\u2011calculation of the slope after the perturbation, and the subsequent distance\u2011to\u2011line computation is therefore baseless.\n\nFurthermore, the distance formula is applied with a numerator claimed to be \u201cidentically zero\u201d without showing the algebraic cancellation. Given the earlier errors, this claim cannot be trusted.\n\n---\n\n### 2.8  Passage to the original configuration\n\nThe author argues that because all identities are polynomial in \\(\\varepsilon\\), setting \\(\\varepsilon=0\\) is legitimate. This is false for several reasons:\n\n1. Many intermediate expressions involve division by \\(\\varepsilon\\) (e.g., the altitude slope \\(-d/\\varepsilon\\)), which are not defined at \\(\\varepsilon=0\\).\n2. The expansions discard \\(O(\\varepsilon^{2})\\) terms, so the resulting formulas are not exact polynomials.\n3. The earlier contradictions (e.g., \\(E=B\\) vs. distinct) are not resolved by taking the limit.\n\nThus the \u201cpassage to the original configuration\u201d does not repair the fundamental flaws.\n\n---\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe solution contains multiple **Critical Errors** that break the logical chain:\n\n* Misidentification of point \\(E\\) with \\(B\\) leading to a degenerate triangle \\(BEF\\).\n* Inconsistent coordinates for \\(E\\) later in the proof.\n* Unjustified claim that the orthocenter \\(H\\) coincides exactly with the circumcenter \\(O_{c}\\).\n* Contradictory statements about the slope of \\(AP\\) (