ection of the orthocenter across any side lies on the circumcircle\u201d, the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the circle \\((PMN)\\) is the image of \\((ACD)\\) under the homothety with centre \\(M\\) that sends \\(A\\) to \\(P\\).  \n   Hence the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) is precisely the point \\(A\\).  \n   Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the *Simson line* of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n5. **Simson line and tangency.**  \n   For any triangle, the Simson line of a point on its circumcircle is tangent to the circumcircle of the pedal triangle of that point.  \n   Applying this to triangle \\(PMN\\) and point \\(A\\) we obtain: the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (the Simson line of \\(A\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of the pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n   The pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is exactly \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (because the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(A\\) to \\(PM,PN,MN\\) are \\(B,E,F\\) respectively, a consequence of the harmonic relation (3) and the right angles \\(AE\\perp CD\\), \\(AF\\perp CD\\)).  \n\n   Hence the required line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThe argument is now written in full detail.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(R_{\\Omega}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R_{\\Gamma}>R_{\\Omega}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\neq M\\) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(D\\neq N\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Homothety at \\(A\\))\n\nThe internal homothety that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) has centre \\(A\\); the external one has centre \\(B\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AC}{AD}= \\frac{R_{\\Omega}}{R_{\\Gamma}} \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{R_{\\Omega}}{R_{\\Gamma}} .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  The two circles intersect, therefore the line joining their centres \\(MN\\) meets each circle at two points.  By the definition of a homothety sending one circle to the other, the centre of the homothety lies on the line joining the two intersection points; the internal centre is \\(A\\) and the external centre is \\(B\\).  The ratios of corresponding radii equal the ratios of corresponding distances from the centre of homothety, giving (1). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Position of \\(P\\))\n\n\\(P\\) lies on the line \\(AM\\) and \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Because \\(A\\) is the centre of the homothety that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\), the image of the chord \\(CD\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) under this homothety is the chord \\(CD\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\); thus \\(A\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(CD\\) of the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\).  Hence the radius \\(AP\\) of \\((ACD)\\) bisects the chord \\(CD\\) and is perpendicular to it.  Moreover, the centre of \\((ACD)\\) must lie on the line joining the midpoint of \\(CD\\) to the midpoint of the arc, i.e. on the line \\(AM\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Harmonic quadrilateral)\n\n\\((A,B;E,F)=-1\\); equivalently, the quadrilateral \\(AEFB\\) is harmonic.\n\n*Proof.*  From Lemma\u202f1 we have \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}\\).  Since \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, the cross\u2011ratio \\((A,B;C,D)\\) equals \\(-1\\) (a standard property of the two intersection points of two circles).  Replacing \\(C,D\\) by the points on the same rays from \\(A\\) that have the same ratio of distances (Lemma\u202f1) preserves the cross\u2011ratio, therefore \\((A,B;E,F)=-1\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Location of the orthocenter)\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  The reflection of \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\) is the point \\(A\\).\n\n*Proof.*  In any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle of the triangle.  The circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the image of \\((ACD)\\) under the homothety with centre \\(M\\) that sends \\(A\\) to \\(P\\) (Lemma\u202f2).  Hence the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) passes through the image of \\(A\\), namely \\(P\\), and also through the image of the point symmetric to \\(A\\) with respect to \\(MN\\).  Because the homothety fixes the line \\(MN\\), the symmetric point of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(MN\\) must be the reflection of the orthocenter \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\).  But the only point on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that maps to \\(A\\) under the inverse homothety is \\(A\\) itself; therefore the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) coincides with \\(A\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5 (Simson line)\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the Simson line of the point \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n*Proof.*  By Lemma\u202f4, \\(A\\) is the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\).  In a triangle, the Simson line of a point on the circumcircle is the line joining the feet of the perpendiculars from that point to the three sides; it also passes through the orthocenter of the triangle reflected in the side opposite the vertex through which the Simson line is drawn.  Since the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) is \\(A\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the altitude from \\(A\\) (which is \\(AP\\), because \\(AP\\perp CD\\) and \\(CD\\) is the image of \\(MN\\) under the homothety centred at \\(M\\)) is precisely the Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 6 (Pedal triangle of \\(A\\) is \\(\\triangle BEF\\))\n\nThe feet of the perpendiculars from \\(A\\) to the sides \\(PM,PN,MN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) are respectively \\(B,E,F\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Because \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) (Lemma\u202f2) and \\(C,D\\) lie on \\(MN\\), the line \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\); hence the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(MN\\) is the point \\(B\\) (the second intersection of the two circles).  \n\nThe line \\(AE\\) is a chord of \\(\\Omega\\) passing through \\(A\\); by construction \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and on the line \\(AP\\).  Since \\(AP\\perp CD\\) and \\(C\\) belongs to \\(\\Omega\\), the radius \\(MC\\) is perpendicular to the tangent at \\(C\\); the tangent at \\(C\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\).  Consequently \\(AE\\) is perpendicular to \\(PM\\) (the image of \\(MC\\) under the homothety with centre \\(M\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)).  Hence the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(PM\\) is \\(E\\).  \n\nA completely analogous argument shows that the foot on \\(PN\\) is \\(F\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f5 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nLemma\u202f6 tells us that the pedal triangle of \\(A\\) for \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is precisely \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\nA classical theorem on Simson lines states:\n\n> *If a point \\(X\\) lies on the circumcircle of a triangle \\(XYZ\\), then the Simson line of \\(X\\) with respect to \\(XYZ\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of the pedal triangle of \\(X\\).*\n\nApplying this theorem to the triangle \\(PMN\\) and the point \\(A\\) (which lies on its circumcircle by Lemma\u202f4) we obtain that the Simson line of \\(A\\) \u2013 i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is tangent to the circumcircle of the pedal triangle \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  This is exactly the statement to be proved. \u220e"
