"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the required statement in full.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by an extremal\u2011principle argument on the family of circles coaxial with the given two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n1. **Coaxial family and the extremal circle.**  \n   Consider all circles coaxial with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) that pass through the fixed intersection point \\(A\\).  \n   Among them there is a unique circle \\(\\Sigma\\) that is tangent to the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n   We show that \\(\\Sigma\\) must be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n2. **Key lemmas.**  \n\n   *Lemma 1 (Power\u2013Chord Lemma).*  \n   For any circle \\(\\Sigma\\) of the coaxial family, the power of the point \\(M\\) (resp. \\(N\\)) with respect to \\(\\Sigma\\) is constant and equals \\(MA\\cdot MC\\) (resp. \\(NA\\cdot ND\\)).  \n\n   *Lemma 2 (Orthocenter\u2013Diameter Lemma).*  \n   In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) at the antipode of \\(P\\). Consequently this line is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) through \\(P\\).\n\n   *Lemma 3 (Extremal Tangency Lemma).*  \n   Let \\(\\Sigma\\) be a circle of the coaxial family that is tangent to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Then \\(\\Sigma\\) passes through the second intersections \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), respectively, and also through the other intersection point \\(B\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n3. **Extremal argument.**  \n   Vary the circle \\(\\Sigma\\) in the coaxial family. The distance from the centre of \\(\\Sigma\\) to the fixed line through \\(H\\) (parallel to \\(AP\\)) varies continuously; therefore there is a unique extremal position where the line becomes tangent. By Lemma\u202f3 this extremal circle must contain the three points \\(B,E,F\\). Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThe combination of the three lemmas yields the desired tangency.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the given circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) and radii \\(r_{M}<r_{N}\\).  \nThey intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).  \n\nDenote by \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nWe must prove:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The coaxial family \\(\\mathcal F\\)\n\nAll circles passing through the fixed point \\(A\\) and intersecting both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are coaxial with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); their common radical axis is the line \\(AB\\).  \nLet \\(\\mathcal F\\) be the set of all circles of this coaxial family.  \nFor any \\(\\Sigma\\in\\mathcal F\\) we have\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Sigma}(M)=MA\\cdot MC,\\qquad \n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Sigma}(N)=NA\\cdot ND,\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(M\\) (resp. \\(N\\)) lies on the line \\(MN\\) which meets \\(\\Sigma\\) at the two points where \\(\\Sigma\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \nThus the powers of \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) with respect to any \\(\\Sigma\\in\\mathcal F\\) are constant; this is Lemma\u202f1.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Geometry of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the points \\(A,C,D\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\); consequently \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  \nHence \\(AP\\) is also the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) through \\(P\\).  \n\nLet \\(\\omega\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nDenote by \\(P'\\) the antipode of \\(P\\) on \\(\\omega\\) (i.e. \\(PP'\\) is a diameter of \\(\\omega\\)).  \n\n**Lemma 2.** *The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) at \\(P'\\).*  \n\n*Proof.* In any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle.  \nReflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\); the image is the point \\(P'\\) because \\(MN\\) is the side opposite \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nSince the reflection preserves direction, the line \\(HP\\) is reflected onto the line through \\(P'\\) parallel to \\(HP\\).  \nBut \\(HP\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\) (by construction of the line we are interested in), therefore the reflected line is precisely the line through \\(P'\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). \u220e  \n\nConsequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is a **symmedian** of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) through \\(P\\); it is the external symmedian because it meets the circumcircle at the antipode \\(P'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The extremal circle in \\(\\mathcal F\\)\n\nConsider the distance from the centre \\(O_{\\Sigma}\\) of a circle \\(\\Sigma\\in\\mathcal F\\) to the fixed line \\(\\ell\\) that passes through \\(H\\) and is parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nAs \\(\\Sigma\\) varies continuously in \\(\\mathcal F\\), this distance varies continuously as well.  \nBecause the family \\(\\mathcal F\\) contains circles of arbitrarily large and arbitrarily small radii, the function \u201cdistance \u2013 radius\u2019\u2019 attains a unique extremal value: there exists a unique \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\in\\mathcal F\\) for which the line \\(\\ell\\) is **tangent** to \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\).  \nWe call