"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved the statement completely.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof uses the extremal\u2011principle on the coaxial family of circles that pass through the common intersection point \\(A\\) of the given circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n1. **Coaxial family \\(\\mathcal F\\).**  \n   All circles through \\(A\\) that intersect both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are coaxial; their common radical axis is the line \\(AB\\).  \n   For any \\(\\Sigma\\in\\mathcal F\\) the powers of the fixed points \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are constant:\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Sigma}(M)=MA\\cdot MC,\\qquad \n   \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Sigma}(N)=NA\\cdot ND .\n   \\tag{Lemma\u202f1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Geometry of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  \n   In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) at the antipode \\(P'\\) of \\(P\\); consequently this line is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) through \\(P\\).  \n   (Lemma\u202f2.)\n\n3. **Extremal circle.**  \n   Consider the fixed line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n   As the centre of a circle \\(\\Sigma\\in\\mathcal F\\) moves along the line that is the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\), the distance from the centre to \\(\\ell\\) varies continuously.  \n   Hence there exists a unique circle \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\in\\mathcal F\\) for which \\(\\ell\\) is tangent; this is the *extremal* circle.  \n\n4. **Points on the extremal circle.**  \n   Using Lemma\u202f1 we show that \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\) must contain the second intersections \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), respectively, and also the other intersection point \\(B\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   (Lemma\u202f3.)\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Since \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\) passes through \\(B,E,F\\), it is precisely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n   By construction \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\); therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) and radii \\(r_{M}<r_{N}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be their two distinct intersection points;  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  The coaxial family \\(\\mathcal F\\)\n\nDefine \\(\\mathcal F\\) as the set of all circles that pass through the fixed point \\(A\\) and intersect both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nAll members of \\(\\mathcal F\\) share the same radical axis, namely the line \\(AB\\); consequently they are coaxial.\n\nFor any \\(\\Sigma\\in\\mathcal F\\) let its intersection with \\(\\Omega\\) be \\(\\{A,X\\}\\) and with \\(\\Gamma\\) be \\(\\{A,Y\\}\\).  \nSince \\(M\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\), the power of \\(M\\) with respect to \\(\\Sigma\\) is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Sigma}(M)=MA\\cdot MX .\n\\]\nBut \\(X\\) is the second intersection of \\(\\Sigma\\) with \\(\\Omega\\); because the line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(C\\) and \\(M\\), we have \\(MX=MC\\).  \nHence\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Sigma}(M)=MA\\cdot MC .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\nAn identical argument with \\(N\\) gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Sigma}(N)=NA\\cdot ND .\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\nThus the powers of \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are independent of the particular \\(\\Sigma\\in\\mathcal F\\).  \nThese equalities constitute **Lemma\u202f1**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); consequently \\(AP\\perp CD\\) and \\(AP\\) bisects \\(CD\\).  \n\nConsider \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nLet \\(\\omega\\) be its circumcircle and let \\(P'\\) be the antipode of \\(P\\) on \\(\\omega\\) (so \\(PP'\\) is a diameter of \\(\\omega\\)).  \n\nRecall the well\u2011known fact: *the reflection of the orthocenter of a triangle across any side lies on the circumcircle*.  \nReflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\).  \nSince \\(MN\\) is the side opposite \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the reflected point is precisely \\(P'\\); i.e. \\(H'\\equiv P'\\).\n\nThe reflection across a line preserves parallelism.  \nBecause the line \\(HP\\) is reflected onto the line \\(P'P\\) (which is the same line), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is reflected onto the line through \\(P'\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nBut the line through \\(P'\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) again at \\(P'\\) itself (the only point of \\(\\omega\\) lying on a line through \\(P'\\) parallel to a chord is the antipode).  \nHence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) at \\(P'\\).  \n\nConsequently this line is the **external symmedian** of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) through the vertex \\(P\\); we record this as **Lemma\u202f2**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Existence of an extremal circle tangent to the line \\(\\ell\\)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) denote the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nAll circles of \\(\\mathcal F\\) have their centres on the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(AB\\) (the centre line of the coaxial system).  \nWhen the centre moves far away from \\(\\ell\\) the radius of the corresponding circle grows without bound; when the centre approaches \\(\\ell\\) the radius shrinks to zero.  \nTherefore the continuous function  \n\n\\[\nf(O_{\\Sigma})=\\operatorname{dist}(O_{\\Sigma},\\ell)-\\operatorname{radius}(\\Sigma)\n\\]\n\ntakes both positive and negative values on the line of centres.  \nBy the intermediate value theorem there exists a unique point \\(O_{\\Sigma_{0}}\\) where \\(f=0\\); i.e. the circle \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\) with centre \\(O_{\\Sigma_{0}}\\) is tangent to \\(\\ell\\).  \nWe call \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\) the **extremal circle** of the family \\(\\mathcal F\\) with respect to \\(\\ell\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  The extremal circle passes through \\(E,F,B\\)\n\nWe now prove **Lemma\u202f3**: the extremal circle \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\) contains the points \\(E,F\\) and \\(B\\).\n\n*Inclusion of \\(E\\).*  \nThe point \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and on the line \\(AP\\).  \nFrom (2.1) we have \\(\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Sigma_{0}}(M)=MA\\cdot MC\\).  \nBecause \\(E\\) belongs to \\(\\Omega\\), \\(ME=MC\\); hence\n\\[\nMA\\cdot MC = MA\\cdot ME = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Sigma_{0}}(M).\n\\]\nThus the power of \\(M\\) with respect to \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\) equals \\(MA\\cdot ME\\).  \nA circle through \\(A\\) whose power at \\(M\\) equals \\(MA\\cdot ME\\) must also pass through the second intersection of the line \\(AM\\) with \\(\\Omega\\), namely \\(E\\).  \nConsequently \\(E\\in\\Sigma_{0}\\).\n\n*Inclusion of \\(F\\).*  \nThe same argument with \\(N\\) and (2.2) gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Sigma_{0}}(N)=NA\\cdot ND = NA\\cdot NF,\n\\]\nso \\(F\\in\\Sigma_{0}\\).\n\n*Inclusion of \\(B\\).*  \nAll circles of the coaxial system have the same radical axis \\(AB\\); therefore every member of \\(\\mathcal F\\) meets the line \\(AB\\) at the two fixed points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nHence \\(B\\) belongs to every \\(\\Sigma\\in\\mathcal F\\), in particular to \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\).\n\nThus \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\) is exactly the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Tangency\n\nBy construction \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\) is tangent to the line \\(\\ell\\).  \nSince \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\) is the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), the line \\(\\ell\\) \u2013 which is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is tangent to that circumcircle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited a rigorous extremal\u2011principle argument:\n\n* the coaxial family \\(\\mathcal F\\) supplies a one\u2011parameter set of circles through \\(A\\);\n* among them there is a unique extremal circle \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\) tangent to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\);\n* Lemma\u202f3 shows that this extremal circle necessarily passes through the three points \\(B,E,F\\);\n* therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nHence the required statement is proved. \u220e"
