\) is clear.  \n  - The claim that *all* such circles share the radical axis \\(AB\\) is **incorrect** without justification. For a family of circles passing through a common point \\(A\\) and intersecting two given circles, the radical axis of any two members is indeed the line through their two common points, but it is not automatically the fixed line \\(AB\\). The solution never proves that \\(B\\) belongs to every member, so the statement that the radical axis is \\(AB\\) is unsupported. This is a **Justification Gap** (later listed as a critical error because the subsequent lemmas rely on it).\n\n#### 2. Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 Powers of \\(M\\) and \\(N\\)\n\n- **Quoted text:** \u201cSince \\(M\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\), the power of \\(M\\) with respect to \\(\\Sigma\\) is \\(\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Sigma}(M)=MA\\cdot MX\\). But \\(X\\) is the second intersection of \\(\\Sigma\\) with \\(\\Omega\\); because the line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(C\\) and \\(M\\), we have \\(MX=MC\\). Hence \\(\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Sigma}(M)=MA\\cdot MC\\).\u201d\n- **Analysis:**  \n  - The formula \\(\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Sigma}(M)=MA\\cdot MX\\) is correct for a circle \\(\\Sigma\\) passing through \\(A\\) and intersecting \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(X\\).  \n  - The step **\\(MX = MC\\)** is **false**: \\(X\\) is the second intersection of the *arbitrary* circle \\(\\Sigma\\) with \\(\\Omega\\); there is no reason for \\(X\\) to coincide with the fixed point \\(C\\). Consequently the equality \\(\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Sigma}(M)=MA\\cdot MC\\) does **not** hold for a general \\(\\Sigma\\). This is a **Critical Error** that invalidates Lemma\u202f1 and all later arguments that depend on it.\n\n#### 3. Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\n- **Quoted text:** \u201cReflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\). Since \\(MN\\) is the side opposite \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the reflected point is precisely \\(P'\\); i.