r a general coaxial family defined only by passing through \\(A\\) and intersecting two given circles. This is a **Critical Error**.\n\n- **Quoted text:** \u201cWhen the centre moves far away from \\(\\ell\\) the radius of the corresponding circle grows without bound; when the centre approaches \\(\\ell\\) the radius shrinks to zero.\u201d\n- **Analysis:**  \n  - The behavior of the radius as a function of the center\u2019s distance from \\(\\ell\\) is asserted without any argument. The continuity of the radius with respect to the center\u2019s position is not established for the specific family \\(\\mathcal F\\). This is a **Critical Error** because the existence of a tangent circle relies on this claim.\n\n- **Quoted text:** \u201cBy the intermediate value theorem there exists a unique point \\(O_{\\Sigma_{0}}\\) where \\(f=0\\); i.e. the circle \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\) with centre \\(O_{\\Sigma_{0}}\\) is tangent to \\(\\ell\\).\u201d\n- **Analysis:**  \n  - The application of the intermediate value theorem presupposes the continuity of the function \\(f\\) on a connected interval of centers, which has not been demonstrated. Moreover, uniqueness is not guaranteed without monotonicity. Hence this step contains a **Justification Gap** (but because the preceding claims are false, the whole argument collapses).\n\n#### 5. Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 The extremal circle passes through \\(E,F,B\\)\n\n- **Quoted text (Inclusion of \\(E\\))**: \u201cBecause \\(E\\) belongs to \\(\\Omega\\), \\(ME=MC\\); hence \\(MA\\cdot MC = MA\\cdot ME = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Sigma_{0}}(M)\\). Thus the power of \\(M\\) with respect to \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\) equals \\(MA\\cdot ME\\). A circle through \\(A\\) whose power at \\(M\\) equals \\(MA\\cdot ME\\) must also pass through the second intersection of the line \\(AM\\) with \\(\\Omega\\), namely \\(E\\). Consequently \\(E\\in\\Sigma_{0}\\).\u201d\n- **Analysis:**  \n  - The equality \\(ME = MC\\) is false: \\(E\\) is the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\), not the point \\(C\\). Hence the derived equality of powers is invalid.  \n  - Even if the power equality held, the inference that a circle through \\(A\\) with that specific power must contain \\(E\\) is not justified; many circles through \\(A\\) can have the same power at \\(M\\) without passing through a given point on \\(\\Omega\\). This is a **Critical Error**.\n\n- **Quoted text (Inclusion of \\(F\\))**: analogous to the case of \\(E\\); the same unjustified reasoning applies, yielding another **Critical Error**.\n\n- **Quoted text (Inclusion of \\(B\\))**: \u201cAll circles of the coaxial system have the same radical axis \\(AB\\); therefore every member of \\(\\mathcal F\\) meets the line \\(AB\\) at the two fixed points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\). Hence \\(B\\) belongs to every \\(\\Sigma\\in\\mathcal F\\).\u201d\n- **Analysis:**  \n  - As previously noted, the claim that every circle of the family passes through \\(B\\) is not proved. The fact that the radical axis of any two circles in the family is \\(AB\\) does not imply that each circle contains \\(B\\). This is a **Justification Gap**.\n\n#### 6. Final Tangency Argument\n\n- **Quoted text:** \u201cBy construction \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\) is tangent to the line \\(\\ell\\). Since \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\) is the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), the line \\(\\ell\\) \u2013 which is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is tangent to that circumcircle.\u201d\n- **Analysis:**  \n  - This conclusion follows from the previous lemmas. Since Lemma\u202f3 (the identification of \\(\\Sigma_{0}\\) with the circumcircle of \\(BEF\\)) is invalid, the final statement is unsupported. Hence the overall proof fails.\n\n---\n\n**Overall Assessment:** The solution contains multiple critical errors\u2014most notably the false identification \\(MX = MC\\) (which invalidates Lemma\u202f1), the incorrect reflection argument concerning the orthocenter and antipode, and the unfounded claims about the 