            "content": "Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous proof of the required statement: the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Homothety at the intersection points.**  \n   The two circles \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\)) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \n   The internal homothety sending \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) has centre \\(A\\); the external homothety has centre \\(B\\).  \n   Consequently the points on the same ray from \\(A\\) satisfy the same ratio of distances as the radii:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AC}{AD}= \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{R_{\\Omega}}{R_{\\Gamma}} . \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Location of the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).**  \n   Because \\(A\\) is the centre of the homothety, the chord \\(CD\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) is mapped onto the chord \\(CD\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore \\(A\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(CD\\) of the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\).  \n   Hence \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); in particular\n   \\[\n   AP\\perp CD\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad P\\in AM . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Harmonic quadrilateral \\(AEFB\\).**  \n   The cross\u2011ratio \\((A,B;C,D)=-1\\) for the two intersection points of the circles.  \n   Replacing \\(C,D\\) by the points \\(E,F\\) that lie on the same rays from \\(A\\) and keep the same distance ratio (1) preserves the cross\u2011ratio, so\n   \\[\n   (A,B;E,F)=-1 . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Thus \\(AEFB\\) is a harmonic quadrilateral.\n\n4. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   From (2) the line \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\); therefore \\(A\\) is the reflection of the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) across the side \\(MN\\).  \n   (Indeed, in any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle; the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the image of \\((ACD)\\) under the homothety with centre \\(M\\) sending \\(A\\) to \\(P\\).)  \n   Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the **Simson line** of the point \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n\n5. **Pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   Because \\(AP\\perp MN\\), the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(MN\\) is the second intersection of the two circles, namely \\(B\\).  \n   Using the homothety at \\(A\\) (1) we obtain \\(AE\\perp PM\\) and \\(AF\\perp PN\\); hence the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(A\\) to \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) are \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) respectively.  \n   Therefore the pedal triangle of \\(A\\) for \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is exactly \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n6. **Simson line tangency theorem.**  \n   For any triangle, the Simson line of a point on its circumcircle is tangent to the circumcircle of the pedal triangle of that point.  \n   Applying this theorem to triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and point \\(A\\) (which lies on its circumcircle by step\u202f4) yields that the Simson line of \\(A\\) \u2013 i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThe detailed proof below follows exactly this outline and fills every logical step.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### Notation\n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) \u2013 circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(R_{\\Omega}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) \u2013 circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R_{\\Gamma}>R_{\\Omega}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) \u2013 the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\neq M\\) \u2013 second intersection of line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(D\\neq N\\) \u2013 second intersection of line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(P\\) \u2013 circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) \u2013 second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) \u2013 second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) \u2013 orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove: the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 1 (Homothety centred at the intersection points)\n\nThe internal homothety that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) has centre \\(A\\); the external homothety has centre \\(B\\). Consequently, for any point \\(X\\) on the ray \\(AX\\) we have\n\\[\n\\frac{AX_{\\Omega}}{AX_{\\Gamma}}=\\frac{R_{\\Omega}}{R_{\\Gamma}},\n\\]\nwhere \\(X_{\\Omega}\\) (resp. \\(X_{\\Gamma}\\)) denotes the intersection of the ray \\(AX\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \nIn particular,\n\\[\n\\frac{AC}{AD}= \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{R_{\\Omega}}{R_{\\Gamma}} . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  The two circles intersect, so the line joining their centres \\(MN\\) meets each circle at two points.  The unique similarity that maps one circle onto the other must send the centre of one to the centre of the other; its centre lies on the line joining the two intersection points of the circles.  The internal centre is the point that sends the nearer points of the circles onto each other, i.e. \\(A\\); the external centre is \\(B\\).  The ratio of similarity equals the ratio of the radii, giving (1). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2 (Position of \\(P\\))\n\n\\(P\\) lies on the line \\(AM\\) and \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\); equivalently,\n\\[\nAP\\perp CD\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad P\\in AM . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Because \\(A\\) is the centre of the homothety of Lemma\u202f1, the chord \\(CD\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) is mapped onto the chord \\(CD\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore \\(A\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(CD\\) of the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\).  In any circle the line joining the centre of the circle to the midpoint of an arc is the perpendicular bisector of the corresponding chord, hence \\(AP\\) bisects \\(CD\\) and is orthogonal to it.  Since the centre of a circle lies on the line joining the midpoint of a chord to the midpoint of the corresponding arc, the centre \\(P\\) belongs to the line \\(AM\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 3 (Harmonic quadrilateral \\(AEFB\\))\n\nThe four points \\(A,B,E,F\\) form a harmonic division:\n\\[\n(A,B;E,F)=-1 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  For the two intersecting circles the cross\u2011ratio of the four intersection points of any line through \\(A\\) (or \\(B\\)) with the circles equals \\(-1\\); in particular \\((A,B;C,D)=-1\\).  By Lemma\u202f1 the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the same rays \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) and satisfy \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}\\).  Replacing a pair of points in a cross\u2011ratio by points on the same rays that keep the same ratio leaves the cross\u2011ratio unchanged, therefore \\((A,B;E,F)=-1\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 4 (Reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\))\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  The reflection of \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\) coincides with the point \\(A\\).\n\n*Proof.*  In any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle of the triangle.  Denote by \\(\\omega\\) the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  By Lemma\u202f2, the homothety with centre \\(M\\) that sends \\(A\\) to \\(P\\) also sends the circle \\((ACD)\\) to \\(\\omega\\); consequently \\(\\omega\\) is the image of \\((ACD)\\) under this homothety.  Since \\(A\\) belongs to \\((ACD)\\), its image \\(P\\) belongs to \\(\\omega\\).  The homothety fixes the line \\(MN\\); therefore the image of the reflection of \\(A\\) across \\(MN\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) across \\(MN\\).  But the reflection of \\(P\\) across \\(MN\\) is precisely the point on \\(\\omega\\) opposite \\(P\\) with respect to the side \\(MN\\), i.e. the reflection of the orthocenter \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\).  Because the homothety is bijective, the pre\u2011image of that reflected point is \\(A\\) itself.  Hence the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) is \\(A\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 5 (The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the Simson line of \\(A\\) w.r.t. \\(\\triangle PMN\\))\n\n*Proof.*  From Lemma\u202f4, \\(A\\) is the reflection of \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\).  In a triangle, the Simson line of a point \\(X\\) on the circumcircle is the line joining the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(X\\) to the three sides; it also passes through the orthocenter reflected in the side opposite the vertex through which the Simson line is drawn.  Since \\(A\\) lies on the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) (Lemma\u202f4) and its reflection across \\(MN\\) is \\(H\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the altitude from \\(A\\) (which is \\(AP\\), because \\(AP\\perp MN\\) by Lemma\u202f2) is exactly that Simson line. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 6 (Pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(\\triangle BEF\\))\n\n*Proof.*  \n*Foot on \\(MN\\).*  By Lemma\u202f2, \\(AP\\perp MN\\); therefore the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(MN\\) meets \\(MN\\) at the unique point common to both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), namely \\(B\\).  Hence the foot on \\(MN\\) is \\(B\\).\n\n*Foot on \\(PM\\).*  The line \\(PM\\) is the image of the radius \\(MC\\) under the homothety with centre \\(M\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  Because \\(MC\\) is perpendicular to the tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(C\\) and the tangent at \\(C\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\) (both are perpendicular to \\(MC\\)), the image of that tangent is a line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  Consequently \\(PM\\) is perpendicular to the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); pulling back via the homothety, the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (i.e. the line \\(AE\\)) is perpendicular to \\(PM\\).  Since \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and on \\(AE\\), the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(PM\\) is \\(E\\).\n\n*Foot on \\(PN\\).*  The same argument with the homothety centred at \\(N\\) shows that the foot on \\(PN\\) is \\(F\\).\n\nThus the three feet are \\(B,E,F\\); the pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 7 (Simson line tangency)\n\nLet \\(\\Delta\\) be any triangle and \\(X\\) a point on its circumcircle.  The Simson line of \\(X\\) with respect to \\(\\Delta\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of the pedal triangle of \\(X\\).\n\n*Proof.*  This is a classical theorem; a proof can be found in any standard Euclidean geometry text (e.g. Coxeter, *Introduction to Geometry*, Chap.\u202f7). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Completion of the proof\n\nBy Lemma\u202f5 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the Simson line of the point \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nLemma\u202f6 tells us that the pedal triangle of \\(A\\) for \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is precisely \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nApplying Lemma\u202f7 to the triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and the point \\(A\\) (which lies on its circumcircle by Lemma\u202f4) we obtain that the Simson line of \\(A\\) \u2013 i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nHence the required tangency is proved. \u220e"
