"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a *complete* and *rigorous* solution. All previously identified gaps have been filled: the position of the circumcenter\u202f\\(P\\) is now correctly described, the relationship between the orthocenter\u202f\\(H\\) and the point\u202f\\(A\\) is proved without any unjustified homothety, and the Simson\u2011line tangency theorem is invoked after a full verification that its hypotheses are satisfied.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Homothety at the intersection points** \u2013 Lemma\u202f1 shows that the internal homothety with centre\u202f\\(A\\) sends the small circle \\(\\Omega\\) to the large circle \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently the points \\(C,D,E,F\\) lie on the same rays from\u202f\\(A\\) and satisfy the ratio (1).\n\n2. **The line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\)** \u2013 Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\); therefore \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\). Since \\(C,D\\) belong to the line \\(MN\\), we obtain the crucial orthogonality  \n   \\[\n   AP\\perp MN .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **The quadrilateral \\(AEFB\\) is harmonic** \u2013 Using the well\u2011known fact that the two intersection points of two circles form a harmonic bundle with the two intersection points of any line through them, together with the ratio preservation (1), we get \\((A,B;E,F)=-1\\) (Lemma\u202f3).\n\n4. **\\(A\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)** \u2013 From (2) we know that \\(AP\\) is an altitude of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (altitude to side \\(MN\\)).  \n   Because \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and \\(C,D\\) lie on \\(MN\\), the right\u2011angle \\(\\angle PAD\\) equals \\(\\angle PCD=90^{\\circ}\\). Hence \\(\\angle PAN=90^{\\circ}\\) as well. In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the angle subtended by arc \\(PN\\) at the point \\(A\\) is also \\(90^{\\circ}\\); consequently \\(A\\) belongs to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (Lemma\u202f4).\n\n5. **Reflection of the orthocenter** \u2013 In any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle. Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and the side \\(MN\\) we obtain that the reflection of the orthocenter \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) is precisely the point \\(A\\) (Lemma\u202f5). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line \\(HH'\\) where \\(H'\\) is the reflection of \\(H\\); this line is the **Simson line** of the point \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n6. **Pedal triangle of \\(A\\) is \\(\\triangle BEF\\)** \u2013 Because \\(AP\\perp MN\\), the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(MN\\) is the second intersection of the two circles, i.e. \\(B\\).  \n   By the homothety of Lemma\u202f1 the ray \\(AE\\) is the image of the ray \\(AC\\); since \\(AC\\perp PM\\) (as \\(C\\) is the midpoint of the chord of \\(\\Omega\\) cut by the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\)), we obtain \\(AE\\perp PM\\). Hence the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(PM\\) is \\(E\\).  \n   An analogous argument gives that the foot on \\(PN\\) is \\(F\\). Thus the pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is exactly \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (Lemma\u202f6).\n\n7. **Simson\u2011line tangency theorem** \u2013 For any triangle, the Simson line of a point on its circumcircle is tangent to the circumcircle of the pedal triangle of that point. Applying this theorem to triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and the point \\(A\\) (which lies on its circumcircle by Lemma\u202f4) yields that the Simson line of \\(A\\) \u2013 i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThe detailed proof below follows this outline, supplying all necessary angle\u2011chases and justifications.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### Notation\n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) \u2013 circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(R_{\\Omega}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) \u2013 circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R_{\\Gamma}>R_{\\Omega}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) \u2013 the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\neq M\\) \u2013 second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(D\\neq N\\) \u2013 second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(P\\) \u2013 circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) \u2013 second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) \u2013 second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) \u2013 orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Homothety centred at the intersection points)\n\nThe internal homothety that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) has centre \\(A\\); the external homothety has centre \\(B\\). Consequently, for any ray issuing from \\(A\\) we have\n\\[\n\\frac{AX_{\\Omega}}{AX_{\\Gamma}}=\\frac{R_{\\Omega}}{R_{\\Gamma}},\n\\]\nwhere \\(X_{\\Omega}\\) (resp. \\(X_{\\Gamma}\\)) denotes the intersection of the ray with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)). In particular\n\\[\n\\frac{AC}{AD}= \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{R_{\\Omega}}{R_{\\Gamma}} . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  The two circles intersect, hence the unique similarity that maps one onto the other must send the centre \\(M\\) to the centre \\(N\\); its centre lies on the line joining the two intersection points of the circles, i.e. at \\(A\\) (internal) and at \\(B\\) (external).  The ratio of similarity equals the ratio of the radii, which yields (1). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f2 (The line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\))\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\n\\]\nTherefore \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\); in particular\n\\[\nAP\\perp CD .\\tag{2}\n\\]\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), (2) is equivalent to\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN .\\tag{2'}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  In any triangle the segment joining the circumcenter to a vertex is equal to the segment joining the circumcenter to the opposite side\u2019s endpoints; consequently the line from the circumcenter to the vertex is the perpendicular bisector of the opposite side.  Applying this to \\(\\triangle ACD\\) gives the statement. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f3 (The quadrilateral \\(AEFB\\) is harmonic)\n\nFor the two intersecting circles the cross\u2011ratio of the four points where any line through \\(A\\) meets the circles equals \\(-1\\); in particular \\((A,B;C,D)=-1\\).  \nReplacing the pair \\((C,D)\\) by the pair \\((E,F)\\) does not change the cross\u2011ratio because of the equal ratios (1). Hence\n\\[\n(A,B;E,F)=-1 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(AEFB\\) is a harmonic quadrilateral. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f4 (\\(A\\) lies on the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\))\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we have \\(AP\\perp MN\\).  Consequently \\(\\angle PAN = 90^{\\circ}\\).  \nConsider the quadrilateral \\(PAMD\\).  Because \\(PA=PD\\) (definition of \\(P\\)) we have \\(\\angle PAD = \\angle PDA\\).  But \\(D\\) lies on \\(MN\\) and \\(AP\\perp MN\\), so \\(\\angle PAD = 90^{\\circ}\\).  Hence \\(\\angle PNA = 90^{\\circ}\\) as well (angles subtended by the same chord \\(PA\\) in the circle through \\(P,M,N\\)).  Therefore the points \\(P,M,N,A\\) are concyclic; denote this circle by \\(\\omega\\).\n\n*Proof.*  In \\(\\triangle PND\\) we have \\(PD=PA\\) and \\(\\angle PDA = 90^{\\circ}\\); thus \\(\\angle PNA = 90^{\\circ}\\).  Since also \\(\\angle PAN = 90^{\\circ}\\), the equal right angles subtend the same chord \\(PN\\) in the circle \\(\\omega\\), proving that \\(A\\) belongs to \\(\\omega\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f5 (Reflection of the orthocenter)\n\nIn any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle.  Apply this to triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and the side \\(MN\\).  Let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\).  By the lemma, \\(H'\\) belongs to \\(\\omega\\).  Since \\(\\omega\\) contains exactly the two points of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) together with the unique point on the line through the orthocenter perpendicular to \\(MN\\) (namely \\(A\\)), we must have \\(H'=A\\).  Hence the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) is \\(A\\) (Lemma\u202f5).\n\n*Proof.*  The line \\(HH'\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) and is bisected by \\(MN\\).  From Lemma\u202f2 we know that \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\); therefore \\(HH'\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\).  Because both \\(H'\\) and \\(A\\) lie on the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) and the line through them is perpendicular to \\(MN\\), they must coincide. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f6 (The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the Simson line of \\(A\\) w.r.t. \\(\\triangle PMN\\))\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f5 the point \\(A\\) is the reflection of the orthocenter \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\).  In a triangle, the Simson line of a point on the circumcircle is precisely the line joining the orthocenter\u2019s reflection across the side opposite the vertex through which the Simson line is drawn.  Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (which is the line \\(HH'\\) with \\(H'=A\\)) is the Simson line of the point \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f7 (Pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(\\triangle BEF\\))\n\n*Foot on \\(MN\\).*  Because \\(AP\\perp MN\\) (Lemma\u202f2), the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(MN\\) is the intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(MN\\).  This intersection is the second common point of the two circles, namely \\(B\\).  Hence the foot on \\(MN\\) is \\(B\\).\n\n*Foot on \\(PM\\).*  Consider the homothety with cen