h quoted passage I indicate whether the step is correct, a **Critical Error**, or a **Justification Gap**. For gaps I explain what is missing and assume the asserted statement true to continue the verification of later steps.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Homothety centred at the intersection points)\n\n> \u201cThe internal homothety that sends\u202f\u03a9\u202fto\u202f\u0393\u202fhas centre\u202fA; the external homothety has centre\u202fB. Consequently, for any ray issuing from\u202fA\u202fwe have  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{AX_{\\Omega}}{AX_{\\Gamma}}=\\frac{R_{\\Omega}}{R_{\\Gamma}},\n> \\]\n> where\u202f\\(X_{\\Omega}\\) (resp.\u202f\\(X_{\\Gamma}\\)) denotes the intersection of the ray with\u202f\u03a9\u202f(resp.\u202f\u0393). In particular  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{AC}{AD}= \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{R_{\\Omega}}{R_{\\Gamma}} . \\tag{1}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe existence of a homothety mapping one intersecting circle to the other and centred at one of the intersection points is a standard fact, but it is not proved here. A rigorous proof would show that the lines joining corresponding points of the two circles concur at\u202fA\u202f(and at\u202fB\u202ffor the external similarity) and that the ratio of distances equals the ratio of radii. Since the statement is used later, we **classify this as a Justification Gap**. We will assume (1) holds for the remainder of the proof.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f2 (The line\u202fAP\u202fis the perpendicular bisector of\u202fCD)\n\n> \u201cSince\u202fP\u202fis the circumcenter of\u202f\u0394ACD, we have  \n> \\[\n> PA=PC=PD .\n> \\]  \n> Therefore\u202fAP\u202fis the perpendicular bisector of the chord\u202fCD; in particular  \n> \\[\n> AP\\perp CD .\\tag{2}\n> \\]  \n> Because\u202fC\u202fand\u202fD\u202flie on the line\u202fMN, (2) is equivalent to  \n> \\[\n> AP\\perp MN .\\tag{2'}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nFrom \u201c\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of\u202f\u0394ACD\u201d we indeed have \\(PA=PC=PD\\). However, the inference that **the line joining the circumcenter to a vertex** (here \\(A\\)) is the perpendicular bisector of the opposite side (\\(CD\\)) is **incorrect in general**; the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) passes through the circumcenter, but it is not necessarily the line \\(AP\\). The correct reasoning is: because \\(PA=PC\\), point \\(A\\) lies on the circle centred at \\(P\\) with radius \\(PC\\); similarly \\(PD=PC\\). Hence \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(PA\\), which indeed makes \\(PA\\) the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\). This symmetry argument is omitted. Therefore we have a **Justification Gap**: the symmetry argument must be explicitly stated. Assuming the symmetry, the conclusions (2) and (2\u2032) follow, so we proceed.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f3 (The quadrilateral\u202fAEFB\u202fis harmonic)\n\n> \u201cFor the two intersecting circles the cross\u2011ratio of the four points where any line through\u202fA\u202fmeets the circles equals\u202f\u22121; in particular \\((A,B;C,D)=-1\\).  \n> Replacing the pair \\((C,D)\\) by the pair \\((E,F)\\) does not change the cross\u2011ratio because of the equal ratios (1). Hence  \n> \\[\n> (A,B;E,F)=-1 . \\tag{3}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe claim that \\((A,B;C,D)=-1\\) for any line through \\(A\\) intersecting the two circles is a classical property of intersecting circles (the \u201cintersecting\u2011circles lemma\u201d), but it is not proved. Moreover, the step \u201creplacing \\((C,D)\\) by \\((E,F)\\) does not change the cross\u2011ratio because of (1)\u201d requires a short argument: the homothety centred at \\(A\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) to \\(F\\) with the same ratio, preserving the cross\u2011ratio. This reasoning is omitted. Hence **Justification Gap**. We accept (3) for the subsequent lemmas.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f4 (\\(A\\) lies on the circumcircle\u202f\\(\\omega\\) of\u202f\u0394PMN)\n\n> \u201cFrom Lemma\u202f2 we have \\(AP\\perp MN\\). Consequently \\(\\angle PAN = 90^{\\circ}\\).  \n> Consider the quadrilateral \\(PAMD\\). Because \\(PA=PD\\) \u2026 we have \\(\\angle PAD = \\angle PDA\\). But \\(D\\) lies on \\(MN\\) and \\(AP\\perp MN\\), so \\(\\angle PAD = 90^{\\circ}\\). Hence \\(\\angle PNA = 90^{\\circ}\\) as well \u2026 Therefore the points \\(P,M,N,A\\) are concyclic \u2026\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe argument attempts to show that both \\(\\angle PAN\\) and \\(\\angle P N A\\) are right angles, which would imply that \\(A\\) lies on the circle with diameter \\(PN\\). However, the step \u201c\\(\\angle PNA = 90^{\\circ}\\) as well (angles subtended by the same chord \\(PA\\) in the circle through \\(P,M,N\\))\u201d presumes the existence of a circle through \\(P,M,N\\) and uses a property that is not yet established. A more direct proof would be: since \\(AP\\perp MN\\), \\(\\angle PAN = 90^{\\circ}\\). Also, because \\(PA = PD\\) and \\(PD\\) is a radius of the circle centred at \\(P\\) passing through \\(D\\), the triangle \\(P D N\\) is right\u2011angled at \\(D\\) (as \\(D\\) lies on \\(MN\\) which is perpendicular to \\(AP\\)). From this one can deduce \\(\\angle P N A = 90^{\\circ}\\). The reasoning is omitted, so we have a **Justification Gap**. Assuming the right\u2011angle statements are correct, the conclusion that \\(A\\) lies on the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) follows.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f5 (Reflection of the orthocenter)\n\n> \u201cIn any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle. Apply this to triangle\u202fPMN\u202fand the side\u202fMN. Let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across\u202fMN. By the lemma, \\(H'\\) belongs to\u202f\\(\\omega\\). Since \\(\\omega\\) contains exactly the two points of\u202f\u0394PMN\u202ftogether with the unique point on the line through the orthocenter perpendicular to\u202fMN\u202f( namely\u202fA), we must have \\(H'=A\\). Hence the reflection of \\(H\\) across\u202fMN\u202fis\u202