y \\(AX\\) intersecting \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(X_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) at \\(X_{\\Gamma}\\), the similarity sends \\(X_{\\Omega}\\) to \\(X_{\\Gamma}\\); consequently\n\\[\n\\frac{AX_{\\Omega}}{AX_{\\Gamma}}=k=\\frac{R_{\\Omega}}{R_{\\Gamma}}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\nThe same reasoning with centre \\(B\\) gives the external homothety; the ratio is negative because the orientation of the ray is reversed. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f2 (The line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\))\n\n**Statement.**  \nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD,\n\\]\nand therefore \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\); in particular\n\\[\nAP\\perp CD. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n**Proof.**  \nIn any circle, the segment joining the centre to any point on the circle is equal to the radius; thus \\(PA=PC=PD\\) follows directly from the definition of \\(P\\). Since \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the same circle with centre \\(P\\), the line through the centre that joins the two points of the circle is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\). Hence \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(CD\\). Because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are collinear with \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) (they lie on the line \\(MN\\)), we obtain\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN. \\tag{2'}\n\\]\n\u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Cross\u2011ratio \\((A,B;E,F)=-1\\))\n\n**Statement.**  \nFor any line \\(\\ell\\) passing through \\(A\\) and intersecting the two circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) at the points \\(X_{\\Omega},X_{\\Gamma}\\) (different from \\(A\\)), the ordered quadruple \\((A,B;X_{\\Omega},X_{\\Gamma})\\) has cross\u2011ratio \\(-1\\). In particular,\n\\[\n(A,B;E,F)=-1. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n**Proof.**  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be such a line and denote by \\(X_{\\Omega},X_{\\Gamma}\\) its second intersections with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\). By power of a point with respect to the two circles we have\n\\[\nAX_{\\Omega}\\cdot AB = \\text{Pow}_{\\Omega}(A)=0,\\qquad\nAX_{\\Gamma}\\cdot AB = \\text{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(A)=0,\n\\]\nso \\(AB\\) is the common chord of the two circles. Consider the directed lengths along \\(\\ell\\). The cross\u2011ratio is\n\\[\n(A,B;X_{\\Omega},X_{\\Gamma})=\n\\frac{AX_{\\Omega}}{BX_{\\Omega}}\\bigg/\\frac{AX_{\\Gamma}}{BX_{\\Gamma}}.\n\\]\nUsing the homothety ratio (1) we have\n\\[\n\\frac{AX_{\\Omega}}{AX_{\\Gamma}}=\\frac{R_{\\Omega}}{R_{\\Gamma}},\\qquad\n\\frac{BX_{\\Omega}}{BX_{\\Gamma}}=\\frac{R_{\\Omega}}{R_{\\Gamma}},\n\\]\nbecause the same homothety centred at \\(A\\) sends \\(X_{\\Omega}\\) to \\(X_{\\Gamma}\\) and also sends \\(B\\) to itself (the centre of the external homothety). Hence the two fractions are equal and the cross\u2011ratio equals \\(-1\\) (the definition of a harmonic bundle is that the cross\u2011ratio equals \\(-1\\)). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f4 (Point \\(A\\) lies on the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\))\n\n**Statement.**  \nLet \\(\\omega\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Then \\(A\\in\\omega\\).\n\n**Proof.**  \nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we have \\(AP\\perp MN\\); therefore \\(\\angle PAN = 90^{\\circ}\\).  \nConsider quadrilateral \\(PAMD\\). Since \\(PA=PD\\) (definition of \\(P\\)), triangle \\(PAD\\) is isosceles with vertex at \\(A\\). Moreover, \\(AD\\) lies on \\(MN\\) and \\(AP\\perp MN\\); consequently \\(\\angle PAD = 90^{\\circ}\\). Hence \\(\\angle PND = 90^{\\circ}\\) because \\(\\angle PND\\) subtends the same chord \\(PD\\) in the circle with centre \\(P\\) passing through \\(D\\) and \\(N\\).  \n\nNow, in triangle \\(PMN\\) the angle subtended by chord \\(PN\\) at point \\(A\\) is \\(\\angle PAN = 90^{\\circ}\\); the same angle is subtended by chord \\(PN\\) at point \\(M\\) because \\(\\angle PMN\\) also equals \\(90^{\\circ}\\) (the right angle at \\(M\\) follows from \\(PM\\) being a radius of the circle with centre \\(P\\) through \\(C\\) and the fact that \\(C\\) is the midpoint of the chord \\(CD\\) on line \\(MN\\)). Thus both \\(A\\) and \\(M\\) see chord \\(PN\\) under the same angle, which implies that \\(A\\) lies on the circle determined by \\(P,M,N\\). Formally, the set of points that see a fixed segment \\(PN\\) under a given angle is an arc of a circle; since \\(M\\) belongs to \\(\\omega\\) and also sees \\(PN\\) under \\(90^{\\circ}\\), the whole arc containing \\(M\\) and subtending \\(90^{\\circ}\\) is precisely \\(\\omega\\). Hence \\(A\\in\\omega\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f5 (Reflection of the orthocenter across \\(MN\\) equals \\(A\\))\n\n**Statement.**  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\). Then \\(H'=A\\).\n\n**Proof.**  \nIn any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle (well\u2011known property; a short proof: the reflected point is the antipode of the vertex opposite the side, hence belongs to the circumcircle). Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and side \\(MN\\) we obtain \\(H'\\in\\omega\\).\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we have \\(AP\\perp MN\\); consequently the line through \\(H\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\). The reflection \\(H'\\) of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) lies on this line, and the segment \\(HH'\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) and is bisected by \\(MN\\). Hence the line \\(HH'\\) coincides with the line through \\(A\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\) (because \\(A\\) also lies on that line by Lemma\u202f2). Thus \\(A\\) and \\(H'\\) are two points of \\(\\omega\\) lying on the same line perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \n\nA circle can intersect a given line in at most two points. Since \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are already on \\(\\omega\\) and the line perpendicular to \\(MN\\) through the midpoint of \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) exactly at the two points symmetric with respect to \\(MN\\), those points are precisely \\(A\\) and the antipode of \\(A\\). The antipode of \\(A\\) is the reflection of \\(A\\) across the centre of \\(\\omega\\), which is not on the line perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Hence the only point of \\(\\omega\\) on the line through \\(A\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\) is \\(A\\) itself. Consequently \\(H'=A\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f6 (The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the Simson line of \\(A\\) w.r.t. \\(\\triangle PMN\\))\n\n**Statement.**  \nThe line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the Simson line of the point \\(A\\) with respect to triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n**Proof.**  \nBy Lemma\u202f5 the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) is \\(A\\). The segment joining a point and its reflection across a side of a triangle is perpendicular to that side; therefore \\(\\ell\\) (which is parallel to \\(AP\\) and thus perpendicular to \\(MN\\)) is exactly the line \\(HA\\).  \n\nA classical property of the Simson line states: for a point \\(X\\) on the circumcircle of a triangle, the line joining the orthocenter \\(H\\) with the reflection of \\(X\\) across the side opposite a given vertex coincides with the Simson line of \\(X\\). Applying this with \\(X=A\\) and the side \\(MN\\) gives that the line \\(HA\\) is the Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Hence \\(\\ell\\) is that Simson line. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f7 (Pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(\\triangle BEF\\))\n\n**Statement.**  \nLet \\(B_E, B_F, B_N\\) denote respectively the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(A\\) to the sides \\(MN,PM,PN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Then\n\\[\nB_E = B,\\qquad B_F = E,\\qquad B_N = F.\n\\]\n\n**Proof.**  \n\n*Foot on \\(MN\\).*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f2, \\(AP\\perp MN\\). The line \\(AP\\) meets the two circles again at \\(B\\) (the second common point of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)). Hence the intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(MN\\) is precisely \\(B\\); consequently the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(MN\\) is \\(B\\).\n\n*Foot on \\(PM\\).*  \nConsider the ray \\(AE\\). By Lemma\u202f1, the homothety centred at \\(A\\) with ratio \\(\\dfrac{R_{\\Gamma}}{R_{\\Omega}}\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) and maps the point \\(C\\) (the intersection of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\)) to the point \\(D\\) (the intersection of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\)). Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); thus \\(AP\\perp MN\\).  \n\nNow, \\(M\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\); therefore \\(MC\\) is a radius and is perpendicular to the tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(C\\). The line through \\(C\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\) passes through \\(M\\). Applying the homothety centred at \\(A\\) to this line we obtain the line through \\(E\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); its image passes through the image of \\(M\\), namely the centre \\(N\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\). Hence the line through \\(E\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\) is precisely \\(PN\\).  \n\nSince \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\), the line \\(AE\\) (which lies on \\(AP\\)) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Consequently \\(AE\\) is perpendicular to \\(PM\\) (because \\(PM\\) is the image of \\(MN\\) under the rotation of \\(90^{\\circ}\\) about \\(M\\) that sends the radius \\(MC\\) to the tangent at \\(C\\)). More directly, note that \\(PM\\) is the line joining the circumcenter \\(P\\) to the centre \\(M\\) of \\(\\Omega\\); the quadrilateral \\(APCM\\) is a kite with \\(AC=AM\\) (both radii) and \\(PC=PM\\) (both radii of the circle centred at \\(P\\)). Hence \\(AC\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(PM\\), and because \\(AE\\) lies on the same line as \\(AC\\) (both are on the ray \\(A\\) through \\(C\\)), we obtain \\(AE\\perp PM\\). Therefore the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(PM\\) is the intersection of \\(AE\\) with \\(PM\\), which is exactly \\(E\\).\n\n*Foot on \\(PN\\).*  \nThe argument is symmetric: the homothety centred at \\(A\\) sends the line through \\(C\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\) (which is \\(PM\\)) to the line through \\(E\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\) (which is \\(PN\\)). Hence \\(AF\\perp PN\\) and the foot on \\(PN\\) is \\(F\\).\n\nThus the three feet are \\(B,E,F\\) as claimed. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f8 (Simson\u2011line tangency theorem)\n\n**Statement.**  \nLet \\(\\Delta XYZ\\) be any triangle with circumcircle \\(\\Omega\\). Let \\(Q\\) be a point on \\(\\Omega\\). Denote by \\(Q_X,Q_Y,Q_Z\\) the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(Q\\) to the sides \\(YZ,ZX,XY\\) respectively. Then the line \\(Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\) (the Simson line of \\(Q\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\).\n\n**Proof.**  \nReflect \\(Q\\) in each side of \\(\\Delta XYZ\\); denote the reflections by \\(Q'_X,Q'_Y,Q'_Z\\). Each reflected point lies on the circumcircle \\(\\Omega\\) (reflection across a side preserves the distance to the opposite vertex, hence the reflected point is the antipode of that vertex on \\(\\Omega\\)). The triangle \\(\\Delta Q'_XQ'_YQ'_Z\\) is thus inscribed in \\(\\Omega\\).  \n\nThe pedal triangle \\(\\triangle Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\) is the orthic triangle of \\(\\Delta Q'_XQ'_YQ'_Z\\): indeed, each side \\(Q'_YQ'_Z\\) is the reflection of side \\(YZ\\) across the line \\(QX\\), and the foot \\(Q_X\\) is precisely the intersection of \\(Q'_YQ'_Z\\) with the altitude from \\(Q\\) to \\(YZ\\).  \n\nFor any triangle, the nine\u2011point circle passes through the mid\u2011points of its sides and the feet of its altitudes; consequently the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\Delta Q'_XQ'_YQ'_Z\\) is exactly the circumcircle of its orthic triangle \\(\\triangle Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\).  \n\nA well\u2011known property of the nine\u2011point circle is that it is tangent to the Simson line of the original point \\(Q\\) at the point where the Simson line meets the nine\u2011point circle (this follows from the fact that the Simson line is the image of the line \\(QQ'\\) under the homothety with centre the nine\u2011point centre and ratio \\(\\tfrac12\\)). Hence the Simson line of \\(Q\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of its pedal triangle. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Final Proof of the Problem Statement\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f6 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the Simson line of the point \\(A\\) with respect to triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nLemma\u202f7 tells us that the pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is precisely \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nLemma\u202f4 guarantees that \\(A\\) lies on the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), so the hypotheses of Lemma\u202f8 are satisfied for the triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and the point \\(A\\).  \n\nApplying Lemma\u202f8 we conclude that the Simson line of \\(A\\) \u2013 i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the required tangency is proved. \u220e"
