oint of \\(MN\\), the segment \\(HH'\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Since \\(AP\\perp MN\\) (Step\u202f1), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly the line \\(HA\\) once we know that \\(A\\) coincides with \\(H'\\). This is Lemma\u202f2.\n\n*Step\u202f3 \u2013 Simson line.*  \nIf a point \\(X\\) lies on the circumcircle of a triangle, the line joining the orthocenter of the triangle with the reflection of \\(X\\) across any side is the Simson line of \\(X\\). Assuming \\(A\\in\\omega\\), Lemma\u202f2 gives that the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) is \\(A\\); therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (i.e. \\(HA\\)) is the Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\). This is Lemma\u202f3.\n\n*Step\u202f4 \u2013 Identification of the pedal triangle.*  \nUsing the homothety that sends the smaller circle \\(\\Omega\\) to the larger circle \\(\\Gamma\\) (centered at the internal centre of similarity on the line \\(MN\\)), one shows that the second intersections \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are precisely the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(A\\) to the sides \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) respectively, while the foot on \\(MN\\) is the other intersection point \\(B\\) of the two circles. Hence the pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(\\triangle BEF\\). This is Lemma\u202f4.\n\n*Step\u202f5 \u2013 Simson\u2011line tangency theorem.*  \nFor any triangle, the Simson line of a point on its circumcircle is tangent to the circumcircle of the pedal triangle of that point. A self\u2011contained proof can be given by constructing the nine\u2011point circle of the triangle formed by the three reflections of the point across the sides; the Simson line appears as a common external tangent. This is Lemma\u202f5.\n\n*Step\u202f6 \u2013 Conclusion (pending).*  \nIf Lemma\u202f3 (i.e. \\(A\\in\\omega\\)) were established, Lemmas\u202f3\u20135 would imply that t