n the circumcircle. Applying this to side \\(MN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) we obtain that the reflection \\(H'\\) of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) belongs to \\(\\omega\\). Because \\(O\\) is the midpoint of \\(MN\\), the reflection across \\(MN\\) is the half\u2011turn about \\(O\\); thus \\(H'\\) is the image of \\(H\\) under a rotation of \\(180^{\\circ}\\) about \\(O\\). Since \\(AP\\) is the line through \\(O\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\) (by (1)), the half\u2011turn sends the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) onto the line \\(HA'\\) where \\(A'=H'\\). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly the line \\(HA'\\).\n\n   Because of Lemma\u202f2, the only point of \\(\\omega\\) that is symmetric to \\(H\\) with respect to \\(MN\\) is the point \\(A\\) itself (the antipode of the vertex opposite \\(MN\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\)). Hence \\(H'=A\\) and we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is }HA.}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   In other words, \\(HA\\) is the Simson line of the point \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n4. **Pedal triangle of \\(A\\) (Lemma\u202f4).**  \n   Let \\(X\\) be the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(MN\\). Because \\(AP\\perp MN\\) (Lemma\u202f1), the foot is the intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(MN\\). The two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); the line \\(MN\\) is the common internal centre\u2011line of the two circles, therefore the second intersection of \\(MN\\) with the circles is precisely \\(B\\). Hence \\(X=B\\).\n\n   Next, let \\(Y\\) be the foot from \\(A\\) to \\(PM\\). Since \\(M\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\), the radius \\(MA\\) is perpendicular to the tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\). The line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\); by definition \\(AE\\) is a chord of \\(\\Omega\\) passing through \\(A\\). The right\u2011angle at \\(A\\) between \\(MA\\) and the tangent implies that \\(AE\\) is symmetric to the tangent with respect to the line \\(AM\\); consequently \\(AE\\) is perpendicular to the line through \\(M\\) that is symmetric to the tangent, i.e. to \\(PM\\). Therefore \\(AE\\perp PM\\) and the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(PM\\) is the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\), namely \\(E\\).\n\n   An entirely analogous argument, using the centre \\(N\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\), shows that the foot from \\(A\\) to \\(PN\\) is the second intersection \\(F\\) of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n   Consequently the pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is precisely \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n5. **Simson\u2011line tangency (Lemma\u202f5).**  \n   *Statement.* For any triangle \\(\\Delta XYZ\\) and any point \\(Q\\) on its circumcircle, the Simson line of \\(Q\\) (the line joining the three feet of the perpendiculars from \\(Q\\) to the sides) is tangent to the circumcircle of the pedal triangle of \\(Q\\).\n\n   *Proof.* Let \\(Q_X,Q_Y,Q_Z\\) be the three feet. Reflect \\(Q\\) across the sides \\(YZ,ZX,XY\\); denote the reflections by \\(Q'_X,Q'_Y,Q'_Z\\). Each reflected point lies on the circumcircle (the reflection of a point on a chord across the chord is the antipode of the opposite vertex). The triangle \\(\\Delta Q'_XQ'_YQ'_Z\\) is therefore inscribed in the same circumcircle as \\(\\Delta XYZ\\).\n\n   The triangle \\(\\Delta Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\) is the orthic triangle of \\(\\Delta Q'_XQ'_YQ'_Z\\): indeed, \\(Q_X\\) is the foot of the altitude from \\(Q'_X\\) onto side \\(Q'_YQ'_Z\\), etc. For any triangle, the nine\u2011point circle passes through the mid\u2011points of its sides and through the feet of its altitudes; consequently the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\Delta Q'_XQ'_YQ'_Z\\) coincides with the circumcircle of its orthic triangle \\(\\Delta Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\).\n\n   The Simson line of \\(Q\\) with respect to \\(\\Delta XYZ\\) is the line joining the three points \\(Q_X,Q_Y,Q_Z\\). A