(AP\\) onto the line \\(HA'\\).  \n\nThe only point of \\(\\omega\\) that is symmetric to \\(H\\) with respect to \\(MN\\) is the vertex opposite the side \\(MN\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\), namely \\(A\\). Therefore \\(H'=A\\) and the image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line \\(HA\\). Consequently the original line itself is \\(HA\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4.  The pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **Foot on \\(MN\\).**  \n   The foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(MN\\) is the intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(MN\\) because \\(AP\\perp MN\\) (Lemma\u202f1). The line \\(MN\\) is the line of centres of the two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); its second intersection with the circles is precisely the point \\(B\\). Hence the foot is \\(B\\).\n\n2. **Foot on \\(PM\\).**  \n   Since \\(M\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\), the radius \\(MA\\) is perpendicular to the tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\). The chord \\(AE\\) is symmetric to that tangent with respect to the line \\(AM\\); therefore \\(AE\\) is perpendicular to the line through \\(M\\) that is symmetric to the tangent, i.e. to \\(PM\\). Thus the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(PM\\) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\), namely \\(E\\).\n\n3. **Foot on \\(PN\\).**  \n   An identical argument using the centre \\(N\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) shows that the foot from \\(A\\) to \\(PN\\) is the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\), namely \\(F\\).\n\nHence the three feet are \\(B,E,F\\); the pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f5.  (Simson\u2011line tangency)  \nLet \\(\\Delta XYZ\\) be any triangle and let \\(Q\\) be 