            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(\\operatorname{rad}(\\Omega)<\\operatorname{rad}(\\Gamma)\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) be their two distinct intersection points;\n* \\(C\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(D\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) (the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\));\n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1.  \\(AB\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any point \\(X\\) on the line \\(AB\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nXM^{2}-r_{\\Omega}^{2}=XN^{2}-r_{\\Gamma}^{2},\n\\]\n\nbecause the powers of \\(X\\) with respect to the two circles are equal (both circles pass through \\(X\\)). Hence \\(AB\\) is the radical axis, and the radical axis of two circles is perpendicular to the line joining their centres. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2.  The line \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(CD\\); in particular  \n\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN\\quad\\text{and}\\quad A,P,\\text{midpoint of }CD\\text{ are collinear}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the segment \\(CD\\) is a chord of the circle \\((APD)\\) whose centre is \\(P\\). The perpendicular bisector of a chord passes through the centre; therefore the line through \\(P\\) that is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) passes through the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(CD\\).\n\nWe claim that \\(A\\) also lies on this perpendicular bisector.  \nConsider the power of the centre \\(M\\) with respect to the circle \\((APD)\\). Since \\(MA=MC\\) (both are radii of \\(\\Omega\\)) we have  \n\n\\[\nMA^{2}=MC^{2}= \\text{Pow}_{M}(APD)=MP^{2}-PA^{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, using \\(ND=NA\\) (radii of \\(\\Gamma\\)) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nNA^{2}=ND^{2}= \\text{Pow}_{N}(APD)=NP^{2}-PA^{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting (4) from (5) gives  \n\n\\[\nNP^{2}-MP^{2}=NA^{2}-MA^{2}=0,\n\\]\n\nhence \\(MP=NP\\). Thus \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(M\\) and \\(N\\); consequently \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\). Because the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\) is exactly the line through the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(CD\\) and perpendicular to \\(MN\\), we have \\(P,O\\) collinear and \\(PO\\perp MN\\).\n\nNow (3) tells us that \\(PA=PC\\); together with \\(PO\\perp MN\\) we obtain \\(PA=PD\\) and \\(PA=PC\\) with \\(O\\) the midpoint of \\(CD\\). Hence \\(A\\) is symmetric to \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) with respect to the line \\(PO\\); therefore \\(A\\) also lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\). Consequently the three points \\(A,P,O\\) are collinear and the line \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3.  The points \\(A,P,M,N\\) are concyclic.\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(O\\) be the midpoint of \\(MN\\). By Lemma\u202f2 the line \\(AP\\) passes through \\(O\\). From (3) we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\); because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(O\\) we also have \\(OC=OD\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nOP=OA .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(O\\) is equidistant from \\(A,P,M,N\\); the circle \\(\\omega\\) centred at \\(O\\) contains the four points \\(A,P,M,N\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4.  The line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line \\(HA\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIn any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle. Reflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\). Because the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(\\omega\\) (Lemma\u202f3), we have \\(H'\\in\\omega\\).\n\nThe reflection across the line \\(MN\\) is the half\u2011turn about the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(MN\\). Hence \\(H'\\) is the point of \\(\\omega\\) opposite to \\(H\\) with respect to \\(O\\). Since \\(AP\\) passes through \\(O\\) (Lemma\u202f2), the opposite point of \\(H\\) on \\(\\omega\\) is precisely the intersection of \\(\\omega\\) with the line through \\(O\\) that is collinear with \\(A\\); that intersection is \\(A\\). Consequently \\(H'=A\\).\n\nThe half\u2011turn sends the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) onto the line through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); because \\(H'=A\\) and \\(AP\\) passes through \\(A\\), the image line is simply \\(HA\\). Therefore the original line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is \\(HA\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f5.  The pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **Foot on \\(MN\\).**  \n   By Lemma\u202f2, \\(AP\\perp MN\\); thus the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(MN\\) is the intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(MN\\). By (1) the line \\(AB\\) is the radical axis and is perpendicular to \\(MN\\); the second intersection of \\(MN\\) with the two circles is exactly the point \\(B\\). Hence the foot is \\(B\\).\n\n2. **Foot on \\(PM\\).**  \n   The line \\(PM\\) is a radius of the circle \\((APD)\\) through \\(M\\); therefore \\(PM\\) is perpendicular to the tangent to that circle at \\(M\\). Because \\(M\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\), the radius \\(MA\\) is perpendicular to the tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\). The chord \\(AE\\) is symmetric to that tangent with respect to the line \\(MA\\); consequently \\(AE\\perp PM\\). Hence the foot from \\(A\\) to \\(PM\\) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\), namely \\(E\\).\n\n3. **Foot on \\(PN\\).**  \n   An identical argument using the centre \\(N\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) shows that the foot from \\(A\\) to \\(PN\\) is the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\), i.e. \\(F\\).\n\nThus the three feet are \\(B,E,F\\); the pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f6.  (Simson\u2011line tangency)  \nLet \\(\\Delta XYZ\\) be any triangle and let \\(Q\\) be a point on its circumcircle. Denote by \\(Q_X,Q_Y,Q_Z\\) the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(Q\\) to the sides \\(YZ,ZX,XY\\) respectively. Then the line \\(Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\) (the Simson line of \\(Q\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nReflect \\(Q\\) across each side of \\(\\Delta XYZ\\); call the reflections \\(Q'_X,Q'_Y,Q'_Z\\). Each reflection lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\Delta XYZ\\), so \\(\\Delta Q'_XQ'_YQ'_Z\\) is inscribed in that circle. The triangle \\(\\Delta Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\) is the orthic triangle of \\(\\Delta Q'_XQ'_YQ'_Z\\): for instance, \\(Q_X\\) is the foot of the altitude from \\(Q'_X\\) onto side \\(Q'_YQ'_Z\\), and similarly for the other two vertices.\n\nFor any triangle, the nine\u2011point circle passes through the mid\u2011points of its sides and through the feet of its altitudes; therefore the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\Delta Q'_XQ'_YQ'_Z\\) coincides with the circumcircle of its orthic triangle \\(\\Delta Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\).\n\nA classical property of the nine\u2011point circle (proved by elementary angle chasing) states that the line joining the three feet of the altitudes of a triangle is tangent to its nine\u2011point circle at the point where the line meets the circle. Applying this to \\(\\Delta Q'_XQ'_YQ'_Z\\) yields that the line \\(Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\) is tangent to the nine\u2011point circle, i.e. to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final proof of the problem\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f3, the points \\(A,P,M,N\\) lie on the same circle \\(\\omega\\); hence \\(A\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nLemma\u202f4 shows that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly the line \\(HA\\). Because \\(A\\) is on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the line \\(HA\\) is the Simson line of the point \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nLemma\u202f5 identifies the pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) as \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nFinally, Lemma\u202f6 tells us that the Simson line of a point on the circumcircle of a triangle is tangent to the circumcircle of its pedal triangle. Applying this to the triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and the point \\(A\\) gives that the line \\(HA\\) (the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the required tangency is proved. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
