g{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the segment \\(CD\\) is a chord of the circle \\((APD)\\) whose centre is \\(P\\). The perpendicular bisector of a chord passes through the centre; therefore the line through \\(P\\) that is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) passes through the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(CD\\).\n\nWe claim that \\(A\\) also lies on this perpendicular bisector.  \nConsider the power of the centre \\(M\\) with respect to the circle \\((APD)\\). Since \\(MA=MC\\) (both are radii of \\(\\Omega\\)) we have  \n\n\\[\nMA^{2}=MC^{2}= \\text{Pow}_{M}(APD)=MP^{2}-PA^{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, using \\(ND=NA\\) (radii of \\(\\Gamma\\)) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nNA^{2}=ND^{2}= \\text{Pow}_{N}(APD)=NP^{2}-PA^{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting (4) from (5) gives  \n\n\\[\nNP^{2}-MP^{2}=NA^{2}-MA^{2}=0,\n\\]\n\nhence \\(MP=NP\\). Thus \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(M\\) and \\(N\\); consequently \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\). Because the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\) is exactly the line through the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(CD\\) and perpendicular to \\(MN\\), we have \\(P,O\\) collinear and \\(PO\\perp MN\\).\n\nNow (3) tells us that \\(PA=PC\\); together with \\(PO\\perp MN\\) we obtain \\(PA=PD\\) and \\(PA=PC\\) with \\(O\\) the midpoint of \\(CD\\). Hence \\(A\\) is symmetric to \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) with respect to the line \\(PO\\); therefore \\(A\\) also lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\). Consequently the three points \\(A,P,O\\) are collinear and the line \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3.  The points \\(A,P,M,N\\) are concyclic.\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(O\\) be the midpoint of \\(MN\\). By Lemma\u202f2 the line \\(AP\\) passes through \\(O\\). From (3) we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\); because \\(C\\) 