\(MN\\) with the two circles is exactly the point \\(B\\). Hence the foot is \\(B\\).\n\n2. **Foot on \\(PM\\).**  \n   The line \\(PM\\) is a radius of the circle \\((APD)\\) through \\(M\\); therefore \\(PM\\) is perpendicular to the tangent to that circle at \\(M\\). Because \\(M\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\), the radius \\(MA\\) is perpendicular to the tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\). The chord \\(AE\\) is symmetric to that tangent with respect to the line \\(MA\\); consequently \\(AE\\perp PM\\). Hence the foot from \\(A\\) to \\(PM\\) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\), namely \\(E\\).\n\n3. **Foot on \\(PN\\).**  \n   An identical argument using the centre \\(N\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) shows that the foot from \\(A\\) to \\(PN\\) is the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\), i.e. \\(F\\).\n\nThus the three feet are \\(B,E,F\\); the pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f6.  (Simson\u2011line tangency)  \nLet \\(\\Delta XYZ\\) be any triangle and let \\(Q\\) be a point on its circumcircle. Denote by \\(Q_X,Q_Y,Q_Z\\) the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(Q\\) to the sides \\(YZ,ZX,XY\\) respectively. Then the line \\(Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\) (the Simson line of \\(Q\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nReflect \\(Q\\) across each side of \\(\\Delta XYZ\\); call the reflections \\(Q'_X,Q'_Y,Q'_Z\\). Each reflection lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\Delta XYZ\\), so \\(\\Delta Q'_XQ'_YQ'_Z\\) is inscribed in that circle. The triangle \\(\\Delta Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\) is the orthic triangle of \\(\\Delta Q'_XQ'_YQ'_Z\\): for instance, \\(Q_X\\) is the foot of the altitude from \\(Q'_X\\) onto side \\(Q'_YQ'_Z\\), and similarly for the other two vertices.\n\nFor any triangle, the nine\u2011point circle passes through the mid\u2011points of its sides 