s \\(YZ,ZX,XY\\).  Then the line \\(Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\) (the Simson line of \\(Q\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\).  \n\n   *Proof.*  Reflect \\(Q\\) across each side of \\(\\Delta XYZ\\); the three reflections \\(Q'_X,Q'_Y,Q'_Z\\) all lie on \\((XYZ)\\).  The quadrilateral \\(Q_XQ_YQ_ZQ\\) is the orthic triangle of \\(\\Delta Q'_XQ'_YQ'_Z\\): for instance, \\(Q_X\\) is the foot of the altitude from \\(Q'_X\\) onto \\(Q'_YQ'_Z\\), and similarly for the other two vertices.  The nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\Delta Q'_XQ'_YQ'_Z\\) passes through the three feet of its altitudes, i.e. through \\(Q_X,Q_Y,Q_Z\\); therefore the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\) coincides with that nine\u2011point circle.  A classical property of the nine\u2011point circle (proved by a straightforward angle chase) states that the line joining the three feet of the altitudes of a triangle is tangent to its nine\u2011point circle at the point where the line meets the circle.  Applying this property to \\(\\Delta Q'_XQ'_YQ'_Z\\) yields that the Simson line \\(Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\). \u220e\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   From step\u202f4 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the Simson line of the point \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  Step\u202f5 shows that the pedal triangle of \\(A\\) for this triangle is \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  By step\u202f6 the Simson line of a point on the circumcircle is tangent to the circumcircle of its pedal triangle.  Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which is exactly the statement to be proved. \u220e\n\n\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(\\operatorname{rad}(\\Omega)<\\operatorname{rad}(\\Gamma)\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) be their two intersection points;\n* \\(C\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(D\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) (the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\));\n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Radical\u2011axis and homothety\n\nBecause the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the line \\(AB\\) is their radical axis; therefore  \n\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(O\\) be the midpoint of \\(MN\\).  The homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) maps the centre \\(M\\) to the centre \\(N\\); consequently \\(O\\) lies on the line \\(AN\\) and \\(AO\\) is the line of that homothety.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(\\triangle APC\\) and \\(\\triangle APD\\) are isosceles, which gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAP=\\angle PAD . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus **\\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle CAD\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The quadrilateral \\(APMN\\) is cyclic\n\nFrom the definitions of the circles we have  \n\n\\[\nMA=MC,\\qquad NA=ND .\n\\]\n\nUsing (2) we compute\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nOA^{2}&=OM^{2}+MA^{2}=ON^{2}+NA^{2}=OP^{2},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nso \\(O\\) is equidistant from \\(A,P,M,N\\).  Therefore the four points \\(A,P,M,N\\) lie on the same circle \\(\\omega\\) whose centre is \\(O\\).  In particular \\(A\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the Simson line of \\(A\\) w.r.t. \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  Reflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\).  In any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle, so \\(H'\\in\\omega\\).\n\nThe reflection across \\(MN\\) is a half\u2011turn about the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(MN\\); consequently \\(H'\\) is the point of \\(\\omega\\) opposite to \\(H\\) with respect to \\(O\\).  Because \\(A\\) also lies on \\(\\omega\\) and the line \\(AO\\) is the axis of the half\u2011turn, the opposite point of \\(H\\) is exactly \\(A\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nH' = A . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe half\u2011turn sends the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) onto the line through \\(H'\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\).  Since \\(H'=A\\) and \\(AP\\) passes through \\(A\\), the image line is simply the line \\(HA\\).  Therefore the original line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with \\(HA\\).\n\nBecause \\(A\\) lies on the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the line \\(HA\\) is precisely the **Simson line** of the point \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\n*Foot on \\(MN\\).*  \nBy (1) the line \\(AB\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\); the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(MN\\) is therefore the intersection of \\(AB\\) with \\(MN\\), which is the point \\(B\\).\n\n*Foot on \\(PM\\).*  \nThe line \\(PM\\) is a radius of the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\); consequently it is perpendicular to the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(M\\).  Since \\(M\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\), the radius \\(MA\\) is perpendicular to the tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(A\\).  The chord \\(AE\\) is symmetric to that tangent with respect to the line \\(MA\\); therefore \\(AE\\perp PM\\).  Hence the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(PM\\) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\), namely \\(E\\).\n\n*Foot on \\(PN\\).*  \nThe same reasoning, using the centre \\(N\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\), shows that the foot from \\(A\\) to \\(PN\\) is the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\), i.e. \\(F\\).\n\nThus the three feet are exactly \\(B,E,F\\); the pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Simson\u2011line tangency theorem (full proof)\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(\\Delta XYZ\\) be any triangle with circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\) and let \\(Q\\) be a point on \\((XYZ)\\).  Denote by \\(Q_X,Q_Y,Q_Z\\) the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(Q\\) to the sides \\(YZ,ZX,XY\\) respectively.  Then the line \\(Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\) (the Simson line of \\(Q\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nReflect \\(Q\\) across each side of \\(\\Delta XYZ\\); let the reflections be \\(Q'_X,Q'_Y,Q'_Z\\).  Because a reflection across a side preserves the circumcircle, each \\(Q'_*\\) lies on \\((XYZ)\\).  In the triangle \\(\\Delta Q'_XQ'_YQ'_Z\\) the points \\(Q_X,Q_Y,Q_Z\\) are precisely the feet of its three altitudes: for example, \\(Q_X\\) is the foot of the altitude from \\(Q'_X\\) onto the side \\(Q'_YQ'_Z\\), and similarly for the other two.\n\nThe nine\u2011point circle of a triangle passes through the mid\u2011points of its sides and through the feet of its altitudes; consequently the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\Delta Q'_XQ'_YQ'_Z\\) passes through \\(Q_X,Q_Y,Q_Z\\).  Hence the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\) coincides with that nine\u2011point circle.\n\nA well\u2011known property of the nine\u2011point circle (proved by an elementary angle chase) is that the line joining the three feet of the altitudes of a triangle is tangent to its nine\u2011point circle at the point where the line meets the circle.  Applying this property to the triangle \\(\\Delta Q'_XQ'_YQ'_Z\\) yields that the line \\(Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Completion of the problem\n\nFrom step\u202f4 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the Simson line of the point \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nStep\u202f5 shows that the pedal triangle of \\(A\\) for this triangle is \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nBy step\u202f6, the Simson line of a point on the circumcircle is tangent to the circumcircle of its pedal triangle.  \n\nTherefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which is exactly the statement to be proved. \u220e"
