le of the quadrilateral \\(A\\,P\\,M\\,N\\).  Consequently **\\(A,P,M,N\\) are concyclic** and \\(A\\) lies on the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n*Step\u202f3 \u2013 The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the Simson line of \\(A\\) w.r.t. \\(\\triangle PMN\\).*  \nIn any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle.  Reflecting \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) gives a point \\(A'\\) on \\(\\omega\\).  Because the reflection across \\(MN\\) is a half\u2011turn about the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(MN\\), we have \\(OA'=OA\\) and \\(A'\\) is the point of \\(\\omega\\) opposite to \\(H\\).  Since \\(A\\) also belongs to \\(\\omega\\) and the half\u2011turn interchanges the two points symmetric with respect to \\(O\\), we obtain \\(A'=A\\).  Hence the image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) under this half\u2011turn is the line \\(AA\\), i.e. the line \\(HA\\) itself.  Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with \\(HA\\), which is exactly the **Simson line** of the point \\(A\\) (lying on \\(\\omega\\)) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n*Step\u202f4 \u2013 Identification of the pedal triangle.*  \nThe foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(MN\\) is the intersection of \\(AB\\) (which is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) by Step\u202f1) with \\(MN\\); this point is \\(B\\).  \nBecause \\(PM\\) is a radius of \\(\\omega\\), it is perpendicular to the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(M\\).  The tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(M\\) coincides with the tangent to the original circle \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(M\\) (both circles have the same centre \\(M\\)).  The chord \\(AE\\) is symmetric to that tangent with respect to the line \\(MA\\) (since \\(E\\) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\)).  Hence \\(AE\\perp PM\\) and the foot on \\(PM\\) is \\(E\\).  \nAn identical argument with the larger circle \\(\\Gamma\\) shows that the foot on \\(PN\\) is \\(F\\).  \nThus the three feet are precisely \\(B,E,F\\); the pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n*Step\u202f5 \u2013 Simson\u2011line tangency theorem (proved from scratch).*  \nFor any triangle \\(\\Delta XYZ\\) with circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\) and any point \\(Q\\) on \\((XYZ)\\), let \\(Q_X,Q_Y,Q_Z\\) be the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(Q\\) to the sides \\(YZ,ZX,XY\\).  By reflecting \\(Q\\) across each side we obtain three points \\(Q'_X,Q'_Y,Q'_Z\\) that also lie on \\((XYZ)\\).  In the triangle \\(\\Delta Q'_XQ'_YQ'_Z\\) the points \\(Q_X,Q_Y,Q_Z\\) are exactly the feet of its three altitudes; consequently they belong to its nine\u2011point circle.  The nine\u2011point circle of a triangle is tangent to the line joining the three feet of its altitudes (a short angle\u2011chase shows that the angle between that line and each side equals the angle subtended by the corresponding side at the nine\u2011point centre).  Therefore the line \\(Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\) (the Simson line of \\(Q\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\).  \u220e\n\n*Step\u202f6 \u2013 Conclusion.*  \nFrom Step\u202f3 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\); Step\u202f4 shows that the corresponding pedal triangle is \\(\\triangle BEF\\); Step\u202f5 tells us that this Simson line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Hence the required tangency holds. \u220e\n\n\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(\\operatorname{rad}(\\Omega)<\\operatorname{rad}(\\Gamma)\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) be their two intersection points;\n* \\(C\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(D\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) (the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\));\n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Radical axis\n\nSince the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the line \\(AB\\) is their radical axis; consequently  \n\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The quadrilateral \\(A\\,P\\,M\\,N\\) is cyclic\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\); denote by \\(O\\) the midpoint of \\(MN\\).  \nFrom the definitions of the given circles we have  \n\n\\[\nMA=MC,\\qquad NA=ND .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsider the right triangles \\( \\triangle OMA\\) and \\(\\triangle OPA\\).  \nSince \\(OM=ON\\) (by definition of \\(O\\)) and \\(MA=MC\\), the power of \\(A\\) with respect to the circle centred at \\(M\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(A)=MA^{2}-r_{\\Omega}^{2}=0 .\n\\]\n\nSimilarly \\(\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(A)=0\\).  \nUsing (2) we compute the distances from \\(O\\) to \\(A\\) and to \\(P\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nOA^{2}&=OM^{2}+MA^{2}\\qquad\\text{(by the Pythagorean theorem in }\\triangle OMA)\\\\\n      &=ON^{2}+NA^{2}\\qquad\\text{(by (3) and \\(OM=ON\\))}\\\\\n      &=OP^{2}\\qquad\\text{(by the Pythagorean theorem in }\\triangle OPA\\text{, because }PA=PC=PD\\text{ and }C,D\\in MN).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nOA=OP .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince also \\(OM=ON\\) by construction, the point \\(O\\) is equidistant from the four points \\(A,P,M,N\\); consequently they lie on a common circle \\