e on a common circle \\(\\omega\\) with centre \\(O\\).  In particular  \n\n\\[\nA,P,M,N\\ \\text{are concyclic}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the Simson line of \\(A\\) w.r.t. \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nReflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\); denote the image by \\(H'\\).  In any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circumcircle, therefore  \n\n\\[\nH'\\in\\omega .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe reflection across the line \\(MN\\) is a half\u2011turn about the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(MN\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nO\\text{ is the midpoint of }HH'.\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (5) the points of \\(\\omega\\) that are symmetric with respect to \\(O\\) are precisely the pairs of antipodal points on \\(\\omega\\).  From (6) and (7) we see that \\(H'\\) is the antipode of \\(H\\) on \\(\\omega\\).  Since \\(A\\) also belongs to \\(\\omega\\) and the antipode of \\(H\\) is unique, we must have  \n\n\\[\nH'=A .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus the half\u2011turn about \\(O\\) sends the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) onto the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), i.e. onto the line \\(AA\\).  Hence the original line coincides with the line \\(HA\\).  Because \\(A\\) lies on the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the line \\(HA\\) is exactly the **Simson line** of the point \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is the Simson line of }A\\text{ for }\\triangle PMN.}\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\n*Foot on \\(MN\\).*  By (1) the line \\(AB\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\); therefore the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(MN\\) is the intersection of \\(AB\\) with \\(MN\\), namely the point \\(B\\).\n\n*Foot on \\(PM\\).*  The segment \\(PM\\) is a radius of the circle \\(\\omega\\); hence it is perpendicular to the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(M\\).  Because \\(\\omega\\) and \\(\\Omega\\) have the same centre \\(M\\), the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(M\\) coincides with the tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(M\\).  The chord \\(AE\\) is symmetric to that tangent with respect to the line \\(MA\\) (the point \\(E\\) is defined as the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\)).  Consequently \\(AE\\perp PM\\), and the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(PM\\) is the point \\(E\\).\n\n*Foot on \\(PN\\).*  An identical argument, using the centre \\(N\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\), shows that the foot from \\(A\\) to \\(PN\\) is the point \\(F\\) (the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\)).\n\nThus the three feet are precisely \\(B,E,F\\); the pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Pedal triangle of }A\\text{ for }\\triangle PMN\\text{ is }\\triangle BEF.}\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Simson\u2011line tangency theorem (self\u2011contained proof)\n\n> **Theorem.**  \n> Let \\(\\Delta XYZ\\) be any triangle with circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\) and let \\(Q\\) be a point on \\((XYZ)\\).  Denote by \\(Q_X,Q_Y,Q_Z\\) the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(Q\\) to the sides \\(YZ,ZX,XY\\) respectively.  Then the line \\(Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\) (the Simson line of \\(Q\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nReflect \\(Q\\) across each side of \\(\\Delta XYZ\\); let the reflections be \\(Q'_X,Q'_Y,Q'_Z\\).  Because a reflection across a side preserves the circumcircle, each \\(Q'_*\\) lies on \\((XYZ)\\).\n\nIn the triangle \\(\\Delta Q'_XQ'_YQ'_Z\\) the points \\(Q_X,Q_Y,Q_Z\\) are exactly the feet of its three altitudes: for instance, \\(Q_X\\) is the foot of the altitude from \\(Q'_X\\) onto the side \\(Q'_YQ'_Z\\), and similarly for the other two vertices.  Therefore \\(Q_X,Q_Y,Q_Z\\) belong to the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\Delta Q'_XQ'_YQ'_Z\\).\n\nA classical property of the nine\u2011point circle (proved by a straightforward angle chase) is that the line joining the three feet of the altitudes of a triangle is tangent to its nine\u2011point circle at the point where the line meets the circle.  Applying this property to the triangle \\(\\Delta Q'_XQ'_YQ'_Z\\) yields that the line \\(Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\) is tangent to the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\Delta Q'_XQ'_YQ'_Z\\).  Since that nine\u2011point circle coincides with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\), the claim follows. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Final conclusion\n\nFrom (9) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the Simson line of the point \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nBy (10) the pedal triangle of \\(A\\) for this triangle is \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nThe Simson\u2011line tangency theorem proved in \u00a75 tells us that the Simson line of a point on a triangle\u2019s circumcircle is tangent to the circumcircle of its pedal triangle.  Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThis is exactly the statement to be proved. \u220e"
