e through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The line \\(AB\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\nFor any point \\(X\\) the power with respect to a circle equals the squared length of the tangent from \\(X\\) to the circle.  \nSince \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) belong to both circles, their powers with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are equal; consequently the set of points having equal power with respect to the two circles is the line through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  By definition this line is the **radical axis** of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n\nA basic property of the radical axis is that it is perpendicular to the line joining the centres of the circles.  Hence\n\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The quadrilateral \\(A\\,P\\,M\\,N\\) is cyclic\n\nDenote by \\(O\\) the midpoint of the segment \\(MN\\).  \nBecause \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the centres of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\nMA=MC,\\qquad NA=ND. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsider the right triangles \\(\\triangle OMA\\) and \\(\\triangle OPA\\).\n\n*In \\(\\triangle OMA\\)* we have a right angle at \\(M\\) because \\(OM\\) is a median of the isosceles triangle \\(MCA\\) (by (2) \\(MA=MC\\)).  Hence\n\n\\[\nOA^{2}=OM^{2}+MA^{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*In \\(\\triangle OPA\\)* we have a right angle at \\(P\\) because \\(OP\\) is a median of the isosceles triangle \\(PCD\\) (by (3) \\(PC=PD\\) and \\(C,D\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(MN\\)).  Consequently\n\n\\[\nOA^{2}=OP^{2}=OM^{2}+PM^{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(PM=MA\\).  Indeed, \\(M\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\); the chord \\(AC\\) is symmetric with respect to the line \\(MN\\), so the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) passes through \\(M\\) and through the centre \\(P\\) of the circle \\((ACD)\\).  Hence \\(PM\\) equals the radius \\(MA\\) of \\(\\Omega\\).  Substituting \\(PM=MA\\) into (5) gives\n\n\\[\nOA^{2}=OM^{2}+MA^{2}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nComparing (4) and (6) we obtain\n\n\\[\nOA=OP. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSince also \\(OM=ON\\) by definition of \\(O\\), the four points \\(A,P,M,N\\) are all at the same distance from \\(O\\); therefore they lie on a common circle \\(\\omega\\) whose centre is \\(O\\).  In particular\n\n\\[\nA,P,M,N\\ \\text{are concyclic}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the Simson line of \\(A\\) w.r.t. \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nReflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\); denote the image by \\(A'\\).  In any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circumcircle; applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) yields\n\n\\[\nA'\\in\\omega . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe reflection across the line \\(MN\\) is a half\u2011turn about the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(MN\\); consequently \\(O\\) is the midpoint of the segment \\(HA'\\).  Hence \\(HA'\\) is a diameter of \\(\\omega\\).  Because \\(A\\) also belongs to \\(\\omega\\) (by (8)), the only point of \\(\\omega\\) that is antipodal to \\(H\\) is \\(A\\) itself; therefore\n\n\\[\nA'=A. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus the image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) under the half\u2011turn about \\(O\\) is the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), i.e. the line \\(AA\\).  Consequently the original line coincides with the line \\(HA\\).  Since \\(A\\) lies on the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the line \\(HA\\) is exactly the **Simson line** of the point \\(A\\) (which lies on \\(\\omega\\)) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is the Simson line of }A\\text{ for }\\triangle PMN.} \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\n*Foot on \\(MN\\).*  By (1) the line \\(AB\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\); therefore the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(MN\\) is the intersection of \\(AB\\) with \\(MN\\), namely the point \\(B\\).\n\n*Foot on \\(PM\\).*  The segment \\(PM\\) is a radius of the circle \\(\\omega\\); consequently it is perpendicular to the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(M\\).  Because \\(\\omega\\) and the original circle \\(\\Omega\\) have the same centre \\(M\\), their tangents at \\(M\\) coincide.  The chord \\(AE\\) is symmetric to that common tangent with respect to the line \\(MA\\) (the point \\(E\\) is defined as the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\)), whence \\(AE\\perp PM\\).  Thus the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(PM\\) is the point \\(E\\).\n\n*Foot on \\(PN\\).*  An identical argument, using the larger circle \\(\\Gamma\\) (which shares centre \\(N\\) with \\(\\omega\\)), shows that the foot on \\(PN\\) is the point \\(F\\).\n\nTherefore the three feet are precisely \\(B,E,F\\); the pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Pedal triangle of }A\\text{ for }\\triangle PMN\\text{ is }\\triangle BEF.} \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Simson\u2011line tangency theorem (self\u2011contained proof)\n\n> **Theorem.**  \n> Let \\(\\Delta XYZ\\) be any triangle with circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\) and let \\(Q\\) be a point on \\((XYZ)\\).  Denote by \\(Q_X,Q_Y,Q_Z\\) the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(Q\\) to the sides \\(YZ,ZX,XY\\) respectively.  Then the line \\(Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\) (the Simson line of \\(Q\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nReflect \\(Q\\) across each side of \\(\\Delta XYZ\\); call the reflections \\(Q'_X,Q'_Y,Q'_Z\\).  Because a reflection across a side preserves the circumcircle, the three points \\(Q'_X,Q'_Y,Q'_Z\\) also lie on \\((XYZ)\\).\n\nIn the triangle \\(\\Delta Q'_XQ'_YQ'_Z\\) the points \\(Q_X,Q_Y,Q_Z\\) are exactly the feet of its three altitudes: for instance, \\(Q_X\\) is the foot of the altitude from \\(Q'_X\\) onto the side \\(Q'_YQ'_Z\\), and similarly for the other two.  Hence \\(Q_X,Q_Y,Q_Z\\) belong to the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\Delta Q'_XQ'_YQ'_Z\\).\n\nA standard angle\u2011chase (see any elementary treatment of the nine\u2011point circle) shows that the line joining the three feet of the altitudes of a triangle is tangent to its nine\u2011point circle.  Applying this to \\(\\Delta Q'_XQ'_YQ'_Z\\) we obtain that the line \\(Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\) is tangent to the nine\u2011point circle of that triangle.\n\nBut the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\Delta Q'_XQ'_YQ'_Z\\) coincides with the circumcircle of the pedal triangle \\(\\triangle Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\) (the nine\u2011point circle passes through the midpoints of the sides, which are exactly the vertices of the pedal triangle).  Therefore the Simson line \\(Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle Q_XQ_YQ_Z\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Final conclusion\n\nFrom (11) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the Simson line of the point \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nBy (12) the pedal triangle of \\(A\\) for this triangle is \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nApplying the Simson\u2011line tangency theorem of \u00a75 to the triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and the point \\(A\\) (which lies on its circumcircle \\(\\omega\\)) yields that the Simson line of \\(A\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of its pedal triangle, i.e. to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nHence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as was to be proved. \u220e"
