ent to its nine\u2011point circle.\u201d  \n  *Issue:* Justification Gap \u2013 the \u201cdirect angle chase\u201d is not supplied; the statement is non\u2011trivial and requires a detailed proof.\n* **Location:** Overall use of the \u201cSimson\u2011line tangency theorem\u201d without a rigorous proof and with an incorrect identification of the Simson line in this configuration.  \n  *Issue:* Critical Error \u2013 because the earlier steps that identify the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) as the Simson line of \\(A\\) are false, the final application of the theorem is invalid.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Verification Log**\n\n---\n\n### Step 1 \u2013 Radical axis\n\n> \u201cFor any point \\(X\\) the power with respect to a circle equals the squared length of the tangent from \\(X\\) to the circle. Since \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) belong to both circles, their powers \u2026 are equal; consequently the set of points having equal power \u2026 is the line through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).\u201d\n\n*Correctness:* The statement that the radical axis of two circles is the locus of points with equal power is standard. Since \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) lie on both circles, they belong to this locus, and the radical axis is indeed the line \\(AB\\). The perpendicularity \\(AB\\perp MN\\) follows from the well\u2011known property that the radical axis is perpendicular to the line of centers.  \n*Verdict:* **Correct** \u2013 no issue.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2 \u2013 Claim that \\(A,P,M,N\\) are concyclic\n\n> \u201cBecause \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the centres \u2026 we have \\(MA=MC,\\ NA=ND\\). \u2026 Since \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), \\(PA=PC=PD\\). \u2026 Consider the powers of the point \\(O\\) (midpoint of \\(MN\\)) \u2026 Using (2) we obtain \\(\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(O)=\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(O)\\). \u2026 Consequently the two circles intersect the line \\(OA\\) at points that are equidistant from \\(O\\); in other words \\(OA^{2}=OP^{2}\\). \u2026 Hence